short-loop streaming is a bad trade, because bypassing `io_imemReady` with an IF-side loop buffer still adds fetch/decode control state (`stream_valid/pos/start_pc/branch_pc`, local-instruction muxing, and hazard special-casing) that costs more Fmax than CoreMark recovers from hiding tiny-loop backedges and random imem stalls.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r9s0 (regression, -3.46%): On `bench`, replacing `rtl/alu.sv`'s separate `mul_ss`/`mul_uu`/`mul_su` products with one opcode-configured 33x33 multiplier regressed, implying the EX bottleneck is not raw multiply duplication and that shared signedness control plus high-half fanout can hurt FPGA DSP mapping/routing more than parallel product expressions.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r9s1 (regression, -12.29%): On `bench`, even a predictor-free two-slot sequential fetch queue regressed, which suggests the extra head/lookahead valid-shift/refill/redirect-clear control in `if_stage` and starvation-based hazard gating costs more Fmax than CoreMark recovers from hiding isolated `io_imemReady` stalls.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r9s2 (regression, -49.09%): On `bench`, moving a backedge predictor's tag match into ID and consuming only registered `predicted_taken`/`predicted_target` state the next cycle still regressed, so the real constraint is not just same-cycle IF compare but the whole predicted-PC carry, EX repair compare, and fetch-target mux plumbing, which costs more Fmax than CoreMark gains from shaving one loop-backedge bubble.","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md","time":{"start":1778872620917,"end":1778872620930}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_024f9a8beb743257016a07712cc4b48199a2df40a53b9d2cb5"}},"id":"prt_e2d121760001WWT0bafvIxK0CZ","sessionID":"ses_1d2ee29dbffeDS6MAKCPHUbUS7","messageID":"msg_e2d11d6880013UJYe1tkFPj6Hv"}}
