{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778876269830,"sessionID":"ses_1d2b93872ffeQM7BArOoRSWwDF","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_EHbbKLVh4O0uAZqdNx9Irr8n","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r12s2.yaml","offset":1,"limit":200},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r12s2.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: id: hyp-20260515-003-r12s2\n2: title: \"Champion-Challenger Backedge Fold Buffer\"\n3: category: predictor\n4: motivation: |\n5:   The predictor history on `bench` is unusually narrow: almost every branch or\n6:   fetch predictor that added lookup, redirect, or target-selection work to the\n7:   live frontend path regressed badly, but the single-slot backedge fold buffer\n8:   matched the current best fitness exactly. That strongly suggests there is\n9:   still predictor headroom, but only inside the same timing-safe fold-and-\n10:   inject shape that leaves `io_imemAddr` on the existing sequential path.\n11: \n12:   The likely remaining loss is one-slot thrash. CoreMark rotates among several\n13:   hot counted loops, so a single fold slot can keep relearning a different\n14:   backedge and give away otherwise-predictable taken-branch bubbles.\n15: hypothesis: |\n16:   Keep the fetch-time predictor lookup exactly one entry wide, but add a small\n17:   off-path challenger slot and repeat counter that are updated only from EX.\n18:   The active fold slot remains the only structure consulted in IF: same one tag\n19:   compare, same cached target instruction injection, same sequential\n20:   `io_imemAddr` path, and no new multi-entry select logic on the live fetch\n21:   cone.\n22: \n23:   When EX resolves a taken backward branch that does not match the active slot,\n24:   train it first into the challenger. Only if that challenger repeats does it\n25:   get promoted into the active fold slot. This attacks the likely one-slot\n26:   aliasing problem across CoreMark's list, matrix, and state loops without\n27:   repeating the two-entry fetch-lookup regression. In effect, hit-rate\n28:   improvement moves into EX-time training policy while IF timing stays at the\n29:   one-slot fold-buffer complexity that already proved safe.\n30: expected_impact:\n31:   fitness_delta_pct: 4\n32:   confidence: medium\n33: changes:\n34:   - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n35:     description: \"Add minimal fold-predictor sideband fields so a folded branch can carry active-slot metadata from IF through EX without changing RVFI-visible state.\"\n36:   - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n37:     description: \"Keep a single active backedge fold lookup on the fetch path, inject the cached target instruction on active hits, and maintain a shadow challenger record that is never consulted in the live PC path.\"\n38:   - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n39:     description: \"Latch the fold sideband alongside the decoded instruction so EX can distinguish active-slot predictions from ordinary sequential fetches.\"\n40:   - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n41:     description: \"Train taken backward branches into active and challenger slots, bump challenger confidence on repeats, and promote only repeated challengers into the active fold slot while keeping EX as the architectural source of truth for redirect recovery.\"\n42:   - file: rtl/core.sv\n43:     description: \"Wire predictor training, promotion, and fold sideband signals between IF and EX without changing the top-level nret=1 contract.\"\n\n(End of file - total 43 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260515-003-r12s2\ntitle: \"Champion-Challenger Backedge Fold Buffer\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The predictor history on `bench` is unusually narrow: almost every branch or\n  fetch predictor that added lookup, redirect, or target-selection work to the\n  live frontend path regressed badly, but the single-slot backedge fold buffer\n  matched the current best fitness exactly. That strongly suggests there is\n  still predictor headroom, but only inside the same timing-safe fold-and-\n  inject shape that leaves `io_imemAddr` on the existing sequential path.\n\n  The likely remaining loss is one-slot thrash. CoreMark rotates among several\n  hot counted loops, so a single fold slot can keep relearning a different\n  backedge and give away otherwise-predictable taken-branch bubbles.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep the fetch-time predictor lookup exactly one entry wide, but add a small\n  off-path challenger slot and repeat counter that are updated only from EX.\n  The active fold slot remains the only structure consulted in IF: same one tag\n  compare, same cached target instruction injection, same sequential\n  `io_imemAddr` path, and no new multi-entry select logic on the live fetch","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r12s2.yaml","time":{"start":1778876269827,"end":1778876269830}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0289d30dbfd39954016a077f6d573c8199b393e493b7db332e"}},"id":"prt_e2d49c384001JI5hiqLrqjDtxD","sessionID":"ses_1d2b93872ffeQM7BArOoRSWwDF","messageID":"msg_e2d49b168001KElFY5Z9zSAK12"}}
