{"type":"text","timestamp":1778877898337,"sessionID":"ses_1d29f1232ffek1ArWlQzcYbbGk","part":{"id":"prt_e2d628b9e001MGx8XDfWjI5q62","messageID":"msg_e2d616898001LceWeaDoAJsvP5","sessionID":"ses_1d29f1232ffek1ArWlQzcYbbGk","type":"text","text":"The untried space now looks mostly backend datapath cleanup, not control. I’m checking whether anyone already targeted the MEM load-align path before I write the YAML.","time":{"start":1778877893534,"end":1778877898337},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"msg_0f603802689d26fc016a0785c567ec819abfcb25840d644e0e","phase":"commentary"}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778877898363,"sessionID":"ses_1d29f1232ffek1ArWlQzcYbbGk","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_hVLl1TQ2E2dgv5bF2vt8ip4Q","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv","offset":1,"limit":260},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: // rtl/mem_stage.sv\n2: //\n3: // Memory access stage. Drives the dmem read/write ports combinationally\n4: // from the EX/MEM register, and sign/zero-extends the loaded data into\n5: // the MEM/WB register.\n6: //\n7: // Byte-lane discipline:\n8: //   - Write data is replicated across all four byte lanes; the\n9: //     byte-mask (mem_wmask) selects the actual destination bytes.\n10: //   - Load: shift the raw word right by `addr[1:0]*8`, then sign- or\n11: //     zero-extend the low 8/16 bits per the LB/LBU/LH/LHU encoding.\n12: //   - For RVFI ALIGNED_MEM, mem_addr is reported word-aligned and the\n13: //     byte position is captured in mem_rmask / mem_wmask.\n14: //\n15: // Latency:        1 cycle (MEM/WB register clocked here).\n16: // RVFI fields:    feeds mem_addr, mem_rmask, mem_wmask, mem_rdata,\n17: //                 mem_wdata, plus rd_wdata via the loaded-data path.\n18: module mem_stage (\n19:   input  logic               clock,\n20:   input  logic               reset,\n21:   input  logic               dmem_ready,\n22:   // hold_wb: dmem stall is in effect. MEM/WB's data fields are RETAINED\n23:   // (so the previously-retired LOAD's `rd` / `read_data` / `ctrl.reg_write`\n24:   // remain visible to forward_unit), but `valid` is cleared so:\n25:   //   - rvfi_order doesn't increment for held cycles\n26:   //   - wb_stage doesn't write the regfile twice\n27:   // Zeroing the whole register would lose the LOAD's load_data, breaking\n28:   // MEM/WB->EX forwarding for any dependent instruction that's also held\n29:   // in ID/EX during the stall (e.g. a BNE that consumes a LOAD's result).\n30:   input  logic               hold_wb,\n31:   // ex_mem_t carries branch_taken / branch_target / pc_next / rs?_val\n32:   // for downstream RVFI use; mem_stage only consumes a subset, so the\n33:   // remaining fields look unused from this module's perspective.\n34:   /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n35:   input  ex_mem_t  in,\n36:   /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n37:   // dmem interface\n38:   output logic [31:0]        dmem_addr,\n39:   output logic [31:0]        dmem_wdata,\n40:   input  logic [31:0]        dmem_rdata,\n41:   output logic [3:0]         dmem_wen,\n42:   output logic               dmem_ren,\n43:   output logic               store_bufferable,\n44:   output logic               store_slot_valid,\n45:   // MEM/WB register output\n46:   output mem_wb_t  out\n47: );\n48: \n49:   logic [31:0] wdata_rep;\n50:   logic [3:0]  byte_mask;\n51:   logic [31:0] aligned_addr;\n52:   // shifted[31:16] is dropped on byte/halfword loads; only [15:0] feeds\n53:   // the sign/zero-extension mux.\n54:   /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n55:   logic [31:0] shifted;\n56:   /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n57:   logic [7:0]  byte_val;\n58:   logic [15:0] hword_val;\n59:   logic [31:0] load_data;\n60:   logic        ram_store;\n61:   logic        capture_store;\n62: \n63:   // Misaligned mem-access trap. RV32I requires word-aligned LW/SW and\n64:   // halfword-aligned LH/LHU/SH; byte ops are always aligned.\n65:   // riscv-formal's RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM contract demands rvfi_trap=1\n66:   // when the effective byte address is not aligned to the access width.\n67:   // On trap, the dmem ports are gated off and ctrl propagates is_illegal.\n68:   logic mem_misalign;\n69:   logic mem_op;\n70:   ctrl_t ctrl_with_trap;\n71: \n72:   logic        store_slot_valid_q;\n73:   logic [31:0] store_slot_addr_q;\n74:   logic [31:0] store_slot_wdata_q;\n75:   logic [3:0]  store_slot_wmask_q;\n76: \n77:   always_comb begin\n78:     // Byte/halfword replication for stores.\n79:     case (in.ctrl.mem_width)\n80:       2'd0:    wdata_rep = {4{in.write_data[7:0]}};\n81:       2'd1:    wdata_rep = {2{in.write_data[15:0]}};\n82:       default: wdata_rep = in.write_data;\n83:     endcase\n84: \n85:     // Byte-lane mask shifted to addr[1:0] * 1.\n86:     case (in.ctrl.mem_width)\n87:       2'd0:    byte_mask = (4'b0001 << in.alu_result[1:0]);\n88:       2'd1:    byte_mask = (4'b0011 << in.alu_result[1:0]);\n89:       default: byte_mask = 4'b1111;\n90:     endcase\n91: \n92:     mem_op       = in.ctrl.mem_read || in.ctrl.mem_write;\n93:     mem_misalign = mem_op && (\n94:                      (in.ctrl.mem_width == 2'd2 && in.alu_result[1:0] != 2'b00) ||\n95:                      (in.ctrl.mem_width == 2'd1 && in.alu_result[0]   != 1'b0)\n96:                      // 2'd0 (byte) is never misaligned.\n97:                    );\n98: \n99:     ctrl_with_trap = in.ctrl;\n100:     if (mem_misalign) begin\n101:       ctrl_with_trap.is_illegal = 1'b1;\n102:       ctrl_with_trap.reg_write  = 1'b0;\n103:     end\n104: \n105:     // Load extraction: shift right then sign/zero-extend the low N bits.\n106:     shifted   = dmem_rdata >> (in.alu_result[1:0] * 8);\n107:     byte_val  = shifted[7:0];\n108:     hword_val = shifted[15:0];\n109:     case (in.ctrl.mem_width)\n110:       2'd0:    load_data = in.ctrl.mem_sext\n111:                          ? {{24{byte_val[7]}},  byte_val}\n112:                          : {24'b0,              byte_val};\n113:       2'd1:    load_data = in.ctrl.mem_sext\n114:                          ? {{16{hword_val[15]}}, hword_val}\n115:                          : {16'b0,               hword_val};\n116:       default: load_data = dmem_rdata;\n117:     endcase\n118: \n119:     // RVFI ALIGNED_MEM expects word-aligned mem_addr; mem_addr=0 if no access.\n120:     aligned_addr = (mem_op && !mem_misalign)\n121:                  ? {in.alu_result[31:2], 2'b00}\n122:                  : 32'b0;\n123: \n124:     ram_store        = in.valid && in.ctrl.mem_write && !mem_misalign\n125:                     && (in.alu_result < 32'h0010_0000);\n126:     store_bufferable = ram_store;\n127:     capture_store    = ram_store && !dmem_ready && !store_slot_valid_q;\n128: \n129:     if (store_slot_valid_q) begin\n130:       dmem_addr  = store_slot_addr_q;\n131:       dmem_wdata = store_slot_wdata_q;\n132:       dmem_wen   = store_slot_wmask_q;\n133:       dmem_ren   = 1'b0;\n134:     end else begin\n135:       dmem_addr  = in.alu_result;\n136:       dmem_wdata = wdata_rep;\n137:       dmem_wen   = (in.ctrl.mem_write && !mem_misalign) ? byte_mask : 4'b0000;\n138:       dmem_ren   = in.ctrl.mem_read  && !mem_misalign;\n139:     end\n140:   end\n141: \n142:   // ── MEM/WB register ───────────────────────────────────────────────────\n143:   mem_wb_t reg_q;\n144: \n145:   always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n146:     if (reset) begin\n147:       reg_q <= '0;\n148:       store_slot_valid_q <= 1'b0;\n149:       store_slot_addr_q  <= 32'b0;\n150:       store_slot_wdata_q <= 32'b0;\n151:       store_slot_wmask_q <= 4'b0;\n152:     end else begin\n153:       if (hold_wb) begin\n154:         // Clear valid (no double-retire / no double-regfile-write), but\n155:         // KEEP every other field. Forwarding from MEM/WB to a stalled\n156:         // dependent in ID/EX needs the held LOAD's rd/read_data/ctrl\n157:         // alive for as many cycles as the memory-side stall lasts.\n158:         reg_q.valid <= 1'b0;\n159:       end else begin\n160:         reg_q.pc         <= in.pc;\n161:         reg_q.alu_result <= in.alu_result;\n162:         reg_q.read_data  <= load_data;\n163:         reg_q.rd         <= in.rd;\n164:         reg_q.rs1_addr   <= in.rs1_addr;\n165:         reg_q.rs2_addr   <= in.rs2_addr;\n166:         reg_q.rs1_val    <= in.rs1_val;\n167:         reg_q.rs2_val    <= in.rs2_val;\n168:         reg_q.pc_next    <= in.pc_next;\n169:         reg_q.mem_addr   <= aligned_addr;\n170:         reg_q.mem_rdata  <= dmem_rdata;\n171:         reg_q.mem_wdata  <= wdata_rep;\n172:         reg_q.mem_wmask  <= (in.ctrl.mem_write && !mem_misalign) ? byte_mask : 4'b0000;\n173:         reg_q.mem_rmask  <= (in.ctrl.mem_read  && !mem_misalign) ? byte_mask : 4'b0000;\n174:         reg_q.ctrl       <= ctrl_with_trap;\n175:         reg_q.instr      <= in.instr;\n176:         reg_q.valid      <= in.valid;\n177:       end\n178: \n179:       if (capture_store) begin\n180:         store_slot_valid_q <= 1'b1;\n181:         store_slot_addr_q  <= in.alu_result;\n182:         store_slot_wdata_q <= wdata_rep;\n183:         store_slot_wmask_q <= byte_mask;\n184:       end else if (store_slot_valid_q && dmem_ready) begin\n185:         store_slot_valid_q <= 1'b0;\n186:       end\n187:     end\n188:   end\n189: \n190:   assign store_slot_valid = store_slot_valid_q;\n191:   assign out = reg_q;\n192: \n193: endmodule\n\n(End of file - total 193 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"// rtl/mem_stage.sv\n//\n// Memory access stage. Drives the dmem read/write ports combinationally\n// from the EX/MEM register, and sign/zero-extends the loaded data into\n// the MEM/WB register.\n//\n// Byte-lane discipline:\n//   - Write data is replicated across all four byte lanes; the\n//     byte-mask (mem_wmask) selects the actual destination bytes.\n//   - Load: shift the raw word right by `addr[1:0]*8`, then sign- or\n//     zero-extend the low 8/16 bits per the LB/LBU/LH/LHU encoding.\n//   - For RVFI ALIGNED_MEM, mem_addr is reported word-aligned and the\n//     byte position is captured in mem_rmask / mem_wmask.\n//\n// Latency:        1 cycle (MEM/WB register clocked here).\n// RVFI fields:    feeds mem_addr, mem_rmask, mem_wmask, mem_rdata,\n//                 mem_wdata, plus rd_wdata via the loaded-data path.\nmodule mem_stage (\n  input  logic               clock,\n  input  logic               reset,","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv","time":{"start":1778877898359,"end":1778877898362}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0f603802689d26fc016a0785ca37a0819a965c6fc0825f529e"}},"id":"prt_e2d629e74002dDSzz89d8e2Zzb","sessionID":"ses_1d29f1232ffek1ArWlQzcYbbGk","messageID":"msg_e2d616898001LceWeaDoAJsvP5"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778877898363,"sessionID":"ses_1d29f1232ffek1ArWlQzcYbbGk","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_ERkPgosyA4AwQKpZQfT4ANQI","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/wb_stage.sv","offset":1,"limit":120},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/wb_stage.sv</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: // rtl/wb_stage.sv\n2: //\n3: // Write-back stage. Pure combinational mux that selects between the\n4: // loaded-data path (mem_to_reg=1) and the ALU-result path. Drives the\n5: // regfile write port; the regfile itself stalls on x0 and gates on\n6: // w_en.\n7: //\n8: // Latency:        combinational.\n9: // RVFI fields:    feeds rd_wdata (= w_data when w_en=1, else 0).\n10: module wb_stage (\n11:   // mem_wb_t fields beyond reg_write/rd/mem_to_reg/read_data/alu_result\n12:   // (pc, mem_*, rs?_*, instr) are RVFI-only and read at top level.\n13:   /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n14:   input  mem_wb_t  in,\n15:   /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n16:   output logic               w_en,\n17:   output logic [4:0]         w_addr,\n18:   output logic [31:0]        w_data\n19: );\n20: \n21:   always_comb begin\n22:     w_en   = in.ctrl.reg_write && in.valid;\n23:     w_addr = in.rd;\n24:     w_data = in.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? in.read_data : in.alu_result;\n25:   end\n26: \n27: endmodule\n\n(End of file - total 27 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"// rtl/wb_stage.sv\n//\n// Write-back stage. Pure combinational mux that selects between the\n// loaded-data path (mem_to_reg=1) and the ALU-result path. Drives the\n// regfile write port; the regfile itself stalls on x0 and gates on\n// w_en.\n//\n// Latency:        combinational.\n// RVFI fields:    feeds rd_wdata (= w_data when w_en=1, else 0).\nmodule wb_stage (\n  // mem_wb_t fields beyond reg_write/rd/mem_to_reg/read_data/alu_result\n  // (pc, mem_*, rs?_*, instr) are RVFI-only and read at top level.\n  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n  input  mem_wb_t  in,\n  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n  output logic               w_en,\n  output logic [4:0]         w_addr,\n  output logic [31:0]        w_data\n);\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/rtl/wb_stage.sv","time":{"start":1778877898360,"end":1778877898363}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0f603802689d26fc016a0785ca37ac819a87ba659de81ae34c"}},"id":"prt_e2d629e77002SxjNtPTeQopTWu","sessionID":"ses_1d29f1232ffek1ArWlQzcYbbGk","messageID":"msg_e2d616898001LceWeaDoAJsvP5"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778877898365,"sessionID":"ses_1d29f1232ffek1ArWlQzcYbbGk","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_6UqToRjJee8TXfoiexnBioy2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"shift|sign-extend|mem_width|load_data|byte_mask|rmask|wmask|barrel|align","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments","include":"log.jsonl"},"output":"Found 9 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Iterative Cold Divider Off EX Path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The frozen bench core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\ncombinational ALU path as ADD, address generation, branch support, and\\nMUL. On a Tang Nano 20K, a full 32-bit divider is a strong candidate for\\nthe universal EX-stage critical path, yet CoreMark's timed loop is mostly\\nmultiply, load/store, and branch traffic and should pay little or no steady\\nstate penalty from slower divide instructions.\\n\\nThis makes divider extraction the highest-leverage micro-opt still missing\\nfrom the frozen bench fixture: it targets Fmax directly while preserving the\\nhot one-cycle behavior for the instructions that dominate CoreMark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The frozen bench core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\ncombinational ALU path as ADD, address generation, branch support, and\\nMUL. On a Tang Nano 20K, a full 32-bit divider is a strong candidate for\\nthe universal EX-stage critical path, yet CoreMark's timed loop is mostly\\nmultiply, load/store, and branch traffic and should pay little or no steady\\nstate penalty from slower divide instructions.\\n\\nThis makes divider extraction the highest-leverage micro-opt still missing\\nfrom the frozen bench fixture: it targets Fmax directly while preserving the\\nhot one-cycle behavior for the instructions that dominate CoreMark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases in `rtl/alu.sv`\\nwith a small iterative divider/remainder unit that is entered only for\\ndivide-class ALU ops. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift/compare, address generation,\\nbranches, jumps, loads/stores, and MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing\\nsingle-cycle datapath.\\n\\nIn EX, detect divide-class operations, latch their operands and control,\\nstall IF and ID while the divider runs, and emit exactly one EX/MEM result\\nwhen the quotient or remainder is ready. Preserve RV32IM edge cases\\nprecisely: divide-...\n  Line 5: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"RTL-only hot/cold ALU opcode split\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best bench result already removed DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU from the\\nhot one-cycle EX path, but `rtl/alu.sv` still uses a dense ALU-op encoding\\nand one broad result case covering common RV32I work, multiply results, and\\ndummy divide-class outputs. That leaves the always-hot ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path sharing decode and mux structure with cold M-extension cases.\\n\\nA sibling-core experiment on `cores/v1` showed that this specific RTL-only\\nreshaping can improve fitness, while more aggressive multiplier rewrites and\\nsource-qualified hazard logic either regressed or broke. That makes the ALU\\nmux shape the best-supported remaining `micro_opt` angle for the frozen\\nbench fixture.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best bench result already removed DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU from the\\nhot one-cycle EX path, but `rtl/alu.sv` still uses a dense ALU-op encoding\\nand one broad result case covering common RV32I work, multiply results, and\\ndummy divide-class outputs. That leaves the always-hot ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path sharing decode and mux structure with cold M-extension cases.\\n\\nA sibling-core experiment on `cores/v1` showed that this specific RTL-only\\nreshaping can improve fitness, while more aggressive multiplier rewrites and\\nsource-qualified hazard logic either regressed or broke. That makes the ALU\\nmux shape the best-supported remaining `micro_opt` angle for the frozen\\nbench fixture.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Re-encode the ALU operation constants so bit 4 cleanly marks RV32M\\noperations, with MUL/MULH/MULHSU/MULHU/DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU grouped as a cold\\n`op[4]=1` class while the common RV32I/simple operations stay in the hot\\n`op[4]=0` class. Then refactor `rtl/alu.sv` into two local result muxes:\\na simple-result mux for ADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI and an M-result mux\\nfor multiply plus the existing placeholder divide outputs use...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench core's biggest proven gain came from a backend-only timing\\nchange: moving DIV/REM off the one-cycle EX path. The recent failed branch\\nprediction attempt showed the opposite boundary on this RTL: pulling more\\ndecode/target work into the frontend can erase the IPC gain with Fmax loss.\\n\\nThe next structural hypothesis should therefore stay off the fetch path and\\nattack the other obvious whole-core bubble source. Today any `io_dmemReady=0`\\ncycle on a STORE freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM even though the store address,\\nreplicated data, and byte mask are already known in MEM, and CoreMark does a\\nsteady stream of normal-RAM stores to stack, list, matrix, and state data.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench core's biggest proven gain came from a backend-only timing\\nchange: moving DIV/REM off the one-cycle EX path. The recent failed branch\\nprediction attempt showed the opposite boundary on this RTL: pulling more\\ndecode/target work into the frontend can erase the IPC gain with Fmax loss.\\n\\nThe next structural hypothesis should therefore stay off the fetch path and\\nattack the other obvious whole-core bubble source. Today any `io_dmemReady=0`\\ncycle on a STORE freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM even though the store address,\\nreplicated data, and byte mask are already known in MEM, and CoreMark does a\\nsteady stream of normal-RAM stores to stack, list, matrix, and state data.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot with a direct ready-store fast\\npath. When an aligned STORE to normal RAM `[0x00000000, 0x00100000)` reaches\\nMEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, capture its byte address, replicated write\\ndata, byte mask, and RVFI-visible memory metadata into the slot, retire that\\nstore once, and drain the physical dmem write oldest-first after the stall.\\n\\nKeep the zero-wait STORE path identical to the current d...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Tiny MEM-stage load replay buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The two successful structural changes on `bench` both stayed off the fetch\\nand hot EX paths: iterative DIV/REM and the one-deep store retirement slot.\\nThe remaining backend stall source is load-side dmem backpressure. Today a\\nnormal-RAM load freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM whenever `io_dmemReady=0`, even\\nif the same aligned word was returned recently and CoreMark's 6 KB working\\nset is revisiting it.\\n\\nThat makes load-side stall hiding the cleanest remaining structural lever:\\nit attacks iterations/cycle without pulling new decode or prediction logic\\nonto the frontend path that already regressed on prior bench attempts.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two successful structural changes on `bench` both stayed off the fetch\\nand hot EX paths: iterative DIV/REM and the one-deep store retirement slot.\\nThe remaining backend stall source is load-side dmem backpressure. Today a\\nnormal-RAM load freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM whenever `io_dmemReady=0`, even\\nif the same aligned word was returned recently and CoreMark's 6 KB working\\nset is revisiting it.\\n\\nThat makes load-side stall hiding the cleanest remaining structural lever:\\nit attacks iterations/cycle without pulling new decode or prediction logic\\nonto the frontend path that already regressed on prior bench attempts.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny normal-RAM load replay structure in MEM, such as a small\\ndirect-mapped raw-word buffer keyed by aligned word address. Live ready\\nloads keep using `io_dmemRData` exactly as they do today and refresh the\\nreplay entry for that word. When `io_dmemReady=0`, a legal aligned\\nnormal-RAM load may complete from the replay buffer on a tag hit: use the\\ncached raw word for sign/zero extension and RVFI `mem_rdata`, suppress the\\nglobal dmem wait for that instruction, and let the pipeline keep retiring in\\norder.\\n\\nReplay must stay conservative. Disable it for...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Deferred Store Queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\\nthat avoid adding logic to the fetch path: first moving DIV/REM off EX, then\\nbuffering one stalled RAM store. The current one-deep store slot still\\nserializes store bursts behind a single pending write, even though CoreMark\\nissues clustered stack, list, matrix, and state stores under the random\\n`io_dmemReady` stall model.\\n\\nA deeper store queue is the next clean extension of the one change that has\\nalready proven it can raise IPC on this RTL without paying the frontend Fmax\\npenalties seen in prior predictor experiments.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\\nthat avoid adding logic to the fetch path: first moving DIV/REM off EX, then\\nbuffering one stalled RAM store. The current one-deep store slot still\\nserializes store bursts behind a single pending write, even though CoreMark\\nissues clustered stack, list, matrix, and state stores under the random\\n`io_dmemReady` stall model.\\n\\nA deeper store queue is the next clean extension of the one change that has\\nalready proven it can raise IPC on this RTL without paying the frontend Fmax\\npenalties seen in prior predictor experiments.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the current one-deep pending-store slot with a two-entry oldest-first\\ndeferred store queue for aligned normal-RAM stores. When `io_dmemReady` is\\nlow, the oldest queued store keeps draining to the physical bus while a\\nyounger bufferable store can still retire into the second queue entry instead\\nof freezing the whole pipeline immediately.\\n\\nKeep the existing zero-wait direct-store fast path when the queue is empty,\\nand preserve architectural ordering by continuing to fence younger loads,\\nnon-bufferable stores, and EBREAK behind any queued store. This should hide\\nmore of CoreMark's store-s...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Store-Data Forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The proven `bench` wins so far have all stayed off the fragile frontend:\\nmoving DIV/REM off the universal EX path and adding the one-deep deferred\\nstore slot. The failed predictor, load-replay, and deeper-store-queue tries\\ndefine the boundary clearly: extra fetch-path logic and wider MEM-side\\nbuffering cost too much Fmax or placement quality on this FPGA.\\n\\nOne structural bubble source remains in the existing 5-stage pipeline. A\\nload followed immediately by a STORE of that loaded register still pays the\\nfull textbook load-use stall even when the STORE only needs the value as\\nwrite data (`rs2`), not as its address base (`rs1`). That dependency does\\nnot need an EX-stage bubble because store data is not consumed until MEM.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The proven `bench` wins so far have all stayed off the fragile frontend:\\nmoving DIV/REM off the universal EX path and adding the one-deep deferred\\nstore slot. The failed predictor, load-replay, and deeper-store-queue tries\\ndefine the boundary clearly: extra fetch-path logic and wider MEM-side\\nbuffering cost too much Fmax or placement quality on this FPGA.\\n\\nOne structural bubble source remains in the existing 5-stage pipeline. A\\nload followed immediately by a STORE of that loaded register still pays the\\nfull textbook load-use stall even when the STORE only needs the value as\\nwrite data (`rs2`), not as its address base (`rs1`). That dependency does\\nnot need an EX-stage bubble because store data is not consumed until MEM.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move STORE data consumption to the stage where the core actually uses it.\\nKeep STORE address generation in EX exactly as it is today, but stop\\ntreating a load->STORE `rs2` match as an EX-time hazard. Instead, let the\\nSTORE advance, carry its `rs2` register number and provisional value through\\nEX/MEM, and in `mem_stage` select the final store data...\n  Line 23: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Zero-Bubble Store Slot Refill\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` core already proved that deferred normal-RAM stores\\nare a winning backend lever, but the one-deep slot still loses a bubble\\nwhenever a pending store finally drains and the next instruction in MEM is\\nanother bufferable RAM store. Hazard control fences that younger store until\\nthe slot bit clears, so bursty CoreMark store traffic still pays a handoff\\npenalty even though the core already knows both stores' addr/data/mask and\\nonly needs to preserve oldest-first external visibility.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` core already proved that deferred normal-RAM stores\\nare a winning backend lever, but the one-deep slot still loses a bubble\\nwhenever a pending store finally drains and the next instruction in MEM is\\nanother bufferable RAM store. Hazard control fences that younger store until\\nthe slot bit clears, so bursty CoreMark store traffic still pays a handoff\\npenalty even though the core already knows both stores' addr/data/mask and\\nonly needs to preserve oldest-first external visibility.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Teach the existing single deferred-store slot to drain and refill in the same\\ncycle instead of growing it into a true two-entry queue. When\\n`store_slot_valid` is set and `io_dmemReady` returns, `mem_stage` should\\ndrive the older deferred store to the bus and, if the current EX/MEM\\ninstruction is another aligned normal-RAM store, immediately capture that\\nyounger store into the same slot while retiring it once through RVFI.\\n\\n`hazard_unit` would then keep fencing loads, MMIO/non-bufferable stores, and\\n`EBREAK` behind a pending slot, but allow exactly one younger bufferable RAM\\nstore through on the drain cycle for a refill. This keeps the proven\\nbackend-only store-buffering strategy, avoids the placement/routing cost that\\nkilled the real two-entry queue, and should recover part of the st...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Decoupled Sequential Fetch Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current `bench` frontend still couples decode directly to the live imem\\nresponse: `if_stage` emits a combinational IF/ID bundle from the current PC,\\nand `hazard_unit` turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into an immediate bubble.\\nThat leaves two costs on the table under the tournament's random iStall model:\\nisolated fetch stalls always reduce IPC, and the raw imem-to-decode path stays\\nin the timing cone.\\n\\nThe recent negative lessons only rule out fetch-side prediction and replay\\nstructures that add tag compares, target muxing, or decode work onto the live\\n`pc -> next fetch` path. They do not rule out a simpler decoupling structure\\nthat stays purely sequential and flushes on EX redirect.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current `bench` frontend still couples decode directly to the live imem\\nresponse: `if_stage` emits a combinational IF/ID bundle from the current PC,\\nand `hazard_unit` turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into an immediate bubble.\\nThat leaves two costs on the table under the tournament's random iStall model:\\nisolated fetch stalls always reduce IPC, and the raw imem-to-decode path stays\\nin the timing cone.\\n\\nThe recent negative lessons only rule out fetch-side prediction and replay\\nstructures that add tag compares, target muxing, or decode work onto the live\\n`pc -> next fetch` path. They do not rule out a simpler decoupling structure\\nthat stays purely sequential and flushes on EX redirect.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the current combinational IF/ID feed with a tiny sequential fetch\\nbuffer: a registered oldest instruction plus one lookahead slot for the next\\nfallthrough word. `if_stage` keeps prefetching sequential `pc`/`instr` pairs\\nwhenever the buffer has space and `io_imemReady` is high, while ID consumes\\nthe oldest buffered instruction only when it is not stalled.\\n\\nThis changes the frontend structurally w...\n  Line 35: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Compact MEM/WB Payload and Registered Rd Data\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current `bench` champion already found the big wins that stay off the\\nfragile fetch path: cold divide offload and a one-deep deferred store slot.\\nThe remaining always-hot backend cone still carries avoidable late-stage\\nbaggage, though. `mem_wb_t` stores both `alu_result` and `read_data` even\\nthough only one can ever become architectural `rd_wdata`, and the full\\n`ctrl_t` bundle is dragged all the way into WB even though only a tiny subset\\nis still live there.\\n\\nOn a Tang Nano 20K, that extra register width, control fanout, and the\\nMEM/WB-to-WB select mux all sit on a path that also feeds regfile writeback,\\nsame-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding, and RVFI `rd_wdata`. This looks\\nlike the cleanest remaining Fmax-focused `micro_opt` that does not revisit\\nthe broken hazard-source space or the already-regressive fetch-side ideas.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current `bench` champion already found the big wins that stay off the\\nfragile fetch path: cold divide offload and a one-deep deferred store slot.\\nThe remaining always-hot backend cone still carries avoidable late-stage\\nbaggage, though. `mem_wb_t` stores both `alu_result` and `read_data` even\\nthough only one can ever become architectural `rd_wdata`, and the full\\n`ctrl_t` bundle is dragged all the way into WB even though only a tiny subset\\nis still live there.\\n\\nOn a Tang Nano 20K, that extra register width, control fanout, and the\\nMEM/WB-to-WB select mux all sit on a path that also feeds regfile writeback,\\nsame-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding, and RVFI `rd_wdata`. This looks\\nlike the cleanest remaining Fmax-focused `micro_opt` that does not revisit\\nthe broken hazard-source space or the already-regressive fetch-side ideas.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Retire the MEM/WB stage around the architectural result that downstream logic\\nactually consumes. In `mem_stage`...","metadata":{"matches":9,"truncated":false},"title":"shift|sign-extend|mem_width|load_data|byte_mask|rmask|wmask|barrel|align","time":{"start":1778877898356,"end":1778877898365}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0f603802689d26fc016a0785ca378c819a9ace6eb04478f6f5"}},"id":"prt_e2d629e62001VbFUBWY9bh5nUX","sessionID":"ses_1d29f1232ffek1ArWlQzcYbbGk","messageID":"msg_e2d616898001LceWeaDoAJsvP5"}}
