{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778877967693,"sessionID":"ses_1d29f1232ffek1ArWlQzcYbbGk","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_USE3NEeyNgpHgbEMLkvji6Av","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r13s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r13s0\n+title: \"Word-Fast MEM Load Aligner\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The current champion already found the two large backend wins that stay off\n+  the fragile fetch path, but the MEM load lane is still relatively generic.\n+  `rtl/mem_stage.sv` always builds a variable right-shifted `dmem_rdata`\n+  view and generic shifted byte masks before selecting LB/LH/LW behavior,\n+  even though CoreMark's hot working set is dominated by aligned word traffic\n+  and the recent MEM/WB regression showed this late load cone is timing\n+  sensitive.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Rewrite the MEM-side load/store byte-lane logic around width-specialized\n+  fast paths instead of a universal barrel-shift style aligner. Keep RVFI,\n+  misalignment trapping, and the existing store-buffer contract identical, but\n+  make `LW`/`SW` and aligned word-address traffic flow through the shortest\n+  direct path, with only small byte/half muxes on the colder subword cases.\n+\n+  In practice, `rtl/mem_stage.sv` would replace `shifted = dmem_rdata >>\n+  (addr*8)` and generic shifted masks with case-split lane extraction:\n+  direct `dmem_rdata` for word loads, a 2:1 halfword select keyed by\n+  `addr[1]`, a 4:1 byte select keyed by `addr[1:0]`, and explicit constant\n+  masks for SB/SH/SW. That targets the sensitive\n+  `dmem_rdata -> load_data -> MEM/WB` cone and the aligned-memory metadata\n+  path without reopening the already-regressive fetch, hazard, or deeper\n+  store-queue spaces.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Replace the generic shifted load-align and mask logic with width-specialized byte/half/word fast paths while preserving misalignment traps and RVFI ALIGNED_MEM metadata.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add focused subword load/store and misalignment coverage so the narrower MEM byte-lane implementation stays bit- and trap-exact.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r13s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r13s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r13s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r13s0.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r13s0\n+title: \"Word-Fast MEM Load Aligner\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The current champion already found the two large backend wins that stay off\n+  the fragile fetch path, but the MEM load lane is still relatively generic.\n+  `rtl/mem_stage.sv` always builds a variable right-shifted `dmem_rdata`\n+  view and generic shifted byte masks before selecting LB/LH/LW behavior,\n+  even though CoreMark's hot working set is dominated by aligned word traffic\n+  and the recent MEM/WB regression showed this late load cone is timing\n+  sensitive.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Rewrite the MEM-side load/store byte-lane logic around width-specialized\n+  fast paths instead of a universal barrel-shift style aligner. Keep RVFI,\n+  misalignment trapping, and the existing store-buffer contract identical, but\n+  make `LW`/`SW` and aligned word-address traffic flow through the shortest\n+  direct path, with only small byte/half muxes on the colder subwor