... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny EX-trained taken-target cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The two failed predictor attempts exposed the boundary on this core: bench\\ndoes not tolerate extra decode, immediate-generation, or replay-control work\\non the frontend critical path. But CoreMark is still rich in tight counted\\nloops and unconditional jumps, so always waiting for EX to redirect leaves\\npredictable control-flow bubbles on the table.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is a much smaller one: predict only from\\nalready-resolved history, with a fetch-side structure light enough that the\\nFmax cost stays below the IPC gain.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two failed predictor attempts exposed the boundary on this core: bench\\ndoes not tolerate extra decode, immediate-generation, or replay-control work\\non the frontend critical path. But CoreMark is still rich in tight counted\\nloops and unconditional jumps, so always waiting for EX to redirect leaves\\npredictable control-flow bubbles on the table.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is a much smaller one: predict only from\\nalready-resolved history, with a fetch-side structure light enough that the\\nFmax cost stays below the IPC gain.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small direct-mapped taken-target cache in IF, trained only by\\ncontrol-flow instructions once EX has already resolved their real next PC.\\nOn a tag hit, IF speculatively uses the cached target instead of `pc + 4`;\\non a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is today. This avoids any ID-stage\\nbranch decode, immediate reconstruction, or same-cycle target add.\\n\\nThe predictor should only cache taken conditional branches and unconditional\\njumps, with EX comparing the predicted next PC against the architectural one\\nand asserting recovery only on mismatch. A last-taken policy is a good fit\\nfor CoreMark's loop backedges: it predicts the many taken iterations while\\nmissing only on loop exit, and the hardware cost is just ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Stage-Accurate Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"After divider offload and the one-deep store retirement slot, the cheapest\\nremaining CoreMark IPC loss is likely the still-conservative load-use\\ninterlock. `hazard_unit` compares a load destination against raw IF/ID rs1\\nand rs2 bitfields even when the younger instruction does not actually need\\nthose fields in EX, and it still forces a bubble for STORE rs2 even though\\nstore data is only consumed one stage later in MEM.\\n\\nThat means the core can still lose cycles to false immediate-field matches\\nand to real load-then-store-data dependencies that do not protect any EX\\ncritical operand. This is a narrow backend/control cleanup that attacks IPC\\nwithout adding predictor state, fetch-path muxing, or a new EX datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"After divider offload and the one-deep store retirement slot, the cheapest\\nremaining CoreMark IPC loss is likely the still-conservative load-use\\ninterlock. `hazard_unit` compares a load destination against raw IF/ID rs1\\nand rs2 bitfields even when the younger instruction does not actually need\\nthose fields in EX, and it still forces a bubble for STORE rs2 even though\\nstore data is only consumed one stage later in MEM.\\n\\nThat means the core can still lose cycles to false immediate-field matches\\nand to real load-then-store-data dependencies that do not protect any EX\\ncritical operand. This is a narrow backend/control cleanup that attacks IPC\\nwithout adding predictor state, fetch-path muxing, or a new EX datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make the load-use detector stage-accurate instead of field-accurate.\\nDecode conservative `uses_rs1_ex` / `uses_rs2_ex` bits from the IF/ID\\ninstruction inside `hazard_unit`, and only stall when 