{"type":"text","timestamp":1778879557405,"sessionID":"ses_1d28632e1ffeyB5vXCsqgnd27t","part":{"id":"prt_e2d7bef1a001Fqd7KNCSsTOWVs","messageID":"msg_e2d7b45b10019xMjVvmbTThiK1","sessionID":"ses_1d28632e1ffeyB5vXCsqgnd27t","type":"text","text":"Writing the hypothesis YAML now.","time":{"start":1778879557402,"end":1778879557404},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"msg_0850ecb642e3d2e6016a078c4545f8819bb2695e4f3799a2e1","phase":"commentary"}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778879568821,"sessionID":"ses_1d28632e1ffeyB5vXCsqgnd27t","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_tym8ORGE1qr9bGjaEIisQivS","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r14s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r14s0\n+title: \"Reset-Shadowed LUTRAM Register File\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The remaining successful `bench` changes have mostly come from shrinking\n+  always-live timing and routing cost rather than adding more control\n+  cleverness. `rtl/reg_file.sv` is still a 32x32 synchronously-reset flop\n+  array, which likely blocks RAM inference and drags a 1024-bit reset network\n+  plus wide read muxing through a core where fetch-side and MEM-side extra\n+  logic have already mostly regressed.\n+\n+  A regfile-local optimization is one of the few unexplored levers left that\n+  can raise Fmax without perturbing CoreMark IPC, fetch behavior, or the\n+  already-proven divider/store-slot wins.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Rework `rtl/reg_file.sv` so the storage array itself is no longer resettable\n+  state. Keep the current two combinational read ports, one synchronous write\n+  port, x0 hardwiring, and write-first same-cycle bypass, but back them with\n+  an unreset 32x32 RAM-style array plus a small resettable per-register valid\n+  bitmap.\n+\n+  On reset, clear only the valid bits; reads of x1..x31 whose valid bit is 0\n+  still return zero, so the architecturally visible post-reset behavior stays\n+  the same and RVFI/cocotb expectations do not weaken. After the first write\n+  to a register, set its valid bit and read from the RAM array normally. This\n+  should let Yosys/Gowin map the bulk storage into LUTRAM or a denser memory\n+  structure, cutting FF count, reset fanout, and routing on a high-fanout\n+  backend resource while leaving CoreMark cycle count unchanged.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n+    description: \"Replace the fully resettable 32x32 flop array with RAM-inferable storage plus a resettable valid-bit shadow, preserving x0 hardwiring and write-first bypass.\"\n+  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n+    description: \"Strengthen the reset test to write non-zero data before reset and verify the valid-bit scheme still makes every architectural register read as zero afterward.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r14s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r14s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r14s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r14s0.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,36 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r14s0\n+title: \"Reset-Shadowed LUTRAM Register File\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The remaining successful `bench` changes have mostly come from shrinking\n+  always-live timing and routing cost rather than adding more control\n+  cleverness. `rtl/reg_file.sv` is still a 32x32 synchronously-reset flop\n+  array, which likely blocks RAM inference and drags a 1024-bit reset network\n+  plus wide read muxing through a core where fetch-side and MEM-side extra\n+  logic have already mostly regressed.\n+\n+  A regfile-local optimization is one of the few unexplored levers left that\n+  can raise Fmax without perturbing CoreMark IPC, fetch behavior, or the\n+  already-proven divider/store-slot wins.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Rework `rtl/r