showed this late load cone is timing\\nsensitive.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rewrite the MEM-side load/store byte-lane logic around width-specialized\\nfast paths instead of a universal barrel-shift style aligner. Keep RVFI,\\nmisalignment trapping, and the existing store-buffer contract identical, but\\nmake `LW`/`SW` and aligned word-address traffic flow through the shortest\\ndirect path, with only small byte/half muxes on the colder subword cases.\\n\\nIn practice, `rtl/mem_stage.sv` would replace `shifted = dmem_rdata >>\\n(addr*8)` and generic shifted masks with case-split lane extraction:\\ndirect `dmem_rdata` for word loads, a 2:1 halfword select keyed by\\n`addr[1]`, a 4:1 byte select keyed by `addr[1:0]`, and explicit constant\\nmasks for SB/SH/SW. That targets the sensitive\\n`dmem_rdata -> load_data -> MEM/WB` cone and the aligned-memory metadata\\npath without reopening the already-regressive fetch, hazard, or deeper\\nstore-queue spaces.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": ...\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Disjoint Load Bypass Around Deferred Store Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current champion already proved that a one-deep deferred RAM-store slot\\ncan recover IPC, but it still fences every younger memory operation until the\\nqueued store physically drains. Under the tournament's random dmem stalls,\\nthat means one stalled store can still block unrelated later loads even when\\ntheir addresses do not overlap and the existing slot already holds the older\\nstore's full aligned word address, mask, and data.\\n\\nPrior structural regressions say not to add fetch-side machinery, larger\\nqueues, or replay caches. The most promising remaining backend lever is to\\nmake the existing single-entry store slot less serializing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current champion already proved that a one-deep deferred RAM-store slot\\ncan recover IPC, but it still fences every younger memory operation until the\\nqueued store physically drains. Under the tournament's random dmem stalls,\\nthat means one stalled store can still block unrelated later loads even when\\ntheir addresses do not overlap and the existing slot already holds the older\\nstore's full aligned word address, mask, and data.\\n\\nPrior structural regressions say not to add fetch-side machinery, larger\\nqueues, or replay caches. The most promising remaining backend lever is to\\nmake the existing single-entry store slot less serializing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the current one-deep deferred-store design, but let safe younger loads\\nbypass a queued RAM store when they target a different aligned word. When the\\nstore slot is occupied, `mem_stage` should arbitrate between draining the old\\nstore and serving the live EX/MEM request: aligned normal-RAM loads whose\\naligned word address differs from the queued store may use the dmem port\\nimmediately and leave the store queued for a later cycle, while same-word\\nloads, stores, MMIO accesses, misaligned accesses,...\n  Line 40: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Accepted-History Backedge Fold Slot\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Predictor work on `bench` has almost no safe frontend budget: ID redirects,\\nBTB-style target lookup, and loop streaming all lost more Fmax than they\\ngained IPC. The only predictor shape that stayed competitive was the\\none-slot backedge fold buffer because it kept `io_imemAddr` on the existing\\nsequential path. Its remaining weakness is fill latency: the slot only\\nbecomes useful after the target instruction is fetched again\\narchitecturally, so the first observed taken backedge and any iStall-delayed\\nrefill still pay full bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Predictor work on `bench` has almost no safe frontend budget: ID redirects,\\nBTB-style target lookup, and loop streaming all lost more Fmax than they\\ngained IPC. The only predictor shape that stayed competitive was the\\none-slot backedge fold buffer because it kept `io_imemAddr` on the existing\\nsequential path. Its remaining weakness is fill latency: the slot only\\nbecomes useful after the target instruction is fetched again\\narchitecturally, so the first observed taken backedge and any iStall-delayed\\nrefill still pay full bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the single active backedge fold slot and the sequential `io_imemAddr`\\npath, but train the slot from a tiny recent accepted-fetch history instead\\nof waiting for a post-redirect refetch of the target word. When EX resolves\\na taken backward branch, search the last few architecturally accepted\\n`(pc, instr)` pairs; if the target PC is present, snapshot that real\\ninstruction immediately into the fold slot and mark it usable for the next\\nloop iteration. Fall back to the old delayed fill only on a history miss.\\n\\nThis stays inside the only predictor shape that has been timing-safe on\\n`bench`: one lookup entry in IF, no extra target mux on the live fetch cone,\\nand no multi-entry select logic. It directly attacks the remaining war...\n  Line 41: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r14s0\", \"title\": \"Reset-Shadowed LUTRAM Register File\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining successful `bench` changes have mostly come from shrinking\\nalways-live timing and routing cost rather than adding more control\\ncleverness. `rtl/reg_file.sv` is still a 32x32 synchronously-reset flop\\narray, which likely blocks RAM inference and drags a 1024-bit reset network\\nplus wide read muxing through a core where fetch-side and MEM-side extra\\nlogic have already mostly regressed.\\n\\nA regfile-local optimization is one of the few unexplored levers left that\\ncan raise Fmax without perturbing CoreMark IPC, fetch behavior, or the\\nalready-proven divider/store-slot wins.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining successful `bench` changes have mostly come from shrinking\\nalways-live timing and routing cost rather than adding more control\\ncleverness. `rtl/reg_file.sv` is still a 32x32 synchronously-reset flop\\narray, which likely blocks RAM inference and drags a 1024-bit reset network\\nplus wide read muxing through a core where fetch-side and MEM-side extra\\nlogic have already mostly regressed.\\n\\nA regfile-local optimization is one of the few unexplored levers left that\\ncan raise Fmax without perturbing CoreMark IPC, fetch behavior, or the\\nalready-proven divider/store-slot wins.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rework `rtl/reg_file.sv` so the storage array itself is no longer resettable\\nstate. Keep the current two combinational read ports, one synchronous write\\nport, x0 hardwiring, and write-first same-cycle bypass, but back them with\\nan unreset 32x32 RAM-style array plus a small resettable per-register valid\\nbitmap.\\n\\nOn reset, clear only the valid bits; reads of x1..x31 whose valid bit is 0\\nstill return zero, so the architecturally visible post-reset behavior stays\\nthe same and RVFI/cocotb expectations do not weaken. After the first write\\nto a register, set its valid bit and read from the RAM array normally. This\\nshould let ...\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Registered One-Entry Load Replay Latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"`bench`'s proven structural wins both came from backend-only stall handling:\\ncold divide offload and the one-deep deferred store slot. The next obvious\\nremaining IPC loss is load-side dmem backpressure. Prior bench experiments\\nalready showed that replaying hot loads can recover cycles, but the direct\\nMEM-stage replay-table lookup, tag compare, and stall-escape logic cost too\\nmuch Fmax when they sat on the live `io_dmemReady` path.\\n\\nThe best structural follow-up is therefore not another wider replay table or\\nfetch-side structure, but a much smaller load replay mechanism that keeps the\\ntiming cost local and attacks only repeated exact-word load stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"`bench`'s proven structural wins both came from backend-only stall handling:\\ncold divide offload and the one-deep deferred store slot. The next obvious\\nremaining IPC loss is load-side dmem backpressure. Prior bench experiments\\nalready showed that replaying hot loads can recover cycles, but the direct\\nMEM-stage replay-table lookup, tag compare, and stall-escape logic cost too\\nmuch Fmax when they sat on the live `io_dmemReady` path.\\n\\nThe best structural follow-up is therefore not another wider replay table or\\nfetch-side structure, but a much smaller load replay mechanism that keeps the\\ntiming cost local and attacks only repeated exact-word load stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry last-load replay latch for aligned normal-RAM words and turn\\nit into a registered replay candidate instead of a same-cycle MEM lookup.\\nSuccessful ready loads refresh the latch with `{aligned_addr, raw_word}`.\\nWhen a younger aligned load to the same word is about to enter MEM, capture a\\nreplay-eligible sideband once; if that load later sees `io_dmemReady=0`, MEM\\nmay complete it from the registered candidate rather than freezing the whole\\npipeline.\\n\\nKeep the m...\n  Line 43: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r14s2\", \"title\": \"Sticky Hot-Loop Backedge Fold Slot\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Predictor work on `bench` has shown that almost any extra work on the live\\nfrontend path costs more Fmax than it recovers in CoreMark IPC. The one\\nexception was the single-slot backedge fold buffer, which matched the current\\nbest fitness while keeping `io_imemAddr` on the simple sequential path.\\n\\nThat points to a very narrow remaining opportunity: keep the exact same\\ntiming-safe one-entry fold/inject mechanism, but make the single active slot\\nless eager to forget a hot loop when CoreMark rotates through colder or\\nshorter-lived backedges.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Predictor work on `bench` has shown that almost any extra work on the live\\nfrontend path costs more Fmax than it recovers in CoreMark IPC. The one\\nexception was the single-slot backedge fold buffer, which matched the current\\nbest fitness while keeping `io_imemAddr` on the simple sequential path.\\n\\nThat points to a very narrow remaining opportunity: keep the exact same\\ntiming-safe one-entry fold/inject mechanism, but make the single active slot\\nless eager to forget a hot loop when CoreMark rotates through colder or\\nshorter-lived backedges.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the fetch-time predictor lookup exactly one entry wide and preserve the\\nexisting fold-buffer shape: one backedge slot, one tag compare, sequential\\n`io_imemAddr`, and target-instruction injection only after a proven taken\\nbackward branch has been trained. Do not add a second lookup entry, earlier\\ntarget fill, or any new redirect mux on the live fetch cone.\\n\\nInstead, change only the EX-time replacement policy. Give the active fold\\nslot a tiny saturating confidence counter. Matching taken backedges refresh\\nthe slot and raise confidence; a different taken backedge does not evict the\\nactive slot immediately, but first decays that confidence and only replaces\\nthe slot once the current entry has gone cold....","metadata":{"matches":39,"truncated":false},"title":"forward|hazard|reg[_-]?file|load[- ]use|store slot|div|alu|branch|bypass|replay","time":{"start":1778882242242,"end":1778882242266}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_088d7f1787defd69016a0796c20ce8819bb289d99229223549"}},"id":"prt_e2da4e6be001lJ8M5ZX7PmrGmc","sessionID":"ses_1d25b7e28ffeGMbdW1RrhOsLQi","messageID":"msg_e2da4bd7f001l6HZLkiN9rJbe4"}}
