{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778882382777,"sessionID":"ses_1d25b7e28ffeGMbdW1RrhOsLQi","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_pA0yaW6Ch9nwep7WHFKIsVPG","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r15s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r15s0\n+title: \"Serial-Scrub LUTRAM Register File\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The remaining `bench` micro-opt space is narrow: fetch-side ideas have been\n+  consistently Fmax-negative, hazard relaxations have been correctness-sensitive,\n+  and the last regfile attempt regressed because it traded the wide reset tree for\n+  extra read-path muxing through a `valid_q` shadow. That still leaves the core's\n+  synchronously-reset 32x32 flop regfile as one of the few always-live structures\n+  likely blocking cleaner LUTRAM inference and adding routing pressure on every\n+  cycle.\n+\n+  A better regfile-local optimization is to remove reset from the storage array\n+  without adding any new per-read validity mux on the hot path.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Rework `rtl/reg_file.sv` around an unreset RAM-style 2R1W array with the same\n+  combinational reads, x0 hardwiring, and same-cycle write-first bypass, but use a\n+  tiny internal post-reset scrub sequencer instead of a shadow valid bitmap. After\n+  external reset deasserts, the regfile would spend 32 cycles writing zeros through\n+  its normal single write port, one architectural register at a time, while the\n+  rest of the core stays in an internal reset/hold state and retires nothing.\n+\n+  This keeps architectural post-reset behavior identical, avoids the read-path\n+  `valid_q` gating that likely caused `r14s0` to regress, and gives synthesis a\n+  cleaner chance to map the storage array to LUTRAM or otherwise shed the current\n+  1024-bit reset network. CoreMark's timed region should not notice the startup\n+  scrub, so any Fmax gain drops directly into fitness.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n+    description: \"Replace the fully resettable flop array with an unreset RAM-style register file plus a 32-cycle zero-scrub sequencer, while preserving x0 and write-first bypass semantics.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Hold the pipeline, RVFI retirement, and writeback side idle behind an internal reset/initialization busy signal until the regfile scrub completes.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r15s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r15s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r15s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r15s0.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r15s0\n+title: \"Serial-Scrub LUTRAM Register File\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The remaining `bench` micro-opt space is narrow: fetch-side ideas have been\n+  consistently Fmax-negative, hazard relaxations have been correctness-sensitive,\n+  and the last regfile attempt regressed because it traded the wide reset tree for\n+  extra read-path muxing through a `valid_q` shadow. That still leaves the core's\n+  synchronously-reset 32x32 flop regfile as one of the few always-live structures\n+  likely blocking cleaner LUTRAM inference and adding routing pressure on every\n+  cycle.\n+\n+  A better regfile-local optimization is to remove reset from the storage array\n+  without adding any new per-read validity mux on the hot path.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Rework `rtl/reg_fil