and all pipeline control unchanged, but make the opcode encoding expose a\\nsimple hot/cold split that synthesis can map into a shallower mux for the\\ncommon RV32I path. This targets median Fmax with no CoreMark cycle-count\\nrisk and avoids the multiplier-primitive rewrites that previously regressed\\nbadly.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design already moved DIV/REM out of the universal EX\\ndatapath and added the one-deep store slot, so the remaining micro-optimum\\nis likely in the hot single-cycle EX logic rather than in another stall\\nhiding structure. `rtl/alu.sv` still selects every RV32I operation and all\\nmultiply-class results through one dense ALU opcode case. Even though\\nDIV/REM now return dummy zero in the ALU, the common ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path still shares decode and mux structure with the cold M-extension\\nresult cases.\\n\\nA previous \\\"hot-bit ALU\\\" attempt was never architecturally evaluated because\\nthe implementation touched an off-limits non-RTL helper file. The idea\\nremains attractive after the divider win: leave every arithmetic primitive\\nand all pipeline contr...\n  Line 29: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-022-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Collapse Redundant EX/MEM Metadata\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already captured the large available wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is out of the one-cycle ALU path, and the pending store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. The remaining micro-opt slot should therefore\\navoid new predictors, queues, or arithmetic units and instead trim logic that\\nis already proven unnecessary on the hot pipeline boundary.\\n\\nThe EX/MEM bundle still carries several fields that do not add architectural\\ninformation. `write_data` is always assigned the same post-forwarded rs2\\nvalue as `rs2_val`, while `branch_taken` and `branch_target` are registered\\nbut never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, forwarding, or hazard logic. Those bits\\nadd about 65 EX/MEM flops plus mux/load on the EX branch-target and rs2 data\\npaths, exactly where the post-DIV design is trying to preserve Fmax. Pruning\\nthis redundant metadata should not change CoreMark cycles, bus behavior,\\nbranch prediction, RVFI ordering, or any ISA-visible result.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already captured the large available wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is out of the one-cycle ALU path, and the pending store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. The remaining micro-opt slot should therefore\\navoid new predictors, queues, or arithmetic units and instead trim logic that\\nis already proven unnecessary on the hot pipeline boundary.\\n\\nThe EX/MEM bundle still carries several fields that do not add architectural\\ninformation. `write_data` is always assigned the same post-forwarded rs2\\nvalue as `rs2_val`, while `branch_taken` and `branch_target` are registered\\nbut never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, forwarding, or hazard logic. Those bits\\nadd about 65 EX/MEM flops plus mux/load on the EX branch-target and rs2 data\\npaths, exactly where the post-DIV design is trying to preserve Fmax. Pruning\\nthis redundant metadata should not change CoreMark cycl...\n  Line 32: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-025-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Segmented RVFI Order Counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large architectural wins:\\nDIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the one-deep store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. Recent micro-opt attempts that reshaped hot\\ndatapaths, predictor target logic, or EX/MEM metadata mostly lost Fmax to\\nplacement noise or new muxing. The next micro-opt should therefore target a\\nguaranteed always-present timing structure without changing IPC.\\n\\nThe top-level RVFI order counter is currently a single 64-bit increment:\\n`rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'b1` on every retirement. The FPGA\\nbenchmark wrapper intentionally retains RVFI fanout by XOR-reducing the RVFI\\noutputs into an LED, so this counter cannot be assumed dead in synthesis.\\nA 64-bit carry self-loop is not architectural work for the CoreMark datapath,\\nbut it can still consume carry-chain/routing budget and compete with the hot\\npipeline for median Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large architectural wins:\\nDIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the one-deep store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. Recent micro-opt attempts that reshaped hot\\ndatapaths, predictor target logic, or EX/MEM metadata mostly lost Fmax to\\nplacement noise or new muxing. The next micro-opt should therefore target a\\nguaranteed always-present timing structure without changing IPC.\\n\\nThe top-level RVFI order counter is currently a single 64-bit increment:\\n`rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'b1` on every retirement. The FPGA\\nbenchmark wrapper intentionally retains RVFI fanout by XOR-reducing the RVFI\\noutputs into an LED, so this counter cannot be assumed dead in synthesis.\\nA 64-bit carry self-loop is not architectural work for the CoreMark datapath,\\nbut it can still consume carry-chain/routing budget and compete with the hot\\npipeline for median Fmax.\\n\", ...\n  Line 35: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-028-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Byte-Segmented RVFI Order Counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a pure timing micro-optimization: replacing\\nthe monolithic 64-bit RVFI order increment with four 16-bit counter chunks.\\nThat change did not alter CoreMark cycles, memory behavior, branch\\nprediction, divider latency, or retirement semantics, but it raised median\\nFmax enough to become the new best fitness. This strongly suggests the\\nRVFI-order feedback path is still visible to synthesis and placement because\\nthe FPGA wrapper keeps RVFI outputs alive.\\n\\nThe current implementation still has a 16-bit carry-chain self-loop in the\\nlow order chunk, and each retirement toggles that chunk. A narrower exact\\nchunk size is the most direct follow-up in the micro_opt slot: it targets the\\nsame measured timing structure that just paid off, avoids another datapath or\\npredictor rewrite, and should leave CoreMark iterations/cycle unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a pure timing micro-optimization: replacing\\nthe monolithic 64-bit RVFI order increment with four 16-bit counter chunks.\\nThat change did not alter CoreMark cycles, memory behavior, branch\\nprediction, divider latency, or retirement semantics, but it raised median\\nFmax enough to become the new best fitness. This strongly suggests the\\nRVFI-order feedback path is still visible to synthesis and placement because\\nthe FPGA wrapper keeps RVFI outputs alive.\\n\\nThe current implementation still has a 16-bit carry-chain self-loop in the\\nlow order chunk, and each retirement toggles that chunk. A narrower exact\\nchunk size is the most direct follow-up in the micro_opt slot: it targets the\\nsame measured timing structure that just paid off, avoids another datapath or\\npredictor rewrite, and should leave CoreMark iterations/cycle unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the RVFI order counter in `rtl/core.sv` from four 16-bit chunks t...\n  Line 38: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-031-r13s0\", \"title\": \"Compressed Resetless I-Fetch Replay Tags\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best fitness comes from the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor: it preserves most of the 256-entry replay table's large\\nrandom-iStall cycle reduction while moving the same-cycle tag lookup off the\\nIF decision path. That win also makes the replay table one of the remaining\\nhigh-leverage timing structures. The table still stores a full 32-bit PC tag\\nfor every entry even though the direct-mapped index already encodes\\n`pc[9:2]` and all reachable fetch PCs are word-aligned. It also resets the\\ntag and instruction arrays even though the valid bits alone determine\\nwhether those payloads can be consumed.\\n\\nRecent micro-opt results show that exact timing-only cleanups can matter:\\nthe 16-bit segmented RVFI order counter improved Fmax without changing\\nCoreMark cycles, while broader datapath rewrites and smaller replay tables\\nlost either hit rate or placement quality. Compressing and de-resetting the\\nexisting replay metadata is the same kind of narrow micro-optimization: keep\\nthe current replay behavior and cycle count, but reduce IF table width,\\nreset fanout, and registered-candidate comparison logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best fitness comes from the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor: it preserves most of the 256-entry replay table's large\\nrandom-iStall cycle reduction while moving the same-cycle tag lookup off the\\nIF decision path. That win also makes the replay table one of the remaining\\nhigh-leverage timing structures. The table still stores a full 32-bit PC tag\\nfor every entry even though the direct-mapped index already encodes\\n`pc[9:2]` and all reachable fetch PCs are word-aligned. It also resets the\\ntag and instruction arrays even though the valid bits alone determine\\nwhether those payloads can be consumed.\\n\\nRecent micro-opt results show that exact timing-on...\n  Line 41: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-034-r14s0\", \"title\": \"Elide Replay Candidate PC Compare\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result is dominated by the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. Compressing the replay table tags improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, which shows the replay-hit/fetch-ready path is now\\none of the most timing-sensitive structures left in the design.\\n\\nThe IF stage still carries a full 32-bit `replay_cand_pc_q` register and\\ncompares it against the current `pc` every cycle before a replay candidate\\ncan satisfy an external imem stall. That guard is logically redundant with\\nthe current lookahead update discipline: on every clock, the replay candidate\\nis computed for exactly the PC that will be held or loaded into the PC\\nregister on the next cycle. Redirect loads both PC and candidate from the\\nredirect target, a non-stalled fetch loads both from `next_pc`, and a stalled\\nfetch recomputes the candidate for the held PC.\\n\\nRemoving this full-width equality check is a narrow micro-optimization in the\\nsame proven area as the current best. It should reduce IF-side compare\\nfanout, remove 32 candidate-PC flops, and shorten the `fetch_ready` decision\\npath without changing replay hit rate, branch/JAL prediction policy,\\npipeline timing, memory behavior, RVFI reporting, or CoreMark cycle count.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result is dominated by the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. Compressing the replay table tags improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, which shows the replay-hit/fetch-ready path is now\\none of the most timing-sensitive structures left in the design.\\n\\nThe IF stage still carries a full 32-bit `replay_cand_pc_q` register and\\ncompares it against the current `pc` every cycle before a replay candidate\\ncan satisfy an external imem stall. That guard is logically redundant with\\nthe current lookahead update discipline: on every clock, the replay c...\n  Line 44: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-037-r15s0\", \"title\": \"Valid-Only Pipeline Payload Reset\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by timing-only wins that do not change\\nCoreMark cycles: the segmented RVFI order counter and the compressed\\nresetless IF replay metadata both improved Fmax by removing unnecessary\\ncarry, compare, and reset pressure from always-present logic. The remaining\\npipeline registers still use broad `<= '0` clears on reset, flush, and\