{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778835357206,"sessionID":"ses_1d52925c1ffeNMU0NKEnVRJtki","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_fDypIoMJ5hjTdBnLvRwQGXCs","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r1s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r1s0\n+title: \"Iterative Cold Divider Off EX Path\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The frozen bench core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\n+  combinational ALU path as ADD, address generation, branch support, and\n+  MUL. On a Tang Nano 20K, a full 32-bit divider is a strong candidate for\n+  the universal EX-stage critical path, yet CoreMark's timed loop is mostly\n+  multiply, load/store, and branch traffic and should pay little or no steady\n+  state penalty from slower divide instructions.\n+\n+  This makes divider extraction the highest-leverage micro-opt still missing\n+  from the frozen bench fixture: it targets Fmax directly while preserving the\n+  hot one-cycle behavior for the instructions that dominate CoreMark.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Replace the real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases in `rtl/alu.sv`\n+  with a small iterative divider/remainder unit that is entered only for\n+  divide-class ALU ops. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift/compare, address generation,\n+  branches, jumps, loads/stores, and MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing\n+  single-cycle datapath.\n+\n+  In EX, detect divide-class operations, latch their operands and control,\n+  stall IF and ID while the divider runs, and emit exactly one EX/MEM result\n+  when the quotient or remainder is ready. Preserve RV32IM edge cases\n+  precisely: divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1 overflow behavior, signed/unsigned\n+  variants, ALTOPS behavior under formal, and the existing single-retire\n+  RVFI/order contract. The expected win is a shorter common EX critical path\n+  and higher median Fmax with negligible CoreMark IPC loss because DIV/REM are\n+  cold in the benchmark's hot region.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n+    description: \"Remove real DIV/REM arithmetic from the hot combinational ALU result mux while preserving the one-cycle RV32I and multiply paths plus ALTOPS behavior.\"\n+  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Add a small iterative divider/remainder unit that produces exact RV32IM DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU results and exposes a busy/done handshake.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Launch divide-class ops into the new unit, hold younger issue while it runs, and write a single EX/MEM result when the divider completes.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Fold divider-busy into the existing stall network so IF/ID freeze cleanly during long divide operations without changing redirect or load-use behavior.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the divider busy/complete signals through the top-level stage connections while keeping the current RVFI retirement boundary and order semantics intact.\"\n+  - file: test/test_alu.py\n+    description: \"Narrow the ALU unit test to the still-combinational operations so it no longer assumes DIV/REM are produced directly by alu.sv.\"\n+  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n+    description: \"Add dedicated cocotb coverage for divide and remainder semantics, including divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1, signedness, and back-to-back operations.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add an end-to-end pipeline test that proves divide instructions stall younger ops but still retire once with correct RVFI data and writeback results.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r1s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r1s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r1s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r1s0.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r1s0\n+title: \"Iterative Cold Divider Off EX Path\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The frozen bench core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\n+  combinational ALU path as ADD, address generation, branch support, and\n+  MUL. On a Tang Nano 20K, a full 32-bit divider is a strong candidate for\n+  the universal EX-stage critical path, yet CoreMark's timed loop is mostly\n+  multiply, load/store, and branch traffic and should pay little or no steady\n+  state penalty from slower divide instructions.\n+\n+  This makes divider extraction the highest-leverage micro-opt still missing\n+  from the frozen bench fixture: it targets Fmax directly while preserving the\n+  hot one-cycle behavior for the instructions that dominate CoreMark.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Replace the real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases in `rtl/alu.sv`\n+  with a small iterative divider/remainder unit that is entered only for\n+  divide-class ALU ops. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift/compare, address generation,\n+  branches, jumps, loads/stores, and MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing\n+  single-cycle datapath.\n+\n+  In EX, detect divide-class operations, latch their operands and control,\n+  stall IF and ID while the divider runs, and emit exactly one EX/MEM result\n+  when the quotient or remainder is ready. Preserve RV32IM edge cases\n+  precisely: divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1 overflow behavior, signed/unsigned\n+  variants, ALTOPS behavior under formal, and the existing single-retire\n+  RVFI/order contract. The expected win is a shorter common EX critical path\n+  and higher median Fmax with negligible CoreMark IPC loss because DIV/REM are\n+  cold in the benchmark's hot region.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n+    description: \"Remove real DIV/REM arithmetic from the hot combinational ALU result mux while preserving the one-cycle RV32I and multiply paths plus ALTOPS behavior.\"\n+  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Add a small iterative divider/remainder unit that produces exact RV32IM DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU results and exposes a busy/done handshake.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Launch divide-class ops into the new unit, hold younger issue while it runs, and write a single EX/MEM result when the divider completes.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Fold divider-busy into the existing stall network so IF/ID freeze cleanly during long divide operations without changing redirect or load-use behavior.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the divider busy/complete signals through the top-level stage connections while keeping the current RVFI retirement boundary and order semantics intact.\"\n+  - file: test/test_alu.py\n+    description: \"Narrow the ALU unit test to the still-combinational operations so it no longer assumes DIV/REM are produced directly by alu.sv.\"\n+  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n+    description: \"Add dedicated cocotb coverage for divide and remainder semantics, including divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1, signedness, and back-to-back operations.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add an end-to-end pipeline test that proves divide instructions stall younger ops but still retire once with correct RVFI data and writeback results.\"\n\n","files":[{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r1s0.yaml","relativePath":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r1s0.yaml","type":"add","patch":"I