urns\\nraw `io_imemReady=0` into a global pipeline stall.\\n\\nA previous generic elastic fetch buffer regressed because it added permanent\\nfrontend queue/arbitration cost before the divider and store-slot timing\\nwins, and it tried to prefetch through all backend stalls. The better\\nstructural angle now is narrower: use a tiny fetch skid queue only to absorb\\ninstruction-memory backpressure and to keep fetch productive while the new\\nstore slot is draining for older stores. This targets the measured\\nstore-slot cycle win's remaining companion bottleneck without touching the\\nhot EX operand path, load data forwarding, branch predictor state, or RVFI\\nretirement width.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design combines the two changes that have paid off most:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and a one-deep pending-store slot\\nlets a stalled normal-RAM store retire while the physical dmem write drains\\nlater. That store slot reduced CoreMark cycles, but the frontend is still\\nstructurally brittle under the tournament's random imem stalls: `if_stage`\\npresents live imem data directly to decode, and `hazard_unit` still turns\\nraw `io_imemReady=0` into a global pipeline stall.\\n\\nA previous generic elastic fetch buffer regressed because it added permanent\\nfrontend queue/arbitration cost before the divider and store-slot timing\\nwins, and it tried to prefetch through all backend stalls. T...\n  Line 29: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-022-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Collapse Redundant EX/MEM Metadata\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already captured the large available wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is out of the one-cycle ALU path, and the pending store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. The remaining micro-opt slot should therefore\\navoid new predictors, queues, or arithmetic units and instead trim logic that\\nis already proven unnecessary on the hot pipeline boundary.\\n\\nThe EX/MEM bundle s