/EBREAK read neither, and JALR reads only rs1. CoreMark's optimized code\\nhas dense immediate arithmetic, address-generation loads, calls, and loop\\ncontrol, so these false one-cycle bubbles are a plausible remaining IPC loss.\\n\\nA prior operand-aware interlock attempt broke selftest, so this hypothesis is\\ndeliberately conservative: decode source-use exactly for known legal\\ninstruction classes, keep reserved or unknown encodings on the current\\nboth-source stall policy, and do not add any MEM-to-EX load-data path. The\\ngoal is to recover only provably false load-use stalls while leaving all true\\nload consumers, dmem waits, branch recovery, divider interlocks, and RVFI\\nordering unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has strong timing wins from the registered instruction\\nreplay predictor, segmented RVFI order counter, pending-store slot, and\\nhot/cold ALU split. The remaining structural improvement should therefore\\navoid another cache, queue, predictor table, or load-data forwarding path that\\ncould disturb Fmax.\\n\\nThe load-use interlock is still structurally conservative. It stalls whenever\\na load in ID/EX h... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-048-r18s2\", \"title\": \"Kill-Gated I-Fetch Replay Fills\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design already has the predictor feature with the strongest\\nmeasured payoff: a 256-entry registered lookahead instruction replay table\\nwith compressed tags. It hides random iStall bubbles without putting a\\nsame-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up predictor attempts\\nthat added branch-direction state, return targets, victim entries, wider\\nreplay lines, or replay-cached next-PC metadata either saved too few cycles\\nor lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe next predictor slot should therefore keep the existing replay structure\\nand target replay quality rather than add another table. The current IF\\nstage refreshes the replay table on every live `io_imemReady` fetch, even\\nwhen that fetch is being killed by an EX redirect or IF flush. Those killed\\nwords are architecturally correct immutable instructions, but they are\\nwrong-path for the current execution and can evict useful hot-loop replay\\nentries in the direct-mapped table. Avoiding this pollution is a narrow\\nreplay-predictor improvement with little timing exposure: it changes the\\nreplay write-enable policy, not the current-cycle fetch-ready, branch/JAL\\ntarget, ALU, memory, or retirement paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design already has the predictor feature with the strongest\\nmeasured payoff: a 256-entry registered lookahead instruction replay table\\nwith compressed tags. It hides random iStall bubbles without putting a\\nsame-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up predictor attempts\\nthat added branch-direction state, return targets, victim entries, wider\\nreplay lines, or replay-cached next-PC metadata either saved too few cycles\\nor lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe next predictor slot should therefore keep the existing replay structure\\nand target replay quality rather than add another table. The cu... (line truncated to 2000 chars)","truncated":true,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl","time":{"start":1778836805759,"end":1778836805779}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0e4bc692395704f6016a06e545a760819b872f9b3047c49448"}},"id":"prt_e2aef987d001kzp38XGfGLDG5d","sessionID":"ses_1d511137cffe4v017SuI15LgTU","messageID":"msg_e2aef8380001e2Zkb6aN4ghv4E"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778836805781,"sessionID":"ses_1d511137cffe4v017SuI15LgTU","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_AneR5HgNnlFax5MW3VMbAymP","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl","offset":7,"limit":4},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n7: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-006-r2s2\", \"title\": \"IF Direct-Jump Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches in IF,\\nbut ex_stage still asserts a redirect for every JAL/JALR. That leaves\\nunconditional direct jumps, calls, and compiler-generated `j` control-flow\\nskips paying the same wrong-path flush as an unpredicted taken branch, even\\nthough a JAL target is fully encoded in the fetched instruction. CoreMark's\\noptimized RV32IM image contains many direct JAL/J sites, so eliminating those\\nredirect bubbles should improve iterations/cycle with much less timing risk\\nthan adding a BTB or learned direction table to the IF path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches in IF,\\nbut ex_stage still asserts a redirect for every JAL/JALR. That leaves\\nunconditional direct jumps, calls, and compiler-generated `j` control-flow\\nskips paying the same wrong-path flush as an unpredicted taken branch, even\\nthough a JAL target is fully encoded in the fetched instruction. CoreMark's\\noptimized RV32IM image contains many direct JAL/J sites, so eliminating those\\nredirect bubbles should improve iterations/cycle with much less timing risk\\nthan adding a BTB or learned direction table to the IF path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the existing IF-stage predictor from backward conditional branches to\\ndirect JAL targets. Recognize opcode 1101111 in IF, form the J immediate,\\npredict pc+imm as the next PC when the target is word-aligned, and carry the\\nexisting predicted_taken/predicted_target metadata with the instruction. In\\nEX, make JAL redirect mispredict-only by comparing the resolved jump target\\nagainst the carried prediction; JALR remains unpredicted and still redirects\\nfrom EX. This should remove the two-cycle recovery penalty from correctly\\npredicted direct jumps and calls while preserving RVFI pc_wdata, misaligned\\njump traps, and the single-retire c... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n8: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Operand-Aware Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use hazard check is deliberately conservative: every load\\nin ID/EX stalls the next instruction when the load destination matches either\\nraw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield in IF/ID. That creates real bubbles for true\\nload consumers, but it also creates false bubbles for common CoreMark\\ninstructions whose rs fields are immediates or destinations rather than\\nsource operands, especially OP-IMM shifts/arithmetic, LOAD, JAL, LUI, and\\nAUIPC. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass recovered many load-use cycles\\nbut cost too much Fmax because it added a new MEM-data path into EX. A\\ndecode-light operand-use filter should recover the false-stall subset with\\nalmost no new datapath depth and no change to branch prediction, memory\\ntiming, or RVFI retirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use hazard check is deliberately conservative: every load\\nin ID/EX stalls the next instruction when the load destination matches either\\nraw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield in IF/ID. That creates real bubbles for true\\nload consumers, but it also creates false bubbles for common CoreMark\\ninstructions whose rs fields are immediates or destinations rather than\\nsource operands, especially OP-IMM shifts/arithmetic, LOAD, JAL, LUI, and\\nAUIPC. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass recovered many load-use cycles\\nbut cost too much Fmax because it added a new MEM-data path into EX. A\\ndecode-light operand-use filter should recover the false-stall subset with\\nalmost no new datapath depth and no change to branch prediction, memory\\ntiming, or RVFI retirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the hazard_unit load-use predicate so it only compares the load rd\\nagainst source fields that the IF/ID instruction actually reads. Compute\\nsimple uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 bits from the IF/ID opcode: rs1 is used by\\nOP, OP-IMM, LOAD, STORE, BRANCH, and JALR; rs2 is used only by... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Retiring Store Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry FIFO for normal RAM stores. When a legal aligned store to the\\nRAM address range reaches MEM and the external dmem port is not immediately\\navailable, retire the store into the FIFO with its address, replicated write\\ndata, byte mask, and RVFI metadata, then let younger non-memory instructions\\ncontinue through the pipeline while the FIFO drains oldest-first on the dmem\\nport. Loads, stores when the FIFO is full, and all MMIO/marker/UART st... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Static-Biased Branch Agree Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already removes most obvious control bubbles by\\npredicting backward conditional branches taken and direct JAL targets in IF.\\nThe remaining predictor loss is now dominated by conditional branches that\\nstatic BTFNT handles poorly: biased forward branches, backward branches with\\nnon-loop behavior, and alias-heavy branch sites in CoreMark's list and state\\nkernels. A small learned direction predictor can attack those residual\\nmispredicts without adding a BTB, a target adder beyond the existing branch\\nimmediate path, or any speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already removes most obvious control bubbles by\\npredicting backward conditional branches taken and direct JAL targets in IF.\\nThe remaining predictor loss is now dominated by conditional branches that\\nstatic BTFNT handles poorly: biased forward branches, backward branches with\\nnon-loop behavior, and alias-heavy branch sites in CoreMark's list and state\\nkernels. A small learned direction predictor ca