live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-imem path whenever the external\\nbus is ready, and consult a tiny cached copy only on cycles where imem would\\notherwise stall. CoreMark's timed code repeatedly executes the same matrix,\\nlist, state, CRC, and loop-control instructions over a 10-iteration run, so\\neven a small direct-mapped instruction replay table should turn many random\\niStall bubbles into normal fetch cycles after warmup.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-im...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-026-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Narrow Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction side. The 256-entry stall-only replay cache cut CoreMark cycles\\nfrom 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, showing that random iStall bubbles are a large\\npart of the remaining IPC loss, but its wide IF table/tag/data mux dropped\\nmedian Fmax enough to miss the current best.\\n\\nThat result points to a narrower replay structure rather than a broader\\nfetch queue. CoreMark's timed hot code repeatedly executes compact inner\\nloops and call bodies, so a smaller direct-mapped replay table should retain\\nmuch of the iStall hit-rate benefit while reducing the IF storage mux,\\ncomparator fanout, and PC-control load that hurt Fmax in the 256-entry\\nversion. This keeps the zero-wait path direct and targets only cycles that\\nwould otherwise be external imem stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction side. The 256-entry stall-only replay cache cut CoreMark cycles\\nfrom 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, showing that random iStall bubbles are a large\\npart of the remaining IPC loss, but its wide IF table/tag/data mux dropped\\nmedian Fmax enough to miss the current best.\\n\\nThat result points to a narrower replay structure rather than a broader\\nfetch queue. CoreMark's timed hot code repeatedly executes compact inner\\nloops and call bodies, so a smaller direct-mapped replay t...\n  Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-027-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Small Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor changes that have paid so far are the ones that avoid broad\\nlearned direction state: backward conditional branches and direct JAL targets\\nare predicted in IF, while the larger branch tables and return predictors\\nlost more Fmax than their cycle savings recovered. The one exception with a\\nlarge cycle signal was the stall-only instruction replay experiment: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles substantially by serving hot-loop instructions during random\\nimem stalls, but its 256-entry full-tag table pulled median Fmax below the\\ncurrent best.\\n\\nThat result suggests a narrower predictor, not a new branch-direction table.\\nCoreMark's timed region repeatedly executes a relatively small hot text\\nfootprint, so a smaller replay predictor should retain much of the random\\niStall hiding while shortening the IF tag/data muxes that hurt the 256-entry\\nattempt. This stays in the predictor slot because the table predicts the\\ninstruction word for the current PC only when the external fetch bus would\\notherwise provide no word; normal ready fetches still use live imem data.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor changes that have paid so far are the ones that avoid broad\\nlearned direction state: backward conditional branches and direct JAL targets\\nare predicted in IF, while the larger branch tables and return predictors\\nlost more Fmax than their cycle savings recovered. The one exception with a\\nlarge cycle signal was the stall-only instruction replay experiment: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles substantially by serving hot-loop instructions during random\\nimem stalls, but its 256-entry full-tag table pulled median Fmax below the\\ncurrent best.\\n\\nThat result suggests a narrower predictor, not a new branch-direction table.\\nCoreMark's timed region repeatedly executes a relatively small hot text\\nfootprint, so a smaller replay predictor should reta...\n  Line 37: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-030-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Registered Lookahead I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is still the simple static frontend:\\nbackward conditional branches and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF.\\nBroader learned branch tables and return predictors reduced a few control\\nbubbles, but their IF reads, compares, and EX update routing lost more Fmax\\nthan the