n the two normal-RAM stores. This targets the\\nsame store-burst pressure as the failed two-entry FIFO, but with one address\\ncompare and one byte-mask merge instead of another queue entry or a wider\\ndmem arbiter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already includes the structural wins that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot for normal-RAM store stalls\\nand a registered instruction replay table for random iStall. Broader store\\nbuffering attempts found the right bottleneck but were too expensive:\\ntwo-entry pending-store designs either failed placement, and the same-cycle\\nrefill variant also failed placement. That suggests the next structural\\nstore-side step should add capacity only for cases the existing single slot\\ncan handle locally.\\n\\nCoreMark updates list nodes, matrix cells, stack frames, and scalar state with\\nshort bursts of stores. When a ...\n  Line 72: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-065-r24s1\", \"title\": \"Replay-Fed Memory Plus ADDI Pair Issue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is a very strong single-issue design. The large wins\\nalready come from removing cold DIV/REM from the hot EX path, splitting the\\nALU hot/cold mux, adding a one-deep pending-store slot, segmenting the RVFI\\norder counter, and using a two-bank registered instruction replay predictor\\nto hide most random imem stalls. Recent structural attempts show that broad\\nbuffers, data replay tables, and general dual issue are too costly or too\\ndifficult, but they also point at the remaining ceiling: only one instruction\\ncan retire per cycle even when CoreMark presents simple pointer updates next\\nto memory operations.\\n\\nCoreMark's list, matrix, state, and stack code is dense with patterns such as\\na load or store followed by an independent ADDI pointer/index/stack update.\\nA full two-wide machine is risky, but this very narrow pair class uses only\\nthe existing single dmem access plus one younger simple immediate add. It\\ntargets a common structural bubble source without adding another memory port,\\nanother replay table, a load-data-to-EX bypass, a branch predictor, or a\\nsecond general ALU issue path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is a very strong single-issue design. The large wins\\nalready come from removing cold DIV/REM from the hot EX path, splitting the\\nALU hot/cold mux, adding a one-deep pending-store slot, segmenting the RVFI\\norder counter, and using a two-bank registered instruction replay predictor\\nto hide most random imem stalls. Recent structural attempts show that broad\\nbuffers, data replay tables, and general dual issue are too costly or too\\ndifficult, but they also point at the remaining ceiling: only one instruction\\ncan retire per cycle even when CoreMark presents simple pointer updates next\\nto memory operations.\\n\\nCoreMark's list, matrix, state, and stack code is dense with patter...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep3/log.jsonl:\n  Line 44: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r15s0\", \"title\": \"Forward load data to stores\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current hazard unit still treats a store's rs2 data operand as an EX-stage\\nconsumer for load-use interlocking. That is conservative for correctness, but\\na store does not actually need its write data until MEM, one cycle later, when\\nthe preceding load has already reached MEM/WB. CoreMark has frequent memory\\ntraffic, so removing this specific load-to-store data bubble can improve CPI\\nwithout touching the fetch predictor, branch redirect, ALU multiplier, or the\\nfailed same-cycle dmem-to-EX load bypass path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current hazard unit still treats a store's rs2 data operand as an EX-stage\\nconsumer for load-use interlocking. That is conservative for correctness, but\\na store does not actually need its write data until MEM, one cycle later, when\\nthe preceding load has already reached MEM/WB. CoreMark has frequent memory\\ntraffic, so removing this specific load-to-store data bubble can improve CPI\\nwithout touching the fetch predictor, branch redirect, ALU multiplier, or the\\nfailed same-cycle dmem-to-EX load bypass path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a MEM/WB-to-MEM store-data forwarding path and narrow the load-use hazard\\nrule so a load followed immediately by a store stalls only when the load feeds\\nthe store base address rs1, not when it feeds only the store data operand rs2.\\nThe store may enter EX with a stale rs2 value, then MEM replaces the store\\nwrite-data payload from the registered MEM/WB result before driving dmem and\\nRVFI. Use the same held MEM/WB forwarding qualification already needed during\\ndmem stalls so a multi-cycle stalled store keeps seeing the completed load\\ndata. This targets a real CPI bubble while adding only a local MEM-stage mux\\nand comparator, avoiding the timing-cost pattern that sank full MEM-to-EX load\\nbypassing.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"cha...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep2/log.jsonl:\n  Line 8: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Late load-store data bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use detector stalls whenever a load in EX has an rd\\nmatching either raw source field of the following IF/ID instruction. That is\\nnecessary for ALU operands, branch compares, and store address bases, but it\\nis over-conservative for a STORE whose rs2 data is not consumed until MEM.\\nCoreMark's timed kernels perform many load/store moves and immediate-form\\noperations, so avoidable one-cycle bubbles directly reduce iterations per\\ncycle without helping correctness.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use detector stalls whenever a load in EX has an rd\\nmatching either raw source field of the following IF/ID instruction. That is\\nnecessary for ALU operands, branch compares, and store address bases, but it\\nis over-conservative for a STORE whose rs2 data is not consumed until MEM.\\nCoreMark's timed kernels perform many load/store moves and immediate-form\\noperations, so avoidable one-cycle bubbles directly reduce iterations per\\ncycle without helping correctness.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the load-use hazard to distinguish operands needed in EX from store\\ndata that can be supplied one stage later. For a following STORE, keep the\\nstall only when the load feeds rs1, the address base; allow an rs2-only\\ndependency to advance and resolve it with a MEM-stage bypass from the current\\nMEM/WB result into the store-data path. The bypass must also update the\\nretiring store's RVFI rs2_rdata and mem_wdata fields so formal/cosim observe\\nthe architectural source value.\\n\\nDefault unknown or illegal encodings to conservative source use in the\\nhazard predecode, preserving trap and RVFI behavior. This should recover IPC\\nfrom load-to-store and false rs2-immediate hazards while adding only a small\\nMEM data mux, avoiding the fetch critical-path costs seen in prior predictor\\nattempts and the EX multiplier regressions from prior micro-opts....\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-to-EX load bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current pipeline pays a one-cycle load-use interlock whenever a load in\\nEX feeds either raw source field of the following instruction. CoreMark's hot\\nloops are load-heavy, and the successful recent changes show that this core\\nis already Fmax-competitive enough that recovering real CPI bubbles may be a\\nbetter structural bet than adding more prediction or buffering state.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added fetch queues, predictors, store buffers,\\nor multi-cycle multiply control regressed, so this proposal keeps the pipeline\\nshape and NRET=1 RVFI contract intact while only adding a local load-result\\nbypass around the existing MEM/EX boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current pipeline pays a one-cycle load-use interlock whenever a load in\\nEX feeds either raw source field of the following instruction. CoreMark's hot\\nloops are load-heavy, and the successful recent changes show that this core\\nis already Fmax-competitive enough that recovering real CPI bubbles may be a\\nbetter structural bet than adding more prediction or buffering state.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added fetch queues, predictors, store buffers,\\nor multi-cycle multiply control regressed, so this proposal keeps the pipeline\\nshape and NRET=1 RVFI contract intact while only adding a local load-result\\nbypass around the existing MEM/EX boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a same-cycle MEM-to-EX forwarding path for loads. When a load is in MEM\\nand its data is available, feed the MEM-stage sign/zero-extended load result\\ninto the existing EX operand forwarding mux instead of forwarding the load's\\naddress from EX/MEM. Then remove the blanket load-use stall for consumers\\nthat can safely wait under the existing dmem stall mechanism.\\n\\nKeep correctness conservative for control-flow consumers: if a dependent\\nbranch or JALR would use a load value before the dmem response is...\n  Line 38: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r13s0\", \"title\": \"Mask false load-use stalls\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares a load destination against both raw\\nsource fields of the following instruction, even when the following opcode\\ndoes not consume rs2 or does not consume any register source. That creates\\noccasional one-cycle bubbles after loads for OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR, LUI, AUIPC,\\nJAL, FENCE, and other non-rs2 consumers whenever immediate bits alias the\\nload rd. Prior lessons show added bypass datapaths often cost too much, so\\nthe safest remaining IPC micro-opt is to remove only false stalls without\\nadding a new data path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares a load destination against both raw\\nsource fields of the following instruction, even when the following opcode\\ndoes not consume rs2 or does not consume any register source. That creates\\noccasional one-cycle bubbles after loads for OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR, LUI, AUIPC,\\nJAL, FENCE, and other non-rs2 consumers whenever immediate bits alias the\\nload rd. Prior lessons show added bypass datapaths often cost too much, so\\nthe safest remaining IPC micro-opt is to remove only false stalls without\\nadding a new data path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small conservative source-use predecode to the hazard unit and gate the\\nload-use comparisons with uses_rs1 and uses_rs2. R-type, branches, and stores\\nkeep both-source behavior, so a load feeding store data still stalls exactly\\nas it does today; OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR only check rs1; LUI, AUIPC, JAL, and\\nFENCE check neither. Unknown or potentially source-reading encodings should\\ndefault to conservative checking so illegal-instruction RVFI source reporting\\nand trap behavior are not weakened.\\n\\nThis differs from the failed late load-store bypass attempt by not adding a\\nMEM-stage store-data mux or changing forwarding. It should recover a modest\\nnumber of CoreMark bubbles with only a small opcode decod...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep1/log.jsonl:\n  Line 11: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Case-based MEM byte lanes\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already moved rare high-half multiply and divide\\nwork out of the common EX path, so the next safest micro-optimization is a\\nhot load/store datapath cleanup rather than another predictor or bypass.\\nCoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word memory accesses, and the MEM\\nstage currently builds masks with variable shifts and extracts load data with\\na variable word shift before sign extension. Even if synthesis simplifies\\nsome of this, the generic shifter/mask form can add LUT depth and routing on\\na path that every load reaches.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already moved rare high-half multiply and divide\\nwork out of the common EX path, so the next safest micro-optimization is a\\nhot load/store datapath cleanup rather than another predictor or bypass.\\nCoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word memory accesses, and the MEM\\nstage currently builds masks with variable shifts and extracts load data with\\na variable word shift before sign extension. Even if synthesis simplifies\\nsome of this, the generic shifter/mask form can add LUT depth and routing on\\na path that every load reaches.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rewrite the MEM-stage byte-lane logic as explicit case statements keyed by\\naddress bits and access width. Generate store masks, replicated write data,\\nload byte/halfword selection, and RVFI masks directly per lane instead of\\nusing `<<` and `>> (addr[1:0] * 8)` expressions. This preserves the existing\\none-cycle MEM latency, dmem handshake behavior, misalignment trapping, and\\nnret=1 RVFI contract while giving yosys/nextpnr a smaller mux network for the\\nload-data and mask paths. The expected gain is mostly Fmax with no intentional\\nCoreMark cycle-count change.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"R...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/log.jsonl:\n  Line 5: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Simplify MEM load lane muxes\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"After the successful M-unit split, the remaining always-active timing paths are more likely to be ordinary RV32I datapath logic than rare multiply/divide arithmetic. The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and shifted masks for byte lanes, which can synthesize into wider mux/barrel-shifter logic on the FPGA load-to-WB path. CoreMark performs many byte, halfword, and word accesses, so a smaller MEM-stage cone should improve Fmax without changing CPI.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rewrite the MEM-stage byte-lane handling as explicit case muxes keyed by addr[1:0] instead of expressions like dmem_rdata >> (addr[1:0] * 8) and mask << addr[1:0]. Keep the same misalignment trap behavior, RVFI aligned mem_addr reporting, replicated store data convention, and MEM/WB retirement boundary. This should reduce LUT depth and routing on the common load-data path while preserving the existing five-stage control flow and single-retire NRET=1 contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace variable load-data shifts and shifted byte masks with explicit addr[1:0] case muxes for LB/LBU/LH/LHU/LW and store masks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace variable load...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep1/log.jsonl:\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Forward loads from store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best structural win is the one-entry posted store buffer, which\\nlets isolated stores retire while a stalled dmem write drains. Its remaining\\nconservative rule is that every younger memory operation stalls behind any\\nbuffered store, even when the younger load reads bytes wholly covered by that\\npending store. CoreMark stack and struct traffic can contain store-then-load\\npairs where random dmem backpressure currently turns a safe forwarding case\\ninto a full pipeline stall.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best structural win is the one-entry posted store buffer, which\\nlets isolated stores retire while a stalled dmem write drains. Its remaining\\nconservative rule is that every younger memory operation stalls behind any\\nbuffered store, even when the younger load reads bytes wholly covered by that\\npending store. CoreMark stack and struct traffic can contain store-then-load\\npairs where random dmem backpressure currently turns a safe forwarding case\\ninto a full pipeline stall.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add store-buffer load forwarding inside the existing MEM-stage decoupling\\nstructure. When the posted store buffer is full and the instruction in EX/MEM\\nis a valid, aligned load to the same word with a byte mask fully covered by\\nthe buffered store mask, synthesize the load word from the buffered replicated\\nstore data, let the load retire normally, and do not assert a dmem read for\\nthat forwarded access. Keep all nonmatching, partially covered, misaligned,\\nand store-following-memory cases stalled behind the buffer exactly as today.\\n\\nThis preserves precise in-order retirement, RVFI memory reporting, and the\\nnret=1 top-level contract while improving the useful decoupling of the proven\\none-entry store buffer. The change stays entirely in the back end, avoiding\\nthe front-end replay and prediction paths that repeatedly regresse...\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Isolate EX MEM request sideband\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The best recent structural result came from adding narrow forwarding sidebands\\nso the EX operand-control cone no longer depended directly on wide RVFI-heavy\\npipeline records. The remaining back-end stall and memory-control path still\\nreaches through the full EX/MEM bundle for mem_read, mem_write, address, store\\ndata, and width information while that same packed record also carries cold\\nRVFI metadata. On Tang Nano 20K this can create unnecessary fanout and routing\\npressure even when CPI behavior is already good.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best recent structural result came from adding narrow forwarding sidebands\\nso the EX operand-control cone no longer depended directly on wide RVFI-heavy\\npipeline records. The remaining back-end stall and memory-control path still\\nreaches through the full EX/MEM bundle for mem_read, mem_write, address, store\\ndata, and width information while that same packed record also carries cold\\nRVFI metadata. On Tang Nano 20K this can create unnecessary fanout and routing\\npressure even when CPI behavior is already good.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a narrow registered memory-request sideband at the EX/MEM boundary for the\\nfields consumed by hazard_unit and mem_stage: valid, mem_read, mem_write,\\nmem_width, mem_sext, effective address, store data, rd, and the small writeback\\ncontrols needed to form wb_data. Keep the existing ex_mem_t record for RVFI PC,\\noperand, instruction, and trap metadata, but stop using the full packed record\\nas the hot memory/stall interface.\\n\\nThis mirrors the successful forwarding-sideband direction while avoiding the\\nfailed broad control-bundle split and duplicate commit sideband. It should\\npreserve retirement orde