{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778856007422,"sessionID":"ses_1d3eea616ffepryMqag26cfmrq","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_zLrwj7VgXCf9sRPRay1D4U7J","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r5s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r5s0\n+title: \"Load-to-Store Data Bypass\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The recent `bench` history says two things clearly: frontend speculation is\n+  toxic on this core's Fmax, while backend-only store-side changes can pay.\n+  The broad stage-accurate load-use idea also already taught the failure mode:\n+  relaxing hazards is only safe if the architectural source value is still\n+  canonical at retirement.\n+\n+  A narrower version of that idea still looks promising. Today `hazard_unit`\n+  treats a younger STORE's `rs2` the same as an EX-time operand, so a\n+  load-then-store-data dependency always burns a bubble even though the store\n+  value is only needed when MEM drives `dmem_wdata`. CoreMark's list, matrix,\n+  and state kernels are store-heavy enough that trimming this one precise stall\n+  class could improve IPC without touching the fetch path or hot ALU cone.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a dedicated load-to-store-data bypass instead of another broad hazard\n+  rewrite. Keep the existing conservative load-use interlock for all normal\n+  EX-time consumers, but special-case the one safe case: if the older\n+  instruction is a load and the younger instruction is a STORE whose only\n+  dependency is `rs2`, let the STORE advance without the bubble.\n+\n+  Feed WB-stage writeback data into `mem_stage` and, for STOREs, choose the\n+  final store payload from that WB value when `wb_w_en && wb_w_addr ==\n+  ex_mem.rs2_addr`. Use that same late-selected value for physical\n+  `dmem_wdata`, for any deferred store-slot capture, and for the retiring\n+  store's RVFI-visible `rs2_rdata` / `mem_wdata` so formal and cosim observe\n+  the true architectural source operand. Keep stalls for load-to-store-address\n+  (`rs1`) hazards and for every other load-use case.\n+\n+  This stays on the backend side of the machine, adds only a small compare/mux\n+  in MEM, and directly converts a common load-update-store pattern from a fixed\n+  bubble into overlap.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Refine load-use detection so a younger STORE no longer stalls when only its `rs2` data depends on the older load; keep `rs1` and all other consumer cases conservative.\"\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add WB-to-MEM store-data bypass selection, and use the late-selected store value for `dmem_wdata`, deferred-store-slot capture, and retiring RVFI source/memory fields.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the IF/ID STORE classification needed by hazard logic and pass WB-stage writeback signals into MEM for the new store-data bypass.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add a pipeline regression covering `LW` followed immediately by dependent `SW`, including stalled-store-slot behavior and RVFI `rs2_rdata`/`mem_wdata` checks.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r5s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r5s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r5s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r5s0.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r5s0\n+title: \"Load-to-Store Data Bypass\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The recent `bench` history says two things clearly: frontend speculation is\n+  toxic on this core's Fmax, while backend-only store-side changes can pay.\n+  The broad stage-accurate load-use idea also already taught the failure mode:\n+  relaxing hazards is only safe if the architectural source value is still\n+  canonical at retirement.\n+\n+  A narrower version of that idea still looks promising. Today `hazard_unit`\n+  treats a younger STORE's `rs2` the same as an EX-time operand, so a\n+  load-then-store-data dependency always burns a bubble even though the store\n+  value is only needed when MEM drives `dmem_wdata`. CoreMark's list, matrix,\n+  and state kernels are store-heavy enough that trimming this one precise stall\n+  class could improve IPC without touching the fetch path or hot ALU cone.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a dedicated load-to-store-data bypass instead of another broad hazard\n+  rewrite. Keep the existing conservative load-use interlock for all normal\n+  EX-time consumers, but special-case the one safe case: if the older\n+  instruction is a load and the younger instruction is a STORE whose only\n+  dependency is `rs2`, let the STORE advance without the bubble.\n+\n+  Feed WB-stage writeback data into `mem_stage` and, for STOREs, choose the\n+  final store payload from that WB value when `wb_w_en && wb_w_addr ==\n+  ex_mem.rs2_addr`. Use that same late-selected value for physical\n+  `dmem_wdata`, for any deferred store-slot capture, and for the retiring\n+  store's RVFI-visible `rs2_rdata` / `mem_wdata` so formal and cosim observe\n+  the true architectural source operand. Keep stalls for load-to-store-address\n+  (`rs1`) hazards and for every other load-use case.\n+\n+  This stays on the backend side of the machine, adds only a small compare/mux\n+  in MEM, and directly converts a common load-update-store pattern from a fixed\n+  bubble into overlap.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Refine load-use detection so a younger STORE no longer stalls when only its `rs2` data depends on the older load; keep `rs1` and all other consumer cases conservative.\"\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add WB-to-MEM store-data bypass selection, and use the late-selected store value for `dmem_wdata`, deferred-store-slot capture, and retiring RVFI source/memory fields.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the IF/ID STORE classification needed by hazard logic and pass WB-stage writeback signals into MEM for the new store-data bypass.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add a pipeline regression covering `LW` followed immediately by dependent `SW`, including stalled-store-slot behavior and RVFI `rs2_rdata`/`mem_wdata` checks.\"\n\n","files":[{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r5s0.yaml","relativePath":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r5s0.yaml","type":"add","patch":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r5s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r5s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r5s0.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r5s0\n+title: \"Load-to-Store Data Bypass\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The recent `bench` history says two things clearly: frontend speculation is\n+  toxic on this core's Fmax, while backend-only store-side changes can pay.\n+  The broad stage-accurate load-use idea also already taught the failure mode:\n+  relaxing hazards is only safe if the architectural source value is still\n+  canonical at retirement.\n+\n+  A narrower version of that idea still looks promising. Today `hazard_unit`\n+  treats a younger STORE's `rs2` the same as an EX-time operand, so a\n+  load-then-store-data dependency always burns a bubble even though the store\n+  value is only needed when MEM drives `dmem_wdata`. CoreMark's list, matrix,\n+  and state kernels are store-heavy enough that trimming this one precise stall\n+  class could improve IPC without touching the fetch path or hot ALU cone.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a dedicated load-to-store-data bypass instead of another broad hazard\n+  rewrite. Keep the existing conservative load-use interlock for all normal\n+  EX-time consumers, but special-case the one safe case: if the older\n+  instruction is a load and the younger instruction is a STORE whose only\n+  dependency is `rs2`, let the STORE advance without the bubble.\n+\n+  Feed WB-stage writeback data into `mem_stage` and, for STOREs, choose the\n+  final store payload from that WB value when `wb_w_en && wb_w_addr ==\n+  ex_mem.rs2_addr`. Use that same late-selected value for physical\n+  `dmem_wdata`, for any deferred store-slot capture, and for the retiring\n+  store's RVFI-visible `rs2_rdata` / `mem_wdata` so formal and cosim observe\n+  the true architectural source operand. Keep stalls for load-to-store-address\n+  (`rs1`) hazards and for every other load-use case.\n+\n+  This stays on the backend side of the machine, adds only a small compare/mux\n+  in MEM, and directly converts a common load-update-store pattern from a fixed\n+  bubble into overlap.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Refine load-use detection so a younger STORE no longer stalls when only its `rs2` data depends on the older load; keep `rs1` and all other consumer cases conservative.\"\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add WB-to-MEM store-data bypass selection, and use the late-selected store value for `dmem_wdata`, deferred-store-slot capture, and retiring RVFI source/memory fields.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the IF/ID STORE classification needed by hazard logic and pass WB-stage writeback signals into MEM for the new store-data bypass.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add a pipeline regression covering `LW` followed immediately by dependent `SW`, including stalled-store-slot behavior and RVFI `rs2_rdata`/`mem_wdata` checks.\"\n","additions":46,"deletions":0}],"diagnostics":{},"truncated":false},"title":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r5s0.yaml","time":{"start":1778856007410,"end":1778856007420}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0f21173b3fb0bd14016a073035179481998c14b7ee2331908f"}},"id":"prt_e2c1450b7001QO8MKc0C711nMJ","sessionID":"ses_1d3eea616ffepryMqag26cfmrq","messageID":"msg_e2c144823001UKxQYtBYix46aw"}}
