{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778862667032,"sessionID":"ses_1d38f39aeffe2eKN4DQTNPadMu","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_ncntUbZ624R7SkH6qUfpX7eV","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r6s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r6s0\n+title: \"RVFI-Safe Load-to-Store Rs2 Bypass\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The remaining bench lessons point at one backend IPC loss that is still both\n+  plausible and narrow: a younger STORE always bubbles behind an older LOAD\n+  when the dependency is only on STORE data (`rs2`), even though the store does\n+  not actually consume that value until MEM. CoreMark is store-heavy enough\n+  that this specific bubble class is worth attacking, and prior results show\n+  the core tolerates backend-only store-side changes much better than any\n+  frontend speculation.\n+\n+  The last two attempts at this idea broke `rvfi_reg_check`, which is the key\n+  lesson to preserve here: the win is not in a broader hazard relaxation, but\n+  in making the store's late data path and its architectural `rs2_rdata`\n+  canonicalize from the same source.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Relax the load-use interlock only for the single safe case where an older\n+  LOAD's `rd` matches a younger STORE's `rs2` but not its address-base `rs1`.\n+  Keep every other load-use case unchanged, so the fetch path, EX operand\n+  timing, and general hazard policy stay exactly as they are today.\n+\n+  To make that safe, move the dependent STORE's final data selection fully into\n+  MEM. Let the STORE flow through EX with provisional `rs2` metadata, then in\n+  `mem_stage` select the canonical store data from either the carried value or\n+  the current WB result when the immediately older LOAD is writing the same\n+  register. Use that one canonical value for all three consumers: live\n+  `io_dmemWData`, deferred-store-slot capture, and the MEM/WB `rs2_val` that\n+  feeds RVFI. This directly addresses the prior formal failure mode while still\n+  reclaiming the unnecessary load->store-data bubble from CoreMark's hot\n+  store-heavy kernels.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Relax the load-use stall only for younger STORE rs2 dependencies; keep rs1/base-address and all non-store consumers conservative.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire raw IF/ID store-opcode context into hazard control and pass WB-stage writeback signals into MEM for late store-data canonicalization.\"\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a late store-data bypass from WB, use it for direct writes and deferred-store-slot capture, and write the same canonical value into MEM/WB.rs2_val for RVFI correctness.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add a load-followed-by-store-data dependency test that checks final memory data and the retiring STORE's RVFI rs2_rdata under the bypassed path.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r6s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r6s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r6s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r6s0.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r6s0\n+title: \"RVFI-Safe Load-to-Store Rs2 Bypass\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The remaining bench lessons point at one backend IPC loss that is still both\n+  plausible and narrow: a younger STORE always bubbles behind an older LOAD\n+  when the dependency is only on STORE data (`rs2`), even though the store does\n+  not actually consume that value until MEM. CoreMark is store-heavy enough\n+  that this specific bubble class is worth attacking, and prior results show\n+  the core tolerates backend-only store-side changes much better than any\n+  frontend speculation.\n+\n+  The last two attempts at this idea broke `rvfi_reg_check`, which is the key\n+  lesson to preserve here: the win is not in a broader hazard relaxation, but\n+  in making the store's late data path and its architectural `rs2_rdata`\n+  canonicalize from the same source.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Relax the load-use interlock only for the single safe case where an older\n+  LOAD's `rd` matches a younger STORE's `rs2` but not its address-base `rs1`.\n+  Keep every other load-use case unchanged, so the fetch path, EX operand\n+  timing, and general hazard policy stay exactly as they are today.\n+\n+  To make that safe, move the dependent STORE's final data selection fully into\n+  MEM. Let the STORE flow through EX with provisional `rs2` metadata, then in\n+  `mem_stage` select the canonical store data from either the carried value or\n+  the current WB result when the immediately older LOAD is writing the same\n+  register. Use that one canonical value for all three consumers: live\n+  `io_dmemWData`, deferred-store-slot capture, and the MEM/WB `rs2_val` that\n+  feeds RVFI. This directly addresses the prior formal failure mode while still\n+  reclaiming the unnecessary load->store-data bubble from CoreMark's hot\n+  store-heavy kernels.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Relax the load-use stall only for younger STORE rs2 dependencies; keep rs1/base-address and all non-store consumers conservative.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire raw IF/ID store-opcode context into hazard control and pass WB-stage 