{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778869263711,"sessionID":"ses_1d3216461ffeckfG8t56A2F4SQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_gQIhGwJfZFBKwLZJ0mC5Qcrx","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/LESSONS.md","offset":1,"limit":400},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.23%): Pulling DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the one-cycle EX ALU and handling them with a stall-on-use iterative unit can buy large Fmax gains because CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by MUL, load/store, and branch traffic, so rare divide stalls cost less than keeping a 32-bit divider on the common critical path.\n2: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, -14.32%): On this core's combinational IF/ID path, adding ID-stage BTFNT and always-taken JAL prediction pulled decode, immediate-target add, and next-PC select onto the fetch critical path, and the resulting Fmax loss outweighed any CoreMark loop-branch bubble savings.\n3: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.57%): After DIV/REM already moved to `rtl/div_unit.sv`, splitting `alu_op` into hot/cold classes and replacing one flat ALU result `case` with separate `hot_result`/`m_result` muxes plus an `op[4]` final select regressed, suggesting bench's real EX critical path is elsewhere and extra class-select structure can cost more Fmax than it saves.\n4: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (improvement, +10.45%): Backend-only buffering of aligned normal-RAM stores is a good lever on this RTL because CoreMark issues enough stack/list/matrix/state stores that turning `io_dmemReady=0` on STOREs from a whole-pipeline freeze into a one-deep deferred write raises IPC, as long as younger memory ops and end-visible markers stay fenced behind the pending slot.\n5: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (regression, +5.80%): On `bench`, an IF replay cache stops being a good trade once it also predicts and carries `predicted_next_pc`/`replayed` metadata for multi-step stall traversal, because the added branch/jump predecode, replay-control muxing, and EX repair compare cost more Fmax than CoreMark gains from hiding random `io_imemReady` bubbles.\n6: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r3s0 (regression, -8.05%): Once divider offload and the one-deep store slot are in place, `bench` is not obviously bound by wide MEM-side compares, since pruning `ex_mem_t` and replacing `instr`/`addr` compares with `is_ebreak` and high-bit-zero decodes still regressed, suggesting this backend control/data plumbing can cost more routing than it saves.\n7: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r3s1 (regression, -24.32%): On `bench`, even stall-only load replay in `mem_stage` is a bad trade when it adds same-cycle replay-hit/tag-compare, `io_dmemReady` hazard escape, and store-invalidation plumbing, because the MEM-side timing/routing cost outweighs CoreMark's gain from hiding repeated normal-RAM load stalls.\n8: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (regression, -9.20%): On `bench`, even an 8-entry EX-trained taken-target cache regressed, which suggests the IF `next_pc` path cannot afford extra tag-compare and target-mux work because CoreMark's loop-backedge hits do not repay fetch-side predictor timing cost on this core.\n9: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): `bench` currently latches raw `instr[19:15]`/`instr[24:20]` reads into RVFI, so relaxing load-use stalls from field matches to true EX-time consumers needs architectural-source canonicalization or earlier forwarding, not a late MEM/WB repair, or riscv-formal `reg` will catch stale aliased `rs*` values.\n10: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On this FPGA, the one-deep deferred-store win does not scale cleanly to a two-entry oldest-first queue, because doubling queued addr/data/mask state and adding same-cycle `store_queue_full`/`store_queue_nonempty` enqueue-dequeue fencing in `mem_stage` and `hazard_unit` can push MEM-side routing past placement even while staying off the fetch path.\n11: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (regression, -45.37%): On `bench`, the safe zone for fetch-side branch prediction is effectively zero, because even a one-slot EX-trained loop-backedge predictor's single IF tag-compare/target-mux plus `predicted_*` carry-and-repair plumbing into EX costs more Fmax than CoreMark's hot loop backedges repay.\n\n(End of file - total 11 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.23%): Pulling DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the one-cycle EX ALU and handling them with a stall-on-use iterative unit can buy large Fmax gains because CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by MUL, load/store, and branch traffic, so rare divide stalls cost less than keeping a 32-bit divider on the common critical path.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, -14.32%): On this core's combinational IF/ID path, adding ID-stage BTFNT and always-taken JAL prediction pulled decode, immediate-target add, and next-PC select onto the fetch critical path, and the resulting Fmax loss outweighed any CoreMark loop-branch bubble savings.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.57%): After DIV/REM already moved to `rtl/div_unit.sv`, splitting `alu_op` into hot/cold classes and replacing one flat ALU result `case` with separate `hot_result`/`m_result` muxes plus an `op[4]` final select regressed, suggesting bench's real EX critical path is elsewhere and extra class-select structure can cost more Fmax than it saves.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (improvement, +10.45%): Backend-only buffering of aligned normal-RAM stores is a good lever on this RTL because CoreMark issues enough stack/list/matrix/state stores that turning `io_dmemReady=0` on STOREs from a whole-pipeline freeze into a one-deep deferred write raises IPC, as long as younger memory ops and end-visible markers stay fenced behind the pending slot.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (regression, +5.80%): On `bench`, an IF replay cache stops being a good trade once it also predicts and carries `predicted_next_pc`/`replayed` metadata for multi-step stall traversal, because the added branch/jump predecode, replay-control muxing, and EX repair compare cost more Fmax than CoreMark gains from hiding random `io_imemReady` bubbles.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r3s0 (regression, -8.05%): Once divider offload and the one-deep store slot are in place, `bench` is not obviously bound by wide MEM-side compares, since pruning `ex_mem_t` and replacing `instr`/`addr` compares with `is_ebreak` and high-bit-zero decodes still regressed, suggesting this backend control/data plumbing can cost more routing than it saves.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r3s1 (regression, -24.32%): On `bench`, even stall-only load replay in `mem_stage` is a bad trade when it adds same-cycle replay-hit/tag-compare, `io_dmemReady` hazard escape, and store-invalidation plumbing, because the MEM-side timing/routing cost outweighs CoreMark's gain from hiding repeated normal-RAM load stalls.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (regression, -9.20%): On `bench`, even an 8-entry EX-trained taken-target cache regressed, which suggests the IF `next_pc` path cannot afford extra tag-compare and target-mux work because CoreMark's loop-backedge hits do not repay fetch-side predictor timing cost on this core.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): `bench` currently latches raw `instr[19:15]`/`instr[24:20]` reads into RVFI, so relaxing load-use stalls from field matches to true EX-time consumers needs architectural-source canonicalization or earlier forwarding, not a late MEM/WB repair, or riscv-formal `reg` will catch stale aliased `rs*` values.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On this FPGA, the one-deep