{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778869486011,"sessionID":"ses_1d3216461ffeckfG8t56A2F4SQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_yGAiRYXzirb5uDf66nwquH14","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r8s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r8s0\n+title: \"Zero-Bubble Store Slot Refill\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The current best `bench` core already proved that deferred normal-RAM stores\n+  are a winning backend lever, but the one-deep slot still loses a bubble\n+  whenever a pending store finally drains and the next instruction in MEM is\n+  another bufferable RAM store. Hazard control fences that younger store until\n+  the slot bit clears, so bursty CoreMark store traffic still pays a handoff\n+  penalty even though the core already knows both stores' addr/data/mask and\n+  only needs to preserve oldest-first external visibility.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Teach the existing single deferred-store slot to drain and refill in the same\n+  cycle instead of growing it into a true two-entry queue. When\n+  `store_slot_valid` is set and `io_dmemReady` returns, `mem_stage` should\n+  drive the older deferred store to the bus and, if the current EX/MEM\n+  instruction is another aligned normal-RAM store, immediately capture that\n+  younger store into the same slot while retiring it once through RVFI.\n+\n+  `hazard_unit` would then keep fencing loads, MMIO/non-bufferable stores, and\n+  `EBREAK` behind a pending slot, but allow exactly one younger bufferable RAM\n+  store through on the drain cycle for a refill. This keeps the proven\n+  backend-only store-buffering strategy, avoids the placement/routing cost that\n+  killed the real two-entry queue, and should recover part of the store-burst\n+  IPC win that the deeper queue was chasing.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a same-cycle drain-and-refill path so a bufferable RAM store can retire into the existing slot while the older deferred store drains to dmem, with RVFI and mem_wdata metadata still tied to the younger retiring store and physical write ordering kept oldest-first.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Refine the pending-store fence so loads, MMIO/non-bufferable stores, and EBREAK still stall behind a full slot, but the exact dmem-ready drain cycle may admit one younger bufferable RAM store for refill.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add a cocotb regression for back-to-back stalled RAM stores that verifies retire-once behavior, same-cycle slot refill, preserved external write order, and continued fencing for EBREAK or a younger load behind the slot.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r8s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r8s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r8s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r8s0.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r8s0\n+title: \"Zero-Bubble Store Slot Refill\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The current best `bench` core already proved that deferred normal-RAM stores\n+  are a winning backend lever, but the one-deep slot still loses a bubble\n+  whenever a pending store finally drains and the next instruction in MEM is\n+  another bufferable RAM store. Hazard control fences that younger store until\n+  the slot bit clears, so bursty CoreMark store traffic still pays a handoff\n+  penalty even though the core already knows both stores' addr/data/mask and\n+  only needs to preserve oldest-first external visibility.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Teach the existing single deferred-store slot to drain and refill in the same\n+  cycle instead of growing it into a true two-entry queue. When\n+  `store_slot_valid` is set and `io_dmemReady` returns, `mem_stage` should\n+  drive the older deferred store to the bus and, if the current EX/MEM\n+  instruction is another aligned normal-RAM store, immediately capture that\n+  younger store into the same slot while retiring it once through RVFI.\n+\n+  `hazard_unit` would then keep fencing loads, MMIO/non-bufferable stores, and\n+  `EBREAK` behind a pending slot, but allow exactly one younger bufferable RAM\n+  store through on the drain cycle for a refill. This keeps the proven\n+  backend-only store-buffering strategy, avoids the placement/routing cost that\n+  killed the real two-entry queue, and should recover part of the store-burst\n+  IPC win that the deeper queue was chasing.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a same-cycle drain-and-refill path so a bufferable RAM store can retire into the existing slot while the older deferred store drains to dmem, with RVFI and mem_wdata metadata still tied to the younger retiring store and physical write ordering kept oldest-first.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Refine the pending-store fence so loads, MMIO/non-bufferable stores, and EBREAK still stall behind a full slot, but the exact dmem-ready drain cycle may admit one younger bufferable RAM store for refill.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add a cocotb regression for back-to-back stalled RAM stores that verifies retire-once behavior, same-cycle slot refill, preserved external write order, and continued fencing for EBREAK or a younger load behind the slot.\"\n\n","files":[