{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778870940624,"sessionID":"ses_1d3082364ffeSUTMfN6QLwgtRN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_SpB9oyB7I4npckqpUCh60Wh2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg '\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r(1|2|3|4|5|6|8)s0\"' \"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","timeout":120000,"workdir":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3","description":"Show prior slot-0 hypothesis records"},"output":"{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Iterative Cold Divider Off EX Path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The frozen bench core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\ncombinational ALU path as ADD, address generation, branch support, and\\nMUL. On a Tang Nano 20K, a full 32-bit divider is a strong candidate for\\nthe universal EX-stage critical path, yet CoreMark's timed loop is mostly\\nmultiply, load/store, and branch traffic and should pay little or no steady\\nstate penalty from slower divide instructions.\\n\\nThis makes divider extraction the highest-leverage micro-opt still missing\\nfrom the frozen bench fixture: it targets Fmax directly while preserving the\\nhot one-cycle behavior for the instructions that dominate CoreMark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The frozen bench core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\ncombinational ALU path as ADD, address generation, branch support, and\\nMUL. On a Tang Nano 20K, a full 32-bit divider is a strong candidate for\\nthe universal EX-stage critical path, yet CoreMark's timed loop is mostly\\nmultiply, load/store, and branch traffic and should pay little or no steady\\nstate penalty from slower divide instructions.\\n\\nThis makes divider extraction the highest-leverage micro-opt still missing\\nfrom the frozen bench fixture: it targets Fmax directly while preserving the\\nhot one-cycle behavior for the instructions that dominate CoreMark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases in `rtl/alu.sv`\\nwith a small iterative divider/remainder unit that is entered only for\\ndivide-class ALU ops. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift/compare, address generation,\\nbranches, jumps, loads/stores, and MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing\\nsingle-cycle datapath.\\n\\nIn EX, detect divide-class operations, latch their operands and control,\\nstall IF and ID while the divider runs, and emit exactly one EX/MEM result\\nwhen the quotient or remainder is ready. Preserve RV32IM edge cases\\nprecisely: divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1 overflow behavior, signed/unsigned\\nvariants, ALTOPS behavior under formal, and the existing single-retire\\nRVFI/order contract. The expected win is a shorter common EX critical path\\nand higher median Fmax with negligible CoreMark IPC loss because DIV/REM are\\ncold in the benchmark's hot region.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 10, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real DIV/REM arithmetic from the hot combinational ALU result mux while preserving the one-cycle RV32I and multiply paths plus ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small iterative divider/remainder unit that produces exact RV32IM DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU results and exposes a busy/done handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Launch divide-class ops into the new unit, hold younger issue while it runs, and write a single EX/MEM result when the divider completes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Fold divider-busy into the existing stall network so IF/ID freeze cleanly during long divide operations without changing redirect or load-use behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the divider busy/complete signals through the top-level stage connections while keeping the current RVFI retirement boundary and order semantics intact.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Narrow the ALU unit test to the still-combinational operations so it no longer assumes DIV/REM are produced directly by alu.sv.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_div_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Add dedicated cocotb coverage for divide and remainder semantics, including divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1, signedness, and back-to-back operations.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add an end-to-end pipeline test that proves divide instructions stall younger ops but still retire once with correct RVFI data and writeback results.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 10, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real DIV/REM arithmetic from the hot combinational ALU result mux while preserving the one-cycle RV32I and multiply paths plus ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small iterative divider/remainder unit that produces exact RV32IM DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU results and exposes a busy/done handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Launch divide-class ops into the new unit, hold younger issue while it runs, and write a single EX/MEM result when the divider completes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Fold divider-busy into the existing stall network so IF/ID freeze cleanly during long divide operations without changing redirect or load-use behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the divider busy/complete signals through the top-level stage connections while keeping the current RVFI retirement boundary and order semantics intact.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Narrow the ALU unit test to the still-combinational operations so it no longer assumes DIV/REM are produced directly by alu.sv.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_div_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Add dedicated cocotb coverage for divide and remainder semantics, including divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1, signedness, and back-to-back operations.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add an end-to-end pipeline test that proves divide instructions stall younger ops but still retire once with correct RVFI data and writeback results.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 405.07, \"delta_pct\": 43.23, \"vs_baseline\": 43.23, \"fmax_mhz\": 181.95, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5818, \"ff\": 2255, \"seeds\": [195.73, 181.95, 181.69], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-15T09:16:55.436320Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.23%): Pulling DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the one-cycle EX ALU and handling them with a stall-on-use iterative unit can buy large Fmax gains because CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by MUL, load/store, and branch traffic, so rare divide stalls cost less than keeping a 32-bit divider on the common critical path.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"RTL-only hot/cold ALU opcode split\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best bench result already removed DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU from the\\nhot one-cycle EX path, but `rtl/alu.sv` still uses a dense ALU-op encoding\\nand one broad result case covering common RV32I work, multiply results, and\\ndummy divide-class outputs. That leaves the always-hot ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path sharing decode and mux structure with cold M-extension cases.\\n\\nA sibling-core experiment on `cores/v1` showed that this specific RTL-only\\nreshaping can improve fitness, while more aggressive multiplier rewrites and\\nsource-qualified hazard logic either regressed or broke. That makes the ALU\\nmux shape the best-supported remaining `micro_opt` angle for the frozen\\nbench fixture.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best bench result already removed DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU from the\\nhot one-cycle EX path, but `rtl/alu.sv` still uses a dense ALU-op encoding\\nand one broad result case covering common RV32I work, multiply results, and\\ndummy divide-class outputs. That leaves the always-hot ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path sharing decode and mux structure with cold M-extension cases.\\n\\nA sibling-core experiment on `cores/v1` showed that this specific RTL-only\\nreshaping can improve fitness, while more aggressive multiplier rewrites and\\nsource-qualified hazard logic either regressed or broke. That makes the ALU\\nmux shape the best-supported remaining `micro_opt` angle for the frozen\\nbench fixture.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Re-encode the ALU operation constants so bit 4 cleanly marks RV32M\\noperations, with MUL/MULH/MULHSU/MULHU/DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU grouped as a cold\\n`op[4]=1` class while the common RV32I/simple operations stay in the hot\\n`op[4]=0` class. Then refactor `rtl/alu.sv` into two local result muxes:\\na simple-result mux for ADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI and an M-result mux\\nfor multiply plus the existing placeholder divide outputs used when the real\\ndivider lives in `rtl/div_unit.sv`.\\n\\nThis keeps the pipeline, RVFI contract, divider protocol, and CoreMark\\ncycle behavior unchanged while giving synthesis a shallower common EX result\\nselect on the instructions that dominate CoreMark. The expected gain is\\nmainly Fmax, not IPC, which fits the post-divider state of `cores/bench`.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Re-encode ALU op localparams so RV32M ops occupy a cold `op[4]=1` class without changing architectural meaning.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Split the broad ALU result case into separate hot RV32I and cold RV32M result muxes, selecting the final output with the new hot/cold opcode bit.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Re-encode ALU op localparams so RV32M ops occupy a cold `op[4]=1` class without changing architectural meaning.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Split the broad ALU result case into separate hot RV32I and cold RV32M result muxes, selecting the final output with the new hot/cold opcode bit.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 427.64, \"delta_pct\": 5.57, \"vs_baseline\": 51.21, \"fmax_mhz\": 192.09, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5819, \"ff\": 2255, \"seeds\": [196.5, 186.85, 192.09], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-15T09:32:08.870362Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.57%): After DIV/REM already moved to `rtl/div_unit.sv`, splitting `alu_op` into hot/cold classes and replacing one flat ALU result `case` with separate `hot_result`/`m_result` muxes plus an `op[4]` final select regressed, suggesting bench's real EX critical path is elsewhere and extra class-select structure can cost more Fmax than it saves.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Prune EX/MEM payload and narrow store-slot control\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` result already found the two large levers that pay\\non this RTL: keep divide/remainder off the hot EX path, and hide some\\nstore-only dmem stalls with the one-deep retirement slot. The failed branch\\nand replay experiments showed the opposite boundary: adding more fetch-side\\ncontrol logic costs too much Fmax on this core.\\n\\nThat leaves the MEM-side control cone as the best micro-opt target. The\\ncurrent store-slot path still carries redundant EX/MEM metadata and uses\\nalways-live wide compares (`instr == 32'h0010_0073`, `addr < 32'h0010_0000`)\\nto drive global stall decisions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` result already found the two large levers that pay\\non this RTL: keep divide/remainder off the hot EX path, and hide some\\nstore-only dmem stalls with the one-deep retirement slot. The failed branch\\nand replay experiments showed the opposite boundary: adding more fetch-side\\ncontrol logic costs too much Fmax on this core.\\n\\nThat leaves the MEM-side control cone as the best micro-opt target. The\\ncurrent store-slot path still carries redundant EX/MEM metadata and uses\\nalways-live wide compares (`instr == 32'h0010_0073`, `addr < 32'h0010_0000`)\\nto drive global stall decisions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Collapse backend metadata and replace wide store-slot control compares with\\nnarrower registered signals. In `ex_mem_t`, remove fields that do not add\\narchitectural information on this core: `write_data` duplicates the already\\nforwarded `rs2_val`, and `branch_taken` / `branch_target` are registered but\\nnever consumed after EX. Replace the top-level EBREAK fence compare with a\\none-bit `is_ebreak` marker captured in EX/MEM, and rewrite the normal-RAM\\nstore-slot range check as an explicit high-bit-zero decode instead of a\\n32-bit less-than comparator.\\n\\nThis keeps CoreMark IPC, RVFI order, and memory ordering unchanged while\\nshrinking the EX/MEM register, the MEM-stage store-data/control muxing, and\\nthe stall network fed by the pending-store slot. It stays off the frontend\\ncritical path and should stack cleanly with the current divider-offload plus\\none-deep-store-slot design.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Trim `ex_mem_t` by removing redundant `write_data`, `branch_taken`, and `branch_target` fields, and add a 1-bit registered `is_ebreak` marker for the store-slot fence path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Stop populating unused branch metadata, keep `rs2_val` as the single post-forwarded store operand, and capture the EX/MEM `is_ebreak` flag without changing redirect or divide behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Build replicated store write data from `rs2_val` instead of a duplicate payload field, and replace the normal-RAM `< 0x0010_0000` check with an equivalent high-bit decode on the store-slot control path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Drive `ex_mem_end_fence` from the registered EX/MEM EBREAK marker instead of a live 32-bit instruction compare, keeping the existing hazard, RVFI, and bus interfaces unchanged.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Trim `ex_mem_t` by removing redundant `write_data`, `branch_taken`, and `branch_target` fields, and add a 1-bit registered `is_ebreak` marker for the store-slot fence path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Stop populating unused branch metadata, keep `rs2_val` as the single post-forwarded store operand, and capture the EX/MEM `is_ebreak` flag without changing redirect or divide behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Build replicated store write data from `rs2_val` instead of a duplicate payload field, and replace the normal-RAM `< 0x0010_0000` check with an equivalent high-bit decode on the store-slot control path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Drive `ex_mem_end_fence` from the registered EX/MEM EBREAK marker instead of a live 32-bit instruction compare, keeping the existing hazard, RVFI, and bus interfaces unchanged.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 411.4, \"delta_pct\": -8.05, \"vs_baseline\": 45.46, \"fmax_mhz\": 183.39, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4457730, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5806, \"ff\": 2257, \"seeds\": [183.39, 172.92, 185.67], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-15T10:03:58.636837Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r3s0 (regression, -8.05%): Once divider offload and the one-deep store slot are in place, `bench` is not obviously bound by wide MEM-side compares, since pruning `ex_mem_t` and replacing `instr`/`addr` compares with `is_ebreak` and high-bit-zero decodes still regressed, suggesting this backend control/data plumbing can cost more routing than it saves.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Stage-Accurate Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"After divider offload and the one-deep store retirement slot, the cheapest\\nremaining CoreMark IPC loss is likely the still-conservative load-use\\ninterlock. `hazard_unit` compares a load destination against raw IF/ID rs1\\nand rs2 bitfields even when the younger instruction does not actually need\\nthose fields in EX, and it still forces a bubble for STORE rs2 even though\\nstore data is only consumed one stage later in MEM.\\n\\nThat means the core can still lose cycles to false immediate-field matches\\nand to real load-then-store-data dependencies that do not protect any EX\\ncritical operand. This is a narrow backend/control cleanup that attacks IPC\\nwithout adding predictor state, fetch-path muxing, or a new EX datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"After divider offload and the one-deep store retirement slot, the cheapest\\nremaining CoreMark IPC loss is likely the still-conservative load-use\\ninterlock. `hazard_unit` compares a load destination against raw IF/ID rs1\\nand rs2 bitfields even when the younger instruction does not actually need\\nthose fields in EX, and it still forces a bubble for STORE rs2 even though\\nstore data is only consumed one stage later in MEM.\\n\\nThat means the core can still lose cycles to false immediate-field matches\\nand to real load-then-store-data dependencies that do not protect any EX\\ncritical operand. This is a narrow backend/control cleanup that attacks IPC\\nwithout adding predictor state, fetch-path muxing, or a new EX datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make the load-use detector stage-accurate instead of field-accurate.\\nDecode conservative `uses_rs1_ex` / `uses_rs2_ex` bits from the IF/ID\\ninstruction inside `hazard_unit`, and only stall when the older load feeds a\\nvalue that the younger instruction truly needs in EX. Keep unknown or\\nillegal encodings conservative, but stop treating OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR,\\nLUI/AUIPC/JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK as two-source consumers.\\n\\nFor STORE specifically, treat rs1 as an EX-time dependency and rs2 as a\\nMEM-time dependency. Allow a load followed immediately by a STORE of that\\nloaded value to advance without the bubble, then repair the data in\\n`mem_stage` with a local MEM/WB-to-store-data bypass that also updates the\\nretiring store's RVFI-visible rs2/mem_wdata fields. The bypass must use the\\nheld MEM/WB payload rather than `valid`, because `hold_wb` intentionally\\nclears only retirement while preserving forwarding data during dmem stalls.\\n\\nThis should recover a small but real class of CoreMark bubbles while keeping\\nthe change off the fetch critical path and away from the hot ALU/branch cone.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the raw rs1/rs2 load-use test with conservative opcode-based EX-operand use bits, so only true EX-time consumers stall and STORE rs2 stops forcing a bubble.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass the IF/ID instruction into hazard detection so the interlock can classify source timing without changing the architectural pipeline fields or top-level IO contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a local MEM/WB-to-store-data bypass and use the forwarded value for store write-data formation plus RVFI rs2/mem_wdata reporting, even across `hold_wb` cycles.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add coverage for non-consuming post-load instructions that should no longer stall spuriously, and for a load-then-store-data sequence that must retire correctly with and without dmem backpressure.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the raw rs1/rs2 load-use test with conservative opcode-based EX-operand use bits, so only true EX-time consumers stall and STORE rs2 stops forcing a bubble.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass the IF/ID instruction into hazard detection so the interlock can classify source timing without changing the architectural pipeline fields or top-level IO contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a local MEM/WB-to-store-data bypass and use the forwarded value for store write-data formation plus RVFI rs2/mem_wdata reporting, even across `hold_wb` cycles.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add coverage for non-consuming post-load instructions that should no longer stall spuriously, and for a load-then-store-data sequence that must retire correctly with and without dmem backpressure.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\nturncode=0)\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_srai_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_srai_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:03 (3)\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_srai_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:02 (2)\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_srai_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_srai_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_srai_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_sub_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:02  Status: