bubbles behind an older LOAD\\nwhen the dependency is only on STORE data (`rs2`), even though the store does\\nnot actually consume that value until MEM. CoreMark is store-heavy enough\\nthat this specific bubble class is worth attacking, and prior results show\\nthe core tolerates backend-only store-side changes much better than any\\nfrontend speculation.\\n\\nThe last two attempts at this idea broke `rvfi_reg_check`, which is the key\\nlesson to preserve here: the win is not in a broader hazard relaxation, but\\nin making the store's late data path and its architectural `rs2_rdata`\\ncanonicalize from the same source.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Relax the load-use interlock only for the single safe case where an older\\nLOAD's `rd` matches a younger STORE's `rs2` but not its address-base `rs1`.\\nKeep every other load-use case unchanged, so the fetch path, EX operand\\ntiming, and general hazard policy stay exactly as they are today.\\n\\nTo make that safe, move the dependent STORE's final data selection fully into\\nMEM. Let the STORE flow through EX with provisional `rs2` metadata, then in\\n`mem_stage` select the canonical store data from either the carried value or\\nthe current WB result when the immediately older LOAD is writing the same\\nregister. Use that one canonical value for all three consumers: live\\n`io_dmemWData`, deferred-store-slot capture, and the MEM/WB `rs2_val` that\\nfeeds RVFI. This directly addresses the prior formal failure mode while still\\nreclaiming the unnecessary load->store-data bubble from CoreMark's hot\\nstore-heavy kernels.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Relax the load-use stall only for younger STORE rs2 dependencies; keep rs1/base-address and all non-store consumers conservative.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire raw IF/ID store-opcode context into hazard control and pass WB-stage writeback signals into MEM for late store-data canonicalization.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a late store-data bypass from WB, use it for direct writes and deferred-store-slot capture, and write the same canonical value into MEM/WB.rs2_val for RVFI correctness.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add a load-followed-by-store-data dependency test that checks final memory data and the retiring STORE's RVFI rs2_rdata under the bypassed path.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Relax the load-use stall only for younger STORE rs2 dependencies; keep rs1/base-address and all non-store consumers conservative.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire raw IF/ID store-opcode context into hazard control and pass WB-stage writeback signals into MEM for late store-data canonicalization.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a late store-data bypass from WB, use it for direct writes and deferred-store-slot capture, and write the same canonical value into MEM/WB.rs2_val for RVFI correctness.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add a load-followed-by-store-data dependency test that checks final memory data and the retiring STORE's RVFI rs2_rdata under the bypassed path.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\nce to VCD file: engine_0/trace0.vcd\\nSBY 13:59:16 [cover] engine_0: ##   0:00:18  Writing trace to Verilog testbench: engine_0/trace0_tb.v\\nSBY 13:59:16 [cover] engine_0: ##   0:00:18  Writing trace to constraints file: engine_0/trace0.smtc\\nSBY 13:59:16 [cover] engine_0: ##   0:00:18  Writing trace to Yosys witness file: engine_0/trace0.yw\\nSBY 13:59:17 [cover] engine_0: ##   0:00:18  Status: passed\\nSBY 13:59:17 [cover] engine_0: finished (returncode=0)\\nSBY 13:59:17 [c