heck.sv:44.6-44.59 (_witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_44_60)\\nSBY 13:59:15 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:17  Writing trace to VCD file: engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 13:59:16 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:17  Writing trace to Verilog testbench: engine_0/trace_tb.v\\nSBY 13:59:16 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:17  Writing trace to constraints file: engine_0/trace.smtc\\nSBY 13:59:16 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:17  Writing trace to Yosys witness file: engine_0/trace.yw\\nSBY 13:59:16 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:17  Status: failed\\nSBY 13:59:16 [reg_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=1)\\nSBY 13:59:16 [reg_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: FAIL\\nSBY 13:59:16 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:18 (18)\\nSBY 13:59:16 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:09 (9)\\nSBY 13:59:16 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned FAIL\\nSBY 13:59:16 [reg_ch0] summary: counterexample trace: reg_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 13:59:16 [reg_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_44_60 at rvfi_reg_check.sv:44.6-44.59 step 20\\nSBY 13:59:16 [reg_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\n\\nFull run log: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260515-001-r6s0/formal/last_run-29898.log\\nPer-check logs in: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260515-001-r6s0/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-29898/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 6, \"scribe_skipped\": \"TimeoutError: scribe timed out after 240s\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Zero-Bubble Store Slot Refill\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` core already proved that deferred normal-RAM stores\\nare a winning backend lever, but the one-deep slot still loses a bubble\\nwhenever a pending store finally drains and the next instruction in MEM is\\nanother bufferable RAM store. Hazard control fences that younger store until\\nthe slot bit clears, so bursty CoreMark store traffic still pays a handoff\\npenalty even though the core already knows both stores' addr/data/mask and\\nonly needs to preserve oldest-first external visibility.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` core already proved that deferred normal-RAM stores\\nare a winning backend lever, but the one-deep slot still loses a bubble\\nwhenever a pending store finally drains and the next instruction in MEM is\\nanother bufferable RAM store. Hazard control fences that younger store until\\nthe slot bit clears, so bursty CoreMark store traffic still pays a handoff\\npenalty even though the core already knows both stores' addr/data/mask and\\nonly needs to preserve oldest-first external visibility.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Teach the existing single deferred-store slot to drain and refill in the same\\ncycle instead of growing it into a true two-entry queue. When\\n`store_slot_valid` is set and `io_dmemReady` returns, `mem_stage` should\\ndrive the older deferred store to the bus and, if the current EX/MEM\\ninstruction is another aligned normal-RAM store, immediately capture that\\nyounger store into the same slot while retiring it once through RVFI.\\n\\n`hazard_unit` would then keep fencing loads, MMIO/non-bufferable stores, and\\n`EBREAK` behind a pending slot, but allow exactly one younger bufferable RAM\\nstore through on the drain cycle for a refill. This keeps the proven\\nbackend-only store-buffering strategy, avoids the placement/routing cost that\\nkilled the real two-entry queue, and should recover part of the store-burst\\nIPC win that the deeper queue was chasing.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a same-cycle drain-and-refill path so a bufferable RAM store can retire into the existing slot while the older deferred store drains to dmem, with RVFI and mem_wdata metadata still tied to the younger retiring store and physical write ordering kept oldest-first.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Refine the pending-store fence so loads, MMIO/non-bufferable stores, and EBREAK still stall behind a full slot, but the exact dmem-ready drain cycle may admit one younger bufferable RAM store for refill.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add a cocotb regression for back-to-back stalled RAM stores that verifies retire-once behavior, same-cycle slot refill, preserved external write order, and continued fencing for EBREAK or a younger load behind the slot.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a same-cycle drain-and-refill path so a bufferable RAM store can retire into the existing slot while the older deferred store drains to dmem, with RVFI and mem_wdata metadata still tied to the younger retiring store and physical write ordering kept oldest-first.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Refine the pending-store fence so loads, MMIO/non-bufferable stores, and EBREAK still stall behind a full slot, but the exact dmem-ready drain cycle may admit one younger bufferable RAM store for refill.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add a cocotb regression for back-to-back stalled RAM stores that verifies retire-once behavior, same-cycle slot refill, preserved external write order, and continued fencing for EBREAK or a younger load behind the slot.\"}], \"outcome\": \"placement_failed\", \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": \"placement_failed\", \"seeds\": [null, null, null], \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r8s0 (placement_failed, n/a): On this FPGA, even a one-deep deferred-store slot can hit placement limits if `hazard_unit` and `mem_stage` try to use same-cycle `io_dmemReady` drain-and-refill control to let a younger RAM store retire into the slot, because the ready-gated refill/fence-release path itself becomes expensive under CoreMark's bursty store traffic.\"}\n","metadata":{"output":"...\n\nonservative load-use\\ninterlock. `hazard_unit` compares a load destination against raw IF/ID rs1\\nand rs2 bitfields even when the younger instruction does not actually need\\nthose fields in EX, and it still forces a bubble for STORE rs2 even though\\nstore data is only consumed one stage later in MEM.\\n\\nThat means the core can still lose cycles to false immediate-field matches\\nand to real load-then-store-data dependencies that do not protect any EX\\ncritical operand. This is a narrow backend/control cleanup that attacks IPC\\nwithout adding predictor state, fetch-path muxing, or a new EX datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make the load-use detector stage-accurate instead of field-accurate.\\nDecode conservative `uses_rs1_ex` / `uses_rs2_ex` bits from the IF/ID\\ninstruction inside `hazard_unit`, and only stall when the older load feeds a\\nvalue that the younger instruction truly needs in EX. Keep unknown or\\nillegal encodings conservative, but stop treating OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR,\\nLUI/AUIPC/JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK as two-source consumers.\\n\\nFor STORE specifically, treat rs1 as an EX-time dependency and rs2 as a\\nMEM-time dependency. Allow a load followed immediately by a STORE of that\\nloaded value to advance without the bubble, then repair the data in\\n`mem_stage` with a local MEM/WB-to-store-data bypass that also updates the\\nretiring store's RVFI-visible rs2/mem_wdata fields. The bypass must use the\\nheld MEM/WB payload rather than `valid`, because `hold_wb` intentionally\\nclears only retirement while preserving forwarding data during dmem stalls.\\n\\nThis should recover a small but real class of CoreMark bubbles while keeping\\nthe change off the fetch critical path and away from the hot ALU/branch cone.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the raw rs1/rs2 load-use test with conservative opcode-based EX-operand use bits, so only true EX-time consumers stall and STORE rs2 stops forcing a bubble.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass the IF/ID instruction into hazard detection so the interlock can classify source timing without changing the architectural pipeline fields or top-level IO contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a local MEM/WB-to-store-data bypass and use the forwarded value for store write-data formation plus RVFI rs2/mem_wdata reporting, even across `hold_wb` cycles.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add coverage for non-consuming post-load instructions that should no longer stall spuriously, and for a load-then-store-data sequence that must retire correctly with and without dmem backpressure.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the raw rs1/rs2 load-use test with conservative opcode-based EX-operand use bits, so only true EX-time consumers stall and STORE rs2 stops forcing a bubble.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass the IF/ID instruction into hazard detection so the interlock can classify source timing without changing the architectural pipeline fields or top-level IO contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a local MEM/WB-to-store-data bypass and use the forwarded value for store write-data formation plus RVFI rs2/mem_wdata reporting, even across `hold_wb` cycles.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add coverage for non-consuming post-load instructions that should no longer stall spuriously, and for a load-then-store-data sequence that must retire correctly with and without dmem backpressure.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\nturncode=0)\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_srai_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_srai_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:03 (3)\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_srai_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:02 (2)\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_srai_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_srai_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_srai_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_sub_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:02  Status: passed\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_sub_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=0)\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_sub_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_sub_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:03 (3)\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_sub_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:02 (2)\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_sub_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_sub_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\\nSBY 11:17:48 [insn_sub_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\n\\nFormal: 52 passed, 1 failed\\nFailed: reg_ch0\\n\\n--- reg_ch0/logfile.txt (last 30 lines) ---\\nSBY 11:17:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 9..\\nSBY 11:17:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 10..\\nSBY 11:17:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 11..\\nSBY 11:17:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 12..\\nSBY 11:17:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 13..\\nSBY 11:17:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 14..\\nSBY 11:17:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 15..\\nSBY 11:17:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:01  Skipping step 16..\\nSBY 11:17:27 [reg_ch0] engi