l run log: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260515-001-r4s0/formal/last_run-32594.log\\nPer-check logs in: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260515-001-r4s0/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-32594/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): `bench` currently latches raw `instr[19:15]`/`instr[24:20]` reads into RVFI, so relaxing load-use stalls from field matches to true EX-time consumers needs architectural-source canonicalization or earlier forwarding, not a late MEM/WB repair, or riscv-formal `reg` will catch stale aliased `rs*` values.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Load-to-Store Data Bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The recent `bench` history says two things clearly: frontend speculation is\\ntoxic on this core's Fmax, while backend-only store-side changes can pay.\\nThe broad stage-accurate load-use idea also already taught the failure mode:\\nrelaxing hazards is only safe if the architectural source value is still\\ncanonical at retirement.\\n\\nA narrower version of that idea still looks promising. Today `hazard_unit`\\ntreats a younger STORE's `rs2` the same as an EX-time operand, so a\\nload-then-store-data dependency always burns a bubble even though the store\\nvalue is only needed when MEM drives `dmem_wdata`. CoreMark's list, matrix,\\nand state kernels are store-heavy enough that trimming this one precise stall\\nclass could improve IPC without touching the fetch path or hot ALU cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The recent `bench` history says two things clearly: frontend speculation is\\ntoxic on this core's Fmax, while backend-only store-side changes can pay.\\nThe broad stage-accurate load-use idea also already taught the failure mode:\\nrelaxing hazards is only safe if the architectural source value is still\\ncanonical at retirement.\\n\\nA narrower version of that idea still looks promising. Today `hazard_unit`\\ntreats a younger STORE's `rs2` the same as an EX-time operand, so a\\nload-then-store-data dependency always burns a bubble even though the store\\nvalue is only needed when MEM drives `dmem_wdata`. CoreMark's list, matrix,\\nand state kernels are store-heavy enough that trimming this one precise stall\\nclass could improve IPC without touching the fetch path or hot ALU cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a dedicated load-to-store-data bypass instead of another broad hazard\\nrewrite. Keep the existing conservative load-use interlock for all normal\\nEX-time consumers, but special-case the one safe case: if the older\\ninstruction is a load and the younger instruction is a STORE whose only\\ndependency is `rs2`, let the STORE advance without the bubble.\\n\\nFeed WB-stage writeback data into `mem_stage` and, for STOREs, choose the\\nfinal store payload from that WB value when `wb_w_en && wb_w_addr ==\\nex_mem.rs2_addr`. Use that same late-selected value for physical\\n`dmem_wdata`, for any deferred store-slot capture, and for the retiring\\nstore's RVFI-visible `rs2_rdata` / `mem_wdata` so formal and cosim observe\\nthe true architectural source operand. Keep stalls for load-to-store-address\\n(`rs1`) hazards and for every other load-use case.\\n\\nThis stays on the backend side of the machine, adds only a small compare/mux\\nin MEM, and directly converts a common load-update-store pattern from a fixed\\nbubble into overlap.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Refine load-use detection so a younger STORE no longer stalls when only its `rs2` data depends on the older load; keep `rs1` and all other consumer cases conservative.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add WB-to-MEM store-data bypass selection, and use the late-selected store value for `dmem_wdata`, deferred-store-slot capture, and retiring RVFI source/memory fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the IF/ID STORE classification needed by hazard logic and pass WB-stage writeback signals into MEM for the new store-data bypass.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add a pipeline regression covering `LW` followed immediately by dependent `SW`, including stalled-store-slot behavior and RVFI `rs2_rdata`/`mem_wdata` checks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Refine load-use detection so a younger STORE no longer stalls when only its `rs2` data depends on the older load; keep `rs1` and all other consumer cases conservative.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add WB-to-MEM store-data bypass selection, and use the late-selected store value for `dmem_wdata`, deferred-store-slot capture, and retiring RVFI source/memory fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the IF/ID STORE classification needed by hazard logic and pass WB-stage writeback signals into MEM for the new store-data bypass.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add a pipeline regression covering `LW` followed immediately by dependent `SW`, including stalled-store-slot behavior and RVFI `rs2_rdata`/`mem_wdata` checks.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\n:01 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:06  Writing trace to Verilog testbench: engine_0/trace_tb.v\\nSBY 11:58:01 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:06  Writing trace to constraints file: engine_0/trace.smtc\\nSBY 11:58:01 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:06  Writing trace to Yosys witness file: engine_0/trace.yw\\nSBY 11:58:01 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:07  Status: failed\\nSBY 11:58:01 