{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778871066848,"sessionID":"ses_1d3082364ffeSUTMfN6QLwgtRN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_hQAfIJSXYbGnysfiCGWiMqzc","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r9s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r9s0\n+title: \"Shared RV32M Multiply Datapath\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The current `bench` core already found its two big wins by removing costly\n+  control and divider work from the always-hot paths, while later fetch-side\n+  predictors and MEM-side queue refinements mostly regressed or failed\n+  placement. One localized datapath cost still stands out in `rtl/alu.sv`:\n+  the real build computes three separate 64-bit products (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`,\n+  `mul_su`) just to serve the four MUL-family instructions.\n+\n+  CoreMark does use multiply in hot code, so turning MUL into a multi-cycle\n+  path would likely hurt IPC. A better micro-opt is to keep one-cycle RV32M\n+  latency but remove redundant multiplier hardware and mux fanout from the EX\n+  datapath.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Replace the three parallel MUL-family product trees in `rtl/alu.sv` with a\n+  single shared sign-configurable multiplier. Use one 33x33 extended product\n+  whose extra sign bits are selected from the opcode so the same result can\n+  produce `MUL`, `MULH`, `MULHU`, and `MULHSU`: low 32 bits for `MUL`, high\n+  32 bits for the three high-half variants, with the existing ALTOPS branch\n+  left unchanged.\n+\n+  This keeps the pipeline, hazards, RVFI retirement behavior, and CoreMark\n+  cycle count unchanged while reducing redundant DSP/LUT usage and routing\n+  pressure inside the hot EX datapath. Relative to prior failed micro-opts,\n+  it does not add frontend prediction, store-slot control, or late-source\n+  bookkeeping; it is a narrow Fmax-focused cleanup with lower correctness\n+  surface area than another hazard relaxation.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n+    description: \"Replace the current `mul_ss`/`mul_uu`/`mul_su` triple-product implementation with one shared sign-configurable multiplier and select the correct low or high half per RV32M op while preserving the existing ALTOPS cases.\"\n+  - file: test/test_alu.py\n+    description: \"Strengthen MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU coverage with signedness-focused edge vectors so the shared multiplier rewrite is pinned to exact RV32M bit results.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r9s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r9s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r9s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r9s0.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r9s0\n+title: \"Shared RV32M Multiply Datapath\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The current `bench` core already found its two big wins by removing costly\n+  control and divider work from the always-hot paths, while later fetch-side\n+  predictors and MEM-side queue refinements mostly regressed or failed\n+  placement. One localized datapath cost still stands out in `rtl/alu.sv`:\n+  the real build computes three separate 64-bit products (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`,\n+  `mul_su`) just to serve the four MUL-family instructions.\n+\n+  CoreMark does use multiply in hot code, so turning MUL into a multi-cycle\n+  path would likely hurt IPC. A better micro-op