{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778873055126,"sessionID":"ses_1d2ee2901fferLJYlGEB1Ino4t","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"call_W8Vdcu56l1acvFcdqgYJdGeL","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Inspect structural levers","prompt":"You are exploring /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3 for cores/bench only. Read cores/bench/rtl/*.sv, cores/bench/LESSONS.md, and cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl structural entries. Do not write code. Identify the 2 most promising remaining structural hypotheses to improve fitness (CoreMark iter/cycle * Fmax) on this RV32IM Tang Nano 20K core, explicitly excluding ideas already tried in these categories: iterative divider offload, one-deep store slot, load replay buffer, two-entry store queue, late store-data lane / load->store rs2 bypass, frontend fetch queue, and all branch predictors. For each candidate, explain why it could improve IPC or Fmax, what files it would likely touch, and the main risk. Return a concise recommendation with the single best candidate.","subagent_type":"explore","task_id":"","command":""},"output":"task_id: ses_1d2e8d263ffeRkVM1od5VA05iL (for resuming to continue this task if needed)\n\n<task_result>\nMost promising remaining structural hypotheses for `cores/bench`:\n\n1. Cold high-half multiply offload (`MULH/MULHU/MULHSU` only)\n- Why it could help: `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv` currently builds three 64-bit products every cycle (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`, `mul_su`) on the always-hot EX path. Divider offload already proved this core benefits when rare RV32M work leaves EX. Offloading just the cold high-half multiply ops while keeping plain `MUL` single-cycle preserves the likely hot CoreMark case but could materially shorten EX routing/DSP pressure and raise Fmax.\n- Likely files:\n  - `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv`\n  - `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv`\n  - `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv`\n  - `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv`\n  - likely a new mul helper alongside `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv`\n- Main risk: if synthesis already maps the existing multiply logic efficiently, the control/stall machinery could cost more than it saves; unexpected `MULH*` frequency would hurt IPC.\n\n2. Decode-aware load-use interlock with architectural source-use bits, excluding load->store-rs2 bypass\n- Why it could help: `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv` still stalls on raw `if_id` field matches, not true EX consumers. That means false bubbles remain for instructions whose rs fields are immediate bits or unused in EX. This is still a real IPC lever, and unlike the excluded late store-data ideas, it can avoid touching the MEM store-data path.\n- Likely files:\n  - `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/decoder.sv`\n  - `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv`\n  - `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv`\n  - `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv`\n  - `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/core.sv`\n- Main risk: gain may be modest, and RVFI correctness is delicate because lessons already showed source canonicalization must be explicit, not repaired late.\n\nRecommendation\n- Best single next candidate: cold high-half multiply offload.\n- Reason: it targets the most obvious remaining always-hot structural cost in the current RTL without re-entering the fetch-path or MEM-buffering areas that already regressed.\n</task_result>","metadata":{"sessionId":"ses_1d2e8d263ffeRkVM1od5VA05iL","model":{"modelID":"gpt-5.4","providerID":"openai"},"truncated":false},"title":"Inspect structural levers","time":{"start":1778872954274,"end":1778873055124}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0c59c43dd748ab77016a077277e404819aa13b6623592785f7"}},"id":"prt_e2d1724b7001LW6t4V2DtoeHLA","sessionID":"ses_1d2ee2901fferLJYlGEB1Ino4t","messageID":"msg_e2d1705a9001WdRazfq5pOLpJX"}}
