ve even though the last attempt broke formal. The\\nfailure was a correctness-accounting problem around canonical RVFI source data,\\nnot evidence that the workload opportunity is gone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\\nthat leave the fragile fetch path alone: first moving DIV/REM off the hot EX\\ncone, then letting stalled normal-RAM stores retire into a one-deep deferred\\nslot. A remaining bubble source is the textbook load-use stall on a younger\\nSTORE whose dependency is only `rs2` store data, not the EX-time address\\noperand.\\n\\nThat lever is still attractive even though the last attempt broke formal. The\\nfailure was a correctness-accounting problem around canonical RVFI source data,\\nnot evidence that the workload opportunity is gone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Retiming STORE data to MEM is still the most promising structural next step,\\nbut it needs an explicit architectural-source shadow instead of a late ad hoc\\nrepair. Relax the load-use interlock only for the safe case where an older\\nLOAD feeds a younger STORE's `rs2` while `rs1` remains independent. Carry\\nstore-data source metadata through EX/MEM, then in `mem_stage` select the\\nfinal store payload from the older writeback/load result when needed.\\n\\nUse that same canonical late-selected value everywhere the STORE becomes\\narchitecturally visible: physical `dmem_wdata`, deferred-store-slot capture,\\nMEM...\n  Line 19: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Slot Backedge Branch Fold Buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Every predictor attempt on `bench` that touched the frontend `pc -> next_pc`\\ncone regressed: ID-stage BTFNT added decode and target-generation delay, and\\neven tiny taken-target caches still paid too much tag-compare and target-mux\\ncost in IF. But CoreMark remains dominated by tight taken loop backedges, so\\nalways waiting for EX to redirect still 