{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778876120099,"sessionID":"ses_1d2b937bbffejBUFyDIrJ1Mo8g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_lBlI5PSE32L0bGs2KEyQ6Tzx","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"IF/ID|fetch buffer|pipeline|6-stage|split decode|regfile|operand|retim|retime|stage","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments","include":"log.jsonl"},"output":"Found 30 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Iterative Cold Divider Off EX Path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The frozen bench core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\ncombinational ALU path as ADD, address generation, branch support, and\\nMUL. On a Tang Nano 20K, a full 32-bit divider is a strong candidate for\\nthe universal EX-stage critical path, yet CoreMark's timed loop is mostly\\nmultiply, load/store, and branch traffic and should pay little or no steady\\nstate penalty from slower divide instructions.\\n\\nThis makes divider extraction the highest-leverage micro-opt still missing\\nfrom the frozen bench fixture: it targets Fmax directly while preserving the\\nhot one-cycle behavior for the instructions that dominate CoreMark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The frozen bench core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\ncombinational ALU path as ADD, address generation, branch support, and\\nMUL. On a Tang Nano 20K, a full 32-bit divider is a strong candidate for\\nthe universal EX-stage critical path, yet CoreMark's timed loop is mostly\\nmultiply, load/store, and branch traffic and should pay little or no steady\\nstate penalty from slower divide instructions.\\n\\nThis makes divider extraction the highest-leverage micro-opt still missing\\nfrom the frozen bench fixture: it targets Fmax directly while preserving the\\nhot one-cycle behavior for the instructions that dominate CoreMark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases in `rtl/alu.sv`\\nwith a small iterative divider/remainder unit that is entered only for\\ndivide-class ALU ops. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift/compare, address generation,\\nbranches, jumps, loads/stores, and MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing\\nsingle-cycle datapath.\\n\\nIn EX, detect divide-class operations, latch their operands and control,\\nstall IF and ID while the divider runs, and emit exactly one EX/MEM result\\nwhen the quotient or remainder is ready. Preserve RV32IM edge cases\\nprecisely: divide-...\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Port v1-proven iterative divider into EX\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench baseline still carries real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operators\\ninside the always-live combinational ALU. Archived bench runs on this same\\nbaseline show that moving divider-class ops off the fast EX path was the\\nbiggest structural win, because CoreMark almost never uses integer division\\nwhile the FPGA still pays its timing and LUT cost on every cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench baseline still carries real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operators\\ninside the always-live combinational ALU. Archived bench runs on this same\\nbaseline show that moving divider-class ops off the fast EX path was the\\nbiggest structural win, because CoreMark almost never uses integer division\\nwhile the FPGA still pays its timing and LUT cost on every cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Reuse the already-proven iterative divider structure from cores/v1 and\\nintegrate it only for DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU, leaving the existing single-cycle\\nRV32I and MUL-family datapaths intact. alu.sv stops synthesizing real\\ndivider hardware; ex_stage.sv launches the divider for divide-class ops,\\nholds the in-flight EX payload until completion, and commits exactly one\\nEX/MEM result with the original RVFI metadata and RV32M corner-case behavior\\npreserved. This is the lowest-risk structural path to a large Fmax gain on\\nthe current baseline because it attacks the known worst cone without adding\\nfetch-side control, store reordering, or multiply bubbles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 20, \"confidence\": \"high\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the v1-proven iterative RV32 divider/remainder unit with ALTOPS compatibility and exact divide-by-zero / INT_MIN-overflow semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU hardware from the normal ALU fast path while keeping existing RV32I and MUL-f...\n  Line 4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Static BTFNT branch predictor with ID redirect\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench core resolves every branch and jump in EX and flushes on every taken redirect. CoreMark is loop-heavy, so repeatedly paying that taken-branch penalty burns IPC. LUT headroom is tight, which makes a low-state predictor more attractive than a larger dynamic structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench core resolves every branch and jump in EX and flushes on every taken redirect. CoreMark is loop-heavy, so repeatedly paying that taken-branch penalty burns IPC. LUT headroom is tight, which makes a low-state predictor more attractive than a larger dynamic structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a decode-stage backward-taken/forward-not-taken predictor for conditional branches and always predict JAL taken, driving a speculative next PC from ID into IF. EX remains the architectural source of truth for branch direction and target, but only flushes and recovers on a mispredict. This should remove most loop-backedge and JAL bubbles in CoreMark while staying much lighter than a tagged BTB/BHT, limiting Fmax and LUT risk.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire an ID-stage prediction path into IF and route EX-stage mispredict recovery into hazard control.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Update the PC from a predicted-taken branch or JAL in ID while keeping EX recovery as the highest-priority redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute a static backward-taken/forward-not-taken decision and early BRANCH/JAL target for speculative fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recompute the real branch or jump outcome and request a flush only when the static prediction was wrong.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Turn redirect flushing into mispredict-only recovery so correct ...\n  Line 5: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"RTL-only hot/cold ALU opcode split\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best bench result already removed DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU from the\\nhot one-cycle EX path, but `rtl/alu.sv` still uses a dense ALU-op encoding\\nand one broad result case covering common RV32I work, multiply results, and\\ndummy divide-class outputs. That leaves the always-hot ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path sharing decode and mux structure with cold M-extension cases.\\n\\nA sibling-core experiment on `cores/v1` showed that this specific RTL-only\\nreshaping can improve fitness, while more aggressive multiplier rewrites and\\nsource-qualified hazard logic either regressed or broke. That makes the ALU\\nmux shape the best-supported remaining `micro_opt` angle for the frozen\\nbench fixture.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best bench result already removed DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU from the\\nhot one-cycle EX path, but `rtl/alu.sv` still uses a dense ALU-op encoding\\nand one broad result case covering common RV32I work, multiply results, and\\ndummy divide-class outputs. That leaves the always-hot ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path sharing decode and mux structure with cold M-extension cases.\\n\\nA sibling-core experiment on `cores/v1` showed that this specific RTL-only\\nreshaping can improve fitness, while more aggressive multiplier rewrites and\\nsource-qualified hazard logic either regressed or broke. That makes the ALU\\nmux shape the best-supported remaining `micro_opt` angle for the frozen\\nbench fixture.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Re-encode the ALU operation constants so bit 4 cleanly marks RV32M\\noperations, with MUL/MULH/MULHSU/MULHU/DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU grouped as a cold\\n`op[4]=1` class while the common RV32I/simple operations stay in the hot\\n`op[4]=0` class. Then refactor `rtl/alu.sv` into two local result muxes:\\na simple-result mux for ADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI and an M-result mux\\nfor multiply plus the existing placeholder divide outputs use...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench core's biggest proven gain came from a backend-only timing\\nchange: moving DIV/REM off the one-cycle EX path. The recent failed branch\\nprediction attempt showed the opposite boundary on this RTL: pulling more\\ndecode/target work into the frontend can erase the IPC gain with Fmax loss.\\n\\nThe next structural hypothesis should therefore stay off the fetch path and\\nattack the other obvious whole-core bubble source. Today any `io_dmemReady=0`\\ncycle on a STORE freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM even though the store address,\\nreplicated data, and byte mask are already known in MEM, and CoreMark does a\\nsteady stream of normal-RAM stores to stack, list, matrix, and state data.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench core's biggest proven gain came from a backend-only timing\\nchange: moving DIV/REM off the one-cycle EX path. The recent failed branch\\nprediction attempt showed the opposite boundary on this RTL: pulling more\\ndecode/target work into the frontend can erase the IPC gain with Fmax loss.\\n\\nThe next structural hypothesis should therefore stay off the fetch path and\\nattack the other obvious whole-core bubble source. Today any `io_dmemReady=0`\\ncycle on a STORE freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM even though the store address,\\nreplicated data, and byte mask are already known in MEM, and CoreMark does a\\nsteady stream of normal-RAM stores to stack, list, matrix, and state data.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot with a direct ready-store fast\\npath. When an aligned STORE to normal RAM `[0x00000000, 0x00100000)` reaches\\nMEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, capture its byte address, replicated write\\ndata, byte mask, and RVFI-visible memory metadata into the slot, retire that\\nstore once, and drain the physical dmem write oldest-first after the stall.\\n\\nKeep the zero-wait STORE path identical to the current d...\n  Line 7: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Registered lookahead I-fetch replay predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench core still turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into a\\nfrontend bubble, even though CoreMark runs under a random-stall fetch model\\nand repeatedly revisits a small hot text footprint. The one local predictor\\nattempt already taught the key negative lesson: pulling branch decode,\\nimmediate reconstruction, and target selection directly onto the current\\nIF/ID path costs too much Fmax on this microarchitecture.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore not another same-cycle\\nbranch-direction table, but a timing-clean replay predictor that hides\\nrandom iStall cycles while leaving the current fetch decision dependent only\\non already-registered metadata.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench core still turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into a\\nfrontend bubble, even though CoreMark runs under a random-stall fetch model\\nand repeatedly revisits a small hot text footprint. The one local predictor\\nattempt already taught the key negative lesson: pulling branch decode,\\nimmediate reconstruction, and target selection directly onto the current\\nIF/ID path costs too much Fmax on this microarchitecture.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore not another same-cycle\\nbranch-direction table, but a timing-clean replay predictor that hides\\nrandom iStall cycles while leaving the current fetch decision dependent only\\non already-registered metadata.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small registered-lookahead instruction replay predictor in IF. On live\\ninstruction fetches, fill a tagged replay table with the fetched word and a\\npredicted next-PC for that fetch PC. In parallel, generate and register the\\nreplay candidate for the PC likely to be needed on the next cycle. When the\\nexternal imem bus stalls, IF can consume the already-registered candidate\\ninstead of injecting a bubble, and can keep advancing throu...\n  Line 8: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Prune EX/MEM payload and narrow store-slot control\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` result already found the two large levers that pay\\non this RTL: keep divide/remainder off the hot EX path, and hide some\\nstore-only dmem stalls with the one-deep retirement slot. The failed branch\\nand replay experiments showed the opposite boundary: adding more fetch-side\\ncontrol logic costs too much Fmax on this core.\\n\\nThat leaves the MEM-side control cone as the best micro-opt target. The\\ncurrent store-slot path still carries redundant EX/MEM metadata and uses\\nalways-live wide compares (`instr == 32'h0010_0073`, `addr < 32'h0010_0000`)\\nto drive global stall decisions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` result already found the two large levers that pay\\non this RTL: keep divide/remainder off the hot EX path, and hide some\\nstore-only dmem stalls with the one-deep retirement slot. The failed branch\\nand replay experiments showed the opposite boundary: adding more fetch-side\\ncontrol logic costs too much Fmax on this core.\\n\\nThat leaves the MEM-side control cone as the best micro-opt target. The\\ncurrent store-slot path still carries redundant EX/MEM metadata and uses\\nalways-live wide compares (`instr == 32'h0010_0073`, `addr < 32'h0010_0000`)\\nto drive global stall decisions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Collapse backend metadata and replace wide store-slot control compares with\\nnarrower registered signals. In `ex_mem_t`, remove fields that do not add\\narchitectural information on this core: `write_data` duplicates the already\\nforwarded `rs2_val`, and `branch_taken` / `branch_target` are registered but\\nnever consumed after EX. Replace the top-level EBREAK fence compare with a\\none-bit `is_ebreak` marker captured in EX/MEM, and rewrite the normal-RAM\\nstore-slot range check as an explicit high-bit-zero decode instead of a\\n32-bit less-than comparator.\\n\\nThis keeps CoreMark IPC, RV...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Tiny MEM-stage load replay buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The two successful structural changes on `bench` both stayed off the fetch\\nand hot EX paths: iterative DIV/REM and the one-deep store retirement slot.\\nThe remaining backend stall source is load-side dmem backpressure. Today a\\nnormal-RAM load freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM whenever `io_dmemReady=0`, even\\nif the same aligned word was returned recently and CoreMark's 6 KB working\\nset is revisiting it.\\n\\nThat makes load-side stall hiding the cleanest remaining structural lever:\\nit attacks iterations/cycle without pulling new decode or prediction logic\\nonto the frontend path that already regressed on prior bench attempts.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two successful structural changes on `bench` both stayed off the fetch\\nand hot EX paths: iterative DIV/REM and the one-deep store retirement slot.\\nThe remaining backend stall source is load-side dmem backpressure. Today a\\nnormal-RAM load freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM whenever `io_dmemReady=0`, even\\nif the same aligned word was returned recently and CoreMark's 6 KB working\\nset is revisiting it.\\n\\nThat makes load-side stall hiding the cleanest remaining structural lever:\\nit attacks iterations/cycle without pulling new decode or prediction logic\\nonto the frontend path that already regressed on prior bench attempts.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny normal-RAM load replay structure in MEM, such as a small\\ndirect-mapped raw-word buffer keyed by aligned word address. Live ready\\nloads keep using `io_dmemRData` exactly as they do today and refresh the\\nreplay entry for that word. When `io_dmemReady=0`, a legal aligned\\nnormal-RAM load may complete from the replay buffer on a tag hit: use the\\ncached raw word for sign/zero extension and RVFI `mem_rdata`, suppress the\\nglobal dmem wait for that instruction, and let the pipeline keep retiring in\\norder.\\n\\nReplay must stay conservative. Disable it for...\n  Line 10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny EX-trained taken-target cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The two failed predictor attempts exposed the boundary on this core: bench\\ndoes not tolerate extra decode, immediate-generation, or replay-control work\\non the frontend critical path. But CoreMark is still rich in tight counted\\nloops and unconditional jumps, so always waiting for EX to redirect leaves\\npredictable control-flow bubbles on the table.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is a much smaller one: predict only from\\nalready-resolved history, with a fetch-side structure light enough that the\\nFmax cost stays below the IPC gain.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two failed predictor attempts exposed the boundary on this core: bench\\ndoes not tolerate extra decode, immediate-generation, or replay-control work\\non the frontend critical path. But CoreMark is still rich in tight counted\\nloops and unconditional jumps, so always waiting for EX to redirect leaves\\npredictable control-flow bubbles on the table.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is a much smaller one: predict only from\\nalready-resolved history, with a fetch-side structure light enough that the\\nFmax cost stays below the IPC gain.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small direct-mapped taken-target cache in IF, trained only by\\ncontrol-flow instructions once EX has already resolved their real next PC.\\nOn a tag hit, IF speculatively uses the cached target instead of `pc + 4`;\\non a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is today. This avoids any ID-stage\\nbranch decode, immediate reconstruction, or same-cycle target add.\\n\\nThe predictor should only cache taken conditional branches and unconditional\\njumps, with EX comparing the predicted next PC against the architectural one\\nand asserting recovery only on mismatch. A last-taken policy is a good fit\\nfor CoreMark's loop backedges: it predicts the many taken iterations while\\nmissing only on loop exit, and the hardware cost is just ...\n  Line 11: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Stage-Accurate Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"After divider offload and the one-deep store retirement slot, the cheapest\\nremaining CoreMark IPC loss is likely the still-conservative load-use\\ninterlock. `hazard_unit` compares a load destination against raw IF/ID rs1\\nand rs2 bitfields even when the younger instruction does not actually need\\nthose fields in EX, and it still forces a bubble for STORE rs2 even though\\nstore data is only consumed one stage later in MEM.\\n\\nThat means the core can still lose cycles to false immediate-field matches\\nand to real load-then-store-data dependencies that do not protect any EX\\ncritical operand. This is a narrow backend/control cleanup that attacks IPC\\nwithout adding predictor state, fetch-path muxing, or a new EX datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"After divider offload and the one-deep store retirement slot, the cheapest\\nremaining CoreMark IPC loss is likely the still-conservative load-use\\ninterlock. `hazard_unit` compares a load destination against raw IF/ID rs1\\nand rs2 bitfields even when the younger instruction does not actually need\\nthose fields in EX, and it still forces a bubble for STORE rs2 even though\\nstore data is only consumed one stage later in MEM.\\n\\nThat means the core can still lose cycles to false immediate-field matches\\nand to real load-then-store-data dependencies that do not protect any EX\\ncritical operand. This is a narrow backend/control cleanup that attacks IPC\\nwithout adding predictor state, fetch-path muxing, or a new EX datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make the load-use detector stage-accurate instead of field-accurate.\\nDecode conservative `uses_rs1_ex` / `uses_rs2_ex` bits from the IF/ID\\ninstruction inside `hazard_unit`, and only stall when the older load feeds a\\nvalue that the younger instruction truly needs in EX. Keep unknown or\\nillegal encodings conservative, but stop treating OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR,\\nLUI/AUIP...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Deferred Store Queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\\nthat avoid adding logic to the fetch path: first moving DIV/REM off EX, then\\nbuffering one stalled RAM store. The current one-deep store slot still\\nserializes store bursts behind a single pending write, even though CoreMark\\nissues clustered stack, list, matrix, and state stores under the random\\n`io_dmemReady` stall model.\\n\\nA deeper store queue is the next clean extension of the one change that has\\nalready proven it can raise IPC on this RTL without paying the frontend Fmax\\npenalties seen in prior predictor experiments.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\\nthat avoid adding logic to the fetch path: first moving DIV/REM off EX, then\\nbuffering one stalled RAM store. The current one-deep store slot still\\nserializes store bursts behind a single pending write, even though CoreMark\\nissues clustered stack, list, matrix, and state stores under the random\\n`io_dmemReady` stall model.\\n\\nA deeper store queue is the next clean extension of the one change that has\\nalready proven it can raise IPC on this RTL without paying the frontend Fmax\\npenalties seen in prior predictor experiments.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the current one-deep pending-store slot with a two-entry oldest-first\\ndeferred store queue for aligned normal-RAM stores. When `io_dmemReady` is\\nlow, the oldest queued store keeps draining to the physical bus while a\\nyounger bufferable store can still retire into the second queue entry instead\\nof freezing the whole pipeline immediately.\\n\\nKeep the existing zero-wait direct-store fast path when the queue is empty,\\nand preserve architectural ordering by continuing to fence younger loads,\\nnon-bufferable stores, and EBREAK behind any queued store. This should hide\\nmore of CoreMark's store-s...\n  Line 13: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Slot EX-Trained Loop Backedge Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The prior predictor regressions define a narrow safe zone on `bench`: same-cycle\\ndecode and target generation in ID cost too much Fmax, and even a small\\nmulti-entry fetch-side target cache adds more compare and mux work to the\\n`pc -> next_pc` cone than CoreMark can repay. But always waiting for EX to\\nredirect still burns bubbles on the tight backward loop branches that dominate\\nCoreMark's hot inner loops.\\n\\nThe best remaining predictor angle is therefore to predict less often with far\\nless hardware: specialize only for the hottest backward branch that has already\\nbeen proven taken, and keep all target information trained from EX rather than\\nrecomputed on the fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The prior predictor regressions define a narrow safe zone on `bench`: same-cycle\\ndecode and target generation in ID cost too much Fmax, and even a small\\nmulti-entry fetch-side target cache adds more compare and mux work to the\\n`pc -> next_pc` cone than CoreMark can repay. But always waiting for EX to\\nredirect still burns bubbles on the tight backward loop branches that dominate\\nCoreMark's hot inner loops.\\n\\nThe best remaining predictor angle is therefore to predict less often with far\\nless hardware: specialize only for the hottest backward branch that has already\\nbeen proven taken, and keep all target information trained from EX rather than\\nrecomputed on the fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry EX-trained loop-backedge predictor. When EX resolves a taken\\nbackward conditional branch, capture just that branch PC and its resolved target\\ninto a registered slot. On a later fetch of the same PC, IF speculates to the\\nstored target instead of `pc + 4`; on a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is\\ntoday.\\n\\nThis is intentionally smaller than both failed bench predictor families. It\\nremoves ID-stage immediate reconstruct...\n  Line 14: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Load-to-Store Data Bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The recent `bench` history says two things clearly: frontend speculation is\\ntoxic on this core's Fmax, while backend-only store-side changes can pay.\\nThe broad stage-accurate load-use idea also already taught the failure mode:\\nrelaxing hazards is only safe if the architectural source value is still\\ncanonical at retirement.\\n\\nA narrower version of that idea still looks promising. Today `hazard_unit`\\ntreats a younger STORE's `rs2` the same as an EX-time operand, so a\\nload-then-store-data dependency always burns a bubble even though the store\\nvalue is only needed when MEM drives `dmem_wdata`. CoreMark's list, matrix,\\nand state kernels are store-heavy enough that trimming this one precise stall\\nclass could improve IPC without touching the fetch path or hot ALU cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The recent `bench` history says two things clearly: frontend speculation is\\ntoxic on this core's Fmax, while backend-only store-side changes can pay.\\nThe broad stage-accurate load-use idea also already taught the failure mode:\\nrelaxing hazards is only safe if the architectural source value is still\\ncanonical at retirement.\\n\\nA narrower version of that idea still looks promising. Today `hazard_unit`\\ntreats a younger STORE's `rs2` the same as an EX-time operand, so a\\nload-then-store-data dependency always burns a bubble even though the store\\nvalue is only needed when MEM drives `dmem_wdata`. CoreMark's list, matrix,\\nand state kernels are store-heavy enough that trimming this one precise stall\\nclass could improve IPC without touching the fetch path or hot ALU cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a dedicated load-to-store-data bypass instead of another broad hazard\\nrewrite. Keep the existing conservative load-use interlock for all normal\\nEX-time consumers, but special-case the one safe case: if the older\\ninstruction is a load and the younger instruction is...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Store-Data Forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The proven `bench` wins so far have all stayed off the fragile frontend:\\nmoving DIV/REM off the universal EX path and adding the one-deep deferred\\nstore slot. The failed predictor, load-replay, and deeper-store-queue tries\\ndefine the boundary clearly: extra fetch-path logic and wider MEM-side\\nbuffering cost too much Fmax or placement quality on this FPGA.\\n\\nOne structural bubble source remains in the existing 5-stage pipeline. A\\nload followed immediately by a STORE of that loaded register still pays the\\nfull textbook load-use stall even when the STORE only needs the value as\\nwrite data (`rs2`), not as its address base (`rs1`). That dependency does\\nnot need an EX-stage bubble because store data is not consumed until MEM.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The proven `bench` wins so far have all stayed off the fragile frontend:\\nmoving DIV/REM off the universal EX path and adding the one-deep deferred\\nstore slot. The failed predictor, load-replay, and deeper-store-queue tries\\ndefine the boundary clearly: extra fetch-path logic and wider MEM-side\\nbuffering cost too much Fmax or placement quality on this FPGA.\\n\\nOne structural bubble source remains in the existing 5-stage pipeline. A\\nload followed immediately by a STORE of that loaded register still pays the\\nfull textbook load-use stall even when the STORE only needs the value as\\nwrite data (`rs2`), not as its address base (`rs1`). That dependency does\\nnot need an EX-stage bubble because store data is not consumed until MEM.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move STORE data consumption to the stage where the core actually uses it.\\nKeep STORE address generation in EX exactly as it is today, but stop\\ntreating a load->STORE `rs2` match as an EX-time hazard. Instead, let the\\nSTORE advance, carry its `rs2` register number and provisional value through\\nEX/MEM, and in `mem_stage` select the final store data...\n  Line 16: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Slot EX-Trained Branch Folding Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor experiments on `bench` have all failed for the same reason:\\nthey improved branch behavior only by adding work to the frontend `pc ->\\nnext_pc` cone. ID-stage BTFNT hurt Fmax by pulling decode and target\\ngeneration forward, and even tiny fetch-side target caches still cost too\\nmuch in tag-compare and target-mux depth for this FPGA.\\n\\nCoreMark is still rich in hot taken loop backedges, though, so there is\\nlikely one remaining predictor shape worth trying: recover a taken-branch\\ncycle without touching `io_imemAddr` generation itself.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor experiments on `bench` have all failed for the same reason:\\nthey improved branch behavior only by adding work to the frontend `pc ->\\nnext_pc` cone. ID-stage BTFNT hurt Fmax by pulling decode and target\\ngeneration forward, and even tiny fetch-side target caches still cost too\\nmuch in tag-compare and target-mux depth for this FPGA.\\n\\nCoreMark is still rich in hot taken loop backedges, though, so there is\\nlikely one remaining predictor shape worth trying: recover a taken-branch\\ncycle without touching `io_imemAddr` generation itself.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry EX-trained branch-folding slot for taken backward branches.\\nWhen EX resolves a taken backedge, record the branch PC and target PC, then\\nfill the slot's cached target instruction the next time that target is fetched\\narchitecturally. On a later hit for the same branch PC, keep the normal\\nsequential PC path, but mark the branch as predicted-taken and inject the\\ncached target instruction/PC into IF->ID on the following cycle instead of the\\nwrong-path sequential word.\\n\\nEX remains the architectural source of truth. A correctly folded branch would\\nrepair the fetch PC to `target + 4` while preserving the injected target in\\nID, and a loop-exit miss would redirect back to `pc ...\n  Line 17: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"RVFI-Safe Load-to-Store Rs2 Bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining bench lessons point at one backend IPC loss that is still both\\nplausible and narrow: a younger STORE always bubbles behind an older LOAD\\nwhen the dependency is only on STORE data (`rs2`), even though the store does\\nnot actually consume that value until MEM. CoreMark is store-heavy enough\\nthat this specific bubble class is worth attacking, and prior results show\\nthe core tolerates backend-only store-side changes much better than any\\nfrontend speculation.\\n\\nThe last two attempts at this idea broke `rvfi_reg_check`, which is the key\\nlesson to preserve here: the win is not in a broader hazard relaxation, but\\nin making the store's late data path and its architectural `rs2_rdata`\\ncanonicalize from the same source.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining bench lessons point at one backend IPC loss that is still both\\nplausible and narrow: a younger STORE always bubbles behind an older LOAD\\nwhen the dependency is only on STORE data (`rs2`), even though the store does\\nnot actually consume that value until MEM. CoreMark is store-heavy enough\\nthat this specific bubble class is worth attacking, and prior results show\\nthe core tolerates backend-only store-side changes much better than any\\nfrontend speculation.\\n\\nThe last two attempts at this idea broke `rvfi_reg_check`, which is the key\\nlesson to preserve here: the win is not in a broader hazard relaxation, but\\nin making the store's late data path and its architectural `rs2_rdata`\\ncanonicalize from the same source.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Relax the load-use interlock only for the single safe case where an older\\nLOAD's `rd` matches a younger STORE's `rs2` but not its address-base `rs1`.\\nKeep every other load-use case unchanged, so the fetch path, EX operand\\ntiming, and general hazard policy stay exactly as they are today.\\n\\nTo make that safe, move the dependent STORE's fina...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"RVFI-Safe Late Store-Data Bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\\nthat leave the fragile fetch path alone: first moving DIV/REM off the hot EX\\ncone, then letting stalled normal-RAM stores retire into a one-deep deferred\\nslot. A remaining bubble source is the textbook load-use stall on a younger\\nSTORE whose dependency is only `rs2` store data, not the EX-time address\\noperand.\\n\\nThat lever is still attractive even though the last attempt broke formal. The\\nfailure was a correctness-accounting problem around canonical RVFI source data,\\nnot evidence that the workload opportunity is gone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\\nthat leave the fragile fetch path alone: first moving DIV/REM off the hot EX\\ncone, then letting stalled normal-RAM stores retire into a one-deep deferred\\nslot. A remaining bubble source is the textbook load-use stall on a younger\\nSTORE whose dependency is only `rs2` store data, not the EX-time address\\noperand.\\n\\nThat lever is still attractive even though the last attempt broke formal. The\\nfailure was a correctness-accounting problem around canonical RVFI source data,\\nnot evidence that the workload opportunity is gone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Retiming STORE data to MEM is still the most promising structural next step,\\nbut it needs an explicit architectural-source shadow instead of a late ad hoc\\nrepair. Relax the load-use interlock only for the safe case where an older\\nLOAD feeds a younger STORE's `rs2` while `rs1` remains independent. Carry\\nstore-data source metadata through EX/MEM, then in `mem_stage` select the\\nfinal store payload from the older writeback/load result when needed.\\n\\nUse that same canonical late-selected value everywhere the STORE becomes\\narchitecturally visible: physical `dmem_wdata`, deferred-store-slot capture,\\nMEM...\n  Line 19: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Slot Backedge Branch Fold Buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Every predictor attempt on `bench` that touched the frontend `pc -> next_pc`\\ncone regressed: ID-stage BTFNT added decode and target-generation delay, and\\neven tiny taken-target caches still paid too much tag-compare and target-mux\\ncost in IF. But CoreMark remains dominated by tight taken loop backedges, so\\nalways waiting for EX to redirect still leaves repeatable control bubbles on\\nthe table.\\n\\nThe best remaining predictor angle is therefore one that predicts only the\\nhottest already-seen backedge while leaving `io_imemAddr` generation on its\\ncurrent simple sequential path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every predictor attempt on `bench` that touched the frontend `pc -> next_pc`\\ncone regressed: ID-stage BTFNT added decode and target-generation delay, and\\neven tiny taken-target caches still paid too much tag-compare and target-mux\\ncost in IF. But CoreMark remains dominated by tight taken loop backedges, so\\nalways waiting for EX to redirect still leaves repeatable control bubbles on\\nthe table.\\n\\nThe best remaining predictor angle is therefore one that predicts only the\\nhottest already-seen backedge while leaving `io_imemAddr` generation on its\\ncurrent simple sequential path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry EX-trained branch-fold buffer for taken backward conditional\\nbranches. When EX resolves a taken backedge, record the branch PC and target\\nPC, then capture the target instruction the next time that target is fetched\\narchitecturally. On a later hit for the same branch PC, keep the normal\\nsequential fetch address path, but tag the branch as predicted-taken and\\ninject the cached target instruction/PC into IF->ID on the following cycle\\ninstead of the wrong-path fallthrough word.\\n\\nEX remains the architectural source of truth. A correct prediction repairs\\nthe fetch PC to `target + 4` after the folded target has already ...\n  Line 23: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Zero-Bubble Store Slot Refill\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` core already proved that deferred normal-RAM stores\\nare a winning backend lever, but the one-deep slot still loses a bubble\\nwhenever a pending store finally drains and the next instruction in MEM is\\nanother bufferable RAM store. Hazard control fences that younger store until\\nthe slot bit clears, so bursty CoreMark store traffic still pays a handoff\\npenalty even though the core already knows both stores' addr/data/mask and\\nonly needs to preserve oldest-first external visibility.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` core already proved that deferred normal-RAM stores\\nare a winning backend lever, but the one-deep slot still loses a bubble\\nwhenever a pending store finally drains and the next instruction in MEM is\\nanother bufferable RAM store. Hazard control fences that younger store until\\nthe slot bit clears, so bursty CoreMark store traffic still pays a handoff\\npenalty even though the core already knows both stores' addr/data/mask and\\nonly needs to preserve oldest-first external visibility.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Teach the existing single deferred-store slot to drain and refill in the same\\ncycle instead of growing it into a true two-entry queue. When\\n`store_slot_valid` is set and `io_dmemReady` returns, `mem_stage` should\\ndrive the older deferred store to the bus and, if the current EX/MEM\\ninstruction is another aligned normal-RAM store, immediately capture that\\nyounger store into the same slot while retiring it once through RVFI.\\n\\n`hazard_unit` would then keep fencing loads, MMIO/non-bufferable stores, and\\n`EBREAK` behind a pending slot, but allow exactly one younger bufferable RAM\\nstore through on the drain cycle for a refill. This keeps the proven\\nbackend-only store-buffering strategy, avoids the placement/routing cost that\\nkilled the real two-entry queue, and should recover part of the st...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Canonical Late Store-Data Lane\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The proven `bench` wins have come from backend-only structural changes that\\nimprove IPC or Fmax without touching the fragile fetch path. The remaining\\nbubble that still looks worth chasing is the forced load-use stall when a\\nyounger STORE depends on an older LOAD only for `rs2` write data: the store\\naddress is ready in EX, but the current pipeline still treats store data as\\nan EX-time operand and burns a full bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The proven `bench` wins have come from backend-only structural changes that\\nimprove IPC or Fmax without touching the fragile fetch path. The remaining\\nbubble that still looks worth chasing is the forced load-use stall when a\\nyounger STORE depends on an older LOAD only for `rs2` write data: the store\\naddress is ready in EX, but the current pipeline still treats store data as\\nan EX-time operand and burns a full bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Retiming STORE data to the stage where it becomes architecturally visible is\\nstill the best structural next step, but it has to be implemented as a\\ncoherent data lane rather than a late one-off bypass. Add explicit metadata\\nfor when store data may arrive from WB and carry it through EX/MEM; relax the\\nload-use interlock only for the safe case where a younger STORE's `rs2`\\ndepends on an older LOAD while `rs1` remains independent. In MEM, choose one\\ncanonical final store-data value and use that same value for physical\\n`dmem_wdata`, deferred-store-slot capture, MEM/WB `rs2_val`, and RVFI\\n`rs2_rdata` / `mem_wdata`. This keeps the fetch path unchanged, preserves the\\nexisting zero-wait store fast path, and directly targets a real CoreMark\\nbubble source that prior attempts exposed but did not yet implement\\ncorrectly.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add late-store-dat...\n  Line 25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"One-Entry Short-Loop Stream Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Every prior `bench` predictor that touched the live fetch `pc -> next_pc`\\ndecision regressed, which says the remaining predictor win cannot be another\\nper-fetch target compare or ID-stage redirect. CoreMark is still dominated by\\ntiny hot counted loops under random `io_imemReady` stalls, so the best\\nremaining opportunity is to amortize predictor cost across several loop-body\\ninstructions instead of paying it every taken branch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every prior `bench` predictor that touched the live fetch `pc -> next_pc`\\ndecision regressed, which says the remaining predictor win cannot be another\\nper-fetch target compare or ID-stage redirect. CoreMark is still dominated by\\ntiny hot counted loops under random `io_imemReady` stalls, so the best\\nremaining opportunity is to amortize predictor cost across several loop-body\\ninstructions instead of paying it every taken branch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry EX-trained short-loop stream buffer for small taken backward\\nconditional branches. When EX resolves a taken backedge whose target-to-branch\\nspan is small (for example `<= 4` instructions), capture the loop start PC,\\nbranch PC, and the sequential instructions in that window into a registered\\nbuffer. On a later hit at the loop entry, IF enters a local stream mode and\\nsources the buffered instructions for the predicted-taken iterations instead of\\nre-fetching them from imem one by one.\\n\\nThis keeps EX as the architectural source of truth and avoids the proven bad\\npath of same-cycle frontend target generation. Compared with the earlier\\nsingle-branch predictors, it can hide both the taken-backedge bubble and\\nrandom imem stall bubbles across the whole buffered loop body, so one entry\\nmay repay its control cost better than another fetch-path BTB or fold slot.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": ...\n  Line 26: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Shared RV32M Multiply Datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current `bench` core already found its two big wins by removing costly\\ncontrol and divider work from the always-hot paths, while later fetch-side\\npredictors and MEM-side queue refinements mostly regressed or failed\\nplacement. One localized datapath cost still stands out in `rtl/alu.sv`:\\nthe real build computes three separate 64-bit products (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`,\\n`mul_su`) just to serve the four MUL-family instructions.\\n\\nCoreMark does use multiply in hot code, so turning MUL into a multi-cycle\\npath would likely hurt IPC. A better micro-opt is to keep one-cycle RV32M\\nlatency but remove redundant multiplier hardware and mux fanout from the EX\\ndatapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current `bench` core already found its two big wins by removing costly\\ncontrol and divider work from the always-hot paths, while later fetch-side\\npredictors and MEM-side queue refinements mostly regressed or failed\\nplacement. One localized datapath cost still stands out in `rtl/alu.sv`:\\nthe real build computes three separate 64-bit products (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`,\\n`mul_su`) just to serve the four MUL-family instructions.\\n\\nCoreMark does use multiply in hot code, so turning MUL into a multi-cycle\\npath would likely hurt IPC. A better micro-opt is to keep one-cycle RV32M\\nlatency but remove redundant multiplier hardware and mux fanout from the EX\\ndatapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel MUL-family product trees in `rtl/alu.sv` with a\\nsingle shared sign-configurable multiplier. Use one 33x33 extended product\\nwhose extra sign bits are selected from the opcode so the same result can\\nproduce `MUL`, `MULH`, `MULHU`, and `MULHSU`: low 32 bits for `MUL`, high\\n32 bits for the three high-half variants, with the existing ALTOPS branch\\nleft unchanged.\\n\\nThis keeps the pipeline, hazards, RVFI retirement behavior, and CoreMark\\ncycle count unchange...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Decoupled Sequential Fetch Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current `bench` frontend still couples decode directly to the live imem\\nresponse: `if_stage` emits a combinational IF/ID bundle from the current PC,\\nand `hazard_unit` turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into an immediate bubble.\\nThat leaves two costs on the table under the tournament's random iStall model:\\nisolated fetch stalls always reduce IPC, and the raw imem-to-decode path stays\\nin the timing cone.\\n\\nThe recent negative lessons only rule out fetch-side prediction and replay\\nstructures that add tag compares, target muxing, or decode work onto the live\\n`pc -> next fetch` path. They do not rule out a simpler decoupling structure\\nthat stays purely sequential and flushes on EX redirect.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current `bench` frontend still couples decode directly to the live imem\\nresponse: `if_stage` emits a combinational IF/ID bundle from the current PC,\\nand `hazard_unit` turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into an immediate bubble.\\nThat leaves two costs on the table under the tournament's random iStall model:\\nisolated fetch stalls always reduce IPC, and the raw imem-to-decode path stays\\nin the timing cone.\\n\\nThe recent negative lessons only rule out fetch-side prediction and replay\\nstructures that add tag compares, target muxing, or decode work onto the live\\n`pc -> next fetch` path. They do not rule out a simpler decoupling structure\\nthat stays purely sequential and flushes on EX redirect.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the current combinational IF/ID feed with a tiny sequential fetch\\nbuffer: a registered oldest instruction plus one lookahead slot for the next\\nfallthrough word. `if_stage` keeps prefetching sequential `pc`/`instr` pairs\\nwhenever the buffer has space and `io_imemReady` is high, while ID consumes\\nthe oldest buffered instruction only when it is not stalled.\\n\\nThis changes the frontend structurally w...\n  Line 28: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Decode-Armed Registered Backedge Redirect\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor history on `bench` is unusually clear: every attempt that put\\nbranch decode, tag compare, target muxing, or stream control directly on the\\nlive IF `pc -> next_pc` path regressed badly on Tang Nano 20K timing, even\\nwhen the IPC idea was sound. But the core still pays a repeatable taken-branch\\nbubble on CoreMark's hot loop backedges because conditional branches only\\nbecome known in EX.\\n\\nThe most promising remaining predictor angle is therefore not another faster\\nsame-cycle frontend redirect, but a predictor that uses already-registered\\ninformation to fetch the target one cycle earlier without lengthening the\\ncurrent decode-to-PC critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor history on `bench` is unusually clear: every attempt that put\\nbranch decode, tag compare, target muxing, or stream control directly on the\\nlive IF `pc -> next_pc` path regressed badly on Tang Nano 20K timing, even\\nwhen the IPC idea was sound. But the core still pays a repeatable taken-branch\\nbubble on CoreMark's hot loop backedges because conditional branches only\\nbecome known in EX.\\n\\nThe most promising remaining predictor angle is therefore not another faster\\nsame-cycle frontend redirect, but a predictor that uses already-registered\\ninformation to fetch the target one cycle earlier without lengthening the\\ncurrent decode-to-PC critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry EX-trained backedge predictor that is looked up in decode and\\nconsumed one cycle later from registered state. When EX resolves a taken\\nbackward conditional branch, capture that branch PC and resolved target PC in\\na tiny slot. When the same branch PC appears again in ID, arm a registered\\nprediction for the following cycle instead of redirecting immediately.\\n\\nOn the next cycle, while that branch is in EX, IF uses the registered target\\nPC as `io_ime...\n  Line 29: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Canonical Decode Source Operands\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock keys off raw IF/ID bitfields, not the\\noperands a legal instruction will actually consume. That means single-source\\nor zero-source instructions can still alias an older load's `rd` through\\nunused immediate bits and burn a bubble. The `r4s0` failure showed why a\\ndirect hazard rewrite was unsafe: `bench` currently carries those raw source\\nfields into RVFI, so any safe relaxation has to start by canonicalizing the\\narchitectural source view.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock keys off raw IF/ID bitfields, not the\\noperands a legal instruction will actually consume. That means single-source\\nor zero-source instructions can still alias an older load's `rd` through\\nunused immediate bits and burn a bubble. The `r4s0` failure showed why a\\ndirect hazard rewrite was unsafe: `bench` currently carries those raw source\\nfields into RVFI, so any safe relaxation has to start by canonicalizing the\\narchitectural source view.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Teach decode which source operands are architecturally live for each\\nvalidated opcode/funct form, and canonicalize unused sources to `x0` before\\nthe regfile, hazard unit, and RVFI pipeline state ever see them. In\\npractice, OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR, JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE, and EBREAK would stop\\nadvertising bogus `rs2` or `rs1` aliases, while R-type, BRANCH, STORE, and\\nother true consumers keep their existing source fields.\\n\\nThen drive the existing load-use detector from those canonical source\\naddresses instead of raw `instr[19:15]` / `instr[24:20]`. This stays off the\\nfragile fetch-target and MEM-store paths, removes false dependency bubbles,\\nand makes RVFI source reporting semantically accurate instead of \\\"whatever\\nbits happened to live in the encoding.\\\"\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/de...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Cold High-Half Multiply Offload\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current `bench` ALU still builds three 64-bit multiply products on every\\nEX cycle (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`, and `mul_su`) even though CoreMark's hot path\\nis dominated by plain `MUL`, load/store traffic, and branches rather than the\\nrarer high-half multiply instructions. The successful divider offload showed\\nthat this core benefits when cold RV32M work leaves the always-hot EX path,\\nwhile the later shared-multiplier regression suggests simply reshaping the\\nhot combinational multiplier cone is not enough.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current `bench` ALU still builds three 64-bit multiply products on every\\nEX cycle (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`, and `mul_su`) even though CoreMark's hot path\\nis dominated by plain `MUL`, load/store traffic, and branches rather than the\\nrarer high-half multiply instructions. The successful divider offload showed\\nthat this core benefits when cold RV32M work leaves the always-hot EX path,\\nwhile the later shared-multiplier regression suggests simply reshaping the\\nhot combinational multiplier cone is not enough.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep single-cycle `MUL` on the fast ALU path, but move only\\n`MULH`/`MULHU`/`MULHSU` onto a small multi-cycle side unit launched from EX,\\nusing the same in-order stall-and-retire discipline already proven by the\\ndivider path. In the real build, `rtl/alu.sv` would stop synthesizing the\\nsigned/high-half multiplier trees on the common EX path; EX would instead\\nlatch operands and retirement metadata for high-half multiply ops, stall\\nyounger issue until the result is ready, and then emit exactly one EX/MEM\\nresult with unchanged RVFI behavior.\\n\\nThis is a structural change rather than another local ALU rewrite: it turns a\\ncold RV32M subset into an explicit long-latency execution lane while\\npreserving one-cycle throughput for the hot `MUL` case. That should reduce EX\\ndatapath pressure an...\n  Line 31: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Slot JAL and Backedge Fold Buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Every `bench` predictor that changed the live fetch `pc -> next_pc` path\\nregressed, but the one-slot backedge fold buffer was effectively timing\\nneutral at 447.40 fitness. That points to a narrow remaining predictor\\nwindow: recover taken-control bubbles by injecting already-cached target\\ninstructions, not by adding another frontend target mux, tag lookup, or\\ndecode-armed redirect.\\n\\nCoreMark still spends cycles on both hot loop backedges and repeated direct\\ncalls. The prior fold buffer only covered backward conditional branches, so\\nit left all direct `JAL` bubbles on the table even though `JAL` is perfectly\\npredictable and should not suffer loop-exit misses.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every `bench` predictor that changed the live fetch `pc -> next_pc` path\\nregressed, but the one-slot backedge fold buffer was effectively timing\\nneutral at 447.40 fitness. That points to a narrow remaining predictor\\nwindow: recover taken-control bubbles by injecting already-cached target\\ninstructions, not by adding another frontend target mux, tag lookup, or\\ndecode-armed redirect.\\n\\nCoreMark still spends cycles on both hot loop backedges and repeated direct\\ncalls. The prior fold buffer only covered backward conditional branches, so\\nit left all direct `JAL` bubbles on the table even though `JAL` is perfectly\\npredictable and should not suffer loop-exit misses.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Introduce a one-slot control-flow fold buffer that trains on two low-entropy\\nclasses only: taken backward conditional branches and direct `JAL`. The slot\\nrecords the control PC, resolved target PC, and the first target instruction\\nonce that target is fetched architecturally. On a later hit, `if_stage`\\nleaves `io_imemAddr` on the normal sequential path but tags the control\\ninstruction so the following cycle can inject the cached target instruction\\ninto IF/ID i...\n  Line 32: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Hazard-Only Live Source Filtering\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"`bench` still detects load-use hazards from raw IF/ID bitfields, so\\ninstructions that do not architecturally read `rs2` or `rs1` can alias an\\nolder load destination through immediate bits and take an unnecessary bubble.\\nThe prior broad hazard-relaxation attempts failed because they also changed\\narchitected source accounting or touched the late STORE-data path; the\\nbackend-only opportunity itself is still there.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"`bench` still detects load-use hazards from raw IF/ID bitfields, so\\ninstructions that do not architecturally read `rs2` or `rs1` can alias an\\nolder load destination through immediate bits and take an unnecessary bubble.\\nThe prior broad hazard-relaxation attempts failed because they also changed\\narchitected source accounting or touched the late STORE-data path; the\\nbackend-only opportunity itself is still there.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep RVFI-visible `rs1_addr` / `rs2_addr` and the existing load->STORE-data\\npolicy unchanged, but make the interlock smarter about which IF/ID sources\\nare actually live for hazard purposes. In `hazard_unit`, derive conservative\\n`uses_rs1_hazard` / `uses_rs2_hazard` bits from the younger instruction's\\nopcode/funct form and ignore only the source fields that are provably unused\\nin EX for legal encodings such as OP-IMM, LOAD, JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE, and\\nEBREAK. Leave STORE `rs2`, BRANCH, R-type, and unknown/illegal encodings\\nconservative.\\n\\nThis targets IPC with almost no new datapath or fetch-path work: it removes\\nfalse load-use bubbles caused by immediate-field aliases while avoiding the\\ncorrectness pitfalls that broke the earlier stage-accurate and\\ncanonicalized-source attempts.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace raw rs-field load-use checks with conservative ...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-Owned Hazard Source Sideband\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining bench structural space is narrow. Fetch-side decoupling,\\nprediction, and wider MEM-side buffering have mostly regressed, while the\\nrecent source-canonicalization failure showed that the false load-use issue\\nis real but cannot be solved by rewriting RVFI-visible `rs1`/`rs2` fields.\\nThe current interlock still compares an older load destination against raw\\nIF/ID bitfields, so legal single-source or zero-source instructions can\\nstill alias through immediate bits and burn bubbles that do not protect any\\nreal dependency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining bench structural space is narrow. Fetch-side decoupling,\\nprediction, and wider MEM-side buffering have mostly regressed, while the\\nrecent source-canonicalization failure showed that the false load-use issue\\nis real but cannot be solved by rewriting RVFI-visible `rs1`/`rs2` fields.\\nThe current interlock still compares an older load destination against raw\\nIF/ID bitfields, so legal single-source or zero-source instructions can\\nstill alias through immediate bits and burn bubbles that do not protect any\\nreal dependency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the decode view used for hazards from the architectural source view\\nused for RVFI. Add conservative `hazard_uses_rs1` / `hazard_uses_rs2`\\nmetadata that says whether the younger instruction truly needs each raw\\nsource for the current pipeline's existing dependency rules, but keep the\\nraw source addresses themselves unchanged through ID/EX, MEM/WB, and RVFI.\\n\\nThen gate the load-use compare with those liveness bits: OP-IMM, LOAD, and\\nJALR stop stalling on bogus `rs2` aliases; JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE, and\\nEBREAK stop stalling on either fake source; BRANCH, R-type, STORE, and\\nother real consumers remain conservative. This stays off the fragile fetch\\npath and avoids the late-store-data / MEM fanout that already regressed...\n  Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Backedge Fold Buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The only predictor family that did not materially hurt `bench` was the\\nsingle-slot backedge fold buffer, which matched the current best fitness at\\n447.40 while keeping `io_imemAddr` on the existing sequential path. That\\nsuggests the remaining predictor headroom is not in more aggressive fetch\\nredirection, but in improving hit rate inside that same timing-safe\\nfold-and-inject shape.\\n\\nCoreMark rotates among several hot counted loops across the list, matrix,\\nand state kernels. A one-entry fold slot likely thrashes between those\\nbackedges and leaves repeatable taken-branch bubbles uncovered even though\\nthe underlying predictor mechanism appears Fmax-safe on this FPGA.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The only predictor family that did not materially hurt `bench` was the\\nsingle-slot backedge fold buffer, which matched the current best fitness at\\n447.40 while keeping `io_imemAddr` on the existing sequential path. That\\nsuggests the remaining predictor headroom is not in more aggressive fetch\\nredirection, but in improving hit rate inside that same timing-safe\\nfold-and-inject shape.\\n\\nCoreMark rotates among several hot counted loops across the list, matrix,\\nand state kernels. A one-entry fold slot likely thrashes between those\\nbackedges and leaves repeatable taken-branch bubbles uncovered even though\\nthe underlying predictor mechanism appears Fmax-safe on this FPGA.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the one-slot backedge fold idea with a two-entry backedge-only fold\\nbuffer trained from EX. Each entry records a taken backward branch PC, its\\nresolved target PC, and the first target instruction once that target is\\nfetched architecturally. On a later hit, `if_stage` still keeps\\n`io_imemAddr` on the normal sequential path, but tags the in-flight branch\\nso the following cycle can inject the cached target instruction into IF/ID\\nand repair the fetch ...","metadata":{"matches":30,"truncated":false},"title":"IF/ID|fetch buffer|pipeline|6-stage|split decode|regfile|operand|retim|retime|stage","time":{"start":1778876120090,"end":1778876120098}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0807ffc85fb35b90016a077ed7f6c8819882385383b8b0a157"}},"id":"prt_e2d477c08002VANGA4GEYU0Yat","sessionID":"ses_1d2b937bbffejBUFyDIrJ1Mo8g","messageID":"msg_e2d470978001O5KD5LchMcQhBT"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778876120101,"sessionID":"ses_1d2b937bbffejBUFyDIrJ1Mo8g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_AryfWZYefSr7DsjFCISJtq1T","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv","offset":1,"limit":220},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: // rtl/id_stage.sv\n2: //\n3: // Decode stage. Combinationally drives the regfile read ports and the\n4: // ID/EX register's next-state from the IF/ID combinational bundle plus\n5: // the regfile read data. The ID/EX register is owned by this module so\n6: // the decoded view of instruction n is latched by end of cycle n+1.\n7: //\n8: // Latency:        1 cycle (ID/EX register clocked here).\n9: // RVFI fields:    feeds rs1_addr, rs1_rdata, rs2_addr, rs2_rdata, insn,\n10: //                 trap (via ctrl.is_illegal).\n11: module id_stage (\n12:   input  logic              clock,\n13:   input  logic              reset,\n14:   input  logic              stall,\n15:   input  logic              flush,\n16:   input  if_id_t  in,\n17:   // regfile read interface\n18:   output logic [4:0]        rs1_addr,\n19:   output logic [4:0]        rs2_addr,\n20:   input  logic [31:0]       rs1_data,\n21:   input  logic [31:0]       rs2_data,\n22:   // ID/EX register output\n23:   output id_ex_t  out\n24: );\n25: \n26:   // ── Combinational decode ────────────────────────────────────────────────\n27:   logic [4:0]  dec_alu_op;\n28:   logic        dec_alu_src;\n29:   logic [2:0]  dec_branch_op;\n30:   logic        dec_is_branch;\n31:   logic        dec_is_jump;\n32:   logic        dec_is_jalr;\n33:   logic        dec_is_lui;\n34:   logic        dec_is_auipc;\n35:   logic        dec_mem_read;\n36:   logic        dec_mem_write;\n37:   logic [1:0]  dec_mem_width;\n38:   logic        dec_mem_sext;\n39:   logic        dec_reg_write;\n40:   logic        dec_mem_to_reg;\n41:   logic        dec_is_illegal;\n42: \n43:   decoder u_decoder (\n44:     .instr      (in.instr),\n45:     .alu_op     (dec_alu_op),\n46:     .alu_src    (dec_alu_src),\n47:     .branch_op  (dec_branch_op),\n48:     .is_branch  (dec_is_branch),\n49:     .is_jump    (dec_is_jump),\n50:     .is_jalr    (dec_is_jalr),\n51:     .is_lui     (dec_is_lui),\n52:     .is_auipc   (dec_is_auipc),\n53:     .mem_read   (dec_mem_read),\n54:     .mem_write  (dec_mem_write),\n55:     .mem_width  (dec_mem_width),\n56:     .mem_sext   (dec_mem_sext),\n57:     .reg_write  (dec_reg_write),\n58:     .mem_to_reg (dec_mem_to_reg),\n59:     .is_illegal (dec_is_illegal)\n60:   );\n61: \n62:   logic [31:0] imm;\n63:   imm_gen u_imm (.instr(in.instr), .imm(imm));\n64: \n65:   // Regfile read addresses come straight from the IF/ID instruction — these\n66:   // are also wired to the hazard unit at top level for load-use detection.\n67:   assign rs1_addr = in.instr[19:15];\n68:   assign rs2_addr = in.instr[24:20];\n69: \n70:   ctrl_t ctrl_decoded;\n71:   always_comb begin\n72:     ctrl_decoded.alu_op     = dec_alu_op;\n73:     ctrl_decoded.alu_src    = dec_alu_src;\n74:     ctrl_decoded.branch_op  = dec_branch_op;\n75:     ctrl_decoded.is_branch  = dec_is_branch;\n76:     ctrl_decoded.is_jump    = dec_is_jump;\n77:     ctrl_decoded.is_jalr    = dec_is_jalr;\n78:     ctrl_decoded.is_lui     = dec_is_lui;\n79:     ctrl_decoded.is_auipc   = dec_is_auipc;\n80:     ctrl_decoded.mem_read   = dec_mem_read;\n81:     ctrl_decoded.mem_write  = dec_mem_write;\n82:     ctrl_decoded.mem_width  = dec_mem_width;\n83:     ctrl_decoded.mem_sext   = dec_mem_sext;\n84:     ctrl_decoded.reg_write  = dec_reg_write;\n85:     ctrl_decoded.mem_to_reg = dec_mem_to_reg;\n86:     ctrl_decoded.is_illegal = dec_is_illegal;\n87:   end\n88: \n89:   // ── ID/EX register ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n90:   id_ex_t reg_q;\n91: \n92:   always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n93:     if (reset || flush) begin\n94:       reg_q <= '0;\n95:     end else if (!stall) begin\n96:       reg_q.pc       <= in.pc;\n97:       reg_q.rs1_val  <= rs1_data;\n98:       reg_q.rs2_val  <= rs2_data;\n99:       reg_q.imm      <= imm;\n100:       reg_q.rd       <= in.instr[11:7];\n101:       reg_q.rs1_addr <= in.instr[19:15];\n102:       reg_q.rs2_addr <= in.instr[24:20];\n103:       reg_q.ctrl     <= ctrl_decoded;\n104:       reg_q.instr    <= in.instr;\n105:       reg_q.valid    <= in.valid;\n106:     end\n107:   end\n108: \n109:   assign out = reg_q;\n110: \n111: endmodule\n\n(End of file - total 111 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"// rtl/id_stage.sv\n//\n// Decode stage. Combinationally drives the regfile read ports and the\n// ID/EX register's next-state from the IF/ID combinational bundle plus\n// the regfile read data. The ID/EX register is owned by this module so\n// the decoded view of instruction n is latched by end of cycle n+1.\n//\n// Latency:        1 cycle (ID/EX register clocked here).\n// RVFI fields:    feeds rs1_addr, rs1_rdata, rs2_addr, rs2_rdata, insn,\n//                 trap (via ctrl.is_illegal).\nmodule id_stage (\n  input  logic              clock,\n  input  logic              reset,\n  input  logic              stall,\n  input  logic              flush,\n  input  if_id_t  in,\n  // regfile read interface\n  output logic [4:0]        rs1_addr,\n  output logic [4:0]        rs2_addr,\n  input  logic [31:0]       rs1_data,","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv","time":{"start":1778876120094,"end":1778876120101}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0807ffc85fb35b90016a077ed7f6d8819898b8281a15792bfb"}},"id":"prt_e2d477c1b001IkrX03h37n9ITk","sessionID":"ses_1d2b937bbffejBUFyDIrJ1Mo8g","messageID":"msg_e2d470978001O5KD5LchMcQhBT"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778876120105,"sessionID":"ses_1d2b937bbffejBUFyDIrJ1Mo8g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_4KajRMKkBYgwxkkx3b8J2M0N","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv","offset":1,"limit":220},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: // rtl/if_stage.sv\n2: //\n3: // Instruction fetch stage. Holds the PC register; the IF/ID payload\n4: // (pc + instr + valid) is *combinational* — there is no separate IF/ID\n5: // flop in this microarchitecture, the next-stage's ID/EX register\n6: // captures everything one cycle later.\n7: //\n8: // On flush or redirect, the instruction emitted to ID is forced to NOP\n9: // (`0x00000013` = ADDI x0,x0,0). This prevents the hazard unit from\n10: // observing a real rs1/rs2 from a wrong-path instruction and inserting\n11: // a spurious load-use stall the cycle after a taken branch.\n12: //\n13: // Latency:        PC-reg update is synchronous; output is combinational.\n14: // RVFI fields:    feeds pc_rdata (via ID/EX/MEM/WB) and pc_wdata (via\n15: //                 EX-stage redirect).\n16: module if_stage (\n17:   input  logic              clock,\n18:   input  logic              reset,\n19:   input  logic              stall,            // hold PC (load-use)\n20:   input  logic              flush,            // emit NOP into ID this cycle\n21:   input  logic              redirect,         // EX has resolved a branch/jump\n22:   input  logic [31:0]       redirect_target,\n23:   output logic [31:0]       imem_addr,\n24:   input  logic [31:0]       imem_data,\n25:   output if_id_t  out\n26: );\n27: \n28:   localparam logic [31:0] RESET_PC = 32'h0000_0000;\n29: \n30:   logic [31:0] pc;\n31:   logic [31:0] next_pc;\n32: \n33:   always_comb begin\n34:     next_pc = redirect ? redirect_target : pc + 32'd4;\n35:   end\n36: \n37:   // Redirect must override stall: a BRANCH/JAL/JALR in EX may fire\n38:   // redirect on the same cycle as imem_stall or dmem_stall — without\n39:   // this priority the redirect target would be silently dropped, the\n40:   // PC would hold its old (wrong-path) value, and execution would\n41:   // resume on the wrong path once the bus unstalls. Verified by the\n42:   // VexRiscv-binary CoreMark sweep with --istall enabled.\n43:   always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n44:     if      (reset)    pc <= RESET_PC;\n45:     else if (redirect) pc <= redirect_target;\n46:     else if (!stall)   pc <= next_pc;\n47:   end\n48: \n49:   assign imem_addr = pc;\n50: \n51:   always_comb begin\n52:     out.pc    = pc;\n53:     out.instr = (flush || redirect) ? 32'h0000_0013 : imem_data;\n54:     out.valid = !(flush || redirect);\n55:   end\n56: \n57: endmodule\n\n(End of file - total 57 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"// rtl/if_stage.sv\n//\n// Instruction fetch stage. Holds the PC register; the IF/ID payload\n// (pc + instr + valid) is *combinational* — there is no separate IF/ID\n// flop in this microarchitecture, the next-stage's ID/EX register\n// captures everything one cycle later.\n//\n// On flush or redirect, the instruction emitted to ID is forced to NOP\n// (`0x00000013` = ADDI x0,x0,0). This prevents the hazard unit from\n// observing a real rs1/rs2 from a wrong-path instruction and inserting\n// a spurious load-use stall the cycle after a taken branch.\n//\n// Latency:        PC-reg update is synchronous; output is combinational.\n// RVFI fields:    feeds pc_rdata (via ID/EX/MEM/WB) and pc_wdata (via\n//                 EX-stage redirect).\nmodule if_stage (\n  input  logic              clock,\n  input  logic              reset,\n  input  logic              stall,            // hold PC (load-use)\n  input  logic              flush,            // emit NOP into ID this cycle","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv","time":{"start":1778876120099,"end":1778876120105}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0807ffc85fb35b90016a077ed7f6e081989a38167cc53d7848"}},"id":"prt_e2d477c1f001GjCLpdfx23YA0X","sessionID":"ses_1d2b937bbffejBUFyDIrJ1Mo8g","messageID":"msg_e2d470978001O5KD5LchMcQhBT"}}
