{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778876222153,"sessionID":"ses_1d2b937bbffejBUFyDIrJ1Mo8g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_CoP6lMWFicB8cVeO1bVkWD0a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"rvfi.*rs1|rvfi.*rs2|source refresh|architectural source|late source|reg_check|stale rs|fresh rs","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments","include":"log.jsonl"},"output":"Found 15 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Static BTFNT branch predictor with ID redirect\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench core resolves every branch and jump in EX and flushes on every taken redirect. CoreMark is loop-heavy, so repeatedly paying that taken-branch penalty burns IPC. LUT headroom is tight, which makes a low-state predictor more attractive than a larger dynamic structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench core resolves every branch and jump in EX and flushes on every taken redirect. CoreMark is loop-heavy, so repeatedly paying that taken-branch penalty burns IPC. LUT headroom is tight, which makes a low-state predictor more attractive than a larger dynamic structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a decode-stage backward-taken/forward-not-taken predictor for conditional branches and always predict JAL taken, driving a speculative next PC from ID into IF. EX remains the architectural source of truth for branch direction and target, but only flushes and recovers on a mispredict. This should remove most loop-backedge and JAL bubbles in CoreMark while staying much lighter than a tagged BTB/BHT, limiting Fmax and LUT risk.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire an ID-stage prediction path i