{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778876290819,"sessionID":"ses_1d2b937bbffejBUFyDIrJ1Mo8g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_IUDKnXglCHqnp1sym1rxP0LW","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1|rvfi_reg_check|stale aliased|fresh `rvfi_rs","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments","include":"log.jsonl"},"output":"Found 7 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 11: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Stage-Accurate Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"After divider offload and the one-deep store retirement slot, the cheapest\\nremaining CoreMark IPC loss is likely the still-conservative load-use\\ninterlock. `hazard_unit` compares a load destination against raw IF/ID rs1\\nand rs2 bitfields even when the younger instruction does not actually need\\nthose fields in EX, and it still forces a bubble for STORE rs2 even though\\nstore data is only consumed one stage later in MEM.\\n\\nThat means the core can still lose cycles to false immediate-field matches\\nand to real load-then-store-data dependencies that do not protect any EX\\ncritical operand. This is a narrow backend/control cleanup that attacks IPC\\nwithout adding predictor state, fetch-path muxing, or a new EX datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"After divider offload and the one-deep store retirement slot, the cheapest\\nremaining CoreMark IPC loss is likely the still-conservative load-use\\ninterlock. `hazard_unit` compares a load destination against raw IF/ID rs1\\nand rs2 bitfields even when the younger instruction does not actually need\\nthose fields in EX, and it still forces a bubble for STORE rs2 even though\\nstore data is only consumed one stage later in MEM.\\n\\nThat means the core can still lose cycles to false immediate-field matches\\nand to real load-then-store-data dependencies that do not protect any EX\\ncritical operand. This is a narrow backend/control cleanup that attacks IPC\\nwithout adding predictor state, fetch-path muxing, or a new EX datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make the load-use detector stage-accurate instead of field-accurate.\\nDecode conservative `uses_rs1_ex` / `uses_rs2_ex` bits from the IF/ID\\ninstruction inside `hazard_unit`, and only stall when the older load feeds a\\nvalue that the younger instruction truly needs in EX. Keep unknown or\\nillegal encodings conservative, but stop treating OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR,\\nLUI/AUIP...\n  Line 14: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Load-to-Store Data Bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The recent `bench` history says two things clearly: frontend speculation is\\ntoxic on this core's Fmax, while backend-only store-side changes can pay.\\nThe broad stage-accurate load-use idea also already taught the failure mode:\\nrelaxing hazards is only safe if the architectural source value is still\\ncanonical at retirement.\\n\\nA narrower version of that idea still looks promising. Today `hazard_unit`\\ntreats a younger STORE's `rs2` the same as an EX-time operand, so a\\nload-then-store-data dependency always burns a bubble even though the store\\nvalue is only needed when MEM drives `dmem_wdata`. CoreMark's list, matrix,\\nand state kernels are store-heavy enough that trimming this one precise stall\\nclass could improve IPC without touching the fetch path or hot ALU cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The recent `bench` history says two things clearly: frontend speculation is\\ntoxic on this core's Fmax, while backend-only store-side changes can pay.\\nThe broad stage-accurate load-use idea also already taught the failure mode:\\nrelaxing hazards is only safe if the architectural source value is still\\ncanonical at retirement.\\n\\nA narrower version of that idea still looks promising. 