{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778876322577,"sessionID":"ses_1d2b937bbffejBUFyDIrJ1Mo8g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_zz3cs93SC7h6Ygbp3zgsJJpm","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r12s0.yaml","offset":1,"limit":220},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r12s0.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: id: hyp-20260515-001-r12s0\n2: title: \"Compact MEM/WB Payload and Registered Rd Data\"\n3: category: micro_opt\n4: motivation: |\n5:   The current `bench` champion already found the big wins that stay off the\n6:   fragile fetch path: cold divide offload and a one-deep deferred store slot.\n7:   The remaining always-hot backend cone still carries avoidable late-stage\n8:   baggage, though. `mem_wb_t` stores both `alu_result` and `read_data` even\n9:   though only one can ever become architectural `rd_wdata`, and the full\n10:   `ctrl_t` bundle is dragged all the way into WB even though only a tiny subset\n11:   is still live there.\n12: \n13:   On a Tang Nano 20K, that extra register width, control fanout, and the\n14:   MEM/WB-to-WB select mux all sit on a path that also feeds regfile writeback,\n15:   same-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding, and RVFI `rd_wdata`. This looks\n16:   like the cleanest remaining Fmax-focused `micro_opt` that does not revisit\n17:   the broken hazard-source space or the already-regressive fetch-side ideas.\n18: hypothesis: |\n19:   Retire the MEM/WB stage around the architectural result that downstream logic\n20:   actually consumes. In `mem_stage`, precompute the final `rd_wdata` once and\n21:   register that canonical value directly in `mem_wb_t` instead of carrying both\n22:   `alu_result` and `read_data` into WB for a late mux. At the same time, trim\n23:   late-stage control so MEM/WB only holds the bits that are still semantically\n24:   live there, rather than the full decode-era `ctrl_t`.\n25: \n26:   Concretely, this means removing redundant `mem_to_reg` plumbing after MEM,\n27:   compacting the MEM/WB payload around `rd`, `rd_wdata`, `reg_write`,\n28:   `is_illegal`, and the existing RVFI-visible fields, and making `wb_stage`\n29:   largely a pass-through. That should cut flop/routing load and remove one mux\n30:   level from the MEM/WB forwarding path without changing CPI, hazard policy,\n31:   store-slot ordering, or any RVFI-visible behavior.\n32: expected_impact:\n33:   fitness_delta_pct: 5\n34:   confidence: medium\n35: changes:\n36:   - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n37:     description: \"Replace the late-stage `alu_result`/`read_data` duality with a single registered `rd_wdata` field in `mem_wb_t`, and split or trim control so WB only carries bits still live after MEM.\"\n38:   - file: rtl/decoder.sv\n39:     description: \"Remove the redundant `mem_to_reg` output so load writeback selection is no longer encoded as a pipeline-wide control bit when `mem_read` already identifies the only load-producing class.\"\n40:   - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n41:     description: \"Stop propagating `mem_to_reg` through decoded control and keep only the control information that is still required by downstream stages.\"\n42:   - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n43:     description: \"Compute the final architectural `rd_wdata` in MEM, pack the compact MEM/WB payload, and preserve stall-hold forwarding semantics using the registered canonical writeback value.\"\n44:   - file: rtl/wb_stage.sv\n45:     description: \"Collapse WB into a direct drive of the already-registered `rd_wdata`, `rd`, and `reg_write` fields instead of a fresh load-vs-ALU mux.\"\n46:   - file: rtl/core.sv\n47:     description: \"Rewire regfile writeback, MEM/WB-to-EX forwarding, and RVFI `rd_wdata` to consume the compact MEM/WB result directly while preserving the top-level nret=1 contract.\"\n\n(End of file - total 47 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260515-001-r12s0\ntitle: \"Compact MEM/WB Payload and Registered Rd Data\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current `bench` champion already found the big wins that stay off the\n  fragile fetch path: cold divide offload and a one-deep deferred store slot.\n  The remaining always-hot backend cone still carries avoidable late-stage\n  baggage, though. `mem_wb_t` stores both `alu_result` and `read_data` even\n  though only one can ever become architectural `rd_wdata`, and the full\n  `ctrl_t` bundle is dragged all the way into WB even though only a tiny subset\n  is still live there.\n\n  On a Tang Nano 20K, that extra register width, control fanout, and the\n  MEM/WB-to-WB select mux all sit on a path that also feeds regfile writeback,\n  same-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding, and RVFI `rd_wdata`. This looks\n  like the cleanest remaining Fmax-focused `micro_opt` that does not revisit\n  the broken hazard-source space or the already-regressive fetch-side ideas.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retire the MEM/WB stage around the architectural result that downstream logic\n  actually consumes. In `mem_stage`, precompute the final `rd_wdata` once and","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r12s0.yaml","time":{"start":1778876322575,"end":1778876322577}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_08e2182b9cdbd6b0016a077fa27424819ba04f4f985c368df3"}},"id":"prt_e2d4a92fa001Z9G6hu55IenBn7","sessionID":"ses_1d2b937bbffejBUFyDIrJ1Mo8g","messageID":"msg_e2d4a75bc001dp4M4X6Cq9UCyF"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778876322583,"sessionID":"ses_1d2b937bbffejBUFyDIrJ1Mo8g","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_nBgXt52ja4vh2GTjQJdqBzr5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r12s2.yaml","offset":1,"limit":220},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r12s2.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: id: hyp-20260515-003-r12s2\n2: title: \"Champion-Challenger Backedge Fold Buffer\"\n3: category: predictor\n4: motivation: |\n5:   The predictor history on `bench` is unusually narrow: almost every branch or\n6:   fetch predictor that added lookup, redirect, or target-selection work to the\n7:   live frontend path regressed badly, but the single-slot backedge fold buffer\n8:   matched the current best fitness exactly. That strongly suggests there is\n9:   still predictor headroom, but only inside the same timing-safe fold-and-\n10:   inject shape that leaves `io_imemAddr` on the existing sequential path.\n11: \n12:   The likely remaining loss is one-slot thrash. CoreMark rotates among several\n13:   hot counted loops, so a single fold slot can keep relearning a different\n14:   backedge and give away otherwise-predictable taken-branch bubbles.\n15: hypothesis: |\n16:   Keep the fetch-time predictor lookup exactly one entry wide, but add a small\n17:   off-path challenger slot and repeat counter that are updated only from EX.\n18:   The active fold slot remains the only structure consulted in IF: same one tag\n19:   compare, same cached target instruction injection, same sequential\n20:   `io_imemAddr` path, and no new multi-entry select logic on the live fetch\n21:   cone.\n22: \n23:   When EX resolves a taken backward branch that does not match the active slot,\n24:   train it first into the challenger. Only if that challenger repeats does it\n25:   get promoted into the active fold slot. This attacks the likely one-slot\n26:   aliasing problem across CoreMark's list, matrix, and state loops without\n27:   repeating the two-entry fetch-lookup regression. In effect, hit-rate\n28:   improvement moves into EX-time training policy while IF timing stays at the\n29:   one-slot fold-buffer complexity that already proved safe.\n30: expected_impact:\n31:   fitness_delta_pct: 4\n32:   confidence: medium\n33: changes:\n34:   - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n35:     description: \"Add minimal fold-predictor sideband fields so a folded branch can carry active-slot metadata from IF through EX without changing RVFI-visible state.\"\n36:   - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n37:     description: \"Keep a single active backedge fold lookup on the fetch path, inject the cached target instruction on active hits, and maintain a shadow challenger record that is never consulted in the live PC path.\"\n38:   - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n39:     description: \"Latch the fold sideband alongside the decoded instruction so EX can distinguish active-slot predictions from ordinary sequential fetches.\"\n40:   - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n41:     description: \"Train taken backward branches into active and challenger slots, bump challenger confidence on repeats, and promote only repeated challengers into the active fold slot while keeping EX as the architectural source of truth for redirect recovery.\"\n42:   - file: rtl/core.sv\n43:     description: \"Wire predictor training, promotion, and fold sideband signals between IF and EX without changing the top-level nret=1 contract.\"\n\n(End of file - total 43 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260515-003-r12s2\ntitle: \"Champion-Challenger Backedge Fold Buffer\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The predictor history on `bench` is unusually narrow: almost every branch or\n  fetch predictor that added lookup, redirect, or target-selection work to the\n  live frontend path regressed badly, but the single-slot backedge fold buffer\n  matched the current best fitness exactly. That strongly suggests there is\n  still predictor headroom, but only inside the same timing-safe fold-and-\n  inject shape that leaves `io_imemAddr` on the existing sequential path.\n\n  The likely remaining loss is one-slot thrash. CoreMark rotates among several\n  hot counted loops, so a single fold slot can keep relearning a different\n  backedge and give away otherwise-predictable taken-branch bubbles.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep the fetch-time predictor lookup exactly one entry wide, but add a small\n  off-path challenger slot and repeat counter that are updated only from EX.\n  The active fold slot remains the only structure consulted in IF: same one tag\n  compare, same cached target instruction injection, same sequential\n  `io_imemAddr` path, and no new multi-entry select logic on the live fetch","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r12s2.yaml","time":{"start":1778876322582,"end":1778876322583}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_08e2182b9cdbd6b0016a077fa27434819b90eefaab0dcddcbd"}},"id":"prt_e2d4a9311002RrGF0QZ6Ra1jni","sessionID":"ses_1d2b937bbffejBUFyDIrJ1Mo8g","messageID":"msg_e2d4a75bc001dp4M4X6Cq9UCyF"}}
