{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778877956038,"sessionID":"ses_1d29f11c7ffeJQzU3ayThiUohG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_6P6ZD7aSXLtTU82oSsKxysHO","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r13s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r13s1\n+title: \"Disjoint Load Bypass Around Deferred Store Slot\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The current champion already proved that a one-deep deferred RAM-store slot\n+  can recover IPC, but it still fences every younger memory operation until the\n+  queued store physically drains. Under the tournament's random dmem stalls,\n+  that means one stalled store can still block unrelated later loads even when\n+  their addresses do not overlap and the existing slot already holds the older\n+  store's full aligned word address, mask, and data.\n+\n+  Prior structural regressions say not to add fetch-side machinery, larger\n+  queues, or replay caches. The most promising remaining backend lever is to\n+  make the existing single-entry store slot less serializing.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Keep the current one-deep deferred-store design, but let safe younger loads\n+  bypass a queued RAM store when they target a different aligned word. When the\n+  store slot is occupied, `mem_stage` should arbitrate between draining the old\n+  store and serving the live EX/MEM request: aligned normal-RAM loads whose\n+  aligned word address differs from the queued store may use the dmem port\n+  immediately and leave the store queued for a later cycle, while same-word\n+  loads, stores, MMIO accesses, misaligned accesses, and EBREAK keep the\n+  existing conservative fence.\n+\n+  This reuses the already-winning backend structure instead of growing it into\n+  a two-entry queue. The added logic is small: one slot/live-address conflict\n+  check, a load-priority dmem mux in MEM, and matching hazard-side gating so\n+  only true store-slot conflicts stall. That should recover cycles in\n+  CoreMark's interleaved stack/list/matrix memory traffic without paying the\n+  frontend or wide-buffer costs that prior structural attempts showed were too\n+  expensive on this FPGA.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Teach the pending-store slot to keep an older RAM store queued while issuing a younger disjoint aligned RAM load on the live dmem port, with same-word loads and all other memory classes staying conservative.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Refine queued-store fencing so a valid store slot only stalls younger memory ops that truly conflict or still require strict ordering, instead of blocking every load unconditionally.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Plumb the extra store-slot conflict and bypass sideband signals between MEM and the hazard logic without changing the top-level I/O or RVFI contract.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r13s1.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r13s1.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r13s1.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r13s1.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,41 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r13s1\n+title: \"Disjoint Load Bypass Around Deferred Store Slot\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The current champion already proved that a one-deep deferred RAM-store slot\n+  can recover IPC, but it still fences every younger memory operation until the\n+  queued store physically drains. Under the tournament's random dmem stalls,\n+  that means one stalled store can still block unrelated later loads even when\n+  their addresses do not overlap and the existing slot already holds the older\n+  store's full aligned word address, mask, and data.\n+\n+  Prior structural regressions say not to add fetch-side machinery, larger\n+  queues, or replay caches. The most promising remaining backend lever is to\n+  make the existing single-entry store slot less serializing.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Keep the current one-deep deferred-store design, but let safe younger loads\n+  bypass a queued RAM store when they target a different aligned word. When the\n+  store slot is occupied, `mem_stage` should arbitrate between draining the old\n+  store and serving the live EX/MEM request: aligned normal-RAM loads whose\n+  aligned word address differs from the queued store may use the dmem port\n+  immediately and leave the store queued for a later cycle, while same-word\n+  loads, stores, MMIO accesses, misaligned accesses, and EBREAK keep the\n+  existing conservative fence.\n+\n+  This reuses the already-winning backend structure instead of growing it into\n+  a two-entry queue. The added logic is small: one slot/live-address conflict\n+  check, a load-priority dmem mux in MEM, and matching hazard-side gating so\n+  only true store-slot conflicts stall. That should recover cycles in\n+  CoreMark's interleaved stack/list/matrix memory traffic without paying the\n+  frontend or wide-buffer costs that prior structural attempts showed were too\n+  expensive on this FPGA.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Teach the pending-store slot to keep an older RAM store queued while issuing a younger disjoint aligned RAM load on the live dmem port, with same-word loads and all other memory classes staying conservative.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Refine queued-store fencing so a valid store slot only stalls younger memory ops that truly conflict or still require strict ordering, instead of blocking every load unconditionally.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Plumb the extra store-slot conflict and bypass sideband signals between MEM and the hazard logic without changing the top-level I/O or RVFI contract.\"\n\n","files":[{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r13s1.yaml","relativePath":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r13s1.yaml","type":"add","patch":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r13s1.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r13s1.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r13s1.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,41 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r13s1\n+title: \"Disjoint Load Bypass Around Deferred Store Slot\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The current champion already proved that a one-deep deferred RAM-store slot\n+  can recover IPC, but it still fences every younger memory operation until the\n+  queued store physically drains. Under the tournament's random dmem stalls,\n+  that means one stalled store can still block unrelated later loads even when\n+  their addresses do not overlap and the existing slot already holds the older\n+  store's full aligned word address, mask, and data.\n+\n+  Prior structural regressions say not to add fetch-side machinery, larger\n+  queues, or replay caches. The most promising remaining backend lever is to\n+  make the existing single-entry store slot less serializing.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Keep the current one-deep deferred-store design, but let safe younger loads\n+  bypass a queued RAM store when they target a different aligned word. When the\n+  store slot is occupied, `mem_stage` should arbitrate between draining the old\n+  store and serving the live EX/MEM request: aligned normal-RAM loads whose\n+  aligned word address differs from the queued store may use the dmem port\n+  immediately and leave the store queued for a later cycle, while same-word\n+  loads, stores, MMIO accesses, misaligned accesses, and EBREAK keep the\n+  existing conservative fence.\n+\n+  This reuses the already-winning backend structure instead of growing it into\n+  a two-entry queue. The added logic is small: one slot/live-address conflict\n+  check, a load-priority dmem mux in MEM, and matching hazard-side gating so\n+  only true store-slot conflicts stall. That should recover cycles in\n+  CoreMark's interleaved stack/list/matrix memory traffic without paying the\n+  frontend or wide-buffer costs that prior structural attempts showed were too\n+  expensive on this FPGA.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Teach the pending-store slot to keep an older RAM store queued while issuing a younger disjoint aligned RAM load on the live dmem port, with same-word loads and all other memory classes staying conservative.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Refine queued-store fencing so a valid store slot only stalls younger memory ops that truly conflict or still require strict ordering, instead of blocking every load unconditionally.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Plumb the extra store-slot conflict and bypass sideband signals between MEM and the hazard logic without changing the top-level I/O or RVFI contract.\"\n","additions":41,"deletions":0}],"