{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778879463539,"sessionID":"ses_1d28633a4ffexDsv3Er74Ax1zs","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_tC0iQQiwNJ4WyOknAgf2TFaf","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"improvement.*structural|structural.*improvement|One-Deep Store|divider|prefetch|scoreboard|branch resolve|operand latch|skid|queue","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/experiments","include":"log.jsonl"},"output":"Found 53 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Move DIV/REM off the single-cycle ALU path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU places RV32M multiplication and division in the same\\ncombinational EX-stage result mux as common integer operations. A 32-bit\\ncombinational divider is very likely to dominate LUT depth and area on\\nthe Tang Nano 20K, yet CoreMark's timed region is matrix/list/state code\\nthat is multiply-heavy and effectively division-free. Keeping DIV/REM\\nsingle-cycle optimizes an uncommon path while lowering Fmax for every\\ninstruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU places RV32M multiplication and division in the same\\ncombinational EX-stage result mux as common integer operations. A 32-bit\\ncombinational divider is very likely to dominate LUT depth and area on\\nthe Tang Nano 20K, yet CoreMark's timed region is matrix/list/state code\\nthat is multiply-heavy and effectively division-free. Keeping DIV/REM\\nsingle-cycle optimizes an uncommon path while lowering Fmax for every\\ninstruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases in rtl/alu.sv with a\\nsmall iterative RV32M divide/remainder unit and add an EX-stage interlock\\nonly when one of those opcodes is in flight. Leave ADD/SUB/logic/shift,\\nbranches, loads/stores, jumps, and MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the current\\nsingle-cycle path. The timed CoreMark loop should pay no meaningful IPC\\npenalty because it does not execute DIV/REM, while the FPGA critical path\\nshould shrink from divider-through-ALU-mux to the existing add/compare/mul\\npaths. Preserve exact RV32M divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 semantics and\\nkeep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions for formal builds.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 15, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational DIV/REM hardware from the normal ALU result\\nmux, retaining single-cycle non-divide ALU and MUL behavior plus the\\nexisting A...\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Elastic Fetch Skid Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF/ID handoff with an elastic 2-entry fetch skid\\nbuffer that decouples instruction fetch from decode. The fetch side advances\\nthe PC and captures sequential instructions whenever io_imemReady is high\\nand the buffer is not full, even while ID/EX is held by a dmem stall. The\\ndecode side consumes one buffered instruction per non-stalled cycle. Preserve\\nthe baseline zero-latency behavior with an empty-buffer bypass from\\nio_imemData to decode when the backend is ready and imem is ready, so the\\nno-stall path and branch target...\n  Line 5: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-004-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Single Shared RV32M Multiplier\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU builds three independent wide products every cycle:\\nsigned*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. Those products feed\\nthe same EX-stage result mux as ordinary integer operations, so their area and\\nrouting pressure can lower Fmax even when most instructions are ADD/logic,\\nbranches, loads, and stores. CoreMark does use MUL, so making multiplication\\nmulti-cycle risks losing IPC; however all RV32M multiply variants can be\\nimplemented by one signedness-controlled 33x33 product with no latency change.\\nThis is a narrower and lower-risk micro-optimization than the prior failed\\nDIV/REM interlock attempt because it leaves divider behavior, pipeline stalls,\\nand RVFI ordering untouched.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU builds three independent wide products every cycle:\\nsigned*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. Those products feed\\nthe same EX-stage result mux as ordinary integer operations, so their area and\\nrouting pressure can lower Fmax even when most instructions are ADD/logic,\\nbranches, loads, and stores. CoreMark does use MUL, so making multiplication\\nmulti-cycle risks losing IPC; however all RV32M multiply variants can be\\nimplemented by one signedness-controlled 33x33 product with no latency change.\\nThis is a narrower and lower-risk micro-optimization than the prior failed\\nDIV/REM interlock attempt because it leaves divider behavior, pipeline stalls,\\nand RVFI ordering untouched.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three unconditional multiplier expressions in rtl/alu.sv with a\\nsingle shared 33-bit-by-33-bit product. For MUL, zero-extend both operands and\\nuse the low 32 bits; for MULH, sign-extend both operands; for MULHSU,\\nsign-extend rs1 and zero-extend rs2; for MULHU, zero-extend both operands.\\nSelect product[63:32] for the high-half operations and product[31:0] for MUL.\\nKeep the RISC...\n  Line 7: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-006-r2s2\", \"title\": \"IF Direct-Jump Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches in IF,\\nbut ex_stage still asserts a redirect for every JAL/JALR. That leaves\\nunconditional direct jumps, calls, and compiler-generated `j` control-flow\\nskips paying the same wrong-path flush as an unpredicted taken branch, even\\nthough a JAL target is fully encoded in the fetched instruction. CoreMark's\\noptimized RV32IM image contains many direct JAL/J sites, so eliminating those\\nredirect bubbles should improve iterations/cycle with much less timing risk\\nthan adding a BTB or learned direction table to the IF path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches in IF,\\nbut ex_stage still asserts a redirect for every JAL/JALR. That leaves\\nunconditional direct jumps, calls, and compiler-generated `j` control-flow\\nskips paying the same wrong-path flush as an unpredicted taken branch, even\\nthough a JAL target is fully encoded in the fetched instruction. CoreMark's\\noptimized RV32IM image contains many direct JAL/J sites, so eliminating those\\nredirect bubbles should improve iterations/cycle with much less timing risk\\nthan adding a BTB or learned direction table to the IF path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the existing IF-stage predictor from backward conditional branches to\\ndirect JAL targets. Recognize opcode 1101111 in IF, form the J immediate,\\npredict pc+imm as the next PC when the target is word-aligned, and carry the\\nexisting predicted_taken/predicted_target metadata with the instruction. In\\nEX, make JAL redirect mispredict-only by comparing the resolved jump target\\nagainst the carried prediction; JALR remains unpredicted and still redirects\\nfrom EX. This should remove the two-cycle recovery penalty from correctly\\npredicted direct jumps and calls while preserving RVFI pc_wdata, misaligned\\njump traps, and the single-retire c...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Retiring Store Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry FIFO for normal RAM stores. When a legal aligned store to the\\nRAM address range reaches MEM and the external dmem port is not immediately\\navailable, retire the store into the FIFO with its address, replicated write\\ndata, byte mask, and RVFI metadata, then let younger non-memory instructions\\ncontinue through the pipeline while the FIFO drains oldest-first on the dmem\\nport. Loads, stores when the FIFO is full, and all MMIO/marker/UART st...\n  Line 14: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-007-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Explicit MEM Byte-Lane Steering\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL still leaves a small but likely timing-relevant barrel\\nshifter in the MEM stage: load data is selected with `dmem_rdata >>\\n(addr[1:0] * 8)`, and store byte masks are formed with variable shifts.\\nOn an FPGA this is usually inferred as wider mux/shifter logic than needed,\\neven though RV32I memory accesses only need four fixed byte lanes and two\\nlegal halfword lanes. CoreMark is load/store heavy, and the dmem read-data\\nto MEM/WB path can compete with the EX ALU path for Fmax. A lane-case rewrite\\nis a narrow micro-optimization that should not affect IPC, branch prediction,\\nmemory ordering, RVFI retirement, or any RV32IM arithmetic behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL still leaves a small but likely timing-relevant barrel\\nshifter in the MEM stage: load data is selected with `dmem_rdata >>\\n(addr[1:0] * 8)`, and store byte masks are formed with variable shifts.\\nOn an FPGA this is usually inferred as wider mux/shifter logic than needed,\\neven though RV32I memory accesses only need four fixed byte lanes and two\\nlegal halfword lanes. CoreMark is load/store heavy, and the dmem read-data\\nto MEM/WB path can compete with the EX ALU path for Fmax. A lane-case rewrite\\nis a narrow micro-optimization that should not affect IPC, branch prediction,\\nmemory ordering, RVFI retirement, or any RV32IM arithmetic behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rewrite `rtl/mem_stage.sv` to decode `in.alu_result[1:0]` explicitly for\\nbyte/halfword steering instead of using variable shifts. Build `byte_val`,\\n`hword_val`, `byte_mask`, and replicated store data from small `case` tables\\nover the low address bits and access width. Preserve the existing misalignment\\ntrap rules, dmem gating on traps, RVFI aligned `mem_addr`, raw `mem_rdata`,\\nreplicated `mem_wdata`, and exact load sign/zero extension semantics. The\\nexpected gain is a mode...\n  Line 17: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-010-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Cold Multi-Cycle DIV/REM Unit\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\ncombinational EX-stage ALU result path as ADD, address generation, branch\\ncompare support, and MUL. The generated FPGA critical paths are dominated by\\nlong EX-stage carry/mux structures, while the CoreMark timed loop is\\neffectively division-cold: the static binary has only a few DIVU sites, and\\nthose are in setup/printing paths rather than the hot matrix/list/state\\niteration body. Prior multiplier and MEM-lane rewrites showed that reshaping\\nalready-mapped hot datapaths can badly hurt placement; the better\\nmicro-optimization is to remove the cold divider/remainder hardware from the\\none-cycle ALU path entirely while leaving multiply and all RV32I operations\\nsingle-cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\ncombinational EX-stage ALU result path as ADD, address generation, branch\\ncompare support, and MUL. The generated FPGA critical paths are dominated by\\nlong EX-stage carry/mux structures, while the CoreMark timed loop is\\neffectively division-cold: the static binary has only a few DIVU sites, and\\nthose are in setup/printing paths rather than the hot matrix/list/state\\niteration body. Prior multiplier and MEM-lane rewrites showed that reshaping\\nalready-mapped hot datapaths can badly hurt placement; the better\\nmicro-optimization is to remove the cold divider/remainder hardware from the\\none-cycle ALU path entirely while leaving multiply and all RV32I operations\\nsingle-cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases with a small\\niterative divider/remainder unit that is only entered for those four ALU\\nopcodes. Normal RV32I operations, branches, jumps, loads/stores, and all MUL\\nvariants remain one-cycle and keep the current forwarding behavior. When a\\ndivide-class instruction reaches...\n  Line 20: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-013-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Cold High-Half Multiply Unit\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from moving cold DIV/REM hardware out of the\\nuniversal EX-stage ALU path: CoreMark cycles barely changed, while median\\nFmax rose sharply and LUT pressure dropped. The same shape remains in the\\nmultiply path, but with a narrower target. `rtl/alu.sv` still builds three\\nindependent 64-bit products every cycle for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU.\\nCoreMark's timed kernels need ordinary low-half MUL, but high-half multiply\\nvariants are normally absent or cold in this benchmark. The previous 33x33\\nshared-multiplier rewrite proved that reshaping the hot MUL primitive itself\\ncan badly hurt placement, so the better micro-optimization is to leave the\\nexisting low-half MUL fast path intact and remove only the cold high-half\\nmultiplier variants from the one-cycle ALU.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from moving cold DIV/REM hardware out of the\\nuniversal EX-stage ALU path: CoreMark cycles barely changed, while median\\nFmax rose sharply and LUT pressure dropped. The same shape remains in the\\nmultiply path, but with a narrower target. `rtl/alu.sv` still builds three\\nindependent 64-bit products every cycle for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU.\\nCoreMark's timed kernels need ordinary low-half MUL, but high-half multiply\\nvariants are normally absent or cold in this benchmark. The previous 33x33\\nshared-multiplier rewrite proved that reshaping the hot MUL primitive itself\\ncan badly hurt placement, so the better micro-optimization is to leave the\\nexisting low-half MUL fast path intact and remove only the cold high-half\\nmultiplier variants from the one-cycle ALU.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep ALU_MUL as the only single-cycle hardware multiply in the hot ALU path,\\nusing the existing unsigned 32x32 product low half. Move MULH, MULHU, and\\nMULHSU into a small long-latency `mulh_unit` launched only for those three\\no...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-014-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Single-Entry Retiring Store Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gained a large Fmax and LUT win by moving DIV/REM out\\nof the hot EX path, so the next structural bottleneck is less likely to be\\nraw datapath area and more likely to be cycles lost to the random dmem\\nhandshake model. Today every dmem-not-ready cycle for a STORE freezes the\\nwhole pipeline, even though the store address, replicated data, byte mask,\\nand RVFI memory metadata are already known in MEM and the store produces no\\nregister result for younger ALU/control instructions. A prior two-entry\\nstore buffer proved the idea through formal and cosim but failed placement\\non the larger pre-DIV RTL. A smaller one-entry RAM-store buffer should retain\\nmost of the useful stall hiding while keeping the arbitration, state, and\\nplacement pressure low enough for the post-DIV design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gained a large Fmax and LUT win by moving DIV/REM out\\nof the hot EX path, so the next structural bottleneck is less likely to be\\nraw datapath area and more likely to be cycles lost to the random dmem\\nhandshake model. Today every dmem-not-ready cycle for a STORE freezes the\\nwhole pipeline, even though the store address, replicated data, byte mask,\\nand RVFI memory metadata are already known in MEM and the store produces no\\nregister result for younger ALU/control instructions. A prior two-entry\\nstore buffer proved the idea through formal and cosim but failed placement\\non the larger pre-DIV RTL. A smaller one-entry RAM-store buffer should retain\\nmost of the useful stall hiding while keeping the arbitration, state, and\\nplacement pressure low enough for the post-DIV design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one in-order retiring store slot for legal aligned stores to normal RAM\\nonly. When such a store reaches MEM and io_dmemReady is low, capture its\\nbyte address, replicated write data, byte mask, and RV...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-017-r8s1\", \"title\": \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot With Direct Fast Path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\\n\\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\\n\\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot that is used only on a...\n  Line 26: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-019-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Hot-Cold ALU Opcode Split\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design already moved DIV/REM out of the universal EX\\ndatapath and added the one-deep store slot, so the remaining micro-optimum\\nis likely in the hot single-cycle EX logic rather than in another stall\\nhiding structure. `rtl/alu.sv` still selects every RV32I operation and all\\nmultiply-class results through one dense ALU opcode case. Even though\\nDIV/REM now return dummy zero in the ALU, the common ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path still shares decode and mux structure with the cold M-extension\\nresult cases.\\n\\nA previous \\\"hot-bit ALU\\\" attempt was never architecturally evaluated because\\nthe implementation touched an off-limits non-RTL helper file. The idea\\nremains attractive after the divider win: leave every arithmetic primitive\\nand all pipeline control unchanged, but make the opcode encoding expose a\\nsimple hot/cold split that synthesis can map into a shallower mux for the\\ncommon RV32I path. This targets median Fmax with no CoreMark cycle-count\\nrisk and avoids the multiplier-primitive rewrites that previously regressed\\nbadly.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design already moved DIV/REM out of the universal EX\\ndatapath and added the one-deep store slot, so the remaining micro-optimum\\nis likely in the hot single-cycle EX logic rather than in another stall\\nhiding structure. `rtl/alu.sv` still selects every RV32I operation and all\\nmultiply-class results through one dense ALU opcode case. Even though\\nDIV/REM now return dummy zero in the ALU, the common ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path still shares decode and mux structure with the cold M-extension\\nresult cases.\\n\\nA previous \\\"hot-bit ALU\\\" attempt was never architecturally evaluated because\\nthe implementation touched an off-limits non-RTL helper file. The idea\\nremains attractive after the divider win: leave every arithmetic primitive\\nand all pipeline contr...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-020-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Store-Slot-Aware Fetch Skid Queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design combines the two changes that have paid off most:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and a one-deep pending-store slot\\nlets a stalled normal-RAM store retire while the physical dmem write drains\\nlater. That store slot reduced CoreMark cycles, but the frontend is still\\nstructurally brittle under the tournament's random imem stalls: `if_stage`\\npresents live imem data directly to decode, and `hazard_unit` still turns\\nraw `io_imemReady=0` into a global pipeline stall.\\n\\nA previous generic elastic fetch buffer regressed because it added permanent\\nfrontend queue/arbitration cost before the divider and store-slot timing\\nwins, and it tried to prefetch through all backend stalls. The better\\nstructural angle now is narrower: use a tiny fetch skid queue only to absorb\\ninstruction-memory backpressure and to keep fetch productive while the new\\nstore slot is draining for older stores. This targets the measured\\nstore-slot cycle win's remaining companion bottleneck without touching the\\nhot EX operand path, load data forwarding, branch predictor state, or RVFI\\nretirement width.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design combines the two changes that have paid off most:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and a one-deep pending-store slot\\nlets a stalled normal-RAM store retire while the physical dmem write drains\\nlater. That store slot reduced CoreMark cycles, but the frontend is still\\nstructurally brittle under the tournament's random imem stalls: `if_stage`\\npresents live imem data directly to decode, and `hazard_unit` still turns\\nraw `io_imemReady=0` into a global pipeline stall.\\n\\nA previous generic elastic fetch buffer regressed because it added permanent\\nfrontend queue/arbitration cost before the divider and store-slot timing\\nwins, and it tried to prefetch through all backend stalls. T...\n  Line 29: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-022-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Collapse Redundant EX/MEM Metadata\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already captured the large available wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is out of the one-cycle ALU path, and the pending store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. The remaining micro-opt slot should therefore\\navoid new predictors, queues, or arithmetic units and instead trim logic that\\nis already proven unnecessary on the hot pipeline boundary.\\n\\nThe EX/MEM bundle still carries several fields that do not add architectural\\ninformation. `write_data` is always assigned the same post-forwarded rs2\\nvalue as `rs2_val`, while `branch_taken` and `branch_target` are registered\\nbut never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, forwarding, or hazard logic. Those bits\\nadd about 65 EX/MEM flops plus mux/load on the EX branch-target and rs2 data\\npaths, exactly where the post-DIV design is trying to preserve Fmax. Pruning\\nthis redundant metadata should not change CoreMark cycles, bus behavior,\\nbranch prediction, RVFI ordering, or any ISA-visible result.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already captured the large available wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is out of the one-cycle ALU path, and the pending store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. The remaining micro-opt slot should therefore\\navoid new predictors, queues, or arithmetic units and instead trim logic that\\nis already proven unnecessary on the hot pipeline boundary.\\n\\nThe EX/MEM bundle still carries several fields that do not add architectural\\ninformation. `write_data` is always assigned the same post-forwarded rs2\\nvalue as `rs2_val`, while `branch_taken` and `branch_target` are registered\\nbut never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, forwarding, or hazard logic. Those bits\\nadd about 65 EX/MEM flops plus mux/load on the EX branch-target and rs2 data\\npaths, exactly where the post-DIV design is trying to preserve Fmax. Pruning\\nthis redundant metadata should not change CoreMark cycl...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-023-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-imem path whenever the external\\nbus is ready, and consult a tiny cached copy only on cycles where imem would\\notherwise stall. CoreMark's timed code repeatedly executes the same matrix,\\nlist, state, CRC, and loop-control instructions over a 10-iteration run, so\\neven a small direct-mapped instruction replay table should turn many random\\niStall bubbles into normal fetch cycles after warmup.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-im...\n  Line 32: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-025-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Segmented RVFI Order Counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large architectural wins:\\nDIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the one-deep store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. Recent micro-opt attempts that reshaped hot\\ndatapaths, predictor target logic, or EX/MEM metadata mostly lost Fmax to\\nplacement noise or new muxing. The next micro-opt should therefore target a\\nguaranteed always-present timing structure without changing IPC.\\n\\nThe top-level RVFI order counter is currently a single 64-bit increment:\\n`rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'b1` on every retirement. The FPGA\\nbenchmark wrapper intentionally retains RVFI fanout by XOR-reducing the RVFI\\noutputs into an LED, so this counter cannot be assumed dead in synthesis.\\nA 64-bit carry self-loop is not architectural work for the CoreMark datapath,\\nbut it can still consume carry-chain/routing budget and compete with the hot\\npipeline for median Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large architectural wins:\\nDIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the one-deep store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. Recent micro-opt attempts that reshaped hot\\ndatapaths, predictor target logic, or EX/MEM metadata mostly lost Fmax to\\nplacement noise or new muxing. The next micro-opt should therefore target a\\nguaranteed always-present timing structure without changing IPC.\\n\\nThe top-level RVFI order counter is currently a single 64-bit increment:\\n`rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'b1` on every retirement. The FPGA\\nbenchmark wrapper intentionally retains RVFI fanout by XOR-reducing the RVFI\\noutputs into an LED, so this counter cannot be assumed dead in synthesis.\\nA 64-bit carry self-loop is not architectural work for the CoreMark datapath,\\nbut it can still consume carry-chain/routing budget and compete with the hot\\npipeline for median Fmax.\\n\", ...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-026-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Narrow Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction side. The 256-entry stall-only replay cache cut CoreMark cycles\\nfrom 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, showing that random iStall bubbles are a large\\npart of the remaining IPC loss, but its wide IF table/tag/data mux dropped\\nmedian Fmax enough to miss the current best.\\n\\nThat result points to a narrower replay structure rather than a broader\\nfetch queue. CoreMark's timed hot code repeatedly executes compact inner\\nloops and call bodies, so a smaller direct-mapped replay table should retain\\nmuch of the iStall hit-rate benefit while reducing the IF storage mux,\\ncomparator fanout, and PC-control load that hurt Fmax in the 256-entry\\nversion. This keeps the zero-wait path direct and targets only cycles that\\nwould otherwise be external imem stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction side. The 256-entry stall-only replay cache cut CoreMark cycles\\nfrom 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, showing that random iStall bubbles are a large\\npart of the remaining IPC loss, but its wide IF table/tag/data mux dropped\\nmedian Fmax enough to miss the current best.\\n\\nThat result points to a narrower replay structure rather than a broader\\nfetch queue. CoreMark's timed hot code repeatedly executes compact inner\\nloops and call bodies, so a smaller direct-mapped replay t...\n  Line 35: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-028-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Byte-Segmented RVFI Order Counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a pure timing micro-optimization: replacing\\nthe monolithic 64-bit RVFI order increment with four 16-bit counter chunks.\\nThat