 comes from the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor: it preserves most of the 256-entry replay table's large\\nrandom-iStall cycle reduction while moving the same-cycle tag lookup off the\\nIF decision path. That win also makes the replay table one of the remaining\\nhigh-leverage timing structures. The table still stores a full 32-bit PC tag\\nfor every entry even though the direct-mapped index already encodes\\n`pc[9:2]` and all reachable fetch PCs are word-aligned. It also resets the\\ntag and instruction arrays even though the valid bits alone determine\\nwhether those payloads can be consumed.\\n\\nRecent micro-opt results show that exact timing-only cleanups can matter:\\nthe 16-bit segmented RVFI order counter improved Fmax without changing\\nCoreMark cycles, while broader datapath rewrites and smaller replay tables\\nlost either hit rate or placement quality. Compressing and de-resetting the\\nexisting replay metadata is the same kind of narrow micro-optimization: keep\\nthe current replay behavior and cycle count, but reduce IF table width,\\nreset fanout, and registered-candidate comparison logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best fitness comes from the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor: it preserves most of the 256-entry replay table's large\\nrandom-iStall cycle reduction while moving the same-cycle tag lookup off the\\nIF decision path. That win also makes the replay table one of the remaining\\nhigh-leverage timing structures. The table still stores a full 32-bit PC tag\\nfor every entry even though the direct-mapped index already encodes\\n`pc[9:2]` and all reachable fetch PCs are word-aligned. It also resets the\\ntag and instruction arrays even though the valid bits alone determine\\nwhether those payloads can be consumed.\\n\\nRecent micro-opt results show that exact timing-on...\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-032-r13s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Load Replay Behind I-Fetch Replay\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its large gain from the registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table: random iStall bubbles are often converted into\\nuseful fetches without putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF critical\\npath. That leaves the data side as the cleanest remaining structural stall\\nsource. A load still freezes the whole in-order backend whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even when the same normal-RAM word was recently read\\nin CoreMark's compact 6 KB working set.\\n\\nThe earlier standalone MEM-stage load replay experiment was the best\\nstructural signal after the store slot: it reduced CoreMark cycles with a\\nsmall, conservative table located away from the IF predictor path and did\\nnot require load data to feed the EX operand mux. It was not combined with\\nthe current registered instruction replay best. Combining the two targets\\nindependent random-stall sources: IF replay hides instruction stalls, while\\nMEM replay can retire repeated loads during data-memory stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its large gain from the registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table: random iStall bubbles are often converted into\\nuseful fetches without putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF critical\\npath. That leaves the data side as the cleanest remaining structural stall\\nsource. A load still freezes the whole in-order backend whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even when the same normal-RAM word was recently read\\nin CoreMark's compact 6 KB working set.\\n\\nThe earlier standalone MEM-stage load replay experiment was the best\\nstructural signal after the store slot: it reduced CoreMark cycles with a\\nsmall, conservative table located away from the IF predictor path and did\\nnot require load data to feed the EX operand mux. It was not combined with\\nthe current registered instruction replay best. Combinin...\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-035-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Registered MEM Load-Replay Candidate\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest IPC gain from registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay: repeated hot instructions can be supplied during random\\nimem stalls without putting a same-cycle replay-table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. The analogous remaining structural loss is on the data side.\\nLoads still freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low,\\neven when the same normal-RAM word was recently returned by dmem.\\n\\nThe earlier MEM-stage load replay experiment showed the opportunity: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles, but the combinational replay-table lookup, tag compare, and\\nstore merge logic in MEM lowered Fmax enough to miss the current best. The\\npromising structural angle is to keep the replay behavior while moving the\\nlookup off the MEM critical path, just as the successful IF replay predictor\\ndid for instruction stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest IPC gain from registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay: repeated hot instructions can be supplied during random\\nimem stalls without putting a same-cycle replay-table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. The analogous remaining structural loss is on the data side.\\nLoads still freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low,\\neven when the same normal-RAM word was recently returned by dmem.\\n\\nThe earlier MEM-stage load replay experiment showed the opportunity: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles, but the combinational replay-table lookup, tag compare, and\\nstore merge logic in MEM lowered Fmax enough to miss the current best. The\\npromising structural angle is to keep the replay behavior while moving the\\nlookup off the MEM critical path, just as the successful IF replay predictor\\ndid for instruction stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small normal-RAM load replay table with a registered candidate for the\\ninstruction entering MEM...\n  Line 43: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-036-r14s2\", \"title\": \"Phase-Aligned Replay Candidate\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result comes from the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay predictor. It keeps the large random-iStall\\ncycle win of the 256-entry replay table while avoiding a same-cycle table\\nread in IF. The predictor experiments since then show that adding more\\nbranch-direction or return-target state is not attractive: those changes\\nsaved a few cycles but lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor timing cost is inside the replay fast decision\\nitself. `if_stage` still carries a full 32-bit `replay_cand_pc_q` and gates\\nreplay hits with `replay_cand_pc_q == pc` before asserting `fetch_ready`.\\nThat compare is on the current IF stall/PC-enable path even though the\\ncandidate is already generated from the exact same redirect/stall/next-PC\\nselection that updates the PC register. After a clock edge, the registered\\nreplay candidate is phase-aligned with the current PC by construction.\\n\\nRemoving the redundant current-PC equality check is a narrower predictor\\nimprovement than changing table capacity or direction policy. It preserves\\nthe current replay hit rate and CoreMark cycle count while shortening the\\nfetch-ready path and dropping the 32-bit candidate-PC register.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result comes from the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay predictor. It keeps the large random-iStall\\ncycle win of the 256-entry replay table while avoiding a same-cycle table\\nread in IF. The predictor experiments since then show that adding more\\nbranch-direction or return-target state is not attractive: those changes\\nsaved a few cycles but lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor timing cost is inside the replay fast decision\\nitself. `if_stage` still carr