y MEM-stage load replay buffer showed that this stall source\\nis real: it reduced CoreMark cycles on top of the instruction replay design.\\nIt missed the current best because the same-cycle table lookup, tag compare,\\nand store-update logic dropped Fmax too much. A much smaller MRU replay latch\\ntargets the same dmem-stall opportunity while keeping the MEM path close to\\nthe current best design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry most-recently-used normal-RAM load replay latch in MEM. Each\\nentry stores a valid bit, an aligned word address tag, and the raw 32-bit word\\nreturned...\n  Line 52: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-045-r17s2\", \"title\": \"Registered Lookahead Agree Branch Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor win is the compressed registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table. It attacks random iStall cycles without putting a\\nsame-cycle replay lookup on the IF decision path. Other predictor attempts\\nshow the remaining tradeoff clearly: learned conditional-branch tables did\\nreduce CoreMark cycles, but their combinational IF table reads and EX update\\nrouting cost more Fmax than the saved redirects recovered. Return predictors\\nand replay capacity variants had the same problem or saved too few cycles.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore to revisit dynamic branch\\ndirection with the timing discipline that made instruction replay work.\\nKeep the existing static BTFNT plus direct-JAL predictor as the cold and\\ntarget-generation path, but move the learned direction-table lookup into a\\nregistered lookahead candidate for the next fetch PC. That preserves the\\ncurrent IF replay structure and avoids adding a current-cycle counter-array\\nread to the branch/JAL target and fetch-ready path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor win is the compressed registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table. It attacks random iStall cycles without putting a\\nsame