not ready, a younger store to the same aligned word does\\nnot need a second physical buffer entry. It can be merged into the pending\\nslot because loads and MMIO are still held behind the slot, and there is no\\narchitectural observer between the two normal-RAM stores. This targets the\\nsame store-burst pressure as the failed two-entry FIFO, but with one address\\ncompare and one byte-mask merge instead of another queue entry or a wider\\ndmem arbiter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already includes the structural wins that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot for normal-RAM store stalls\\nand a registered instruction replay table for random iStall. Broader store\\nbuffering attempts found the right bottleneck but were too expensive:\\ntwo-entry pending-store designs either failed placement, and the same-cycle\\nrefill variant also failed placement. That suggests the next structural\\nstore-side step should add capacity only for cases the existing single slot\\ncan handle locally.\\n\\nCoreMark updates list nodes, matrix cells, stack frames, and scalar state with\\nshort bursts of stores. When a ...\n  Line 61: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-054-r20s2\", \"title\": \"Banked Registered I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the 256-entry registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table with compressed resetless tags. It is the only\\npredictor-family change with a large measured cycle signal, because it turns\\nrandom imem stalls into useful hot-instruction replays while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts that added branch-direction state, return targets,\\nvictim entries, wider lines, cached next-PC metadata, or XOR indexing either\\nsaved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe best remaining replay opportunity is to reduce direct-mapped conflict\\nmisses without adding an associative lookup or changing the current-cycle\\nfetch-ready path. A full victim buffer added several tag compares and\\nregressed. XOR indexing changed conflicts but did not improve the measured\\nhit-rate/Fmax product. A banked direct-mapped predictor is a narrower\\nalternative: keep two small direct-mapped replay banks and choose between\\nthem with a simple per-set MRU bit during registered lookahead generation.\\nThe current cycle still consumes only the already registered replay\\ncandidate, so any extra bank selection work stays off the IF replay-hit path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the 256-entry registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table with compressed resetless tags. It is the only\\npredictor-family change with a large measured cycle signal, because it turns\\nrandom imem stalls into useful hot-instruction replays while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts that added branch-direction state, return targets,\\nvictim entries, wider lines, cached next-PC metadata, or XOR indexing either\\nsaved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe best...\n  Line 62: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-055-r21s0\", \"title\": \"Bank-Aware Replay Fill Select\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the banked registered instruction\\nreplay predictor plus the hot/cold ALU split. The replay predictor is now\\nboth the largest remaining IPC win and one of the most timing-sensitive IF\\nstructures. Follow-up replay experiments show that changing capacity,\\nindexing, victim search, or cached next-PC metadata usually loses more Fmax\\nthan it recovers, so the next micro-opt should keep the successful two-bank\\norganization and trim redundant logic inside it.\\n\\nThe current banked replay table still does two same-cycle tag comparisons for\\nevery live imem fill:\\n`replay_fill_bank0_match` and `replay_fill_bank1_match` re-read the current\\nPC's indexed set to decide whether to refresh bank 0, refresh bank 1, or\\nreplace the non-MRU bank. But the registered lookahead candidate for the\\ncurrent fetch PC was produced by comparing exactly those two banks on the\\nprevious cycle. If that candidate hits and its PC guard matches the current\\nPC, it already identifies that the word is present and which bank supplied\\nit. If it misses, the previous lookup already proved that neither bank held\\nthe current tag, so replacement can use the non-MRU bank directly.\\n\\nReusing the registered candidate's bank for live fills should remove two\\ncurrent-PC tag compare trees and part of the fill-bank muxing from the IF\\nreplay block while keeping the current replay hit rate, table size, MRU\\npolicy, candidate PC guard, and live-imem-first behavior intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the banked registered instruction\\nreplay predictor plus the hot/cold ALU split. The replay predictor is now\\nboth the largest remaining IPC win and one of the most timing-sensitive IF\\nstructures. Follow-up replay experiments show that changing capacity,\\nindexing, victim search, or cached next-PC metadata usually loses ...\n  Line 63: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-056-r21s1\", \"title\": \"Pending Store Slot Load Forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural pieces that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot hides normal-RAM store\\nbackpressure, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random iStall bubbles. The structural experiments since then show the\\nboundary clearly. Larger store buffers and same-cycle refill failed\\nplacement, same-word store coalescing did not reduce CoreMark cycles, and\\ndata replay tables recovered some load-stall cycles but cost too much Fmax.\\n\\nThe remaining store-slot bottleneck is narrower. When a normal-RAM store has\\nretired into the pending slot, the core currently blocks every younger memory\\noperation until the physical write drains, even if the younger operation is a\\nload of bytes that are already fully present in the pending store entry. That\\ncase does not require a second store-buffer entry or a dmem access: in\\nprogram order the store is already older and retired, so a same-word covered\\nload should read the store slot's bytes. CoreMark's stack, list, and state\\ncode can produce store/load reuse on nearby words, especially under random\\ndmem stalls that keep the slot valid for multiple cycles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural pieces that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot hides normal-RAM store\\nbackpressure, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random iStall bubbles. The structural experiments since then show the\\nboundary clearly. Larger store buffers and same-cycle refill failed\\nplacement, same-word store coalescing did not reduce CoreMark cycles, and\\ndata replay tables recovered some load-stall cycles but cost too much Fmax.\\n\\nThe remaining store-slot bottleneck is narrower. When a normal-RAM store has\\nretired into the pending slot, the core currentl...\n  Line 64: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-057-r21s2\", \"title\": \"Skewed-Bank I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the two-bank registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay table. It is the only predictor-family structure that has delivered a\\nlarge CoreMark gain and a strong Fmax result: it hides random imem stalls by\\nreplaying hot instructions, while the current IF cycle still chooses only\\nbetween live imem data and an already registered candidate.\\n\\nThe remaining replay misses are likely conflict misses. The current two-bank\\ndesign stores two tags per raw `pc[8:2]` set, so any hot instructions spaced\\nby 512-byte multiples still contend for the same two physical slots. Earlier\\nalternatives show the tradeoff boundary: a single-bank XOR index changed the\\nconflict pattern but gave up the benefit of two-way capacity, and a victim\\nbuffer added associative compares that cost more Fmax than the extra hits\\nrecovered. A skewed second bank targets the same conflict class with no extra\\ntable entries, no victim search, and no current-cycle table read.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the two-bank registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay table. It is the only predictor-family structure that has delivered a\\nlarge CoreMark gain and a strong Fmax result: it hides random imem stalls by\\nreplaying hot instructions, while the current IF cycle still chooses only\\nbetween live imem data and an already registered candidate.\\n\\nThe remaining replay misses are likely conflict misses. The current two-bank\\ndesign stores two tags per raw `pc[8:2]` set, so any hot instructions spaced\\nby 512-byte multiples still contend for the same two physical slots. Earlier\\nalternatives show the tradeoff boundary: a single-bank XOR index changed the\\nconflict pattern but gave up the benefit of two-way capacity, and a victim\\nbuffer added associative compares that cost more Fmax than the extra hits\\nrecovered. A skewed second bank...\n  Line 65: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-058-r22s0\", \"title\": \"Resetless Replay MRU State\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay predictor. It is the only predictor-family structure that has both\\nreduced random-iStall cycles and improved Fmax, and the successful\\ncompressed-tag follow-up showed that narrow timing cleanups inside this IF\\nreplay block can still move median fitness without changing CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nThe replay table already leaves tag and instruction payload arrays resetless\\nbehind valid bits, but the per-index MRU replacement bits are still\\nsynchronously reset. Those MRU bits are not architectural state and are not\\nneeded for correctness after reset: both replay-bank valid bits are cleared,\\nso the first live fill for a set may choose either bank. After that fill, the\\nMRU bit is immediately written to the bank that was filled and normal\\nreplacement behavior resumes.\\n\\nRemoving reset from this 128-bit replacement-state array is a narrower\\nversion of the reset-elision idea that has already worked for replay\\npayloads. It targets reset fanout and local IF replay placement pressure\\nwithout changing the current-cycle fetch-ready path, replay hit rate after\\nwarmup, branch/JAL prediction policy, ALU datapath, memory behavior, or RVFI\\nretirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay predictor. It is the only predictor-family structure that has both\\nreduced random-iStall cycles and improved Fmax, and the successful\\ncompressed-tag follow-up showed that narrow timing cleanups inside this IF\\nreplay block can still move median fitness without changing CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nThe replay table already leaves tag and instruction payload arrays resetless\\nbehind valid bits, but the per-index MRU replacement bits are still\\nsynchronously reset. Those MRU bits are not architectural state and are not\\nneeded for ...\n  Line 66: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-059-r22s1\", \"title\": \"Replay-Assisted ALU Pair Dual Issue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is now a fast single-issue design: cold DIV/REM is\\noff the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split improved Fmax, the one-entry\\nstore slot hides some dmem store stalls, and the two-bank registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall bubbles. The largest\\nremaining structural ceiling is therefore the single-retire backend. The\\ntop-level RVFI contract already exposes NRET=2, but channel 1 is unused.\\n\\nA normal dual-issue frontend would be unattractive because the external imem\\nport supplies only one 32-bit word per cycle. The banked replay predictor\\nchanges that tradeoff: after warmup, hot straight-line CoreMark code often\\nhas both the current instruction and the next sequential instruction in the\\nreplay table. That creates a narrow path to use RVFI channel 1 without\\nwidening the external memory interface or adding a full instruction cache.\\n\\nThe most promising structural angle is to issue a second instruction only\\nfor low-risk adjacent simple-integer pairs. This targets CoreMark's dense\\naddress, loop, CRC, and scalar arithmetic sequences while avoiding the\\nplacement failures seen from larger store buffers and the Fmax losses seen\\nfrom load replay tables, return predictors, and wider replay structures.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is now a fast single-issue design: cold DIV/REM is\\noff the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split improved Fmax, the one-entry\\nstore slot hides some dmem store stalls, and the two-bank registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall bubbles. The largest\\nremaining structural ceiling is therefore the single-retire backend. The\\ntop-level RVFI contract already exposes NRET=2, but channel 1 is unused.\\n\\nA normal dual-issue frontend would be unattractive because the external imem\\nport supplies only one 32-bit word per cycle. The b...\n  Line 67: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-060-r22s2\", \"title\": \"Stream-Resistant Replay Insertion\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the two-bank registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay table, which reached 577.76 fitness by hiding random imem stalls\\nwithout putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts show the remaining boundary clearly: adding more branch\\ndirection state, return prediction, victim search, skewed indexing, cached\\nnext-PC metadata, or bank-aware fill logic either saved too few cycles or\\ncost more Fmax than it recovered.\\n\\nThe least intrusive remaining replay opportunity is replacement policy. The\\ncurrent two-bank table uses a per-index MRU bit and marks every miss-filled\\nentry as MRU. That is a normal two-way cache policy, but it is vulnerable to\\none-shot or low-reuse instruction streams, wrong-path fetches, and cold helper\\ncode displacing hot-loop replay entries. CoreMark repeatedly executes compact\\nloops, so protecting the most recently hit replay word from streaming\\npollution may recover some random-iStall hits without adding a table, a tag\\ncompare, a branch update path, or any current-cycle fetch-ready logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the two-bank registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay table, which reached 577.76 fitness by hiding random imem stalls\\nwithout putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts show the remaining boundary clearly: adding more branch\\ndirection state, return prediction, victim search, skewed indexing, cached\\nnext-PC metadata, or bank-aware fill logic either saved too few cycles or\\ncost more Fmax than it recovered.\\n\\nThe least intrusive remaining replay opportunity is replacement policy. The\\ncurrent two-bank table uses a per-index MRU bit and marks every miss-filled\\nentry as MRU. That is a normal two-way cache policy, but it is vulnerable to\\none-shot or low-reuse...\n  Line 68: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-061-r23s0\", \"title\": \"Power-of-Two RAM Range Decode\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large timing wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split simplified the common\\nresult mux, the RVFI order counter is segmented, and the banked registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall loss. Recent broader\\nmicro-opt attempts that changed replay replacement, reset policy, ALU\\nfunct3 encoding, or source-qualified hazards tended to lose Fmax through\\nplacement noise or extra muxing. The next micro_opt should therefore be a\\nvery narrow equivalence rewrite on logic that is still always present.\\n\\nThe MEM stage still classifies bufferable stores with an unsigned 32-bit\\ncomparison against `32'h0010_0000`. That limit is exactly 1 MiB, so for a\\n32-bit byte address the predicate is equivalent to `addr[31:20] == 0`.\\nThe result feeds store-slot enqueue and MEM backpressure, which in turn\\ndrives the global stall network. Replacing the generic less-than form with\\nan explicit high-bit decode should remove carry/comparator structure from\\nthe store-bufferable path without changing the direct ready-store behavior,\\npending-store semantics, memory ordering, or CoreMark cycle count.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large timing wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split simplified the common\\nresult mux, the RVFI order counter is segmented, and the banked registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall loss. Recent broader\\nmicro-opt attempts that changed replay replacement, reset policy, ALU\\nfunct3 encoding, or source-qualified hazards tended to lose Fmax through\\nplacement noise or extra muxing. The next micro_opt should therefore be a\\nvery narrow equivalence rewrite on logic that is still always present.\\n\\nThe MEM stage still classifies bufferable stores with an unsigned ...\n  Line 69: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-062-r23s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Wait Simple-ALU Skid Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is a fast single-issue design with the largest proven\\nwins already in place: cold DIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU\\nsplit improved Fmax, the one-entry pending-store slot hides some store-only\\ndmem stalls, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random imem stalls. The remaining structural loss is therefore mainly\\nbackend pressure from dmem waits, especially loads. Today a load in MEM with\\n`io_dmemReady=0` freezes IF, ID, and EX even when the younger instruction in\\nID/EX is an independent simple ALU operation whose operands are already\\navailable and whose execution has no memory or control-flow side effects.\\n\\nPrior attempts to attack this bottleneck by replaying load data or adding\\nbroader store buffering reduced cycles but cost too much Fmax or placement\\nquality. A smaller structural decoupling point is more promising: let EX do\\none cycle of useful independent simple-ALU work while the older MEM load or\\nnonbufferable store is waiting, but keep retirement strictly in order. This\\ntargets the exact full-pipeline freeze caused by random dmem backpressure\\nwithout adding load data to EX, another replay table, another store-buffer\\nentry, a branch predictor, or a general superscalar backend.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is a fast single-issue design with the largest proven\\nwins already in place: cold DIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU\\nsplit improved Fmax, the one-entry pending-store slot hides some store-only\\ndmem stalls, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random imem stalls. The remaining structural loss is therefore mainly\\nbackend pressure from dmem waits, especially loads. Today a load in MEM with\\n`io_dmemReady=0` freezes IF, ID, and EX even when the younger instruction in\\nID/EX is an independent simp...\n  Line 70: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-063-r23s2\", \"title\": \"Low-Bit Static Target Alignment Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result is the two-bank registered instruction replay\\npredictor plus the hot/cold ALU split. The replay predictor is the only\\npredictor-family structure that has produced a large CoreMark cycle win and\\na strong Fmax result. Follow-up predictor attempts that added more state,\\nchanged replacement, added victim/skewed indexing, cached next-PC metadata,\\nor added branch-direction tables generally lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe next predictor hypothesis should therefore avoid new predictor memories\\nand preserve the successful two-bank replay structure. The remaining\\nlow-risk predictor timing issue is in the zero-state static control\\npredictor itself: IF currently waits for the full branch and JAL target\\nadders to produce `[1:0]` before deciding whether the predicted target is\\nword-aligned. For B-type and J-type immediates, bit 0 is always zero and bit\\n1 of the target can be derived from `pc[1]` and one encoded immediate bit.\\n\\nA previous single-adder static-control rewrite removed a target adder but\\nintroduced a selected-immediate mux before the target add and regressed. This\\nhypothesis is narrower: keep the existing branch and JAL target adders, but\\nremove their low-bit outputs from the prediction-enable path. That should\\nkeep CoreMark cycles identical while shortening the IF predictor select path\\nin the current best frontend.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result is the two-bank registered instruction replay\\npredictor plus the hot/cold ALU split. The replay predictor is the only\\npredictor-family structure that has produced a large CoreMark cycle win and\\na strong Fmax result. Follow-up predictor attempts that added more state,\\nchanged replacement, added victim/skewed indexing, cached next-PC metadata,\\nor added branch-direction tables generally lost more Fmax than they recovered...\n  Line 71: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-064-r24s0\", \"title\": \"Unsigned Multiplier With Signed High Correction\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already harvested the broad timing wins: DIV/REM is\\niterative, the ALU has a hot/cold RV32I/RV32M split, RVFI order is segmented,\\nand the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall cycles. The next micro_opt should therefore avoid new predictor state,\\nqueues, reset rewrites, or latency changes and instead trim logic still\\npresent in the hot EX arithmetic block.\\n\\n`rtl/alu.sv` still builds three independent 64-bit products for RV32M\\nmultiply: unsigned*unsigned, signed*signed, and signed*unsigned. CoreMark's\\ntimed region needs low-half MUL, but high-half MULH/MULHU/MULHSU are cold.\\nPrior multiplier experiments showed what not to do: a muxed 33x33 shared\\nmultiplier badly hurt Fmax, and moving high-half multiply to a long-latency\\nunit also regressed. A better micro-optimization is to keep the existing\\nunsigned 32x32 product for the hot MUL path and derive the signed high halves\\nwith narrow correction subtracts after the product.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already harvested the broad timing wins: DIV/REM is\\niterative, the ALU has a hot/cold RV32I/RV32M split, RVFI order is segmented,\\nand the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall cycles. The next micro_opt should therefore avoid new predictor state,\\nqueues, reset rewrites, or latency changes and instead trim logic still\\npresent in the hot EX arithmetic block.\\n\\n`rtl/alu.sv` still builds three independent 64-bit products for RV32M\\nmultiply: unsigned*unsigned, signed*signed, and signed*unsigned. CoreMark's\\ntimed region needs low-half MUL, but high-half MULH/MULHU/MULHSU are cold.\\nPrior multiplier experiments showed what not to do: a muxed 33x33 shared\\nmultiplier badly hurt Fmax, and moving high-half multiply to a long-latency\\nunit also regressed. A b...\n  Line 72: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-065-r24s1\", \"title\": \"Replay-Fed Memory Plus ADDI Pair Issue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is a very strong single-issue design. The large wins\\nalready come from removing cold DIV/REM from the hot EX path, splitting the\\nALU hot/cold mux, adding a one-deep pending-store slot, segmenting the RVFI\\norder counter, and using a two-bank registered instruction replay predictor\\nto hide most random imem stalls. Recent structural attempts show that broad\\nbuffers, data replay tables, and general dual issue are too costly or too\\ndifficult, but they also point at the remaining ceiling: only one instruction\\ncan retire per cycle even when CoreMark presents simple pointer updates next\\nto memory operations.\\n\\nCoreMark's list, matrix, state, and stack code is dense with patterns such as\\na load or store followed by an independent ADDI pointer/index/stack update.\\nA full two-wide machine is risky, but this very narrow pair class uses only\\nthe existing single dmem access plus one younger simple immediate add. It\\ntargets a common structural bubble source without adding another memory port,\\nanother replay table, a load-data-to-EX bypass, a branch predictor, or a\\nsecond general ALU issue path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is a very strong single-issue design. The large wins\\nalready come from removing cold DIV/REM from the hot EX path, splitting the\\nALU hot/cold mux, adding a one-deep pending-store slot, segmenting the RVFI\\norder counter, and using a two-bank registered instruction replay predictor\\nto hide most random imem stalls. Recent structural attempts show that broad\\nbuffers, data replay tables, and general dual issue are too costly or too\\ndifficult, but they also point at the remaining ceiling: only one instruction\\ncan retire per cycle even when CoreMark presents simple pointer updates next\\nto memory operations.\\n\\nCoreMark's list, matrix, state, and stack code is dense with patter...\n  Line 73: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-066-r24s2\", \"title\": \"Registered Lookahead JALR Target Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay table. It is the only predictor-family mechanism that has produced a\\nlarge CoreMark cycle win without putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. Follow-up replay changes, direction tables, return stacks,\\nvictim buffers, skewed indexes, and cached static next-PC metadata mostly\\nlost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining control-flow hole is narrower: JALR still resolves only in EX.\\nEvery return and indirect call therefore pays the wrong-path recovery cost\\neven when the target is stable from one execution of that JALR PC to the\\nnext. Earlier return predictors showed that JALR bubbles exist, but they put\\ncurrent-cycle compares or stack bookkeeping directly in IF. The successful\\nreplay predictor suggests a better timing shape: look up any target state one\\ncycle ahead, register a candidate for the current fetch PC, and keep the\\ncurrent IF cycle dependent only on that candidate plus the fetched\\ninstruction's opcode bits.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay table. It is the only predictor-family mechanism that has produced a\\nlarge CoreMark cycle win without putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. Follow-up replay changes, direction tables, return stacks,\\nvictim buffers, skewed indexes, and cached static next-PC metadata mostly\\nlost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining control-flow hole is narrower: JALR still resolves only in EX.\\nEvery return and indirect call therefore pays the wrong-path recovery cost\\neven when the target is stable from one execution of that JALR PC to the\\nnext. Earlier return predictors showed that JALR bubbles exist, but they put\\ncurrent-cycle compares or stack bookkeeping directly in IF. ...\n  Line 74: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-068-r25s1\", \"title\": \"Single-Entry Last-Load Replay Latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural wins that have survived the\\nFPGA gate: the one-deep pending-store slot hides some store backpressure, and\\nthe two-bank registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall bubbles. The remaining structural stalls are mainly data-side load\\nwaits. When a normal-RAM load reaches MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, the\\nwhole in-order backend freezes even if the same aligned word was just read by\\nan earlier load.\\n\\nLarger load replay attempts proved that this opportunity is real because they\\nreduced CoreMark cycles, but their table lookups, tag compares, and store\\nupdate paths cost too much Fmax. The next structural bet should keep only the\\ncheapest useful form of that idea: one last-load replay entry. CoreMark's\\nstack, list, state, and matrix kernels often reload nearby or identical words\\nacross short loops, so a single MRU word can catch some random dStall cases\\nwithout a table, replacement policy, associative search, or load-data path\\ninto EX.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural wins that have survived the\\nFPGA gate: the one-deep pending-store slot hides some store backpressure, and\\nthe two-bank registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall bubbles. The remaining structural stalls are mainly data-side load\\nwaits. When a normal-RAM load reaches MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, the\\nwhole in-order backend freezes even if the same aligned word was just read by\\nan earlier load.\\n\\nLarger load replay attempts proved that this opportunity is real because they\\nreduced CoreMark cycles, but their table lookups, tag compares, and store\\nupdate paths cost too much Fmax. The next structural bet should keep only the\\ncheapest useful form of that idea: one last-load replay entry. CoreMark's\\nstack, list, state, and matrix ke...","metadata":{"matches":53,"truncated":false},"title":"improvement.*structural|structural.*improvement|One-Deep Store|divider|prefetch|scoreboard|branch resolve|operand latch|skid|queue","time":{"start":1778879463500,"end":1778879463537}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_093f311838bf58a2016a078be758b8819aabb6dd62c4a6c619"}},"id":"prt_e2d7a8049001htKmlOpMvKx8O5","sessionID":"ses_1d28633a4ffexDsv3Er74Ax1zs","messageID":"msg_e2d7a647d001xwAkuhdpCEMTQ9"}}
