{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778882425900,"sessionID":"ses_1d25b7e98ffeiWzznDDW6uMkiU","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_rLYcvmmSRkBRKJjDluaCug8m","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r15s0.yaml","offset":1,"limit":200},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r15s0.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: id: hyp-20260515-001-r15s0\n2: title: \"Serial-Scrub LUTRAM Register File\"\n3: category: micro_opt\n4: motivation: |\n5:   The remaining `bench` micro-opt space is narrow: fetch-side ideas have been\n6:   consistently Fmax-negative, hazard relaxations have been correctness-sensitive,\n7:   and the last regfile attempt regressed because it traded the wide reset tree for\n8:   extra read-path muxing through a `valid_q` shadow. That still leaves the core's\n9:   synchronously-reset 32x32 flop regfile as one of the few always-live structures\n10:   likely blocking cleaner LUTRAM inference and adding routing pressure on every\n11:   cycle.\n12: \n13:   A better regfile-local optimization is to remove reset from the storage array\n14:   without adding any new per-read validity mux on the hot path.\n15: hypothesis: |\n16:   Rework `rtl/reg_file.sv` around an unreset RAM-style 2R1W array with the same\n17:   combinational reads, x0 hardwiring, and same-cycle write-first bypass, but use a\n18:   tiny internal post-reset scrub sequencer instead of a shadow valid bitmap. After\n19:   external reset deasserts, the regfile would spend 32 cycles writing zeros through\n20:   its normal single write port, one architectural register at a time, while the\n21:   rest of the core stays in an internal reset/hold state and retires nothing.\n22: \n23:   This keeps architectural post-reset behavior identical, avoids the read-path\n24:   `valid_q` gating that likely caused `r14s0` to regress, and gives synthesis a\n25:   cleaner chance to map the storage array to LUTRAM or otherwise shed the current\n26:   1024-bit reset network. CoreMark's timed region should not notice the startup\n27:   scrub, so any Fmax gain drops directly into fitness.\n28: expected_impact:\n29:   fitness_delta_pct: 4\n30:   confidence: medium\n31: changes:\n32:   - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n33:     description: \"Replace the fully resettable flop array with an unreset RAM-style register file plus a 32-cycle zero-scrub sequencer, while preserving x0 and write-first bypass semantics.\"\n34:   - file: rtl/core.sv\n35:     description: \"Hold the pipeline, RVFI retirement, and writeback side idle behind an internal reset/initialization busy signal until the regfile scrub completes.\"\n\n(End of file - total 35 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260515-001-r15s0\ntitle: \"Serial-Scrub LUTRAM Register File\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The remaining `bench` micro-opt space is narrow: fetch-side ideas have been\n  consistently Fmax-negative, hazard relaxations have been correctness-sensitive,\n  and the last regfile attempt regressed because it traded the wide reset tree for\n  extra read-path muxing through a `valid_q` shadow. That still leaves the core's\n  synchronously-reset 32x32 flop regfile as one of the few always-live structures\n  likely blocking cleaner LUTRAM inference and adding routing pressure on every\n  cycle.\n\n  A better regfile-local optimization is to remove reset from the storage array\n  without adding any new per-read validity mux on the hot path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Rework `rtl/reg_file.sv` around an unreset RAM-style 2R1W array with the same\n  combinational reads, x0 hardwiring, and same-cycle write-first bypass, but use a\n  tiny internal post-reset scrub sequencer instead of a shadow valid bitmap. After\n  external reset deasserts, the regfile would spend 32 cycles writing zeros through\n  its normal single write port, one architectural register at a time, while the","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r15s0.yaml","time":{"start":1778882425880,"end":1778882425900}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0129a6ef59a2ca77016a079779be58819ab426ed1db2caf7c1"}},"id":"prt_e2da7b405001gwOu44CyTdVq9I","sessionID":"ses_1d25b7e98ffeiWzznDDW6uMkiU","messageID":"msg_e2da791de001SYlse4wYUg2fmx"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778882425903,"sessionID":"ses_1d25b7e98ffeiWzznDDW6uMkiU","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_OfP7iMdrOHMRKeskL4hcKBRS","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260515-002-r15s1.log","offset":1,"limit":200},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260515-002-r15s1.log</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1778882217960,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d25b7e98ffeiWzznDDW6uMkiU\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_e2da487e60015fmx3FUQAQnlnO\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2da481f8001JdTEYjUbNDmYHe\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d25b7e98ffeiWzznDDW6uMkiU\",\"snapshot\":\"7fc99072b896b95c42103984b447546424d951ca\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n2: {\"type\":\"text\",\"timestamp\":1778882233056,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d25b7e98ffeiWzznDDW6uMkiU\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_e2da4af44001Ru4t2L0Jnf144V\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2da481f8001JdTEYjUbNDmYHe\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d25b7e98ffeiWzznDDW6uMkiU\",\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Checking prior structural attempts and the current bench pipeline to pick one structural change with the best odds.\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778882228036,\"end\":1778882233051},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"msg_053dcad8ff8c744d016a0796b3e9e0819b999fa9402f0c2889\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"}}}}\n3: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778882233122,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d25b7e98ffeiWzznDDW6uMkiU\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_YDq8MxY3sLFLDhVZ2AjlbujR\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"fold|predict|queue|replay|branch|jal|jalr|store slot|store_slot|deferred-store|div|divider|load-use|structural\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 41 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Iterative Cold Divider Off EX Path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The frozen bench core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\\\ncombinational ALU path as ADD, address generation, branch support, and\\\\nMUL. On a Tang Nano 20K, a full 32-bit divider is a strong candidate for\\\\nthe universal EX-stage critical path, yet CoreMark's timed loop is mostly\\\\nmultiply, load/store, and branch traffic and should pay little or no steady\\\\nstate penalty from slower divide instructions.\\\\n\\\\nThis makes divider extraction the highest-leverage micro-opt still missing\\\\nfrom the frozen bench fixture: it targets Fmax directly while preserving the\\\\nhot one-cycle behavior for the instructions that dominate CoreMark.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The frozen bench core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\\\ncombinational ALU path as ADD, address generation, branch support, and\\\\nMUL. On a Tang Nano 20K, a full 32-bit divider is a strong candidate for\\\\nthe universal EX-stage critical path, yet CoreMark's timed loop is mostly\\\\nmultiply, load/store, and branch traffic and should pay little or no steady\\\\nstate penalty from slower divide instructions.\\\\n\\\\nThis makes divider extraction the highest-leverage micro-opt still missing\\\\nfrom the frozen bench fixture: it targets Fmax directly while... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n4: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778882233125,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d25b7e98ffeiWzznDDW6uMkiU\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_x9Bx0qCtGhQjb3p3OWQkxymn\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"category[\\\"']?\\\\s*[:=]\\\\s*[\\\"']structural[\\\"']|\\\"category\\\":\\\"structural\\\"|category: structural\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 14 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Port v1-proven iterative divider into EX\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench baseline still carries real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operators\\\\ninside the always-live combinational ALU. Archived bench runs on this same\\\\nbaseline show that moving divider-class ops off the fast EX path was the\\\\nbiggest structural win, because CoreMark almost never uses integer division\\\\nwhile the FPGA still pays its timing and LUT cost on every cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench baseline still carries real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operators\\\\ninside the always-live combinational ALU. Archived bench runs on this same\\\\nbaseline show that moving divider-class ops off the fast EX path was the\\\\nbiggest structural win, because CoreMark almost never uses integer division\\\\nwhile the FPGA still pays its timing and LUT cost on every cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the already-proven iterative divider structure from cores/v1 and\\\\nintegrate it only for DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU, leaving the existing single-cycle\\\\nRV32I and MUL-family datapaths intact. alu.sv stops synthesizing real\\\\ndivider hardware; ex_stage.sv launches the divider for divide-class ops,\\\\nholds the in-flight EX payload until completion, and commits exactly one\\\\nEX/MEM result with the original RVFI metadata and RV32M corner-case be... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n5: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778882233146,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d25b7e98ffeiWzznDDW6uMkiU\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_EAhxyMDaG2384pfvYkHXNd0e\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":400},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.23%): Pulling DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the one-cycle EX ALU and handling them with a stall-on-use iterative unit can buy large Fmax gains because CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by MUL, load/store, and branch traffic, so rare divide stalls cost less than keeping a 32-bit divider on the common critical path.\\n2: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, -14.32%): On this core's combinational IF/ID path, adding ID-stage BTFNT and always-taken JAL prediction pulled decode, immediate-target add, and next-PC select onto the fetch critical path, and the resulting Fmax loss outweighed any CoreMark loop-branch bubble savings.\\n3: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.57%): After DIV/REM already moved to `rtl/div_unit.sv`, splitting `alu_op` into hot/cold classes and replacing one flat ALU result `case` with separate `hot_result`/`m_result` muxes plus an `op[4]` final select regressed, suggesting bench's real EX critical path is elsewhere and extra class-select structure can cost more Fmax than it saves.\\n4: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (improvement, +10.45%): Backend-only buffering of aligned normal-RAM stores is a good lever on this RTL because CoreMark issues enough stack/list/matrix/state stores that turning `io_dmemReady=0` on STOREs from a whole-pipeline freeze into a one-deep deferred write raises IPC, as long as younger memory ops and end-visible markers stay fenced behind the pending slot... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n6: {\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\"