tion is in MEM. Stores are roughly 20-25% of the dynamic\\ninstruction stream and the cosim model injects ~22% random dmem stalls, so about\\n5% of total cycles are wasted waiting for the store bus. Because stores do not\\nproduce register results, the instructions behind them are almost always\\nindependent and could continue executing if the store were decoupled. A 1-entry\\nstore buffer captures a stalled store and lets the pipeline advance while the\\nbuffer drains asynchronously, converting store-bus stalls into useful execution\\ncycles with minimal area and no new long combinational paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a 1-entry store buffer between the MEM stage and the external dmem interface.\\nWhen a store reaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, the buffer captures the address,\\ndata, and byte mask instead of asserting a global stall. The buffer continuously\\nretries the write whenever dmem_ready is high. The top-level dmem outputs are\\nmuxed between the buffer (when valid) and the live M...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"(slot 1 hypothesis-gen failed)\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"hypothesis_gen_failed: Hypothesis agent did not write a file for slot hyp-20260514-002-r12s1 (allowed_yaml_ids=['hyp-20260514-001-r12s0', 'hyp-20260514-002-r12s1', 'hyp-20260514-003-r12s2'])\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 12}\n\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"(slot 1 hypothesis-gen failed)\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"hypothesis_gen_failed: Hypothesis agent did not write a file for slot hyp-20260514-002-r13s1 (allowed_yaml_ids=['hyp-20260514-001-r13s0', 'hyp-20260514-002-r13s1', 'hyp-20260514-003-r13s2'])\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 13}\n\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"(slot 1 hypothesis-gen failed)\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"hypothesis_gen_failed: Hypothesis agent did not write a file for slot hyp-20260514-002-r14s1 (allowed_yaml_ids=['hyp-20260514-001-r14s0', 'hyp-20260514-002-r14s1', 'hyp-20260514-003-r14s2'])\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 14}\n\n  Line 45: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-002-r15s1\", \"title\": \"(slot 1 hypothesis-gen failed)\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"hypothesis_gen_failed: Hypothesis agent modified off-limits paths and was rolled back: ['cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv']\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 15, \"scribe_skipped\": \"PermissionError: scribe rewrote /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/kimi-k2_6-rep1/cores/bench/LESSONS.md instead of appending; reverted to prior content\"}\n\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep3/log.jsonl:\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV/REM to multicycle EX unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and\\nremainder logic in the single-cycle EX path. On a small FPGA this is likely\\na major LUT and timing cost even though CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by\\nadds, compares, loads, stores, branches, and multiplies rather than divides.\\nRetiming only DIV/REM into a small multicycle unit should raise median Fmax\\nand reduce routing pressure while preserving single-cycle behavior for the\\ncommon integer operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and\\nremainder logic in the single-cycle EX path. On a small FPGA this is likely\\na major LUT and timing cost even though CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by\\nadds, compares, loads, stores, branches, and multiplies rather than divides.\\nRetiming only DIV/REM into a small multicycle unit should raise median Fmax\\nand reduce routing pressure while preserving single-cycle behavior for the\\ncommon integer operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split RV32M division and remainder out of rtl/alu.sv into a dedicated\\niterative divider/remainder unit issued by EX. When EX sees DIV, DIVU, REM, or\\nREMU, it should hold IF/ID/EX, keep the operation's operands and RVFI payload\\nstable, and release one EX/MEM result only when the unit completes. MUL/MULH\\noperations remain in the existing combinational ALU so CoreMark's multiply\\nthroughput is not sacrificed. The added rare-operation stalls should cost few\\nCoreMark cycles, while the shorter common EX critical path should improve the\\nFmax side of the fitness metric.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an iterative RV32 DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU unit with RV32-defined divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remo...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Share M-extension multiplier datapath\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The successful structural change already removed DIV/REM from the common EX\\ncritical path, leaving the ALU's parallel multiplier bank as a likely large\\ntiming and routing cost. The current ALU computes signed*signed,\\nunsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned 64-bit products in parallel even\\nthough only one MUL-family result can be selected for a retiring instruction.\\nCoreMark likely uses MUL often enough that making multiplication multicycle is\\nrisky, but sharing the single-cycle datapath should reduce LUT/DSP routing\\npressure without adding any stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The successful structural change already removed DIV/REM from the common EX\\ncritical path, leaving the ALU's parallel multiplier bank as a likely large\\ntiming and routing cost. The current ALU computes signed*signed,\\nunsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned 64-bit products in parallel even\\nthough only one MUL-family result can be selected for a retiring instruction.\\nCoreMark likely uses MUL often enough that making multiplication multicycle is\\nrisky, but sharing the single-cycle datapath should reduce LUT/DSP routing\\npressure without adding any stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU product trees in rtl/alu.sv\\nwith one sign-configurable 33x33 multiplier. Select the sign extension of each\\noperand from the ALU op, use the low 32 bits for MUL, and use product[63:32]\\nfor the high-half variants. Keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions and the\\nexisting DIV/REM handoff unchanged. This preserves the current five-stage\\nstructure and single-cycle MUL throughput while trimming a high-fanout EX-stage\\ndatapath, so any Fmax improvement should translate directly into better\\nCoreMark iter/sec.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Collapse...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Add sequential prefetch FIFO\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end directly couples decode consumption to raw imemReady: every\\ninstruction-memory stall injects a bubble, and backend stalls also stop the PC\\neven when the instruction bus could have fetched useful sequential work. Prior\\npredictor attempts showed that adding target decode/BTB logic on the IF path can\\nlose Fmax, so the safer structural opportunity is to decouple fetch from decode\\nwithout predicting control flow.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end directly couples decode consumption to raw imemReady: every\\ninstruction-memory stall injects a bubble, and backend stalls also stop the PC\\neven when the instruction bus could have fetched useful sequential work. Prior\\npredictor attempts showed that adding target decode/BTB logic on the IF path can\\nlose Fmax, so the safer structural opportunity is to decouple fetch from decode\\nwithout predicting control flow.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny two-entry fall-through sequential prefetch FIFO in the IF stage that\\nstores fetched {pc,instr} pairs. When ID/EX is stalled by load-use, dmem, or DIV\\nbackpressure, the IF side should keep requesting sequential instructions while\\nFIFO space remains; when imemReady drops but the FIFO is non-empty, decode can\\ncontinue from the buffered head instead of taking an immediate bubble. On any\\nEX redirect, flush the FIFO and restart fetch at the resolved target, preserving\\nthe existing no-predictor control-flow semantics and exact RVFI pc/instruction\\nordering. This should improve CoreMark iterations/cycle under the 22 percent\\nrandomized iStall+dStall methodology while adding only small FIFO control muxes,\\nnot branch target adders or BTB lookup logic, to the front end.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the PC-only fetch path with a ...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Add conservative store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Prior structural and predictor attempts show that adding more IF-side control\\nhurts this core, while the benchmark still runs with randomized dmem stalls.\\nStores currently freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever dmemReady is low,\\neven though normal memory stores produce no register result and many following\\nALU/control instructions do not need the data port immediately.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior structural and predictor attempts show that adding more IF-side control\\nhurts this core, while the benchmark still runs with randomized dmem stalls.\\nStores currently freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever dmemReady is low,\\neven though normal memory stores produce no register result and many following\\nALU/control instructions do not need the data port immediately.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry store buffer in the MEM side that accepts aligned normal-memory\\nstores when dmemReady is low, retires the store through the existing MEM/WB and\\nRVFI path, and drains the buffered write on the dmem port as soon as the bus is\\nready. Keep all MMIO stores, including CoreMark start/stop markers and UART, on\\nthe current blocking path so externally observed timing is unchanged. While the\\nbuffer is full, stall younger loads and stores until it drains, but allow younger\\nnon-memory instructions to keep flowing. This targets CoreMark cycles lost to\\nrandom store backpressure without adding branch prediction, fetch FIFO, or other\\nfront-end critical-path logic.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry buffered normal-memory store path, drain muxing for dmem outputs, and MMIO/blocking-store bypass rules.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single dmem_stall condition with load/MMIO-store/buffer-full blocking while al...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Add MEM-to-EX load bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The recent hazard-only source-use interlock removed false load-use stalls, but\\ntrue load-use pairs still always pay a bubble even when the data memory is\\nready. CoreMark is load-heavy, and the benchmark injects randomized dmem\\nstalls, so letting a dependent instruction wait in EX and consume the load as\\nsoon as the MEM-stage data is available can improve iterations per cycle\\nwithout adding the front-end predictor, FIFO, or store-buffer structures that\\nregressed in prior rounds.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The recent hazard-only source-use interlock removed false load-use stalls, but\\ntrue load-use pairs still always pay a bubble even when the data memory is\\nready. CoreMark is load-heavy, and the benchmark injects randomized dmem\\nstalls, so letting a dependent instruction wait in EX and consume the load as\\nsoon as the MEM-stage data is available can improve iterations per cycle\\nwithout adding the front-end predictor, FIFO, or store-buffer structures that\\nregressed in prior rounds.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a combinational MEM-to-EX forwarding source for aligned ready loads. The\\nMEM stage should expose the same sign/zero-extended load value it will commit\\ninto MEM/WB, and the forwarding unit should select that value for a younger\\ndependent instruction when EX/MEM holds a valid load whose dmem transaction is\\nready and non-trapping. The load-use interlock should then stall only when the\\nload result cannot be forwarded, such as dmemReady being low or the access\\nbeing misaligned; otherwise the dependent instruction advances into EX and\\nreceives the load value through the normal operand muxes. This preserves the\\nnret=1 RVFI retirement boundary and architectural rs fields while targeting\\none of the remaining CoreMark CPI costs.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"d...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Register writeback payload in MEM/WB\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added prediction, queues, store buffering, or\\nsame-cycle load bypassing all paid more timing/control cost than CoreMark\\nrecovered in CPI. The remaining safer structural opportunity is to shorten an\\nexisting high-fanout pipeline boundary without adding new stalls or state\\nmachines. Today the MEM/WB register carries both ALU and load data, then the\\nWB-stage mux drives regfile writeback, MEM/WB forwarding, and RVFI rd_wdata in\\nthe same cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added prediction, queues, store buffering, or\\nsame-cycle load bypassing all paid more timing/control cost than CoreMark\\nrecovered in CPI. The remaining safer structural opportunity is to shorten an\\nexisting high-fanout pipeline boundary without adding new stalls or state\\nmachines. Today the MEM/WB register carries both ALU and load data, then the\\nWB-stage mux drives regfile writeback, MEM/WB forwarding, and RVFI rd_wdata in\\nthe same cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Fold the final writeback-data selection into the MEM/WB registered payload.\\nThe MEM stage already has the load result, ALU result, and mem_to_reg control\\nwhen it clocks the retirement bundle, so it can register a single rd_wdata\\nfield alongside the existing RVFI fields. The WB stage can then become a thin\\nvalid/reg_write/rd gate that forwards this registered payload directly to the\\nregfile and existing forwarding path. This preserves the five-stage schedule,\\nnret=1 RVFI contract, load-use behavior, and all trap/memory semantics while\\nremoving a 32-bit mux from the writeback-to-ID bypass and RVFI output cones.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered rd_wdata/writeback-result field to mem_wb_t so the retirement boundary carries the final re...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Precompute PC targets in ID\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added front-end speculation, FIFOs, store buffering,\\nor same-cycle load bypassing regressed because the added control landed on already\\nsensitive paths. The last successful structural change was a pure pipeline-boundary\\nretime that removed a mux from writeback without changing CPI. After that, the EX\\nredirect path still recomputes pc+imm for every branch and direct jump in the same\\ncycle as branch comparison and PC redirection.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added front-end speculation, FIFOs, store buffering,\\nor same-cycle load bypassing regressed because the added control landed on already\\nsensitive paths. The last successful structural change was a pure pipeline-boundary\\nretime that removed a mux from writeback without changing CPI. After that, the EX\\nredirect path still recomputes pc+imm for every branch and direct jump in the same\\ncycle as branch comparison and PC redirection.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a registered PC-relative target field to the ID/EX payload, computed in ID as\\npc+imm using the existing decoded immediate. EX should use this registered value for\\nconditional branch targets, direct JAL targets, and AUIPC results, while keeping JALR\\ntarget calculation in EX because it depends on forwarded rs1. This does not predict\\nor redirect earlier, so branch penalty, RVFI pc_wdata, trap behavior, stalls, and the\\nnret=1 port contract stay unchanged. The expected win is a shorter EX-to-IF redirect\\ntiming path and less duplicate 32-bit adder/routing pressure, with only one pipeline\\npayload field added.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered pc_target field to id_ex_t for the decoded pc+imm value.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute pc+imm beside the...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Register IF/ID fetch boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end has no real IF/ID register: io_imemData feeds the\\ndecoder, immediate generator, register-file read addresses, hazard predecode,\\nand then the ID/EX register in the same cycle. In the FPGA Fmax harness that\\npath is driven by a live LFSR input, so it is a plausible remaining timing\\ncone after the successful MEM/WB writeback retime. Prior structural attempts\\nthat added prediction, FIFOs, store buffers, or same-cycle load bypassing\\nregressed because they added control or long bypass paths; a plain fetch\\nboundary register is a narrower retime.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end has no real IF/ID register: io_imemData feeds the\\ndecoder, immediate generator, register-file read addresses, hazard predecode,\\nand then the ID/EX register in the same cycle. In the FPGA Fmax harness that\\npath is driven by a live LFSR input, so it is a plausible remaining timing\\ncone after the successful MEM/WB writeback retime. Prior structural attempts\\nthat added prediction, FIFOs, store buffers, or same-cycle load bypassing\\nregressed because they added control or long bypass paths; a plain fetch\\nboundary register is a narrower retime.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the IF output from a combinational {pc,instr,valid} bundle into a\\nregistered IF/ID payload that captures only when io_imemReady is high and ID\\ncan advance. Decode then runs from this local registered instruction instead\\nof directly from io_imemData, cutting the external imem-data-to-ID/EX timing\\npath and making the pipeline a conventional IF/ID/EX/MEM/WB structure. On EX\\nredirects the IF/ID register must be cleared and the PC redirected exactly as\\ntoday; on load-use, dmem, and DIV stalls it must hold without duplicating\\nretirements. This may add one cycle of fill/redirect latency, but the expected\\nFmax gain should outweigh the small CoreMark CPI cost if the fron...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Valid-gated pipeline bubbles\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The recent structural winners were narrow retimes that removed existing muxing,\\nwhile predictors, FIFOs, store buffers, load bypassing, and extra fetch staging\\nregressed by adding new control on sensitive paths. The current pipeline still\\nflushes or bubbles whole packed ID/EX and EX/MEM payloads to zero, which puts\\nhigh-fanout load-use, redirect, and divider-busy controls on many data bits that\\nare architecturally irrelevant when valid is low.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The recent structural winners were narrow retimes that removed existing muxing,\\nwhile predictors, FIFOs, store buffers, load bypassing, and extra fetch staging\\nregressed by adding new control on sensitive paths. The current pipeline still\\nflushes or bubbles whole packed ID/EX and EX/MEM payloads to zero, which puts\\nhigh-fanout load-use, redirect, and divider-busy controls on many data bits that\\nare architecturally irrelevant when valid is low.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Change pipeline bubbles to be valid-gated instead of full-payload zeroed: on\\nload-use, redirect, and divider wait cycles, clear only the valid bit and the\\nsmall set of side-effect controls needed to prevent redirects, memory requests,\\nregfile writes, and RVFI retirement. Let datapath and RVFI payload fields retain\\ntheir previous values when invalid, and gate EX redirects and MEM requests with\\nthe stage valid bit. This keeps the five-stage schedule, forwarding behavior,\\ntrap rules, and nret=1 RVFI contract unchanged while reducing wide mux/reset\\npressure on the inter-stage register cones.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"On flush, clear ID/EX valid and side-effect controls instead of zeroing the full payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate branch/jump redirects and EX/MEM side effects with val...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Stage-local control bundles\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has mostly rewarded structural changes that remove existing muxing or fanout without adding speculation, queues, or new stall policy.  After EX, the pipeline still carries the full decoded ctrl_t through EX/MEM and MEM/WB even though most decode, branch, jump, ALU, and immediate-control bits are dead in those later stages.  Those unused packed-control bits still participate in wide register clears, holds, routing, and packed-struct assignment cones.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has mostly rewarded structural changes that remove existing muxing or fanout without adding speculation, queues, or new stall policy.  After EX, the pipeline still carries the full decoded ctrl_t through EX/MEM and MEM/WB even though most decode, branch, jump, ALU, and immediate-control bits are dead in those later stages.  Those unused packed-control bits still participate in wide register clears, holds, routing, and packed-struct assignment cones.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the pipeline control payload by stage while keeping the five-stage schedule unchanged.  ID/EX should retain the full decoded ctrl_t, EX/MEM should carry only the memory, writeback, and trap bits MEM and forwarding still consume, and MEM/WB should carry only reg_write plus is_illegal because rd_wdata, masks, and RVFI memory fields are already final.  This should trim flip-flops, reset/flush muxing, and high-fanout packed-control routing without changing CPI, branch behavior, memory handshakes, RVFI ordering, or the nret=1 port contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Introduce narrower EX/MEM and MEM/WB control payload typedefs while leaving ID/EX decode control intact.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Map the full ID/EX control word into the reduced EX/...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Reset-light pipeline payloads\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The recent winners for this core removed existing fanout and packed-control\\nrouting without adding new speculation, queues, bypass paths, or stall policy.\\nThe register file reset removal was a modest win, and the stage-local control\\nsplit was a larger win, which suggests the FPGA is still sensitive to reset and\\nhold mux pressure on wide pipeline registers. ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB still\\nsynchronously reset full packed payloads even though only valid and side-effect\\ncontrols need architecturally defined reset values.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The recent winners for this core removed existing fanout and packed-control\\nrouting without adding new speculation, queues, bypass paths, or stall policy.\\nThe register file reset removal was a modest win, and the stage-local control\\nsplit was a larger win, which suggests the FPGA is still sensitive to reset and\\nhold mux pressure on wide pipeline registers. ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB still\\nsynchronously reset full packed payloads even though only valid and side-effect\\ncontrols need architecturally defined reset values.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace full-payload synchronous resets on the pipeline registers with\\nreset-light stage registers: keep valid, reg_write, mem_read, mem_write, and\\ntrap/control bits reset to zero, but leave wide datapath and RVFI payload fields\\nwithout a live reset mux, using initial zero values only for deterministic\\nsimulation if needed. Do not change normal bubble, flush, dmem-hold, divider,\\nforwarding, or retirement behavior; this is reset-structure cleanup rather than\\nthe failed valid-gated bubble scheme. Removing reset fanout from pc, instr,\\nrs values, immediates, ALU results, memory data, and RVFI sideband fields should\\nimprove packing and routing while preserving CoreMark CPI and the nret=1 RVFI\\ncontract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confiden...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Remove IF NOP injection mux\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench history shows that added predictors, FIFOs, store buffers, load bypasses, and broad valid-gated rewrites tend to lose more Fmax than they recover. The reliable structural wins have instead removed existing muxing and packed-control fanout without changing CPI. The current IF stage still rewrites every fetched instruction through a 32-bit NOP mux on redirect or imem stall so the hazard unit and ID/EX controls see a harmless bubble.\\n\\nThat NOP mux sits directly on the live io_imemData-to-decode, regfile-address, immediate, and hazard-predecode path. The pipeline already carries an IF/ID valid bit, so the same bubble semantics can be represented with valid plus explicit side-effect-control zeroing in ID instead of modifying the instruction word before decode.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench history shows that added predictors, FIFOs, store buffers, load bypasses, and broad valid-gated rewrites tend to lose more Fmax than they recover. The reliable structural wins have instead removed existing muxing and packed-control fanout without changing CPI. The current IF stage still rewrites every fetched instruction through a 32-bit NOP mux on redirect or imem stall so the hazard unit and ID/EX controls see a harmless bubble.\\n\\nThat NOP mux sits directly on the live io_imemData-to-decode, regfile-address, immediate, and hazard-predecode path. The pipeline already carries an IF/ID valid bit, so the same bubble semantics can be represented with valid plus explicit side-effect-control zeroing in ID instead of modifying the instruction word before decode.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Stop forcing IF/ID instr to ADDI x0,x0,0 on flush, redirect, and imem stall. Let if_stage pass the fetched instruction bits through unchanged while marking the IF/ID payload invalid, then make id_stage register a side-effect-free control word whenever in.valid is low. Add IF/I...\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Split RVFI shadow pipeline\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The strongest structural wins for this core have come from removing existing\\nfanout and packed pipeline routing without adding prediction, buffering, or\\nnew stall policy.  Stage-local control bundles were a large improvement, but\\nthe execution stage payloads still carry many RVFI-only fields through the\\nsame packed structs and hold/reset paths used by the hot ALU, forwarding,\\nmemory, and writeback logic.  Prior single-field cleanup was too QoR-sensitive,\\nso this should be treated as a deliberate structural split rather than another\\nisolated payload deletion.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The strongest structural wins for this core have come from removing existing\\nfanout and packed pipeline routing without adding prediction, buffering, or\\nnew stall policy.  Stage-local control bundles were a large improvement, but\\nthe execution stage payloads still carry many RVFI-only fields through the\\nsame packed structs and hold/reset paths used by the hot ALU, forwarding,\\nmemory, and writeback logic.  Prior single-field cleanup was too QoR-sensitive,\\nso this should be treated as a deliberate structural split rather than another\\nisolated payload deletion.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Separate the architectural execution pipeline from a parallel RVFI shadow\\npipeline.  Keep ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB execution structs limited to values\\nthat are consumed for forwarding, memory handshakes, redirects, or regfile\\nwriteback, while a sideband shadow path carries pc, instr, raw rs addresses,\\nraw rs values, pc_next, memory masks/data, and trap metadata to the single\\nRVFI retirement channel.  The shadow path should advance, hold, and flush on\\nexactly the same conditions as the execution stage it mirrors, preserving the\\nnret=1 contract and all RVFI ordering/trap semantics.  This keeps CoreMark CPI\\nunchanged while reducing hot packed-struct fanout and routing pressur...\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Multicycle high-half multiply side unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core still keeps one sign-selectable 33x33 multiplier in the\\ncommon combinational ALU so MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU can all complete in a\\nsingle EX cycle. Prior history showed that sharing the multiplier was a small\\nwin, while moving rare DIV/REM work into a side unit was a large structural\\nwin once it reused a disciplined EX-busy stall path. CoreMark is much more\\nlikely to need low-half MUL than high-half multiply variants, so the high-half\\ncases can afford extra latency if the common EX arithmetic cone becomes\\nsmaller and easier to route.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core still keeps one sign-selectable 33x33 multiplier in the\\ncommon combinational ALU so MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU can all complete in a\\nsingle EX cycle. Prior history showed that sharing the multiplier was a small\\nwin, while moving rare DIV/REM work into a side unit was a large structural\\nwin once it reused a disciplined EX-busy stall path. CoreMark is much more\\nlikely to need low-half MUL than high-half multiply variants, so the high-half\\ncases can afford extra latency if the common EX arithmetic cone becomes\\nsmaller and easier to route.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep ALU_MUL single-cycle, but implement it as a low-half-only 32x32 product\\nwhose upper product bits and signed high-half selection are not on the normal\\nALU result path. Move MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU into a small multicycle high-\\nmultiply unit issued from EX, with operand/RVFI payload capture and completion\\nbehavior mirroring the existing divider side path. The existing ex_busy stall\\nmechanism can freeze IF/ID/EX and let older EX/MEM contents drain, so this\\nshould not introduce a new global stall policy. In normal CoreMark execution,\\nrare high-half multiplies pay side-unit latency while common adds, branches,\\nloads, stores, and low-half MUL see a shorter ALU...\n  Line 45: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r15s1\", \"title\": \"Split divider result lane\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best structural wins came from removing existing muxing and packed\\ncontrol fanout, while added predictors, FIFOs, store buffers, and new side\\nunits mostly regressed. The divider is already iterative, but its accept/done\\npath still sits inside the common EX/MEM register update: every normal EX\\nresult is muxed against saved DIV/REM context, divider completion, and bubble\\ninjection. CoreMark rarely executes DIV/REM, so the common ALU/branch path\\nshould not pay a wide long-operation mux on every cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best structural wins came from removing existing muxing and packed\\ncontrol fanout, while added predictors, FIFOs, store buffers, and new side\\nunits mostly regressed. The divider is already iterative, but its accept/done\\npath still sits inside the common EX/MEM register update: every normal EX\\nresult is muxed against saved DIV/REM context, divider completion, and bubble\\ninjection. CoreMark rarely executes DIV/REM, so the common ALU/branch path\\nshould not pay a wide long-operation mux on every cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the existing divider completion path into a sideband result lane. Keep\\nthe normal EX/MEM register update as a straight normal-instruction path, and\\ncapture DIV/REM architectural context in a compact divider context register\\nthat only drives MEM when the divider produces its single completion result.\\nWhile DIV/REM is running, feed MEM bubbles through a narrow valid/control path\\nrather than routing every EX/MEM payload bit through the divider mux. This\\npreserves the five-stage schedule, existing ex_busy stall policy, nret=1 RVFI\\ncontract, and rare DIV/REM latency, but should shorten the common EX-to-EX/MEM\\ntiming cone used by CoreMark's hot ALU, branch, load, and store instructions.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep2/log.jsonl:\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple RV32M from EX critical path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage contains the full RV32IM ALU, including three 64-bit multiply products and combinational divide/remainder logic. Even when CoreMark is executing ordinary RV32I ALU, branch, and memory instructions, that wide M-extension datapath remains part of the synthesized execute-stage cone and likely limits Fmax near the Tang Nano 20K LUT budget.\\n\\nA structural split that keeps common RV32I operations single-cycle while moving RV32M work into a dedicated busy/ready unit should raise Fmax and reduce routing pressure. CoreMark uses multiply but rarely needs divide in the timed loop, so a modest multiply latency penalty should be outweighed by a shorter critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage contains the full RV32IM ALU, including three 64-bit multiply products and combinational divide/remainder logic. Even when CoreMark is executing ordinary RV32I ALU, branch, and memory instructions, that wide M-extension datapath remains part of the synthesized execute-stage cone and likely limits Fmax near the Tang Nano 20K LUT budget.\\n\\nA structural split that keeps common RV32I operations single-cycle while moving RV32M work into a dedicated busy/ready unit should raise Fmax and reduce routing pressure. CoreMark uses multiply but rarely needs divide in the timed loop, so a modest multiply latency penalty should be outweighed by a shorter critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a separate M-unit and make EX a one-cycle stage for RV32I plus a multi-cycle/busy stage only for ALU_MUL*, ALU_DIV*, and ALU_REM*. Implement multiply as a short pipelined DSP-friendly path and division/remainder as an iterative unit, with the hazard unit freezing IF/ID/EX while an M operation is outstanding and releasing exactly one EX/MEM result when ready.\\n\\nUnder RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, keep the M-unit's substituted algebraic results one-cycle or...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple fetch with skid queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current frontend treats every low io_imemReady cycle as an immediate\\ndecode bubble, and backend stalls hold the PC so the core cannot fetch ahead\\nwhile memory, load-use, or iterative divide latency is already stopping\\nretirement. In the tournament cosim mode both instruction and data buses see\\nrandom stalls, so overlapping independent frontend availability with backend\\nstalls can improve CoreMark cycles without relying on the failed IF-stage\\ninstruction predecode path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current frontend treats every low io_imemReady cycle as an immediate\\ndecode bubble, and backend stalls hold the PC so the core cannot fetch ahead\\nwhile memory, load-use, or iterative divide latency is already stopping\\nretirement. In the tournament cosim mode both instruction and data buses see\\nrandom stalls, so overlapping independent frontend availability with backend\\nstalls can improve CoreMark cycles without relying on the failed IF-stage\\ninstruction predecode path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a small two-entry instruction skid/prefetch queue between the fetch PC\\nand ID. The fetch side advances and enqueues whenever io_imemReady is high and\\nthe queue is not full; the decode side consumes only when the existing backend\\nhazard logic allows ID to advance. Redirects flush the queue and restart fetch\\nat the resolved target, preserving precise RVFI retirement and the NRET=1 port\\ncontract.\\n\\nThis structural decoupling should hide a fraction of random iStall cycles\\nbehind dmem stalls, load-use bubbles, and divide busy cycles, while adding only\\na shallow FIFO and PC muxes instead of putting branch predecode on the\\nimem_data-to-next_pc critical path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single PC-to-ID combinational fetch path with ...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"One-entry RAM store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current in-order pipeline freezes behind every STORE whenever io_dmemReady\\nis low, even though most stores do not produce a value needed by the following\\ninstruction. In the tournament cosim mode dmem sees random backpressure, so\\nCoreMark pays avoidable full-pipeline bubbles on ordinary RAM stores.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts put new logic on the fetch path or tried to decouple\\nthe whole M-extension unit. A narrower backend store buffer should attack a\\ncommon stall source while leaving fetch, branch resolution, RVFI width, and the\\nworking iterative divider structure unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current in-order pipeline freezes behind every STORE whenever io_dmemReady\\nis low, even though most stores do not produce a value needed by the following\\ninstruction. In the tournament cosim mode dmem sees random backpressure, so\\nCoreMark pays avoidable full-pipeline bubbles on ordinary RAM stores.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts put new logic on the fetch path or tried to decouple\\nthe whole M-extension unit. A narrower backend store buffer should attack a\\ncommon stall source while leaving fetch, branch resolution, RVFI width, and the\\nworking iterative divider structure unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry write buffer for aligned, non-MMIO RAM stores. When a store\\nreaches MEM and the dmem bus is not ready, capture its address, byte mask, and\\nreplicated write data into the buffer, retire the store once architecturally\\nenqueued, and let younger non-memory instructions continue. Drain the buffer\\nto dmem as soon as io_dmemReady returns.\\n\\nPreserve ordering by stalling any younger load/store while the buffer is full\\nunless the pending store has drained, and keep UART/timing-marker MMIO stores\\nunbuffered so CoreMark brackets and output timing remain tied to real bus\\nacceptance. This should hide a fraction of random dmem ...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Split high-half multiplier\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The best bench result came from removing cold divide/remainder hardware from\\nthe common execute-stage ALU path, while later attempts that added fetch\\nqueues, predictors, or store buffering lost more Fmax than they recovered in\\ncycles. The remaining ALU still builds signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and\\nsigned*unsigned 64-bit multiplier cones even though CoreMark's hot path is\\nexpected to use ordinary low-half MUL far more often than MULH/MULHU/MULHSU.\\n\\nA narrower structural split should preserve CoreMark's common single-cycle\\nmultiply behavior while removing the high-half signedness/product logic from\\nthe timing-critical EX result mux.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best bench result came from removing cold divide/remainder hardware from\\nthe common execute-stage ALU path, while later attempts that added fetch\\nqueues, predictors, or store buffering lost more Fmax than they recovered in\\ncycles. The remaining ALU still builds signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and\\nsigned*unsigned 64-bit multiplier cones even though CoreMark's hot path is\\nexpected to use ordinary low-half MUL far more often than MULH/MULHU/MULHSU.\\n\\nA narrower structural split should preserve CoreMark's common single-cycle\\nmultiply behavior while removing the high-half signedness/product logic from\\nthe timing-critical EX result mux.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep ALU_MUL as a simple single-cycle low-32-bit product, because that is the\\nmultiply form most likely used by compiled CoreMark matrix code. Move only\\nMULH, MULHU, and MULHSU into a small iterative high-half multiply unit with\\nthe same start/done/ack discipline already proven necessary for the divider.\\n\\nIn the core build, EX should treat the high-half multiply variants like the\\nexisting iterative divider: hold ID/EX while the unit runs, latch the original\\nforwarded operands for RVFI, and emit exactly one EX/MEM res...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Pipelined full multiplier unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The best measured improvement came from removing cold divide/remainder logic from\\nthe common execute-stage ALU path. The remaining wide arithmetic limiter is the\\nsingle-cycle RV32M multiplier cone: the ALU still builds multiple 64-bit products\\nin the same result path used by ordinary adds, branches, address generation, and\\nstores. Prior high-half-only attempts regressed because low-half MUL stayed in\\nthat common cone, so the critical path was not structurally removed.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best measured improvement came from removing cold divide/remainder logic from\\nthe common execute-stage ALU path. The remaining wide arithmetic limiter is the\\nsingle-cycle RV32M multiplier cone: the ALU still builds multiple 64-bit products\\nin the same result path used by ordinary adds, branches, address generation, and\\nstores. Prior high-half-only attempts regressed because low-half MUL stayed in\\nthat common cone, so the critical path was not structurally removed.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move all real MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU execution out of rtl/alu.sv into a dedicated\\ntwo-cycle multiplier unit built from registered 16x16 partial products followed\\nby a registered combine/sign-select stage. EX should treat every real multiply\\nlike the existing iterative divider: latch the forwarded operands, assert a\\nbusy stall while the unit is active, and emit exactly one EX/MEM result with the\\noriginal operands preserved for RVFI.\\n\\nThis pays one extra cycle on CoreMark multiply instructions, but it removes the\\n32x32/64-bit product cone from the common single-cycle RV32I execute path and\\nshould improve Tang Nano 20K Fmax more than the modest MUL CPI loss. Under\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, keep the existing algebraic substitutions single-cycle so\\nthe formal harness remains tractable and consistent with the spec files.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\"...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Resetless LUTRAM register file\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Prior structural attempts that added frontend predictors, fetch queues, store buffering, or multi-cycle multiplier control lost more Fmax than they recovered in CoreMark cycles. The remaining low-risk structural target is the integer register file: it is currently a resettable 32x32 flop bank even though only x0 has architectural zero behavior, and the full synchronous reset adds muxing and routing to every stored bit.\\n\\nRetargeting this storage without changing pipeline timing should improve FPGA placement/routing pressure while preserving the existing single-issue CPI, hazard behavior, forwarding, and RVFI contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior structural attempts that added frontend predictors, fetch queues, store buffering, or multi-cycle multiplier control lost more Fmax than they recovered in CoreMark cycles. The remaining low-risk structural target is the integer register file: it is currently a resettable 32x32 flop bank even though only x0 has architectural zero behavior, and the full synchronous reset adds muxing and routing to every stored bit.\\n\\nRetargeting this storage without changing pipeline timing should improve FPGA placement/routing pressure while preserving the existing single-issue CPI, hazard behavior, forwarding, and RVFI contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rebuild rtl/reg_file.sv as resetless storage for x1-x31, with x0 represented only by constant-zero read behavior and dropped writes. Preserve the same two asynchronous read ports, one synchronous write port, and same-cycle write-first bypass, but remove the per-bit synchronous reset from the data array.\\n\\nUse deterministic power-up initialization if needed for simulation/FPGA startup, but do not clear the data array through reset. This should let Yosys map the register file to distributed RAM or at least smaller unreset storage, cutting reset/write mux depth and routing...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Valid-gated resetless pipeline payloads\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added predictors, queues, store buffering, or multi-cycle multiply control regressed because their extra muxing landed on already-sensitive common paths. The strongest recent positive signal was pruning dead pipeline control state, which suggests this core is still limited by fanout/routing and unnecessary pipeline register plumbing rather than by a missing large feature.\\n\\nThe ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB registers currently reset entire wide payload structs even though only valid bits and side-effect controls must be deterministic after reset or flush. Resetting every PC, operand, immediate, instruction, RVFI payload, and memory-data field adds high-fanout reset muxing to hundreds of flops without improving steady-state CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added predictors, queues, store buffering, or multi-cycle multiply control regressed because their extra muxing landed on already-sensitive common paths. The strongest recent positive signal was pruning dead pipeline control state, which suggests this core is still limited by fanout/routing and unnecessary pipeline register plumbing rather than by a missing large feature.\\n\\nThe ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB registers currently reset entire wide payload structs even though only valid bits and side-effect controls must be deterministic after reset or flush. Resetting every PC, operand, immediate, instruction, RVFI payload, and memory-data field adds high-fanout reset muxing to hundreds of flops without improving steady-state CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the inter-stage pipeline registers to a valid-gated reset discipline: reset and flush only the valid bits plus control fields that can cause side effects, while leaving bulk data payload flops resetless. Gate redirect, memory request, writeback, forwarding, and ...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-to-EX load bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current pipeline pays a one-cycle load-use interlock whenever a load in\\nEX feeds either raw source field of the following instruction. CoreMark's hot\\nloops are load-heavy, and the successful recent changes show that this core\\nis already Fmax-competitive enough that recovering real CPI bubbles may be a\\nbetter structural bet than adding more prediction or buffering state.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added fetch queues, predictors, store buffers,\\nor multi-cycle multiply control regressed, so this proposal keeps the pipeline\\nshape and NRET=1 RVFI contract intact while only adding a local load-result\\nbypass around the existing MEM/EX boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current pipeline pays a one-cycle load-use interlock whenever a load in\\nEX feeds either raw source field of the following instruction. CoreMark's hot\\nloops are load-heavy, and the successful recent changes show that this core\\nis already Fmax-competitive enough that recovering real CPI bubbles may be a\\nbetter structural bet than adding more prediction or buffering state.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added fetch queues, predictors, store buffers,\\nor multi-cycle multiply control regressed, so this proposal keeps the pipeline\\nshape and NRET=1 RVFI contract intact while only adding a local load-result\\nbypass around the existing MEM/EX boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a same-cycle MEM-to-EX forwarding path for loads. When a load is in MEM\\nand its data is available, feed the MEM-stage sign/zero-extended load result\\ninto the existing EX operand forwarding mux instead of forwarding the load's\\naddress from EX/MEM. Then remove the blanket load-use stall for consumers\\nthat can safely wait under the existing dmem stall mechanism.\\n\\nKeep correctness conservative for control-flow consumers: if a dependent\\nbranch or JALR would use a load value before the dmem response is...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Registered writeback result path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent winning changes improved fitness by removing late pipeline baggage\\nrather than adding predictors, queues, or buffers. The current MEM/WB stage\\nstill carries both alu_result and read_data plus a mem_to_reg control bit,\\nthen rebuilds the final writeback value through a combinational mux in WB.\\nThat mux output fans out to the register file, MEM/WB-to-EX forwarding, and\\nRVFI rd_wdata, so it can sit on the common dependent-instruction path even\\nthough the load-versus-ALU choice is already known when MEM/WB captures.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent winning changes improved fitness by removing late pipeline baggage\\nrather than adding predictors, queues, or buffers. The current MEM/WB stage\\nstill carries both alu_result and read_data plus a mem_to_reg control bit,\\nthen rebuilds the final writeback value through a combinational mux in WB.\\nThat mux output fans out to the register file, MEM/WB-to-EX forwarding, and\\nRVFI rd_wdata, so it can sit on the common dependent-instruction path even\\nthough the load-versus-ALU choice is already known when MEM/WB captures.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move final writeback-value selection into the MEM stage and store a single\\nregistered wb_data field in MEM/WB. Remove the separate MEM/WB read_data,\\nalu_result, and mem_to_reg payload/control used only to recreate that value\\none cycle later, and make wb_stage a simple valid/reg_write gate around the\\nregistered result.\\n\\nThis keeps pipeline timing, NRET=1 RVFI behavior, memory ordering, dmem-stall\\nhold semantics, and CoreMark instruction count unchanged while shortening the\\nMEM/WB-to-EX forwarding cone and trimming one 32-bit late-stage payload. The\\nchange follows the successful control-pruning direction but avoids the r8s0\\nmistake of increasing store-data fanout, because it only collapses mutually\\nexclusive writeback-result copies after MEM has alre...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Split writeback bypass from regfile reads\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The strongest positive bench changes have removed fanout and muxing from existing pipeline paths rather than adding predictors, buffers, or extra memory machinery. The register file still has write-first read bypass muxes on both asynchronous read ports, so every ID-stage source read carries same-cycle WB address compares and write-data muxing even though most dynamic reads do not hit the current WB instruction.\\n\\nPrior resetless-regfile work showed the reset tree was not the interesting part of this block; the remaining structural target is the read/write bypass coupling itself. Moving the rare same-cycle WB dependency into the existing EX forwarding network should shorten the imem-data-to-ID/EX register-file read path without changing CoreMark CPI.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The strongest positive bench changes have removed fanout and muxing from existing pipeline paths rather than adding predictors, buffers, or extra memory machinery. The register file still has write-first read bypass muxes on both asynchronous read ports, so every ID-stage source read carries same-cycle WB address compares and write-data muxing even though most dynamic reads do not hit the current WB instruction.\\n\\nPrior resetless-regfile work showed the reset tree was not the interesting part of this block; the remaining structural target is the read/write bypass coupling itself. Moving the rare same-cycle WB dependency into the existing EX forwarding network should shorten the imem-data-to-ID/EX register-file read path without changing CoreMark CPI.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make rtl/reg_file.sv a simple 2R1W architectural array: x0 remains hardwired to zero, writes remain synchronous, but read ports no longer mux in same-cycle w_data. Add a one-cycle registered WB-bypass source in core.sv that captures each real WB write, extend forward_unit.sv to select it as the...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Wider resetless ifetch replay cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The latest winning change showed that the safest remaining cycle gain is not\\nbranch prediction, but replaying immutable hot-loop instructions when the\\nrandom instruction bus stalls. The current replay table is only 8 entries and\\ndirect-mapped, so CoreMark loop bodies can still conflict on pc[4:2] and fall\\nback to bubbles even after the instruction was previously fetched.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added queues, store buffers, bypass paths, or\\npredictor metadata regressed because they lengthened common control paths.\\nA larger direct-mapped replay cache keeps the same no-repair frontend shape\\nwhile improving the hit rate of the one mechanism that has already produced a\\nlarge fitness win.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The latest winning change showed that the safest remaining cycle gain is not\\nbranch prediction, but replaying immutable hot-loop instructions when the\\nrandom instruction bus stalls. The current replay table is only 8 entries and\\ndirect-mapped, so CoreMark loop bodies can still conflict on pc[4:2] and fall\\nback to bubbles even after the instruction was previously fetched.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added queues, store buffers, bypass paths, or\\npredictor metadata regressed because they lengthened common control paths.\\nA larger direct-mapped replay cache keeps the same no-repair frontend shape\\nwhile improving the hit rate of the one mechanism that has already produced a\\nlarge fitness win.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rebuild the IF replay table as a 16-entry direct-mapped, valid-gated replay\\ncache indexed by pc[5:2]. Fill it only on accepted io_imemReady fetches, and\\non io_imemReady=0 hits keep supplying the cached instruction through the\\nexisting imem_effective_ready path. Do not add branch prediction metadata,\\ntarget repair state, or any EX feedback.\\n\\nMake the replay tag and instruction payload arrays reset...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Warm replay during backend stalls\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current 8-entry IF replay table produced the largest recent improvement by\\nconverting random instruction-bus stalls into useful hot-loop instructions.\\nFollow-up attempts to widen or reset-retime the table regressed, which points\\naway from adding capacity on the fetch critical path and toward improving how\\noften the existing table is populated.\\n\\nBackend stalls from dmem backpressure, load-use bubbles, and iterative divide\\nalready stop decode from consuming the current fetch slot. Those cycles can be\\nused to issue harmless sequential instruction fetches and train the replay\\ntable, because instruction memory is immutable and wrong-path replay fills do\\nnot affect architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current 8-entry IF replay table produced the largest recent improvement by\\nconverting random instruction-bus stalls into useful hot-loop instructions.\\nFollow-up attempts to widen or reset-retime the table regressed, which points\\naway from adding capacity on the fetch critical path and toward improving how\\noften the existing table is populated.\\n\\nBackend stalls from dmem backpressure, load-use bubbles, and iterative divide\\nalready stop decode from consuming the current fetch slot. Those cycles can be\\nused to issue harmless sequential instruction fetches and train the replay\\ntable, because instruction memory is immutable and wrong-path replay fills do\\nnot affect architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add an opportunistic replay warmer inside IF. When the architectural PC is\\nheld for a non-redirect backend stall and the instruction bus is ready, drive\\nio_imemAddr from a small sequential prefetch cursor instead of the held PC and\\nfill the existing replay table with the returned instruction. Keep out.pc,\\nRVFI-visible instruction flow, redirect handling, and decode consumption tied\\nto the architectural PC; the wa...\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Register fetch-decode boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench core still has an unusually long frontend timing path: the IF\\nstage emits a combinational IF/ID payload, so io_imemData or replay-table data\\nflows through the fetch mux, decoder, immediate generator, register-file read\\naddresses, and into the ID/EX register in one cycle. Recent structural history\\nshows that adding predictors, queues, wider replay, and bypass paths usually\\nlost timing, while pruning existing stage plumbing helped. A minimal pipeline\\nboundary is therefore a better structural bet than adding more speculation.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench core still has an unusually long frontend timing path: the IF\\nstage emits a combinational IF/ID payload, so io_imemData or replay-table data\\nflows through the fetch mux, decoder, immediate generator, register-file read\\naddresses, and into the ID/EX register in one cycle. Recent structural history\\nshows that adding predictors, queues, wider replay, and bypass paths usually\\nlost timing, while pruning existing stage plumbing helped. A minimal pipeline\\nboundary is therefore a better structural bet than adding more speculation.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a real registered IF/ID boundary owned by the fetch stage, while keeping\\nthe existing 8-entry accepted-fetch replay table and NRET=1 retirement contract.\\nFetch should capture pc/instruction/valid only when the current fetch is usable\\nvia io_imemReady or a replay hit and ID can accept it; redirects flush the IF/ID\\nregister and update the PC to the resolved target. ID then decodes only the\\nregistered IF/ID payload, shortening the common FPGA path from instruction data\\nto decoded ID/EX controls and regfile source reads.\\n\\nThis will likely add one extra bubble on taken redirects compared with the\\ncurrent combinational IF/ID design, so the implementation should keep the\\nbranch/hazard machinery conservati...\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Valid-gated fetch bubbles\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Prior structural attempts that added predictors, queues, store buffers, or wider replay state generally regressed because they put more muxing and control on already-sensitive paths. The successful changes instead pruned existing pipeline state or let the accepted-fetch replay table hide iStalls without branch-repair metadata.\\n\\nThe current frontend still forces every flush, redirect, or imem-miss bubble through a 32-bit NOP mux before decode. That mux sits directly on the imem/replay-data to decoder, immediate generator, regfile-address, and ID/EX capture path even though invalid instructions never retire.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior structural attempts that added predictors, queues, store buffers, or wider replay state generally regressed because they put more muxing and control on already-sensitive paths. The successful changes instead pruned existing pipeline state or let the accepted-fetch replay table hide iStalls without branch-repair metadata.\\n\\nThe current frontend still forces every flush, redirect, or imem-miss bubble through a 32-bit NOP mux before decode. That mux sits directly on the imem/replay-data to decoder, immediate generator, regfile-address, and ID/EX capture path even though invalid instructions never retire.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Represent frontend bubbles with the existing valid bit instead of replacing the instruction word with ADDI x0,x0,0. IF should always pass the fetched or replayed instruction bits forward and set out.valid low for flush/redirect/imem-miss cycles. ID should zero side-effect controls and destination metadata whenever in.valid is low, and the load-use hazard should be gated by IF/ID and ID/EX validity so stale invalid instruction bits cannot create false interlocks.\\n\\nThis removes a normal-cycle 32-bit instruction mux from the fetch-to-decode cone and moves correctness onto compact valid/control gat...\n  Line 45: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r15s1\", \"title\": \"Operand-aware load interlock\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current hazard unit stalls after every load when the load destination\\nmatches either raw register field of the following instruction. That is\\nconservative for RVFI, but it also creates false bubbles for immediates,\\njumps, upper-immediate ops, fences, and traps whose bit fields alias the\\nload rd without being execution operands. CoreMark has many load-adjacent\\npointer and immediate operations, so recovering even a small fraction of\\nthese bubbles can improve iterations per cycle.\\n\\nA prior false-load-use attempt broke because unused raw rs1/rs2 fields still\\nneed correct RVFI register data. The structural opportunity remains if the\\ncore separates execution operand readiness from RVFI source reporting instead\\nof treating every raw field as a data dependency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current hazard unit stalls after every load when the load destination\\nmatches either raw register field of the following instruction. That is\\nconservative for RVFI, but it also creates false bubbles for immediates,\\njumps, upper-immediate ops, fences, and traps whose bit fields alias the\\nload rd without being execution operands. CoreMark has many load-adjacent\\npointer and immediate operations, so recovering even a small fraction of\\nthese bubbles can improve iterations per cycle.\\n\\nA prior false-load-use attempt broke because unused raw rs1/rs2 fields still\\nneed correct RVFI register data. The structural opportunity remains if the\\ncore separates execution operand readiness from RVFI source reporting instead\\nof treating every raw field as a data dependency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add explicit source-use metadata for the instruction entering ID/EX and use\\nit only for the load-use interlock. Branches, stores, JALR, and R-type ops\\nstill stall when they consume a load result, but I-type immediates, LUI,\\nAUIPC, JAL, FENCE, EBREAK, and illegal instruct...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep1/log.jsonl:\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple slow RV32M ops from EX\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage routes every instruction through a fully combinational\\nRV32IM ALU that includes parallel high-half multipliers plus DIV/REM logic.\\nCoreMark's timed region is dominated by integer ALU, load/store, branch, and\\nlow-half MUL work, while DIV/REM and high-half multiply are either absent or\\nrare. Keeping those slow operators on the single-cycle EX timing path likely\\ndepresses Fmax without improving CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage routes every instruction through a fully combinational\\nRV32IM ALU that includes parallel high-half multipliers plus DIV/REM logic.\\nCoreMark's timed region is dominated by integer ALU, load/store, branch, and\\nlow-half MUL work, while DIV/REM and high-half multiply are either absent or\\nrare. Keeping those slow operators on the single-cycle EX timing path likely\\ndepresses Fmax without improving CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure EX into a fast single-cycle I-ALU plus a separate RV32M slow-unit.\\nKeep ALU_MUL's low-half multiply on a one-cycle fast path for CoreMark, but\\nmove MULH/MULHSU/MULHU and DIV/REM/REMU into a shared multi-cycle unit that\\nstalls the in-order pipeline only while those rare operations are in flight.\\nThis should remove the divider and redundant high-multiply logic from the\\ncommon EX critical path, reduce LUT pressure near the current budget, and\\nraise Fmax with minimal impact on the benchmark instruction stream.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Reduce the combinational ALU to RV32I operations plus the one-cycle MUL low-half fast path; remove DIV/REM and high-half multiply from the common result mux.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch slow RV32M operations to a separate multi-cycle unit, hold EX state until result_valid, and ...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Elastic IF fetch buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end ties instruction fetch directly to decode: when ID is\\nstalled for load-use, dmem backpressure, or a slow M operation, the PC also\\nholds and no useful instruction fetch can be performed. With the tournament\\ncosim injecting about 22 percent random instruction stalls, those missed\\nopportunities turn independent stalls into serialized bubbles. The failed\\nstatic predictor showed that adding next-PC computation in IF is too costly,\\nso the better structural target is decoupling fetch from decode without\\nadding branch-target logic to the IF critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end ties instruction fetch directly to decode: when ID is\\nstalled for load-use, dmem backpressure, or a slow M operation, the PC also\\nholds and no useful instruction fetch can be performed. With the tournament\\ncosim injecting about 22 percent random instruction stalls, those missed\\nopportunities turn independent stalls into serialized bubbles. The failed\\nstatic predictor showed that adding next-PC computation in IF is too costly,\\nso the better structural target is decoupling fetch from decode without\\nadding branch-target logic to the IF critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a tiny elastic fetch buffer between IF and ID. The buffer should keep\\nthe existing direct fall-through path when decode is ready, but when ID/EX is\\nstalled it should let IF continue fetching sequential instructions into one\\nor two registered slots until the buffer is full. Decode then consumes from\\nthe buffer before requesting a fresh instruction, hiding later imemReady=0\\ncycles behind earlier load-use, dmem, or slow-M stalls. Redirects flush the\\nbuffer and restart fetch at the resolved target, preserving precise single\\nretirement and the existing RVFI channel-0 contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"chang...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-to-EX load-use bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline always inserts a bubble when an instruction\\nconsumes the destination of the immediately preceding load. That is safe but\\npessimistic for the zero-wait or ready dmem cycles that dominate the FPGA and\\nrandom-stall CoreMark runs: the load value is already formed in MEM while the\\ndependent instruction would be in EX. Previous frontend structural work lost\\ntoo much Fmax, so the better IPC target is a local backend bypass that leaves\\nIF next-PC timing untouched.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline always inserts a bubble when an instruction\\nconsumes the destination of the immediately preceding load. That is safe but\\npessimistic for the zero-wait or ready dmem cycles that dominate the FPGA and\\nrandom-stall CoreMark runs: the load value is already formed in MEM while the\\ndependent instruction would be in EX. Previous frontend structural work lost\\ntoo much Fmax, so the better IPC target is a local backend bypass that leaves\\nIF next-PC timing untouched.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a MEM-stage load-data bypass into the EX operand forwarding network and\\nchange load-use hazard handling so the dependent instruction stalls only when\\nthe load is actually waiting on dmem or cannot produce a valid value. The MEM\\nstage should expose the sign/zero-extended load result plus a valid rd tag;\\nEX should select that value ahead of the older MEM/WB path for matching rs1 or\\nrs2 operands. This should remove common one-cycle load-use bubbles in\\nCoreMark while preserving the existing single-retire RVFI contract and keeping\\nbranch prediction/front-end timing unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire a MEM-stage load bypass value/tag/valid path from mem_stage into the EX forwarding network while preserving the nret=1 ...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Register forwarding selects\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural IPC attempts added frontend/backend muxing and lost more Fmax than they recovered in cycles. The remaining attractive structural target is the EX operand path itself: live rd/rs compare and priority-select logic currently feeds the ALU and low-half MUL muxes every cycle. Retiming those decisions into the ID/EX register should shorten the hot EX cone without changing branch prediction, load-use behavior, or the nret=1 RVFI contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural IPC attempts added frontend/backend muxing and lost more Fmax than they recovered in cycles. The remaining attractive structural target is the EX operand path itself: live rd/rs compare and priority-select logic currently feeds the ALU and low-half MUL muxes every cycle. Retiming those decisions into the ID/EX register should shorten the hot EX cone without changing branch prediction, load-use behavior, or the nret=1 RVFI contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute the forwarding source for the instruction while it is still in decode, then carry two registered forwarding-select fields in id_ex_t. Decode can compare the IF/ID rs addresses against the current ID/EX producer, which will be EX/MEM next cycle, and the current EX/MEM producer, which will be MEM/WB next cycle. The existing load-use bubble remains in place, so a load immediately ahead is not treated as an EX/MEM-forwardable producer. EX then uses only the registered select bits plus the existing forwarded data buses, removing the live comparator/priority network from the ALU and one-cycle MUL timing path while preserving identical IPC and stall semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add rs1/rs2 forwarding-select fields to the ID/EX pipeline bundle so forwarding decisions are registered with the instruction.\"...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Register WB forwarding data\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The r4 structural win registered forwarding selects, which suggests the EX\\noperand cone is still timing-sensitive after the slow RV32M work was moved\\noff the common path. MEM/WB forwarding still feeds EX through the live WB\\nwrite-data mux, so a dependent instruction forwarded from WB carries\\nmem_to_reg selection into the EX operand mux, ALU, and one-cycle MUL path.\\nThat mux is small, but it sits on the hottest every-cycle datapath and does\\nnot buy CoreMark any IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The r4 structural win registered forwarding selects, which suggests the EX\\noperand cone is still timing-sensitive after the slow RV32M work was moved\\noff the common path. MEM/WB forwarding still feeds EX through the live WB\\nwrite-data mux, so a dependent instruction forwarded from WB carries\\nmem_to_reg selection into the EX operand mux, ALU, and one-cycle MUL path.\\nThat mux is small, but it sits on the hottest every-cycle datapath and does\\nnot buy CoreMark any IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a registered final writeback value at the MEM/WB boundary and use it for\\nthe regfile write port, RVFI rd_wdata, and MEM/WB-to-EX forwarding. MEM still\\ncomputes load extension and ALU-vs-load selection before the MEM/WB flop; EX\\nthen sees only a registered 32-bit forwarding value rather than the live\\nWB-stage result mux. This preserves the five-stage retirement point, load-use\\nbubble policy, dmem hold semantics, and nret=1 RVFI contract while shortening\\nthe post-r4 EX operand path with no intentional cycle-count change.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a final writeback-data field to mem_wb_t so the MEM/WB boundary carries the value used for writes and forwarding.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and register the final WB data alongside exi...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Slim unused pipeline sidebands\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural wins came from shortening hot pipeline cones without adding\\nnew IPC machinery, while fetch buffers, load bypasses, and predictors lost\\nmore Fmax than they recovered. The current RTL still carries some metadata\\nthat is not consumed after EX, including EX/MEM branch_taken and\\nbranch_target fields plus an is_lui control bit that is redundant with\\nALU_LUI. Those dead sidebands add registers, mux fanout, and routing pressure\\non a design where small retiming changes have already moved fitness.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural wins came from shortening hot pipeline cones without adding\\nnew IPC machinery, while fetch buffers, load bypasses, and predictors lost\\nmore Fmax than they recovered. The current RTL still carries some metadata\\nthat is not consumed after EX, including EX/MEM branch_taken and\\nbranch_target fields plus an is_lui control bit that is redundant with\\nALU_LUI. Those dead sidebands add registers, mux fanout, and routing pressure\\non a design where small retiming changes have already moved fitness.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Prune the unused pipeline sidebands rather than changing execution behavior:\\nremove branch_taken and branch_target from ex_mem_t and stop registering them,\\nand remove the dead is_lui control bit from ctrl_t while keeping LUI legality\\nrepresented by ALU_LUI and the existing immediate path. Branch redirect and\\npc_next calculation still use local EX signals in the same cycle, so IPC,\\nstall behavior, traps, and the nret=1 RVFI contract are unchanged. The smaller\\npipeline payload should reduce EX/MEM fanout, backend register count, and\\nnextpnr routing pressure, yielding a modest Fmax-driven fitness gain.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the unused is_lui control bit and dead EX/MEM b...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"LUTRAM-backed unreset regfile\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added frontend prediction, fetch buffering,\\nMEM bypassing, or extra WB state regressed because they added muxing and\\ncontrol to already sensitive paths. The current register file is still a\\n32x32 reset-cleared, two-read flop array with write-first bypass muxes; that\\nreset loop and wide read muxing consume many FFs and routing resources even\\nthough RV32I only requires x0 to be zero after reset. Removing the\\nnon-architectural full-register reset is a localized structural change with\\nno intended CoreMark cycle-count impact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added frontend prediction, fetch buffering,\\nMEM bypassing, or extra WB state regressed because they added muxing and\\ncontrol to already sensitive paths. The current register file is still a\\n32x32 reset-cleared, two-read flop array with write-first bypass muxes; that\\nreset loop and wide read muxing consume many FFs and routing resources even\\nthough RV32I only requires x0 to be zero after reset. Removing the\\nnon-architectural full-register reset is a localized structural change with\\nno intended CoreMark cycle-count impact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Recode rtl/reg_file.sv as an unreset, duplicated two-read-bank register file\\nsuitable for distributed LUT RAM inference. Keep both banks written together,\\nkeep x0 hardwired to zero, and retain the explicit same-cycle write-first\\nbypass so the existing decode/forwarding contract is unchanged. With reset\\nremoved from the 31 writable registers, yosys/nextpnr should have less reset\\nfanout, fewer flop-based read muxes, and better packing/routing around the\\ndecode-to-ID/EX path, improving Fmax without changing IPC, RVFI ordering, or\\nany hard invariant.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Registered low-half MUL path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Prior structural wins came from taking rare RV32M work out of the common EX\\nresult cone, while frontend predictors, fetch buffering, MEM bypassing, and\\nregfile rewrites all lost more timing than they recovered. The remaining\\nhigh-cost logic still present on every fast EX timing analysis path is the\\nsingle-cycle 32x32 low-half MUL in alu.sv. CoreMark needs MUL support, but it\\ndoes not execute MUL every cycle, so a small MUL-only latency penalty may be\\ncheaper than keeping the multiplier in the ADD/load-address/branch ALU cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior structural wins came from taking rare RV32M work out of the common EX\\nresult cone, while frontend predictors, fetch buffering, MEM bypassing, and\\nregfile rewrites all lost more timing than they recovered. The remaining\\nhigh-cost logic still present on every fast EX timing analysis path is the\\nsingle-cycle 32x32 low-half MUL in alu.sv. CoreMark needs MUL support, but it\\ndoes not execute MUL every cycle, so a small MUL-only latency penalty may be\\ncheaper than keeping the multiplier in the ADD/load-address/branch ALU cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split ALU_MUL out of the combinational fast ALU and handle it with a tiny\\nregistered low-half multiply side path in EX. Non-MUL RV32I ALU operations,\\nbranch compares, load/store address generation, JAL/JALR link values, and the\\nexisting slow high-half/DIV/REM m_unit behavior should remain unchanged. When\\nALU_MUL reaches EX, the pipeline holds for the registered product, then emits\\nexactly one EX/MEM result with the original RVFI operands and order. Under\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS the side path should register the same formal substitute\\nexpression instead of the real product. This should remove the DSP/multiplier\\npath from the common ALU output mux and improve Tang Nano Fmax enough to\\noffset occasional MUL bubbles in CoreMark.\\n\", \"expected_impa...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Unified RV32M side unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The strongest structural gains so far came from removing RV32M arithmetic from\\nthe common EX-stage ALU cone, culminating in a registered low-half MUL side\\npath. That latest change improved Fmax, but it left EX with two separate M\\nside-unit handshakes, two result-valid paths, and a three-way result select for\\nlogic that is only active on M-extension instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The strongest structural gains so far came from removing RV32M arithmetic from\\nthe common EX-stage ALU cone, culminating in a registered low-half MUL side\\npath. That latest change improved Fmax, but it left EX with two separate M\\nside-unit handshakes, two result-valid paths, and a three-way result select for\\nlogic that is only active on M-extension instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Fold the standalone registered low-half MUL path into the existing RV32M side\\nunit while preserving the one-cycle registered MUL latency and the existing\\nmulti-cycle behavior for MULH/DIV/REM. EX should classify every M-extension\\ninstruction as one side-unit operation, use one start/result_valid/result_ready\\nhandshake, and feed EX/MEM through one registered M result path. This should\\nkeep the r8 MUL timing win, remove duplicate busy/control muxing from EX, and\\nslightly improve Tang Nano Fmax without changing CoreMark IPC except for the\\nalready-accepted MUL bubble.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the separate mul_unit and slow m_unit dispatch paths with a single M-extension side-unit handshake and one result mux path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/m_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add ALU_MUL support as a one-cycle registered low-half multiply result, including the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitute expression.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mul_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the standalone low-half MUL side ...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Posted store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes all stages whenever a store reaches MEM while\\nio_dmemReady is low, even though the store has no register result and many\\nfollowing CoreMark instructions are independent integer work. Prior\\nstructural attempts that added frontend prediction or load-use bypassing\\npaid too much timing cost, but store-side decoupling can target dStall IPC\\nloss with only local MEM/hazard changes and no IF next-PC cone growth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes all stages whenever a store reaches MEM while\\nio_dmemReady is low, even though the store has no register result and many\\nfollowing CoreMark instructions are independent integer work. Prior\\nstructural attempts that added frontend prediction or load-use bypassing\\npaid too much timing cost, but store-side decoupling can target dStall IPC\\nloss with only local MEM/hazard changes and no IF next-PC cone growth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry posted store buffer at the MEM stage. When a store is in\\nEX/MEM and dmem is not ready, capture its address, replicated write data,\\nmask, and RVFI memory fields, allow the store to retire once into MEM/WB,\\nand let younger non-memory instructions continue while the buffer drains to\\nthe external dmem port. Stall subsequent loads or stores until the buffered\\nstore has been accepted, preserving in-order memory semantics without a\\nload-forwarding network. This should hide a meaningful fraction of random\\nstore dStall cycles in CoreMark while keeping the nret=1 RVFI retirement\\ncontract unchanged and avoiding the frontend timing penalties that hurt\\nearlier predictor/buffer experiments.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add minimal store-buffer metadata fields or helper typedefs if needed, without changing the nret=1 RVFI port contr...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Split WB forwarding shadow\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Prior structural wins came from removing hot-path logic without adding new\\nfrontend prediction or memory-order machinery. The current MEM/WB register\\nretains every RVFI and datapath field during a dmem stall solely so a held\\ndependent instruction can keep seeing the previous WB forwarding value. That\\nmakes hold_mem_wb fan out across a wide retirement bundle even though only\\nthe WB forwarding data must survive the stall.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior structural wins came from removing hot-path logic without adding new\\nfrontend prediction or memory-order machinery. The current MEM/WB register\\nretains every RVFI and datapath field during a dmem stall solely so a held\\ndependent instruction can keep seeing the previous WB forwarding value. That\\nmakes hold_mem_wb fan out across a wide retirement bundle even though only\\nthe WB forwarding data must survive the stall.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the wide MEM/WB hold behavior with a narrow forwarding shadow for the\\nWB value used by EX-stage MEM/WB forwarding. During dmem stalls, MEM/WB can\\ncontinue capturing a valid-cleared bubble from the held EX/MEM load/store,\\nwhile a small shadow register preserves the previous wb_w_data for any held\\ndependent instruction whose ID/EX forwarding select is FWD_MEM_WB. This keeps\\nRVFI retirement precise, avoids double writes, preserves the existing load-use\\nand dmem-stall semantics, and should reduce high-fanout hold routing around\\nthe wide MEM/WB/RVFI bundle for a modest Fmax-driven gain.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"A