{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778835229084,"sessionID":"ses_1d5292540ffepjDgtvUr54dZ4p","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_jvLIjkFfSIn37HPoOzIefEnf","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"\"title\":|\"id\":|\"outcome\":|\"fitness\":|\"delta_pct\":|\"hypothesis\":","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench","include":"log.jsonl"},"output":"Found 675 matches (showing first 100)\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/kimi-k2_6/rep3/log.jsonl:\n  Line 1: {\"id\": \"baseline-bench-be29084\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 282.82, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9563, \"ff\": 1866, \"seeds\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-14T10:10:45.477521Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\n\n  Line 2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Guard ALU multipliers off critical path for non-M ops\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU unconditionally computes three 64-bit products on every\\ncycle, even for simple ADD/SUB/AND/OR/XOR/SLT/SLTU/SLL/SRL/SRA/LUI\\noperations. On the Tang Nano 20K (GW2AR-18C) these inferred multipliers\\nsit on the combinational critical path and limit Fmax. CoreMark is\\ndominated by integer ALU and memory ops, so the multiplier is almost\\nnever exercised, yet it still degrades timing for every other operation.\\nRemoving it from the critical path should raise Fmax with zero IPC risk.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU unconditionally computes three 64-bit products on every\\ncycle, even for simple ADD/SUB/AND/OR/XOR/SLT/SLTU/SLL/SRL/SRA/LUI\\noperations. On the Tang Nano 20K (GW2AR-18C) these inferred multipliers\\nsit on the combinational critical path and limit Fmax. CoreMark is\\ndominated by integer ALU and memory ops, so the multiplier is almost\\nnever exercised, yet it still degrades timing for every other operation.\\nRemoving it from the critical path should raise Fmax with zero IPC risk.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure alu.sv so the 64-bit multiply assignments (mul_ss, mul_uu,\\nmul_su) are only evaluated when the ALU operation is MUL, MULH, MULHU,\\nor MULHSU. For all other opcodes the products are tied to zero inside\\nthe always_comb block. This tells the synthesis tool that the multiplier\\nhardware is not needed on the fast-path case items (ADD, SUB, logic,\\nshifts, etc.), shortening the critical path and raising Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Guard the 64-bit multiply computations inside a conditional case block so they only execute for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU ops. Default the product variables to zero so the main output case statement remains unchanged.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5,...\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Cold RV32M High-Half and Divide Unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU unconditionally builds three independent 64-bit multiply\\nproducts (signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned, signed*unsigned) and a\\ncombinational 32-bit divider on every cycle. This bloats area to ~9500 LUT4\\nand places heavy arithmetic on the EX-stage critical path for *all*\\ninstructions, even simple ADD or BRANCH. CoreMark's timed path is multiply-\\nheavy but only uses the low-half MUL result; it is effectively cold for\\nDIV/REM and high-half multiplies (MULH/MULHU/MULHSU). Prior work on similar\\nRV32IM baselines showed that moving cold division off the universal ALU path\\nwas one of the largest pure Fmax wins. Extending that idea to also remove the\\ntwo unused high-half multipliers should compound the area and timing benefit\\nwithout touching the benchmark's hot IPC path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU unconditionally builds three independent 64-bit multiply\\nproducts (signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned, signed*unsigned) and a\\ncombinational 32-bit divider on every cycle. This bloats area to ~9500 LUT4\\nand places heavy arithmetic on the EX-stage critical path for *all*\\ninstructions, even simple ADD or BRANCH. CoreMark's timed path is multiply-\\nheavy but only uses the low-half MUL result; it is effectively cold for\\nDIV/REM and high-half multiplies (MULH/MULHU/MULHSU). Prior work on similar\\nRV32IM baselines showed that moving cold division off the universal ALU path\\nwas one of the largest pure Fmax wins. Extending that idea to also remove the\\ntwo unused high-half multipliers should compound the area and timing benefit\\nwithout touching the benchmark's hot IPC path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure the EX stage into a fast single-cycle integer ALU plus a\\ndedicated cold Multiply-Divide Unit (MDU).\\n\\n- The ALU keeps ADD/SUB/logic/shift/compare/LUI and the existing single-\\n  cycle low-half MUL (mul_uu[31:0]). Al...\n  Line 4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"32-entry 2-bit dynamic branch predictor in IF stage\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline always fetches pc+4 and only redirects after a branch resolves in EX,\\ncosting a 2-cycle bubble for every taken branch. CoreMark is loop-heavy; even a\\nsimple dynamic predictor eliminates most of these bubbles, directly improving IPC.\\nA 32-entry PHT adds only 64 flops and a small combinational read port, well within\\nthe ~400 LUT4 headroom on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline always fetches pc+4 and only redirects after a branch resolves in EX,\\ncosting a 2-cycle bubble for every taken branch. CoreMark is loop-heavy; even a\\nsimple dynamic predictor eliminates most of these bubbles, directly improving IPC.\\nA 32-entry PHT adds only 64 flops and a small combinational read port, well within\\nthe ~400 LUT4 headroom on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 32-entry 2-bit saturating counter branch predictor (PHT) indexed by PC[6:2].\\nIn the IF stage, detect conditional branches (opcode 1100011) and JAL (opcode 1101111);\\nextract the immediate via a small imm_gen instance and compute target = pc + imm.\\nQuery the PHT for branches; always predict JAL as taken. A new predict_taken bit is\\nadded to the IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles. In EX, redirect is asserted only on\\nmispredict (predicted != actual outcome), with the redirect target selecting the\\ncorrect path (branch target or pc+4). The PHT is updated when a branch resolves in EX.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 10, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predict_taken bit to if_id_t and id_ex_t pipeline structs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"New 32-entry 2-bit saturating counter PHT module with async read and synchronous update.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add branch/JAL detection, imm_gen instance, target adder, and predictor integ...\n  Line 5: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Pre-compute branch target and pc+4 in earlier stages\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The EX stage currently computes two 32-bit additions on its critical path:\\nbranch_target = pc + imm (for branches and JAL) and pc + 4 (for JAL/JALR\\nreturn address and pc_next on not-taken paths). These adders sit between\\nthe ID/EX register and the redirect/pc_next muxes, lengthening the path\\nfrom branch resolution back to the IF stage PC register. By pre-computing\\nthese values in IF (pc+4) and ID (pc+imm) and carrying them through the\\npipeline registers, we remove two 32-bit adders from the EX combinational\\ncloud. This shortens the redirect critical path and should raise worst-case\\nFmax seeds without any IPC change.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The EX stage currently computes two 32-bit additions on its critical path:\\nbranch_target = pc + imm (for branches and JAL) and pc + 4 (for JAL/JALR\\nreturn address and pc_next on not-taken paths). These adders sit between\\nthe ID/EX register and the redirect/pc_next muxes, lengthening the path\\nfrom branch resolution back to the IF stage PC register. By pre-computing\\nthese values in IF (pc+4) and ID (pc+imm) and carrying them through the\\npipeline registers, we remove two 32-bit adders from the EX combinational\\ncloud. This shortens the redirect critical path and should raise worst-case\\nFmax seeds without any IPC change.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add pc_plus4 to if_id_t and id_ex_t, and add branch_target to id_ex_t.\\nIn if_stage, compute out.pc_plus4 = pc + 4. In id_stage, compute\\nbranch_target = in.pc + imm and capture both pc_plus4 and branch_target\\ninto the ID/EX register. In ex_stage, replace every occurrence of\\nin.pc + 4 with in.pc_plus4 and every occurrence of in.pc + in.imm with\\nin.branch_target. This removes the pc+4 and pc+imm adders from EX,\\nleaving only the JALR rs1+imm adder and the ALU itself.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"},...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Early branch and jump resolution in ID stage\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current 5-stage pipeline resolves all control-flow instructions in EX,\\ncausing a 2-cycle bubble for every taken branch and jump. CoreMark's\\ntight loops and frequent function calls spend a significant fraction of\\ncycles recovering from these mispredicts. Moving branch condition\\nevaluation and jump target computation to the ID stage cuts the penalty\\nto 1 cycle, directly improving iterations/cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current 5-stage pipeline resolves all control-flow instructions in EX,\\ncausing a 2-cycle bubble for every taken branch and jump. CoreMark's\\ntight loops and frequent function calls spend a significant fraction of\\ncycles recovering from these mispredicts. Moving branch condition\\nevaluation and jump target computation to the ID stage cuts the penalty\\nto 1 cycle, directly improving iterations/cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Relocate unconditional jump (JAL/JALR) target calculation and conditional\\nbranch resolution from EX to ID. Add operand forwarding paths from\\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB into the ID stage so branches dependent on recent\\nresults resolve without stalling. Introduce a load-to-branch stall in\\nthe hazard unit when a branch in ID consumes a load result still in EX.\\nPipeline an early-redirect flag so EX does not re-assert flush on an\\ninstruction already redirected in ID.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add early_redirect bit to id_ex_t pipeline bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Insert branch target adder, branch condition comparator, and\\nforwarding muxes for rs1/rs2. Drive id_redirect and\\nid_redirect_target to IF stage.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept id_redirect/id_redirect_target and prioritize them over EX redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"des...\n  Line 7: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Static backward-taken predictor with early JAL resolution in IF\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline fetches sequentially and resolves all control flow in EX,\\nincurring a 2-cycle bubble for every taken branch and unconditional jump.\\nCoreMark is loop-heavy, so backward conditional branches (loop edges)\\ndominate the dynamic branch count. A purely static predictor