round the existing MEM/EX boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current pipeline pays a one-cycle load-use interlock whenever a load in\\nEX feeds either raw source field of the following instruction. CoreMark's hot\\nloops are load-heavy, and the successful recent changes show that this core\\nis already Fmax-competitive enough that recovering real CPI bubbles may be a\\nbetter structural bet than adding more prediction or buffering state.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added fetch queues, predictors, store buffers,\\nor multi-cycle multiply control regressed, so this proposal keeps the pipeline\\nshape and NRET=1 RVFI contract intact while only adding a local load-result\\nbypass around the existing MEM/EX boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a same-cycle MEM-to-EX forwarding path for loads. When a load is in MEM\\nand its data is available, feed the MEM-stage sign/zero-extended load result\\ninto the existing EX operand forwarding mux instead of forwarding the load's\\naddress from EX/MEM. Then remove the blanket load-use stall for consumers\\nthat can safely wait under the existing dmem stall mechanism.\\n\\nKeep correctness conservative for control-flow consumers: if a dependent\\nbranch or JALR would use a load value before the dmem response is...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Registered writeback result path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent winning changes improved fitness by removing late pipeline baggage\\nrather than adding predictors, queues, or buffers. The current MEM/WB stage\\nstill carries both alu_result and read_data plus a mem_to_reg control bit,\\nthen rebuilds the final writeback value through a combinational mux in WB.\\nThat mux output fans out to the register file, MEM/WB-to-EX forwarding, and\\nRVFI rd_wdata, so it can sit on the common dependent-instruction path even\\nthough the load-versus-ALU choice is already known when MEM/WB captures.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent winning changes improved fitness by removing late pipeline baggage\\nrather than adding predictors, queues, or buffers. The current MEM/WB stage\\nstill carries both alu_result and read_data plus a mem_to_reg control bit,\\nthen rebuilds the final writeback value through a combinational mux in WB.\\nThat mux output fans out to the register file, MEM/WB-to-EX forwarding, and\\nRVFI rd_wdata, so it can sit on the common dependent-instruction path even\\nthough the load-versus-ALU choice is already known when MEM/WB captures.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move final writeback-value selection into the MEM stage and store a single\\nregistered wb_data field in MEM/WB. Remove the separate MEM/WB read_data,\\nalu_result, and mem_to_reg payload/control used only to recreate that value\\none cycle later, and make wb_stage a simple valid/reg_write gate around the\\nregistered result.\\n\\nThis keeps pipeline timing, NRET=1 RVFI behavior, memory ordering, dmem-stall\\nhold semantics, and CoreMark instruction count unchanged while shortening the\\nMEM/WB-to-EX forwarding cone and trimming one 32-bit late-stage payload. The\\nchange follows the successful control-pruning direction but avoids the r8s0\\nmistake of increasing store-data fanout, because it only collapses mutually\\nexclusive writeback-result copies after MEM has alre...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Split writeback bypass from regfile reads\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The strongest positive bench changes have removed fanout and muxing from existing pipeline paths rather than adding predictors, buffers, or extra memory machinery. The register file still has write-first read bypass muxes on both asynchronous read ports, so every ID-stage source read carries same-cycle WB address compares and write-data muxing even though most dynamic reads do not hit the current WB instruction.\\n\\nPrior resetless-regfile work showed the reset tree was not the interesting part of this block; the remaining structural target is the read/write bypass coupling itself. Moving the rare same-cycle WB dependency into the existing EX forwarding network should shorten the imem-data-to-ID/EX register-file read path without changing CoreMark CPI.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The strongest positive bench changes have removed fanout and muxing from existing pipeline paths rather than adding predictors, buffers, or extra memory machinery. The register file still has write-first read bypass muxes on both asynchronous read ports, so every ID-stage source read carries same-cycle WB address compares and write-data muxing even though most dynamic reads do not hit the current WB instruction.\\n\\nPrior resetless-regfile work showed the reset tree was not the interesting part of this block; the remaining structural target is the read/write bypass coupling itself. Moving the rare same-cycle WB dependency into the existing EX forwarding network should shorten the imem-data-to-ID/EX register-file read path without changing CoreMark CPI.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make rtl/reg_file.sv a simple 2R1W architectural array: x0 remains hardwired to zero, writes remain synchronous, but read ports no longer mux in same-cycle w_data. Add a one-cycle registered WB-bypass source in core.sv that captures each real WB write, extend forward_unit.sv to select it as the...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Wider resetless ifetch replay cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The latest winning change showed that the safest remaining cycle gain is not\\nbranch prediction, but replaying immutable hot-loop instructions when the\\nrandom instruction bus stalls. The current replay table is only 8 entries and\\ndirect-mapped, so CoreMark loop bodies can still conflict on pc[4:2] and fall\\nback to bubbles even after the instruction was previously fetched.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added queues, store buffers, bypass paths, or\\npredictor metadata regressed because they lengthened common control paths.\\nA larger direct-mapped replay cache keeps the same no-repair frontend shape\\nwhile improving the hit rate of the one mechanism that has already produced a\\nlarge fitness win.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The latest winning change showed that the safest remaining cycle gain is not\\nbranch prediction, but replaying immutable hot-loop instructions when the\\nrandom instruction bus stalls. The current replay table is only 8 entries and\\ndirect-mapped, so CoreMark loop bodies can still conflict on pc[4:2] and fall\\nback to bubbles even after the instruction was previously fetched.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added queues, store buffers, bypass paths, or\\npredictor metadata regressed because they lengthened common control paths.\\nA larger direct-mapped replay cache keeps the same no-repair frontend shape\\nwhile improving the hit rate of the one mechanism that has already produced a\\nlarge fitness win.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rebuild the IF replay table as a 16-entry direct-mapped, valid-gated replay\\ncache indexed by pc[5:2]. Fill it only on accepted io_imemReady fetches, and\\non io_imemReady=0 hits keep supplying the cached instruction through the\\nexisting imem_effective_ready path. Do not add branch prediction metadata,\\ntarget repair state, or any EX feedback.\\n\\nMake the replay tag and instruction payload arrays reset...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Warm replay during backend stalls\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current 8-entry IF replay table produced the largest recent improvement by\\nconverting random instruction-bus stalls into useful hot-loop instructions.\\nFollow-up attempts to widen or reset-retime the table regressed, which points\\naway from adding capacity on the fetch critical path and toward improving how\\noften the existing table is populated.\\n\\nBackend stalls from dmem backpressure, load-use bubbles, and iterative divide\\nalready stop decode from consuming the current fetch slot. Those cycles can be\\nused to issue harmless sequential instruction fetches and train the replay\\ntable, because instruction memory is immutable and wrong-path replay fills do\\nnot affect architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current 8-entry IF replay table produced the largest recent improvement by\\nconverting random instruction-bus stalls into useful hot-loop instructions.\\nFollow-up attempts to widen or reset-retime the table regressed, which points\\naway from adding capacity on the fetch critical path and toward improving how\\noften the existing table is populated.\\n\\nBackend stalls from dmem backpressure, load-use bubbles, and iterative divide\\nalready stop decode from consuming the current fetch slot. Those cycles can be\\nused to issue harmless sequential instruction fetches and train the replay\\ntable, because instruction memory is immutable and wrong-path replay fills do\\nnot affect architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add an opportunistic replay warmer inside IF. When the architectural PC is\\nheld for a non-redirect backend stall and the instruction bus is ready, drive\\nio_imemAddr from a small sequential prefetch cursor instead of the held PC and\\nfill the existing replay table with the returned instruction. Keep out.pc,\\nRVFI-visible instruction flow, redirect handling, and decode consumption tied\\nto the architectural PC; the wa...\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Register fetch-decode boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench core still has an unusually long frontend timing path: the IF\\nstage emits a combinational IF/ID payload, so io_imemData or replay-table data\\nflows through the fetch mux, decoder, immediate generator, register-file read\\naddresses, and into the ID/EX register in one cycle. Recent structural history\\nshows that adding predictors, queues, wider replay, and bypass paths usually\\nlost timing, while pruning existing stage plumbing helped. A minimal pipeline\\nboundary is therefore a better structural bet than adding more speculation.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench core still has an unusually long frontend timing path: the IF\\nstage emits a combinational IF/ID payload, so io_imemData or replay-table data\\nflows through the fetch mux, decoder, immediate generator, register-file read\\naddresses, and into the ID/EX register in one cycle. Recent structural history\\nshows that adding predictors, queues, wider replay, and bypass paths usually\\nlost timing, while pruning existing stage plumbing helped. A minimal pipeline\\nboundary is therefore a better structural bet than adding more speculation.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a real registered IF/ID boundary owned by the fetch stage, while keeping\\nthe existing 8-entry accepted-fetch replay table and NRET=1 retirement contract.\\nFetch should capture pc/instruction/valid only when the current fetch is usable\\nvia io_imemReady or a replay hit and ID can accept it; redirects flush the IF/ID\\nregister and update the PC to the resolved target. ID then decodes only the\\nregistered IF/ID payload, shortening the common FPGA path from instruction data\\nto decoded ID/EX controls and regfile source reads.\\n\\nThis will likely add one extra bubble on taken redirects compared with the\\ncurrent combinational IF/ID design, so the implementation should keep the\\nbranch/hazard machinery conservati...\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Valid-gated fetch bubbles\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Prior structural attempts that added predictors, queues, store buffers, or wider replay state generally regressed because they put more muxing and control on already-sensitive paths. The successful changes instead pruned existing pipeline state or let the accepted-fetch replay table hide iStalls without branch-repair metadata.\\n\\nThe current frontend still forces every flush, redirect, or imem-miss bubble through a 32-bit NOP mux before decode. That mux sits directly on the imem/replay-data to decoder, immediate generator, regfile-address, and ID/EX capture path even though invalid instructions never retire.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior structural attempts that added predictors, queues, store buffers, or wider replay state generally regressed because they put more muxing and control on already-sensitive paths. The successful changes instead pruned existing pipeline state or let the accepted-fetch replay table hide iStalls without branch-repair metadata.\\n\\nThe current frontend still forces every flush, redirect, or imem-miss bubble through a 32-bit NOP mux before decode. That mux sits directly on the imem/replay-data to decoder, immediate generator, regfile-address, and ID/EX capture path even though invalid instructions never retire.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Represent frontend bubbles with the existing valid bit instead of replacing the instruction word with ADDI x0,x0,0. IF should always pass the fetched or replayed instruction bits forward and set out.valid low for flush/redirect/imem-miss cycles. ID should zero side-effect controls and destination metadata whenever in.valid is low, and the load-use hazard should be gated by IF/ID and ID/EX validity so stale invalid instruction bits cannot create false interlocks.\\n\\nThis removes a normal-cycle 32-bit instruction mux from the fetch-to-decode cone and moves correctness onto compact valid/control gat...\n  Line 45: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r15s1\", \"title\": \"Operand-aware load interlock\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current hazard unit stalls after every load when the load destination\\nmatches either raw register field of the following instruction. That is\\nconservative for RVFI, but it also creates false bubbles for immediates,\\njumps, upper-immediate ops, fences, and traps whose bit fields alias the\\nload rd without being execution operands. CoreMark has many load-adjacent\\npointer and immediate operations, so recovering even a small fraction of\\nthese bubbles can improve iterations per cycle.\\n\\nA prior false-load-use attempt broke because unused raw rs1/rs2 fields still\\nneed correct RVFI register data. The structural opportunity remains if the\\ncore separates execution operand readiness from RVFI source reporting instead\\nof treating every raw field as a data dependency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current hazard unit stalls after every load when the load destination\\nmatches either raw register field of the following instruction. That is\\nconservative for RVFI, but it also creates false bubbles for immediates,\\njumps, upper-immediate ops, fences, and traps whose bit fields alias the\\nload rd without being execution operands. CoreMark has many load-adjacent\\npointer and immediate operations, so recovering even a small fraction of\\nthese bubbles can improve iterations per cycle.\\n\\nA prior false-load-use attempt broke because unused raw rs1/rs2 fields still\\nneed correct RVFI register data. The structural opportunity remains if the\\ncore separates execution operand readiness from RVFI source reporting instead\\nof treating every raw field as a data dependency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add explicit source-use metadata for the instruction entering ID/EX and use\\nit only for the load-use interlock. Branches, stores, JALR, and R-type ops\\nstill stall when they consume a load result, but I-type immediates, LUI,\\nAUIPC, JAL, FENCE, EBREAK, and illegal instruct...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep1/log.jsonl:\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple slow RV32M ops from EX\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage routes every instruction through a fully combinational\\nRV32IM ALU that includes parallel high-half multipliers plus DIV/REM logic.\\nCoreMark's timed region is dominated by integer ALU, load/store, branch, and\\nlow-half MUL work, while DIV/REM and high-half multiply are either absent or\\nrare. Keeping those slow operators on the single-cycle EX timing path likely\\ndepresses Fmax without improving CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage routes every instruction through a fully combinational\\nRV32IM ALU that includes parallel high-half multipliers plus DIV/REM logic.\\nCoreMark's timed region is dominated by integer ALU, load/store, branch, and\\nlow-half MUL work, while DIV/REM and high-half multiply are either absent or\\nrare. Keeping those slow operators on the single-cycle EX timing path likely\\ndepresses Fmax without improving CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure EX into a fast single-cycle I-ALU plus a separate RV32M slow-unit.\\nKeep ALU_MUL's low-half multiply on a one-cycle fast path for CoreMark, but\\nmove MULH/MULHSU/MULHU and DIV/REM/REMU into a shared multi-cycle unit that\\nstalls the in-order pipeline only while those rare operations are in flight.\\nThis should remove the divider and redundant high-multiply logic from the\\ncommon EX critical path, reduce LUT pressure near the current budget, and\\nraise Fmax with minimal impact on the benchmark instruction stream.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Reduce the combinational ALU to RV32I operations plus the one-cycle MUL low-half fast path; remove DIV/REM and high-half multiply from the common result mux.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch slow RV32M operations to a separate multi-cycle unit, hold EX state until result_valid, and ...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Elastic IF fetch buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end ties instruction fetch directly to decode: when ID is\\nstalled for load-use, dmem backpressure, or a slow M operation, the PC also\\nholds and no useful instruction fetch can be performed. With the tournament\\ncosim injecting about 22 percent random instruction stalls, those missed\\nopportunities turn independent stalls into serialized bubbles. The failed\\nstatic predictor showed that adding next-PC computation in IF is too costly,\\nso the better structural target is decoupling fetch from decode without\\nadding branch-target logic to the IF critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end ties instruction fetch directly to decode: when ID is\\nstalled for load-use, dmem backpressure, or a slow M operation, the PC also\\nholds and no useful instruction fetch can be performed. With the tournament\\ncosim injecting about 22 percent random instruction stalls, those missed\\nopportunities turn independent stalls into serialized bubbles. The failed\\nstatic predictor showed that adding next-PC computation in IF is too costly,\\nso the better structural target is decoupling fetch from decode without\\nadding branch-target logic to the IF critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a tiny elastic fetch buffer between IF and ID. The buffer should keep\\nthe existing direct fall-through path when decode is ready, but when ID/EX is\\nstalled it should let IF continue fetching sequential instructions into one\\nor two registered slots until the buffer is full. Decode then consumes from\\nthe buffer before requesting a fresh instruction, hiding later imemReady=0\\ncycles behind earlier load-use, dmem, or slow-M stalls. Redirects flush the\\nbuffer and restart fetch at the resolved target, preserving precise single\\nretirement and the existing RVFI channel-0 contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"chang...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-to-EX load-use bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline always inserts a bubble when an instruction\\nconsumes the destination of the immediately preceding load. That is safe but\\npessimistic for the zero-wait or ready dmem cycles that dominate the FPGA and\\nrandom-stall CoreMark runs: the load value is already formed in MEM while the\\ndependent instruction would be in EX. Previous frontend structural work lost\\ntoo much Fmax, so the better IPC target is a local backend bypass that leaves\\nIF next-PC timing untouched.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline always inserts a bubble when an instruction\\nconsumes the destination of the immediately preceding load. That is safe but\\npessimistic for the zero-wait or ready dmem cycles that dominate the FPGA and\\nrandom-stall CoreMark runs: the load value is already formed in MEM while the\\ndependent instruction would be in EX. Previous frontend structural work lost\\ntoo much Fmax, so the better IPC target is a local backend bypass that leaves\\nIF next-PC timing untouched.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a MEM-stage load-data bypass into the EX operand forwarding network and\\nchange load-use hazard handling so the dependent instruction stalls only when\\nthe load is actually waiting on dmem or cannot produce a valid value. The MEM\\nstage should expose the sign/zero-extended load result plus a valid rd tag;\\nEX should select that value ahead of the older MEM/WB path for matching rs1 or\\nrs2 operands. This should remove common one-cycle load-use bubbles in\\nCoreMark while preserving the existing single-retire RVFI contract and keeping\\nbranch prediction/front-end timing unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire a MEM-stage load bypass value/tag/valid path from mem_stage into the EX forwarding network while preserving the nret=1 ...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Register forwarding selects\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural IPC attempts added frontend/backend muxing and lost more Fmax than they recovered in cycles. The remaining attractive structural target is the EX operand path itself: live rd/rs compare and priority-select logic currently feeds the ALU and low-half MUL muxes every cycle. Retiming those decisions into the ID/EX register should shorten the hot EX cone without changing branch prediction, load-use behavior, or the nret=1 RVFI contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural IPC attempts added frontend/backend muxing and lost more Fmax than they recovered in cycles. The remaining attractive structural target is the EX operand path itself: live rd/rs compare and priority-select logic currently feeds the ALU and low-half MUL muxes every cycle. Retiming those decisions into the ID/EX register should shorten the hot EX cone without changing branch prediction, load-use behavior, or the nret=1 RVFI contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute the forwarding source for the instruction while it is still in decode, then carry two registered forwarding-select fields in id_ex_t. Decode can compare the IF/ID rs addresses against the current ID/EX producer, which will be EX/MEM next cycle, and the current EX/MEM producer, which will be MEM/WB next cycle. The existing load-use bubble remains in place, so a load immediately ahead is not treated as an EX/MEM-forwardable producer. EX then uses only the registered select bits plus the existing forwarded data buses, removing the live comparator/priority network from the ALU and one-cycle MUL timing path while preserving identical IPC and stall semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add rs1/rs2 forwarding-select fields to the ID/EX pipeline bundle so forwarding decisions are registered with the instruction.\"...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Register WB forwarding data\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The r4 structural win registered forwarding selects, which suggests the EX\\noperand cone is still timing-sensitive after the slow RV32M work was moved\\noff the common path. MEM/WB forwarding still feeds EX through the live WB\\nwrite-data mux, so a dependent instruction forwarded from WB carries\\nmem_to_reg selection into the EX operand mux, ALU, and one-cycle MUL path.\\nThat mux is small, but it sits on the hottest every-cycle datapath and does\\nnot buy CoreMark any IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The r4 structural win registered forwarding selects, which suggests the EX\\noperand cone is still timing-sensitive after the slow RV32M work was moved\\noff the common path. MEM/WB forwarding still feeds EX through the live WB\\nwrite-data mux, so a dependent instruction forwarded from WB carries\\nmem_to_reg selection into the EX operand mux, ALU, and one-cycle MUL path.\\nThat mux is small, but it sits on the hottest every-cycle datapath and does\\nnot buy CoreMark any IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a registered final writeback value at the MEM/WB boundary and use it for\\nthe regfile write port, RVFI rd_wdata, and MEM/WB-to-EX forwarding. MEM still\\ncomputes load extension and ALU-vs-load selection before the MEM/WB flop; EX\\nthen sees only a registered 32-bit forwarding value rather than the live\\nWB-stage result mux. This preserves the five-stage retirement point, load-use\\nbubble policy, dmem hold semantics, and nret=1 RVFI contract while shortening\\nthe post-r4 EX operand path with no intentional cycle-count change.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a final writeback-data field to mem_wb_t so the MEM/WB boundary carries the value used for writes and forwarding.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and register the final WB data alongside exi...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Slim unused pipeline sidebands\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural wins came from shortening hot pipeline cones without adding\\nnew IPC machinery, while fetch buffers, load bypasses, and predictors lost\\nmore Fmax than they recovered. The current RTL still carries some metadata\\nthat is not consumed after EX, including EX/MEM branch_taken and\\nbranch_target fields plus an is_lui control bit that is redundant with\\nALU_LUI. Those dead sidebands add registers, mux fanout, and routing pressure\\non a design where small retiming changes have already moved fitness.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural wins came from shortening hot pipeline cones without adding\\nnew IPC machinery, while fetch buffers, load bypasses, and predictors lost\\nmore Fmax than they recovered. The current RTL still carries some metadata\\nthat is not consumed after EX, including EX/MEM branch_taken and\\nbranch_target fields plus an is_lui control bit that is redundant with\\nALU_LUI. Those dead sidebands add registers, mux fanout, and routing pressure\\non a design where small retiming changes have already moved fitness.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Prune the unused pipeline sidebands rather than changing execution behavior:\\nremove branch_taken and branch_target from ex_mem_t and stop registering them,\\nand remove the dead is_lui control bit from ctrl_t while keeping LUI legality\\nrepresented by ALU_LUI and the existing immediate path. Branch redirect and\\npc_next calculation still use local EX signals in the same cycle, so IPC,\\nstall behavior, traps, and the nret=1 RVFI contract are unchanged. The smaller\\npipeline payload should reduce EX/MEM fanout, backend register count, and\\nnextpnr routing pressure, yielding a modest Fmax-driven fitness gain.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the unused is_lui control bit and dead EX/MEM b...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"LUTRAM-backed unreset regfile\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added frontend prediction, fetch buffering,\\nMEM bypassing, or extra WB state regressed because they added muxing and\\ncontrol to already sensitive paths. The current register file is still a\\n32x32 reset-cleared, two-read flop array with write-first bypass muxes; that\\nreset loop and wide read muxing consume many FFs and routing resources even\\nthough RV32I only requires x0 to be zero after reset. Removing the\\nnon-architectural full-register reset is a localized structural change with\\nno intended CoreMark cycle-count impact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added frontend prediction, fetch buffering,\\nMEM bypassing, or extra WB state regressed because they added muxing and\\ncontrol to already sensitive paths. The current register file is still a\\n32x32 reset-cleared, two-read flop array with write-first bypass muxes; that\\nreset loop and wide read muxing consume many FFs and routing resources even\\nthough RV32I only requires x0 to be zero after reset. Removing the\\nnon-architectural full-register reset is a localized structural change with\\nno intended CoreMark cycle-count impact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Recode rtl/reg_file.sv as an unreset, duplicated two-read-bank register file\\nsuitable for distributed LUT RAM inference. Keep both banks written together,\\nkeep x0 hardwired to zero, and retain the explicit same-cycle write-first\\nbypass so the existing decode/forwarding contract is unchanged. With reset\\nremoved from the 31 writable registers, yosys/nextpnr should have less reset\\nfanout, fewer flop-based read muxes, and better packing/routing around the\\ndecode-to-ID/EX path, improving Fmax without changing IPC, RVFI ordering, or\\nany hard invariant.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Registered low-half MUL path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Prior structural wins came from taking rare RV32M work out of the common EX\\nresult cone, while frontend predictors, fetch buffering, MEM bypassing, and\\nregfile rewrites all lost more timing than they recovered. The remaining\\nhigh-cost logic still present on every fast EX timing analysis path is the\\nsingle-cycle 32x32 low-half MUL in alu.sv. CoreMark needs MUL support, but it\\ndoes not execute MUL every cycle, so a small MUL-only latency penalty may be\\ncheaper than keeping the multiplier in the ADD/load-address/branch ALU cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior structural wins came from taking rare RV32M work out of the common EX\\nresult cone, while frontend predictors, fetch buffering, MEM bypassing, and\\nregfile rewrites all lost more timing than they recovered. The remaining\\nhigh-cost logic still present on every fast EX timing analysis path is the\\nsingle-cycle 32x32 low-half MUL in alu.sv. CoreMark needs MUL support, but it\\ndoes not execute MUL every cycle, so a small MUL-only latency penalty may be\\ncheaper than keeping the multiplier in the ADD/load-address/branch ALU cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split ALU_MUL out of the combinational fast ALU and handle it with a tiny\\nregistered low-half multiply side path in EX. Non-MUL RV32I ALU operations,\\nbranch compares, load/store address generation, JAL/JALR link values, and the\\nexisting slow high-half/DIV/REM m_unit behavior should remain unchanged. When\\nALU_MUL reaches EX, the pipeline holds for the registered product, then emits\\nexactly one EX/MEM result with the original RVFI operands and order. Under\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS the side path should register the same formal substitute\\nexpression instead of the real product. This should remove the DSP/multiplier\\npath from the common ALU output mux and improve Tang Nano Fmax enough to\\noffset occasional MUL bubbles in CoreMark.\\n\", \"expected_impa...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Unified RV32M side unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The strongest structural gains so far came from removing RV32M arithmetic from\\nthe common EX-stage ALU cone, culminating in a registered low-half MUL side\\npath. That latest change improved Fmax, but it left EX with two separate M\\nside-unit handshakes, two result-valid paths, and a three-way result select for\\nlogic that is only active on M-extension instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The strongest structural gains so far came from removing RV32M arithmetic from\\nthe common EX-stage ALU cone, culminating in a registered low-half MUL side\\npath. That latest change improved Fmax, but it left EX with two separate M\\nside-unit handshakes, two result-valid paths, and a three-way result select for\\nlogic that is only active on M-extension instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Fold the standalone registered low-half MUL path into the existing RV32M side\\nunit while preserving the one-cycle registered MUL latency and the existing\\nmulti-cycle behavior for MULH/DIV/REM. EX should classify every M-extension\\ninstruction as one side-unit operation, use one start/result_valid/result_ready\\nhandshake, and feed EX/MEM through one registered M result path. This should\\nkeep the r8 MUL timing win, remove duplicate busy/control muxing from EX, and\\nslightly improve Tang Nano Fmax without changing CoreMark IPC except for the\\nalready-accepted MUL bubble.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the separate mul_unit and slow m_unit dispatch paths with a single M-extension side-unit handshake and one result mux path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/m_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add ALU_MUL support as a one-cycle registered low-half multiply result, including the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitute expression.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mul_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the standalone low-half MUL side ...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Posted store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes all stages whenever a store reaches MEM while\\nio_dmemReady is low, even though the store has no register result and many\\nfollowing CoreMark instructions are independent integer work. Prior\\nstructural attempts that added frontend prediction or load-use bypassing\\npaid too much timing cost, but store-side decoupling can target dStall IPC\\nloss with only local MEM/hazard changes and no IF next-PC cone growth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes all stages whenever a store reaches MEM while\\nio_dmemReady is low, even though the store has no register result and many\\nfollowing CoreMark instructions are independent integer work. Prior\\nstructural attempts that added frontend prediction or load-use bypassing\\npaid too much timing cost, but store-side decoupling can target dStall IPC\\nloss with only local MEM/hazard changes and no IF next-PC cone growth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry posted store buffer at the MEM stage. When a store is in\\nEX/MEM and dmem is not ready, capture its address, replicated write data,\\nmask, and RVFI memory fields, allow the store to retire once into MEM/WB,\\nand let younger non-memory instructions continue while the buffer drains to\\nthe external dmem port. Stall subsequent loads or stores until the buffered\\nstore has been accepted, preserving in-order memory semantics without a\\nload-forwarding network. This should hide a meaningful fraction of random\\nstore dStall cycles in CoreMark while keeping the nret=1 RVFI retirement\\ncontract unchanged and avoiding the frontend timing penalties that hurt\\nearlier predictor/buffer experiments.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add minimal store-buffer metadata fields or helper typedefs if needed, without changing the nret=1 RVFI port contr...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Split WB forwarding shadow\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Prior structural wins came from removing hot-path logic without adding new\\nfrontend prediction or memory-order machinery. The current MEM/WB register\\nretains every RVFI and datapath field during a dmem stall solely so a held\\ndependent instruction can keep seeing the previous WB forwarding value. That\\nmakes hold_mem_wb fan out across a wide retirement bundle even though only\\nthe WB forwarding data must survive the stall.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior structural wins came from removing hot-path logic without adding new\\nfrontend prediction or memory-order machinery. The current MEM/WB register\\nretains every RVFI and datapath field during a dmem stall solely so a held\\ndependent instruction can keep seeing the previous WB forwarding value. That\\nmakes hold_mem_wb fan out across a wide retirement bundle even though only\\nthe WB forwarding data must survive the stall.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the wide MEM/WB hold behavior with a narrow forwarding shadow for the\\nWB value used by EX-stage MEM/WB forwarding. During dmem stalls, MEM/WB can\\ncontinue capturing a valid-cleared bubble from the held EX/MEM load/store,\\nwhile a small shadow register preserves the previous wb_w_data for any held\\ndependent instruction whose ID/EX forwarding select is FWD_MEM_WB. This keeps\\nRVFI retirement precise, avoids double writes, preserves the existing load-use\\nand dmem-stall semantics, and should reduce high-fanout hold routing around\\nthe wide MEM/WB/RVFI bundle for a modest Fmax-driven gain.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a narrow WB-forwarding shadow register and feed EX with the shadowed value only while MEM/WB is valid-cleared for a dmem stall.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Change dmem-stall handling so MEM/WB no longer retains all data fie...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Predecode M dispatch flags\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The best structural gains for this core came from keeping RV32M arithmetic\\noff the common EX path and from registering forwarding decisions before EX.\\nThe current EX stage still re-decodes the registered ALU op every cycle to\\nclassify low-half MUL versus slow MULH/DIV/REM work, and those compare\\nresults feed the EX stall, start, ready, and result-select cones. A small\\nID-stage retime can shorten that control path without changing CoreMark IPC\\nor adding predictor, memory, or bypass machinery that has regressed before.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best structural gains for this core came from keeping RV32M arithmetic\\noff the common EX path and from registering forwarding decisions before EX.\\nThe current EX stage still re-decodes the registered ALU op every cycle to\\nclassify low-half MUL versus slow MULH/DIV/REM work, and those compare\\nresults feed the EX stall, start, ready, and result-select cones. A small\\nID-stage retime can shorten that control path without changing CoreMark IPC\\nor adding predictor, memory, or bypass machinery that has regressed before.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add registered M-dispatch metadata to the ID/EX payload: one flag for\\nALU_MUL and one flag for the slow high-half/DIV/REM RV32M group, computed\\nonce in decode from the already-decoded alu_op and valid legality context.\\nEX should use these registered flags for mul_start, m_start, ex_slow_busy,\\nresult_ready, operand latching, and the M-result select, while preserving\\nthe separate one-cycle mul_unit and the existing multi-cycle m_unit. This\\nfollows the proven pattern of moving small compare/priority decisions out\\nof the EX cone, avoids reconnecting MUL to the slow shared unit, and should\\nprovide a modest Fmax-driven fitness gain with no intentional cycle-count\\nchange.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\":...\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Segment RVFI order counter\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The FPGA Fmax wrapper deliberately XOR-reduces all RVFI outputs, so the\\nretirement sidecar remains live in timing even though it does not affect\\nCoreMark execution. The current core increments rvfi_order with a single\\n64-bit +1 operation on every retirement, creating a wide carry path in a\\ndesign where recent useful gains came from removing or retiming hot cones\\nwithout adding prediction, buffering, or bypass machinery.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The FPGA Fmax wrapper deliberately XOR-reduces all RVFI outputs, so the\\nretirement sidecar remains live in timing even though it does not affect\\nCoreMark execution. The current core increments rvfi_order with a single\\n64-bit +1 operation on every retirement, creating a wide carry path in a\\ndesign where recent useful gains came from removing or retiming hot cones\\nwithout adding prediction, buffering, or bypass machinery.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the monolithic rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'd1 update with an\\nexact segmented retirement-order generator, for example four 16-bit limbs\\nor two 32-bit halves with explicit carry enables. The channel-0 RVFI output\\nstill presents the full concatenated 64-bit order before increment, reset\\nstill starts at zero, and each valid retirement still advances the order by\\nexactly one with no gaps or duplicates. This should shorten the retirement\\ncounter register-to-register path and reduce RVFI-side routing pressure in\\nthe Fmax wrapper while leaving the pipeline, CoreMark cycle count, nret=1\\nport contract, trap behavior, and memory semantics unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Retire through an exact segmented 64-bit RVFI order counter instead of a single wide +1 adder, preserving channel-0 order semantics and all existing RVFI fields.\"}]}, \"expected_impac...\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Registered RVFI sidecar\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The FPGA benchmark wrapper keeps the RVFI outputs live by reducing them, so\\nretirement metadata can still influence Fmax even though it does not drive\\nexecution. Prior structural wins came from removing load from hot EX/RV32M\\ncones, while prediction, buffering, MEM bypassing, store buffering, and\\nregfile rewrites repeatedly lost more timing than they recovered. A narrow\\nremaining structural target is the direct fanout from MEM/WB and the live WB\\nresult mux into the wide RVFI output bus.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The FPGA benchmark wrapper keeps the RVFI outputs live by reducing them, so\\nretirement metadata can still influence Fmax even though it does not drive\\nexecution. Prior structural wins came from removing load from hot EX/RV32M\\ncones, while prediction, buffering, MEM bypassing, store buffering, and\\nregfile rewrites repeatedly lost more timing than they recovered. A narrow\\nremaining structural target is the direct fanout from MEM/WB and the live WB\\nresult mux into the wide RVFI output bus.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-cycle RVFI sidecar register stage in rtl/core.sv. Capture the\\ncurrent MEM/WB retirement fields, final rd_wdata, memory masks/data, trap\\nstate, and order into dedicated RVFI output flops, then drive io_rvfi_*_0 only\\nfrom those flops while incrementing rvfi_order when a MEM/WB retirement is\\naccepted into the sidecar.\\nThe architectural trace is delayed by one cycle but remains strictly in order\\nwith the same nret=1 channel-0 contract and the same trap, memory, and rd\\nvalues. This should reduce MEM/WB register fanout into the FPGA wrapper's\\nRVFI XOR cone and improve placement/timing without changing IF, EX, MEM,\\ndmem-stall, branch, or CoreMark IPC behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered RVFI sidecar/outpu...\n  Line 45: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r15s1\", \"title\": \"Direct-mapped IF hit cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Prior structural attempts show that this core benefits most when a change\\nremoves repeated hot-path stalls without adding branch prediction or broad\\nbypass machinery. The failed elastic IF buffer could only overlap fetch\\nwith other stalls, so it did not directly eliminate the tournament's random\\nimemReady bubbles. CoreMark repeatedly executes a small set of hot loop\\ninstruction PCs, making repeated instruction fetch stalls a promising target.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior structural attempts show that this core benefits most when a change\\nremoves repeated hot-path stalls without adding branch prediction or broad\\nbypass machinery. The failed elastic IF buffer could only overlap fetch\\nwith other stalls, so it did not directly eliminate the tournament's random\\nimemReady bubbles. CoreMark repeatedly executes a small set of hot loop\\ninstruction PCs, making repeated instruction fetch stalls a promising target.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small direct-mapped instruction hit cache inside the IF stage. On a\\ntag-valid hit for the current PC, IF supplies the cached instruction and\\ntreats fetch as ready even when the external io_imemReady is low; on a miss,\\nit preserves the current behavior, waits for io_imemReady, fills the entry,\\nand then advances. Redirects keep the existing EX-resolved flush behavior,\\nwith no branch predictor, no target lookup, and no suppression metadata.\\nThis should recover many random imem-stall cycles in CoreMark hot loops\\nwhile keeping the nret=1 RVFI contract, retirement ordering, traps, and\\ndmem semantics unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small reset-valid direct-mapped instruction cache keyed by PC, serve cached hits independent of external imemReady, and fill entries on ready misses while preserving red...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep3/log.jsonl:\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move RV32M to multicycle side unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage contains full combinational RV32M multiply and divide logic inside the same ALU case as single-cycle integer operations. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely contributes a long critical path and high LUT pressure even though CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by simple integer, branch, and memory operations. Removing the divider and wide multiplier from the regular EX datapath should improve Fmax and may also improve placement by reducing EX fan-in.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage contains full combinational RV32M multiply and divide logic inside the same ALU case as single-cycle integer operations. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely contributes a long critical path and high LUT pressure even though CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by simple integer, branch, and memory operations. Removing the divider and wide multiplier from the regular EX datapath should improve Fmax and may also improve placement by reducing EX fan-in.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure the pipeline so RV32M operations dispatch from EX into a dedicated multicycle M unit while ordinary RV32I ALU, branch, jump, and address-generation operations remain single-cycle. When an M instruction reaches EX, hold IF/ID/EX behind it until the unit returns the result, then capture that result into EX/MEM and retire normally through the existing MEM/WB and RVFI path. This should preserve single-retire ordering and correctness while replacing the worst combinational arithmetic path with shorter iterative or staged logic; the added stalls should be infrequent enough in CoreMark to be outweighed by Fmax gains.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real multiply/divide/remainder operators from the normal combinational ALU path, keeping only simple RV32I operations and the ex...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Bypass ready load data to EX\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline pays a mandatory one-cycle load-use bubble whenever the instruction immediately after a load consumes the loaded register. CoreMark's list and matrix kernels contain many pointer and array dependent loads, and the benchmark bus model often has dmem ready in the same cycle. Recovering those bubbles should improve iterations/cycle without changing the retirement width or adding the broad pipeline stalls that hurt the prior structural M-unit attempt.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline pays a mandatory one-cycle load-use bubble whenever the instruction immediately after a load consumes the loaded register. CoreMark's list and matrix kernels contain many pointer and array dependent loads, and the benchmark bus model often has dmem ready in the same cycle. Recovering those bubbles should improve iterations/cycle without changing the retirement width or adding the broad pipeline stalls that hurt the prior structural M-unit attempt.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a MEM-to-EX load-result bypass path for the specific case where the instruction in EX/MEM is a load, dmem is ready, and the younger ID/EX instruction uses that load's rd. The MEM stage already computes sign/zero-extended load_data combinationally before the MEM/WB register; expose that value and let the forwarding network select it for EX operands when dmem_ready is high. Keep the existing load-use interlock only for dmem stalls or cases where the load data is not yet ready, so correctness and RVFI ordering remain unchanged while ready loads avoid the one-cycle bubble.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"If needed, add a compact forwarding selector encoding for the new MEM-stage load-data bypass while preserving existing pipeline bundle semantics.\"}, {\"file\"...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Register fetch-decode boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current core has no real IF/ID pipeline register: the fetched instruction and PC flow combinationally from the PC/imem side into decoder, immediate generation, regfile read addressing, hazard detection, and the ID/EX register. That likely creates a broad timing path through external instruction data and decode fanout, and prior predictor work showed that adding more fetch/decode combinational logic can severely hurt Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core has no real IF/ID pipeline register: the fetched instruction and PC flow combinationally from the PC/imem side into decoder, immediate generation, regfile read addressing, hazard detection, and the ID/EX register. That likely creates a broad timing path through external instruction data and decode fanout, and prior predictor work showed that adding more fetch/decode combinational logic can severely hurt Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the IF/ID boundary into a registered fetch-decode stage, optionally with a one-entry valid/skid behavior for stalls, so imem_data and pc are captured before decode. Preserve the existing single-retire RVFI contract and branch/load-use/dmem stall semantics by flushing or holding the registered IF/ID payload using the same hazard priorities. This should shorten the highest-fanout front-end timing path and improve FPGA Fmax with only a small control change and no new load-data or RV32M critical path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep or minimally adjust the IF/ID bundle so it can represent a registered valid payload and bubble without changing RVFI-visible pipeline metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an IF/ID register or one-entry fetch buffer that captures pc, instruction, and valid, with redirect and flush taking priority over ...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Add RAM store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes all younger work whenever a store in EX/MEM sees\\nio_dmemReady low, even though stores do not produce a register result for the\\nfollowing instructions. Under the benchmark's random dmem backpressure model,\\nCoreMark can lose cycles waiting on regular RAM stores that could instead be\\nqueued and drained in order while independent ALU/control work continues.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that changed the M datapath, load-use timing, or the\\nfetch/decode boun