\": \"The current EX stage resolves branches and jumps while also running the ALU,\\nforwarding muxes, branch comparators, and RVFI next-PC construction. Prior\\nstructural attempts that added pipeline latency, broad stalls, or early\\nfront-end redirects regressed, so the next structural experiment should be a\\npure retiming that shortens EX without changing prediction policy or IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute PC+4 and the PC-relative target PC+imm in ID and store them in the\\nexisting ID/EX pipeline register. EX can then use the precomputed PC+imm for\\nconditional branch targets, JAL targets, and AUIPC results, and use the\\nprecomputed PC+4 for fall-through, JAL/JALR link data, and RVFI pc_wdata.\\n\\nThis removes duplicated 32-bit PC adders from the EX control path and lowers\\nfanout around branch/jump resolution while preserving the same five-stage\\ntiming, load-use behavior, RVFI order, and single-channel nret=1 contract.\\nUnlike the failed decode-prediction attempts, the ID-side target add feeds\\nonly a register and never drives the fetch PC in the same cycle, so it should\\navoid creating a new imem_data-to-PC critical loop.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend id_ex_t with registered pc_plus4 and pc_imm_target fields used by EX for branch, JAL, AUIPC, and RVFI next-PC construction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute pc+4 and pc+imm f...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Add fetch skid buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The benchmark model injects independent instruction and data bus stalls, and the current pipeline freezes fetch whenever a data-memory operation is held in EX/MEM. If instruction memory is ready during that backend hold, the core wastes an opportunity to capture the next sequential instruction and can then immediately lose more cycles to a later imem stall after dmem unblocks.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that always inserted an IF/ID register or added store-buffer control regressed, so this hypothesis keeps the normal zero-wait path combinational and only adds a one-entry skid slot for cycles where the backend is already unable to advance.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The benchmark model injects independent instruction and data bus stalls, and the current pipeline freezes fetch whenever a data-memory operation is held in EX/MEM. If instruction memory is ready during that backend hold, the core wastes an opportunity to capture the next sequential instruction and can then immediately lose more cycles to a later imem stall after dmem unblocks.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that always inserted an IF/ID register or added store-buffer control regressed, so this hypothesis keeps the normal zero-wait path combinational and only adds a one-entry skid slot for cycles where the backend is already unable to advance.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry fetch skid buffer in the IF stage that captures the current imem instruction and PC when dmem_stall holds ID/EX and EX/MEM but imemReady is high. When the backend releases, feed the buffered instruction to ID before requesting/advancing to the next PC; invalidate the buffer on any EX redirect so wrong-path contents never retire.\\n\\nThis should improve CoreMark iterations/cycle under the random stall model by overlapping useful instruction fetch with data-memory wait cycles, without changing retirement width, branch resol...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Bypass ALU for memory addresses\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage routes load and store effective-address generation through\\nthe same combinational ALU result mux used for arithmetic, branches, jumps,\\nand RV32M operations. CoreMark executes many memory operations, so their MEM\\naddress path can inherit unnecessary muxing and arithmetic fanout even though\\nthe required operation is only rs1 + immediate.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added front-end state, store queues, or load\\nbypasses regressed because they added control complexity or new critical\\npaths. A narrower structural split inside EX keeps the pipeline timing and IPC\\nidentical while trying to shorten a hot address-generation path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage routes load and store effective-address generation through\\nthe same combinational ALU result mux used for arithmetic, branches, jumps,\\nand RV32M operations. CoreMark executes many memory operations, so their MEM\\naddress path can inherit unnecessary muxing and arithmetic fanout even though\\nthe required operation is only rs1 + immediate.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added front-end state, store queues, or load\\nbypasses regressed because they added control complexity or new critical\\npaths. A narrower structural split inside EX keeps the pipeline timing and IPC\\nidentical while trying to shorten a hot address-generation path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a dedicated simple effective-address adder in rtl/ex_stage.sv for\\nload/store instructions, fed by the existing forwarded rs1 value and the\\nregistered immediate. When mem_read or mem_write is set, capture this adder's\\nresult into ex_mem.alu_result instead of the general ALU output; all other\\ninstructions keep the existing ALU, branch, jump, AUIPC, and RVFI behavior.\\n\\nThis should let synthesis keep memory address generation out of the wider ALU\\nresult cone that also contains M-extension and c...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Register writeback result\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM/WB stage carries both alu_result and read_data, then the WB\\nstage selects the final writeback value combinationally every cycle. That\\nselected value feeds the regfile write port, MEM/WB forwarding into EX, and\\nRVFI rd_wdata, so the small load-vs-ALU mux sits on several timing-relevant\\nfanout paths even though its decision is already known when MEM/WB captures.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added front-end state, prediction metadata,\\nstore buffering, or same-cycle load bypasses regressed by lengthening hotter\\npaths or changing stall behavior. This is a narrower pipeline retiming that\\nshould preserve IPC exactly while shortening writeback and forwarding logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM/WB stage carries both alu_result and read_data, then the WB\\nstage selects the final writeback value combinationally every cycle. That\\nselected value feeds the regfile write port, MEM/WB forwarding into EX, and\\nRVFI rd_wdata, so the small load-vs-ALU mux sits on several timing-relevant\\nfanout paths even though its decision is already known when MEM/WB captures.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added front-end state, prediction metadata,\\nstore buffering, or same-cycle load bypasses regressed by lengthening hotter\\npaths or changing stall behavior. This is a narrower pipeline retiming that\\nshould preserve IPC exactly while shortening writeback and forwarding logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a registered wb_data field to the MEM/WB bundle and compute it inside\\nmem_stage at the same time read_data is formed. For loads, wb_data captures\\nthe sign/zero-extended load data; for all other writeback instructions it\\ncaptures the EX/MEM alu_result. Then simplify wb_stage so w_data is just the\\nregistered wb_data field, and keep RVFI rd_wdata using that same value.\\n\\nThis should remove the live MEM/WB mem_to_reg mux from the regfile w...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Split M arithmetic sidecar\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The successful current best isolated the MUL-family datapath inside the ALU, but the EX-stage result mux still mixes ordinary RV32I operations, memory address generation, branch support, multiply, and divide/remainder in one large combinational block. Prior structural attempts that added stalls, predictors, fetch buffers, or broad pipeline retiming mostly regressed, so the next structural angle should preserve the existing five-stage timing and IPC while giving synthesis a cleaner separation between the hot RV32I path and rarely used M-extension hardware.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The successful current best isolated the MUL-family datapath inside the ALU, but the EX-stage result mux still mixes ordinary RV32I operations, memory address generation, branch support, multiply, and divide/remainder in one large combinational block. Prior structural attempts that added stalls, predictors, fetch buffers, or broad pipeline retiming mostly regressed, so the next structural angle should preserve the existing five-stage timing and IPC while giving synthesis a cleaner separation between the hot RV32I path and rarely used M-extension hardware.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the current monolithic ALU into a small RV32I fast ALU plus a separate combinational RV32M sidecar module. The fast ALU handles ADD/SUB/logical/compare/shift/LUI and remains the default source for CoreMark's common arithmetic, branch compare operands, and load/store address generation. The M sidecar receives gated operands only for MUL/DIV/REM opcodes and returns an M result that is selected at the final EX result mux when the decoded op is an M instruction.\\n\\nThis is intentionally not a multicycle M-unit: it preserves single-cycle retirement behavior, forwarding, load-use handling, RVFI metadata, and the nret=1 interface. The expected benefit is mostly Fmax and routing quality from reducing fa...\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Hazard-local source-use bits\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares a load destination against both raw source fields of the following instruction, even when the following instruction does not architecturally consume one of those fields. CoreMark has enough pointer and immediate-heavy code that false load-use bubbles can reduce iterations per cycle, especially under the random stall model.\\n\\nTwo earlier source-qualification attempts broke because they either used approximate opcode logic or let source-use information alter RVFI/register metadata. The lesson is useful: source-use qualification should exist only in the hazard decision, while rs addresses, rs data, forwarding payloads, and retirement metadata remain exactly as they are today.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares a load destination against both raw source fields of the following instruction, even when the following instruction does not architecturally consume one of those fields. CoreMark has enough pointer and immediate-heavy code that false load-use bubbles can reduce iterations per cycle, especially under the random stall model.\\n\\nTwo earlier source-qualification attempts broke because they either used approximate opcode logic or let source-use information alter RVFI/register metadata. The lesson is useful: source-use qualification should exist only in the hazard decision, while rs addresses, rs data, forwarding payloads, and retirement metadata remain exactly as they are today.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add exact decoder-derived uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 signals for the current IF/ID instruction and feed them only into hazard_unit. The load-use detector should stall only when the pending load's rd matches a source field that the younger instruction truly reads; it must not zero or mask ID/EX source fields, regfile read addresses, forwarding metadata, or RVFI rs fields.\\n\\nThis is a stru...\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Predecode branch compare class\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage still decodes the branch comparison class from branch_op\\nwhile it is also resolving forwarded operands, ALU output, redirect, and RVFI\\npc_next. Prior front-end prediction and target-injection attempts regressed\\nbecause they added fetch-PC muxing or mutable prediction metadata, while the\\nsuccessful structural win was a pure ID-to-EX retiming of PC target values.\\n\\nA similarly narrow retiming can move branch comparator control preparation out\\nof the EX hot path without changing when branches resolve, adding pipeline\\nlatency, or touching memory/M-extension behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage still decodes the branch comparison class from branch_op\\nwhile it is also resolving forwarded operands, ALU output, redirect, and RVFI\\npc_next. Prior front-end prediction and target-injection attempts regressed\\nbecause they added fetch-PC muxing or mutable prediction metadata, while the\\nsuccessful structural win was a pure ID-to-EX retiming of PC target values.\\n\\nA similarly narrow retiming can move branch comparator control preparation out\\nof the EX hot path without changing when branches resolve, adding pipeline\\nlatency, or touching memory/M-extension behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add compact decoded branch-compare control bits to the ID/EX pipeline payload,\\nderived from the already-decoded branch_op in ID. EX should use those registered\\nbits to select equality, signed-less-than, or unsigned-less-than comparison and\\na final invert flag, instead of carrying the raw funct3-style branch_op through\\na full case statement on the EX redirect path.\\n\\nThis preserves the five-stage pipeline, single nret=1 retirement contract,\\nexisting forwarding operands, misalignment trap behavior, and EX-stage branch\\nresolution point. The expected gain is Fmax from simplifying the branch\\ncondition/control cone in EX; IP...\n  Line 45: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r15s1\", \"title\": \"Retire PC-next in MEM\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The only structural improvement that has held up so far was a pure retiming of PC-relative sums from EX back into ID. Most later structural attempts regressed by adding front-end PC muxes, same-cycle load-data paths, store-buffer control, or wider prediction metadata. The current EX stage still builds the RVFI next-PC value in the same block that resolves redirects, selects jump link data, runs forwarding, and captures EX/MEM, even though the RVFI pc_wdata value is not observed until retirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The only structural improvement that has held up so far was a pure retiming of PC-relative sums from EX back into ID. Most later structural attempts regressed by adding front-end PC muxes, same-cycle load-data paths, store-buffer control, or wider prediction metadata. The current EX stage still builds the RVFI next-PC value in the same block that resolves redirects, selects jump link data, runs forwarding, and captures EX/MEM, even though the RVFI pc_wdata value is not observed until retirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move RVFI pc_next construction from EX into the MEM/WB capture path. EX should continue resolving branch/jump redirects exactly as today, but EX/MEM only needs to carry the resolved control-flow target and branch-taken state; mem_stage can compute the retiring pc_next from registered EX/MEM metadata as either pc+4 or the resolved target before writing MEM/WB.\\n\\nThis keeps the five-stage pipeline, nret=1 RVFI contract, load-use behavior, redirect timing, and CoreMark IPC unchanged. The expected benefit is Fmax from shortening the EX-stage register input cone and reducing fanout around the pc_next mux, while avoiding any new fetch-side feedback or predictor state that prior structural attempts showed to be expensive on Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": ...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/log.jsonl:\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Add fetch skid buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end has no real IF/ID storage: the IF payload is combinational from the current PC and imem data, and an imem-not-ready cycle injects a bubble directly into decode. With the tournament cosim using roughly 22% random instruction stalls, CoreMark loses many cycles even when the back end is already stalled by load-use, dmem, or the multicycle M unit. A small fetch buffer is a structural way to recover iterations/cycle without revisiting the prior broken ID-stage branch-resolution path or adding fetch-path target adders that hurt Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end has no real IF/ID storage: the IF payload is combinational from the current PC and imem data, and an imem-not-ready cycle injects a bubble directly into decode. With the tournament cosim using roughly 22% random instruction stalls, CoreMark loses many cycles even when the back end is already stalled by load-use, dmem, or the multicycle M unit. A small fetch buffer is a structural way to recover iterations/cycle without revisiting the prior broken ID-stage branch-resolution path or adding fetch-path target adders that hurt Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a one- or two-entry instruction skid buffer between IF and ID. The PC/imem side should continue fetching sequential instructions whenever the buffer has space, while the ID side consumes one buffered instruction only when ID/EX is ready. On redirect, flush the buffer and restart fetch at the EX-resolved target; on imem stalls, hold the outstanding fetch instead of emitting a synthetic NOP into decode. This should overlap random iStall cycles with existing back-end stalls and reduce front-end bubbles while preserving single-retire RVFI ordering and the existing EX-stage control-flow correctness.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"descr...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Load-use MEM bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The previous structural attempts spent LUTs and timing on front-end control-flow machinery, and the log shows those changes either broke correctness or regressed fitness. The current five-stage core still has the classic one-cycle load-use interlock even though the benchmark memory is zero-wait in FPGA and only randomly stalled in cosim. CoreMark has frequent dependent loads through pointer-heavy list, matrix, and state code, so removing this bubble can improve iterations per cycle without adding predictor target adders to the fetch critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The previous structural attempts spent LUTs and timing on front-end control-flow machinery, and the log shows those changes either broke correctness or regressed fitness. The current five-stage core still has the classic one-cycle load-use interlock even though the benchmark memory is zero-wait in FPGA and only randomly stalled in cosim. CoreMark has frequent dependent loads through pointer-heavy list, matrix, and state code, so removing this bubble can improve iterations per cycle without adding predictor target adders to the fetch critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a MEM-to-EX load-data bypass path that lets an instruction immediately behind a load proceed into EX when the load is in MEM and dmem is ready. Compute the existing load extraction result combinationally in MEM, expose it to the forwarding network, and add a forwarding select for EX operands that chooses the current MEM load result over the older EX/MEM ALU address. Keep the load-use stall only when the load will not have valid data that cycle, such as a dmem stall or a misaligned/trapping load. This structural bypass should remove many single-cycle bubbles while preserving the NRET=1 retirement boundary and RVFI order at MEM/WB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\"...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Ordered one-entry store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts spent timing and LUTs on the front end or on load\\nbypass paths, and those changes either regressed fitness or broke stall\\nhandling. The current core still freezes the entire pipeline whenever a store\\nreaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, even though independent ALU, branch, and\\nregister-only instructions behind that store could execute while the store is\\nwaiting. CoreMark has frequent data stores and the apples-to-apples cosim\\ninjects random dmem stalls, so store backpressure is a promising structural\\ntarget that avoids fetch-path target adders and load-result critical muxes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts spent timing and LUTs on the front end or on load\\nbypass paths, and those changes either regressed fitness or broke stall\\nhandling. The current core still freezes the entire pipeline whenever a store\\nreaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, even though independent ALU, branch, and\\nregister-only instructions behind that store could execute while the store is\\nwaiting. CoreMark has frequent data stores and the apples-to-apples cosim\\ninjects random dmem stalls, so store backpressure is a promising structural\\ntarget that avoids fetch-path target adders and load-result critical muxes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry ordered store buffer at the MEM boundary. A regular data\\nstore can retire into the buffer with its address, byte mask, write data, and\\nRVFI metadata captured, allowing the front of the pipeline to keep executing\\nnon-memory instructions while the buffer drives dmem until dmem_ready accepts\\nthe write. Preserve precise in-order behavior by stalling any younger load,\\nstore, MMIO access, FENCE, EBREAK, or illegal/trapping instruction until the\\nbuffer drains; MMIO marker/UART stores should bypass the buffer and keep the\\nexisting blocking behavior so CoreMark timing brackets re...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Pipeline EX operand retiming\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the existing EX stage into a lightweight EX1 operand/metadata register and an EX2\\nexecute/redirect stage. EX1 captures the forwarded rs1/rs2 values, selected ALU operands,\\ndecoded control, immediate, PC, and RVFI source data; EX2 performs the ALU operation,\\nbranch/jump resolution, M-unit handshake, and drives the existing EX/MEM payload. Add the\\nminimal hazard/forwarding adjustments needed for the extra execute stage, preferring a\\none-cycle interlock on immediately dependent ALU consumers over wide new bypass muxes if\\nthat keeps timing short. This structural retiming should shorten the common forwarding-to-\\nALU-to-EX/MEM cone and may raise Tang Nano 20K Fmax enough to offset a small CPI cost,\\nwhile preserving NRET=1 retirement at MEM/WB and all RVFI ordering/trap behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\"...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Register WB result at MEM boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The recent structural attempts mostly lost fitness by adding control-flow\\nprediction, buffering, memory overlap, or deeper execute-stage interlocks.\\nA safer structural target is the existing MEM/WB-to-WB combinational path:\\nthe selected writeback value is still muxed after the MEM/WB register, then\\nfans out to the regfile write port, same-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding,\\nand RVFI rd_wdata. Registering that selected value at the retirement boundary\\nshould shorten a hot fanout path without changing CPI, branch timing, memory\\nordering, or the NRET=1 external contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The recent structural attempts mostly lost fitness by adding control-flow\\nprediction, buffering, memory overlap, or deeper execute-stage interlocks.\\nA safer structural target is the existing MEM/WB-to-WB combinational path:\\nthe selected writeback value is still muxed after the MEM/WB register, then\\nfans out to the regfile write port, same-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding,\\nand RVFI rd_wdata. Registering that selected value at the retirement boundary\\nshould shorten a hot fanout path without changing CPI, branch timing, memory\\nordering, or the NRET=1 external contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a preselected rd_wdata/writeback-result field to the MEM/WB pipeline\\nbundle and compute it inside mem_stage before the MEM/WB register captures.\\nFor loads this field captures the sign/zero-extended load result; for ALU,\\nJAL, JALR, AUIPC, LUI, and M-extension operations it captures the existing\\nEX