ts one cycle earlier than EX; when it redirects, IF emits a bubble and\\nID/EX captures a bubble so the control-transfer instruction still retires\\nthrough the normal pipeline with correct RVFI pc_wdata. Cases needing an\\nunavailable producer, especially load-use and EX/MEM load results, fall back\\nto the existing EX resolution path for correctness.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire an early ID redirect signal into IF and hazard control while preserving the existing EX redirect fallback and single-channel RVFI retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute branch and jump targets in ID, compare available operands with WB bypassing, emit an early redirect, and carry the resolved pc_next metadat...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Move M extension to multicycle unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage instantiates three 64-bit multipliers plus divider and\\nremainder operators inside the always-active combinational ALU. Even when\\nCoreMark is executing ordinary integer, branch, and address-generation ops,\\nsynthesis must carry that large structural cone through the ALU module and\\nEX-stage muxing, which is a likely Fmax limiter on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\\nCoreMark's hot loops use mostly RV32I control, load/store, and simple ALU\\noperations, so paying extra cycles only on rare RV32M instructions should be\\na favorable trade if the common EX path becomes smaller and faster.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage instantiates three 64-bit multipliers plus divider and\\nremainder operators inside the always-active combinational ALU. Even when\\nCoreMark is executing ordinary integer, branch, and address-generation ops,\\nsynthesis must carry that large structural cone through the ALU module and\\nEX-stage muxing, which is a likely Fmax limiter on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\\nCoreMark's hot loops use mostly RV32I control, load/store, and simple ALU\\noperations, so paying extra cycles only on rare RV32M instructions should be\\na favorable trade if the common EX path becomes smaller and faster.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split RV32M operations out of the combinational ALU into a dedicated\\nmulticycle mdu module, leaving alu.sv as a compact single-cycle RV32I datapath\\nfor add/sub/logic/compare/shift/LUI/address generation. The EX stage detects\\nALU_MUL* and ALU_DIV/REM* operations, launches the MDU, and stalls the front\\nhalf of the pipeline until the result is available, then writes the result\\ninto the normal EX/MEM register so RVFI ordering and single-retire semantics\\nstay unchanged.\\n\\nThis structural decoupling should raise Fmax by removing the multiplier and\\ndivider hardware from the common EX critical path. The CPI loss should b...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Add one-entry fetch skid buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end presents imem_data directly into decode and only holds\\nthe PC when the back half of the pipeline stalls. Under the benchmark's\\nrandom instruction stalls plus occasional load-use, dmem, and MDU stalls,\\nthis tightly couples fetch availability to decode acceptance and can waste\\ncycles where the instruction bus is ready but ID/EX cannot yet consume the\\nfetched word.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts at earlier branch resolution and predictor-like\\nfront-end changes regressed or broke because they added control-path work on\\nthe fetch next-PC path. A tiny skid buffer is a narrower structural change:\\nit should improve useful instruction delivery during stalls while preserving\\nthe existing EX-stage authoritative redirect and RVFI pc_wdata semantics.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end presents imem_data directly into decode and only holds\\nthe PC when the back half of the pipeline stalls. Under the benchmark's\\nrandom instruction stalls plus occasional load-use, dmem, and MDU stalls,\\nthis tightly couples fetch availability to decode acceptance and can waste\\ncycles where the instruction bus is ready but ID/EX cannot yet consume the\\nfetched word.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts at earlier branch resolution and predictor-like\\nfront-end changes regressed or broke because they added control-path work on\\nthe fetch next-PC path. A tiny skid buffer is a narrower structural change:\\nit should improve useful instruction delivery during stalls while preserving\\nthe existing EX-stage authoritative redirect and RVFI pc_wdata semantics.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry IF/ID fetch skid buffer in the front end. When imemReady is\\nhigh but decode is stalled by load-use, dmem backpressure, or an MDU busy\\nperiod, capture the current fetched instruction and PC instead of emitting a\\nbubble or requiring the instruction bus to be ...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Add posted store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes the entire front half whenever a store sits in\\nEX/MEM and io_dmemReady is low. In the benchmark's random dmem-stall mode,\\nthat means a store with no register result can block unrelated following ALU\\nwork even though in-order retirement only requires the store request to be\\npreserved and later memory operations to remain ordered.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that touched fetch replay or prediction regressed,\\nwhile the successful changes reduced hot-path pressure without changing the\\nfront-end next-PC path. A tiny posted store buffer is a back-end structural\\ndecoupling point: it targets random dmem backpressure and CoreMark's store\\ntraffic without adding IF critical-path logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes the entire front half whenever a store sits in\\nEX/MEM and io_dmemReady is low. In the benchmark's random dmem-stall mode,\\nthat means a store with no register result can block unrelated following ALU\\nwork even though in-order retirement only requires the store request to be\\npreserved and later memory operations to remain ordered.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that touched fetch replay or prediction regressed,\\nwhile the successful changes reduced hot-path pressure without changing the\\nfront-end next-PC path. A tiny posted store buffer is a back-end structural\\ndecoupling point: it targets random dmem backpressure and CoreMark's store\\ntraffic without adding IF critical-path logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry posted store buffer at the MEM stage. When a valid store\\nreaches EX/MEM while io_dmemReady is low and the buffer is empty, capture its\\naddress, replicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI sideband, let the store\\nretire exactly once through the normal channel-0 path, and allow subsequent\\nnon-memory instructions to keep flowing. The buffer continues driving the\\ndmem write r...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Bypass ready load-use pairs\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still pays a fixed one-cycle bubble whenever\\na load in EX is followed by an instruction that consumes its rd, even when\\nthe data-memory port is ready the next cycle. CoreMark has many pointer and\\narray dependent load-use pairs, and the benchmark's random dmem stalls already\\nprovide the necessary full-pipeline hold when the load data is not available.\\n\\nPrior structural wins came from back-end decoupling and keeping the fragile\\nfetch path unchanged. A MEM-to-EX load-result bypass targets a direct CPI\\nloss in the back half of the pipeline without adding branch prediction,\\nreplay buffering, or deeper store-queue placement pressure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still pays a fixed one-cycle bubble whenever\\n