tivation\": \"The current EX stage resolves both operand-forwarding mux selects with two\\n5-bit destination comparisons against EX/MEM and MEM/WB immediately before\\nthe ALU, branch comparator, MDU launch, and EX/MEM register boundary. After\\nthe successful MDU split, Tang Nano 20K fitness appears dominated by hot\\nRV32I timing and routing rather than rare long-latency M operations.\\n\\nPrior structural wins came from removing common-path pressure, while attempts\\nto add front-end prediction, replay, or deeper queues regressed. Moving the\\nforwarding compare work one stage earlier is a structural retiming that keeps\\nfetch, branch resolution, memory ordering, and the nret=1 RVFI contract\\nunchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute the EX operand forwarding selects while the consumer instruction is\\nstill in ID, comparing IF/ID rs1/rs2 against the instruction currently in EX\\nand the instruction currently in MEM. Latch those two 2-bit selects into the\\nID/EX payload, then make ex_stage use the precomputed selects directly for\\nits rs1/rs2 muxes.\\n\\nThis removes forward_unit's register-number comparator cone from the EX-stage\\nALU/branch critical path and...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Register final writeback data\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM/WB boundary carries both alu_result and read_data plus a\\nmem_to_reg control bit, then wb_stage selects the final value on the same\\npath that drives regfile write data, MEM/WB forwarding, and RVFI rd_wdata.\\nPrior successful structural work came from removing common-path pressure and\\npipeline metadata, while front-end replay/prediction changes and deeper\\nqueues have repeatedly regressed on Tang Nano 20K.\\n\\nA narrow back-end retiming can reduce late writeback muxing and MEM/WB\\nrouting without touching fetch, branch resolution, memory ordering, the\\nposted store buffer depth, or the nret=1 RVFI contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM/WB boundary carries both alu_result and read_data plus a\\nmem_to_reg control bit, then wb_stage selects the final value on the same\\npath that drives regfile write data, MEM/WB forwarding, and RVFI rd_wdata.\\nPrior successful structural work came from removing common-path pressure and\\npipeline metadata, while front-end replay/prediction changes and deeper\\nqueues have repeatedly regressed on Tang Nano 20K.\\n\\nA narrow back-end retiming can reduce late writeback muxing and MEM/WB\\nrouting without touching fetch, branch resolution, memory ordering, the\\nposted store buffer depth, or the nret=1 RVFI contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a final wb_data field to the MEM/WB payload and compute it inside\\nmem_stage before the MEM/WB register captures: load instructions write the\\nalready sign/zero-extended load_data, while all other register-writing\\ninstructions write the EX/MEM alu_result. Then make wb_stage and the RVFI\\nrd_wdata path consume the registered wb_data directly, and remove the\\nlate mem_to_reg read_data/alu_result mux from the writeback/forwarding path.\\n\\nUnlike the earlier mem_to_reg pruning attempt, this does not make mem_read\\ndrive a wider fanout cone. It preserves the exist...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Split EX common add path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural wins came from removing common-path pressure in the back end,\\nwhile front-end replay, prediction, and deeper queues repeatedly regressed on\\nTang Nano 20K. The current EX stage still routes every ordinary register result\\nthrough the general ALU case mux, which includes compare and variable-shift\\nalternatives even for CoreMark's common add, immediate-add, load/store address,\\nAUIPC, and jump-link paths.\\n\\nA narrow EX-stage datapath split can reduce the hot forwarding-to-EX/MEM timing\\ncone without changing fetch, branch prediction, memory ordering, MDU behavior,\\nor the nret=1 RVFI retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural wins came from removing common-path pressure in the back end,\\nwhile front-end replay, prediction, and deeper queues repeatedly regressed on\\nTang Nano 20K. The current EX stage still routes every ordinary register result\\nthrough the general ALU case mux, which includes compare and variable-shift\\nalternatives even for CoreMark's common add, immediate-add, load/store address,\\nAUIPC, and jump-link paths.\\n\\nA narrow EX-stage datapath split can reduce the hot forwarding-to-EX/MEM timing\\ncone without changing fetch, branch prediction, memory ordering, MDU behavior,\\nor the nret=1 RVFI retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure ex_stage so common add-like operations use a dedicated fast result\\npath: compute add/sub/address/AUIPC/JAL link results separately from the general\\nshift/logic/compare ALU cone, then select the small fast result for ALU_ADD,\\nALU_SUB, ALU_LUI, AUIPC, load/store address generation, and JAL/JALR link writes.\\nKeep the existing alu.sv for less-common logic, compare, and shift operations,\\nand preserve the multicycle MDU launch/commit path exactly as today.\\n\\nThis should improve Fmax by keeping CoreMark's dominant integer/address path out\\nof the wide ALU o...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Forward loads from store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best structural win is the one-entry posted store buffer, which\\nlets isolated stores retire while a stalled dmem write drains. Its remaining\\nconservative rule is that every younger memory operation stalls behind any\\nbuffered store, even when the younger load reads bytes wholly covered by that\\npending store. CoreMark stack and struct traffic can contain store-then-load\\npairs where random dmem backpressure currently turns a safe forwarding case\\ninto a full pipeline stall.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best structural win is the one-entry posted store buffer, which\\nlets isolated stores retire while a stalled dmem write drains. Its remaining\\nconservative rule is that every younger memory operation stalls behind any\\nbuffered store, even when the younger load reads bytes wholly covered by that\\npending store. CoreMark stack and struct traffic can contain store-then-load\\npairs where random dmem backpressure currently turns a safe forwarding case\\ninto a full pipeline stall.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add store-buffer load forwarding inside the existing MEM-stage decoupling\\nstructure. When the posted store buffer is full and the instruction in EX/MEM\\nis a valid, aligned load to the same word with a byte mask fully covered by\\nthe buffered store mask, synthesize the load word from the buffered replicated\\nstore data, let the load retire normally, and do not assert a dmem read for\\nthat forwarded access. Keep all nonmatching, partially covered, misaligned,\\nand store-following-memory cases stalled behind the buffer exactly as today.\\n\\nThis preserves precise in-order retirement, RVFI memory reporting, and the\\nnret=1 top-level contract while improving the useful decoupling of the proven\\none-entry store buffer. The change stays entirely in the back end, avoiding\\nthe front-end replay and prediction paths that repeatedly regresse...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Add narrow forwarding sideband\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already improved by registering final writeback data,\\nbut the EX forwarding decision still reaches back into the wide MEM/WB and\\nEX/MEM pipeline records for rd and reg_write metadata. Those packed records\\nalso feed RVFI, memory reporting, and hold-on-dmem-stall behavior, so their\\nhot fields can inherit unnecessary fanout and routing pressure on Tang Nano\\n20K.\\n\\nPrior structural wins came from narrow back-end decoupling and