ly wide because it carries PC, operand, memory, trap, and instruction metadata for RVFI. Those fields are cold for the hot regfile/writeback path, so keeping the commit datapath tied to the same packed struct can cost routing and mux delay on Tang Nano 20K without improving CPI.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The latest structural improvement came from adding narrow forwarding sidebands so EX no longer depends directly on wide RVFI-heavy pipeline bundles. A similar fanout issue remains at the final writeback boundary: the full MEM/WB record still feeds regfile writeback, write-first register-file bypass, forwarding sideband generation, and RVFI reporting.\\n\\nThe MEM/WB record is intentionally wide because it carries PC, operand, memory, trap, and instruction metadata for RVFI. Those fields are cold for the hot regfile/writeback path, so keeping the commit datapath tied to the same packed struct can cost routing and mux delay on Tang Nano 20K without improving CPI.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add an explicit narrow writeback commit sideband alongside the existing MEM/WB RVFI record. The sideband should carry only valid, reg_write, rd, and the already-registered wb_data from mem_stage to wb_stage, the regfile write port, and the MEM/WB forwarding sideband. Keep the full mem_wb_t record for RVFI retirement and memory reporting only, with the same hold-on-dmem-stall semantics that prevent double retirement while preserving old forwarding data.\\n\\nThis is a structural isolation/re...\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Isolate EX MEM request sideband\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The best recent structural result came from adding narrow forwarding sidebands\\nso the EX operand-control cone no longer depended directly on wide RVFI-heavy\\npipeline records. The remaining back-end stall and memory-control path still\\nreaches through the full EX/MEM bundle for mem_read, mem_write, address, store\\ndata, and width information while that same packed record also carries cold\\nRVFI metadata. On Tang Nano 20K this can create unnecessary fanout and routing\\npressure even when CPI behavior is already good.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best recent structural result came from adding narrow forwarding sidebands\\nso the EX operand-control cone no longer depended directly on wide RVFI-heavy\\npipeline records. The remaining back-end stall and memory-control path still\\nreaches through the full EX/MEM bundle for mem_read, mem_write, address, store\\ndata, and width information while that same packed record also carries cold\\nRVFI metadata. On Tang Nano 20K this can create unnecessary fanout and routing\\npressure even when CPI behavior is already good.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a narrow registered memory-request sideband at the EX/MEM boundary for the\\nfields consumed by hazard_unit and mem_stage: valid, mem_read, mem_write,\\nmem_width, mem_sext, effective address, store data, rd, and the small writeback\\ncontrols needed to form wb_data. Keep the existing ex_mem_t record for RVFI PC,\\noperand, instruction, and trap metadata, but stop using the full packed record\\nas the hot memory/stall interface.\\n\\nThis mirrors the successful forwarding-sideband direction while avoiding the\\nfailed broad control-bundle split and duplicate commit sideband. It should\\npreserve retirement order, store-buffer semantics, dmem backpressure behavior,\\nand the nret=1 IO contract, while giving synthesis smaller local cones for the\\ndmem port muxes and hazard stall d...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_high/rep3/log.jsonl:\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV/REM off the ALU critical path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current execute stage contains a fully combinational RV32IM ALU, including\\nSystemVerilog division and remainder operators in the same result mux as the\\ncommon RV32I and MUL operations. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely bloats LUT\\nusage and lengthens the EX critical path even though CoreMark is dominated by\\nordinary integer, load/store, branch, and multiply instructions rather than\\ndivide/remainder.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current execute stage contains a fully combinational RV32IM ALU, including\\nSystemVerilog division and remainder operators in the same result mux as the\\ncommon RV32I and MUL operations. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely bloats LUT\\nusage and lengthens the EX critical path even though CoreMark is dominated by\\nordinary integer, load/store, branch, and multiply instructions rather than\\ndivide/remainder.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational ALU into a small iterative\\nmulti-cycle M-extension side unit in EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift/compare and\\nthe MUL-family operations on the existing single-cycle path, but detect divide\\nand remainder opcodes in EX, start the divider, and stall the pipeline until\\nthe result is available. This structural decoupling should reduce the normal\\nALU critical path and LUT pressure with negligible CoreMark CPI cost because\\nrare DIV/REM instructions pay the added latency only when they occur.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real DIV/REM operators from the combinational ALU path while preserving ALTOPS behavior for formal builds.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add DIV/REM detection, launch/consume the iterative result, and hold the EX/MEM register while a divide operation is in progress.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\":...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Decoupled fetch skid buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end is tightly coupled to the rest of the pipeline: any\\ndmem stall, load-use stall, divide stall, or raw imem-not-ready cycle holds\\nthe PC and prevents useful fetch progress. In the tournament CoreMark mode\\nboth instruction and data ports see random backpressure, so exposed fetch\\nbubbles reduce iterations per cycle even when the instruction port could\\nhave fetched ahead during unrelated back-end stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end is tightly coupled to the rest of the pipeline: any\\ndmem stall, load-use stall, divide stall, or raw imem-not-ready cycle holds\\nthe PC and prevents useful fetch progress. In the tournament CoreMark mode\\nboth instruction and data ports see random backpressure, so exposed fetch\\nbubbles reduce iterations per cycle even when the instruction port could\\nhave fetched ahead during unrelated back-end stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny two-entry fetch skid buffer between IF and ID. The IF stage should\\nkeep issuing sequential fetches while the decode/back-end side is stalled and\\nthere is queue space, and ID should consume from the queue whenever it can\\nadvance. Redirects still flush the queue and restart at the resolved target,\\npreserving the current EX-resolution semantics and RVFI ordering. This\\nstructural decoupling should hide a fraction of random imem stalls behind\\ndmem/load-use/div stalls with modest LUT/FF cost and less timing risk than\\nputting branch prediction logic back on the fetch critical path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the purely combinational IF/ID payload with a small valid-ready fetch queue that can fill while ID is stalled, drains to ID when available, and flushes on redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Base front-end stalls ...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Register the IF/ID boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end has no real IF/ID register: io_imemData feeds decode, immediate generation, regfile address selection, hazard inputs, and the ID/EX register in the same cycle. That structure keeps CPI low but likely puts external instruction-memory output, decoder fanout, and regfile read timing on one FPGA-critical path. Prior fetch-ahead structures lost badly because queue control and prediction logic made the front end more complex; a single registered IF/ID boundary is the minimal structural retiming that targets the same timing path without adding speculation or buffering policy.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end has no real IF/ID register: io_imemData feeds decode, immediate generation, regfile address selection, hazard inputs, and the ID/EX register in the same cycle. That structure keeps CPI low but likely puts external instruction-memory output, decoder fanout, and regfile read timing on one FPGA-critical path. Prior fetch-ahead structures lost badly because queue control and prediction logic made the front end more complex; a single registered IF/ID boundary is the minimal structural retiming that targets the same timing path without adding speculation or buffering policy.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert if_stage from a combinational IF/ID payload producer into a simple one-entry registered fetch/decode boundary. The PC still advances sequentially when the front end is not stalled, redirects still override stalls and flush the younger instruction, and ID consumes the registered instruction exactly as today. This should break the imem_data-to-decoder-to-ID/EX critical path and improve Fmax while preserving single-issue throughput; the expected CPI cost is limited to unchanged redirect/load-use behavior rather than the extra queue-control overhead seen in the failed skid-buffer attempt.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Iterative high-half multiply side unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The successful DIV/REM side-unit hypothesis showed that this FPGA benefits\\nwhen rare, wide arithmetic is removed from the common EX-stage ALU result\\npath. The current ALU still computes three 64-bit multiply variants every\\ncycle and muxes their high halves alongside the common RV32I and low-half\\nMUL operations. CoreMark should mostly exercise ordinary integer ops and\\nlow-half MUL, while MULH/MULHU/MULHSU are uncommon in compiler output.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The successful DIV/REM side-unit hypothesis showed that this FPGA benefits\\nwhen rare, wide arithmetic is removed from the common EX-stage ALU result\\npath. The current ALU still computes three 64-bit multiply variants every\\ncycle and muxes their high halves alongside the common RV32I and low-half\\nMUL operations. CoreMark should mostly exercise ordinary integer ops and\\nlow-half MUL, while MULH/MULHU/MULHSU are uncommon in compiler output.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep ALU_MUL as a single-cycle low-half multiply, but move ALU_MULH,\\nALU_MULHU, and ALU_MULHSU into a small multi-cycle high-multiply side unit\\nowned by EX, using the same stall/consume pattern already proven for DIV/REM.\\nThe common ALU path then only needs the low-half unsigned product for MUL and\\nno longer carries signed high-product result muxing on every instruction.\\nRare high-half multiply instructions pay a short structural stall, but the\\nexpected CoreMark mix should recover more Fmax than it loses in IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real ALU_MULH, ALU_MULHU, and ALU_MULHSU product logic from the normal combinational ALU path while preserving RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Detect high-half multiply operations, start a small side unit, stall EX until the r...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"MEM load-data bypass to EX\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline always injects a bubble for a load-use pair,\\neven when the data memory is ready and the load data is available during the\\nfollowing MEM cycle. CoreMark is load/store heavy, and the recent MEM-stage\\nbyte-lane improvement indicates this benchmark is sensitive to the memory\\ndatapath. Prior structural frontend decoupling and prediction attempts paid\\ntoo much fetch-path timing cost, so a backend bypass is a more focused IPC\\nimprovement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline always injects a bubble for a load-use pair,\\neven when the data memory is ready and the load data is available during the\\nfollowing MEM cycle. CoreMark is load/store heavy, and the recent MEM-stage\\nbyte-lane improvement indicates this benchmark is sensitive to the memory\\ndatapath. Prior structural frontend decoupling and prediction attempts paid\\ntoo much fetch-path timing cost, so a backend bypass is a more focused IPC\\nimprovement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a direct MEM-stage load-result bypass into the EX operand forwarding\\nnetwork. When a load sits in EX/MEM and a younger instruction in ID/EX reads\\nthe same rd, EX should use the current MEM load_data instead of stalling for\\nthe value to reach MEM/WB. The bypass must be disabled for misaligned/trapping\\nloads so dependent instructions retain the old architectural register value,\\nand dmem backpressure should still freeze the pipeline until valid load data\\nis available. This removes the normal one-cycle load-use penalty without\\nadding branch prediction, fetch queues, or nret changes.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the combinational aligned load_data value and a bypass-valid signal gated off for misaligned or trapping loads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"descripti...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Precompute MEM access descriptor in EX\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The strongest recent non-predictor gain came from simplifying MEM-stage byte-lane muxing, which suggests the load/store formatting path is still important for Tang Nano 20K fitness. The current MEM stage still derives misalignment, byte masks, replicated store data, aligned RVFI addresses, and dmem enables in the same cycle that dmem_rdata is selected and forwarded to writeback. Front-end queues, IF retiming, high-multiply side units, and direct load-data bypassing have already shown poor tradeoffs, so the next structural target should be a no-CPI retiming of an existing hot back-end path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The strongest recent non-predictor gain came from simplifying MEM-stage byte-lane muxing, which suggests the load/store formatting path is still important for Tang Nano 20K fitness. The current MEM stage still derives misalignment, byte masks, replicated store data, aligned RVFI addresses, and dmem enables in the same cycle that dmem_rdata is selected and forwarded to writeback. Front-end queues, IF retiming, high-multiply side units, and direct load-data bypassing have already shown poor tradeoffs, so the next structural target should be a no-CPI retiming of an existing hot back-end path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move the address-dependent memory access descriptor generation from MEM into EX, immediately after the effective address is computed. EX should precompute the byte mask, replicated store data, word-aligned RVFI address, memory-misalignment trap bit, and trap-gated control for load/store instructions, then carry those registered fields in EX/MEM. MEM can then drive dmem_wen/dmem_ren, RVFI masks, and store write data mostly from registered descriptor fields while retaining the existing load-data extraction from dmem_rdata. This should shorten the MEM address/control-to-writeback and dmem-enable paths with no CPI cost, while...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Precompute PC-relative targets in ID\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already benefits from a registered lookahead hot-branch predictor, but EX still recomputes pc+4 and pc+imm for branches, JAL, AUIPC, link writeback, redirect targets, prediction correction, and RVFI pc_wdata. Prior IF-side prediction and buffering attempts regressed because they put extra compares, target adds, or queue control on the fetch PC path. A narrower structural retime can instead move PC-relative adders behind the existing ID/EX register and shorten the EX resolve/writeback muxing without changing speculation policy.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already benefits from a registered lookahead hot-branch predictor, but EX still recomputes pc+4 and pc+imm for branches, JAL, AUIPC, link writeback, redirect targets, prediction correction, and RVFI pc_wdata. Prior IF-side prediction and buffering attempts regressed because they put extra compares, target adds, or queue control on the fetch PC path. A narrower structural retime can instead move PC-relative adders behind the existing ID/EX register and shorten the EX resolve/writeback muxing without changing speculation policy.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add registered pc_plus4 and pc_plus_imm fields to the ID/EX payload, computed in id_stage from the fetched PC and decoded immediate. In ex_stage, use those registered values for branch targets, JAL targets, JAL/JALR link data, normal sequential pc_next, and AUIPC writeback, while keeping JALR's rs1+imm calculation in EX because it depends on forwarded rs1. This should reduce the always-active EX pc-add and branch/jump result mux depth while preserving the single-issue nret=1 RVFI contract, existing hot-branch predictor behavior, and all memory/divide stall rules.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend id_ex_t w...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Move all MUL ops to a side unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The successful DIV/REM side unit showed that this core benefits when rare or\\nheavy RV32M arithmetic is removed from the always-active EX ALU result path.\\nA later high-half-multiply side unit regressed because low-half MUL still left\\na 32x32 multiplier in the common ALU mux, so the structural timing bottleneck\\nwas not actually removed while new stall control was added.\\n\\nThe remaining untried structural split is to move the entire MUL family,\\nincluding ALU_MUL, out of alu.sv. CoreMark will pay extra cycles on multiply\\ninstructions, but the normal integer, branch, load/store, and predictor paths\\nshould lose the parallel signed/unsigned multiplier fanout and wide M-result\\nmuxing that currently sit in the EX stage.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The successful DIV/REM side unit showed that this core benefits when rare or\\nheavy RV32M arithmetic is removed from the always-active EX ALU result path.\\nA later high-half-multiply side unit regressed because low-half MUL still left\\na 32x32 multiplier in the common ALU mux, so the structural timing bottleneck\\nwas not actually removed while new stall control was added.\\n\\nThe remaining untried structural split is to move the entire MUL family,\\nincluding ALU_MUL, out of alu.sv. CoreMark will pay extra cycles on multiply\\ninstructions, but the normal integer, branch, load/store, and predictor paths\\nshould lose the parallel signed/unsigned multiplier fanout and wide M-result\\nmuxing that currently sit in the EX stage.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a dedicated multi-cycle multiply side unit for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and\\nMULHSU, using the same start/wait/consume pattern as the existing DIV/REM\\nunit. In normal builds, alu.sv should no longer implement real multiply\\ndatapaths; EX detects multiply opcodes, starts the side unit, emits bubbles\\nwhile the result is pending, and writes the side-unit result into EX/ME...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Store-drain buffer for dStall\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current pipeline globally freezes when a store reaches MEM while dmem is not ready, even though the store address, data, mask, and RVFI payload are already known. CoreMark runs with randomized data-memory backpressure, so store stalls can expose dead cycles that younger independent ALU and branch instructions could otherwise use. Prior structural front-end and arithmetic retiming attempts usually lost Fmax, so the most promising structural angle is a back-end decoupling point that leaves IF, prediction, decode, and the common EX ALU path unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current pipeline globally freezes when a store reaches MEM while dmem is not ready, even though the store address, data, mask, and RVFI payload are already known. CoreMark runs with randomized data-memory backpressure, so store stalls can expose dead cycles that younger independent ALU and branch instructions could otherwise use. Prior structural front-end and arithmetic retiming attempts usually lost Fmax, so the most promising structural angle is a back-end decoupling point that leaves IF, prediction, decode, and the common EX ALU path unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry store-drain buffer after MEM for aligned, non-MMIO stores. When such a store cannot be accepted by dmem, capture its addr/wdata/wmask, allow the store to advance to MEM/WB and retire once, and keep driving the buffered write until io_dmemReady accepts it. While the buffer is pending, stall younger LOAD/STORE instructions and any MMIO/timing-marker store, but allow younger non-memory instructions to pass through MEM so random store backpressure is partly hidden without changing nret or RVFI order. This is a refined version of the earlier broken store-buffer idea: keep memory ordering conservative, do not buffer UART or CoreMark marker writes, and do not add load forwarding from the buff...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Compact EX branch comparator unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already recovered useful CoreMark IPC with the registered\\nhot-edge predictor, so the next structural opportunity should preserve that\\nbehavior while reducing the EX redirect path. ex_stage currently builds branch\\nequality, signed less-than, unsigned less-than, greater/equal variants, target\\nselection, misalignment handling, and ALU result muxing in one large block.\\nPrior front-end queues, IF/ID retiming, store buffers, and multiplier side\\nunits mostly lost because they added control or CPI cost; a local EX compare\\nreshaping has no new stalls and avoids touching the fetch predictor policy.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already recovered useful CoreMark IPC with the registered\\nhot-edge predictor, so the next structural opportunity should preserve that\\nbehavior while reducing the EX redirect path. ex_stage currently builds branch\\nequality, signed less-than, unsigned less-than, greater/equal variants, target\\nselection, misalignment handling, and ALU result muxing in one large block.\\nPrior front-end queues, IF/ID retiming, store buffers, and multiplier side\\nunits mostly lost because they added control or CPI cost; a local EX compare\\nreshaping has no new stalls and avoids touching the fetch predictor policy.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split branch condition evaluation out of ex_stage into a small branch_unit that\\ncomputes BEQ/BNE/BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU from one equality check and one subtractor\\nflag path. Use sign-bit aware subtract flags for signed and unsigned ordering\\ninstead of inferring several parallel 32-bit comparators in the EX stage case\\nstatement. Keep branch_target, jump_target, misalignment trap behavior, RVFI\\npc_wdata, and predictor updates semantically unchanged. This should shorten\\nthe branch-resolve/control cone and improve Fmax without changing nret, memory\\ntiming, predictor capaci...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Register writeback result\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM/WB stage still computes the final writeback value with a\\ncombinational mem_to_reg mux in wb_stage. That mux feeds both the regfile\\nwrite port and the MEM/WB-to-EX forwarding path, so dependent CoreMark\\ninstructions can see an active path from the MEM/WB register through the\\nwriteback mux, EX operand mux, ALU, and EX/MEM register. Prior structural\\nattempts that changed fetch policy, store buffering, branch factoring, or\\nM-extension latency often paid control or CPI costs; this is a narrower\\nretiming of an existing datapath with no new stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM/WB stage still computes the final writeback value with a\\ncombinational mem_to_reg mux in wb_stage. That mux feeds both the regfile\\nwrite port and the MEM/WB-to-EX forwarding path, so dependent CoreMark\\ninstructions can see an active path from the MEM/WB register through the\\nwriteback mux, EX operand mux, ALU, and EX/MEM register. Prior structural\\nattempts that changed fetch policy, store buffering, branch factoring, or\\nM-extension latency often paid control or CPI costs; this is a narrower\\nretiming of an existing datapath with no new stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Precompute and register the final rd writeback data at the MEM/WB boundary.\\nmem_stage should latch a wb_data field equal to load_data for loads and\\nalu_result for non-load register writes, while preserving the existing\\nread_data, alu_result, RVFI memory fields, and hold_wb behavior. wb_stage\\ncan then drive w_data directly from the registered wb_data field instead of\\nselecting between read_data and alu_result every cycle. This should shorten\\nthe hot MEM/WB forwarding and regfile-write path without changing nret=1,\\nRVFI rd_wdata semantics, load/store timing, predictor behavior, or CoreMark\\niterations per instruction.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": ...\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Fetch replay latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end discards an instruction-memory response whenever the back end is stalled by dmem or DIV/REM, because the PC is held and the IF/ID payload is not a registered valid-ready boundary. In the randomized CoreMark mode, this means an instruction that was available during a back-end stall may need to be refetched and can then be delayed by a later independent imem stall. Prior fetch queues regressed because they advanced PC and added queue control; a one-entry replay latch only captures the current PC response and should be much smaller.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end discards an instruction-memory response whenever the back end is stalled by dmem or DIV/REM, because the PC is held and the IF/ID payload is not a registered valid-ready boundary. In the randomized CoreMark mode, this means an instruction that was available during a back-end stall may need to be refetched and can then be delayed by a later independent imem stall. Prior fetch queues regressed because they advanced PC and added queue control; a one-entry replay latch only captures the current PC response and should be much smaller.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry fetch replay latch in if_stage that records pc, predicted_pc_next, and instr when the pipeline is stalled behind IF but io_imemReady is high. When the back-end stall clears, ID consumes the latched instruction even if io_imemReady is low that cycle, and the PC advances exactly once; redirects and flushes invalidate the latch. This structurally overlaps random instruction-memory availability with dmem/div stalls without adding fetch-ahead policy, branch predictor entries, memory reordering, or nret changes, so it should recover some CoreMark iterations per cycle with less Fmax risk than the failed two-entry skid buffer.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\":...\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"One-cycle registered multiply sidecar\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage still carries three real 32x32 multiply products through\\nthe common combinational ALU result path. Prior high-half-only and long-latency\\nall-MUL side units regressed: the former left low-half MUL on the hot path,\\nwhile the latter paid too much CoreMark CPI on ordinary MUL instructions.\\nA narrower structural split can remove multiplier logic from the normal\\ninteger/branch/load/store cone while keeping multiply latency to one extra\\ncycle instead of an iterative stall.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage still carries three real 32x32 multiply products through\\nthe common combinational ALU result path. Prior high-half-only and long-latency\\nall-MUL side units regressed: the former left low-half MUL on the hot path,\\nwhile the latter paid too much CoreMark CPI on ordinary MUL instructions.\\nA narrower structural split can remove multiplier logic from the normal\\ninteger/branch/load/store cone while keeping multiply latency to one extra\\ncycle instead of an iterative stall.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a registered one-cycle multiply sidecar in EX for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and\\nMULHSU. On a real M-extension multiply, EX captures the forwarded operands\\nand opcode into a small mul_unit, emits a single bubble into EX/MEM, then\\nconsumes the registered result on the next cycle using the existing ex_busy\\nstall path. In non-ALTOPS builds, alu.sv no longer computes real multiplier\\nproducts; in RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS builds, keep the current algebraic stand-ins\\nin the ALU so formal remains tractable. This should improve Fmax by removing\\nmultiplier fanout and wide M-result muxing from the common EX cone while\\nlimiting CoreMark IPC loss to one cycle per multiply.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real non-ALTOPS MUL/MULH/MULHU/M...\n\n(Results truncated: showing 100 of 142 matches (42 hidden). Consider using a more specific path or pattern.)","metadata":{"matches":142,"truncated":true},"title":"\"category\": \"structural\".*\"outcome\": \"(improvement|regression|neutral)\"","time":{"start":1778835238136,"end":1778835238171}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0d91c4ac19a9ea75016a06df25d1a4819a8c91e8209e9411e1"}},"id":"prt_e2ad7ac3b0017aP6Z7Ipx53zwd","sessionID":"ses_1d5292540ffepjDgtvUr54dZ4p","messageID":"msg_e2ad78b40001k9QHWskWFUmsli"}}
