{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778835265156,"sessionID":"ses_1d5292540ffepjDgtvUr54dZ4p","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_ta26l21LmKWZnlIHKgj6qLVw","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"DIV/REM|divider|div_unit|high-half|MULH|store buffer|skid buffer|fetch buffer","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench","include":"log.jsonl"},"output":"Found 159 matches (showing first 100)\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/kimi-k2_6/rep3/log.jsonl:\n  Line 2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Guard ALU multipliers off critical path for non-M ops\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU unconditionally computes three 64-bit products on every\\ncycle, even for simple ADD/SUB/AND/OR/XOR/SLT/SLTU/SLL/SRL/SRA/LUI\\noperations. On the Tang Nano 20K (GW2AR-18C) these inferred multipliers\\nsit on the combinational critical path and limit Fmax. CoreMark is\\ndominated by integer ALU and memory ops, so the multiplier is almost\\nnever exercised, yet it still degrades timing for every other operation.\\nRemoving it from the critical path should raise Fmax with zero IPC risk.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU unconditionally computes three 64-bit products on every\\ncycle, even for simple ADD/SUB/AND/OR/XOR/SLT/SLTU/SLL/SRL/SRA/LUI\\noperations. On the Tang Nano 20K (GW2AR-18C) these inferred multipliers\\nsit on the combinational critical path and limit Fmax. CoreMark is\\ndominated by integer ALU and memory ops, so the multiplier is almost\\nnever exercised, yet it still degrades timing for every other operation.\\nRemoving it from the critical path should raise Fmax with zero IPC risk.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure alu.sv so the 64-bit multiply assignments (mul_ss, mul_uu,\\nmul_su) are only evaluated when the ALU operation is MUL, MULH, MULHU,\\nor MULHSU. For all other opcodes the products are tied to zero inside\\nthe always_comb block. This tells the synthesis tool that the multiplier\\nhardware is not needed on the fast-path case items (ADD, SUB, logic,\\nshifts, etc.), shortening the critical path and raising Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Guard the 64-bit multiply computations inside a conditional case block so they only execute for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU ops. Default the product variables to zero so the main output case statement remains unchanged.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5,...\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Cold RV32M High-Half and Divide Unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU unconditionally builds three independent 64-bit multiply\\nproducts (signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned, signed*unsigned) and a\\ncombinational 32-bit divider on every cycle. This bloats area to ~9500 LUT4\\nand places heavy arithmetic on the EX-stage critical path for *all*\\ninstructions, even simple ADD or BRANCH. CoreMark's timed path is multiply-\\nheavy but only uses the low-half MUL result; it is effectively cold for\\nDIV/REM and high-half multiplies (MULH/MULHU/MULHSU). Prior work on similar\\nRV32IM baselines showed that moving cold division off the universal ALU path\\nwas one of the largest pure Fmax wins. Extending that idea to also remove the\\ntwo unused high-half multipliers should compound the area and timing benefit\\nwithout touching the benchmark's hot IPC path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU unconditionally builds three independent 64-bit multiply\\nproducts (signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned, signed*unsigned) and a\\ncombinational 32-bit divider on every cycle. This bloats area to ~9500 LUT4\\nand places heavy arithmetic on the EX-stage critical path for *all*\\ninstructions, even simple ADD or BRANCH. CoreMark's timed path is multiply-\\nheavy but only uses the low-half MUL result; it is effectively cold for\\nDIV/REM and high-half multiplies (MULH/MULHU/MULHSU). Prior work on similar\\nRV32IM baselines showed that moving cold division off the universal ALU path\\nwas one of the largest pure Fmax wins. Extending that idea to also remove the\\ntwo unused high-half multipliers should compound the area and timing benefit\\nwithout touching the benchmark's hot IPC path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure the EX stage into a fast single-cycle integer ALU plus a\\ndedicated cold Multiply-Divide Unit (MDU).\\n\\n- The ALU keeps ADD/SUB/logic/shift/compare/LUI and the existing single-\\n  cycle low-half MUL (mul_uu[31:0]). Al...\n  Line 14: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Gate divider evaluation by opcode in ALU\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU currently gates 64-bit multiply product evaluation behind an\\nopcode check, which was proven in a prior iteration to raise Fmax by\\nremoving the multiplier from the critical path of non-M instructions.\\nThe divide/remainder operations (DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU) are still computed\\ninside the case statement without an explicit input gate. On FPGA\\ninference targets the divider is a large combinational cell whose\\nphysical path from a/b to out is analyzed by the STA tool even when\\nthe current instruction is not a divide, keeping the divider on the\\ncritical path for ADD/SHIFT/LOGIC operations. CoreMark rarely (if ever)\\nuses division, so any IPC impact is negligible.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU currently gates 64-bit multiply product evaluation behind an\\nopcode check, which was proven in a prior iteration to raise Fmax by\\nremoving the multiplier from the critical path of non-M instructions.\\nThe divide/remainder operations (DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU) are still computed\\ninside the case statement without an explicit input gate. On FPGA\\ninference targets the divider is a large combinational cell whose\\nphysical path from a/b to out is analyzed by the STA tool even when\\nthe current instruction is not a divide, keeping the divider on the\\ncritical path for ADD/SHIFT/LOGIC operations. CoreMark rarely (if ever)\\nuses division, so any IPC impact is negligible.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Introduce intermediate variables (div_result, divu_result, rem_result,\\nremu_result) and guard their computation with the same if-gate pattern\\nalready used for multiplies: only evaluate the `/` and `%` operators\\nwhen op is ALU_DIV/ALU_DIVU/ALU_REM/ALU_REMU.  The case statement then\\nselects the pre-computed variables.  This physically disconnects the\\ndivider inputs for non-DIV instructions, shortening the worst-case\\ncombinational path and raising Fmax with no ar...\n  Line 23: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Split ALU into fast and M-extension paths with final 2:1 mux\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"A prior hypothesis proved that gating multiply evaluation inside alu.sv\\nraises Fmax by removing multiplier hardware from the critical path of\\nnon-M ops.  However, the ALU still selects its result through a single\\n19-entry case statement where the large multiplier/divider outputs are\\ninputs to the same mux tree as the fast ADD/SUB/LOGIC/SHIFT ops.\\nSynthesis tools may not fully prune those heavy inputs from the fast-path\\nmux delay.  By explicitly separating the ALU into a fast path (ADD/SUB/\\nLOGIC/SHIFT/SLT/LUI) and a slow M-extension path (MUL/DIV/REM) and\\nselecting between them with a single 2:1 mux, the fast path sees a much\\nsmaller combinational cloud and should achieve a slightly higher Fmax\\nwith zero IPC impact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"A prior hypothesis proved that gating multiply evaluation inside alu.sv\\nraises Fmax by removing multiplier hardware from the critical path of\\nnon-M ops.  However, the ALU still selects its result through a single\\n19-entry case statement where the large multiplier/divider outputs are\\ninputs to the same mux tree as the fast ADD/SUB/LOGIC/SHIFT ops.\\nSynthesis tools may not fully prune those heavy inputs from the fast-path\\nmux delay.  By explicitly separating the ALU into a fast path (ADD/SUB/\\nLOGIC/SHIFT/SLT/LUI) and a slow M-extension path (MUL/DIV/REM) and\\nselecting between them with a single 2:1 mux, the fast path sees a much\\nsmaller combinational cloud and should achieve a slightly higher Fmax\\nwith zero IPC impact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure rtl/alu.sv so that:\\n1. A \\\"fast\\\" always_comb block computes ADD, SUB, AND, OR, XOR, SLT,\\n   SLTU, SLL, SRL, SRA, and LUI into a local `fast_out` variable.\\n2. A separate \\\"muldiv\\\" always_comb block (guarded by the existing\\n   opcode if-gate) computes the 64-bit products and DIV/REM results\\n   into a local `m_ou...\n  Line 29: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"(slot 0 hypothesis-gen failed)\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"hypothesis_gen_failed: Hypothesis agent modified off-limits paths and was rolled back: ['cores/bench/rtl/core.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv']\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 10, \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench ALU currently computes three separate 32\\u00d732 products\\n(signed\\u00d7signed, unsigned\\u00d7unsigned, signed\\u00d7unsigned). Yosys does not\\nmerge them, so the design pays for three multipliers worth of DSP/LUT\\narea.  With LUT4 already at 9927/10000, placement congestion is severe\\nand likely limits the achievable Fmax of the fast integer path.\\nRemoving two of the three multipliers frees substantial area without\\nchanging IPC or adding pipeline stages.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace mul_ss, mul_uu and mul_su with a single unsigned\\u00d7unsigned\\nproduct.  MUL/MULHU reuse the low/high halves directly.  MULH and\\nMULHSU high halves are recovered with a cheap 32-bit correction\\nsubtractor: MULHSU subtracts b when a is negative; MULH additionally\\nsubtracts a when b is negative.  The ALTOPS formal branch and the\\ndivider path are untouched, and the fast-path 2:1 mux is preserved.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Collapse three multipliers into one unsigned product; add correction subtractors for MULH/MULHSU high halves.\"}]}}\n\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/kimi-k2_6/rep2/log.jsonl:\n  Line 2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Multi-cycle iterative divider to break ALU critical path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The bench core's ALU implements all M-extension ops combinationally, including 32-bit\\nsigned/unsigned division and remainder. On the Tang Nano 20K (GW2AR-LV18) FPGA,\\ncombinational division synthesizes to a very large pure-LUT network that dominates\\nstatic-timing critical path. The current Fmax is 127 MHz; meeting the 135 MHz target\\nrequires shortening the longest combinational path. CoreMark uses division minimally\\n(matrix operations are MUL-heavy, not DIV-heavy), so a multi-cycle divider will have\\nnegligible IPC impact while removing the slow path and raising Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench core's ALU implements all M-extension ops combinationally, including 32-bit\\nsigned/unsigned division and remainder. On the Tang Nano 20K (GW2AR-LV18) FPGA,\\ncombinational division synthesizes to a very large pure-LUT network that dominates\\nstatic-timing critical path. The current Fmax is 127 MHz; meeting the 135 MHz target\\nrequires shortening the longest combinational path. CoreMark uses division minimally\\n(matrix operations are MUL-heavy, not DIV-heavy), so a multi-cycle divider will have\\nnegligible IPC impact while removing the slow path and raising Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU paths in alu.sv with a compact 32-cycle\\niterative divider (shift-and-subtract / non-restoring) that exposes a start/done\\ninterface. The ex_stage will detect divide ops and hold the EX/MEM register while\\nthe divider runs, asserting a new div_busy signal back to the hazard unit. The\\nhazard unit stalls the entire pipeline upstream (IF, ID, EX) during division,\\nanalogous to a dmem stall. Non-divide ALU ops remain combinational single-cycle.\\nThe ALTOPS formal branch stays unchanged so riscv-formal still sees combinational\\nbehaviour. Removing the massive combinational divider from the ALU output ...\n  Line 8: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Remove redundant EX-stage adder by reusing IF precomputed branch target\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The IF stage already computes branch/JAL targets as predict_target for its static\\npredictor. The EX stage currently recomputes the same pc+imm sum redundantly for\\nbranch resolution, misalign checks, and pc_next selection. This 32-bit adder sits on\\nthe EX-stage critical path alongside the branch comparator, limiting Fmax. Reusing\\nthe precomputed value from the pipeline register removes the adder delay without\\naffecting IPC. Additionally, the ALU eagerly computes all three 64-bit multiplier\\nintermediates on every op; gating them to only the active M-extension multiply op\\nreduces switching and gives the synthesizer a cleaner non-M critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The IF stage already computes branch/JAL targets as predict_target for its static\\npredictor. The EX stage currently recomputes the same pc+imm sum redundantly for\\nbranch resolution, misalign checks, and pc_next selection. This 32-bit adder sits on\\nthe EX-stage critical path alongside the branch comparator, limiting Fmax. Reusing\\nthe precomputed value from the pipeline register removes the adder delay without\\naffecting IPC. Additionally, the ALU eagerly computes all three 64-bit multiplier\\nintermediates on every op; gating them to only the active M-extension multiply op\\nreduces switching and gives the synthesizer a cleaner non-M critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"In ex_stage.sv, replace all occurrences of (in.pc + in.imm) for branch_target and\\nfor the non-JALR jump_target with the existing in.predict_target field that was\\nprecomputed in IF stage. This removes a 32-bit adder from the branch-resolve and\\nmisalign-check paths in EX. In alu.sv, guard each 64-bit product (mul_ss, mul_uu,\\nmul_su) so it is computed only when the specific ALU op that consumes it is\\nselected, eliminating unnecessary multiplier switching and helping sy...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Replace combinational divider with iterative multi-cycle unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU currently implements DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU as combinational 32-bit\\ndivide/modulo using SystemVerilog / and % operators. On FPGA these\\nsynthesize to enormous combinational networks that create the longest\\ncritical-path delay and consume a large fraction of LUT budget.\\nCoreMark's hot paths contain essentially no integer divisions, so the\\nIPC cost of a multi-cycle divider is negligible while the Fmax gain\\napplies to every retired instruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU currently implements DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU as combinational 32-bit\\ndivide/modulo using SystemVerilog / and % operators. On FPGA these\\nsynthesize to enormous combinational networks that create the longest\\ncritical-path delay and consume a large fraction of LUT budget.\\nCoreMark's hot paths contain essentially no integer divisions, so the\\nIPC cost of a multi-cycle divider is negligible while the Fmax gain\\napplies to every retired instruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a dedicated 32-cycle it