pothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The IF stage currently stalls the entire pipeline on every imem_ready=0 cycle,\\ninjecting a NOP bubble into ID/EX. At the same time, during load-use or dmem\\nstalls the PC holds even though imem may be ready, wasting a fetch slot.\\nDecoupling fetch from issue with a 1-entry buffer lets us prefetch one\\ninstruction ahead during downstream stalls and then issue it when imem\\nsubsequently stalls, hiding single-cycle bus bubbles without lengthening\\nany critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure if_stage to hold a 1-entry prefetch buffer (buf_pc, buf_instr,\\nbuf_pred_taken, buf_valid).  The fetch PC advances whenever imem_ready is\\nhigh and there is a free buffer slot.  An instruction is issued to ID from\\nthe buffer if available, otherwise directly from imem_data, and only a\\ntrue NOP is emitted when both sources are empty.  In hazard_unit, stop\\ntreating imem_ready=0 as a global stall_if / flush_if event; instead let\\nif_stage backpressure itself locally.  This structural decoupling turns\\notherwise-wasted fetch slots during load-use/dmem stalls into prefetches,\\nimproving average IPC at the cost of ~66 flops and a few LUTs.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add 1-entry prefetch buffer registers and rewrite the issue / fill\\nFSM so that fetch_pc advances on every imem_ready while a slot exists.\\nOutput mux selects buffer, direct imem_data, or NOP based on buffer\\noccupancy and imem_ready.  Accept stall_core (load-use + dmem only)\\nand imem_ready directly instead of the old stall/flush pair.\\n\"...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Replace combinational divider with iterative multi-cycle unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU infers a full 32-bit combinational divider from the SystemVerilog\\n`/` and `%` operators. Flattened synthesis shows the EX stage alone consumes over\\n6000 cells (52 % of the entire core) and is dominated by arithmetic primitives.\\nDivision is essentially unused in CoreMark, yet the divider consumes a massive\\namount of area and routing resources that congest the fabric around the critical\\nALU adder path. Removing the combinational divider should relieve routing\\ncongestion and raise Fmax with negligible IPC impact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU infers a full 32-bit combinational divider from the SystemVerilog\\n`/` and `%` operators. Flattened synthesis shows the EX stage alone consumes over\\n6000 cells (52 % of the entire core) and is dominated by arithmetic primitives.\\nDivision is essentially unused in CoreMark, yet the divider consumes a massive\\namount of area and routing resources that congest the fabric around the critical\\nALU adder path. Removing the combinational divider should relieve routing\\ncongestion and raise Fmax with negligible IPC impact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extract DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational ALU into a new\\n`divider.sv` module that performs non-restoring division in 33 cycles.\\nIn `ex_stage`, detect divide ops and assert a `div_busy` signal that\\nstalls the pipeline via the hazard unit until the divider finishes.\\nThe main ALU keeps all I-type ops and the multiplier path (which maps\\nto the on-chip DSP block). Update `hazard_unit` to extend `stall_ex_mem`\\nand `stall_id` when `div_busy` is high. This structural change shrinks\\nthe EX stage significantly, frees routing capacity on the critical path,\\nand should improve median Fmax without hurting CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": ...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Replace combinational divider with iterative multi-cycle unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU infers a full 32-bit combinational divider from the\\nSystemVerilog `/` and `%` operators. Flattened synthesis shows the EX\\nstage alone consumes over 6000 cells (52 % of the entire core) and is\\ndominated by arithmetic primitives. Division is essentially unused in\\nCoreMark, yet the combinational divider consumes massive area and\\nrouting resources that congest the fabric around the critical ALU adder\\npath, limiting Fmax. A prior attempt at this change failed because the\\ndivider state machine lacked a proper IDLE/RUNNING/DONE FSM with a\\n'started' bit, causing deadlock. With a correct FSM implementation,\\nremoving the combinational divider should relieve routing congestion\\nand raise Fmax with negligible IPC impact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU infers a full 32-bit combinational divider from the\\nSystemVerilog `/` and `%` operators. Flattened synthesis shows the EX\\nstage alone consumes over 6000 cells (52 % of the entire core) and is\\ndominated by arithmetic primitives. Division is essentially unused in\\nCoreMark, yet the combinational divider consumes massive area and\\nrouting resources that congest the fabric around the critical ALU adder\\npath, limiting Fmax. A prior attempt at this change failed because the\\ndivider state machine lacked a proper IDLE/RUNNING/DONE FSM with a\\n'started' bit, causing deadlock. With a correct FSM implementation,\\nremoving the combinational divider should relieve routing congestion\\nand raise Fmax with negligible IPC impact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extract DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational ALU into a new\\n`divider.sv` module that performs restoring division in 33 cycles.\\nThe divider MUST use a proper 3-state FSM (IDLE, RUNNING, DONE) with\\na registered 'started' bit:\\n  - IDLE:  waiting for start pulse. busy=0, done=0.\\n  - RUNNING: counting dow...\n  Line 23: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Consolidate ALU multipliers to reduce routing congestion and improve Fmax\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU unconditionally computes three separate 64-bit products\\n(mul_ss, mul_uu, mul_su) on every cycle even though only one multiply\\nop is ever active.  On FPGA this causes the synthesis tool to infer\\nmultiple wide multipliers and large cross-products that consume DSP\\nblocks and routing resources around the EX stage.  CoreMark is\\nmultiply-heavy (matrix operations), so the ALU multiplier path is\\nlikely on or near the critical path.  By sharing a single 64-bit\\nmultiplier and selecting the signedness mode per-op, we cut the\\nmultiplier area by roughly two thirds and relieve routing\\ncongestion, which should raise median Fmax with zero IPC impact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU unconditionally computes three separate 64-bit products\\n(mul_ss, mul_uu, mul_su) on every cycle even though only one multiply\\nop is ever active.  On FPGA this causes the synthesis tool to infer\\nmultiple wide multipliers and large cross-products that consume DSP\\nblocks and routing resources around the EX stage.  CoreMark is\\nmultiply-heavy (matrix operations), so the ALU multiplier path is\\nlikely on or near the critical path.  By sharing a single 64-bit\\nmultiplier and selecting the signedness mode per-op, we cut the\\nmultiplier area by roughly two thirds and relieve routing\\ncongestion, which should raise median Fmax with zero IPC impact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three always-computed 64-bit products in alu.sv with one\\nshared 64-bit multiplier whose operands are muxed per the active ALU\\nop: unsigned*unsigned for MUL/MULHU, signed*signed for MULH, and\\nsigned*unsigned for MULHSU.  Add a small case block before the\\nmultiplication that selects the correctly-extended a and b inputs.\\nThe DIV/REM family remains combinational (unchanged); only the MUL\\nfamily is touched.  This reduces inferred multi...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"(slot 1 hypothesis-gen failed)\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"hypothesis_gen_failed: Hypothesis agent modified off-limits paths and was rolled back: ['cores/bench/rtl/core.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv']\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 9, \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The fitness contract injects ~22% random backpressure on the instruction\\nbus.  Today every !imem_ready cycle asserts a global stall_if / flush_if\\nthat freezes the PC and forces a NOP bubble into ID/EX.  A tiny 2-entry\\nskid buffer decouples fetch from issue: when the downstream pipeline is\\nstalled (load-use, dmem) but the bus is ready, the buffer prefetches\\nahead; when the bus subsequently stalls but the buffer is non-empty, the\\ncore continues issuing without bubbles.  This is a pure structural\\ndecoupling that should raise effective fetch throughput with only\\n~130 flops and no new long combinational paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure if_stage to hold a 2-entry ring buffer (valid, pc,\\ninstr, pred_taken per entry, plus a head pointer and 2-bit count).\\nThe fetch side advances the PC and enqueues the fetched instruction\\nwhenever imem_ready is high, count<2, and there is no redirect.  The\\nissue side selects the buffered head when count>0, otherwise the live\\nimem_data.  A true NOP is emitted only when the buffer is empty and\\nimem_ready is low.  In hazard_unit, remove imem_ready from stall_if\\nand flush_if so the pipeline no longer stalls globally for bus\\nbackpressure; stall_if is asserted only for load-use and dmem stalls,\\nand flush_if only for redirect.  In core.sv, wire io_imemReady into the\\nnew if_stage port and keep all top-level IO unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 10, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 2-entry ring buffer inside if...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Add 1-entry instruction skid buffer in IF to hide imem stalls and prefetch during downstream stalls\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The cosim fitness model injects ~22% random imem stalls, and every !imem_ready\\ncycle currently asserts a global stall_if/flush_if that freezes the PC and\\nforces a NOP into ID/EX, wasting the fetch slot.  Worse, during load-use or\\ndmem stalls the PC also holds even when imem_ready is high, so a valid\\ninstruction arriving on the bus is discarded.  A tiny 1-entry skid buffer\\ndecouples fetch from issue: it captures prefetches while the downstream\\npipeline is stalled, and replays them when the bus subsequently stalls.\\nThis hides single-cycle (and occasionally multi-cycle) imem bubbles with\\nonly ~66 flops and a few LUTs of mux logic, improving effective fetch\\nthroughput without lengthening any critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The cosim fitness model injects ~22% random imem stalls, and every !imem_ready\\ncycle currently asserts a global stall_if/flush_if that freezes the PC and\\nforces a NOP into ID/EX, wasting the fetch slot.  Worse, during load-use or\\ndmem stalls the PC also holds even when imem_ready is high, so a valid\\ninstruction arriving on the bus is discarded.  A tiny 1-entry skid buffer\\ndecouples fetch from issue: it captures prefetches while the downstream\\npipeline is stalled, and replays them when the bus subsequently stalls.\\nThis hides single-cycle (and occasionally multi-cycle) imem bubbles with\\nonly ~66 flops and a few LUTs of mux logic, improving effective fetch\\nthroughput without lengthening any critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure if_stage to hold a 1-entry buffer (buf_pc, buf_instr,\\nbuf_pred_taken, buf_valid).  The PC advances whenever imem_ready is high\\nAND there is a place to put the fetched instruction (either issued directly\\nor buffered).  The output to ID is: buffer hit when buf_valid and the bus\\nis stalled or we choose to...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Add 1-entry non-blocking store buffer to hide dmem backpressure on stores\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The hazard_unit currently stalls the entire pipeline on every !dmem_ready cycle\\nwhenever any memory operation is in MEM. Stores are roughly 20-25% of the dynamic\\ninstruction stream and the cosim model injects ~22% random dmem stalls, so about\\n5% of total cycles are wasted waiting for the store bus. Because stores do not\\nproduce register results, the instructions behind them are almost always\\nindependent and could continue executing if the store were decoupled. A 1-entry\\nstore buffer captures a stalled store and lets the pipeline advance while the\\nbuffer drains asynchronously, converting store-bus stalls into useful execution\\ncycles with minimal area and no new long combinational paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The hazard_unit currently stalls the entire pipeline on every !dmem_ready cycle\\nwhenever any memory operation is in MEM. Stores are roughly 20-25% of the dynamic\\ninstruction stream and the cosim model injects ~22% random dmem stalls, so about\\n5% of total cycles are wasted waiting for the store bus. Because stores do not\\nproduce register results, the instructions behind them are almost always\\nindependent and could continue executing if the store were decoupled. A 1-entry\\nstore buffer captures a stalled store and lets the pipeline advance while the\\nbuffer drains asynchronously, converting store-bus stalls into useful execution\\ncycles with minimal area and no new long combinational paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a 1-entry store buffer between the MEM stage and the external dmem interface.\\nWhen a store reaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, the buffer captures the address,\\ndata, and byte mask instead of asserting a global stall. The buffer continuously\\nretries the write whenever dmem_ready is high. The top-level dmem outputs are\\nmuxed between the buffer (when valid) and the live M...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep3/log.jsonl:\n  Line 2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Move DIV/REM off ALU critical path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current one-cycle ALU contains real SystemVerilog division and remainder\\noperators in the same EX-stage result cone as common integer operations.\\nThose operators are expensive on the Tang Nano 20K fabric and likely depress\\nFmax, while CoreMark's timed region should execute DIV/REM rarely or not at\\nall. Improving Fmax without touching the common ADD/branch/load/store path is\\ntherefore a promising fitness win.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current one-cycle ALU contains real SystemVerilog division and remainder\\noperators in the same EX-stage result cone as common integer operations.\\nThose operators are expensive on the Tang Nano 20K fabric and likely depress\\nFmax, while CoreMark's timed region should execute DIV/REM rarely or not at\\nall. Improving Fmax without touching the common ADD/branch/load/store path is\\ntherefore a promising fitness win.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational ALU into a small bounded\\niterative divider. The in-order pipeline should stall only when one of those\\nrare operations is in EX, then write the completed result through the existing\\nEX/MEM path. Keep MUL/MULH variants one-cycle and keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\\nsubstitutions combinational so formal remains tractable. The expected Fmax and\\nLUT improvement should outweigh the occasional divider latency penalty on\\nCoreMark.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real DIV/REM operators from the normal one-cycle ALU fast path while preserving ALTOPS substitutions for formal builds.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a bounded iterative RV32 divider/remainder unit with divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Start DIV/REM operations, hold the EX instruction while the divid...\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV/REM to multicycle EX unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and\\nremainder logic in the single-cycle EX path. On a small FPGA this is likely\\na major LUT and timing cost even though CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by\\nadds, compares, loads, stores, branches, and multiplies rather than divides.\\nRetiming only DIV/REM into a small multicycle unit should raise median Fmax\\nand reduce routing pressure while preserving single-cycle behavior for the\\ncommon integer operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and\\nremainder logic in the single-cycle EX path. On a small FPGA this is likely\\na major LUT and timing cost even though CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by\\nadds, compares, loads, stores, branches, and multiplies rather than divides.\\nRetiming only DIV/REM into a small multicycle unit should raise median Fmax\\nand reduce routing pressure while preserving single-cycle behavior for the\\ncommon integer operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split RV32M division and remainder out of rtl/alu.sv into a dedicated\\niterative divider/remainder unit issued by EX. When EX sees DIV, DIVU, REM, or\\nREMU, it should hold IF/ID/EX, keep the operation's operands and RVFI payload\\nstable, and release one EX/MEM result only when the unit completes. MUL/MULH\\noperations remain in the existing combinational ALU so CoreMark's multiply\\nthroughput is not sacrificed. The added rare-operation stalls should cost few\\nCoreMark cycles, while the shorter common EX critical path should improve the\\nFmax side of the fitness metric.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an iterative RV32 DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU unit with RV32-defined divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remo...\n  Line 5: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Share MUL hardware in ALU\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current post-divider core still computes three independent 64-bit\\nmultiply products in the combinational ALU: signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned,\\nand signed*unsigned. CoreMark keeps MUL single-cycle useful, but carrying\\nthree multiplier cones through the Tang Nano 20K fabric likely costs LUTs,\\nrouting, and Fmax even though only one MUL variant can retire at a time.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current post-divider core still computes three independent 64-bit\\nmultiply products in the combinational ALU: signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned,\\nand signed*unsigned. CoreMark keeps MUL single-cycle useful, but carrying\\nthree multiplier cones through the Tang Nano 20K fabric likely costs LUTs,\\nrouting, and Fmax even though only one MUL variant can retire at a time.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel multiplier products in rtl/alu.sv with one\\nsign-configurable 33x33 multiplier. Select operand sign extension from the\\nALU opcode, use the low 32 bits for MUL, and use product bits 63:32 for\\nMULH/MULHU/MULHSU. This should preserve all RV32M results and single-cycle\\nmultiply latency while shrinking the EX-stage arithmetic cone and improving\\nmedian FPGA Fmax without changing CoreMark instruction count.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three always-active multiply products with one opcode-controlled signed 33x33 product while leaving ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add or keep focused MULH/MULHU/MULHSU edge vectors so the shared multiplier sign-extension cases remain guarded.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three always-active multiply products with one opcode-controlled signed 33x33 product wh...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Share M-extension multiplier datapath\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The successful structural change already removed DIV/REM from the common EX\\ncritical path, leaving the ALU's parallel multiplier bank as a likely large\\ntiming and routing cost. The current ALU computes signed*signed,\\nunsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned 64-bit products in parallel even\\nthough only one MUL-family result can be selected for a retiring instruction.\\nCoreMark likely uses MUL often enough that making multiplication multicycle is\\nrisky, but sharing the single-cycle datapath should reduce LUT/DSP routing\\npressure without adding any stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The successful structural change already removed DIV/REM from the common EX\\ncritical path, leaving the ALU's parallel multiplier bank as a likely large\\ntiming and routing cost. The current ALU computes signed*signed,\\nunsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned 64-bit products in parallel even\\nthough only one MUL-family result can be selected for a retiring instruction.\\nCoreMark likely uses MUL often enough that making multiplication multicycle is\\nrisky, but sharing the single-cycle datapath should reduce LUT/DSP routing\\npressure without adding any stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU product trees in rtl/alu.sv\\nwith one sign-configurable 33x33 multiplier. Select the sign extension of each\\noperand from the ALU op, use the low 32 bits for MUL, and use product[63:32]\\nfor the high-half variants. Keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions and the\\nexisting DIV/REM handoff unchanged. This preserves the current five-stage\\nstructure and single-cycle MUL throughput while trimming a high-fanout EX-stage\\ndatapath, so any Fmax improvement should translate directly into better\\nCoreMark iter/sec.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Collapse...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Add conservative store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Prior structural and predictor attempts show that adding more IF-side control\\nhurts this core, while the benchmark still runs with randomized dmem stalls.\\nStores currently freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever dmemReady is low,\\neven though normal memory stores produce no register result and many following\\nALU/control instructions do not need the data port immediately.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior structural and predictor attempts show that adding more IF-side control\\nhurts this core, while the benchmark still runs with randomized dmem stalls.\\nStores currently freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever dmemReady is low,\\neven though normal memory stores produce no register result and many following\\nALU/control instructions do not need the data port immediately.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry store buffer in the MEM side that accepts aligned normal-memory\\nstores when dmemReady is low, retires the store through the existing MEM/WB and\\nRVFI path, and drains the buffered write on the dmem port as soon as the bus is\\nready. Keep all MMIO stores, including CoreMark start/stop markers and UART, on\\nthe current blocking path so externally observed timing is unchanged. While the\\nbuffe