 or bubbles whole packed ID/EX and EX/MEM payloads to zero, which puts\\nhigh-fanout load-use, redirect, and divider-busy controls on many data bits that\\nare architecturally irrelevant when valid is low.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Change pipeline bubbles to be valid-gated instead of full-payload zeroed: on\\nload-use, redirect, and divider wait cycles, clear only the valid bit and the\\nsmall set of side-effect controls needed to prevent redirects, memory requests,\\nregfile writes, and RVFI retirement. Let datapath and RVFI payload fields retain\\ntheir previous values when invalid, and gate EX redirects and MEM requests with\\nthe stage valid bit. This keeps the five-stage schedule, forwarding behavior,\\ntrap rules, and nret=1 RVFI contract unchanged while reducing wide mux/reset\\npressure on the inter-stage register cones.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"On flush, clear ID/EX valid and side-effect controls instead of zeroing the full payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate branch/jump redirects and EX/MEM side effects with val...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Stage-local control bundles\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has mostly rewarded structural changes that remove existing muxing or fanout without adding speculation, queues, or new stall policy.  After EX, the pipeline still carries the full decoded ctrl_t through EX/MEM and MEM/WB even though most decode, branch, jump, ALU, and immediate-control bits are dead in those later stages.  Those unused packed-control bits still participate in wide register clears, holds, routing, and packed-struct assignment cones.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has mostly rewarded structural changes that remove existing muxing or fanout without adding speculation, queues, or new stall policy.  After EX, the pipeline still carries the full decoded ctrl_t through EX/MEM and MEM/WB even though most decode, branch, jump, ALU, and immediate-control bits are dead in those later stages.  Those unused packed-control bits still participate in wide register clears, holds, routing, and packed-struct assignment cones.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the pipeline control payload by stage while keeping the five-stage schedule unchanged.  ID/EX should retain the full decoded ctrl_t, EX/MEM should carry only the memory, writeback, and trap bits MEM and forwarding still consume, and MEM/WB should carry only reg_write plus is_illegal because rd_wdata, masks, and RVFI memory fields are already final.  This should trim flip-flops, reset/flush muxing, and high-fanout packed-control routing without changing CPI, branch behavior, memory handshakes, RVFI ordering, or the nret=1 port contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Introduce narrower EX/MEM and MEM/WB control payload typedefs while leaving ID/EX decode control intact.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Map the full ID/EX control word into the reduced EX/...\n  Line 31: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Add redirect target replay predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The bench history shows that conventional branch predictors have regressed\\nbecause they put decode, target adders, BTB/RAS lookup, instruction matching,\\nor full prediction repair metadata on timing-sensitive front-end paths. The\\nremaining predictor opportunity is to recover some taken-control-flow cost\\nwithout predicting the next PC before EX resolves the branch.\\n\\nCoreMark is loop-heavy and the apples-to-apples cosim injects randomized\\ninstruction-memory stalls. A hot taken branch repeatedly fetches the same\\nredirect target, so remembering the target instruction can avoid target-fetch\\niStall bubbles while leaving the existing not-taken front end and EX redirect\\nsemantics intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench history shows that conventional branch predictors have regressed\\nbecause they put decode, target adders, BTB/RAS lookup, instruction matching,\\nor full prediction repair metadata on timing-sensitive front-end paths. The\\nremaining predictor opportunity is to recover some taken-control-flow cost\\nwithout predicting the next PC before EX resolves the branch.\\n\\nCoreMark is loop-heavy and the apples-to-apples cosim injects randomized\\ninstruction-memory stalls. A hot taken branch repeatedly fetches the same\\nredirect target, so remembering the target instruction can avoid target-fetch\\niStall bubbles while leaving the existing not-taken front end and EX redirect\\nsemantics intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry redirect-target replay predictor in IF. When a taken branch\\nor jump redirects to a target and the target instruction is later fetched\\nsuccessfully, remember {target_pc, target_instr}. On later fetches of the\\nsame PC, especially immediately after another EX redirect, allow IF to supply\\nthe remembered instruction and treat the fetch as internally ready even if\\nio_imemReady is low. The external imem address ...\n  Line 32: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Drop dead LUI control bit\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The strongest recent win came from narrowing stage-local control payloads, which suggests packed-control routing and reset/flush muxing are still important on this FPGA.  The current ID/EX control bundle still carries an `is_lui` bit from the decoder even though execute never consumes it; LUI behavior is already fully encoded by `alu_op = ALU_LUI`, `alu_src = 1`, and the U-immediate.\\n\\nThis is a narrower follow-up than removing larger payload fields such as the EX/MEM branch metadata that previously regressed.  It targets one demonstrably dead decode/control signal with no CPI, RVFI, memory, divider, or branch-policy changes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The strongest recent win came from narrowing stage-local control payloads, which suggests packed-control routing and reset/flush muxing are still important on this FPGA.  The current ID/EX control bundle still carries an `is_lui` bit from the decoder even though execute never consumes it; LUI behavior is already fully encoded by `alu_op = ALU_LUI`, `alu_src = 1`, and the U-immediate.\\n\\nThis is a narrower follow-up than removing larger payload fields such as the EX/MEM branch metadata that previously regressed.  It targets one demonstrably dead decode/control signal with no CPI, RVFI, memory, divider, or branch-policy changes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Delete the `is_lui` control signal from `ctrl_t`, remove the decoder output port and ID-stage plumbing for it, and keep LUI decode legal by continuing to drive `ALU_LUI`, `alu_src`, and `reg_write` exactly as today.  This should trim one bit from the packed ID/EX control register plus its flush/reset assignment cone and reduce decoder-to-ID/EX routing fanout.\\n\\nBecause no downstream logic reads `is_lui`, the transformation should be functionally neutral: RVFI traps still come from `is_illegal`, LUI writeback still comes from the existing ALU_LUI path, and the...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Reset-light pipeline payloads\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The recent winners for this core removed existing fanout and packed-control\\nrouting without adding new speculation, queues, bypass paths, or stall policy.\\nThe register file reset removal was a modest win, and the stage-local control\\nsplit was a larger win, which suggests the FPGA is still sensitive to reset and\\nhold mux pressure on wide pipeline registers. ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB still\\nsynchronously reset full packed payloads even though only valid and side-effect\\ncontrols need architecturally defined reset values.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The recent winners for this core removed existing fanout and packed-control\\nrouting without adding new speculation, queues, bypass paths, or stall policy.\\nThe register file reset removal was a modest win, and the stage-local control\\nsplit was a larger win, which suggests the FPGA is still sensitive to reset and\\nhold mux pressure on wide pipeline registers. ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB still\\nsynchronously reset full packed payloads even though only valid and side-effect\\ncontrols need architecturally defined reset values.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace full-payload synchronous resets on the pipeline registers with\\nreset-light stage registers: keep valid, reg_write, mem_read, mem_write, and\\ntrap/control bits reset to zero, but leave wide datapath and RVFI payload fields\\nwithout a live reset mux, using initial zero values only for deterministic\\nsimulation if needed. Do not change normal bubble, flush, dmem-hold, divider,\\nforwarding, or retirement behavior; this is reset-structure cleanup rather than\\nthe failed valid-gated bubble scheme. Removing reset fanout from pc, instr,\\nrs values, immediates, ALU results, memory data, and RVFI sideband fields should\\nimprove packing and routing while preserving CoreMark CPI and the nret=1 RVFI\\ncontract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confiden...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Remove IF NOP injection mux\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench history shows that added predictors, FIFOs, store buffers, load bypasses, and broad valid-gated rewrites tend to lose more Fmax than they recover. The reliable structural wins have instead removed existing muxing and packed-control fanout without changing CPI. The current IF stage still rewrites every fetched instruction through a 32-bit NOP mux on redirect or imem stall so the hazard unit and