ite-first bypass muxes; that\\nreset loop and wide read muxing consume many FFs and routing resources even\\nthough RV32I only requires x0 to be zero after reset. Removing the\\nnon-architectural full-register reset is a localized structural change with\\nno intended CoreMark cycle-count impact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added frontend prediction, fetch buffering,\\nMEM bypassing, or extra WB state regressed because they added muxing and\\ncontrol to already sensitive paths. The current register file is still a\\n32x32 reset-cleared, two-read flop array with write-first bypass muxes; that\\nreset loop and wide read muxing consume many FFs and routing resources even\\nthough RV32I only requires x0 to be zero after reset. Removing the\\nnon-architectural full-register reset is a localized structural change with\\nno intended CoreMark cycle-count impact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Recode rtl/reg_file.sv as an unreset, duplicated two-read-bank register file\\nsuitable for distributed LUT RAM inference. Keep both banks written together,\\nkeep x0 hardwired to zero, and retain the explicit same-cycle write-first\\nbypass so the existing decode/forwarding contract is unchanged. With reset\\nremoved from the 31 writable registers, yosys/nextpnr should have less reset\\nfanout, fewer flop-based read muxes, and better packing/routing around the\\ndecode-to-ID/EX path, improving Fmax without changing IPC, RVFI ordering, or\\nany hard invariant.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the...\n  Line 23: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Narrow slow MULH adder\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The best prior micro-optimizations improved fitness by shortening FPGA timing\\ncones without adding frontend prediction or load bypass control. The slow\\nRV32M unit is still synthesized into the design, and its high-half multiply\\nloop currently uses a 64-bit accumulator plus a shifted 64-bit multiplicand,\\ncreating a wide carry path for operations that CoreMark rarely executes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best prior micro-optimizations improved fitness by shortening FPGA timing\\ncones without adding frontend prediction or load bypass control. The slow\\nRV32M unit is still synthesized into the design, and its high-half multiply\\nloop currently uses a 64-bit accumulator plus a shifted 64-bit multiplicand,\\ncreating a wide carry path for operations that CoreMark rarely executes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the slow MULH/MULHU/MULHSU iterative multiply datapath in m_unit.sv\\nwith a right-shift partial-product algorithm that adds the 32-bit magnitude\\noperand into the upper half of a combined product register. This keeps the\\nexisting multi-cycle behavior and separate finish-state sign correction, but\\nreduces the per-cycle multiply carry path from roughly 64 bits to about 33\\nbits. CoreMark should see nearly unchanged cycle count because low-half MUL\\nremains single-cycle in the fast ALU and slow high-half multiplies are rare,\\nwhile median FPGA Fmax may improve from the narrower m_unit timing cone.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/m_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Rewrite the real slow high-half multiply loop to use a combined right-shift product register and a narrower upper-half add, preserving ALTOPS behavior, result_ready holding, and existing DIV/REM logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Keep or add focused MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU edge vectors that cover sign correction, hi...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Registered low-half MUL path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Prior structural wins came from taking rare RV32M work out of the common EX\\nresult cone, while frontend predictors, fetch buffering, MEM bypassing, and\\nregfile rewrites all lost more timing than they recovered. The remaining\\nhigh-cost logic still present on every fast EX timing analysis path is the\\nsingle-cycle 32x32 low-half MUL in alu.sv. CoreMark needs MUL support, but it\\ndoes not execute MUL every cycle, so a small MUL-only latency penalty may be\\ncheaper than keeping the multiplier in the ADD/load-address/branch ALU cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior structural wins came from taking rare RV32M work out of the common EX\\nresult cone, while frontend predictors, fetch buffering, MEM bypassing, and\\nregfile rewrites all lost more timing than they recovered. The remaining\\nhigh-cost logic still present on every fast EX timing analysis path is the\\nsingle-cycle 32x32 low-half MUL in alu.sv. CoreMark needs MUL support, but it\\ndoes not execute MUL every cycle, so a small MUL-only latency penalty may be\\ncheaper than keeping the multiplier in the ADD/load-address/branch ALU cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split ALU_MUL out of the combinational fast ALU and handle it with a tiny\\nregistered low-half multiply side path in EX. Non-MUL RV32I ALU operations,\\nbranch compares, load/store address generation, JAL/JALR link values, and the\\nexisting slow high-half/DIV/REM m_unit behavior should remain unchanged. When\\nALU_MUL reaches EX, the pipeline holds for the registered product, then emits\\nexactly one EX/MEM result with the original RVFI operands and order. Under\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS the side path should register the same formal substitute\\nexpression instead of the real product. This should remove the DSP/multiplier\\npath from the common ALU output mux and improve Tang Nano Fmax enough to\\noffset occasional MUL bubbles in CoreMark.\\n\", \"expected_impa...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Unified RV32M side unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The strongest structural gains so far came from removing RV32M arithmetic from\\nthe common EX-stage ALU cone, culminating in a registered low-half MUL side\\npath. That latest change improved Fmax, but it left EX with two separate M\\nside-unit handshakes, two result-valid paths, and a three-way result select for\\nlogic that is only active on M-extension instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The strongest structural gains so far came from removing RV32M arithmetic from\\nthe common EX-stage ALU cone, culminating in a registered low-half MUL side\\npath. That latest change improved Fmax, but it left EX with two separate M\\nside-unit handshakes, two result-valid paths, and a three-way result select for\\nlogic that is only active on M-extension instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Fold the standalone registered low-half MUL path into the existing RV32M side\\nunit while preserving the one-cycle registered MUL latency and the existing\\nmulti-cycle behavior for MULH/DIV/REM. EX should classify every M-extension\\ninstruction as one side-unit operation, use one start/result_valid/result_ready\\nhandshake, and feed EX/MEM through one registered M result path. This should\\nkeep the r8 MUL timing win, remove duplicate busy/control muxing from EX, and\\nslightly improve Tang Nano Fmax without changing CoreMark IPC except for the\\nalready-accepted MUL bubble.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the separate mul_unit and slow m_unit dispatch paths with a single M-extension side-unit handshake and one result mux path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/m_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add ALU_MUL support as a one-cycle registered low-half multiply result, including the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitute expression.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mul_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the standalone low-half MUL side ...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Posted store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes all stages whenever a store reaches MEM while\\nio_dmemReady is low, even though the store has no register result and many\\nfollowing CoreMark instructions are independent integer work. Prior\\nstructural attempts that added frontend prediction or load-use bypassing\\npaid too much timing cost, but store-side decoupling can target dStall IPC\\nloss with only local MEM/hazard changes and no IF next-PC cone growth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes all stages whenever a store reaches MEM while\\nio_dmemReady is low, even though the store has no register result and many\\nfollowing CoreMark instructions are independent integer work. Prior\\nstructural attempts that added frontend prediction or load-use bypassing\\npaid too much timing cost, but store-side decoupling can target dStall IPC\\nloss with only local MEM/hazard changes and no IF next-PC cone growth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry posted store buffer at the MEM stage. When a store is in\\nEX/MEM and dmem is not ready, capture its address, replicated write data,\\nmask, and RVFI memory fields, allow the store to retire once into MEM/WB,\\nand let younger non-memory instructions continue while the buffer drains to\\nthe external dmem port. Stall subsequent loads or stores until the buffered\\nstore has been accepted, preserving in-order memory semantics without a\\nload-forwarding network. This should hide a meaningful fraction of random\\nstore dStall cycles in CoreMark while keeping the nret=1 RVFI retirement\\ncontract unchanged and avoiding the frontend timing penalties that hurt\\nearlier predictor/buffer experiments.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add minimal store-buffer metadata fields or helper typedefs if needed, without changing the nret=1 RVFI port contr...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Predecode M dispatch flags\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The best structural gains for this core came from keeping RV32M arithmetic\\noff the common EX path and from registering forwarding decisions before EX.\\nThe current EX stage still re-decodes the registered ALU op every cycle to\\nclassify low-half MUL versus slow MULH/DIV/REM work, and those compare\\nresults feed the EX stall, start, ready, and result-select cones. A small\\nID-stage retime can shorten that control path without changing CoreMark IPC\\nor adding predictor, memory, or bypass machinery that has regressed before.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best structural gains for this core came from keeping RV32M arithmetic\\noff the common EX path and from registering forwarding decisions before EX.\\nThe current EX stage still re-decodes the registered ALU op every cycle to\\nclassify low-half MUL versus slow MULH/DIV/REM work, and those compare\\nresults feed the EX stall, start, ready, and result-select cones. A small\\nID-stage retime can shorten that control path without changing CoreMark IPC\\nor adding predictor, memory, or bypass machinery that has regressed before.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add registered M-dispatch metadata to the ID/EX payload: one flag for\\nALU_MUL and one flag for the slow high-half/DIV/REM RV32M group, computed\\nonce in decode from the already-decoded alu_op and valid legality context.\\nEX should use these registered flags for mul_start, m_start, ex_slow_busy,\\nresult_ready, operand latching, and the M-result select, while preserving\\nthe separate one-cycle mul_unit and the existing multi-cycle m_unit. This\\nfollows the proven pattern of moving small compare/priority decisions out\\nof the EX cone, avoids reconnecting MUL to the slow shared unit, and should\\nprovide a modest Fmax-driven fitness gain with no intentional cycle-count\\nchange.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\":...\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Registered RVFI sidecar\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The FPGA benchmark wrapper keeps the RVFI outputs live by reducing them, so\\nretirement metadata can still influence Fmax even though it does not drive\\nexecution. Prior structural wins came from removing load from hot EX/RV32M\\ncones, while prediction, buffering, MEM bypassing, store buffering, and\\nregfile rewrites repeatedly lost more timing than they recovered. A narrow\\nremaining structural target is the direct fanout from MEM/WB and the live WB\\nresult mux into the wide RVFI output bus.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The FPGA benchmark wrapper keeps the RVFI outputs live by reducing them, so\\nretirement metadata can still influence Fmax even though it does not drive\\nexecution. Prior structural wins came from removing load from hot EX/RV32M\\ncones, while prediction, buffering, MEM bypassing, store buffering, and\\nregfile rewrites repeatedly lost more timing than they recovered. A narrow\\nremaining structural target is the direct fanout from MEM/WB and the live WB\\nresult mux into the wide RVFI output bus.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-cycle RVFI sidecar register stage in rtl/core.sv. Capture the\\ncurrent MEM/WB retirement fields, final rd_wdata, memory masks/data, trap\\nstate, and order into dedicated RVFI output flops, then drive io_rvfi_*_0 only\\nfrom those flops while incrementing rvfi_order when a MEM/WB retirement is\\naccepted into the sidecar.\\nThe architectural trace is delayed by one cycle but remains strictly in order\\nwith the same nret=1 channel-0 contract and the same trap, memory, and rd\\nvalues. This should reduce MEM/WB register fanout into the FPGA wrapper's\\nRVFI XOR cone and improve placement/timing without changing IF, EX, MEM,\\ndmem-stall, branch, or CoreMark IPC behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered RVFI sidecar/outpu...\n  Line 44: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r15s0\", \"title\": \"Retimed divider iteration\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The best bench-core gains have come from removing or retiming RV32M logic\\nthat affects FPGA Fmax while CoreMark rarely pays the added slow-operation\\nlatency. The remaining real DIV/REM loop in rtl/m_unit.sv still combines a\\n33-bit shifted-remainder compare, conditional subtract, quotient update, and\\nremainder feedback in one cycle. That path is independent of the already\\nisolated low-half MUL fast path and is a plausible residual timing limiter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best bench-core gains have come from removing or retiming RV32M logic\\nthat affects FPGA Fmax while CoreMark rarely pays the added slow-operation\\nlatency. The remaining real DIV/REM loop in rtl/m_unit.sv still combines a\\n33-bit shifted-remainder compare, conditional subtract, quotient update, and\\nremainder feedback in one cycle. That path is independent of the already\\nisolated low-half MUL fast path and is a plausible residual timing limiter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the real-hardware divider loop into two shorter registered phases per\\nquotient bit: one phase forms the shifted remainder and comparison decision,\\nand the next phase applies the conditional subtract and updates quotient,\\ndividend, and remainder state. Keep divide-by-zero and signed-overflow\\nshortcuts unchanged, keep the ALTOPS formal branch unchanged, and preserve\\nthe existing result_valid/result_ready contract. DIV/REM latency increases,\\nbut CoreMark should execute those operations rarely enough that any cycle\\ncost is outweighed by a modest Tang Nano Fmax improvement.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/m_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retiming the real DIV/REM iteration into compare and subtract/update phases while preserving shortcuts, ALTOPS behavior, and the existing handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain or ext...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep3/log.jsonl:\n  Line 2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Gate and share M-extension ALU hardware\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU unconditionally forms three independent 64-bit products for every instruction, even though CoreMark is dominated by RV32I arithmetic, branches, and memory traffic. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely burns LUTs and routing and may sit on or near the EX-stage critical path despite M operations being rare in the benchmark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU unconditionally forms three independent 64-bit products for every instruction, even though CoreMark is dominated by RV32I arithmetic, branches, and memory traffic. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely burns LUTs and routing and may sit on or near the EX-stage critical path despite M operations being rare in the benchmark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor rtl/alu.sv so MUL-family operations use a single op-selected signedness/extension path and only feed meaningful operands into the multiplier when the decoded ALU op is an M multiply. Keep the existing RV32M results and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS behavior unchanged, but make non-M ALU operations see simpler combinational logic and give yosys/nextpnr less multiplier-related muxing and routing to optimize through. This is a micro-optimization aimed primarily at improving Fmax and secondarily reducing LUT pressure without changing pipeline timing, RVFI behavior, or architectural state.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Share multiply operand preparation across MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU and gate multiplier inputs for non-multiply ALU ops while preserving exact RV32M and ALTOPS semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend or retain M-extension ALU vectors to guard signed, unsigned, and mixed-sign multiply corner cases after the multiplier datapath refactor.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"c...\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move RV32M to multicycle side unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage contains full combinational RV32M multiply and divide logic inside the same ALU case as single-cycle integer operations. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely contributes a long critical path and high LUT pressure even though CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by simple integer, branch, and memory operations. Removing the divider and wide multiplier from the regular EX datapath should improve Fmax and may also improve placement by reducing EX fan-in.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage contains full combinational RV32M multiply and divide logic inside the same ALU case as single-cycle integer operations. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely contributes a long critical path and high LUT pressure even though CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by simple integer, branch, and memory operations. Removing the divider and wide multiplier from the regular EX datapath should improve Fmax and may also improve placement by reducing EX fan-in.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure the pipeline so RV32M operations dispatch from EX into a dedicated multicycle M unit while ordinary RV32I ALU, branch, jump, and address-generation operations remain single-cycle. When an M instruction reaches EX, hold IF/ID/EX behind it until the unit returns the result, then capture that result into EX/MEM and retire normally through the existing MEM/WB and RVFI path. This should preserve single-retire ordering and correctness while replacing the worst combinational arithmetic path with shorter iterative or staged logic; the added stalls should be infrequent enough in CoreMark to be outweighed by Fmax gains.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real multiply/divide/remainder operators from the normal combinational ALU path, keeping only simple RV32I operations and the ex...\n  Line 8: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Share RV32M divide datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a narrow ALU micro-optimization: sharing and gating the MUL-family datapath improved Tang Nano Fmax without adding pipeline stalls. The remaining RV32M divide/remainder logic still exposes separate signed and unsigned / and % operators in the combinational ALU case, which can leave yosys with multiple wide divider/remainder cones and extra muxing even though CoreMark mostly executes simpler RV32I operations.\\n\\nPrior attempts to move M-extension work into a multicycle side unit regressed because the added pipeline holds cost more than the Fmax gain. A same-cycle datapath sharing pass keeps the existing timing model and RVFI retirement behavior while attacking the remaining large arithmetic hardware in the common EX block.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a narrow ALU micro-optimization: sharing and gating the MUL-family datapath improved Tang Nano Fmax without adding pipeline stalls. The remaining RV32M divide/remainder logic still exposes separate signed and unsigned / and % operators in the combinational ALU case, which can leave yosys with multiple wide divider/remainder cones and extra muxing even though CoreMark mostly executes simpler RV32I operations.\\n\\nPrior attempts to move M-extension work into a multicycle side unit regressed because the added pipeline holds cost more than the Fmax gain. A same-cycle datapath sharing pass keeps the existing timing model and RVFI retirement behavior while attacking the remaining large arithmetic hardware in the common EX block.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor rtl/alu.sv so DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU use one shared operand-normalization path, one gated unsigned quotient/remainder datapath, and lightweight sign/zero correction for the four RV32M results. Force divider operands to harmless constants outside divide/remainder ops so non-M ALU operations do not c...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Register fetch-decode boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current core has no real IF/ID pipeline register: the fetched instruction and PC flow combinationally from the PC/imem side into decoder, immediate generation, regfile read addressing, hazard detection, and the ID/EX register. That likely creates a broad timing path through external instruction data and decode fanout, and prior predictor work showed that adding more fetch/decode combinational logic can severely hurt Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core has no real IF/ID pipeline register: the fetched instruction and PC flow combinationally from the PC/imem side into decoder, immediate generation, regfile read addressing, hazard detection, and the ID/EX register. That likely creates a broad timing path through external instruction data and decode fanout, and prior predictor work showed that adding more fetch/decode combinational logic can severely hurt Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the IF/ID boundary into a registered fetch-decode stage, optionally with a one-entry valid/skid behavior for stalls, so imem_data and pc are captured before decode. Preserve the existing single-retire RVFI contract and branch/load-use/dmem stall semantics by flushing or holding the registered IF/ID payload using the same hazard priorities. This should shorten the highest-fanout front-end timing path and improve FPGA Fmax with only a small control change and no new load-data or RV32M critical path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep or minimally adjust the IF/ID bundle so it can represent a registered valid payload and bubble without changing RVFI-visible pipeline metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an IF/ID register or one-entry fetch buffer that captures pc, instruction, and valid, with redirect and flush taking priority over ...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Add RAM store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes all younger work whenever a store in EX/MEM sees\\nio_dmemReady low, even though stores do not produce a register result for the\\nfollowing instructions. Under the benchmark's random dmem backpressure model,\\nCoreMark can lose cycles waiting on regular RAM stores that could instead be\\nqueued and drained in order while independent ALU/control work continues.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that changed the M datapath, load-use timing, or the\\nfetch/decode boundary regressed because they lengthened hot combinational paths\\nor inserted broad stalls. A one-entry store buffer is a narrower structural\\nchange: it targets store-only dmem stalls, keeps loads conservative, and avoids\\nnew ALU or branch critical-path logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes all younger work whenever a store in EX/MEM sees\\nio_dmemReady low, even though stores do not produce a register result for the\\nfollowing instructions. Under the benchmark's random dmem backpressure model,\\nCoreMark can lose cycles waiting on regular RAM stores that could instead be\\nqueued and drained in order while independent ALU/control work continues.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that changed the M datapath, load-use timing, or the\\nfetch/decode boundary regressed because they lengthened hot combinational paths\\nor inserted broad stalls. A one-entry store buffer is a narrower structural\\nchange: it targets store-only dmem stalls, keeps loads conservative, and avoids\\nnew ALU or branch critical-path logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry in-order store buffer for normal RAM stores only. When a\\nnon-MMIO store reaches MEM and the buffer is empty, capture its address, data,\\nmask, and RVFI memory metadata, allow the store instruction to retire, and let\\nthe buffer continue driving dmem_wen until io_dmemReady accepts it. Stall on\\nloads, MMIO stores, misali...\n  Line 17: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Gate divider operands only\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best bench core already benefited strongly from isolating the MUL-family datapath from ordinary RV32I ALU operations. The remaining DIV/REM operators still consume the live ALU operands directly, so yosys may keep wide divider/remainder cones and routing coupled to the EX-stage result mux even when CoreMark is mostly executing adds, compares, loads, stores, and branches.\\n\\nA prior attempt to share the divider datapath regressed because it introduced signed normalization, restore muxing, and simultaneous quotient/remainder logic. The safer micro-optimization is to avoid that structural rewrite and only gate the raw divider operands outside actual DIV/REM operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best bench core already benefited strongly from isolating the MUL-family datapath from ordinary RV32I ALU operations. The remaining DIV/REM operators still consume the live ALU operands directly, so yosys may keep wide divider/remainder cones and routing coupled to the EX-stage result mux even when CoreMark is mostly executing adds, compares, loads, stores, and branches.\\n\\nA prior attempt to share the divider datapath regressed because it introduced signed normalization, restore muxing, and simultaneous quotient/remainder logic. The safer micro-optimization is to avoid that structural rewrite and only gate the raw divider operands outside actual DIV/REM operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add an is_divrem_op decode inside rtl/alu.sv and feed the real a/b operands to division and remainder operators only for ALU_DIV, ALU_DIVU, ALU_REM, and ALU_REMU. For all other ALU ops, drive the divider operand wires with harmless constants, then use those gated wires in the existing RV32M divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 cases.\\n\\nThis keeps the pipeline single-cycle, preserves RV32M and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS semantics, and avoids the failed normalization/shari...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Add fetch skid buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The benchmark model injects independent instruction and data bus stalls, and the current pipeline freezes fetch whenever a data-memory operation is held in EX/MEM. If instruction memory is ready during that backend hold, the core wastes an opportunity to capture the next sequential instruction and can then immediately lose more cycles to a later imem stall after dmem unblocks.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that always inserted an IF/ID register or added store-buffer control regressed, so this hypothesis keeps the normal zero-wait path combinational and only adds a one-entry skid slot for cycles where the backend is already unable to advance.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The benchmark model injects independent instruction and data bus stalls, and the current pipeline freezes fetch whenever a data-memory operation is held in EX/MEM. If instruction memory is ready during that backend hold, the core wastes an opportunity to capture the next sequential instruction and can then immediately lose more cycles to a later imem stall after dmem unblocks.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that always inserted an IF/ID register or added store-buffer control regressed, so this hypothesis keeps the normal zero-wait path combinational and only adds a one-entry skid slot for cycles where the backend is already unable to advance.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry fetch skid buffer in the IF stage that captures the current imem instruction and PC when dmem_stall holds ID/EX and EX/MEM but imemReady is high. When the backend releases, feed the buffered instruction to ID before requesting/advancing to the next PC; invalidate the buffer on any EX redirect so wrong-path contents never retire.\\n\\nThis should improve CoreMark iterations/cycle under the random stall model by overlapping useful instruction fetch with data-memory wait cycles, without changing retirement width, branch resol...\n  Line 20: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Trim dead pipeline metadata\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already benefited from narrow datapath cleanup, while later attempts to add gating, prediction, buffering, or divider rewrites mostly lost Fmax or broke formal. There are still dead bits carried through hot pipeline bundles: ctrl.is_lui is decoded but never consumed, and EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target are written but not read by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. On a small FPGA these unused flops and their assignment cones can still consume routing, reset/load muxing, and placement freedom.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already benefited from narrow datapath cleanup, while later attempts to add gating, prediction, buffering, or divider rewrites mostly lost Fmax or broke formal. There are still dead bits carried through hot pipeline bundles: ctrl.is_lui is decoded but never consumed, and EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target are written but not read by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. On a small FPGA these unused flops and their assignment cones can still consume routing, reset/load muxing, and placement freedom.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Remove unused pipeline metadata instead of adding new behavior. Delete ctrl_t.is_lui and its decoder/id_stage assignments, and delete ex_mem_t.branch_taken and ex_mem_t.branch_target plus the corresponding ex_stage register writes. LUI behavior is already represented by ALU_LUI with alu_src/reg_write, branch redirects use the combinational EX outputs, and RVFI pc_next uses the resolved value already stored in EX/MEM, so these fields should not affect architectural state or retirement.\\n\\nThis is a pure micro-optimization: fewer registered bits, less control fanout, and less unnecessary mux/load/reset logic in the ID/EX and EX/MEM registers. The expected gain is modest but safer than another arithmetic or predictor change because it does not change pipelin...\n  Line 23: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Mux load byte lanes\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM stage extracts byte and halfword loads with a variable right\\nshift by addr[1:0] * 8. CoreMark has frequent loads, and on the Tang Nano 20K\\nthat small barrel-shifter style cone may sit on the dmem_rdata-to-MEM/WB path\\nand consume more LUT/routing than a direct byte-lane mux.\\n\\nRecent micro-opt attempts that added operand gating, divider rewrites, or\\npipeline metadata churn regressed, so the most promising next angle is a\\nbehavior-preserving local simplification of an existing hot datapath rather\\nthan adding new control or state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM stage extracts byte and halfword loads with a variable right\\nshift by addr[1:0] * 8. CoreMark has frequent loads, and on the Tang Nano 20K\\nthat small barrel-shifter style cone may sit on the dmem_rdata-to-MEM/WB path\\nand consume more LUT/routing than a direct byte-lane mux.\\n\\nRecent micro-opt attempts that added operand gating, divider rewrites, or\\npipeline metadata churn regressed, so the most promising next angle is a\\nbehavior-preserving local simplification of an existing hot datapath rather\\nthan adding new control or state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor rtl/mem_stage.sv so load extraction uses explicit case statements\\nover in.alu_result[1:0] to select byte_val and hword_val from dmem_rdata,\\ninstead of computing shifted = dmem_rdata >> (in.alu_result[1:0] * 8).\\nKeep the existing sign/zero extension, misalignment trapping, byte masks,\\ndmem port behavior, and RVFI memory fields unchanged.\\n\\nThis should reduce LUT depth and routing in the load-data path without\\nchanging pipeline timing, stalls, retirement order, or the nret=1 RVFI port\\ncontract. The expected gain is modest Fmax improvement with essentially no\\nIPC downside.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Repla...\n  Line 26: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Trim pipeline reset muxes\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The best successful changes for this core have been narrow timing and routing cleanups that preserve the five-stage behavior, while recent predictor, buffer, divider, and metadata edits mostly regressed. The current pipeline still resets entire packed ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB records, adding reset muxing and fanout to many datapath bits whose values are architecturally ignored whenever valid is low.\\n\\nUnlike removing reset from the architectural register file, trimming reset on invalid pipeline payload fields should not expose software-visible state. It targets placement and Fmax pressure in stage registers without changing IPC, branch policy, memory timing, RVFI order, or the nret=1 interface.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best successful changes for this core have been narrow timing and routing cleanups that preserve the five-stage behavior, while recent predictor, buffer, divider, and metadata edits mostly regressed. The current pipeline still resets entire packed ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB records, adding reset muxing and fanout to many datapath bits whose values are architecturally ignored whenever valid is low.\\n\\nUnlike removing reset from the architectural register file, trimming reset on invalid pipeline payload fields should not expose software-visible state. It targets placement and Fmax pressure in stage registers without changing IPC, branch policy, memory timing, RVFI order, or the nret=1 interface.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor the pipeline stage registers so reset clears the valid bits and the small set of side-effecting control bits that can drive writes, memory requests, traps, or hazard decisions, while non-side-effect payload fields are either left unchanged or only written on normal captures. Keep MEM/WB and top-level RVFI outputs deterministic by ensuring invalid retirements report valid=0 and no register or memory side effects, ...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Register writeback result\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM/WB stage carries both alu_result and read_data, then the WB\\nstage selects the final writeback value combinationally every cycle. That\\nselected value feeds the regfile write port, MEM/WB forwarding into EX, and\\nRVFI rd_wdata, so the small load-vs-ALU mux sits on several timing-relevant\\nfanout paths even though its decision is already known when MEM/WB captures.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added front-end state, prediction metadata,\\nstore buffering, or same-cycle load bypasses regressed by lengthening hotter\\npaths or changing stall behavior. This is a narrower pipeline retiming that\\nshould preserve IPC exactly while shortening writeback and forwarding logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM/WB stage carries both alu_result and read_data, then the WB\\nstage selects the final writeback value combinationally every cycle. That\\nselected value feeds the regfile write port, MEM/WB forwarding into EX, and\\nRVFI rd_wdata, so the small load-vs-ALU mux sits on several timing-relevant\\nfanout paths even though its decision is already known when MEM/WB captures.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that added front-end state, prediction metadata,\\nstore buffering, or same-cycle load bypasses regressed by lengthening hotter\\npaths or changing stall behavior. This is a narrower pipeline retiming that\\nshould preserve IPC exactly while shortening writeback and forwarding logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a registered wb_data field to the MEM/WB bundle and compute it inside\\nmem_stage at the same time read_data is formed. For loads, wb_data captures\\nthe sign/zero-extended load data; for all other writeback instructions it\\ncaptures the EX/MEM alu_result. Then simplify wb_stage so w_data is just the\\nregistered wb_data field, and keep RVFI rd_wdata using that same value.\\n\\nThis should remove the live MEM/WB mem_to_reg mux from the regfile w...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Multicycle divider only\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already gained most of its Fmax by sharing the MUL-family datapath, but DIV/REM still live as full combinational operators inside the normal ALU result mux. Prior attempts show that moving all RV32M work into a side unit regresses because CoreMark uses multiply enough that broad M stalls are costly, and operand gating alone does not remove the divider cones from synthesis.\\n\\nA narrower structural split should target only DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU. These operations are rare in the timed CoreMark hot path compared with adds, loads, stores, branches, and multiplies, so paying multicycle latency for divider-class instructions is likely cheaper than carrying same-cycle divide/remainder hardware on every EX-stage timing path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already gained most of its Fmax by sharing the MUL-family datapath, but DIV/REM still live as full combinational operators inside the normal ALU result mux. Prior attempts show that moving all RV32M work into a side unit regresses because CoreMark uses multiply enough that broad M stalls are costly, and operand gating alone does not remove the divider cones from synthesis.\\n\\nA narrower structural split should target only DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU. These operations are rare in the timed CoreMark hot path compared with adds, loads, stores, branches, and multiplies, so paying multicycle latency for divider-class instructions is likely cheaper than carrying same-cycle divide/remainder hardware on every EX-stage timing path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the ALU's real DIV/REM combinational operators with a dedicated iterative divider/remainder unit that is invoked only when an ALU_DIV, ALU_DIVU, ALU_REM, or ALU_REMU instruction reaches EX. Keep RV32I operations and the successful shared MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU datapath single-cycle in the existing ALU.\\n\\nOn a divider instruction, hold IF...\n  Line 32: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Gate shift operand path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a narrow ALU cleanup that kept the pipeline unchanged while reducing always-live arithmetic hardware around common RV32I operations. Most later attempts regressed when they added control/state, altered forwarding, or rewrote divider and branch paths. The remaining safer micro-opt target is the ALU shift cone: SLL/SRL/SRA are relatively infrequent in CoreMark, but the live shifter still shares the EX result mux and operand routing with adds, compares, branches, and memory address generation.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a narrow ALU cleanup that kept the pipeline unchanged while reducing always-live arithmetic hardware around common RV32I operations. Most later attempts regressed when they added control/state, altered forwarding, or rewrote divider and branch paths. The remaining safer micro-opt target is the ALU shift cone: SLL/SRL/SRA are relatively infrequent in CoreMark, but the live shifter still shares the EX result mux and operand routing with adds, compares, branches, and memory address generation.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor rtl/alu.sv so shift operations use a small is_shift_op predicate and gated shift operands. Feed the real a operand and b[4:0] shamt only for ALU_SLL, ALU_SRL, and ALU_SRA; otherwise drive the shifter inputs with constants and use those gated wires in the existing shift case arms. Keep all ALU op encodings, RV32M behavior, RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions, and single-cycle pipeline timing unchanged.\\n\\nThis should reduce switching, LUT input pressure, and routing from the barrel-shift cone into the common EX-stage ALU result path without adding any new state, stalls, predictor metadata, memory behavior, or RVFI-visible changes. The expected upside is modest but it follows the one proven pattern for this core: isolate a rarely used ALU sub-datapath while prese...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Split M arithmetic sidecar\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The successful current best isolated the MUL-family datapath inside the ALU, but the EX-stage result mux still mixes ordinary RV32I operations, memory address generation, branch support, multiply, and divide/remainder in one large combinational block. Prior structural attempts that added stalls, predictors, fetch buffers, or broad pipeline retiming mostly regressed, so the next structural angle should preserve the existing five-stage timing and IPC while giving synthesis a cleaner separation between the hot RV32I path and rarely used M-extension hardware.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The successful current best isolated the MUL-family datapath inside the ALU, but the EX-stage result mux still mixes ordinary RV32I operations, memory address generation, branch support, multiply, and divide/remainder in one large combinational block. Prior structural attempts that added stalls, predictors, fetch buffers, or broad pipeline retiming mostly regressed, so the next structural angle should preserve the existing five-stage timing and IPC while giving synthesis a cleaner separation between the hot RV32I path and rarely used M-extension hardware.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the current monolithic ALU into a small RV32I fast ALU plus a separate combinational RV32M sidecar module. The fast ALU handles ADD/SUB/logical/compare/shift/LUI and remains the default source for CoreMark's common arithmetic, branch compare operands, and load/store address generation. The M sidecar receives gated operands only for MUL/DIV/REM opcodes and returns an M result that is selected at the final EX result mux when the decoded op is an M instruction.\\n\\nThis is intentionally not a multicycle M-unit: it preserves single-cycle retirement behavior, forwarding, load-use handling, RVFI metadata, and the nret=1 interface. The expected benefit is mostly Fmax and routing quality from reducing fa...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Clean divider EX handshake\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core still carries full combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU\\noperators in the ALU result cone, while the successful multiply sharing pass\\nalready removed much of the always-live M-extension pressure. Prior broad\\nmulticycle M-unit attempts regressed or broke because they stalled common\\nmultiply operations and used ad hoc control that desynchronized EX/MEM and\\nRVFI payloads. A divider-only structural split remains attractive if the EX\\nstage owns a precise valid/hold/done handshake and captures the entire\\ninstruction payload atomically with the divider result.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core still carries full combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU\\noperators in the ALU result cone, while the successful multiply sharing pass\\nalready removed much of the always-live M-extension pressure. Prior broad\\nmulticycle M-unit attempts regressed or broke because they stalled common\\nmultiply operations and used ad hoc control that desynchronized EX/MEM and\\nRVFI payloads. A divider-only structural split remains attractive if the EX\\nstage owns a precise valid/hold/done handshake and captures the entire\\ninstruction payload atomically with the divider result.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace only the real DIV/REM combinational datapath with an iterative or\\nstaged divider unit that starts when a valid divider-class instruction enters\\nEX. While the divider is busy, hold PC, ID/EX, and the in-flight EX payload;\\ndo not bubble EX/MEM with partial metadata. When done, commit exactly one\\nEX/MEM register update containing the original instruction metadata,\\nforwarded rs values, pc_next, control bits, and the final quotient or\\nremainder. Keep RV32I and the existing shared multiply family single-cycle.\\n\\nThis should improve Fmax by removing the remaining same-cycle divide and\\nremainder cones from the hot ALU path, with limited CoreMark IPC co...\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Hazard-local source-use bits\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares a load destination against both raw source fields of the following instruction, even when the following instruction does not architecturally consume one of those fields. CoreMark has enough pointer and immediate-heavy code that false load-use bubbles can reduce iterations per cycle, especially under the random stall model.\\n\\nTwo earlier source-qualification attempts broke because they either used approximate opcode logic or let source-use information alter RVFI/register metadata. The lesson is useful: source-use qualification should exist only in the hazard decision, while rs addresses, rs data, forwarding payloads, and retirement metadata remain exactly as they are today.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares a load destination against both raw source fields of the following instruction, even when the following instruction does not architecturally consume one of those fields. CoreMark has enough pointer and immediate-heavy code that false load-use bubbles can reduce iterations per cycle, especially under the random stall model.\\n\\nTwo earlier source-qualification attempts broke because they either used approximate opcode logic or let source-use information alter RVFI/register metadata. The lesson is useful: source-use qualification should exist only in the hazard decision, while rs addresses, rs data, forwarding payloads, and retirement metadata remain exactly as they are today.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add exact decoder-derived uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 signals for the current IF/ID instruction and feed them only into hazard_unit. The load-use detector should stall only when the pending load's rd matches a source field that the younger instruction truly reads; it must not zero or mask ID/EX source fields, regfile read addresses, forwarding metadata, or RVFI rs fields.\\n\\nThis is a stru...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep2/log.jsonl:\n  Line 2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Multicycle RV32M arithmetic unit\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU computes every multiply and divide result combinationally in\\nthe EX stage, including signed and unsigned 64-bit products plus divider and\\nremainder operators. That wide arithmetic cone is a likely Fmax limiter on\\nTang Nano 20K, while CoreMark's hot path should not need single-cycle DIV/REM\\nthroughput. Trading rare M-extension latency for a shorter common EX critical\\npath should improve iterations/second through higher clock frequency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split RV32M operations out of the always-combinational ALU path behind a\\nsmall multicycle M-unit handshake in EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/branch/address\\noperations purely combinational, but when the decoded alu_op is MUL/DIV/REM,\\nhold ID/EX and EX/MEM until the iterative arithmetic result is ready, then\\nretire normally through the existing MEM/WB and RVFI path. This should remove\\ndivider and large multiplier logic from the ordinary EX-stage timing path;\\nthe added stalls only affect uncommon M instructions and should be outweighed\\nby improved Fmax on the FPGA fitness metric.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Restrict the combinational ALU to fast RV32I operations and expose only lightweight M-op selection or helper logic needed by the new M-unit.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a multicycl...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Add fetch skid buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end has no real IF/ID storage: the IF payload is combinational from the current PC and imem data, and an imem-not-ready cycle injects a bubble directly into decode. With the tournament cosim using roughly 22% random instruction stalls, CoreMark loses many cycles even when the back end is already stalled by load-use, dmem, or the multicycle M unit. A small fetch buffer is a structural way to recover iterations/cycle without revisiting the prior broken ID-stage branch-resolution path or adding fetch-path target adders that hurt Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end has no real IF/ID storage: the IF payload is combinational from the current PC and imem data, and an imem-not-ready cycle injects a bubble directly into decode. With the tournament cosim using roughly 22% random instruction stalls, CoreMark loses many cycles even when the back end is already stalled by load-use, dmem, or the multicycle M unit. A small fetch buffer is a structural way to recover iterations/cycle without revisiting the prior broken ID-stage branch-resolution path or adding fetch-path target adders that hurt Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a one- or two-entry instruction skid buffer between IF and ID. The PC/imem side should continue fetching sequential instructions whenever the buffer has space, while the ID side consumes one buffered instruction only when ID/EX is ready. On redirect, flush the buffer and restart fetch at the EX-resolved target; on imem stalls, hold the outstanding fetch instead of emitting a synthetic NOP into decode. This should overlap random iStall cycles with existing back-end stalls and reduce front-end bubbles while preserving single-retire RVFI ordering and the existing EX-stage control-flow correctness.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"descr...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Ordered one-entry store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts spent timing and LUTs on the front end or on load\\nbypass paths, and those changes either regressed fitness or broke stall\\nhandling. The current core still freezes the entire pipeline whenever a store\\nreaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, even though independent ALU, branch, and\\nregister-only instructions behind that store could execute while the store is\\nwaiting. CoreMark has frequent data stores and the apples-to-apples cosim\\ninjects random dmem stalls, so store backpressure is a promising structural\\ntarget that avoids fetch-path target adders and load-result critical muxes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts spent timing and LUTs on the front end or on load\\nbypass paths, and those changes either regressed fitness or broke stall\\nhandling. The current core still freezes the entire pipeline whenever a store\\nreaches MEM and dmem_ready is low, even though independent ALU, branch, and\\nregister-only instructions behind that store could execute while the store is\\nwaiting. CoreMark has frequent data stores and the apples-to-apples cosim\\ninjects random dmem stalls, so store backpressure is a promising structural\\ntarget that avoids fetch-path target adders and load-result critical muxes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry ordered store buffer at the MEM boundary. A regular data\\nstore can retire into the buffer with its address, byte mask, write data, and\\nRVFI metadata captured, allowing the front of the pipeline to keep executing\\nnon-memory instructions while the buffer drives dmem until dmem_ready accepts\\nthe write. Preserve precise in-order behavior by stalling any younger load,\\nstore, MMIO access, FENCE, EBREAK, or illegal/trapping instruction until the\\nbuffer drains; MMIO marker/UART stores should bypass the buffer and keep the\\nexisting blocking behavior so CoreMark timing brackets re...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Pipeline EX operand retiming\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added fetch prediction, skid buffering, load bypassing,\\nor store buffering all paid too much control-path cost. The current core's remaining\\nhigh-fanout structural path is more basic: ID/EX values pass through forwarding muxes,\\nALU operand selection, ALU or branch compare/target logic, and into EX/MEM plus the\\nredirect path in one cycle. Retiming that boundary can target Fmax without adding a\\npredictor, memory ordering structure, or RVFI contract change.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the existing EX stage into a lightweight EX1 operand/metadata register and an EX2\\nexecute/redirect stage. EX1 captures the forwarded rs1/rs2 values, selected ALU operands,\\ndecoded control, immediate, PC, and RVFI source data; EX2 performs the ALU operation,\\nbranch/jump resolution, M-unit handshake, and drives the existing EX/MEM payload. Add the\\nminimal hazard/forwarding adjustments needed for the extra execute stage, preferring a\\none-cycle interlock on immediately dependent ALU consumers over wide new bypass muxes if\\nthat keeps timing short. This structural retiming should shorten the common forwarding-to-\\nALU-to-EX/MEM cone and may raise Tang Nano 20K Fmax enough to offset a small CPI cost,\\nwhile preserving NRET=1 retirement at MEM/WB and all RVFI ordering/trap behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\"...\n  Line 20: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Iterative M divider\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\nRV32I pipeline.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The largest proven fitness gain for this core came from moving RV32M arithmetic\\nout of the ordinary EX ALU path, but the current m_unit still contains wide\\ncombinational DIV/REM operators behind a one-cycle result register. Even if\\nthose operations are rare in CoreMark, the FPGA still has to place and time\\nthe divider/remainder cone, so it can cap Fmax or perturb routing for the hot\\nRV32I pipeline.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the one-cycle DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU implementation in m_unit with a small\\niterative restoring divider that runs only after a divide or remainder starts.\\nKeep MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing short registered path so any\\nmultiply use does not pay a 32-cycle penalty, and keep the existing EX-stage\\nhold/valid handshake as the only pipeline interface. This should remove the\\nexpensive combinational divider/remainder logic from the FPGA timing closure\\nproblem while adding CPI only for uncommon divide/remainder instructions.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/m_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU with an iterative signed/unsigned divider state machine while preserving ALTOPS behavior under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Adjust the existing M-unit busy/valid handshake only as needed so EX holds until multi-cycle DIV/REM completion and still clears completed results exactly once.\"}]}, \"expec...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Register dmem stall boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts added front-end prediction, fetch buffering, load\\nbypassing, store buffering, or a deeper EX pipe, and each paid too much in\\nmux/control cost or correctness complexity. A smaller structural target is\\nthe current dmem stall path: io_dmemReady fans combinationally through the\\nhazard unit into PC hold, ID hold, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB valid clearing in\\nthe same cycle. CoreMark's apples-to-apples mode injects random dmem stalls,\\nso this control path is active during evaluation, and reducing its fanout may\\nimprove Fmax without changing the visible memory ordering contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts added front-end prediction, fetch buffering, load\\nbypassing, store buffering, or a deeper EX pipe, and each paid too much in\\nmux/control cost or correctness complexity. A smaller structural target is\\nthe current dmem stall path: io_dmemReady fans combinationally through the\\nhazard unit into PC hold, ID hold, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB valid clearing in\\nthe same cycle. CoreMark's apples-to-apples mode injects random dmem stalls,\\nso this control path is active during evaluation, and reducing its fanout may\\nimprove Fmax without changing the visible memory ordering contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-cycle registered MEM busy/accepted boundary for data-memory\\noperations. When a load or store enters MEM, capture whether it is waiting for\\ndmem_ready in a small state bit local to the MEM/hazard interface; while that\\nbit is set, hold EX/MEM and clear MEM/WB valid exactly as today, but drive the\\nglobal pipeline stalls from the registered busy state rather than directly\\nfrom io_dmemReady. Keep dmem_addr, dmem_wdata, dmem_wen, dmem_ren, RVFI memory\\nmasks/data, misalignment traps, and all in-order retirement behavior\\nunchanged. This deliberately accepts at most one extra cycle on the first\\nstal...\n  Line 23: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Share M-unit multiplier hardware\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\npipeline.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already removed RV32M arithmetic from the ordinary EX\\nALU path, but m_unit still builds three separate 64-bit multiplier products\\nin parallel for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU signedness variants. CoreMark uses\\nM operations rarely enough that throughput beyond the existing one-cycle\\nregistered M-unit is not the main limiter, while the extra DSP/LUT/routing\\npressure can still depress FPGA Fmax or perturb placement of the hot RV32I\\npipeline.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the parallel signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned\\nproduct generation in m_unit with one shared signed multiplier fed by\\nsign/zero-extended operands selected from the M opcode. MUL can take the low\\nhalf of the same product because low 32 bits are independent of operand\\nsignedness; MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU select the high half with the appropriate\\noperand extension. Keep the existing start/valid/clear handshake, one-cycle\\nM latency, DIV/REM behavior, and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.\\nThis should trim multiplier area and routing without adding CPI, front-end\\nprediction logic, memory ordering state, or any RVFI-visible behavior change.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/m_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use a single sign-controlled widened multiplier for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU while pres...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Split MEM RVFI sideband path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The latest successful structural change split RVFI-only metadata away from the hot execution payloads and improved fitness without changing CPI. The remaining MEM stage still computes and registers RVFI memory sideband fields in the same combinational block and pipeline boundary that drives dmem, load extraction, writeback, and forwarding. A narrower structural separation in MEM is a safer follow-on than adding predictors, store buffers, load bypasses, or deeper execute retiming, all of which have regressed or broken previously.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The latest successful structural change split RVFI-only metadata away from the hot execution payloads and improved fitness without changing CPI. The remaining MEM stage still computes and registers RVFI memory sideband fields in the same combinational block and pipeline boundary that drives dmem, load extraction, writeback, and forwarding. A narrower structural separation in MEM is a safer follow-on than adding predictors, store buffers, load bypasses, or deeper execute retiming, all of which have regressed or broken previously.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the MEM-stage memory sideband work into a parallel RVFI-only path while keeping the execution path limited to dmem request signals, load_data, rd, compact control, and valid. The hot MEM/WB execution register should no longer share bundle assignment, hold, and mux fanout with RVFI-only mem_addr, mem_rmask, mem_wmask, mem_rdata, mem_wdata, pc, source values, and instruction fields. Preserve the existing single-retire MEM/WB retirement point, dmem stall behavior, misalignment traps, RVFI aligned-memory reporting, and NRET=1 port shape. This should reduce routing and enable fanout around the MEM boundary with little CPI risk, extending the proven RVFI-shadow split into the last stage where sideband metadata still sits next to timing-sensitive load/w...\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Localize WB-to-ID bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Prior structural attempts show that adding predictors, skid buffers, store buffers,\\nload bypasses, or deeper execute retiming usually costs more FPGA fitness than it\\nrecovers. The remaining promising structural direction is to reduce always-active\\nfanout and muxing without changing CPI or control-flow timing. The integer regfile\\ncurrently embeds write-first same-cycle bypass compares and 32-bit muxes on both\\ncombinational read ports, so WB write-enable/address/data fan out through the\\nregister file as well as to forwarding, RVFI, and writeback.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior structural attempts show that adding predictors, skid buffers, store buffers,\\nload bypasses, or deeper execute retiming usually costs more FPGA fitness than it\\nrecovers. The remaining promising structural direction is to reduce always-active\\nfanout and muxing without changing CPI or control-flow timing. The integer regfile\\ncurrently embeds write-first same-cycle bypass compares and 32-bit muxes on both\\ncombinational read ports, so WB write-enable/address/data fan out through the\\nregister file as well as to forwarding, RVFI, and writeback.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move the same-cycle WB-to-ID bypass out of reg_file and into id_stage as two local\\nread-data selects after the raw register-file read. The reg_file would become a\\nsimpler 2-read/1-write array with x0 forcing only, while id_stage receives the WB\\nwrite port and selects wb_w_data for rs1/rs2 when the decoded source matches the\\nretiring destination. This preserves the existing architectural behavior needed\\nfor consumers that are too far from the writer to be covered by EX forwarding, but\\nlocalizes the write-first muxing near decode and may let synthesis pack the regfile\\narray and WB fanout better. It avoids changing pipeline depth, redirect timing,\\nmemory ordering, RVFI retirement, or the NRET=1 top-level con...\n  Line 41: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r14s0\", \"title\": \"Retimed M-unit operand stage\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The largest proven bench-core gains came from keeping rare RV32M arithmetic off the ordinary RV32I execute path, and the smaller multiplier-sharing win suggests M-unit placement and timing still matter on the Tang Nano 20K. The current M unit still evaluates multiply/divide/remainder results directly from EX operands into the result register on the start edge, so a wide arithmetic cone remains a possible global Fmax limiter even though CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The largest proven bench-core gains came from keeping rare RV32M arithmetic off the ordinary RV32I execute path, and the smaller multiplier-sharing win suggests M-unit placement and timing still matter on the Tang Nano 20K. The current M unit still evaluates multiply/divide/remainder results directly from EX operands into the result register on the start edge, so a wide arithmetic cone remains a possible global Fmax limiter even though CoreMark rarely needs M-op throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a registered operand/opcode stage inside m_unit so start only captures the selected RV32M operation and operands, then compute and register the arithmetic result on the following cycle. Adjust the existing EX-stage hold logic to wait on the M unit's valid output rather than assuming exactly one bubble. This trades one extra cycle on rare MUL/DIV/REM instructions for a shorter start-to-register timing path, without touching branch prediction, memory bypassing, load-use behavior, RVFI fields, or the NRET=1 top-level interface.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/m_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Insert registered op/a/b operands ahead of the existing RV32M arithmetic and produce valid one cycle later while preserving RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS results.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the M-uni...\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Source-aware load interlock\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still treats both raw rs bitfields of the\\ninstruction in IF/ID as real consumers when deciding whether a preceding load\\nmust insert a bubble. That is structurally conservative but overly broad:\\nOP-IMM, LOAD, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK either ignore rs2 or\\nignore both source fields, so accidental matches against the load rd can\\ncreate false load-use stalls. CoreMark is load-heavy, and reducing bubbles\\nwithout adding predictors, MEM bypass muxes, store buffers, or deeper EX\\nstaging is one of the few remaining CPI improvements with modest timing risk.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still treats both raw rs bitfields of the\\ninstruction in IF/ID as real consumers when deciding whether a preceding load\\nmust insert a bubble. That is structurally conservative but overly broad:\\nOP-IMM, LOAD, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK either ignore rs2 or\\nignore both source fields, so accidental matches against the load rd can\\ncreate false load-use stalls. CoreMark is load-heavy, and reducing bubbles\\nwithout adding predictors, MEM bypass muxes, store buffers, or deeper EX\\nstaging is one of the few remaining CPI improvements with modest timing risk.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny source-use classifier for the IF/ID instruction and feed its\\nuses_rs1 and uses_rs2 bits only into the load-use hazard comparison. Keep the\\nexisting one-cycle interlock for real consumers, but suppress the stall when\\nthe matching raw bitfield is actually an immediate, destination, or unused\\nSYSTEM/FENCE field. This should improve iterations/cycle by removing false\\nbubbles while leaving RVFI rs1/rs2 address and data reporting unchanged, which\\navoids