ions.\\nThe divide/remainder operations (DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU) are still computed\\ninside the case statement without an explicit input gate. On FPGA\\ninference targets the divider is a large combinational cell whose\\nphysical path from a/b to out is analyzed by the STA tool even when\\nthe current instruction is not a divide, keeping the divider on the\\ncritical path for ADD/SHIFT/LOGIC operations. CoreMark rarely (if ever)\\nuses division, so any IPC impact is negligible.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU currently gates 64-bit multiply product evaluation behind an\\nopcode check, which was proven in a prior iteration to raise Fmax by\\nremoving the multiplier from the critical path of non-M instructions.\\nThe divide/remainder operations (DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU) are still computed\\ninside the case statement without an explicit input gate. On FPGA\\ninference targets the divider is a large combinational cell whose\\nphysical path from a/b to out is analyzed by the STA tool even when\\nthe current instruction is not a divide, keeping the divider on the\\ncritical path for ADD/SHIFT/LOGIC operations. CoreMark rarely (if ever)\\nuses division, so any IPC impact is negligible.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Introduce intermediate variables (div_result, divu_result, rem_result,\\nremu_result) and guard their computation with the same if-gate pattern\\nalready used for multiplies: only evaluate the `/` and `%` operators\\nwhen op is ALU_DIV/ALU_DIVU/ALU_REM/ALU_REMU.  The case statement then\\nselects the pre-computed variables.  This physically disconnects the\\ndivider inputs for non-DIV instructions, shortening the worst-case\\ncombinational path and raising Fmax with no ar...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"8-entry direct-mapped instruction cache in IF to absorb imem stalls\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The fitness metric multiplies FPGA Fmax (measured with zero-wait imem) by\\nCoreMark IPC (measured under ~22% r