X control bundle still carries an `is_lui` bit from the decoder even though execute never consumes it; LUI behavior is already fully encoded by `alu_op = ALU_LUI`, `alu_src = 1`, and the U-immediate.\\n\\nThis is a narrower follow-up than removing larger payload fields such as the EX/MEM branch metadata that previously regressed.  It targets one demonstrably dead decode/control signal with no CPI, RVFI, memory, divider, or branch-policy changes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The strongest recent win came from narrowing stage-local control payloads, which suggests packed-control routing and reset/flush muxing are still important on this FPGA.  The current ID/EX control bundle still carries an `is_lui` bit from the decoder even though execute never consumes it; LUI behavior is already fully encoded by `alu_op = ALU_LUI`, `alu_src = 1`, and the U-immediate.\\n\\nThis is a narrower follow-up than removing larger payload fields such as the EX/MEM branch metadata that previously regressed.  It targets one demonstrably dead decode/control signal with no CPI, RVFI, memory, divider, or branch-policy changes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Delete the `is_lui` control signal from `ctrl_t`, remove the decoder output port and ID-stage plumbing for it, and keep LUI decode legal by continuing to drive `ALU_LUI`, `alu_src`, and `reg_write` exactly as today.  This should trim one bit from the packed ID/EX control register plus its flush/reset assignment cone and reduce decoder-to-ID/EX routing fanout.\\n\\nBecause no downstream logic reads `is_lui`, the transformation should be functionally neutral: RVFI traps still come from `is_illegal`, LUI writeback still comes from the existing ALU_LUI path, and the...\n  Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Direct-JAL target memo predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The bench-core predictor history shows that conditional branch prediction,\\nBTBs, RAS schemes, exact-instruction matching, and target replay have all\\ntended to lose more Fmax than they recover in CoreMark CPI. The remaining\\nuseful predictor target is direct JAL: it is always taken, has a stable\\nresolved target after first execution, and needs no branch-condition compare,\\ncounter update policy, return stack, or instruction-side immediate decode in\\nthe fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench-core predictor history shows that conditional branch prediction,\\nBTBs, RAS schemes, exact-instruction matching, and target replay have all\\ntended to lose more Fmax than they recover in CoreMark CPI. The remaining\\nuseful predictor target is direct JAL: it is always taken, has a stable\\nresolved target after first execution, and needs no branch-condition compare,\\ncounter update policy, return stack, or instruction-side immediate decode in\\nthe fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry direct-JAL target memo. EX updates the entry only when a\\nvalid, aligned, non-JALR JAL retires through the existing resolved-target\\npath. IF predicts only on a full-PC tag hit, selecting the stored target\\ninstead of pc+4, and carries a predicted-next-PC value with that instruction\\nso EX can suppress the duplicate redirect when correct or repair any stale\\nsymbolic-stream mismatch precisely. This should save repeated direct-call and\\nunconditional-jump bubbles in CoreMark while being narrower than prior\\npredictor attempts: no conditional branch table, no counters, no RAS, no\\nfetched-instruction match feeding the PC mux, and no target adder in IF.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add minimal predicted-next-PC metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX for JAL-memo hits only.\"},...\n  Line 35: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Bypass AUIPC ALU input mux\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"Recent wins came from removing existing muxing and packed-control fanout,\\nwhile predictors, load bypasses, and fine-grained bundle edits have often\\nregressed. The EX stage still routes every ALU operation through an\\nAUIPC-only operand-A mux even though AUIPC is the only instruction that\\nneeds pc+imm as an arithmetic result, and the EX stage already computes\\npc+imm for branch and direct-jump targets.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent wins came from removing existing muxing and packed-control fanout,\\nwhile predictors, load bypasses, and fine-grained bundle edits have often\\nregressed. The EX stage still routes every ALU operation through an\\nAUIPC-only operand-A mux even though AUIPC is the only instruction that\\nneeds pc+imm as an arithmetic result, and the EX stage already computes\\npc+imm for branch and direct-jump targets.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move AUIPC result selection out of the common ALU operand path. Keep the\\nALU operand A as the forwarded rs1 value for normal operations, compute one\\nlocal pc+imm sum in EX, reuse that sum for branch targets, direct JAL\\ntargets, and AUIPC writeback, and select the AUIPC result at the existing\\nEX/MEM result boundary. This should remove a 32-bit mux from the hot\\nforwarded-operand-to-ALU cone and reduce duplicate pc+imm adder routing\\nwithout changing CPI, branch policy, RVFI fields, traps, memory behavior,\\nor the nret=1 top-level contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Reuse a single pc+imm sum for branch/direct-JAL/AUIPC and remove the AUIPC select from the common ALU operand-A path.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Reuse a single pc+imm sum for branch/direct-JAL/AUIPC and remove the AUIPC select from the co...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Remove IF NOP injection mux\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench history shows that added predictors, FIFOs, store buffers, load bypasses, and broad valid-gated rewrites tend to lose more Fmax than they recover. The reliable structural wins have instead removed existing muxing and packed-control fanout without changing CPI. The current IF stage still rewrites every fetched instruction through a 32-bit NOP mux on redirect or imem stall so the hazard unit and ID/EX controls see a harmless bubble.\\n\\nThat NOP mux sits directly on the live io_imemData-to-decode, regfile-address, immediate, and hazard-predecode path. The pipeline already carries an IF/ID valid bit, so the same bubble semantics can be represented with valid plus explicit side-effect-control zeroing in ID instead of modifying the instruction word before decode.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench history shows that added predictors, FIFOs, store buffers, load bypasses, and broad valid-gated rewrites tend to lose more Fmax than they recover. The reliable structural wins have instead removed existing muxing and packed-control fanout without changing CPI. The current IF stage still rewrites every fetched instruction through a 32-bit NOP mux on redirect or imem stall so the hazard unit and ID/EX controls see a harmless bubble.\\n\\nThat NOP mux sits directly on the live io_imemData-to-decode, regfile-address, immediate, and hazard-predecode path. The pipeline already carries an IF/ID valid bit, so the same bubble semantics can be represented with valid plus explicit side-effect-control zeroing in ID instead of modifying the instruction word before decode.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Stop forcing IF/ID instr to ADDI x0,x0,0 on flush, redirect, and imem stall. Let if_stage pass the fetched instruction bits through unchanged while marking the IF/ID payload invalid, then make id_stage register a side-effect-free control word whenever in.valid is low. Add IF/I...\n  Line 37: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Predict imem stall replay hits\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior branch predictors for this core repeatedly regressed because they put\\ntarget lookup, target arithmetic, instruction matching, or wide repair\\nmetadata on the PC redirect path. CoreMark is still evaluated with randomized\\ninstruction-memory stalls, and hot loop bodies repeatedly refetch the same\\nsmall set of instruction PCs. A predictor that only speculates on the fetched\\ninstruction word during an imem stall can target CPI without predicting a new\\nPC or changing control-flow resolution.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior branch predictors for this core repeatedly regressed because they put\\ntarget lookup, target arithmetic, instruction matching, or wide repair\\nmetadata on the PC redirect path. CoreMark is still evaluated with randomized\\ninstruction-memory stalls, and hot loop bodies repeatedly refetch the same\\nsmall set of instruction PCs. A predictor that only speculates on the fetched\\ninstruction word during an imem stall can target CPI without predicting a new\\nPC or changing control-flow resolution.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny direct-mapped fetch replay predictor inside IF that remembers a few\\nsuccessfully fetched {pc, instr} pairs. On a later cycle where io_imemReady is\\nlow, IF may supply the cached instruction as valid only when the current PC tag\\nhits; otherwise it preserves today's NOP/bubble behavior. The PC still advances\\nsequentially or redirects exactly as it does now, and EX remains the sole\\nbranch/jump resolver, so this avoids the failed BTB/BTFNT/RAS pattern of\\nfeeding predicted targets into the PC mux. Under the 22 percent randomized\\niStall model, repeated CoreMark loop instructions should occasionally decode\\nthrough an otherwise lost imem-stall cycle, while misses and redirects fall\\nback to the existing conservative path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"f...\n  Line 38: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r13s0\", \"title\": \"Factor bypass active gating\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The bench core's successful recent changes have mostly removed existing\\nfanout and packed-control routing without adding new speculation or stall\\npolicy. The forwarding and regfile bypass paths still repeat the same\\nproducer-active checks for both source operands: write enable, x0 filtering,\\nand load exclusion. CoreMark's dependency-heavy integer code exercises these\\npaths constantly, and the previous one-hot forwarding attempt showed that a\\nbroader interface rewrite can regress, so the safer angle is a local factoring\\nof the existing encoded bypass logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench core's successful recent changes have mostly removed existing\\nfanout and packed-control routing without adding new speculation or stall\\npolicy. The forwarding and regfile bypass paths still repeat the same\\nproducer-active checks for both source operands: write enable, x0 filtering,\\nand load exclusion. CoreMark's dependency-heavy integer code exercises these\\npaths constantly, and the previous one-hot forwarding attempt showed that a\\nbroader interface rewrite can regress, so the safer angle is a local factoring\\nof the existing encoded bypass logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the 2-bit forwarding select interface and all current priority rules,\\nbut precompute shared active-producer terms inside the bypass blocks. In\\nrtl/forward_unit.sv, form one EX/MEM-can-forward signal and one MEM/WB-can-\\nforward signal, then reuse them for the rs1 and rs2 hit comparisons instead\\nof duplicating write-enable, load, and x0 checks in two always_comb blocks. In\\nrtl/reg_file.sv, similarly form one write_active term for the synchronous\\nwrite and both same-cycle read bypass checks. This should trim small but hot\\ncomparator/control fanout on the EX operand and ID read paths without