{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778835273546,"sessionID":"ses_1d5292540ffepjDgtvUr54dZ4p","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_d6UTheNWjVGy0npSBB5TxrNw","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep3/log.jsonl","offset":1,"limit":25},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep3/log.jsonl</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\"id\": \"baseline-bench-4123a25\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 282.82, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9563, \"ff\": 1866, \"seeds\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-12T20:27:54.639446Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\n2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Move DIV/REM off ALU critical path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current one-cycle ALU contains real SystemVerilog division and remainder\\noperators in the same EX-stage result cone as common integer operations.\\nThose operators are expensive on the Tang Nano 20K fabric and likely depress\\nFmax, while CoreMark's timed region should execute DIV/REM rarely or not at\\nall. Improving Fmax without touching the common ADD/branch/load/store path is\\ntherefore a promising fitness win.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current one-cycle ALU contains real SystemVerilog division and remainder\\noperators in the same EX-stage result cone as common integer operations.\\nThose operators are expensive on the Tang Nano 20K fabric and likely depress\\nFmax, while CoreMark's timed region should execute DIV/REM rarely or not at\\nall. Improving Fmax without touching the common ADD/branch/load/store path is\\ntherefore a promising fitness win.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational ALU into a small bounded\\niterative divider. The in-order pipeline should stall only when one of those\\nrare operations is in EX, then write the completed result through the existing\\nEX/MEM path. Keep MUL/MULH variants one-cycle and keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\\nsubstitutions combinational so formal remains tractable. The expected Fmax and\\nLUT improvement should outweigh the occasional divider latency penalty on\\nCoreMark.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real DIV/REM operators from the normal one-cycle ALU fast path while preserving ALTOPS substitutions for formal builds.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a bounded iterative RV32 divider/remainder unit with divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Start DIV/REM operations, hold the EX instruction while the divid... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV/REM to multicycle EX unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and\\nremainder logic in the single-cycle EX path. On a small FPGA this is likely\\na major LUT and timing cost even though CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by\\nadds, compares, loads, stores, branches, and multiplies rather than divides.\\nRetiming only DIV/REM into a small multicycle unit should raise median Fmax\\nand reduce routing pressure while preserving single-cycle behavior for the\\ncommon integer operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and\\nremainder logic in the single-cycle EX path. On a small FPGA this is likely\\na major LUT and timing cost even though CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by\\nadds, compares, loads, stores, branches, and multiplies rather than divides.\\nRetiming only DIV/REM into a small multicycle unit should raise median Fmax\\nand reduce routing pressure while preserving single-cycle behavior for the\\ncommon integer operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split RV32M division and remainder out of rtl/alu.sv into a dedicated\\niterative divider/remainder unit issued by EX. When EX sees DIV, DIVU, REM, or\\nREMU, it should hold IF/ID/EX, keep the operation's operands and RVFI payload\\nstable, and release one EX/MEM result only when the unit completes. MUL/MULH\\noperations remain in the existing combinational ALU so CoreMark's multiply\\nthroughput is not sacrificed. The added rare-operation stalls should cost few\\nCoreMark cycles, while the shorter common EX critical path should improve the\\nFmax side of the fitness metric.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an iterative RV32 DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU unit with RV32-defined divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remo... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Add BTFNT fetch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and only redirects when a branch or jump reaches EX, so every taken CoreMark loop branch pays a multi-cycle wrong-path bubble. CoreMark is loop-heavy, and the present bench history has no prior predictor attempts, making branch penalty reduction the cleanest predictor-side opportunity.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and only redirects when a branch or jump reaches EX, so every taken CoreMark loop branch pays a multi-cycle wrong-path bubble. CoreMark is loop-heavy, and the present bench history has no prior predictor attempts, making branch penalty reduction the cleanest predictor-side opportunity.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small static fetch predictor that predicts JAL and backward conditional branches taken, predicts forward conditional branches not taken, and carries the predicted next PC with the instruction into EX. EX should redirect only when the resolved next PC differs from the predicted next PC, eliminating the normal taken-backward-loop penalty while preserving exact RVFI pc_wdata, trap behavior, and the nret=1 port contract. This should improve CoreMark cycles with modest LUT/critical-path risk because it avoids tables and uses only IF-side opcode/offset decode plus one predicted-PC field in the pipeline bundles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted next-PC metadata to the IF/ID, ID/EX, and EX/MEM pipeline bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode fetched branch and JAL immediates in IF and select a BTFNT/JAL predicted next PC instead of always pc+4.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the predicted next PC from fetch into the ID/EX register across stalls and flushes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_st... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n5: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Share MUL hardware in ALU\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current post-divider core still computes three independent 64-bit\\nmultiply products in the combinational ALU: signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned,\\nand signed*unsigned. CoreMark keeps MUL single-cycle useful, but carrying\\nthree multiplier cones through the Tang Nano 20K fabric likely costs LUTs,\\nrouting, and Fmax even though only one MUL variant can retire at a time.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current post-divider core still computes three independent 64-bit\\nmultiply products in the combinational ALU: signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned,\\nand signed*unsigned. CoreMark keeps MUL single-cycle useful, but carrying\\nthree multiplier cones through the Tang Nano 20K fabric likely costs LUTs,\\nrouting, and Fmax even though only one MUL variant can retire at a time.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel multiplier products in rtl/alu.sv with one\\nsign-configurable 33x33 multiplier. Select operand sign extension from the\\nALU opcode, use the low 32 bits for MUL, and use product bits 63:32 for\\nMULH/MULHU/MULHSU. This should preserve all RV32M results and single-cycle\\nmultiply latency while shrinking the EX-stage arithmetic cone and improving\\nmedian FPGA Fmax without changing CoreMark instruction count.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three always-active multiply products with one opcode-controlled signed 33x33 product while leaving ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add or keep focused MULH/MULHU/MULHSU edge vectors so the shared multiplier sign-extension cases remain guarded.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three always-active multiply products with one opcode-controlled signed 33x33 product wh... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Share M-extension multiplier datapath\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The successful structural change already removed DIV/REM from the common EX\\ncritical path, leaving the ALU's parallel multiplier bank as a likely large\\ntiming and routing cost. The current ALU computes signed*signed,\\nunsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned 64-bit products in parallel even\\nthough only one MUL-family result can be selected for a retiring instruction.\\nCoreMark likely uses MUL often enough that making multiplication multicycle is\\nrisky, but sharing the single-cycle datapath should reduce LUT/DSP routing\\npressure without adding any stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The successful structural change already removed DIV/REM from the common EX\\ncritical path, leaving the ALU's parallel multiplier bank as a likely large\\ntiming and routing cost. The current ALU computes signed*signed,\\nunsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned 64-bit products in parallel even\\nthough only one MUL-family result can be selected for a retiring instruction.\\nCoreMark likely uses MUL often enough that making multiplication multicycle is\\nrisky, but sharing the single-cycle datapath should reduce LUT/DSP routing\\npressure without adding any stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU product trees in rtl/alu.sv\\nwith one sign-configurable 33x33 multiplier. Select the sign extension of each\\noperand from the ALU op, use the low 32 bits for MUL, and use product[63:32]\\nfor the high-half variants. Keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions and the\\nexisting DIV/REM handoff unchanged. This preserves the current five-stage\\nstructure and single-cycle MUL throughput while trimming a high-fanout EX-stage\\ndatapath, so any Fmax improvement should translate directly into better\\nCoreMark iter/sec.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Collapse... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n7: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Add tiny EX-updated BTB\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current core predicts every control-flow instruction as not taken and only redirects in EX, so hot CoreMark loop branches and direct jumps pay wrong-path bubbles repeatedly. The prior BTFNT predictor tried to remove those bubbles but added opcode decode, immediate extraction, and target adders to the IF/PC path, which likely consumed the cycle savings as Fmax loss.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core predicts every control-flow instruction as not taken and only redirects in EX, so hot CoreMark loop branches and direct jumps pay wrong-path bubbles repeatedly. The prior BTFNT predictor tried to remove those bubbles but added opcode decode, immediate extraction, and target adders to the IF/PC path, which likely consumed the cycle savings as Fmax loss.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small direct-mapped branch target buffer that is read in IF and updated only from resolved control-flow instructions in EX. Each entry stores valid, a compact PC tag, a resolved target, and a 2-bit taken counter; IF predicts taken only on tag hit with a taken counter and otherwise preserves the current sequential fetch behavior. This targets the same CoreMark loop-branch penalty as BTFNT while avoiding IF-side instruction decode and target addition, replacing them with a narrow tag compare and stored-target mux on the PC path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC and prediction-hit/taken metadata to the pipeline bundles so EX can distinguish correct predictions from mispredictions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Read a tiny direct-mapped BTB using the current PC and select the stored target only on a valid tag hit with a taken counter.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare resolved next PC against the carried prediction, re... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n8: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Source-aware load-use stalls\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current hazard unit stalls after every load when the following\\ninstruction's raw rs1 or rs2 bit fields match the load destination. That is\\nconservative but over-stalls instructions whose matching field is actually\\nan immediate, destination, or unused encoding field, such as OP-IMM, LOAD,\\nLUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK. CoreMark is load-heavy, so removing\\nthese false load-use bubbles can improve iterations per cycle without\\ntouching the already-sensitive fetch prediction or multiplier paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current hazard unit stalls after every load when the following\\ninstruction's raw rs1 or rs2 bit fields match the load destination. That is\\nconservative but over-stalls instructions whose matching field is actually\\nan immediate, destination, or unused encoding field, such as OP-IMM, LOAD,\\nLUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK. CoreMark is load-heavy, so removing\\nthese false load-use bubbles can improve iterations per cycle without\\ntouching the already-sensitive fetch prediction or multiplier paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Teach the load-use interlock which source operands the IF/ID instruction\\nreally consumes, then qualify the existing rd comparisons with use_rs1 and\\nuse_rs2. This keeps the mandatory one-cycle stall for true load consumers\\nsuch as OP, STORE, BRANCH, JALR, and LOAD base-address dependencies, while\\nallowing non-consuming or immediate-field matches to proceed. The change is\\na small decode-side micro-optimization: it should reduce CoreMark cycles and\\nadd only simple opcode checks off the critical IF PC path, preserving the\\nnret=1 RVFI contract and all trap semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add opcode-based use_rs1/use_rs2 qualification to the load-use hazard comparison so only true consumers st... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Add sequential prefetch FIFO\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end directly couples decode consumption to raw imemReady: every\\ninstruction-memory stall injects a bubble, and backend stalls also stop the PC\\neven when the instruction bus could have fetched useful sequential work. Prior\\npredictor attempts showed that adding target decode/BTB logic on the IF path can\\nlose Fmax, so the safer structural opportunity is to decouple fetch from decode\\nwithout predicting control flow.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end directly couples decode consumption to raw imemReady: every\\ninstruction-memory stall injects a bubble, and backend stalls also stop the PC\\neven when the instruction bus could have fetched useful sequential work. Prior\\npredictor attempts showed that adding target decode/BTB logic on the IF path can\\nlose Fmax, so the safer structural opportunity is to decouple fetch from decode\\nwithout predicting control flow.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny two-entry fall-through sequential prefetch FIFO in the IF stage that\\nstores fetched {pc,instr} pairs. When ID/EX is stalled by load-use, dmem, or DIV\\nbackpressure, the IF side should keep requesting sequential instructions while\\nFIFO space remains; when imemReady drops but the FIFO is non-empty, decode can\\ncontinue from the buffered head instead of taking an immediate bubble. On any\\nEX redirect, flush the FIFO and restart fetch at the resolved target, preserving\\nthe existing no-predictor control-flow semantics and exact RVFI pc/instruction\\nordering. This should improve CoreMark iterations/cycle under the 22 percent\\nrandomized iStall+dStall methodology while adding only small FIFO control muxes,\\nnot branch target adders or BTB lookup logic, to the front end.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the PC-only fetch path with a ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Pipeline lookahead BTB\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The core still fetches sequentially until control flow resolves in EX, so hot CoreMark loop branches and direct jumps pay wrong-path bubbles. Prior predictor attempts showed that the main risk is not correctness but Fmax: IF-side BTFNT decode/target adders and same-cycle BTB lookup logic lengthened the PC feedback path enough to erase cycle savings. The remaining promising predictor angle is to keep dynamic branch learning while moving lookup work off the PC critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The core still fetches sequentially until control flow resolves in EX, so hot CoreMark loop branches and direct jumps pay wrong-path bubbles. Prior predictor attempts showed that the main risk is not correctness but Fmax: IF-side BTFNT decode/target adders and same-cycle BTB lookup logic lengthened the PC feedback path enough to erase cycle savings. The remaining promising predictor angle is to keep dynamic branch learning while moving lookup work off the PC critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny direct-mapped lookahead BTB whose tag/counter/target lookup is performed for the next sequential PC and registered one cycle before that PC reaches IF. The PC update path should then choose between EX redirect, the already-registered prediction for the current PC, and pc+4; no same-cycle table read, tag compare, counter check, or target read feeds the PC register. EX updates the table on resolved conditional branches and direct JALs, carries the predicted next PC with each instruction, and redirects only when the resolved next PC differs from the carried prediction. This should recover repeated taken-loop and direct-jump bubbles while avoiding the Fmax loss seen in the prior IF-side predictors.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC and prediction-val... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n11: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Hazard-only source-use interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use detector stalls whenever a load destination matches either\\nraw source field in the following instruction, even when that field is actually\\nan immediate, destination, or otherwise unused encoding bit. CoreMark is\\nload-heavy, and the previous source-aware attempt failed from changing decoded\\nsource/RVFI fields rather than from the interlock idea itself.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use detector stalls whenever a load destination matches either\\nraw source field in the following instruction, even when that field is actually\\nan immediate, destination, or otherwise unused encoding bit. CoreMark is\\nload-heavy, and the previous source-aware attempt failed from changing decoded\\nsource/RVFI fields rather than from the interlock idea itself.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small hazard-only predecode for the IF/ID instruction's real source\\noperands and qualify the load-use rd comparisons with those bits. Keep the raw\\nrs1/rs2 addresses and values in the pipeline exactly as encoded so cosim and\\nRVFI continue to see the architectural instruction fields, while only the stall\\ndecision skips false dependencies for OP-IMM, LOAD, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and\\nEBREAK-style non-consuming forms. This should reduce unnecessary bubbles with\\nlittle timing risk because it touches only the existing hazard decision and adds\\nno predictor, FIFO, multiplier, or critical fetch-target machinery.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify load-use rd comparisons with hazard-only use_rs1/use_rs2 bits derived from the IF/ID opcode, keeping unknown source-consuming classes conservative.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass the IF/ID instruction or opcode into the hazard unit without changing ID/EX raw source fields, regfile reads, or... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Add conservative store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Prior structural and predictor attempts show that adding more IF-side control\\nhurts this core, while the benchmark still runs with randomized dmem stalls.\\nStores currently freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever dmemReady is low,\\neven though normal memory stores produce no register result and many following\\nALU/control instructions do not need the data port immediately.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior structural and predictor attempts show that adding more IF-side control\\nhurts this core, while the benchmark still runs with randomized dmem stalls.\\nStores currently freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever dmemReady is low,\\neven though normal memory stores produce no register result and many following\\nALU/control instructions do not need the data port immediately.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry store buffer in the MEM side that accepts aligned normal-memory\\nstores when dmemReady is low, retires the store through the existing MEM/WB and\\nRVFI path, and drains the buffered write on the dmem port as soon as the bus is\\nready. Keep all MMIO stores, including CoreMark start/stop markers and UART, on\\nthe current blocking path so externally observed timing is unchanged. While the\\nbuffer is full, stall younger loads and stores until it drains, but allow younger\\nnon-memory instructions to keep flowing. This targets CoreMark cycles lost to\\nrandom store backpressure without adding branch prediction, fetch FIFO, or other\\nfront-end critical-path logic.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry buffered normal-memory store path, drain muxing for dmem outputs, and MMIO/blocking-store bypass rules.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single dmem_stall condition with load/MMIO-store/buffer-full blocking while al... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n13: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Add one-entry loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The core still pays an EX-stage redirect bubble on every taken loop branch and direct jump.\\nPrior predictor attempts lost fitness because IF-side instruction decode, target adders,\\nBTB arrays, tag/counter lookup, or broad prediction metadata increased front-end timing\\ncost more than they saved in CoreMark cycles. A narrower predictor should target only the\\nhottest repeated taken control-flow PCs while keeping the PC path close to the current\\nsequential fetch design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The core still pays an EX-stage redirect bubble on every taken loop branch and direct jump.\\nPrior predictor attempts lost fitness because IF-side instruction decode, target adders,\\nBTB arrays, tag/counter lookup, or broad prediction metadata increased front-end timing\\ncost more than they saved in CoreMark cycles. A narrower predictor should target only the\\nhottest repeated taken control-flow PCs while keeping the PC path close to the current\\nsequential fetch design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry last-taken target predictor for conditional branches and direct JALs.\\nThe entry stores one full PC tag, one resolved target, and a tiny 2-bit confidence counter;\\nIF predicts taken only on an exact tag hit with the counter MSB set, otherwise it fetches\\npc+4 exactly as today. EX carries and compares the predicted next PC against the resolved\\nnext PC, redirecting only on mismatch, so correct hot-loop predictions avoid the normal\\ntaken-branch bubble while loop exits are repaired precisely. This avoids IF opcode decode,\\nimmediate generation, multi-entry BTB muxing, and lookahead-table plumbing, trading one\\ncomparator and one target mux for fewer CoreMark loop-control cycles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry predicted-next-PC validity and value from IF ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n14: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Case decode memory lanes\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM stage extracts load bytes with a variable right shift and\\nbuilds byte masks with variable left shifts. Even though the shift amount is\\nonly two address bits, FPGA synthesis can still leave a deeper mux/shifter\\ncone on the dmem read-data to MEM/WB path and on store mask generation.\\nCoreMark is load/store heavy, so trimming this path should improve Fmax\\nwithout adding control state or changing the instruction schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM stage extracts load bytes with a variable right shift and\\nbuilds byte masks with variable left shifts. Even though the shift amount is\\nonly two address bits, FPGA synthesis can still leave a deeper mux/shifter\\ncone on the dmem read-data to MEM/WB path and on store mask generation.\\nCoreMark is load/store heavy, so trimming this path should improve Fmax\\nwithout adding control state or changing the instruction schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the dynamic load-data shift and shifted byte-mask expressions in\\nrtl/mem_stage.sv with explicit case-decode lane muxes for addr[1:0] and\\naccess width. Use direct byte and halfword selection for LB/LBU/LH/LHU/LW,\\nand generate SB/SH/SW masks with small fixed cases while preserving the\\nexisting misalignment trap gating and RVFI aligned-memory reporting. This\\nshould reduce LUT depth and routing on the memory data path while keeping\\ncycle count, RVFI fields, and all memory-bound invariants unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace variable shifts for load extraction and byte-mask generation with explicit addr[1:0] case muxes while preserving misalignment traps and RVFI masks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace variable shift... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Add MEM-to-EX load bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The recent hazard-only source-use interlock removed false load-use stalls, but\\ntrue load-use pairs still always pay a bubble even when the data memory is\\nready. CoreMark is load-heavy, and the benchmark injects randomized dmem\\nstalls, so letting a dependent instruction wait in EX and consume the load as\\nsoon as the MEM-stage data is available can improve iterations per cycle\\nwithout adding the front-end predictor, FIFO, or store-buffer structures that\\nregressed in prior rounds.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The recent hazard-only source-use interlock removed false load-use stalls, but\\ntrue load-use pairs still always pay a bubble even when the data memory is\\nready. CoreMark is load-heavy, and the benchmark injects randomized dmem\\nstalls, so letting a dependent instruction wait in EX and consume the load as\\nsoon as the MEM-stage data is available can improve iterations per cycle\\nwithout adding the front-end predictor, FIFO, or store-buffer structures that\\nregressed in prior rounds.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a combinational MEM-to-EX forwarding source for aligned ready loads. The\\nMEM stage should expose the same sign/zero-extended load value it will commit\\ninto MEM/WB, and the forwarding unit should select that value for a younger\\ndependent instruction when EX/MEM holds a valid load whose dmem transaction is\\nready and non-trapping. The load-use interlock should then stall only when the\\nload result cannot be forwarded, such as dmemReady being low or the access\\nbeing misaligned; otherwise the dependent instruction advances into EX and\\nreceives the load value through the normal operand muxes. This preserves the\\nnret=1 RVFI retirement boundary and architectural rs fields while targeting\\none of the remaining CoreMark CPI costs.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"d... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n16: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Decode-stage JAL predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The previous predictor attempts all attacked conditional loop branches from the\\nfetch side and regressed because IF-side decode, BTB lookup, target muxing, or\\nbroad prediction metadata lengthened the front-end timing path more than they\\nrecovered in CoreMark cycles. The remaining predictor opportunity is to target\\nonly an always-taken control-flow class with much narrower hardware.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The previous predictor attempts all attacked conditional loop branches from the\\nfetch side and regressed because IF-side decode, BTB lookup, target muxing, or\\nbroad prediction metadata lengthened the front-end timing path more than they\\nrecovered in CoreMark cycles. The remaining predictor opportunity is to target\\nonly an always-taken control-flow class with much narrower hardware.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Predict direct JAL instructions as taken in decode, using the decoder and\\nimmediate generator that already exist in the ID stage, and redirect the PC one\\ncycle earlier than the current EX-stage jump resolution. Carry a minimal\\npredicted-next-PC bit/value with the JAL into EX so EX suppresses the duplicate\\nredirect when the predicted target matches, but still repairs any mismatch and\\nleaves JALR and conditional branches on the current conservative path. Gate the\\nearly redirect on valid, unstalled, aligned direct JALs so misaligned jump traps,\\nRVFI pc_wdata, and the nret=1 contract remain unchanged. This should save a\\nwrong-path bubble on calls and unconditional loop/control jumps without adding\\ntables, counters, branch comparators, or IF-side opcode/branch-immediate logic.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add minimal predicted-next-PC metadata to the ID/EX payload for decode-predicted direct JALs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n17: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Trim dead EX/MEM branch payload\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core appears sensitive to small timing and routing changes:\\npredictor, FIFO, store-buffer, and load-bypass attempts regressed, while the\\nsuccessful changes were narrow datapath or hazard micro-optimizations. The\\nEX/MEM bundle still carries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are\\nassigned in EX but never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, or redirect repair.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core appears sensitive to small timing and routing changes:\\npredictor, FIFO, store-buffer, and load-bypass attempts regressed, while the\\nsuccessful changes were narrow datapath or hazard micro-optimizations. The\\nEX/MEM bundle still carries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are\\nassigned in EX but never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, or redirect repair.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Remove the unused branch_taken and branch_target fields from the EX/MEM\\npipeline payload and delete their EX-stage assignments. Redirect correctness\\nalready comes from the same-cycle redirect/redirect_target outputs, and RVFI\\nonly needs pc_next at retirement, so these flops and their write-enable muxing\\nare dead state. Trimming them should slightly reduce FF count, fanout, and\\nEX/MEM routing pressure without changing instruction count, branch behavior,\\nmemory timing, or the nret=1 RVFI contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove unused branch_taken and branch_target fields from ex_mem_t while keeping pc_next for RVFI.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Delete assignments to the removed EX/MEM branch metadata in normal and DIV/REM completion paths.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Update comments/lint assumptions that mention unused branch metadata, without changing MEM behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\"... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Register writeback payload in MEM/WB\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added prediction, queues, store buffering, or\\nsame-cycle load bypassing all paid more timing/control cost than CoreMark\\nrecovered in CPI. The remaining safer structural opportunity is to shorten an\\nexisting high-fanout pipeline boundary without adding new stalls or state\\nmachines. Today the MEM/WB register carries both ALU and load data, then the\\nWB-stage mux drives regfile writeback, MEM/WB forwarding, and RVFI rd_wdata in\\nthe same cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added prediction, queues, store buffering, or\\nsame-cycle load bypassing all paid more timing/control cost than CoreMark\\nrecovered in CPI. The remaining safer structural opportunity is to shorten an\\nexisting high-fanout pipeline boundary without adding new stalls or state\\nmachines. Today the MEM/WB register carries both ALU and load data, then the\\nWB-stage mux drives regfile writeback, MEM/WB forwarding, and RVFI rd_wdata in\\nthe same cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Fold the final writeback-data selection into the MEM/WB registered payload.\\nThe MEM stage already has the load result, ALU result, and mem_to_reg control\\nwhen it clocks the retirement bundle, so it can register a single rd_wdata\\nfield alongside the existing RVFI fields. The WB stage can then become a thin\\nvalid/reg_write/rd gate that forwards this registered payload directly to the\\nregfile and existing forwarding path. This preserves the five-stage schedule,\\nnret=1 RVFI contract, load-use behavior, and all trap/memory semantics while\\nremoving a 32-bit mux from the writeback-to-ID bypass and RVFI output cones.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered rd_wdata/writeback-result field to mem_wb_t so the retirement boundary carries the final re... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n19: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"ID backward branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still pays the EX-stage redirect penalty on hot taken loop branches, but every prior fetch-side predictor in this core lost more Fmax than it recovered in CPI. The remaining predictor opportunity is to target only high-frequency backward conditional branches while avoiding BTB arrays, counters, direct-JAL handling, and full predicted-PC metadata.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still pays the EX-stage redirect penalty on hot taken loop branches, but every prior fetch-side predictor in this core lost more Fmax than it recovered in CPI. The remaining predictor opportunity is to target only high-frequency backward conditional branches while avoiding BTB arrays, counters, direct-JAL handling, and full predicted-PC metadata.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a narrow decode-stage static predictor for valid backward conditional branches only. When ID sees a backward branch, redirect fetch to pc+imm one cycle before the current EX resolution point and carry only a single predicted-taken bit in ID/EX; EX recomputes the exact branch result and either suppresses the duplicate redirect on a correct taken prediction or repairs loop exits by redirecting to pc+4. This should recover one bubble on CoreMark loop backedges with less control/routing cost than the failed BTFNT, BTB, loop-entry, and direct-JAL predictors because there is no table lookup and no 32-bit predicted-next-PC field carried through the pipe.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-bit predicted-taken flag to the ID/EX payload, avoiding full predicted-PC metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize valid backward conditional branches, compute the early pc+imm target with the existing immediate path, and emit a predicted redirect plus the one-bit ID/EX marker.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n20: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Retiming divider final fixup\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already moved DIV/REM out of the common ALU path, but\\nthe iterative divider still has a likely long registered path on its final\\niteration: restoring compare/subtract, quotient-bit insertion, optional\\nquotient or remainder negation, result muxing, and result_q capture all sit\\nin one cycle. CoreMark should execute DIV/REM rarely in the timed region, so\\npaying one extra divider-only cycle is a good trade if it raises FPGA Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already moved DIV/REM out of the common ALU path, but\\nthe iterative divider still has a likely long registered path on its final\\niteration: restoring compare/subtract, quotient-bit insertion, optional\\nquotient or remainder negation, result muxing, and result_q capture all sit\\nin one cycle. CoreMark should execute DIV/REM rarely in the timed region, so\\npaying one extra divider-only cycle is a good trade if it raises FPGA Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the divider's final sign-correction/result-selection work into a small\\nregistered fixup state. The 32 restoring iterations should only compute and\\nregister the unsigned quotient/remainder state; the following cycle should\\napply the signed quotient/remainder two's-complement correction and choose\\nthe architectural DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU result before entering DONE. Keep the\\ndivide-by-zero, signed-overflow, and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS fast paths as they\\nare today. This preserves the pipeline contract and common ALU throughput\\nwhile removing a second carry-chain-style negate from the divider's final\\niteration timing path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered final-fixup state for normal DIV/REM results so the last restoring step no longer also performs signed result correction.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/te... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Precompute PC targets in ID\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added front-end speculation, FIFOs, store buffering,\\nor same-cycle load bypassing regressed because the added control landed on already\\nsensitive paths. The last successful structural change was a pure pipeline-boundary\\nretime that removed a mux from writeback without changing CPI. After that, the EX\\nredirect path still recomputes pc+imm for every branch and direct jump in the same\\ncycle as branch comparison and PC redirection.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added front-end speculation, FIFOs, store buffering,\\nor same-cycle load bypassing regressed because the added control landed on already\\nsensitive paths. The last successful structural change was a pure pipeline-boundary\\nretime that removed a mux from writeback without changing CPI. After that, the EX\\nredirect path still recomputes pc+imm for every branch and direct jump in the same\\ncycle as branch comparison and PC redirection.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a registered PC-relative target field to the ID/EX payload, computed in ID as\\npc+imm using the existing decoded immediate. EX should use this registered value for\\nconditional branch targets, direct JAL targets, and AUIPC results, while keeping JALR\\ntarget calculation in EX because it depends on forwarded rs1. This does not predict\\nor redirect earlier, so branch penalty, RVFI pc_wdata, trap behavior, stalls, and the\\nnret=1 port contract stay unchanged. The expected win is a shorter EX-to-IF redirect\\ntiming path and less duplicate 32-bit adder/routing pressure, with only one pipeline\\npayload field added.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered pc_target field to id_ex_t for the decoded pc+imm value.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute pc+imm beside the... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n22: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Two-entry return predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Every prior conditional-branch, BTB, loop, and direct-JAL predictor for this\\ncore has regressed because it added IF-side target lookup, branch immediate\\naddition, broad prediction metadata, or repair muxing that cost more Fmax than\\nCoreMark recovered in branch bubbles. The remaining predictor class not yet\\ntried is return prediction: CoreMark still executes function calls and returns,\\nand canonical JALR returns are always-taken control-flow instructions whose\\ntarget can come from a tiny return-address stack instead of an IF-side table or\\ntarget adder.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every prior conditional-branch, BTB, loop, and direct-JAL predictor for this\\ncore has regressed because it added IF-side target lookup, branch immediate\\naddition, broad prediction metadata, or repair muxing that cost more Fmax than\\nCoreMark recovered in branch bubbles. The remaining predictor class not yet\\ntried is return prediction: CoreMark still executes function calls and returns,\\nand canonical JALR returns are always-taken control-flow instructions whose\\ntarget can come from a tiny return-address stack instead of an IF-side table or\\ntarget adder.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry return-address stack updated by resolved call and return\\ninstructions in EX, and consult it only in ID for canonical returns such as\\nJALR x0, 0(x1). When ID sees a valid unstalled return and the stack is\\nnon-empty, redirect the PC for the next fetch to the predicted return address\\nwhile allowing the return instruction itself to advance normally. EX carries the\\npredicted target, suppresses the normal JALR redirect only when the resolved\\ntarget matches, and repairs mismatches exactly like the current EX redirect.\\nThis targets always-taken return bubbles with much less front-end timing risk\\nthan BTFNT, BTB, loop, or direct-JAL prediction because the predicted target is\\na smal... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n23: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"One-hot forwarding selects\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already benefited from narrow datapath retiming,\\nespecially registering the final writeback payload in MEM/WB. The remaining\\nEX operand path still sends forwarding decisions through a binary 2-bit\\nencode in forward_unit and decodes those selects again in ex_stage before\\nevery ALU operation and branch compare. CoreMark's dependency-heavy integer\\ncode exercises this path frequently, so shortening it can improve Fmax\\nwithout changing instruction count or adding new control state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already benefited from narrow datapath retiming,\\nespecially registering the final writeback payload in MEM/WB. The remaining\\nEX operand path still sends forwarding decisions through a binary 2-bit\\nencode in forward_unit and decodes those selects again in ex_stage before\\nevery ALU operation and branch compare. CoreMark's dependency-heavy integer\\ncode exercises this path frequently, so shortening it can improve Fmax\\nwithout changing instruction count or adding new control state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the 2-bit forwarding select interface with direct one-hot priority\\nhit signals, or equivalently fold the forwarding priority mux directly into\\nEX. Keep the exact current priority of EX/MEM over MEM/WB over ID/EX, keep\\nEX/MEM loads excluded because their data is not ready, and keep MEM/WB\\nforwarding from the held MEM/WB payload during dmem stalls. Removing the\\nencode/decode layer should slightly shorten the rs1/rs2-to-ALU and\\nrs1/rs2-to-branch-compare cones with no new stalls, no RVFI-visible behavior\\nchange, and no extra predictor or memory-side timing risk.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Change forwarding outputs from 2-bit encoded selects to direct one-hot EX/MEM and MEM/WB hit si... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Register IF/ID fetch boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end has no real IF/ID register: io_imemData feeds the\\ndecoder, immediate generator, register-file read addresses, hazard predecode,\\nand then the ID/EX register in the same cycle. In the FPGA Fmax harness that\\npath is driven by a live LFSR input, so it is a plausible remaining timing\\ncone after the successful MEM/WB writeback retime. Prior structural attempts\\nthat added prediction, FIFOs, store buffers, or same-cycle load bypassing\\nregressed because they added control or long bypass paths; a plain fetch\\nboundary register is a narrower retime.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end has no real IF/ID register: io_imemData feeds the\\ndecoder, immediate generator, register-file read addresses, hazard predecode,\\nand then the ID/EX register in the same cycle. In the FPGA Fmax harness that\\npath is driven by a live LFSR input, so it is a plausible remaining timing\\ncone after the successful MEM/WB writeback retime. Prior structural attempts\\nthat added prediction, FIFOs, store buffers, or same-cycle load bypassing\\nregressed because they added control or long bypass paths; a plain fetch\\nboundary register is a narrower retime.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the IF output from a combinational {pc,instr,valid} bundle into a\\nregistered IF/ID payload that captures only when io_imemReady is high and ID\\ncan advance. Decode then runs from this local registered instruction instead\\nof directly from io_imemData, cutting the external imem-data-to-ID/EX timing\\npath and making the pipeline a conventional IF/ID/EX/MEM/WB structure. On EX\\nredirects the IF/ID register must be cleared and the PC redirected exactly as\\ntoday; on load-use, dmem, and DIV stalls it must hold without duplicating\\nretirements. This may add one cycle of fill/redirect latency, but the expected\\nFmax gain should outweigh the small CoreMark CPI cost if the fron... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"EX-local return target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they put opcode decode,\\ntarget adders, BTB/RAS lookup, or prediction-repair metadata on the front-end PC\\npath. The closest miss was the return predictor, which suggests return targets are\\nrelevant but the ID-to-IF early redirect machinery was too expensive. A predictor\\nthat stays local to EX can target Fmax by removing canonical-return JALR target\\ncalculation from the same-cycle redirect path without adding another fetch-stage\\npredictor.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they put opcode decode,\\ntarget adders, BTB/RAS lookup, or prediction-repair metadata on the front-end PC\\npath. The closest miss was the return predictor, which suggests return targets are\\nrelevant but the ID-to-IF early redirect machinery was too expensive. A predictor\\nthat stays local to EX can target Fmax by removing canonical-return JALR target\\ncalculation from the same-cycle redirect path without adding another fetch-stage\\npredictor.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny EX-local return target predictor for canonical returns. Track two\\nreturn addresses from resolved JAL/JALR call instructions in EX, and when EX sees a\\nvalid canonical return such as JALR x0, 0(x1), drive the existing redirect target\\nfrom the predicted return address rather than from the forwarded rs1+imm adder.\\nCompute the architectural JALR target in parallel only for RVFI pc_wdata, trap\\ndetection, and a registered next-cycle repair if the prediction was wrong or the\\ntarget was misaligned. Route non-return JALR instructions through the same delayed\\nredirect path so the forwarded rs1+imm adder no longer feeds the same-cycle PC\\nupdate mux; leave conditional branches and direct JALs unchanged. This avoids the\\nfailed IF/ID predictor pattern while potentially improving Fmax on the EX-to-IF\\nred... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n\n(Showing lines 1-25 of 46. Use offset=26 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-4123a25\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 282.82, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9563, \"ff\": 1866, \"seeds\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-12T20:27:54.639446Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Move DIV/REM off ALU critical path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current one-cycle ALU contains real SystemVerilog division and remainder\\noperators in the same EX-stage result cone as common integer operations.\\nThose operators are expensive on the Tang Nano 20K fabric and likely depress\\nFmax, while CoreMark's timed region should execute DIV/REM rarely or not at\\nall. Improving Fmax without touching the common ADD/branch/load/store path is\\ntherefore a promising fitness win.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current one-cycle ALU contains real SystemVerilog division and remainder\\noperators in the same EX-stage result cone as common integer operations.\\nThose operators are expensive on the Tang Nano 20K fabric and likely depress\\nFmax, while CoreMark's timed region should execute DIV/REM rarely or not at\\nall. Improving Fmax without touching the common ADD/branch/load/store path is\\ntherefore a promising fitness win.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational ALU into a small bounded\\niterative divider. The in-order pipeline should stall only when one of those\\nrare operations is in EX, then write the completed result through the existing\\nEX/MEM path. Keep MUL/MULH variants one-cycle and keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\\nsubstitutions combinational so formal remains tractable. The expected Fmax and\\nLUT improvement should outweigh the occasional divider latency penalty on\\nCoreMark.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real DIV/REM operators from the normal one-cycle ALU fast path while preserving ALTOPS substitutions for formal builds.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a bounded iterative RV32 divider/remainder unit with divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Start DIV/REM operations, hold the EX instruction while the divid... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV/REM to multicycle EX unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and\\nremainder logic in the single-cycle EX path. On a small FPGA this is likely\\na major LUT and timing cost even though CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by\\nadds, compares, loads, stores, branches, and multiplies rather than divides.\\nRetiming only DIV/REM into a small multicycle unit should raise median Fmax\\nand reduce routing pressure while preserving single-cycle behavior for the\\ncommon integer operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU contains combinational signed and unsigned division and\\nremainder logic in the single-cycle EX path. On a small FPGA this is likely\\na major LUT and timing cost even though CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by\\nadds, compares, loads, stores, branches, and multiplies rather than divides.\\nRetiming only DIV/REM into a small multicycle unit should raise median Fmax\\nand reduce routing pressure while preserving single-cycle behavior for the\\ncommon integer operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split RV32M division and remainder out of rtl/alu.sv into a dedicated\\niterative divider/remainder unit issued by EX. When EX sees DIV, DIVU, REM, or\\nREMU, it should hold IF/ID/EX, keep the operation's operands and RVFI payload\\nstable, and release one EX/MEM result only when the unit completes. MUL/MULH\\noperations remain in the existing combinational ALU so CoreMark's multiply\\nthroughput is not sacrificed. The added rare-operation stalls should cost few\\nCoreMark cycles, while the shorter common EX critical path should improve the\\nFmax side of the fitness metric.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an iterative RV32 DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU unit with RV32-defined divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remo... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Add BTFNT fetch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and only redirects when a branch or jump reaches EX, so every taken CoreMark loop branch pays a multi-cycle wrong-path bubble. CoreMark is loop-heavy, and the present bench history has no prior predictor attempts, making branch penalty reduction the cleanest predictor-side opportunity.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and only redirects when a branch or jump reaches EX, so every taken CoreMark loop branch pays a multi-cycle wrong-path bubble. CoreMark is loop-heavy, and the present bench history has no prior predictor attempts, making branch penalty reduction the cleanest predictor-side opportunity.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small static fetch predictor that predicts JAL and backward conditional branches taken, predicts forward conditional branches not taken, and carries the predicted next PC with the instruction into EX. EX should redirect only when the resolved next PC differs from the predicted next PC, eliminating the normal taken-backward-loop penalty while preserving exact RVFI pc_wdata, trap behavior, and the nret=1 port contract. This should improve CoreMark cycles with modest LUT/critical-path risk because it avoids tables and uses only IF-side opcode/offset decode plus one predicted-PC field in the pipeline bundles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted next-PC metadata to the IF/ID, ID/EX, and EX/MEM pipeline bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode fetched branch and JAL immediates in IF and select a BTFNT/JAL predicted next PC instead of always pc+4.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the predicted next PC from fetch into the ID/EX register across stalls and flushes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_st... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Share MUL hardware in ALU\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current post-divider core still computes three independent 64-bit\\nmultiply products in the combinational ALU: signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned,\\nand signed*unsigned. CoreMark keeps MUL single-cycle useful, but carrying\\nthree multiplier cones through the Tang Nano 20K fabric likely costs LUTs,\\nrouting, and Fmax even though only one MUL variant can retire at a time.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current post-divider core still computes three independent 64-bit\\nmultiply products in the combinational ALU: signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned,\\nand signed*unsigned. CoreMark keeps MUL single-cycle useful, but carrying\\nthree multiplier cones through the Tang Nano 20K fabric likely costs LUTs,\\nrouting, and Fmax even though only one MUL variant can retire at a time.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel multiplier products in rtl/alu.sv with one\\nsign-configurable 33x33 multiplier. Select operand sign extension from the\\nALU opcode, use the low 32 bits for MUL, and use product bits 63:32 for\\nMULH/MULHU/MULHSU. This should preserve all RV32M results and single-cycle\\nmultiply latency while shrinking the EX-stage arithmetic cone and improving\\nmedian FPGA Fmax without changing CoreMark instruction count.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three always-active multiply products with one opcode-controlled signed 33x33 product while leaving ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add or keep focused MULH/MULHU/MULHSU edge vectors so the shared multiplier sign-extension cases remain guarded.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three always-active multiply products with one opcode-controlled signed 33x33 product wh... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Share M-extension multiplier datapath\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The successful structural change already removed DIV/REM from the common EX\\ncritical path, leaving the ALU's parallel multiplier bank as a likely large\\ntiming and routing cost. The current ALU computes signed*signed,\\nunsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned 64-bit products in parallel even\\nthough only one MUL-family result can be selected for a retiring instruction.\\nCoreMark likely uses MUL often enough that making multiplication multicycle is\\nrisky, but sharing the single-cycle datapath should reduce LUT/DSP routing\\npressure without adding any stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The successful structural change already removed DIV/REM from the common EX\\ncritical path, leaving the ALU's parallel multiplier bank as a likely large\\ntiming and routing cost. The current ALU computes signed*signed,\\nunsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned 64-bit products in parallel even\\nthough only one MUL-family result can be selected for a retiring instruction.\\nCoreMark likely uses MUL often enough that making multiplication multicycle is\\nrisky, but sharing the single-cycle datapath should reduce LUT/DSP routing\\npressure without adding any stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU product trees in rtl/alu.sv\\nwith one sign-configurable 33x33 multiplier. Select the sign extension of each\\noperand from the ALU op, use the low 32 bits for MUL, and use product[63:32]\\nfor the high-half variants. Keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions and the\\nexisting DIV/REM handoff unchanged. This preserves the current five-stage\\nstructure and single-cycle MUL throughput while trimming a high-fanout EX-stage\\ndatapath, so any Fmax improvement should translate directly into better\\nCoreMark iter/sec.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Collapse... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Add tiny EX-updated BTB\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current core predicts every control-flow instruction as not taken and only redirects in EX, so hot CoreMark loop branches and direct jumps pay wrong-path bubbles repeatedly. The prior BTFNT predictor tried to remove those bubbles but added opcode decode, immediate extraction, and target adders to the IF/PC path, which likely consumed the cycle savings as Fmax loss.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core predicts every control-flow instruction as not taken and only redirects in EX, so hot CoreMark loop branches and direct jumps pay wrong-path bubbles repeatedly. The prior BTFNT predictor tried to remove those bubbles but added opcode decode, immediate extraction, and target adders to the IF/PC path, which likely consumed the cycle savings as Fmax loss.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small direct-mapped branch target buffer that is read in IF and updated only from resolved control-flow instructions in EX. Each entry stores valid, a compact PC tag, a resolved target, and a 2-bit taken counter; IF predicts taken only on tag hit with a taken counter and otherwise preserves the current sequential fetch behavior. This targets the same CoreMark loop-branch penalty as BTFNT while avoiding IF-side instruction decode and target addition, replacing them with a narrow tag compare and stored-target mux on the PC path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC and prediction-hit/taken metadata to the pipeline bundles so EX can distinguish correct predictions from mispredictions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Read a tiny direct-mapped BTB using the current PC and select the stored target only on a valid tag hit with a taken counter.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare resolved next PC against the carried prediction, re... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Source-aware load-use stalls\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current hazard unit stalls after every load when the following\\ninstruction's raw rs1 or rs2 bit fields match the load destination. That is\\nconservative but over-stalls instructions whose matching field is actually\\nan immediate, destination, or unused encoding field, such as OP-IMM, LOAD,\\nLUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK. CoreMark is load-heavy, so removing\\nthese false load-use bubbles can improve iterations per cycle without\\ntouching the already-sensitive fetch prediction or multiplier paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current hazard unit stalls after every load when the following\\ninstruction's raw rs1 or rs2 bit fields match the load destination. That is\\nconservative but over-stalls instructions whose matching field is actually\\nan immediate, destination, or unused encoding field, such as OP-IMM, LOAD,\\nLUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK. CoreMark is load-heavy, so removing\\nthese false load-use bubbles can improve iterations per cycle without\\ntouching the already-sensitive fetch prediction or multiplier paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Teach the load-use interlock which source operands the IF/ID instruction\\nreally consumes, then qualify the existing rd comparisons with use_rs1 and\\nuse_rs2. This keeps the mandatory one-cycle stall for true load consumers\\nsuch as OP, STORE, BRANCH, JALR, and LOAD base-address dependencies, while\\nallowing non-consuming or immediate-field matches to proceed. The change is\\na small decode-side micro-optimization: it should reduce CoreMark cycles and\\nadd only simple opcode checks off the critical IF PC path, preserving the\\nnret=1 RVFI contract and all trap semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add opcode-based use_rs1/use_rs2 qualification to the load-use hazard comparison so only true consumers st... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Add sequential prefetch FIFO\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end directly couples decode consumption to raw imemReady: every\\ninstruction-memory stall injects a bubble, and backend stalls also stop the PC\\neven when the instruction bus could have fetched useful sequential work. Prior\\npredictor attempts showed that adding target decode/BTB logic on the IF path can\\nlose Fmax, so the safer structural opportunity is to decouple fetch from decode\\nwithout predicting control flow.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end directly couples decode consumption to raw imemReady: every\\ninstruction-memory stall injects a bubble, and backend stalls also stop the PC\\neven when the instruction bus could have fetched useful sequential work. Prior\\npredictor attempts showed that adding target decode/BTB logic on the IF path can\\nlose Fmax, so the safer structural opportunity is to decouple fetch from decode\\nwithout predicting control flow.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny two-entry fall-through sequential prefetch FIFO in the IF stage that\\nstores fetched {pc,instr} pairs. When ID/EX is stalled by load-use, dmem, or DIV\\nbackpressure, the IF side should keep requesting sequential instructions while\\nFIFO space remains; when imemReady drops but the FIFO is non-empty, decode can\\ncontinue from the buffered head instead of taking an immediate bubble. On any\\nEX redirect, flush the FIFO and restart fetch at the resolved target, preserving\\nthe existing no-predictor control-flow semantics and exact RVFI pc/instruction\\nordering. This should improve CoreMark iterations/cycle under the 22 percent\\nrandomized iStall+dStall methodology while adding only small FIFO control muxes,\\nnot branch target adders or BTB lookup logic, to the front end.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the PC-only fetch path with a ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Pipeline lookahead BTB\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The core still fetches sequentially until control flow resolves in EX, so hot CoreMark loop branches and direct jumps pay wrong-path bubbles. Prior predictor attempts showed that the main risk is not correctness but Fmax: IF-side BTFNT decode/target adders and same-cycle BTB lookup logic lengthened the PC feedback path enough to erase cycle savings. The remaining promising predictor angle is to keep dynamic branch learning while moving lookup work off the PC critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The core still fetches sequentially until control flow resolves in EX, so hot CoreMark loop branches and direct jumps pay wrong-path bubbles. Prior predictor attempts showed that the main risk is not correctness but Fmax: IF-side BTFNT decode/target adders and same-cycle BTB lookup logic lengthened the PC feedback path enough to erase cycle savings. The remaining promising predictor angle is to keep dynamic branch learning while moving lookup work off the PC critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny direct-mapped lookahead BTB whose tag/counter/target lookup is performed for the next sequential PC and registered one cycle before that PC reaches IF. The PC update path should then choose between EX redirect, the already-registered prediction for the current PC, and pc+4; no same-cycle table read, tag compare, counter check, or target read feeds the PC register. EX updates the table on resolved conditional branches and direct JALs, carries the predicted next PC with each instruction, and redirects only when the resolved next PC differs from the carried prediction. This should recover repeated taken-loop and direct-jump bubbles while avoiding the Fmax loss seen in the prior IF-side predictors.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC and prediction-val... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Hazard-only source-use interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use detector stalls whenever a load destination matches either\\nraw source field in the following instruction, even when that field is actually\\nan immediate, destination, or otherwise unused encoding bit. CoreMark is\\nload-heavy, and the previous source-aware attempt failed from changing decoded\\nsource/RVFI fields rather than from the interlock idea itself.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use detector stalls whenever a load destination matches either\\nraw source field in the following instruction, even when that field is actually\\nan immediate, destination, or otherwise unused encoding bit. CoreMark is\\nload-heavy, and the previous source-aware attempt failed from changing decoded\\nsource/RVFI fields rather than from the interlock idea itself.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small hazard-only predecode for the IF/ID instruction's real source\\noperands and qualify the load-use rd comparisons with those bits. Keep the raw\\nrs1/rs2 addresses and values in the pipeline exactly as encoded so cosim and\\nRVFI continue to see the architectural instruction fields, while only the stall\\ndecision skips false dependencies for OP-IMM, LOAD, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and\\nEBREAK-style non-consuming forms. This should reduce unnecessary bubbles with\\nlittle timing risk because it touches only the existing hazard decision and adds\\nno predictor, FIFO, multiplier, or critical fetch-target machinery.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify load-use rd comparisons with hazard-only use_rs1/use_rs2 bits derived from the IF/ID opcode, keeping unknown source-consuming classes conservative.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass the IF/ID instruction or opcode into the hazard unit without changing ID/EX raw source fields, regfile reads, or... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Add conservative store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Prior structural and predictor attempts show that adding more IF-side control\\nhurts this core, while the benchmark still runs with randomized dmem stalls.\\nStores currently freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever dmemReady is low,\\neven though normal memory stores produce no register result and many following\\nALU/control instructions do not need the data port immediately.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior structural and predictor attempts show that adding more IF-side control\\nhurts this core, while the benchmark still runs with randomized dmem stalls.\\nStores currently freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever dmemReady is low,\\neven though normal memory stores produce no register result and many following\\nALU/control instructions do not need the data port immediately.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry store buffer in the MEM side that accepts aligned normal-memory\\nstores when dmemReady is low, retires the store through the existing MEM/WB and\\nRVFI path, and drains the buffered write on the dmem port as soon as the bus is\\nready. Keep all MMIO stores, including CoreMark start/stop markers and UART, on\\nthe current blocking path so externally observed timing is unchanged. While the\\nbuffer is full, stall younger loads and stores until it drains, but allow younger\\nnon-memory instructions to keep flowing. This targets CoreMark cycles lost to\\nrandom store backpressure without adding branch prediction, fetch FIFO, or other\\nfront-end critical-path logic.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry buffered normal-memory store path, drain muxing for dmem outputs, and MMIO/blocking-store bypass rules.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single dmem_stall condition with load/MMIO-store/buffer-full blocking while al... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Add one-entry loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The core still pays an EX-stage redirect bubble on every taken loop branch and direct jump.\\nPrior predictor attempts lost fitness because IF-side instruction decode, target adders,\\nBTB arrays, tag/counter lookup, or broad prediction metadata increased front-end timing\\ncost more than they saved in CoreMark cycles. A narrower predictor should target only the\\nhottest repeated taken control-flow PCs while keeping the PC path close to the current\\nsequential fetch design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The core still pays an EX-stage redirect bubble on every taken loop branch and direct jump.\\nPrior predictor attempts lost fitness because IF-side instruction decode, target adders,\\nBTB arrays, tag/counter lookup, or broad prediction metadata increased front-end timing\\ncost more than they saved in CoreMark cycles. A narrower predictor should target only the\\nhottest repeated taken control-flow PCs while keeping the PC path close to the current\\nsequential fetch design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry last-taken target predictor for conditional branches and direct JALs.\\nThe entry stores one full PC tag, one resolved target, and a tiny 2-bit confidence counter;\\nIF predicts taken only on an exact tag hit with the counter MSB set, otherwise it fetches\\npc+4 exactly as today. EX carries and compares the predicted next PC against the resolved\\nnext PC, redirecting only on mismatch, so correct hot-loop predictions avoid the normal\\ntaken-branch bubble while loop exits are repaired precisely. This avoids IF opcode decode,\\nimmediate generation, multi-entry BTB muxing, and lookahead-table plumbing, trading one\\ncomparator and one target mux for fewer CoreMark loop-control cycles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry predicted-next-PC validity and value from IF ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Case decode memory lanes\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM stage extracts load bytes with a variable right shift and\\nbuilds byte masks with variable left shifts. Even though the shift amount is\\nonly two address bits, FPGA synthesis can still leave a deeper mux/shifter\\ncone on the dmem read-data to MEM/WB path and on store mask generation.\\nCoreMark is load/store heavy, so trimming this path should improve Fmax\\nwithout adding control state or changing the instruction schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM stage extracts load bytes with a variable right shift and\\nbuilds byte masks with variable left shifts. Even though the shift amount is\\nonly two address bits, FPGA synthesis can still leave a deeper mux/shifter\\ncone on the dmem read-data to MEM/WB path and on store mask generation.\\nCoreMark is load/store heavy, so trimming this path should improve Fmax\\nwithout adding control state or changing the instruction schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the dynamic load-data shift and shifted byte-mask expressions in\\nrtl/mem_stage.sv with explicit case-decode lane muxes for addr[1:0] and\\naccess width. Use direct byte and halfword selection for LB/LBU/LH/LHU/LW,\\nand generate SB/SH/SW masks with small fixed cases while preserving the\\nexisting misalignment trap gating and RVFI aligned-memory reporting. This\\nshould reduce LUT depth and routing on the memory data path while keeping\\ncycle count, RVFI fields, and all memory-bound invariants unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace variable shifts for load extraction and byte-mask generation with explicit addr[1:0] case muxes while preserving misalignment traps and RVFI masks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace variable shift... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Add MEM-to-EX load bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The recent hazard-only source-use interlock removed false load-use stalls, but\\ntrue load-use pairs still always pay a bubble even when the data memory is\\nready. CoreMark is load-heavy, and the benchmark injects randomized dmem\\nstalls, so letting a dependent instruction wait in EX and consume the load as\\nsoon as the MEM-stage data is available can improve iterations per cycle\\nwithout adding the front-end predictor, FIFO, or store-buffer structures that\\nregressed in prior rounds.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The recent hazard-only source-use interlock removed false load-use stalls, but\\ntrue load-use pairs still always pay a bubble even when the data memory is\\nready. CoreMark is load-heavy, and the benchmark injects randomized dmem\\nstalls, so letting a dependent instruction wait in EX and consume the load as\\nsoon as the MEM-stage data is available can improve iterations per cycle\\nwithout adding the front-end predictor, FIFO, or store-buffer structures that\\nregressed in prior rounds.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a combinational MEM-to-EX forwarding source for aligned ready loads. The\\nMEM stage should expose the same sign/zero-extended load value it will commit\\ninto MEM/WB, and the forwarding unit should select that value for a younger\\ndependent instruction when EX/MEM holds a valid load whose dmem transaction is\\nready and non-trapping. The load-use interlock should then stall only when the\\nload result cannot be forwarded, such as dmemReady being low or the access\\nbeing misaligned; otherwise the dependent instruction advances into EX and\\nreceives the load value through the normal operand muxes. This preserves the\\nnret=1 RVFI retirement boundary and architectural rs fields while targeting\\none of the remaining CoreMark CPI costs.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"d... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Decode-stage JAL predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The previous predictor attempts all attacked conditional loop branches from the\\nfetch side and regressed because IF-side decode, BTB lookup, target muxing, or\\nbroad prediction metadata lengthened the front-end timing path more than they\\nrecovered in CoreMark cycles. The remaining predictor opportunity is to target\\nonly an always-taken control-flow class with much narrower hardware.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The previous predictor attempts all attacked conditional loop branches from the\\nfetch side and regressed because IF-side decode, BTB lookup, target muxing, or\\nbroad prediction metadata lengthened the front-end timing path more than they\\nrecovered in CoreMark cycles. The remaining predictor opportunity is to target\\nonly an always-taken control-flow class with much narrower hardware.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Predict direct JAL instructions as taken in decode, using the decoder and\\nimmediate generator that already exist in the ID stage, and redirect the PC one\\ncycle earlier than the current EX-stage jump resolution. Carry a minimal\\npredicted-next-PC bit/value with the JAL into EX so EX suppresses the duplicate\\nredirect when the predicted target matches, but still repairs any mismatch and\\nleaves JALR and conditional branches on the current conservative path. Gate the\\nearly redirect on valid, unstalled, aligned direct JALs so misaligned jump traps,\\nRVFI pc_wdata, and the nret=1 contract remain unchanged. This should save a\\nwrong-path bubble on calls and unconditional loop/control jumps without adding\\ntables, counters, branch comparators, or IF-side opcode/branch-immediate logic.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add minimal predicted-next-PC metadata to the ID/EX payload for decode-predicted direct JALs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Trim dead EX/MEM branch payload\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core appears sensitive to small timing and routing changes:\\npredictor, FIFO, store-buffer, and load-bypass attempts regressed, while the\\nsuccessful changes were narrow datapath or hazard micro-optimizations. The\\nEX/MEM bundle still carries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are\\nassigned in EX but never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, or redirect repair.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core appears sensitive to small timing and routing changes:\\npredictor, FIFO, store-buffer, and load-bypass attempts regressed, while the\\nsuccessful changes were narrow datapath or hazard micro-optimizations. The\\nEX/MEM bundle still carries branch_taken and branch_target fields that are\\nassigned in EX but never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, or redirect repair.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Remove the unused branch_taken and branch_target fields from the EX/MEM\\npipeline payload and delete their EX-stage assignments. Redirect correctness\\nalready comes from the same-cycle redirect/redirect_target outputs, and RVFI\\nonly needs pc_next at retirement, so these flops and their write-enable muxing\\nare dead state. Trimming them should slightly reduce FF count, fanout, and\\nEX/MEM routing pressure without changing instruction count, branch behavior,\\nmemory timing, or the nret=1 RVFI contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove unused branch_taken and branch_target fields from ex_mem_t while keeping pc_next for RVFI.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Delete assignments to the removed EX/MEM branch metadata in normal and DIV/REM completion paths.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Update comments/lint assumptions that mention unused branch metadata, without changing MEM behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\"... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Register writeback payload in MEM/WB\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added prediction, queues, store buffering, or\\nsame-cycle load bypassing all paid more timing/control cost than CoreMark\\nrecovered in CPI. The remaining safer structural opportunity is to shorten an\\nexisting high-fanout pipeline boundary without adding new stalls or state\\nmachines. Today the MEM/WB register carries both ALU and load data, then the\\nWB-stage mux drives regfile writeback, MEM/WB forwarding, and RVFI rd_wdata in\\nthe same cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent structural attempts that added prediction, queues, store buffering, or\\nsame-cycle load bypassing all paid more timing/control cost than CoreMark\\nrecovered in CPI. The remaining safer structural opportunity is to shorten an\\nexisting high-fanout pipeline boundary without adding new stalls or state\\nmachines. Today the MEM/WB register carries both ALU and load data, then the\\nWB-stage mux drives regfile writeback, MEM/WB forwarding, and RVFI rd_wdata in\\nthe same cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Fold the final writeback-data selection into the MEM/WB registered payload.\\nThe MEM stage already has the load result, ALU result, and mem_to_reg control\\nwhen it clocks the retirement bundle, so it can register a single rd_wdata\\nfield alongside the existing RVFI fields. The WB stage can then become a thin\\nvalid/reg_write/rd gate that forwards this registered payload directly to the\\nregfile and existing forwarding path. This preserves the five-stage schedule,\\nnret=1 RVFI contract, load-use behavior, and all trap/memory semantics while\\nremoving a 32-bit mux from the writeback-to-ID bypass and RVFI output cones.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered rd_wdata/writeback-result field to mem_wb_t so the retirement boundary carries the final re... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"ID backward branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still pays the EX-stage redirect penalty on hot taken loop branches, but every prior fetch-side predictor in this core lost more Fmax than it recovered in CPI. The remaining predictor opportunity is to target only high-frequency backward conditional branches while avoiding BTB arrays, counters, direct-JAL handling, and full predicted-PC metadata.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still pays the EX-stage redirect penalty on hot taken loop branches, but every prior fetch-side predictor in this core lost more Fmax than it recovered in CPI. The remaining predictor opportunity is to target only high-frequency backward conditional branches while avoiding BTB arrays, counters, direct-JAL handling, and full predicted-PC metadata.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a narrow decode-stage static predictor for valid backward conditional branches only. When ID sees a backward branch, redirect fetch to pc+imm one cycle before the current EX resolution point and carry only a single predicted-taken bit in ID/EX; EX recomputes the exact branch result and either suppresses the duplicate redirect on a correct taken prediction or repairs loop exits by redirecting to pc+4. This should recover one bubble on CoreMark loop backedges with less control/routing cost than the failed BTFNT, BTB, loop-entry, and direct-JAL predictors because there is no table lookup and no 32-bit predicted-next-PC field carried through the pipe.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-bit predicted-taken flag to the ID/EX payload, avoiding full predicted-PC metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize valid backward conditional branches, compute the early pc+imm target with the existing immediate path, and emit a predicted redirect plus the one-bit ID/EX marker.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Retiming divider final fixup\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already moved DIV/REM out of the common ALU path, but\\nthe iterative divider still has a likely long registered path on its final\\niteration: restoring compare/subtract, quotient-bit insertion, optional\\nquotient or remainder negation, result muxing, and result_q capture all sit\\nin one cycle. CoreMark should execute DIV/REM rarely in the timed region, so\\npaying one extra divider-only cycle is a good trade if it raises FPGA Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already moved DIV/REM out of the common ALU path, but\\nthe iterative divider still has a likely long registered path on its final\\niteration: restoring compare/subtract, quotient-bit insertion, optional\\nquotient or remainder negation, result muxing, and result_q capture all sit\\nin one cycle. CoreMark should execute DIV/REM rarely in the timed region, so\\npaying one extra divider-only cycle is a good trade if it raises FPGA Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the divider's final sign-correction/result-selection work into a small\\nregistered fixup state. The 32 restoring iterations should only compute and\\nregister the unsigned quotient/remainder state; the following cycle should\\napply the signed quotient/remainder two's-complement correction and choose\\nthe architectural DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU result before entering DONE. Keep the\\ndivide-by-zero, signed-overflow, and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS fast paths as they\\nare today. This preserves the pipeline contract and common ALU throughput\\nwhile removing a second carry-chain-style negate from the divider's final\\niteration timing path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered final-fixup state for normal DIV/REM results so the last restoring step no longer also performs signed result correction.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/te... (line truncated to 2000 chars)","truncated":true,"loaded":[]},"title":"bench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep3/log.jsonl","time":{"start":1778835273535,"end":1778835273544}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0a1adc6c8a66bead016a06df4969c4819aaf044988409d410c"}},"id":"prt_e2ad83737001i5joaEQzB1J1xA","sessionID":"ses_1d5292540ffepjDgtvUr54dZ4p","messageID":"msg_e2ad81851001ayJRBTrPq3dvFA"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778835273554,"sessionID":"ses_1d5292540ffepjDgtvUr54dZ4p","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_11Hr50vduvQNTpFkWbp7VMTX","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep3/log.jsonl","offset":1,"limit":20},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep3/log.jsonl</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\"id\": \"baseline-bench-5172724\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 282.82, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9563, \"ff\": 1866, \"seeds\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-13T13:13:53.650168Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\n2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Gate and share M-extension ALU hardware\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU unconditionally forms three independent 64-bit products for every instruction, even though CoreMark is dominated by RV32I arithmetic, branches, and memory traffic. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely burns LUTs and routing and may sit on or near the EX-stage critical path despite M operations being rare in the benchmark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU unconditionally forms three independent 64-bit products for every instruction, even though CoreMark is dominated by RV32I arithmetic, branches, and memory traffic. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely burns LUTs and routing and may sit on or near the EX-stage critical path despite M operations being rare in the benchmark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor rtl/alu.sv so MUL-family operations use a single op-selected signedness/extension path and only feed meaningful operands into the multiplier when the decoded ALU op is an M multiply. Keep the existing RV32M results and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS behavior unchanged, but make non-M ALU operations see simpler combinational logic and give yosys/nextpnr less multiplier-related muxing and routing to optimize through. This is a micro-optimization aimed primarily at improving Fmax and secondarily reducing LUT pressure without changing pipeline timing, RVFI behavior, or architectural state.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Share multiply operand preparation across MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU and gate multiplier inputs for non-multiply ALU ops while preserving exact RV32M and ALTOPS semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend or retain M-extension ALU vectors to guard signed, unsigned, and mixed-sign multiply corner cases after the multiplier datapath refactor.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"c... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move RV32M to multicycle side unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage contains full combinational RV32M multiply and divide logic inside the same ALU case as single-cycle integer operations. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely contributes a long critical path and high LUT pressure even though CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by simple integer, branch, and memory operations. Removing the divider and wide multiplier from the regular EX datapath should improve Fmax and may also improve placement by reducing EX fan-in.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage contains full combinational RV32M multiply and divide logic inside the same ALU case as single-cycle integer operations. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely contributes a long critical path and high LUT pressure even though CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by simple integer, branch, and memory operations. Removing the divider and wide multiplier from the regular EX datapath should improve Fmax and may also improve placement by reducing EX fan-in.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure the pipeline so RV32M operations dispatch from EX into a dedicated multicycle M unit while ordinary RV32I ALU, branch, jump, and address-generation operations remain single-cycle. When an M instruction reaches EX, hold IF/ID/EX behind it until the unit returns the result, then capture that result into EX/MEM and retire normally through the existing MEM/WB and RVFI path. This should preserve single-retire ordering and correctness while replacing the worst combinational arithmetic path with shorter iterative or staged logic; the added stalls should be infrequent enough in CoreMark to be outweighed by Fmax gains.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real multiply/divide/remainder operators from the normal combinational ALU path, keeping only simple RV32I operations and the ex... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Add tiny backward-branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves every conditional branch in EX and then\\nflushes two younger wrong-path instructions on taken branches. CoreMark has\\nmany small loop backedges, so the static not-taken policy wastes cycles on the\\nhottest control-flow pattern while also exposing random instruction stalls to\\nextra refetch work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves every conditional branch in EX and then\\nflushes two younger wrong-path instructions on taken branches. CoreMark has\\nmany small loop backedges, so the static not-taken policy wastes cycles on the\\nhottest control-flow pattern while also exposing random instruction stalls to\\nextra refetch work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a minimal static predictor in fetch/decode that predicts backward\\nconditional branches taken and redirects the PC to the decoded B-immediate\\ntarget before EX. Carry predicted-next-PC metadata through ID/EX so EX can\\ndistinguish correct predictions from mispredictions, only flushing on actual\\nmismatches. This should remove most taken-loop branch penalties with very low\\nLUT cost and little Fmax risk compared with a dynamic BHT/BTB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles with predicted PC and predicted-taken metadata for branch recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept an early decode redirect for backward branches and report the predicted next PC alongside the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Detect backward conditional branches from the fetched instruction, compute the B-immediate target, and pass prediction metadata into EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the predicted next PC against the resolved branch or jump next PC and redirec... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n5: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Suppress false load-use stalls\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current hazard unit stalls on any instruction following a load when its\\nrd matches bits [19:15] or [24:20] of the next instruction. Those bit fields\\nare not always live source operands: I-type ALU/load/JALR only reads rs1,\\nstores and branches read both, and U/J/FENCE/EBREAK read neither. False\\nload-use bubbles directly reduce CoreMark iterations/cycle and become more\\nexpensive under the random instruction/data stall model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current hazard unit stalls on any instruction following a load when its\\nrd matches bits [19:15] or [24:20] of the next instruction. Those bit fields\\nare not always live source operands: I-type ALU/load/JALR only reads rs1,\\nstores and branches read both, and U/J/FENCE/EBREAK read neither. False\\nload-use bubbles directly reduce CoreMark iterations/cycle and become more\\nexpensive under the random instruction/data stall model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add lightweight source-use qualification to the load-use hazard path so the\\npipeline only inserts the one-cycle bubble when the IF/ID instruction will\\nactually consume the matching source register. Derive rs1_used and rs2_used\\ncombinationally from opcode/funct class in the hazard unit or adjacent decode\\nwiring, preserving the existing forwarding, RVFI ordering, and memory-stall\\nbehavior. This is a small IPC-focused micro-optimization with low Fmax risk\\nbecause it removes unnecessary stalls without changing pipeline depth or the\\ncritical ALU/M-extension datapath.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify load-use detection with source-register usage so rd matches against immediate, U/J, FENCE, or EBREAK bitfields do not stall the pipeline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass the IF/ID instruction opcode context needed for rs1_used/r... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Bypass ready load data to EX\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline pays a mandatory one-cycle load-use bubble whenever the instruction immediately after a load consumes the loaded register. CoreMark's list and matrix kernels contain many pointer and array dependent loads, and the benchmark bus model often has dmem ready in the same cycle. Recovering those bubbles should improve iterations/cycle without changing the retirement width or adding the broad pipeline stalls that hurt the prior structural M-unit attempt.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline pays a mandatory one-cycle load-use bubble whenever the instruction immediately after a load consumes the loaded register. CoreMark's list and matrix kernels contain many pointer and array dependent loads, and the benchmark bus model often has dmem ready in the same cycle. Recovering those bubbles should improve iterations/cycle without changing the retirement width or adding the broad pipeline stalls that hurt the prior structural M-unit attempt.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a MEM-to-EX load-result bypass path for the specific case where the instruction in EX/MEM is a load, dmem is ready, and the younger ID/EX instruction uses that load's rd. The MEM stage already computes sign/zero-extended load_data combinationally before the MEM/WB register; expose that value and let the forwarding network select it for EX operands when dmem_ready is high. Keep the existing load-use interlock only for dmem stalls or cases where the load data is not yet ready, so correctness and RVFI ordering remain unchanged while ready loads avoid the one-cycle bubble.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"If needed, add a compact forwarding selector encoding for the new MEM-stage load-data bypass while preserving existing pipeline bundle semantics.\"}, {\"file\"... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n7: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Correct ID backward-branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core pays a two-instruction flush penalty on every taken conditional branch because branches resolve in EX and fetch is effectively static not-taken. CoreMark's inner loops should contain frequent backward taken branches, so recovering even part of that penalty can improve iterations per cycle without touching the already successful M-extension ALU optimization.\\n\\nThe previous predictor attempt identified the right low-cost direction but failed because EX compared against IF fall-through metadata rather than the branch's decoded target, so nominally correct backward predictions still looked wrong. A corrected metadata path is the most promising predictor-only follow-up.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core pays a two-instruction flush penalty on every taken conditional branch because branches resolve in EX and fetch is effectively static not-taken. CoreMark's inner loops should contain frequent backward taken branches, so recovering even part of that penalty can improve iterations per cycle without touching the already successful M-extension ALU optimization.\\n\\nThe previous predictor attempt identified the right low-cost direction but failed because EX compared against IF fall-through metadata rather than the branch's decoded target, so nominally correct backward predictions still looked wrong. A corrected metadata path is the most promising predictor-only follow-up.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a minimal static predictor that operates after decode: when ID sees a valid conditional branch with a negative B-immediate, redirect fetch to the decoded branch target and mark that branch with predicted_taken and predicted_pc_next in the ID/EX payload. EX then computes the resolved next PC for branches and jumps and redirects only when resolved_pc_next differs from the carried predicted_pc_next.\\n\\nThis keeps th... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n8: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Share RV32M divide datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a narrow ALU micro-optimization: sharing and gating the MUL-family datapath improved Tang Nano Fmax without adding pipeline stalls. The remaining RV32M divide/remainder logic still exposes separate signed and unsigned / and % operators in the combinational ALU case, which can leave yosys with multiple wide divider/remainder cones and extra muxing even though CoreMark mostly executes simpler RV32I operations.\\n\\nPrior attempts to move M-extension work into a multicycle side unit regressed because the added pipeline holds cost more than the Fmax gain. A same-cycle datapath sharing pass keeps the existing timing model and RVFI retirement behavior while attacking the remaining large arithmetic hardware in the common EX block.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a narrow ALU micro-optimization: sharing and gating the MUL-family datapath improved Tang Nano Fmax without adding pipeline stalls. The remaining RV32M divide/remainder logic still exposes separate signed and unsigned / and % operators in the combinational ALU case, which can leave yosys with multiple wide divider/remainder cones and extra muxing even though CoreMark mostly executes simpler RV32I operations.\\n\\nPrior attempts to move M-extension work into a multicycle side unit regressed because the added pipeline holds cost more than the Fmax gain. A same-cycle datapath sharing pass keeps the existing timing model and RVFI retirement behavior while attacking the remaining large arithmetic hardware in the common EX block.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor rtl/alu.sv so DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU use one shared operand-normalization path, one gated unsigned quotient/remainder datapath, and lightweight sign/zero correction for the four RV32M results. Force divider operands to harmless constants outside divide/remainder ops so non-M ALU operations do not c... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Register fetch-decode boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current core has no real IF/ID pipeline register: the fetched instruction and PC flow combinationally from the PC/imem side into decoder, immediate generation, regfile read addressing, hazard detection, and the ID/EX register. That likely creates a broad timing path through external instruction data and decode fanout, and prior predictor work showed that adding more fetch/decode combinational logic can severely hurt Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core has no real IF/ID pipeline register: the fetched instruction and PC flow combinationally from the PC/imem side into decoder, immediate generation, regfile read addressing, hazard detection, and the ID/EX register. That likely creates a broad timing path through external instruction data and decode fanout, and prior predictor work showed that adding more fetch/decode combinational logic can severely hurt Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the IF/ID boundary into a registered fetch-decode stage, optionally with a one-entry valid/skid behavior for stalls, so imem_data and pc are captured before decode. Preserve the existing single-retire RVFI contract and branch/load-use/dmem stall semantics by flushing or holding the registered IF/ID payload using the same hazard priorities. This should shorten the highest-fanout front-end timing path and improve FPGA Fmax with only a small control change and no new load-data or RV32M critical path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep or minimally adjust the IF/ID bundle so it can represent a registered valid payload and bubble without changing RVFI-visible pipeline metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an IF/ID register or one-entry fetch buffer that captures pc, instruction, and valid, with redirect and flush taking priority over ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Add tiny fetch BTB predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current core still resolves all branches and jumps in EX, so every taken\\ncontrol transfer pays a two-younger-instruction flush. Prior predictor\\nattempts showed that decode-time backward prediction is risky on this FPGA\\nbecause the imem_data-to-decode-to-target-add path can become part of the\\nfetch critical path. A fetch-PC-indexed BTB avoids that failed path while\\nstill targeting CoreMark's hot loop backedges and repeated call/return-like\\nJAL/JALR targets.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core still resolves all branches and jumps in EX, so every taken\\ncontrol transfer pays a two-younger-instruction flush. Prior predictor\\nattempts showed that decode-time backward prediction is risky on this FPGA\\nbecause the imem_data-to-decode-to-target-add path can become part of the\\nfetch critical path. A fetch-PC-indexed BTB avoids that failed path while\\nstill targeting CoreMark's hot loop backedges and repeated call/return-like\\nJAL/JALR targets.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small direct-mapped BTB in the fetch stage, indexed only by the\\nregistered PC and updated from EX when a branch or jump resolves. Each entry\\nstores valid, tag, target, and a one-bit or two-bit taken confidence bit;\\nfetch predicts to the stored target only on a tag hit, otherwise it keeps the\\nexisting pc+4 behavior. Carry predicted_pc_next through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX\\nredirects only when the resolved next PC differs from the prediction, and\\nupdate or allocate entries for taken branches/JAL/JALR while weakening or\\nclearing entries on not-taken branches.\\n\\nThis is the most promising predictor-only follow-up because it removes the\\nknown ID predictor critical path, learns actual targets instead of relying on\\na static backward heuristic, and should recover taken-control IPC on stable\\nCoreMark loops with modest LUT/FF cost.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_p... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n11: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Remove regfile reset fanout\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current register file synchronously resets all 32 architectural registers, which forces reset muxing and routing across 1024 state bits. On a small FPGA this can prevent efficient distributed-RAM inference and add reset fanout to a block that is read on the decode critical path.\\n\\nRV32I only requires x0 to read as zero; the other architectural registers do not need a defined hardware reset value as long as software initializes the registers it uses and RVFI reports the actual values read. Removing this reset is a narrow micro-optimization aimed at improving Fmax and possibly LUT pressure without changing pipeline depth, stalls, forwarding, memory behavior, or retirement width.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current register file synchronously resets all 32 architectural registers, which forces reset muxing and routing across 1024 state bits. On a small FPGA this can prevent efficient distributed-RAM inference and add reset fanout to a block that is read on the decode critical path.\\n\\nRV32I only requires x0 to read as zero; the other architectural registers do not need a defined hardware reset value as long as software initializes the registers it uses and RVFI reports the actual values read. Removing this reset is a narrow micro-optimization aimed at improving Fmax and possibly LUT pressure without changing pipeline depth, stalls, forwarding, memory behavior, or retirement width.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor rtl/reg_file.sv so reset no longer clears regs[1:31]. Keep x0 hardwired through the existing read and write gating, preserve write-first same-cycle bypass behavior, and leave the write port synchronous.\\n\\nThis should let yosys/nextpnr implement the 32x32 two-read/one-write file with less reset-related muxing and routing, potentially improving the decode/regfile-read timing path and reducing LUT/FF pressure. Because the change only affects ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Add RAM store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes all younger work whenever a store in EX/MEM sees\\nio_dmemReady low, even though stores do not produce a register result for the\\nfollowing instructions. Under the benchmark's random dmem backpressure model,\\nCoreMark can lose cycles waiting on regular RAM stores that could instead be\\nqueued and drained in order while independent ALU/control work continues.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that changed the M datapath, load-use timing, or the\\nfetch/decode boundary regressed because they lengthened hot combinational paths\\nor inserted broad stalls. A one-entry store buffer is a narrower structural\\nchange: it targets store-only dmem stalls, keeps loads conservative, and avoids\\nnew ALU or branch critical-path logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes all younger work whenever a store in EX/MEM sees\\nio_dmemReady low, even though stores do not produce a register result for the\\nfollowing instructions. Under the benchmark's random dmem backpressure model,\\nCoreMark can lose cycles waiting on regular RAM stores that could instead be\\nqueued and drained in order while independent ALU/control work continues.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that changed the M datapath, load-use timing, or the\\nfetch/decode boundary regressed because they lengthened hot combinational paths\\nor inserted broad stalls. A one-entry store buffer is a narrower structural\\nchange: it targets store-only dmem stalls, keeps loads conservative, and avoids\\nnew ALU or branch critical-path logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry in-order store buffer for normal RAM stores only. When a\\nnon-MMIO store reaches MEM and the buffer is empty, capture its address, data,\\nmask, and RVFI memory metadata, allow the store instruction to retire, and let\\nthe buffer continue driving dmem_wen until io_dmemReady accepts it. Stall on\\nloads, MMIO stores, misali... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n13: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Predict JAL in decode\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current core resolves every taken control transfer in EX, so unconditional\\nJAL instructions pay the same two-younger-instruction flush penalty as taken\\nconditional branches. Prior predictor attempts showed that conditional\\nbackward prediction lengthened the imem/decode/target path and that mutable\\nBTB arrays complicated formal convergence, so the next predictor experiment\\nshould target a smaller, deterministic subset of control flow.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core resolves every taken control transfer in EX, so unconditional\\nJAL instructions pay the same two-younger-instruction flush penalty as taken\\nconditional branches. Prior predictor attempts showed that conditional\\nbackward prediction lengthened the imem/decode/target path and that mutable\\nBTB arrays complicated formal convergence, so the next predictor experiment\\nshould target a smaller, deterministic subset of control flow.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a static early predictor only for valid JAL instructions in the decode\\nstage. JAL is always taken and its target is encoded in the instruction, so ID\\ncan compute pc+J-immediate and redirect fetch one cycle earlier without any\\ndynamic predictor state, branch comparison, or BTB tag lookup. Carry a\\npredicted_pc_next bit/value through ID/EX and EX/MEM so EX suppresses the\\nnormal redirect when the resolved JAL target matches the decode prediction,\\nwhile branches and JALR keep the existing EX-resolution behavior.\\n\\nThis should recover some CoreMark IPC on function-call and unconditional-jump\\npaths while avoiding the two known predictor hazards: conditional decode\\nprediction on every branch and mutable fetch-side predictor arrays. The change\\nis intentionally narrow and should be easier for riscv-formal than a BTB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"des... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n14: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Gate EX control-transfer datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage computes branch comparisons, branch targets, and JALR target sums for every instruction, even though CoreMark's hot path is dominated by ordinary ALU and memory operations. Prior results show that narrow gating of always-live datapaths can improve Tang Nano Fmax, while larger structural or predictor changes have repeatedly lost more timing than they recovered in IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage computes branch comparisons, branch targets, and JALR target sums for every instruction, even though CoreMark's hot path is dominated by ordinary ALU and memory operations. Prior results show that narrow gating of always-live datapaths can improve Tang Nano Fmax, while larger structural or predictor changes have repeatedly lost more timing than they recovered in IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor rtl/ex_stage.sv so control-transfer hardware is only active for actual branch or jump instructions. Gate the branch comparator operands when is_branch is false, compute only the selected branch comparison for the decoded branch_op, and feed harmless constants into the branch-target and JALR-target adders when their instruction class is not active. Preserve the existing EX-stage resolution point, misalignment trap behavior, RVFI pc_next reporting, and redirect semantics.\\n\\nThis should reduce EX-stage LUT/routing pressure and remove always-live comparator/adder cones from common RV32I ALU and load/store cycles without adding state, stalls, or front-end prediction metadata. The expected gain is primarily Fmax, with essentially no IPC downside because pipeline timing and retirement behavior remain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate branch comparator inputs and control-target adders so non-control instructions do not k... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Precompute PC targets in decode\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage resolves branches and jumps while also running the ALU,\\nforwarding muxes, branch comparators, and RVFI next-PC construction. Prior\\nstructural attempts that added pipeline latency, broad stalls, or early\\nfront-end redirects regressed, so the next structural experiment should be a\\npure retiming that shortens EX without changing prediction policy or IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage resolves branches and jumps while also running the ALU,\\nforwarding muxes, branch comparators, and RVFI next-PC construction. Prior\\nstructural attempts that added pipeline latency, broad stalls, or early\\nfront-end redirects regressed, so the next structural experiment should be a\\npure retiming that shortens EX without changing prediction policy or IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute PC+4 and the PC-relative target PC+imm in ID and store them in the\\nexisting ID/EX pipeline register. EX can then use the precomputed PC+imm for\\nconditional branch targets, JAL targets, and AUIPC results, and use the\\nprecomputed PC+4 for fall-through, JAL/JALR link data, and RVFI pc_wdata.\\n\\nThis removes duplicated 32-bit PC adders from the EX control path and lowers\\nfanout around branch/jump resolution while preserving the same five-stage\\ntiming, load-use behavior, RVFI order, and single-channel nret=1 contract.\\nUnlike the failed decode-prediction attempts, the ID-side target add feeds\\nonly a register and never drives the fetch PC in the same cycle, so it should\\navoid creating a new imem_data-to-PC critical loop.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend id_ex_t with registered pc_plus4 and pc_imm_target fields used by EX for branch, JAL, AUIPC, and RVFI next-PC construction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute pc+4 and pc+imm f... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n16: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"One-entry JALR return predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The existing core resolves JALR in EX, so every function return pays the same two-younger-instruction flush as a taken branch. Prior predictor attempts lost fitness or broke formal because they added decode-time target generation on the imem critical path or introduced a general mutable BTB array. A much narrower predictor can target repeated return PCs without touching conditional branches or adding a broad fetch/decode target path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The existing core resolves JALR in EX, so every function return pays the same two-younger-instruction flush as a taken branch. Prior predictor attempts lost fitness or broke formal because they added decode-time target generation on the imem critical path or introduced a general mutable BTB array. A much narrower predictor can target repeated return PCs without touching conditional branches or adding a broad fetch/decode target path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single scalar JALR target cache in fetch, updated only when EX resolves a legal JALR. The cache stores one valid bit, one PC tag, and one resolved target; when the current fetch PC matches the tag, IF predicts the cached target as the next PC and carries predicted_pc_next metadata through ID/EX. EX compares the resolved next PC against the carried prediction and redirects only on mismatch, while all branches, JAL, and cache misses keep the current pc+4/static-not-taken behavior.\\n\\nThis is deliberately smaller than the failed BTB attempt: no predictor array, no counters, no conditional-branch update policy, and no imem_data-to-immediate-to-PC combinational redirect. It should recover recurring CoreMark return penalties after warm-up with limited LUT/FF cost and lower formal risk than a general fetch BTB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add pre... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n17: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Gate divider operands only\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best bench core already benefited strongly from isolating the MUL-family datapath from ordinary RV32I ALU operations. The remaining DIV/REM operators still consume the live ALU operands directly, so yosys may keep wide divider/remainder cones and routing coupled to the EX-stage result mux even when CoreMark is mostly executing adds, compares, loads, stores, and branches.\\n\\nA prior attempt to share the divider datapath regressed because it introduced signed normalization, restore muxing, and simultaneous quotient/remainder logic. The safer micro-optimization is to avoid that structural rewrite and only gate the raw divider operands outside actual DIV/REM operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best bench core already benefited strongly from isolating the MUL-family datapath from ordinary RV32I ALU operations. The remaining DIV/REM operators still consume the live ALU operands directly, so yosys may keep wide divider/remainder cones and routing coupled to the EX-stage result mux even when CoreMark is mostly executing adds, compares, loads, stores, and branches.\\n\\nA prior attempt to share the divider datapath regressed because it introduced signed normalization, restore muxing, and simultaneous quotient/remainder logic. The safer micro-optimization is to avoid that structural rewrite and only gate the raw divider operands outside actual DIV/REM operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add an is_divrem_op decode inside rtl/alu.sv and feed the real a/b operands to division and remainder operators only for ALU_DIV, ALU_DIVU, ALU_REM, and ALU_REMU. For all other ALU ops, drive the divider operand wires with harmless constants, then use those gated wires in the existing RV32M divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 cases.\\n\\nThis keeps the pipeline single-cycle, preserves RV32M and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS semantics, and avoids the failed normalization/shari... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Add fetch skid buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The benchmark model injects independent instruction and data bus stalls, and the current pipeline freezes fetch whenever a data-memory operation is held in EX/MEM. If instruction memory is ready during that backend hold, the core wastes an opportunity to capture the next sequential instruction and can then immediately lose more cycles to a later imem stall after dmem unblocks.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that always inserted an IF/ID register or added store-buffer control regressed, so this hypothesis keeps the normal zero-wait path combinational and only adds a one-entry skid slot for cycles where the backend is already unable to advance.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The benchmark model injects independent instruction and data bus stalls, and the current pipeline freezes fetch whenever a data-memory operation is held in EX/MEM. If instruction memory is ready during that backend hold, the core wastes an opportunity to capture the next sequential instruction and can then immediately lose more cycles to a later imem stall after dmem unblocks.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that always inserted an IF/ID register or added store-buffer control regressed, so this hypothesis keeps the normal zero-wait path combinational and only adds a one-entry skid slot for cycles where the backend is already unable to advance.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry fetch skid buffer in the IF stage that captures the current imem instruction and PC when dmem_stall holds ID/EX and EX/MEM but imemReady is high. When the backend releases, feed the buffered instruction to ID before requesting/advancing to the next PC; invalidate the buffer on any EX redirect so wrong-path contents never retire.\\n\\nThis should improve CoreMark iterations/cycle under the random stall model by overlapping useful instruction fetch with data-memory wait cycles, without changing retirement width, branch resol... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n19: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Add loop-target prefetch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The existing core still pays a taken-control penalty, but prior predictor\\nattempts show that decode-time redirects and mutable BTB-style PC metadata\\ncan lose more Fmax or formal convergence than they recover in IPC. CoreMark\\nnevertheless has tight repeated loops, and the random imem backpressure model\\nmakes avoiding wrong-path fetch work valuable if the predictor can stay off\\nthe critical PC-update path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The existing core still pays a taken-control penalty, but prior predictor\\nattempts show that decode-time redirects and mutable BTB-style PC metadata\\ncan lose more Fmax or formal convergence than they recover in IPC. CoreMark\\nnevertheless has tight repeated loops, and the random imem backpressure model\\nmakes avoiding wrong-path fetch work valuable if the predictor can stay off\\nthe critical PC-update path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny one-entry loop-target prefetch predictor that learns only resolved\\ntaken backward conditional branches in EX, storing the branch PC and target in\\nflops. Fetch keeps the architecturally visible PC policy unchanged until a\\nregistered PC tag hit is observed, then uses the stored target as the next PC\\nand carries a predicted_pc_next value with that fetched instruction so EX can\\nsuppress the normal redirect only when the resolved next PC exactly matches.\\n\\nThis is deliberately narrower than the failed BTB and decode predictors: no\\npredictor array, no instruction-decode-to-PC immediate path, no JAL/JALR\\ntarget speculation, and no change to branch condition resolution. It should\\nrecover the hottest loop backedge penalties after warm-up while limiting Fmax\\nrisk to a single PC comparator and target mux driven by registered predictor\\nstate.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n20: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Trim dead pipeline metadata\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already benefited from narrow datapath cleanup, while later attempts to add gating, prediction, buffering, or divider rewrites mostly lost Fmax or broke formal. There are still dead bits carried through hot pipeline bundles: ctrl.is_lui is decoded but never consumed, and EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target are written but not read by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. On a small FPGA these unused flops and their assignment cones can still consume routing, reset/load muxing, and placement freedom.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already benefited from narrow datapath cleanup, while later attempts to add gating, prediction, buffering, or divider rewrites mostly lost Fmax or broke formal. There are still dead bits carried through hot pipeline bundles: ctrl.is_lui is decoded but never consumed, and EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target are written but not read by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. On a small FPGA these unused flops and their assignment cones can still consume routing, reset/load muxing, and placement freedom.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Remove unused pipeline metadata instead of adding new behavior. Delete ctrl_t.is_lui and its decoder/id_stage assignments, and delete ex_mem_t.branch_taken and ex_mem_t.branch_target plus the corresponding ex_stage register writes. LUI behavior is already represented by ALU_LUI with alu_src/reg_write, branch redirects use the combinational EX outputs, and RVFI pc_next uses the resolved value already stored in EX/MEM, so these fields should not affect architectural state or retirement.\\n\\nThis is a pure micro-optimization: fewer registered bits, less control fanout, and less unnecessary mux/load/reset logic in the ID/EX and EX/MEM registers. The expected gain is modest but safer than another arithmetic or predictor change because it does not change pipelin... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n\n(Showing lines 1-20 of 46. Use offset=21 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-5172724\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 282.82, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9563, \"ff\": 1866, \"seeds\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-13T13:13:53.650168Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Gate and share M-extension ALU hardware\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU unconditionally forms three independent 64-bit products for every instruction, even though CoreMark is dominated by RV32I arithmetic, branches, and memory traffic. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely burns LUTs and routing and may sit on or near the EX-stage critical path despite M operations being rare in the benchmark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU unconditionally forms three independent 64-bit products for every instruction, even though CoreMark is dominated by RV32I arithmetic, branches, and memory traffic. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely burns LUTs and routing and may sit on or near the EX-stage critical path despite M operations being rare in the benchmark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor rtl/alu.sv so MUL-family operations use a single op-selected signedness/extension path and only feed meaningful operands into the multiplier when the decoded ALU op is an M multiply. Keep the existing RV32M results and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS behavior unchanged, but make non-M ALU operations see simpler combinational logic and give yosys/nextpnr less multiplier-related muxing and routing to optimize through. This is a micro-optimization aimed primarily at improving Fmax and secondarily reducing LUT pressure without changing pipeline timing, RVFI behavior, or architectural state.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Share multiply operand preparation across MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU and gate multiplier inputs for non-multiply ALU ops while preserving exact RV32M and ALTOPS semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend or retain M-extension ALU vectors to guard signed, unsigned, and mixed-sign multiply corner cases after the multiplier datapath refactor.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"c... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move RV32M to multicycle side unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage contains full combinational RV32M multiply and divide logic inside the same ALU case as single-cycle integer operations. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely contributes a long critical path and high LUT pressure even though CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by simple integer, branch, and memory operations. Removing the divider and wide multiplier from the regular EX datapath should improve Fmax and may also improve placement by reducing EX fan-in.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage contains full combinational RV32M multiply and divide logic inside the same ALU case as single-cycle integer operations. On the Tang Nano 20K this likely contributes a long critical path and high LUT pressure even though CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by simple integer, branch, and memory operations. Removing the divider and wide multiplier from the regular EX datapath should improve Fmax and may also improve placement by reducing EX fan-in.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure the pipeline so RV32M operations dispatch from EX into a dedicated multicycle M unit while ordinary RV32I ALU, branch, jump, and address-generation operations remain single-cycle. When an M instruction reaches EX, hold IF/ID/EX behind it until the unit returns the result, then capture that result into EX/MEM and retire normally through the existing MEM/WB and RVFI path. This should preserve single-retire ordering and correctness while replacing the worst combinational arithmetic path with shorter iterative or staged logic; the added stalls should be infrequent enough in CoreMark to be outweighed by Fmax gains.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real multiply/divide/remainder operators from the normal combinational ALU path, keeping only simple RV32I operations and the ex... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Add tiny backward-branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves every conditional branch in EX and then\\nflushes two younger wrong-path instructions on taken branches. CoreMark has\\nmany small loop backedges, so the static not-taken policy wastes cycles on the\\nhottest control-flow pattern while also exposing random instruction stalls to\\nextra refetch work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves every conditional branch in EX and then\\nflushes two younger wrong-path instructions on taken branches. CoreMark has\\nmany small loop backedges, so the static not-taken policy wastes cycles on the\\nhottest control-flow pattern while also exposing random instruction stalls to\\nextra refetch work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a minimal static predictor in fetch/decode that predicts backward\\nconditional branches taken and redirects the PC to the decoded B-immediate\\ntarget before EX. Carry predicted-next-PC metadata through ID/EX so EX can\\ndistinguish correct predictions from mispredictions, only flushing on actual\\nmismatches. This should remove most taken-loop branch penalties with very low\\nLUT cost and little Fmax risk compared with a dynamic BHT/BTB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles with predicted PC and predicted-taken metadata for branch recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept an early decode redirect for backward branches and report the predicted next PC alongside the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Detect backward conditional branches from the fetched instruction, compute the B-immediate target, and pass prediction metadata into EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the predicted next PC against the resolved branch or jump next PC and redirec... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Suppress false load-use stalls\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current hazard unit stalls on any instruction following a load when its\\nrd matches bits [19:15] or [24:20] of the next instruction. Those bit fields\\nare not always live source operands: I-type ALU/load/JALR only reads rs1,\\nstores and branches read both, and U/J/FENCE/EBREAK read neither. False\\nload-use bubbles directly reduce CoreMark iterations/cycle and become more\\nexpensive under the random instruction/data stall model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current hazard unit stalls on any instruction following a load when its\\nrd matches bits [19:15] or [24:20] of the next instruction. Those bit fields\\nare not always live source operands: I-type ALU/load/JALR only reads rs1,\\nstores and branches read both, and U/J/FENCE/EBREAK read neither. False\\nload-use bubbles directly reduce CoreMark iterations/cycle and become more\\nexpensive under the random instruction/data stall model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add lightweight source-use qualification to the load-use hazard path so the\\npipeline only inserts the one-cycle bubble when the IF/ID instruction will\\nactually consume the matching source register. Derive rs1_used and rs2_used\\ncombinationally from opcode/funct class in the hazard unit or adjacent decode\\nwiring, preserving the existing forwarding, RVFI ordering, and memory-stall\\nbehavior. This is a small IPC-focused micro-optimization with low Fmax risk\\nbecause it removes unnecessary stalls without changing pipeline depth or the\\ncritical ALU/M-extension datapath.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify load-use detection with source-register usage so rd matches against immediate, U/J, FENCE, or EBREAK bitfields do not stall the pipeline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass the IF/ID instruction opcode context needed for rs1_used/r... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Bypass ready load data to EX\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline pays a mandatory one-cycle load-use bubble whenever the instruction immediately after a load consumes the loaded register. CoreMark's list and matrix kernels contain many pointer and array dependent loads, and the benchmark bus model often has dmem ready in the same cycle. Recovering those bubbles should improve iterations/cycle without changing the retirement width or adding the broad pipeline stalls that hurt the prior structural M-unit attempt.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline pays a mandatory one-cycle load-use bubble whenever the instruction immediately after a load consumes the loaded register. CoreMark's list and matrix kernels contain many pointer and array dependent loads, and the benchmark bus model often has dmem ready in the same cycle. Recovering those bubbles should improve iterations/cycle without changing the retirement width or adding the broad pipeline stalls that hurt the prior structural M-unit attempt.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a MEM-to-EX load-result bypass path for the specific case where the instruction in EX/MEM is a load, dmem is ready, and the younger ID/EX instruction uses that load's rd. The MEM stage already computes sign/zero-extended load_data combinationally before the MEM/WB register; expose that value and let the forwarding network select it for EX operands when dmem_ready is high. Keep the existing load-use interlock only for dmem stalls or cases where the load data is not yet ready, so correctness and RVFI ordering remain unchanged while ready loads avoid the one-cycle bubble.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"If needed, add a compact forwarding selector encoding for the new MEM-stage load-data bypass while preserving existing pipeline bundle semantics.\"}, {\"file\"... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Correct ID backward-branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core pays a two-instruction flush penalty on every taken conditional branch because branches resolve in EX and fetch is effectively static not-taken. CoreMark's inner loops should contain frequent backward taken branches, so recovering even part of that penalty can improve iterations per cycle without touching the already successful M-extension ALU optimization.\\n\\nThe previous predictor attempt identified the right low-cost direction but failed because EX compared against IF fall-through metadata rather than the branch's decoded target, so nominally correct backward predictions still looked wrong. A corrected metadata path is the most promising predictor-only follow-up.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core pays a two-instruction flush penalty on every taken conditional branch because branches resolve in EX and fetch is effectively static not-taken. CoreMark's inner loops should contain frequent backward taken branches, so recovering even part of that penalty can improve iterations per cycle without touching the already successful M-extension ALU optimization.\\n\\nThe previous predictor attempt identified the right low-cost direction but failed because EX compared against IF fall-through metadata rather than the branch's decoded target, so nominally correct backward predictions still looked wrong. A corrected metadata path is the most promising predictor-only follow-up.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a minimal static predictor that operates after decode: when ID sees a valid conditional branch with a negative B-immediate, redirect fetch to the decoded branch target and mark that branch with predicted_taken and predicted_pc_next in the ID/EX payload. EX then computes the resolved next PC for branches and jumps and redirects only when resolved_pc_next differs from the carried predicted_pc_next.\\n\\nThis keeps th... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Share RV32M divide datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a narrow ALU micro-optimization: sharing and gating the MUL-family datapath improved Tang Nano Fmax without adding pipeline stalls. The remaining RV32M divide/remainder logic still exposes separate signed and unsigned / and % operators in the combinational ALU case, which can leave yosys with multiple wide divider/remainder cones and extra muxing even though CoreMark mostly executes simpler RV32I operations.\\n\\nPrior attempts to move M-extension work into a multicycle side unit regressed because the added pipeline holds cost more than the Fmax gain. A same-cycle datapath sharing pass keeps the existing timing model and RVFI retirement behavior while attacking the remaining large arithmetic hardware in the common EX block.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a narrow ALU micro-optimization: sharing and gating the MUL-family datapath improved Tang Nano Fmax without adding pipeline stalls. The remaining RV32M divide/remainder logic still exposes separate signed and unsigned / and % operators in the combinational ALU case, which can leave yosys with multiple wide divider/remainder cones and extra muxing even though CoreMark mostly executes simpler RV32I operations.\\n\\nPrior attempts to move M-extension work into a multicycle side unit regressed because the added pipeline holds cost more than the Fmax gain. A same-cycle datapath sharing pass keeps the existing timing model and RVFI retirement behavior while attacking the remaining large arithmetic hardware in the common EX block.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor rtl/alu.sv so DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU use one shared operand-normalization path, one gated unsigned quotient/remainder datapath, and lightweight sign/zero correction for the four RV32M results. Force divider operands to harmless constants outside divide/remainder ops so non-M ALU operations do not c... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Register fetch-decode boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current core has no real IF/ID pipeline register: the fetched instruction and PC flow combinationally from the PC/imem side into decoder, immediate generation, regfile read addressing, hazard detection, and the ID/EX register. That likely creates a broad timing path through external instruction data and decode fanout, and prior predictor work showed that adding more fetch/decode combinational logic can severely hurt Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core has no real IF/ID pipeline register: the fetched instruction and PC flow combinationally from the PC/imem side into decoder, immediate generation, regfile read addressing, hazard detection, and the ID/EX register. That likely creates a broad timing path through external instruction data and decode fanout, and prior predictor work showed that adding more fetch/decode combinational logic can severely hurt Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the IF/ID boundary into a registered fetch-decode stage, optionally with a one-entry valid/skid behavior for stalls, so imem_data and pc are captured before decode. Preserve the existing single-retire RVFI contract and branch/load-use/dmem stall semantics by flushing or holding the registered IF/ID payload using the same hazard priorities. This should shorten the highest-fanout front-end timing path and improve FPGA Fmax with only a small control change and no new load-data or RV32M critical path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep or minimally adjust the IF/ID bundle so it can represent a registered valid payload and bubble without changing RVFI-visible pipeline metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an IF/ID register or one-entry fetch buffer that captures pc, instruction, and valid, with redirect and flush taking priority over ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Add tiny fetch BTB predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current core still resolves all branches and jumps in EX, so every taken\\ncontrol transfer pays a two-younger-instruction flush. Prior predictor\\nattempts showed that decode-time backward prediction is risky on this FPGA\\nbecause the imem_data-to-decode-to-target-add path can become part of the\\nfetch critical path. A fetch-PC-indexed BTB avoids that failed path while\\nstill targeting CoreMark's hot loop backedges and repeated call/return-like\\nJAL/JALR targets.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core still resolves all branches and jumps in EX, so every taken\\ncontrol transfer pays a two-younger-instruction flush. Prior predictor\\nattempts showed that decode-time backward prediction is risky on this FPGA\\nbecause the imem_data-to-decode-to-target-add path can become part of the\\nfetch critical path. A fetch-PC-indexed BTB avoids that failed path while\\nstill targeting CoreMark's hot loop backedges and repeated call/return-like\\nJAL/JALR targets.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small direct-mapped BTB in the fetch stage, indexed only by the\\nregistered PC and updated from EX when a branch or jump resolves. Each entry\\nstores valid, tag, target, and a one-bit or two-bit taken confidence bit;\\nfetch predicts to the stored target only on a tag hit, otherwise it keeps the\\nexisting pc+4 behavior. Carry predicted_pc_next through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX\\nredirects only when the resolved next PC differs from the prediction, and\\nupdate or allocate entries for taken branches/JAL/JALR while weakening or\\nclearing entries on not-taken branches.\\n\\nThis is the most promising predictor-only follow-up because it removes the\\nknown ID predictor critical path, learns actual targets instead of relying on\\na static backward heuristic, and should recover taken-control IPC on stable\\nCoreMark loops with modest LUT/FF cost.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_p... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Remove regfile reset fanout\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current register file synchronously resets all 32 architectural registers, which forces reset muxing and routing across 1024 state bits. On a small FPGA this can prevent efficient distributed-RAM inference and add reset fanout to a block that is read on the decode critical path.\\n\\nRV32I only requires x0 to read as zero; the other architectural registers do not need a defined hardware reset value as long as software initializes the registers it uses and RVFI reports the actual values read. Removing this reset is a narrow micro-optimization aimed at improving Fmax and possibly LUT pressure without changing pipeline depth, stalls, forwarding, memory behavior, or retirement width.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current register file synchronously resets all 32 architectural registers, which forces reset muxing and routing across 1024 state bits. On a small FPGA this can prevent efficient distributed-RAM inference and add reset fanout to a block that is read on the decode critical path.\\n\\nRV32I only requires x0 to read as zero; the other architectural registers do not need a defined hardware reset value as long as software initializes the registers it uses and RVFI reports the actual values read. Removing this reset is a narrow micro-optimization aimed at improving Fmax and possibly LUT pressure without changing pipeline depth, stalls, forwarding, memory behavior, or retirement width.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor rtl/reg_file.sv so reset no longer clears regs[1:31]. Keep x0 hardwired through the existing read and write gating, preserve write-first same-cycle bypass behavior, and leave the write port synchronous.\\n\\nThis should let yosys/nextpnr implement the 32x32 two-read/one-write file with less reset-related muxing and routing, potentially improving the decode/regfile-read timing path and reducing LUT/FF pressure. Because the change only affects ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Add RAM store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes all younger work whenever a store in EX/MEM sees\\nio_dmemReady low, even though stores do not produce a register result for the\\nfollowing instructions. Under the benchmark's random dmem backpressure model,\\nCoreMark can lose cycles waiting on regular RAM stores that could instead be\\nqueued and drained in order while independent ALU/control work continues.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that changed the M datapath, load-use timing, or the\\nfetch/decode boundary regressed because they lengthened hot combinational paths\\nor inserted broad stalls. A one-entry store buffer is a narrower structural\\nchange: it targets store-only dmem stalls, keeps loads conservative, and avoids\\nnew ALU or branch critical-path logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current pipeline freezes all younger work whenever a store in EX/MEM sees\\nio_dmemReady low, even though stores do not produce a register result for the\\nfollowing instructions. Under the benchmark's random dmem backpressure model,\\nCoreMark can lose cycles waiting on regular RAM stores that could instead be\\nqueued and drained in order while independent ALU/control work continues.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that changed the M datapath, load-use timing, or the\\nfetch/decode boundary regressed because they lengthened hot combinational paths\\nor inserted broad stalls. A one-entry store buffer is a narrower structural\\nchange: it targets store-only dmem stalls, keeps loads conservative, and avoids\\nnew ALU or branch critical-path logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry in-order store buffer for normal RAM stores only. When a\\nnon-MMIO store reaches MEM and the buffer is empty, capture its address, data,\\nmask, and RVFI memory metadata, allow the store instruction to retire, and let\\nthe buffer continue driving dmem_wen until io_dmemReady accepts it. Stall on\\nloads, MMIO stores, misali... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Predict JAL in decode\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current core resolves every taken control transfer in EX, so unconditional\\nJAL instructions pay the same two-younger-instruction flush penalty as taken\\nconditional branches. Prior predictor attempts showed that conditional\\nbackward prediction lengthened the imem/decode/target path and that mutable\\nBTB arrays complicated formal convergence, so the next predictor experiment\\nshould target a smaller, deterministic subset of control flow.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core resolves every taken control transfer in EX, so unconditional\\nJAL instructions pay the same two-younger-instruction flush penalty as taken\\nconditional branches. Prior predictor attempts showed that conditional\\nbackward prediction lengthened the imem/decode/target path and that mutable\\nBTB arrays complicated formal convergence, so the next predictor experiment\\nshould target a smaller, deterministic subset of control flow.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a static early predictor only for valid JAL instructions in the decode\\nstage. JAL is always taken and its target is encoded in the instruction, so ID\\ncan compute pc+J-immediate and redirect fetch one cycle earlier without any\\ndynamic predictor state, branch comparison, or BTB tag lookup. Carry a\\npredicted_pc_next bit/value through ID/EX and EX/MEM so EX suppresses the\\nnormal redirect when the resolved JAL target matches the decode prediction,\\nwhile branches and JALR keep the existing EX-resolution behavior.\\n\\nThis should recover some CoreMark IPC on function-call and unconditional-jump\\npaths while avoiding the two known predictor hazards: conditional decode\\nprediction on every branch and mutable fetch-side predictor arrays. The change\\nis intentionally narrow and should be easier for riscv-formal than a BTB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"des... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Gate EX control-transfer datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage computes branch comparisons, branch targets, and JALR target sums for every instruction, even though CoreMark's hot path is dominated by ordinary ALU and memory operations. Prior results show that narrow gating of always-live datapaths can improve Tang Nano Fmax, while larger structural or predictor changes have repeatedly lost more timing than they recovered in IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage computes branch comparisons, branch targets, and JALR target sums for every instruction, even though CoreMark's hot path is dominated by ordinary ALU and memory operations. Prior results show that narrow gating of always-live datapaths can improve Tang Nano Fmax, while larger structural or predictor changes have repeatedly lost more timing than they recovered in IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refactor rtl/ex_stage.sv so control-transfer hardware is only active for actual branch or jump instructions. Gate the branch comparator operands when is_branch is false, compute only the selected branch comparison for the decoded branch_op, and feed harmless constants into the branch-target and JALR-target adders when their instruction class is not active. Preserve the existing EX-stage resolution point, misalignment trap behavior, RVFI pc_next reporting, and redirect semantics.\\n\\nThis should reduce EX-stage LUT/routing pressure and remove always-live comparator/adder cones from common RV32I ALU and load/store cycles without adding state, stalls, or front-end prediction metadata. The expected gain is primarily Fmax, with essentially no IPC downside because pipeline timing and retirement behavior remain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate branch comparator inputs and control-target adders so non-control instructions do not k... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Precompute PC targets in decode\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage resolves branches and jumps while also running the ALU,\\nforwarding muxes, branch comparators, and RVFI next-PC construction. Prior\\nstructural attempts that added pipeline latency, broad stalls, or early\\nfront-end redirects regressed, so the next structural experiment should be a\\npure retiming that shortens EX without changing prediction policy or IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage resolves branches and jumps while also running the ALU,\\nforwarding muxes, branch comparators, and RVFI next-PC construction. Prior\\nstructural attempts that added pipeline latency, broad stalls, or early\\nfront-end redirects regressed, so the next structural experiment should be a\\npure retiming that shortens EX without changing prediction policy or IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute PC+4 and the PC-relative target PC+imm in ID and store them in the\\nexisting ID/EX pipeline register. EX can then use the precomputed PC+imm for\\nconditional branch targets, JAL targets, and AUIPC results, and use the\\nprecomputed PC+4 for fall-through, JAL/JALR link data, and RVFI pc_wdata.\\n\\nThis removes duplicated 32-bit PC adders from the EX control path and lowers\\nfanout around branch/jump resolution while preserving the same five-stage\\ntiming, load-use behavior, RVFI order, and single-channel nret=1 contract.\\nUnlike the failed decode-prediction attempts, the ID-side target add feeds\\nonly a register and never drives the fetch PC in the same cycle, so it should\\navoid creating a new imem_data-to-PC critical loop.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend id_ex_t with registered pc_plus4 and pc_imm_target fields used by EX for branch, JAL, AUIPC, and RVFI next-PC construction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute pc+4 and pc+imm f... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"One-entry JALR return predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The existing core resolves JALR in EX, so every function return pays the same two-younger-instruction flush as a taken branch. Prior predictor attempts lost fitness or broke formal because they added decode-time target generation on the imem critical path or introduced a general mutable BTB array. A much narrower predictor can target repeated return PCs without touching conditional branches or adding a broad fetch/decode target path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The existing core resolves JALR in EX, so every function return pays the same two-younger-instruction flush as a taken branch. Prior predictor attempts lost fitness or broke formal because they added decode-time target generation on the imem critical path or introduced a general mutable BTB array. A much narrower predictor can target repeated return PCs without touching conditional branches or adding a broad fetch/decode target path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single scalar JALR target cache in fetch, updated only when EX resolves a legal JALR. The cache stores one valid bit, one PC tag, and one resolved target; when the current fetch PC matches the tag, IF predicts the cached target as the next PC and carries predicted_pc_next metadata through ID/EX. EX compares the resolved next PC against the carried prediction and redirects only on mismatch, while all branches, JAL, and cache misses keep the current pc+4/static-not-taken behavior.\\n\\nThis is deliberately smaller than the failed BTB attempt: no predictor array, no counters, no conditional-branch update policy, and no imem_data-to-immediate-to-PC combinational redirect. It should recover recurring CoreMark return penalties after warm-up with limited LUT/FF cost and lower formal risk than a general fetch BTB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add pre... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Gate divider operands only\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best bench core already benefited strongly from isolating the MUL-family datapath from ordinary RV32I ALU operations. The remaining DIV/REM operators still consume the live ALU operands directly, so yosys may keep wide divider/remainder cones and routing coupled to the EX-stage result mux even when CoreMark is mostly executing adds, compares, loads, stores, and branches.\\n\\nA prior attempt to share the divider datapath regressed because it introduced signed normalization, restore muxing, and simultaneous quotient/remainder logic. The safer micro-optimization is to avoid that structural rewrite and only gate the raw divider operands outside actual DIV/REM operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best bench core already benefited strongly from isolating the MUL-family datapath from ordinary RV32I ALU operations. The remaining DIV/REM operators still consume the live ALU operands directly, so yosys may keep wide divider/remainder cones and routing coupled to the EX-stage result mux even when CoreMark is mostly executing adds, compares, loads, stores, and branches.\\n\\nA prior attempt to share the divider datapath regressed because it introduced signed normalization, restore muxing, and simultaneous quotient/remainder logic. The safer micro-optimization is to avoid that structural rewrite and only gate the raw divider operands outside actual DIV/REM operations.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add an is_divrem_op decode inside rtl/alu.sv and feed the real a/b operands to division and remainder operators only for ALU_DIV, ALU_DIVU, ALU_REM, and ALU_REMU. For all other ALU ops, drive the divider operand wires with harmless constants, then use those gated wires in the existing RV32M divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 cases.\\n\\nThis keeps the pipeline single-cycle, preserves RV32M and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS semantics, and avoids the failed normalization/shari... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Add fetch skid buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The benchmark model injects independent instruction and data bus stalls, and the current pipeline freezes fetch whenever a data-memory operation is held in EX/MEM. If instruction memory is ready during that backend hold, the core wastes an opportunity to capture the next sequential instruction and can then immediately lose more cycles to a later imem stall after dmem unblocks.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that always inserted an IF/ID register or added store-buffer control regressed, so this hypothesis keeps the normal zero-wait path combinational and only adds a one-entry skid slot for cycles where the backend is already unable to advance.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The benchmark model injects independent instruction and data bus stalls, and the current pipeline freezes fetch whenever a data-memory operation is held in EX/MEM. If instruction memory is ready during that backend hold, the core wastes an opportunity to capture the next sequential instruction and can then immediately lose more cycles to a later imem stall after dmem unblocks.\\n\\nPrior structural attempts that always inserted an IF/ID register or added store-buffer control regressed, so this hypothesis keeps the normal zero-wait path combinational and only adds a one-entry skid slot for cycles where the backend is already unable to advance.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry fetch skid buffer in the IF stage that captures the current imem instruction and PC when dmem_stall holds ID/EX and EX/MEM but imemReady is high. When the backend releases, feed the buffered instruction to ID before requesting/advancing to the next PC; invalidate the buffer on any EX redirect so wrong-path contents never retire.\\n\\nThis should improve CoreMark iterations/cycle under the random stall model by overlapping useful instruction fetch with data-memory wait cycles, without changing retirement width, branch resol... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Add loop-target prefetch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The existing core still pays a taken-control penalty, but prior predictor\\nattempts show that decode-time redirects and mutable BTB-style PC metadata\\ncan lose more Fmax or formal convergence than they recover in IPC. CoreMark\\nnevertheless has tight repeated loops, and the random imem backpressure model\\nmakes avoiding wrong-path fetch work valuable if the predictor can stay off\\nthe critical PC-update path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The existing core still pays a taken-control penalty, but prior predictor\\nattempts show that decode-time redirects and mutable BTB-style PC metadata\\ncan lose more Fmax or formal convergence than they recover in IPC. CoreMark\\nnevertheless has tight repeated loops, and the random imem backpressure model\\nmakes avoiding wrong-path fetch work valuable if the predictor can stay off\\nthe critical PC-update path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny one-entry loop-target prefetch predictor that learns only resolved\\ntaken backward conditional branches in EX, storing the branch PC and target in\\nflops. Fetch keeps the architecturally visible PC policy unchanged until a\\nregistered PC tag hit is observed, then uses the stored target as the next PC\\nand carries a predicted_pc_next value with that fetched instruction so EX can\\nsuppress the normal redirect only when the resolved next PC exactly matches.\\n\\nThis is deliberately narrower than the failed BTB and decode predictors: no\\npredictor array, no instruction-decode-to-PC immediate path, no JAL/JALR\\ntarget speculation, and no change to branch condition resolution. It should\\nrecover the hottest loop backedge penalties after warm-up while limiting Fmax\\nrisk to a single PC comparator and target mux driven by registered predictor\\nstate.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260513-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Trim dead pipeline metadata\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already benefited from narrow datapath cleanup, while later attempts to add gating, prediction, buffering, or divider rewrites mostly lost Fmax or broke formal. There are still dead bits carried through hot pipeline bundles: ctrl.is_lui is decoded but never consumed, and EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target are written but not read by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. On a small FPGA these unused flops and their assignment cones can still consume routing, reset/load muxing, and placement freedom.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already benefited from narrow datapath cleanup, while later attempts to add gating, prediction, buffering, or divider rewrites mostly lost Fmax or broke formal. There are still dead bits carried through hot pipeline bundles: ctrl.is_lui is decoded but never consumed, and EX/MEM branch_taken/branch_target are written but not read by MEM, WB, RVFI, hazard, or forwarding logic. On a small FPGA these unused flops and their assignment cones can still consume routing, reset/load muxing, and placement freedom.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Remove unused pipeline metadata instead of adding new behavior. Delete ctrl_t.is_lui and its decoder/id_stage assignments, and delete ex_mem_t.branch_taken and ex_mem_t.branch_target plus the corresponding ex_stage register writes. LUI behavior is already represented by ALU_LUI with alu_src/reg_write, branch redirects use the combinational EX outputs, and RVFI pc_next uses the resolved value already stored in EX/MEM, so these fields should not affect architectural state or retirement.\\n\\nThis is a pure micro-optimization: fewer registered bits, less control fanout, and less unnecessary mux/load/reset logic in the ID/EX and EX/MEM registers. The expected gain is modest but safer than another arithmetic or predictor change because it does not change pipelin... (line truncated to 2000 chars)","truncated":true,"loaded":[]},"title":"bench/gpt-5_5_medium/rep3/log.jsonl","time":{"start":1778835273538,"end":1778835273552}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0a1adc6c8a66bead016a06df4969d4819a8feb2977ee7884c4"}},"id":"prt_e2ad83740001oV5HXqF1wMx6kW","sessionID":"ses_1d5292540ffepjDgtvUr54dZ4p","messageID":"msg_e2ad81851001ayJRBTrPq3dvFA"}}
