{"type":"text","timestamp":1778836789548,"sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","part":{"id":"prt_e2aef4ba6001OWAHAp3b3ICZUt","messageID":"msg_e2aef2ba8001K7KjSJXLttbFok","sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","type":"text","text":"The only prior `structural` bench attempt was the divider extraction idea, which is already in the current RTL. Next I’m checking adjacent core history for structural ideas that helped without repeating the failed fetch-side predictor path.","time":{"start":1778836786086,"end":1778836789546},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"msg_0bfc3983a645a890016a06e531fbb0819b9fe81095639db486","phase":"commentary"}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778836789601,"sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_XxZedYTdoQpUC4PdBZv4uI4y","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"\"category\"\\s*:\\s*\"structural\"","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/experiments","include":"log.jsonl"},"output":"Found 25 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Elastic Fetch Skid Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF/ID handoff with an elastic 2-entry fetch skid\\nbuffer that decouples instruction fetch from decode. The fetch side advances\\nthe PC and captures sequential instructions whenever io_imemReady is high\\nand the buffer is not full, even while ID/EX is held by a dmem stall. The\\ndecode side consumes one buffered instruction per non-stalled cycle. Preserve\\nthe baseline zero-latency behavior with an empty-buffer bypass from\\nio_imemData to decode when the backend is ready and imem is ready, so the\\nno-stall path and branch target...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-005-r2s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-to-EX Load-Use Bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still has the textbook structural load-use gap: a load in EX always forces the immediately following consumer to wait one extra cycle, even when the dmem bus is ready and the loaded word is already available combinationally in MEM. CoreMark's list, matrix, and state kernels contain many load-to-use pairs, so this fixed bubble directly lowers iterations per cycle. The recent predictor win improved control flow without changing the backend; the next most promising structural angle is to add the missing MEM-to-EX load-data path and remove the mandatory interlock while preserving the single-issue in-order retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still has the textbook structural load-use gap: a load in EX always forces the immediately following consumer to wait one extra cycle, even when the dmem bus is ready and the loaded word is already available combinationally in MEM. CoreMark's list, matrix, and state kernels contain many load-to-use pairs, so this fixed bubble directly lowers iterations per cycle. The recent predictor win improved control flow without changing the backend; the next most promising structural angle is to add the missing MEM-to-EX load-data path and remove the mandatory interlock while preserving the single-issue in-order retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a direct load-result forwarding path from the MEM stage's existing sign/zero-extended load_data to the EX operand muxes. When an instruction in EX consumes the rd of a load currently in MEM, select this MEM load result for rs1 and/or rs2 instead of stalling for the value to appear in MEM/WB. If io_dmemReady is low for that load, keep the current dmem hold behavior and suppress any younger EX redirect until the load data is valid; once ready, the dependent instruction executes in the same cycle the load ...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Retiring Store Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry FIFO for normal RAM stores. When a legal aligned store to the\\nRAM address range reaches MEM and the external dmem port is not immediately\\navailable, retire the store into the FIFO with its address, replicated write\\ndata, byte mask, and RVFI metadata, then let younger non-memory instructions\\ncontinue through the pipeline while the FIFO drains oldest-first on the dmem\\nport. Loads, stores when the FIFO is full, and all MMIO/marker/UART st...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-005-r4s1\", \"title\": \"ID-Stage JALR Redirect\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core predicts backward conditional branches and direct JALs\\nin IF, but JALR remains structurally resolved only in EX. Every return and\\nindirect jump therefore still injects the same wrong-path recovery bubbles\\nthat direct-JAL prediction just removed. CoreMark's timed path has many\\ncall/return edges around the list, matrix, state, CRC, and benchmark harness\\nroutines, so unresolved returns are now one of the cleaner remaining control\\nbubbles. A small decode-stage JALR resolver targets that specific structural\\nlatency without adding a predictor table, widening retirement, or putting\\nload data on the EX operand path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core predicts backward conditional branches and direct JALs\\nin IF, but JALR remains structurally resolved only in EX. Every return and\\nindirect jump therefore still injects the same wrong-path recovery bubbles\\nthat direct-JAL prediction just removed. CoreMark's timed path has many\\ncall/return edges around the list, matrix, state, CRC, and benchmark harness\\nroutines, so unresolved returns are now one of the cleaner remaining control\\nbubbles. A small decode-stage JALR resolver targets that specific structural\\nlatency without adding a predictor table, widening retirement, or putting\\nload data on the EX operand path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add an early JALR redirect path in ID for cases where the rs1 base is already\\navailable from the regfile or same-cycle WB write-first bypass. ID computes\\nthe JALR target from rs1 plus the I-immediate, verifies the aligned target,\\nand sends a PC redirect to IF in the same cycle the JALR enters ID/EX. The\\nID/EX prediction metadata is overwritten with that resolved target so the\\nlater EX-stage instance of the same JALR does not redirect a second time.\\nWhen rs1 is waiting on an older instruction in ID/EX or EX/MEM, or when the\\nbackend is ...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-008-r5s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Store-Data Forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core still treats every immediate load-use match as a\\nfull one-cycle interlock, including the common case where the younger\\ninstruction is a STORE that only needs the loaded register as store data\\n(rs2), not as the address base (rs1). That bubble is structurally avoidable:\\nstore data is not consumed by the EX ALU, and the actual dmem write data is\\nformed one stage later in MEM. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass showed\\nthat CoreMark has meaningful load-use pressure, but it lost too much Fmax by\\nrouting dmem load data into the EX operand muxes. A late store-data bypass\\ntargets a useful subset of those bubbles while keeping load data off the ALU,\\nbranch compare, and JALR critical paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core still treats every immediate load-use match as a\\nfull one-cycle interlock, including the common case where the younger\\ninstruction is a STORE that only needs the loaded register as store data\\n(rs2), not as the address base (rs1). That bubble is structurally avoidable:\\nstore data is not consumed by the EX ALU, and the actual dmem write data is\\nformed one stage later in MEM. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass showed\\nthat CoreMark has meaningful load-use pressure, but it lost too much Fmax by\\nrouting dmem load data into the EX operand muxes. A late store-data bypass\\ntargets a useful subset of those bubbles while keeping load data off the ALU,\\nbranch compare, and JALR critical paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Allow a legal STORE immediately after a LOAD to proceed without the normal\\nload-use bubble when the load destination matches only the STORE rs2 data\\nfield and not the STORE rs1 address field. The store computes its address in\\nEX as usual, carries its rs2 register number into MEM, and selects the final\\nstore data in MEM from the MEM/WB writeback value when that older i...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-011-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Registered Fetch/Decode Cutpoint\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core still has no real IF/ID register: the PC drives imem, the returned instruction is decoded, the register file is read, and the ID/EX register captures the result in one cycle. That preserves a short frontend latency, but it also leaves a large structural timing path through instruction memory data, IF prediction decode, full instruction decode, immediate generation, and regfile read fanout. Prior elastic-fetch work kept an empty-buffer bypass to avoid latency, so it retained much of this path and lost Fmax. A cleaner structural bet is to deliberately insert the missing fetch/decode cutpoint and trade one extra frontend pipeline stage for a higher median FPGA clock.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core still has no real IF/ID register: the PC drives imem, the returned instruction is decoded, the register file is read, and the ID/EX register captures the result in one cycle. That preserves a short frontend latency, but it also leaves a large structural timing path through instruction memory data, IF prediction decode, full instruction decode, immediate generation, and regfile read fanout. Prior elastic-fetch work kept an empty-buffer bypass to avoid latency, so it retained much of this path and lost Fmax. A cleaner structural bet is to deliberately insert the missing fetch/decode cutpoint and trade one extra frontend pipeline stage for a higher median FPGA clock.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the IF/ID handoff into a real registered pipeline boundary with no zero-latency imem-data-to-decode bypass. IF captures {pc, instr, predicted_taken, predicted_target, valid} when io_imemReady is high and the decode side can accept an entry; ID decodes only the registered entry on the following cycle. Redirects and reset flush the IF/ID register and restart fetch at the resolved target, while load-use and dmem stalls hold the re...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-014-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Single-Entry Retiring Store Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gained a large Fmax and LUT win by moving DIV/REM out\\nof the hot EX path, so the next structural bottleneck is less likely to be\\nraw datapath area and more likely to be cycles lost to the random dmem\\nhandshake model. Today every dmem-not-ready cycle for a STORE freezes the\\nwhole pipeline, even though the store address, replicated data, byte mask,\\nand RVFI memory metadata are already known in MEM and the store produces no\\nregister result for younger ALU/control instructions. A prior two-entry\\nstore buffer proved the idea through formal and cosim but failed placement\\non the larger pre-DIV RTL. A smaller one-entry RAM-store buffer should retain\\nmost of the useful stall hiding while keeping the arbitration, state, and\\nplacement pressure low enough for the post-DIV design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gained a large Fmax and LUT win by moving DIV/REM out\\nof the hot EX path, so the next structural bottleneck is less likely to be\\nraw datapath area and more likely to be cycles lost to the random dmem\\nhandshake model. Today every dmem-not-ready cycle for a STORE freezes the\\nwhole pipeline, even though the store address, replicated data, byte mask,\\nand RVFI memory metadata are already known in MEM and the store produces no\\nregister result for younger ALU/control instructions. A prior two-entry\\nstore buffer proved the idea through formal and cosim but failed placement\\non the larger pre-DIV RTL. A smaller one-entry RAM-store buffer should retain\\nmost of the useful stall hiding while keeping the arbitration, state, and\\nplacement pressure low enough for the post-DIV design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one in-order retiring store slot for legal aligned stores to normal RAM\\nonly. When such a store reaches MEM and io_dmemReady is low, capture its\\nbyte address, replicated write data, byte mask, and RV...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-017-r8s1\", \"title\": \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot With Direct Fast Path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\\n\\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\\n\\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot that is used only on a...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-020-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Store-Slot-Aware Fetch Skid Queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design combines the two changes that have paid off most:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and a one-deep pending-store slot\\nlets a stalled normal-RAM store retire while the physical dmem write drains\\nlater. That store slot reduced CoreMark cycles, but the frontend is still\\nstructurally brittle under the tournament's random imem stalls: `if_stage`\\npresents live imem data directly to decode, and `hazard_unit` still turns\\nraw `io_imemReady=0` into a global pipeline stall.\\n\\nA previous generic elastic fetch buffer regressed because it added permanent\\nfrontend queue/arbitration cost before the divider and store-slot timing\\nwins, and it tried to prefetch through all backend stalls. The better\\nstructural angle now is narrower: use a tiny fetch skid queue only to absorb\\ninstruction-memory backpressure and to keep fetch productive while the new\\nstore slot is draining for older stores. This targets the measured\\nstore-slot cycle win's remaining companion bottleneck without touching the\\nhot EX operand path, load data forwarding, branch predictor state, or RVFI\\nretirement width.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design combines the two changes that have paid off most:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and a one-deep pending-store slot\\nlets a stalled normal-RAM store retire while the physical dmem write drains\\nlater. That store slot reduced CoreMark cycles, but the frontend is still\\nstructurally brittle under the tournament's random imem stalls: `if_stage`\\npresents live imem data directly to decode, and `hazard_unit` still turns\\nraw `io_imemReady=0` into a global pipeline stall.\\n\\nA previous generic elastic fetch buffer regressed because it added permanent\\nfrontend queue/arbitration cost before the divider and store-slot timing\\nwins, and it tried to prefetch through all backend stalls. T...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-023-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-imem path whenever the external\\nbus is ready, and consult a tiny cached copy only on cycles where imem would\\notherwise stall. CoreMark's timed code repeatedly executes the same matrix,\\nlist, state, CRC, and loop-control instructions over a 10-iteration run, so\\neven a small direct-mapped instruction replay table should turn many random\\niStall bubbles into normal fetch cycles after warmup.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-im...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-026-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Narrow Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction side. The 256-entry stall-only replay cache cut CoreMark cycles\\nfrom 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, showing that random iStall bubbles are a large\\npart of the remaining IPC loss, but its wide IF table/tag/data mux dropped\\nmedian Fmax enough to miss the current best.\\n\\nThat result points to a narrower replay structure rather than a broader\\nfetch queue. CoreMark's timed hot code repeatedly executes compact inner\\nloops and call bodies, so a smaller direct-mapped replay table should retain\\nmuch of the iStall hit-rate benefit while reducing the IF storage mux,\\ncomparator fanout, and PC-control load that hurt Fmax in the 256-entry\\nversion. This keeps the zero-wait path direct and targets only cycles that\\nwould otherwise be external imem stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction side. The 256-entry stall-only replay cache cut CoreMark cycles\\nfrom 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, showing that random iStall bubbles are a large\\npart of the remaining IPC loss, but its wide IF table/tag/data mux dropped\\nmedian Fmax enough to miss the current best.\\n\\nThat result points to a narrower replay structure rather than a broader\\nfetch queue. CoreMark's timed hot code repeatedly executes compact inner\\nloops and call bodies, so a smaller direct-mapped replay t...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-029-r12s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Load Replay Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already captured the two strongest proven wins:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and the one-deep pending-store\\nslot hides store-only dmem backpressure while preserving the ready-store fast\\npath. The next structural bottleneck is therefore load-side dmem stalls. A\\nload whose word was recently read still freezes the whole pipeline whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even though CoreMark repeatedly touches a compact 6 KB\\nworking set across list, matrix, state, stack, and CRC code.\\n\\nInstruction replay experiments showed that serving repeated hot accesses\\nduring random bus stalls can cut many cycles, but putting a wide replay table\\nin IF cost too much Fmax. A smaller load replay structure in MEM attacks the\\nsame random-stall problem on the data side, where the lookup is off the\\nbranch/JAL predictor path and does not add load data to the EX operand muxes.\\nThis is a structural stall-hiding change, not a broader predictor or\\narithmetic rewrite.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already captured the two strongest proven wins:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and the one-deep pending-store\\nslot hides store-only dmem backpressure while preserving the ready-store fast\\npath. The next structural bottleneck is therefore load-side dmem stalls. A\\nload whose word was recently read still freezes the whole pipeline whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even though CoreMark repeatedly touches a compact 6 KB\\nworking set across list, matrix, state, stack, and CRC code.\\n\\nInstruction replay experiments showed that serving repeated hot accesses\\nduring random bus stalls can cut many cycles, but putting a wide replay table\\nin IF cost too much Fmax. A smaller load replay structure in MEM attacks the\\nsame random-stall problem on the data side, where the lookup is off the\\nbranch/JAL predictor pat...\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-032-r13s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Load Replay Behind I-Fetch Replay\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its large gain from the registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table: random iStall bubbles are often converted into\\nuseful fetches without putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF critical\\npath. That leaves the data side as the cleanest remaining structural stall\\nsource. A load still freezes the whole in-order backend whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even when the same normal-RAM word was recently read\\nin CoreMark's compact 6 KB working set.\\n\\nThe earlier standalone MEM-stage load replay experiment was the best\\nstructural signal after the store slot: it reduced CoreMark cycles with a\\nsmall, conservative table located away from the IF predictor path and did\\nnot require load data to feed the EX operand mux. It was not combined with\\nthe current registered instruction replay best. Combining the two targets\\nindependent random-stall sources: IF replay hides instruction stalls, while\\nMEM replay can retire repeated loads during data-memory stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its large gain from the registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table: random iStall bubbles are often converted into\\nuseful fetches without putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF critical\\npath. That leaves the data side as the cleanest remaining structural stall\\nsource. A load still freezes the whole in-order backend whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even when the same normal-RAM word was recently read\\nin CoreMark's compact 6 KB working set.\\n\\nThe earlier standalone MEM-stage load replay experiment was the best\\nstructural signal after the store slot: it reduced CoreMark cycles with a\\nsmall, conservative table located away from the IF predictor path and did\\nnot require load data to feed the EX operand mux. It was not combined with\\nthe current registered instruction replay best. Combinin...\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-035-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Registered MEM Load-Replay Candidate\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest IPC gain from registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay: repeated hot instructions can be supplied during random\\nimem stalls without putting a same-cycle replay-table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. The analogous remaining structural loss is on the data side.\\nLoads still freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low,\\neven when the same normal-RAM word was recently returned by dmem.\\n\\nThe earlier MEM-stage load replay experiment showed the opportunity: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles, but the combinational replay-table lookup, tag compare, and\\nstore merge logic in MEM lowered Fmax enough to miss the current best. The\\npromising structural angle is to keep the replay behavior while moving the\\nlookup off the MEM critical path, just as the successful IF replay predictor\\ndid for instruction stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest IPC gain from registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay: repeated hot instructions can be supplied during random\\nimem stalls without putting a same-cycle replay-table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. The analogous remaining structural loss is on the data side.\\nLoads still freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low,\\neven when the same normal-RAM word was recently returned by dmem.\\n\\nThe earlier MEM-stage load replay experiment showed the opportunity: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles, but the combinational replay-table lookup, tag compare, and\\nstore merge logic in MEM lowered Fmax enough to miss the current best. The\\npromising structural angle is to keep the replay behavior while moving the\\nlookup off the MEM critical path, just as the successful IF replay predictor\\ndid for instruction stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small normal-RAM load replay table with a registered candidate for the\\ninstruction entering MEM...\n  Line 45: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-038-r15s1\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Pending Store Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the proven structural pieces: cold DIV/REM is\\nout of the universal EX path, the one-deep pending-store slot hides some\\nnormal-RAM store backpressure, and the registered instruction replay table\\nremoves most random iStall loss. The remaining structural stalls are now\\nconcentrated on the data side. A single pending-store entry lets one stalled\\nRAM store retire, but the next store still freezes the whole pipeline while\\nthe older slot drains, even though both stores already have their address,\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI memory metadata available in MEM.\\n\\nA two-entry store buffer was attempted before the large post-DIV and replay\\ntiming wins and failed placement on a much larger design. The current RTL is\\nsubstantially smaller and already has the timing-local one-entry direct fast\\npath, so revisiting only a second pending-store slot is the cleanest\\nstructural bet: it targets store bursts under the 22% random dStall model\\nwithout adding load data to EX, changing the branch predictor, widening\\nretirement, or touching the IF replay timing path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the proven structural pieces: cold DIV/REM is\\nout of the universal EX path, the one-deep pending-store slot hides some\\nnormal-RAM store backpressure, and the registered instruction replay table\\nremoves most random iStall loss. The remaining structural stalls are now\\nconcentrated on the data side. A single pending-store entry lets one stalled\\nRAM store retire, but the next store still freezes the whole pipeline while\\nthe older slot drains, even though both stores already have their address,\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI memory metadata available in MEM.\\n\\nA two-entry store buffer was attempted before the large post-DIV and replay\\ntiming wins and failed placement on a much lar...\n  Line 48: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-041-r16s1\", \"title\": \"Same-Cycle Store Slot Refill\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the two structural wins that matter most:\\nregistered instruction replay hides many random iStall cycles, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot lets a stalled normal-RAM store retire before\\nthe external dmem write is accepted. The remaining store-side bottleneck is\\nnarrower than a full second buffer entry. When the pending slot is valid, the\\nMEM stage currently treats every younger memory operation as blocked even on\\nthe exact cycle where `io_dmemReady` will drain the older buffered store.\\n\\nThat leaves a small but avoidable bubble for bursts of normal-RAM stores:\\nthe older store uses the physical dmem port, while the younger store already\\nhas its address, replicated data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible memory fields\\navailable in MEM and could refill the just-drained slot. A previous\\ntwo-entry FIFO targeted the same store-burst pressure but failed placement.\\nThe existing `store_slot` state machine already supports simultaneous drain\\nand enqueue, so the lower-risk structural angle is to expose that fast refill\\ncase without adding another entry or touching the IF replay path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the two structural wins that matter most:\\nregistered instruction replay hides many random iStall cycles, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot lets a stalled normal-RAM store retire before\\nthe external dmem write is accepted. The remaining store-side bottleneck is\\nnarrower than a full second buffer entry. When the pending slot is valid, the\\nMEM stage currently treats every younger memory operation as blocked even on\\nthe exact cycle where `io_dmemReady` will drain the older buffered store.\\n\\nThat leaves a small but avoidable bubble for bursts of normal-RAM stores:\\nthe older store uses the physical dmem port, while the younger store already\\nhas its address, replicated da...\n  Line 51: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-044-r17s1\", \"title\": \"Tiny MRU Load Replay Latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already uses the two structural ideas that have paid\\nclearly: a one-deep pending-store slot for store-only dmem backpressure and\\na registered instruction replay table for random iStall. The remaining\\nstructural stalls are concentrated on normal-RAM loads. A load still freezes\\nthe whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low, even if the same\\naligned word was returned recently.\\n\\nThe prior 16-entry MEM-stage load replay buffer showed that this stall source\\nis real: it reduced CoreMark cycles on top of the instruction replay design.\\nIt missed the current best because the same-cycle table lookup, tag compare,\\nand store-update logic dropped Fmax too much. A much smaller MRU replay latch\\ntargets the same dmem-stall opportunity while keeping the MEM path close to\\nthe current best design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already uses the two structural ideas that have paid\\nclearly: a one-deep pending-store slot for store-only dmem backpressure and\\na registered instruction replay table for random iStall. The remaining\\nstructural stalls are concentrated on normal-RAM loads. A load still freezes\\nthe whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low, even if the same\\naligned word was returned recently.\\n\\nThe prior 16-entry MEM-stage load replay buffer showed that this stall source\\nis real: it reduced CoreMark cycles on top of the instruction replay design.\\nIt missed the current best because the same-cycle table lookup, tag compare,\\nand store-update logic dropped Fmax too much. A much smaller MRU replay latch\\ntargets the same dmem-stall opportunity while keeping the MEM path close to\\nthe current best design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry most-recently-used normal-RAM load replay latch in MEM. Each\\nentry stores a valid bit, an aligned word address tag, and the raw 32-bit word\\nreturned...\n  Line 54: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-047-r18s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-Exact Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has strong timing wins from the registered instruction\\nreplay predictor, segmented RVFI order counter, pending-store slot, and\\nhot/cold ALU split. The remaining structural improvement should therefore\\navoid another cache, queue, predictor table, or load-data forwarding path that\\ncould disturb Fmax.\\n\\nThe load-use interlock is still structurally conservative. It stalls whenever\\na load in ID/EX has a nonzero destination matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2\\nbitfield of the IF/ID instruction. That is correct for true consumers, but it\\ncreates false bubbles for instructions where one or both fields are not\\nsource operands: OP-IMM and LOAD do not read rs2, JAL/LUI/AUIPC read neither,\\nFENCE/EBREAK read neither, and JALR reads only rs1. CoreMark's optimized code\\nhas dense immediate arithmetic, address-generation loads, calls, and loop\\ncontrol, so these false one-cycle bubbles are a plausible remaining IPC loss.\\n\\nA prior operand-aware interlock attempt broke selftest, so this hypothesis is\\ndeliberately conservative: decode source-use exactly for known legal\\ninstruction classes, keep reserved or unknown encodings on the current\\nboth-source stall policy, and do not add any MEM-to-EX load-data path. The\\ngoal is to recover only provably false load-use stalls while leaving all true\\nload consumers, dmem waits, branch recovery, divider interlocks, and RVFI\\nordering unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has strong timing wins from the registered instruction\\nreplay predictor, segmented RVFI order counter, pending-store slot, and\\nhot/cold ALU split. The remaining structural improvement should therefore\\navoid another cache, queue, predictor table, or load-data forwarding path that\\ncould disturb Fmax.\\n\\nThe load-use interlock is still structurally conservative. It stalls whenever\\na load in ID/EX h...\n  Line 57: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-050-r19s1\", \"title\": \"Backend-Stall Shadow I-Fetch Prefetcher\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest cycle-count win from the registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table: hot instructions can be supplied during\\nrandom imem stalls without a same-cycle replay lookup on the IF critical\\npath. The remaining structural stalls are now mostly backend stalls from\\ndmem waits and long operations. During those cycles the frontend PC is held,\\ndecode is not accepting a new instruction, and the independent imem port is\\nusually wasted refreshing the same held PC or sitting idle.\\n\\nPrior fetch queues tried to decouple fetch and decode directly, but they put\\nqueue control on the normal zero-wait frontend path and lost Fmax. A more\\ntargeted structural angle is to use backend-stall cycles only as replay-table\\nwarmup time. The architectural fetch/decode stream remains held exactly as it\\nis today, while a shadow prefetch PC walks ahead through the existing static\\nbranch/direct-JAL prediction policy and fills the replay table with future\\ninstruction words. When the backend releases, later external imem stalls are\\nmore likely to hit in the already-proven registered replay structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest cycle-count win from the registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table: hot instructions can be supplied during\\nrandom imem stalls without a same-cycle replay lookup on the IF critical\\npath. The remaining structural stalls are now mostly backend stalls from\\ndmem waits and long operations. During those cycles the frontend PC is held,\\ndecode is not accepting a new instruction, and the independent imem port is\\nusually wasted refreshing the same held PC or sitting idle.\\n\\nPrior fetch queues tried to decouple fetch and decode directly, but they put\\nqueue control on the normal zero-wait frontend path and lost Fmax. A more\\ntargeted structural angle is to use ...\n  Line 60: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-053-r20s1\", \"title\": \"Same-Word Store Slot Coalescing\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already includes the structural wins that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot for normal-RAM store stalls\\nand a registered instruction replay table for random iStall. Broader store\\nbuffering attempts found the right bottleneck but were too expensive:\\ntwo-entry pending-store designs either failed placement, and the same-cycle\\nrefill variant also failed placement. That suggests the next structural\\nstore-side step should add capacity only for cases the existing single slot\\ncan handle locally.\\n\\nCoreMark updates list nodes, matrix cells, stack frames, and scalar state with\\nshort bursts of stores. When a pending normal-RAM store is already occupying\\nthe slot and dmem is not ready, a younger store to the same aligned word does\\nnot need a second physical buffer entry. It can be merged into the pending\\nslot because loads and MMIO are still held behind the slot, and there is no\\narchitectural observer between the two normal-RAM stores. This targets the\\nsame store-burst pressure as the failed two-entry FIFO, but with one address\\ncompare and one byte-mask merge instead of another queue entry or a wider\\ndmem arbiter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already includes the structural wins that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot for normal-RAM store stalls\\nand a registered instruction replay table for random iStall. Broader store\\nbuffering attempts found the right bottleneck but were too expensive:\\ntwo-entry pending-store designs either failed placement, and the same-cycle\\nrefill variant also failed placement. That suggests the next structural\\nstore-side step should add capacity only for cases the existing single slot\\ncan handle locally.\\n\\nCoreMark updates list nodes, matrix cells, stack frames, and scalar state with\\nshort bursts of stores. When a ...\n  Line 63: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-056-r21s1\", \"title\": \"Pending Store Slot Load Forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural pieces that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot hides normal-RAM store\\nbackpressure, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random iStall bubbles. The structural experiments since then show the\\nboundary clearly. Larger store buffers and same-cycle refill failed\\nplacement, same-word store coalescing did not reduce CoreMark cycles, and\\ndata replay tables recovered some load-stall cycles but cost too much Fmax.\\n\\nThe remaining store-slot bottleneck is narrower. When a normal-RAM store has\\nretired into the pending slot, the core currently blocks every younger memory\\noperation until the physical write drains, even if the younger operation is a\\nload of bytes that are already fully present in the pending store entry. That\\ncase does not require a second store-buffer entry or a dmem access: in\\nprogram order the store is already older and retired, so a same-word covered\\nload should read the store slot's bytes. CoreMark's stack, list, and state\\ncode can produce store/load reuse on nearby words, especially under random\\ndmem stalls that keep the slot valid for multiple cycles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural pieces that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot hides normal-RAM store\\nbackpressure, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random iStall bubbles. The structural experiments since then show the\\nboundary clearly. Larger store buffers and same-cycle refill failed\\nplacement, same-word store coalescing did not reduce CoreMark cycles, and\\ndata replay tables recovered some load-stall cycles but cost too much Fmax.\\n\\nThe remaining store-slot bottleneck is narrower. When a normal-RAM store has\\nretired into the pending slot, the core currentl...\n  Line 66: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-059-r22s1\", \"title\": \"Replay-Assisted ALU Pair Dual Issue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is now a fast single-issue design: cold DIV/REM is\\noff the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split improved Fmax, the one-entry\\nstore slot hides some dmem store stalls, and the two-bank registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall bubbles. The largest\\nremaining structural ceiling is therefore the single-retire backend. The\\ntop-level RVFI contract already exposes NRET=2, but channel 1 is unused.\\n\\nA normal dual-issue frontend would be unattractive because the external imem\\nport supplies only one 32-bit word per cycle. The banked replay predictor\\nchanges that tradeoff: after warmup, hot straight-line CoreMark code often\\nhas both the current instruction and the next sequential instruction in the\\nreplay table. That creates a narrow path to use RVFI channel 1 without\\nwidening the external memory interface or adding a full instruction cache.\\n\\nThe most promising structural angle is to issue a second instruction only\\nfor low-risk adjacent simple-integer pairs. This targets CoreMark's dense\\naddress, loop, CRC, and scalar arithmetic sequences while avoiding the\\nplacement failures seen from larger store buffers and the Fmax losses seen\\nfrom load replay tables, return predictors, and wider replay structures.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is now a fast single-issue design: cold DIV/REM is\\noff the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split improved Fmax, the one-entry\\nstore slot hides some dmem store stalls, and the two-bank registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall bubbles. The largest\\nremaining structural ceiling is therefore the single-retire backend. The\\ntop-level RVFI contract already exposes NRET=2, but channel 1 is unused.\\n\\nA normal dual-issue frontend would be unattractive because the external imem\\nport supplies only one 32-bit word per cycle. The b...\n  Line 69: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-062-r23s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Wait Simple-ALU Skid Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is a fast single-issue design with the largest proven\\nwins already in place: cold DIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU\\nsplit improved Fmax, the one-entry pending-store slot hides some store-only\\ndmem stalls, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random imem stalls. The remaining structural loss is therefore mainly\\nbackend pressure from dmem waits, especially loads. Today a load in MEM with\\n`io_dmemReady=0` freezes IF, ID, and EX even when the younger instruction in\\nID/EX is an independent simple ALU operation whose operands are already\\navailable and whose execution has no memory or control-flow side effects.\\n\\nPrior attempts to attack this bottleneck by replaying load data or adding\\nbroader store buffering reduced cycles but cost too much Fmax or placement\\nquality. A smaller structural decoupling point is more promising: let EX do\\none cycle of useful independent simple-ALU work while the older MEM load or\\nnonbufferable store is waiting, but keep retirement strictly in order. This\\ntargets the exact full-pipeline freeze caused by random dmem backpressure\\nwithout adding load data to EX, another replay table, another store-buffer\\nentry, a branch predictor, or a general superscalar backend.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is a fast single-issue design with the largest proven\\nwins already in place: cold DIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU\\nsplit improved Fmax, the one-entry pending-store slot hides some store-only\\ndmem stalls, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random imem stalls. The remaining structural loss is therefore mainly\\nbackend pressure from dmem waits, especially loads. Today a load in MEM with\\n`io_dmemReady=0` freezes IF, ID, and EX even when the younger instruction in\\nID/EX is an independent simp...\n  Line 72: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-065-r24s1\", \"title\": \"Replay-Fed Memory Plus ADDI Pair Issue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is a very strong single-issue design. The large wins\\nalready come from removing cold DIV/REM from the hot EX path, splitting the\\nALU hot/cold mux, adding a one-deep pending-store slot, segmenting the RVFI\\norder counter, and using a two-bank registered instruction replay predictor\\nto hide most random imem stalls. Recent structural attempts show that broad\\nbuffers, data replay tables, and general dual issue are too costly or too\\ndifficult, but they also point at the remaining ceiling: only one instruction\\ncan retire per cycle even when CoreMark presents simple pointer updates next\\nto memory operations.\\n\\nCoreMark's list, matrix, state, and stack code is dense with patterns such as\\na load or store followed by an independent ADDI pointer/index/stack update.\\nA full two-wide machine is risky, but this very narrow pair class uses only\\nthe existing single dmem access plus one younger simple immediate add. It\\ntargets a common structural bubble source without adding another memory port,\\nanother replay table, a load-data-to-EX bypass, a branch predictor, or a\\nsecond general ALU issue path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is a very strong single-issue design. The large wins\\nalready come from removing cold DIV/REM from the hot EX path, splitting the\\nALU hot/cold mux, adding a one-deep pending-store slot, segmenting the RVFI\\norder counter, and using a two-bank registered instruction replay predictor\\nto hide most random imem stalls. Recent structural attempts show that broad\\nbuffers, data replay tables, and general dual issue are too costly or too\\ndifficult, but they also point at the remaining ceiling: only one instruction\\ncan retire per cycle even when CoreMark presents simple pointer updates next\\nto memory operations.\\n\\nCoreMark's list, matrix, state, and stack code is dense with patter...\n  Line 74: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-068-r25s1\", \"title\": \"Single-Entry Last-Load Replay Latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural wins that have survived the\\nFPGA gate: the one-deep pending-store slot hides some store backpressure, and\\nthe two-bank registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall bubbles. The remaining structural stalls are mainly data-side load\\nwaits. When a normal-RAM load reaches MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, the\\nwhole in-order backend freezes even if the same aligned word was just read by\\nan earlier load.\\n\\nLarger load replay attempts proved that this opportunity is real because they\\nreduced CoreMark cycles, but their table lookups, tag compares, and store\\nupdate paths cost too much Fmax. The next structural bet should keep only the\\ncheapest useful form of that idea: one last-load replay entry. CoreMark's\\nstack, list, state, and matrix kernels often reload nearby or identical words\\nacross short loops, so a single MRU word can catch some random dStall cases\\nwithout a table, replacement policy, associative search, or load-data path\\ninto EX.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural wins that have survived the\\nFPGA gate: the one-deep pending-store slot hides some store backpressure, and\\nthe two-bank registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall bubbles. The remaining structural stalls are mainly data-side load\\nwaits. When a normal-RAM load reaches MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, the\\nwhole in-order backend freezes even if the same aligned word was just read by\\nan earlier load.\\n\\nLarger load replay attempts proved that this opportunity is real because they\\nreduced CoreMark cycles, but their table lookups, tag compares, and store\\nupdate paths cost too much Fmax. The next structural bet should keep only the\\ncheapest useful form of that idea: one last-load replay entry. CoreMark's\\nstack, list, state, and matrix ke...","metadata":{"matches":25,"truncated":false},"title":"\"category\"\\s*:\\s*\"structural\"","time":{"start":1778836789578,"end":1778836789599}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0bfc3983a645a890016a06e5356e00819b8055339b3a41c058"}},"id":"prt_e2aef592e001ImwyAliMxIqTpI","sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","messageID":"msg_e2aef2ba8001K7KjSJXLttbFok"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778836789742,"sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_G2qQUE0DupjUj8svUn9maIAa","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"stall|bubble|flush|branch|load-use|forward|bypass|pipeline|structural","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/experiments","include":"log.jsonl"},"output":"Found 74 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 1: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-001\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest (no implementation change)\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"Used by the orchestrator integration test (--from-hypothesis path).\\nMeasures the unchanged baseline through the full eval pipeline so the\\nresulting log entry locks in the first fitness number for phase 8.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"No RTL changes \\u2014 exercises the worktree -> emit_verilog -> formal ->\\ncosim -> fpga path on the existing rtl/*.sv to confirm the orchestrator\\nproduces a structured log entry.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 0, \"confidence\": \"high\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Sentinel \\u2014 no real change. skip_implementation:true below bypasses\\nthe implementation agent, so the file path here is metadata only;\\nit just has to satisfy the schema's rtl/<file> pattern. core.sv is\\nalways present and untouched.\\n\"}], \"skip_implementation\": true, \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 301.04, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 135.21, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"lut4\": 9880, \"ff\": 1858, \"seeds\": [121.29, 135.21, 136.07], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T02:16:49.012016+00:00\"}\n\n  Line 2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Move DIV/REM off the single-cycle ALU path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU places RV32M multiplication and division in the same\\ncombinational EX-stage result mux as common integer operations. A 32-bit\\ncombinational divider is very likely to dominate LUT depth and area on\\nthe Tang Nano 20K, yet CoreMark's timed region is matrix/list/state code\\nthat is multiply-heavy and effectively division-free. Keeping DIV/REM\\nsingle-cycle optimizes an uncommon path while lowering Fmax for every\\ninstruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU places RV32M multiplication and division in the same\\ncombinational EX-stage result mux as common integer operations. A 32-bit\\ncombinational divider is very likely to dominate LUT depth and area on\\nthe Tang Nano 20K, yet CoreMark's timed region is matrix/list/state code\\nthat is multiply-heavy and effectively division-free. Keeping DIV/REM\\nsingle-cycle optimizes an uncommon path while lowering Fmax for every\\ninstruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases in rtl/alu.sv with a\\nsmall iterative RV32M divide/remainder unit and add an EX-stage interlock\\nonly when one of those opcodes is in flight. Leave ADD/SUB/logic/shift,\\nbranches, loads/stores, jumps, and MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the current\\nsingle-cycle path. The timed CoreMark loop should pay no meaningful IPC\\npenalty because it does not execute DIV/REM, while the FPGA critical path\\nshould shrink from divider-through-ALU-mux to the existing add/compare/mul\\npaths. Preserve exact RV32M divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 semantics and\\nkeep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions for formal builds.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 15, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational DIV/REM hardware from the normal ALU result\\nmux, retaining single-cycle non-divide ALU and MUL behavior plus the\\nexisting A...\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Elastic Fetch Skid Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF/ID handoff with an elastic 2-entry fetch skid\\nbuffer that decouples instruction fetch from decode. The fetch side advances\\nthe PC and captures sequential instructions whenever io_imemReady is high\\nand the buffer is not full, even while ID/EX is held by a dmem stall. The\\ndecode side consumes one buffered instruction per non-stalled cycle. Preserve\\nthe baseline zero-latency behavior with an empty-buffer bypass from\\nio_imemData to decode when the backend is ready and imem is ready, so the\\nno-stall path and branch target...\n  Line 4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Backward-Branch Taken Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves all branches and jumps in EX and fetches strictly pc+4 until that resolution, so every taken loop branch in CoreMark pays a two-instruction wrong-path flush. CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by short backward conditional branches whose direction is highly biased toward taken. A small static predictor can remove most loop-back redirect bubbles without adding a learned-state table, RAM, or speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves all branches and jumps in EX and fetches strictly pc+4 until that resolution, so every taken loop branch in CoreMark pays a two-instruction wrong-path flush. CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by short backward conditional branches whose direction is highly biased toward taken. A small static predictor can remove most loop-back redirect bubbles without adding a learned-state table, RAM, or speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a decode-light static branch predictor in IF that recognizes B-type instructions and predicts backward branches taken when the sign bit of the branch immediate is set. Compute the B immediate in IF, redirect the next PC to pc+imm for predicted-taken backward branches, and carry predicted_taken plus predicted_target through IF/ID and ID/EX. In EX, compare the actual branch result and target against the prediction, redirect only on mispredict, and set rvfi pc_wdata to the actual next PC as today. JAL/JALR remain resolved in EX to keep the predictor slot focused on conditional branches and avoid adding a return-address stack or BTB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8.0, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles with predicted_taken and predicted_target fields so EX can distinguish correct predictions f...\n  Line 5: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-004-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Single Shared RV32M Multiplier\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU builds three independent wide products every cycle:\\nsigned*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. Those products feed\\nthe same EX-stage result mux as ordinary integer operations, so their area and\\nrouting pressure can lower Fmax even when most instructions are ADD/logic,\\nbranches, loads, and stores. CoreMark does use MUL, so making multiplication\\nmulti-cycle risks losing IPC; however all RV32M multiply variants can be\\nimplemented by one signedness-controlled 33x33 product with no latency change.\\nThis is a narrower and lower-risk micro-optimization than the prior failed\\nDIV/REM interlock attempt because it leaves divider behavior, pipeline stalls,\\nand RVFI ordering untouched.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU builds three independent wide products every cycle:\\nsigned*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. Those products feed\\nthe same EX-stage result mux as ordinary integer operations, so their area and\\nrouting pressure can lower Fmax even when most instructions are ADD/logic,\\nbranches, loads, and stores. CoreMark does use MUL, so making multiplication\\nmulti-cycle risks losing IPC; however all RV32M multiply variants can be\\nimplemented by one signedness-controlled 33x33 product with no latency change.\\nThis is a narrower and lower-risk micro-optimization than the prior failed\\nDIV/REM interlock attempt because it leaves divider behavior, pipeline stalls,\\nand RVFI ordering untouched.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three unconditional multiplier expressions in rtl/alu.sv with a\\nsingle shared 33-bit-by-33-bit product. For MUL, zero-extend both operands and\\nuse the low 32 bits; for MULH, sign-extend both operands; f