putting\\nload data on the EX operand path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add an early JALR redirect path in ID for cases where the rs1 base is already\\navailable from the regfile or same-cycle WB write-first bypass. ID computes\\nthe JALR target from rs1 plus the I-immediate, verifies the aligned target,\\nand sends a PC redirect to IF in the same cycle the JALR enters ID/EX. The\\nID/EX prediction metadata is overwritten with that resolved target so the\\nlater EX-stage instance of the same JALR does not redirect a second time.\\nWhen rs1 is waiting on an older instruction in ID/EX or EX/MEM, or when the\\nbackend is ...\n  Line 13: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-006-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Return-Address Stack JALR Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already predicts backward conditional branches and\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, which removes most loop-back and direct-call\\ncontrol bubbles. The remaining obvious predictor hole is JALR: every return\\nstill resolves in EX and forces the same wrong-path flush even though CoreMark\\nuses direct calls paired with register-indirect returns. A tiny return-address\\nstack targets that specific loss with lower timing risk than another indexed\\nbranch table, because IF only needs to recognize the canonical return\\nencoding and mux in a registered stack top.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already predicts backward conditional branches and\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, which removes most loop-back and direct-call\\ncontrol bubbles. The remaining obvious predictor hole is JALR: every return\\nstill resolves in EX and forces the same wrong-path flush even though CoreMark\\nuses direct calls paired with register-indirect returns. A tiny return-address\\nstack targets that specific loss with lower timing risk than another indexed\\nbranch table, because IF only needs to recognize the canonical return\\nencoding and mux in a registered stack top.\\n\", \"hypo