 The remaining micro-opt slot should therefore\\navoid new predictors, queues, or arithmetic units and instead trim logic that\\nis already proven unnecessary on the hot pipeline boundary.\\n\\nThe EX/MEM bundle still carries several fields that do not add architectural\\ninformation. `write_data` is always assigned the same post-forwarded rs2\\nvalue as `rs2_val`, while `branch_taken` and `branch_target` are registered\\nbut never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, forwarding, or hazard logic. Those bits\\nadd about 65 EX/MEM flops plus mux/load on the EX branch-target and rs2 data\\npaths, exactly where the post-DIV design is trying to preserve Fmax. Pruning\\nthis redundant metadata should not change CoreMark cycles, bus behavior,\\nbranch prediction, RVFI ordering, or any ISA-visible result.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already captured the large available wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is out of the one-cycle ALU path, and the pending store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. The remaining micro-opt slot should therefore\\navoid new predictors, queues, or arithmetic units and instead trim logic that\\nis already proven unnecessary on the hot pipeline boundary.\\n\\nThe EX/MEM bundle still carries several fields that do not add architectural\\ninformation. `write_data` is always assigned the same post-forwarded rs2\\nvalue as `rs2_val`, while `branch_taken` and `branch_target` are registered\\nbut never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, forwarding, or hazard logic. Those bits\\nadd about 65 EX/MEM flops plus mux/load on the EX branch-target and rs2 data\\npaths, exactly where the post-DIV design is trying to preserve Fmax. Pruning\\nthis redundant metadata should not change CoreMark cycl...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-023-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-imem path whenever the external\\nbus is ready, and consult a tiny cached copy only on cycles where imem would\\notherwise stall. CoreMark's timed code repeatedly executes the same matrix,\\nlist, state, CRC, and loop-control instructions over a 10-iteration run, so\\neven a small direct-mapped instruction replay table should turn many random\\niStall bubbles into normal fetch cycles after warmup.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-im...\n  Line 31: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-024-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Adder Static Control Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor features are the simple ones that already paid\\nfor themselves: backward conditional branches are predicted taken and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned direction tables and return\\npredictors reduced CoreMark cycles slightly, but each added enough IF-state,\\ncomparison, or EX-to-IF update routing to lose more Fmax than it recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is therefore to make the profitable\\nstatic predictor cheaper rather than adding more prediction state. Today\\n`if_stage` forms branch and JAL targets with two separate `pc + imm` adders\\nand uses the full adder results for target-alignment gating before choosing\\nthe next PC. Since B- and J-type immediates always have bit 0 clear, the\\ntarget alignment test can be derived from the encoded imm[1] bit and the PC\\nlow bits without waiting for a 32-bit target adder. Branch and JAL are also\\nmutually exclusive opcodes, so one selected-immediate target adder is enough.\\n\\nThis attacks predictor-path Fmax with no intended CoreMark cycle-count risk:\\nthe set of predicted conditional branches and direct JALs remains the same\\nas the current best design for all aligned PCs, and EX still repairs any\\nwrong prediction through the existing resolved-next-PC mismatch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor features are the simple ones that already paid\\nfor themselves: backward conditional branches are predicted taken and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned direction tables and return\\npredictors reduced CoreMark cycles slightly, but each added enough IF-state,\\ncomparison, or EX-to-IF update routing to lose more Fmax than it recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is therefore to make the profitable\\nstatic predictor cheaper rather than adding more prediction state. Today\\n`if_stage` forms bra...\n  Line 32: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-025-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Segmented RVFI Order Counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large architectural wins:\\nDIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the one-deep store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. Recent micro-opt attempts that reshaped hot\\ndatapaths, predictor target logic, or EX/MEM metadata mostly lost Fmax to\\nplacement noise or new muxing. The next micro-opt should therefore target a\\nguaranteed always-present timing structure without changing IPC.\\n\\nThe top-level RVFI order counter is currently a single 64-bit increment:\\n`rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'b1` on every retirement. The FPGA\\nbenchmark wrapper intentionally retains RVFI fanout by XOR-reducing the RVFI\\noutputs into an LED, so this counter cannot be assumed dead in synthesis.\\nA 64-bit carry self-loop is not architectural work for the CoreMark datapath,\\nbut it can still consume carry-chain/routing budget and compete with the hot\\npipeline for median Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large architectural wins:\\nDIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the one-deep store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. Recent micro-opt attempts that reshaped hot\\ndatapaths, predictor target logic, or EX/MEM metadata mostly lost Fmax to\\nplacement noise or new muxing. The next micro-opt should therefore target a\\nguaranteed always-present timing structure without changing IPC.\\n\\nThe top-level RVFI order counter is currently a single 64-bit increment:\\n`rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'b1` on every retirement. The FPGA\\nbenchmark wrapper intentionally retains RVFI fanout by XOR-reducing the RVFI\\noutputs into an LED, so this counter cannot be assumed dead in synthesis.\\nA 64-bit carry self-loop is not architectural work for the CoreMark datapath,\\nbut it can still consume carry-chain/routing budget and compete with the hot\\npipeline for median Fmax.\\n\", ...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-026-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Narr