 Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-027-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Small Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor changes that have paid so far are the ones that avoid broad\\nlearned direction state: backward conditional branches and direct JAL targets\\nare predicted in IF, while the larger branch tables and return predictors\\nlost more Fmax than their cycle savings recovered. The one exception with a\\nlarge cycle signal was the stall-only instruction replay experiment: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles substantially by serving hot-loop instructions during random\\nimem stalls, but its 256-entry full-tag table pulled median Fmax below the\\ncurrent best.\\n\\nThat result suggests a narrower predictor, not a new branch-direction table.\\nCoreMark's timed region repeatedly executes a relatively small hot text\\nfootprint, so a smaller replay predictor should retain much of the random\\niStall hiding while shortening the IF tag/data muxes that hurt the 256-entry\\nattempt. This stays in the predictor slot because the table predicts the\\ninstruction word for the current PC only when the external fetch bus would\\notherwise provide no word; normal ready fetches still use live imem data.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor changes that have paid so far are the ones that avoid broad\\nlearned direction state: backward conditional branches and direct JAL targets\\nare predicted in IF, while the larger branch tables and return predictors\\nlost more Fmax than their cycle savings recovered. The one exception with a\\nlarge cycle signal was the stall-only instruction replay experiment: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles substantially by serving hot-loop instructions during random\\nimem stalls, but its 256-entry full-tag table pulled median Fmax below the\\ncurrent best.\\n\\nThat result suggests a narrower predictor, not a new branch-direction table.\\nCoreMark's timed region repeatedly executes a relatively small hot text\\nfootprint, so a smaller replay predictor should reta...\n  Line 35: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-028-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Byte-Segmented RVFI Order Counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a pure timing micro-optimization: replacing\\nthe monolithic 64-bit RVFI order increment with four 16-bit counter chunks.\\nThat change did not alter CoreMark cycles, memory behavior, branch\\nprediction, divider latency, or retirement semantics, but it raised median\\nFmax enough to become the new best fitness. This strongly suggests the\\nRVFI-order feedback path is still visible to synthesis and placement because\\nthe FPGA wrapper keeps RVFI outputs alive.\\n\\nThe current implementation still has a 16-bit carry-chain self-loop in the\\nlow order chunk, and each retirement toggles that chunk. A narrower exact\\nchunk size is the most direct follow-up in the micro_opt slot: it targets the\\nsame measured timing structure that just paid off, avoids another datapath or\\npredictor rewrite, and should leave CoreMark iterations/cycle unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a pure timing micro-optimization: replacing\\nthe monolithic 64-bit RVFI order increment with four 16-bit counter chunks.\\nThat change did not alter CoreMark cycles, memory behavior, branch\\nprediction, divider latency, or retirement semantics, but it raised median\\nFmax enough to become the new best fitness. This strongly suggests the\\nRVFI-order feedback path is still visible to synthesis and placement because\\nthe FPGA wrapper keeps RVFI outputs alive.\\n\\nThe current implementation still has a 16-bit carry-chain self-loop in the\\nlow order chunk, and each retirement toggles that chunk. A narrower exact\\nchunk size is the most direct follow-up in the micro_opt slot: it targets the\\nsame measured timing structure that just paid off, avoids another datapath or\\npredictor rewrite, and should leave CoreMark iterations/cycle unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the RVFI order counter in `rtl/core.sv` from four 16-bit chunks t...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-029-r12s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Load Replay Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already captured the two strongest proven wins:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and the one-deep pending-store\\nslot hides store-only dmem backpressure while preserving the ready-store fast\\npath. The next structural bottleneck is therefore load-side dmem stalls. A\\nload whose word was recently read still freezes the whole pipeline whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even though CoreMark repeatedly touches a compact 6 KB\\nworking set across list, matrix, state, stack, and CRC code.\\n\\nInstruction replay experiments showed that serving repeated hot accesses\\nduring random bus stalls can cut many cycles, but putting a wide replay table\\nin IF cost too much Fmax. A smaller load replay structure in MEM attacks the\\nsame random-stall problem on the data side, where the lookup is off the\\nbranch/JAL predictor path and does not add load data to the EX operand muxes.\\nThis is a structural stall-hiding change, not a broader predictor or\\narithmetic rewrite.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already captured the two strongest proven wins:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and the one-deep pending-store\\nslot hides store-only dmem backpressure while preserving the ready-store fast\\npath. The next structural bottleneck is therefore load-side dmem stalls. A\\nload whose word was recently read still freezes the whole pipeline whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even though CoreMark repeatedly touches a compact 6 KB\\nworking set across list, matrix, state, stack, and CRC code.\\n\\nInstruction replay experiments showed that serving repeated hot accesses\\nduring random bus stalls can cut many cycles, but putting a wide rep