{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1778836791274,"sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","part":{"id":"prt_e2aef5fe8001BqWyiJ8MVnWJsA","messageID":"msg_e2aef5b5a001rnth245WLJRZyG","sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","snapshot":"1d9dbb277b18d73c1b6ac9048206bd0aaaeca881","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1778836828372,"sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","part":{"id":"prt_e2aefec56001wMorVwXCzEQZJ7","messageID":"msg_e2aef5b5a001rnth245WLJRZyG","sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","type":"text","text":"The strongest non-frontend structural signal in adjacent history is store-side decoupling: hide store-only `dmem` stalls without reintroducing fetch-path timing risk. I’m checking which version looked most timing-local so the bench hypothesis stays focused.","time":{"start":1778836827222,"end":1778836828368},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"msg_0ff51306bf3497a2016a06e55b1ef8819985b0322fd6b58731","phase":"commentary"}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778836828424,"sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_HjeorAj86FzxobGsraCVSKtT","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"hyp-20260428-047-r18s1|hyp-20260428-062-r23s1|hyp-20260427-005-r2s1|hyp-20260428-008-r5s1","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/experiments","include":"log.jsonl"},"output":"Found 4 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-005-r2s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-to-EX Load-Use Bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still has the textbook structural load-use gap: a load in EX always forces the immediately following consumer to wait one extra cycle, even when the dmem bus is ready and the loaded word is already available combinationally in MEM. CoreMark's list, matrix, and state kernels contain many load-to-use pairs, so this fixed bubble directly lowers iterations per cycle. The recent predictor win improved control flow without changing the backend; the next most promising structural angle is to add the missing MEM-to-EX load-data path and remove the mandatory interlock while preserving the single-issue in-order retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still has the textbook structural load-use gap: a load in EX always forces the immediately following consumer to wait one extra cycle, even when the dmem bus is ready and the loaded word is already available combinationally in MEM. CoreMark's list, matrix, and state kernels contain many load-to-use pairs, so this fixed bubble directly lowers iterations per cycle. The recent predictor win improved control flow without changing the backend; the next most promising structural angle is to add the missing MEM-to-EX load-data path and remove the mandatory interlock while preserving the single-issue in-order retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a direct load-result forwarding path from the MEM stage's existing sign/zero-extended load_data to the EX operand muxes. When an instruction in EX consumes the rd of a load currently in MEM, select this MEM load result for rs1 and/or rs2 instead of stalling for the value to appear in MEM/WB. If io_dmemReady is low for that load, keep the current dmem hold behavior and suppress any younger EX redirect until the load data is valid; once ready, the dependent instruction executes in the same cycle the load ...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-008-r5s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Store-Data Forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core still treats every immediate load-use match as a\\nfull one-cycle interlock, including the common case where the younger\\ninstruction is a STORE that only needs the loaded register as store data\\n(rs2), not as the address base (rs1). That bubble is structurally avoidable:\\nstore data is not consumed by the EX ALU, and the actual dmem write data is\\nformed one stage later in MEM. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass showed\\nthat CoreMark has meaningful load-use pressure, but it lost too much Fmax by\\nrouting dmem load data into the EX operand muxes. A late store-data bypass\\ntargets a useful subset of those bubbles while keeping load data off the ALU,\\nbranch compare, and JALR critical paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core still treats every immediate load-use match as a\\nfull one-cycle interlock, including the common case where the younger\\ninstruction is a STORE that only needs the loaded register as store data\\n(rs2), not as the address base (rs1). That bubble is structurally avoidable:\\nstore data is not consumed by the EX ALU, and the actual dmem write data is\\nformed one stage later in MEM. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass showed\\nthat CoreMark has meaningful load-use pressure, but it lost too much Fmax by\\nrouting dmem load data into the EX operand muxes. A late store-data bypass\\ntargets a useful subset of those bubbles while keeping load data off the ALU,\\nbranch compare, and JALR critical paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Allow a legal STORE immediately after a LOAD to proceed without the normal\\nload-use bubble when the load destination matches only the STORE rs2 data\\nfield and not the STORE rs1 address field. The store computes its address in\\nEX as usual, carries its rs2 register number into MEM, and selects the final\\nstore data in MEM from the MEM/WB writeback value when that older i...\n  Line 54: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-047-r18s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-Exact Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has strong timing wins from the registered instruction\\nreplay predictor, segmented RVFI order counter, pending-store slot, and\\nhot/cold ALU split. The remaining structural improvement should therefore\\navoid another cache, queue, predictor table, or load-data forwarding path that\\ncould disturb Fmax.\\n\\nThe load-use interlock is still structurally conservative. It stalls whenever\\na load in ID/EX has a nonzero destination matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2\\nbitfield of the IF/ID instruction. That is correct for true consumers, but it\\ncreates false bubbles for instructions where one or both fields are not\\nsource operands: OP-IMM and LOAD do not read rs2, JAL/LUI/AUIPC read neither,\\nFENCE/EBREAK read neither, and JALR reads only rs1. CoreMark's optimized code\\nhas dense immediate arithmetic, address-generation loads, calls, and loop\\ncontrol, so these false one-cycle bubbles are a plausible remaining IPC loss.\\n\\nA prior operand-aware interlock attempt broke selftest, so this hypothesis is\\ndeliberately conservative: decode source-use exactly for known legal\\ninstruction classes, keep reserved or unknown encodings on the current\\nboth-source stall policy, and do not add any MEM-to-EX load-data path. The\\ngoal is to recover only provably false load-use stalls while leaving all true\\nload consumers, dmem waits, branch recovery, divider interlocks, and RVFI\\nordering unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has strong timing wins from the registered instruction\\nreplay predictor, segmented RVFI order counter, pending-store slot, and\\nhot/cold ALU split. The remaining structural improvement should therefore\\navoid another cache, queue, predictor table, or load-data forwarding path that\\ncould disturb Fmax.\\n\\nThe load-use interlock is still structurally conservative. It stalls whenever\\na load in ID/EX h...\n  Line 69: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-062-r23s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Wait Simple-ALU Skid Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is a fast single-issue design with the largest proven\\nwins already in place: cold DIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU\\nsplit improved Fmax, the one-entry pending-store slot hides some store-only\\ndmem stalls, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random imem stalls. The remaining structural loss is therefore mainly\\nbackend pressure from dmem waits, especially loads. Today a load in MEM with\\n`io_dmemReady=0` freezes IF, ID, and EX even when the younger instruction in\\nID/EX is an independent simple ALU operation whose operands are already\\navailable and whose execution has no memory or control-flow side effects.\\n\\nPrior attempts to attack this bottleneck by replaying load data or adding\\nbroader store buffering reduced cycles but cost too much Fmax or placement\\nquality. A smaller structural decoupling point is more promising: let EX do\\none cycle of useful independent simple-ALU work while the older MEM load or\\nnonbufferable store is waiting, but keep retirement strictly in order. This\\ntargets the exact full-pipeline freeze caused by random dmem backpressure\\nwithout adding load data to EX, another replay table, another store-buffer\\nentry, a branch predictor, or a general superscalar backend.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is a fast single-issue design with the largest proven\\nwins already in place: cold DIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU\\nsplit improved Fmax, the one-entry pending-store slot hides some store-only\\ndmem stalls, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random imem stalls. The remaining structural loss is therefore mainly\\nbackend pressure from dmem waits, especially loads. Today a load in MEM with\\n`io_dmemReady=0` freezes IF, ID, and EX even when the younger instruction in\\nID/EX is an independent simp...","metadata":{"matches":4,"truncated":false},"title":"hyp-20260428-047-r18s1|hyp-20260428-062-r23s1|hyp-20260427-005-r2s1|hyp-20260428-008-r5s1","time":{"start":1778836828391,"end":1778836828423}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0ff51306bf3497a2016a06e55c47fc81998ac8e5a66b8e2450"}},"id":"prt_e2aeff0e6001p2IlMsDlAjpi2X","sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","messageID":"msg_e2aef5b5a001rnth245WLJRZyG"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778836828425,"sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_cQcl7UNBqdLyxFl4EQzy9L7J","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"hyp-20260428-014-r7s1|hyp-20260428-017-r8s1|hyp-20260428-041-r16s1|hyp-20260428-053-r20s1|hyp-20260428-056-r21s1|hyp-20260428-068-r25s1","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/experiments","include":"log.jsonl"},"output":"Found 6 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-014-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Single-Entry Retiring Store Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gained a large Fmax and LUT win by moving DIV/REM out\\nof the hot EX path, so the next structural bottleneck is less likely to be\\nraw datapath area and more likely to be cycles lost to the random dmem\\nhandshake model. Today every dmem-not-ready cycle for a STORE freezes the\\nwhole pipeline, even though the store address, replicated data, byte mask,\\nand RVFI memory metadata are already known in MEM and the store produces no\\nregister result for younger ALU/control instructions. A prior two-entry\\nstore buffer proved the idea through formal and cosim but failed placement\\non the larger pre-DIV RTL. A smaller one-entry RAM-store buffer should retain\\nmost of the useful stall hiding while keeping the arbitration, state, and\\nplacement pressure low enough for the post-DIV design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gained a large Fmax and LUT win by moving DIV/REM out\\nof the hot EX path, so the next structural bottleneck is less likely to be\\nraw datapath area and more likely to be cycles lost to the random dmem\\nhandshake model. Today every dmem-not-ready cycle for a STORE freezes the\\nwhole pipeline, even though the store address, replicated data, byte mask,\\nand RVFI memory metadata are already known in MEM and the store produces no\\nregister result for younger ALU/control instructions. A prior two-entry\\nstore buffer proved the idea through formal and cosim but failed placement\\non the larger pre-DIV RTL. A smaller one-entry RAM-store buffer should retain\\nmost of the useful stall hiding while keeping the arbitration, state, and\\nplacement pressure low enough for the post-DIV design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one in-order retiring store slot for legal aligned stores to normal RAM\\nonly. When such a store reaches MEM and io_dmemReady is low, capture its\\nbyte address, replicated write data, byte mask, and RV...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-017-r8s1\", \"title\": \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot With Direct Fast Path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\\n\\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\\n\\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot that is used only on a...\n  Line 48: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-041-r16s1\", \"title\": \"Same-Cycle Store Slot Refill\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the two structural wins that matter most:\\nregistered instruction replay hides many random iStall cycles, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot lets a stalled normal-RAM store retire before\\nthe external dmem write is accepted. The remaining store-side bottleneck is\\nnarrower than a full second buffer entry. When the pending slot is valid, the\\nMEM stage currently treats every younger memory operation as blocked even on\\nthe exact cycle where `io_dmemReady` will drain the older buffered store.\\n\\nThat leaves a small but avoidable bubble for bursts of normal-RAM stores:\\nthe older store uses the physical dmem port, while the younger store already\\nhas its address, replicated data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible memory fields\\navailable in MEM and could refill the just-drained slot. A previous\\ntwo-entry FIFO targeted the same store-burst pressure but failed placement.\\nThe existing `store_slot` state machine already supports simultaneous drain\\nand enqueue, so the lower-risk structural angle is to expose that fast refill\\ncase without adding another entry or touching the IF replay path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the two structural wins that matter most:\\nregistered instruction replay hides many random iStall cycles, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot lets a stalled normal-RAM store retire before\\nthe external dmem write is accepted. The remaining store-side bottleneck is\\nnarrower than a full second buffer entry. When the pending slot is valid, the\\nMEM stage currently treats every younger memory operation as blocked even on\\nthe exact cycle where `io_dmemReady` will drain the older buffered store.\\n\\nThat leaves a small but avoidable bubble for bursts of normal-RAM stores:\\nthe older store uses the physical dmem port, while the younger store already\\nhas its address, replicated da...\n  Line 60: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-053-r20s1\", \"title\": \"Same-Word Store Slot Coalescing\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already includes the structural wins that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot for normal-RAM store stalls\\nand a registered instruction replay table for random iStall. Broader store\\nbuffering attempts found the right bottleneck but were too expensive:\\ntwo-entry pending-store designs either failed placement, and the same-cycle\\nrefill variant also failed placement. That suggests the next structural\\nstore-side step should add capacity only for cases the existing single slot\\ncan handle locally.\\n\\nCoreMark updates list nodes, matrix cells, stack frames, and scalar state with\\nshort bursts of stores. When a pending normal-RAM store is already occupying\\nthe slot and dmem is not ready, a younger store to the same aligned word does\\nnot need a second physical buffer entry. It can be merged into the pending\\nslot because loads and MMIO are still held behind the slot, and there is no\\narchitectural observer between the two normal-RAM stores. This targets the\\nsame store-burst pressure as the failed two-entry FIFO, but with one address\\ncompare and one byte-mask merge instead of another queue entry or a wider\\ndmem arbiter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already includes the structural wins that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot for normal-RAM store stalls\\nand a registered instruction replay table for random iStall. Broader store\\nbuffering attempts found the right bottleneck but were too expensive:\\ntwo-entry pending-store designs either failed placement, and the same-cycle\\nrefill variant also failed placement. That suggests the next structural\\nstore-side step should add capacity only for cases the existing single slot\\ncan handle locally.\\n\\nCoreMark updates list nodes, matrix cells, stack frames, and scalar state with\\nshort bursts of stores. When a ...\n  Line 63: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-056-r21s1\", \"title\": \"Pending Store Slot Load Forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural pieces that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot hides normal-RAM store\\nbackpressure, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random iStall bubbles. The structural experiments since then show the\\nboundary clearly. Larger store buffers and same-cycle refill failed\\nplacement, same-word store coalescing did not reduce CoreMark cycles, and\\ndata replay tables recovered some load-stall cycles but cost too much Fmax.\\n\\nThe remaining store-slot bottleneck is narrower. When a normal-RAM store has\\nretired into the pending slot, the core currently blocks every younger memory\\noperation until the physical write drains, even if the younger operation is a\\nload of bytes that are already fully present in the pending store entry. That\\ncase does not require a second store-buffer entry or a dmem access: in\\nprogram order the store is already older and retired, so a same-word covered\\nload should read the store slot's bytes. CoreMark's stack, list, and state\\ncode can produce store/load reuse on nearby words, especially under random\\ndmem stalls that keep the slot valid for multiple cycles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural pieces that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot hides normal-RAM store\\nbackpressure, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random iStall bubbles. The structural experiments since then show the\\nboundary clearly. Larger store buffers and same-cycle refill failed\\nplacement, same-word store coalescing did not reduce CoreMark cycles, and\\ndata replay tables recovered some load-stall cycles but cost too much Fmax.\\n\\nThe remaining store-slot bottleneck is narrower. When a normal-RAM store has\\nretired into the pending slot, the core currentl...\n  Line 74: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-068-r25s1\", \"title\": \"Single-Entry Last-Load Replay Latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural wins that have survived the\\nFPGA gate: the one-deep pending-store slot hides some store backpressure, and\\nthe two-bank registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall bubbles. The remaining structural stalls are mainly data-side load\\nwaits. When a normal-RAM load reaches MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, the\\nwhole in-order backend freezes even if the same aligned word was just read by\\nan earlier load.\\n\\nLarger load replay attempts proved that this opportunity is real because they\\nreduced CoreMark cycles, but their table lookups, tag compares, and store\\nupdate paths cost too much Fmax. The next structural bet should keep only the\\ncheapest useful form of that idea: one last-load replay entry. CoreMark's\\nstack, list, state, and matrix kernels often reload nearby or identical words\\nacross short loops, so a single MRU word can catch some random dStall cases\\nwithout a table, replacement policy, associative search, or load-data path\\ninto EX.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural wins that have survived the\\nFPGA gate: the one-deep pending-store slot hides some store backpressure, and\\nthe two-bank registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall bubbles. The remaining structural stalls are mainly data-side load\\nwaits. When a normal-RAM load reaches MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, the\\nwhole in-order backend freezes even if the same aligned word was just read by\\nan earlier load.\\n\\nLarger load replay attempts proved that this opportunity is real because they\\nreduced CoreMark cycles, but their table lookups, tag compares, and store\\nupdate paths cost too much Fmax. The next structural bet should keep only the\\ncheapest useful form of that idea: one last-load replay entry. CoreMark's\\nstack, list, state, and matrix ke...","metadata":{"matches":6,"truncated":false},"title":"hyp-20260428-014-r7s1|hyp-20260428-017-r8s1|hyp-20260428-041-r16s1|hyp-20260428-053-r20s1|hyp-20260428-056-r21s1|hyp-20260428-068-r25s1","time":{"start":1778836828389,"end":1778836828425}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0ff51306bf3497a2016a06e55c47ec8199a59bfb2061eb2e38"}},"id":"prt_e2aeff0d40018jgqCsPWIMfSU5","sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","messageID":"msg_e2aef5b5a001rnth245WLJRZyG"}}
