{"type":"text","timestamp":1778836934128,"sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","part":{"id":"prt_e2af18b3b00143YHhOKs17PQsJ","messageID":"msg_e2af14345001ouSuVH5bJzQ3Hr","sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","type":"text","text":"Writing the hypothesis YAML now under `cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/` with the backend-local store-slot proposal.","time":{"start":1778836933435,"end":1778836934127},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"msg_082df3819a148ae5016a06e5c552248199a4cdc035c6fafd3a","phase":"commentary"}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778836944908,"sessionID":"ses_1d51113c0ffengOn9ellsHEzV4","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_gpvQSvKUiejdvR12ulaemJxQ","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r2s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r2s1\n+title: \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The current bench core's biggest proven gain came from a backend-only timing\n+  change: moving DIV/REM off the one-cycle EX path. The recent failed branch\n+  prediction attempt showed the opposite boundary on this RTL: pulling more\n+  decode/target work into the frontend can erase the IPC gain with Fmax loss.\n+\n+  The next structural hypothesis should therefore stay off the fetch path and\n+  attack the other obvious whole-core bubble source. Today any `io_dmemReady=0`\n+  cycle on a STORE freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM even though the store address,\n+  replicated data, and byte mask are already known in MEM, and CoreMark does a\n+  steady stream of normal-RAM stores to stack, list, matrix, and state data.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot with a direct ready-store fast\n+  path. When an aligned STORE to normal RAM `[0x00000000, 0x00100000)` reaches\n+  MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, capture its byte address, replicated write\n+  data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible memory metadata into the slot, retire that\n+  store once, and drain the physical dmem write oldest-first after the stall.\n+\n+  Keep the zero-wait STORE path identical to the current design when the bus is\n+  ready. Keep MMIO / UART / CoreMark marker stores unbuffered, and treat slot\n+  occupancy as a fence for younger memory operations and externally visible end\n+  conditions so ordering stays exact. The key win is that younger non-memory\n+  instructions no longer have to pay every store-only dStall cycle, which\n+  should raise CoreMark IPC without reintroducing fetch-path timing risk.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add the one-deep pending-store slot, preserve the direct ready-store fast path, and arbitrate between immediate stores and deferred slot drains while keeping RVFI mem metadata precise.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Refine dmem stall handling so bufferable RAM stores can retire into the slot while younger memory ops still fence behind an occupied slot.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire store-slot status between MEM and hazard control and keep the top-level dmem / RVFI behavior consistent with the single-retire contract.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add cocotb coverage for stalled RAM stores draining through the slot, younger ALU progress during the drain window, and unbuffered MMIO or end-of-program ordering.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r2s1.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r2s1.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r2s1.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r2s1.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r2s1\n+title: \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The current bench core's biggest proven gain came from a backend-only timing\n+  change: moving DIV/REM off the one-cycle EX path. The recent failed branch\n+  prediction attempt showed the opposite boundary on this RTL: pulling more\n+  decode/target work into the frontend can erase the IPC gain with Fmax loss.\n+\n+  The next structural hypothesis should therefore stay off the fetch path and\n+  attack the other obvious whole-core bubble source. Today any `io_dmemReady=0`\n+  cycle on a STORE freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM even though the store address,\n+  replicated data, and byte mask are already known in MEM, and CoreMark does a\n+  steady stream of normal-RAM stores to stack, list, matrix, and state data.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot with a direct ready-store fast\n+  path. When an aligned STORE to normal RAM `[0x00000000, 0x00100000)` reaches\n+  MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, capture its byte address, replicated write\n+  data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible memory metadata into the slot, retire that\n+  store once, and drain the physical dmem write oldest-first after the stall.\n+\n+  Keep the zero-wait STORE path identical to the current design when the bus is\n+  ready. Keep MMIO / UART / CoreMark marker stores unbuffered, and treat slot\n+  occupancy as a fence for younger memory operations and externally visible end\n+  conditions so ordering stays exact. The key win is that younger non-memory\n+  instructions no longer have to pay every store-only dStall cycle, which\n+  should raise CoreMark IPC without reintroducing fetch-path timing risk.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add the one-deep pending-store slot, preserve the direct ready-store fast path, and arbitrate between immediate stores and deferred slot drains while keeping RVFI mem metadata precise.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Refine dmem stall handling so bufferable RAM stores can retire into the slot while younger memory ops still fence behind an occupied slot.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire store-slot status between MEM and hazard control and keep the top-level dmem / RVFI behavior consistent with the single-retire contract.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add cocotb coverage for stalled RAM stores draining through the slot, younger ALU progress during the drain window, and unbuffered MMIO or end-of-program ordering.\"\n\n","files":[{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r2s1.yaml","relativePath":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r2s1.yaml","type":"add","patch":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r2s1.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r2s1.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r2s1.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r2s1\n+title: \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The current bench core's biggest proven gain came from a backend-only timing\n+  change: moving DIV/REM off the one-cycle EX path. The recent failed branch\n+  prediction attempt showed the opposite boundary on this RTL: pulling more\n+  decode/target work into the frontend can erase the IPC gain with Fmax loss.\n+\n+  The next structural hypothesis should therefore stay off the fetch path and\n+  attack the other obvious whole-core bubble source. Today any `io_dmemReady=0`\n+  cycle on a STORE freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM even though the store address,\n+  replicated data, and byte mask are already known in MEM, and CoreMark does a\n+  steady stream of normal-RAM stores to stack, list, matrix, and state data.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot with a direct ready-store fast\n+  path. When an aligned STORE to normal RAM `[0x00000000, 0x00100000)` reaches\n+  MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, capture its byte address, replicated write\n+  data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible memory metadata into the slot, retire that\n+  store once, and drain the physical dmem write oldest-first after the stall.\n+\n+  Keep the zero-wait STORE path identical to the current design when the bus is\n+  ready. Keep MMIO / UART / CoreMark marker stores unbuffered, and treat slot\n+  occupancy as a fence for younger memory operations and externally visible end\n+  conditions so ordering stays exact. The key win is that younger non-memory\n+  instructions no longer have to pay every store-only dStall cycle, which\n+  should raise CoreMark IPC without reintroducing fetch-path timing risk.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add the one-deep pending-store slot, preserve the direct ready-store fast path, and arbitrate between immediate stores and deferred slot drains while keeping RVFI mem metadata precise.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Refine dmem stall handling so bufferable RAM stores can retire into the slot while younger memory ops still fence behind an occupied slot.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire store-slot status between MEM and hazard control and keep the top-level dmem / RVFI behavior consistent with the single-retire contract.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add cocotb coverage for stalled RAM stores draining through the slot, younger ALU progress during the drain window, and unbuffered MMIO or end-of-program ordering.\"\n","additions":39,"deletions":0}],"diagnostics":{},"truncated":false},"title":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-202605