cle behavior for the instructions that dominate CoreMark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases in `rtl/alu.sv`\\nwith a small iterative divider/remainder unit that is entered only for\\ndivide-class ALU ops. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift/compare, address generation,\\nbranches, jumps, loads/stores, and MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing\\nsingle-cycle datapath.\\n\\nIn EX, detect divide-class operations, latch their operands and control,\\nstall IF and ID while the divider runs, and emit exactly one EX/MEM result\\nwhen the quotient or remainder is ready. Preserve RV32IM edge cases\\nprecisely: divide-...\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Port v1-proven iterative divider into EX\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench baseline still carries real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operators\\ninside the always-live combinational ALU. Archived bench runs on this same\\nbaseline show that moving divider-class ops off the fast EX path was the\\nbiggest structural win, because CoreMark almost never uses integer division\\nwhile the FPGA still pays its timing and LUT cost on every cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench baseline still carries real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operators\\ninside the always-live combinational ALU. Archived bench runs on this same\\nbaseline show that moving divider-class ops off the fast EX path was the\\nbiggest structural win, because CoreMark almost never uses integer division\\nwhile the FPGA still pays its timing and LUT cost on every cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Reuse the already-proven iterative divider structure from cores/v1 and\\nintegrate it only for DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU, leaving the existing single-cycle\\nRV32I and MUL-family datapaths intact. alu.sv stops synthesizing real\\ndivider hardware; ex_stage.sv launches the divider for divide-class ops,\\nholds the in-flight EX payload until completion, and commits exactly one\\nEX/MEM result with the original RVFI metadata and RV32M corner-case behavior\\npreserved. This is the lowest-risk structural path to a large Fmax gain on\\nthe current baseline because it attacks the known worst cone without adding\\nfetch-side control, store reordering, or multiply bubbles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 20, \"confidence\": \"high\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the v1-proven iterative RV32 divider/remainder unit with ALTOPS compatibility and exact divide-by-zero / INT_MIN-overflow semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU hardware from the normal ALU fast path while keeping existing RV32I and MUL-f...\n  Line 4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Static BTFNT branch predictor with ID redirect\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench core resolves every branch and jump in EX and flushes on every taken redirect. CoreMark is loop-heavy, so repeatedly paying that taken-branch penalty burns IPC. LUT headroom is tight, which makes a low-state predictor more attractive than a larger dynamic structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench core resolves every branch and jump in EX and flushes on every taken redirect. CoreMark is loop-heavy, so repeatedly paying that taken-branch penalty burns IPC. LUT headroom is tight, which makes a low-state predictor more attractive than a larger dynamic structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a decode-stage backward-taken/forward-not-taken predictor for conditional branches and always predict JAL taken, driving a speculative next PC from ID into IF. EX remains the architectural source of truth for branch direction and target, but only flushes and recovers on a mispredict. This should remove most loop-backedge and JAL bubbles in CoreMark while staying much lighter than a tagged BTB/BHT, limiting Fmax and LUT risk.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire an ID-stage prediction path into IF and route EX-stage mispredict recovery into hazard control.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Update the PC from a predicted-taken branch or JAL in ID while keeping EX recovery as the highest-priority redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute a static backward-taken/forward-not-taken decision and early BRANCH/JAL target for speculative fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recompute the real branch or jump outcome and request a flush only when the static prediction was wrong.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Turn redirect flushing into mispredict-only recovery so correct ...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench core's biggest proven gain came from a backend-only timing\\nchange: moving DIV/REM off the one-cycle EX path. The recent failed branch\\nprediction attempt showed the opposite boundary on this RTL: pulling more\\ndecode/target work into the frontend can erase the IPC gain with Fmax loss.\\n\\nThe next structural hypothesis should therefore stay off the fetch path and\\nattack the other obvious whole-core bubble source. Today any `io_dmemReady=0`\\ncycle on a STORE freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM even though the store address,\\nreplicated data, and byte mask are already known in MEM, and CoreMark does a\\nsteady stream of normal-RAM stores to stack, list, matrix, and state data.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench core's biggest proven gain came from a backend-only timing\\nchange: moving DIV/REM off the one-cycle EX path. The recent failed branch\\nprediction attempt showed the opposite boundary on this RTL: pulling more\\ndecode/target work into the frontend can erase the IPC gain with Fmax loss.\\n\\nThe next structural hypothesis should therefore stay off the fetch path and\\nattack the other obvious whole-core bubble source. Today any `io_dmemReady=0`\\ncycle on a STORE freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM even though the store address,\\nreplicated data, and byte mask are already known in MEM, and CoreMark does a\\nsteady stream of normal-RAM stores to stack, list, matrix, and state data.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot with a direct ready-store fast\\npath. When an aligned STORE to normal RAM `[0x00000000, 0x00100000)` reaches\\nMEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, capture its byte address, replicated write\\ndata, byte mask, and RVFI-visible memory metadata into the slot, retire that\\nstore once, and drain the physical dmem write oldest-first after the stall.\\n\\nKeep the zero-wait STORE path identical to the current d...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 1: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-001\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest (no implementation change)\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"Used by the orchestrator integration test (--from-hypothesis path).\\nMeasures the unchanged baseline through the full eval pipeline so the\\nresulting log entry locks in the first fitness number for phase 8.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"No RTL changes \\u2014 exercises the worktree -> emit_verilog -> formal ->\\ncosim -> fpga path on the existing rtl/*.sv to confirm the orchestrator\\nproduces a structured log entry.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 0, \"confidence\": \"high\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Sentinel \\u2014 no real change. skip_implementation:true below bypasses\\nthe implementation agent, so the file path here is metadata only;\\nit just has to satisfy the schema's rtl/<file> pattern. core.sv is\\nalways present and untouched.\\n\"}], \"skip_implementation\": true, \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 301.04, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 135.21, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"lut4\": 9880, \"ff\": 1858, \"seeds\": [121.29, 135.21, 136.07], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T02:16:49.012016+00:00\"}\n\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Elastic Fetch Skid Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF/ID handoff with an elastic 2-entry fetch skid\\nbuffer that decouples instruction fetch from decode. The fetch side advances\\nthe PC and captures sequential instructions whenever io_imemReady is high\\nand the buffer is not full, even while ID/EX is held by a dmem stall. The\\ndecode side consumes one buffered instruction per non-stalled cycle. Preserve\\nthe baseline zero-latency behavior with an empty-buffer bypass from\\nio_imemData to decode when the backend is ready and imem is ready, so the\\nno-stall path and branch target...\n  Line 4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Backward-Branch Taken Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves all branches and jumps in EX and fetches strictly pc+4 until that resolution, so every taken loop branch in CoreMark pays a two-instruction wrong-path flush. CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by short backward conditional branches whose direction is highly biased toward taken. A small static predictor can remove most loop-back redirect bubbles without adding a learned-state table, RAM, or speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves all branches and jumps in EX and fetches strictly pc+4 until that resolution, so every taken loop branch in CoreMark pays a two-instruction wrong-path flush. CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by short backward conditional branches whose direction is highly biased toward taken. A small static predictor can remove most loop-back redirect bubbles without adding a learned-state table, RAM, or speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a decode-light static branch predictor in IF that recognizes B-type instructions and predicts backward branches taken when the sign bit of the branch immediate is set. Compute the B immediate in IF, redirect the next PC to pc+imm for predicted-taken backward branches, and carry predicted_taken plus predicted_target through IF/ID and ID/EX. In EX, compare the actual branch result and target against the prediction, redirect only on mispredict, and set rvfi pc_wdata to the actual next PC as today. JAL/JALR remain resolved in EX to keep the predictor slot focused on conditional branches and avoid adding a return-address stack or BTB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8.0, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles with predicted_taken and predicted_target fields so EX can distinguish correct predictions f...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-005-r2s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-to-EX Load-Use Bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still has the textbook structural load-use gap: a load in EX always forces the immediately following consumer to wait one extra cycle, even when the dmem bus is ready and the loaded word is already available combinationally in MEM. CoreMark's list, matrix, and state kernels contain many load-to-use pairs, so this fixed bubble directly lowers iterations per cycle. The recent predictor win improved control flow without changing the backend; the next most promising structural angle is to add the missing MEM-to-EX load-data path and remove the mandatory interlock while preserving the single-issue in-order retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still has the textbook structural load-use gap: a load in EX always forces the immediately following consumer to wait one extra cycle, even when the dmem bus is ready and the loaded word is already available combinationally in MEM. CoreMark's list, matrix, and state kernels contain many load-to-use pairs, so this fixed bubble directly lowers iterations per cycle. The recent predictor win improved control flow without changing the backend; the next most promising structural angle is to add the missing MEM-to-EX load-data path and remove the mandatory interlock while preserving the single-issue in-order retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a direct load-result forwarding path from the MEM stage's existing sign/zero-extended load_data to the EX operand muxes. When an instruction in EX consumes the rd of a load currently in MEM, select this MEM load result for rs1 and/or rs2 instead of stalling for the value to appear in MEM/WB. If io_dmemReady is low for that load, keep the current dmem hold behavior and suppress any younger EX redirect until the load data is valid; once ready, the dependent instruction executes in the same cycle the load ...\n  Line 8: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Operand-Aware Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use hazard check is deliberately conservative: every load\\nin ID/EX stalls the next instruction when the load destination matches either\\nraw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield in IF/ID. That creates real bubbles for true\\nload consumers, but it also creates false bubbles for common CoreMark\\ninstructions whose rs fields are immediates or destinations rather than\\nsource operands, especially OP-IMM shifts/arithmetic, LOAD, JAL, LUI, and\\nAUIPC. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass recovered many load-use cycles\\nbut cost too much Fmax because it added a new MEM-data path into EX. A\\ndecode-light operand-use filter should recover the false-stall subset with\\nalmost no new datapath depth and no change to branch prediction, memory\\ntiming, or RVFI retirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use hazard check is deliberately conservative: every load\\nin ID/EX stalls the next instruction when the load destination matches either\\nraw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield in IF/ID. That creates real bubbles for true\\nload consumers, but it also creates false bubbles for common CoreMark\\ninstructions whose rs fields are immediates or destinations rather than\\nsource operands, especially OP-IMM shifts/arithmetic, LOAD, JAL, LUI, and\\nAUIPC. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass recovered many load-use cycles\\nbut cost too much Fmax because it added a new MEM-data path into EX. A\\ndecode-light operand-use filter should recover the false-stall subset with\\nalmost no new datapath depth and no change to branch prediction, memory\\ntiming, or RVFI retirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the hazard_unit load-use predicate so it only compares the load rd\\nagainst source fields that the IF/ID instruction actually reads. Compute\\nsimple uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 bits from the IF/ID opcode: rs1 is used by\\nOP, OP-IMM, LOAD, STORE, BRANCH, and JALR; rs2 is used only by...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Retiring Store Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry FIFO for normal RAM stores. When a legal aligned store to the\\nRAM address range reaches MEM and the external dmem port is not immediately\\navailable, retire the store into the FIFO with its address, replicated write\\ndata, byte mask, and RVFI metadata, then let younger non-memory instructions\\ncontinue through the pipeline while the FIFO drains oldest-first on the dmem\\nport. Loads, stores when the FIFO is full, and all MMIO/marker/UART st...\n  Line 10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Static-Biased Branch Agree Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already removes most obvious control bubbles by\\npredicting backward conditional branches taken and direct JAL targets in IF.\\nThe remaining predictor loss is now dominated by conditional branches that\\nstatic BTFNT handles poorly: biased forward branches, backward branches with\\nnon-loop behavior, and alias-heavy branch sites in CoreMark's list and state\\nkernels. A small learned direction predictor can attack those residual\\nmispredicts without adding a BTB, a target adder beyond the existing branch\\nimmediate path, or any speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already removes most obvious control bubbles by\\npredicting backward conditional branches taken and direct JAL targets in IF.\\nThe remaining predictor loss is now dominated by conditional branches that\\nstatic BTFNT handles poorly: biased forward branches, backward branches with\\nnon-loop behavior, and alias-heavy branch sites in CoreMark's list and state\\nkernels. A small learned direction predictor can attack those residual\\nmispredicts without adding a BTB, a target adder beyond the existing branch\\nimmediate path, or any speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 64-entry, PC-indexed, two-bit \\\"agree\\\" predictor for conditional branch\\ndirection. IF still computes the existing static BTFNT direction and branch\\ntarget from the fetched instruction; the table counter predicts whether the\\ndynamic outcome will agree with that static direction. Initialize counters to\\nstrongly agree so cold branches behave exactly like the current predictor.\\nFor legal aligned B-type branches, predict taken when the table prediction\\ncombined with the static direction says taken, and carry the existing\\npredicted_taken/predicted_target metadata into EX. Direct JAL prediction\\nremains unchanged, and JALR remains ...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-005-r4s1\", \"title\": \"ID-Stage JALR Redirect\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core predicts backward conditional branches and direct JALs\\nin IF, but JALR remains structurally resolved only in EX. Every return and\\nindirect jump therefore still injects the same wrong-path recovery bubbles\\nthat direct-JAL prediction just removed. CoreMark's timed path has many\\ncall/return edges around the list, matrix, state, CRC, and benchmark harness\\nroutines, so unresolved returns are now one of the cleaner remaining control\\nbubbles. A small decode-stage JALR resolver targets that specific structural\\nlatency without adding a predictor table, widening retirement, or putting\\nload data on the EX operand path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core predicts backward conditional branches and direct JALs\\nin IF, but JALR remains structurally resolved only in EX. Every return and\\nindirect jump therefore still injects the same wrong-path recovery bubbles\\nthat direct-JAL prediction just removed. CoreMark's timed path has many\\ncall/return edges around the list, matrix, state, CRC, and benchmark harness\\nroutines, so unresolved returns are now one of the cleaner remaining control\\nbubbles. A small decode-stage JALR resolver targets that specific structural\\nlatency without adding a predictor table, widening retirement, or putting\\nload data on the EX operand path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add an early JALR redirect path in ID for cases where the rs1 base is already\\navailable from the regfile or same-cycle WB write-first bypass. ID computes\\nthe JALR target from rs1 plus the I-immediate, verifies the aligned target,\\nand sends a PC redirect to IF in the same cycle the JALR enters ID/EX. The\\nID/EX prediction metadata is overwritten with that resolved target so the\\nlater EX-stage instance of the same JALR does not redirect a second time.\\nWhen rs1 is waiting on an older instruction in ID/EX or EX/MEM, or when the\\nbackend is ...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-008-r5s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Store-Data Forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core still treats every immediate load-use match as a\\nfull one-cycle interlock, including the common case where the younger\\ninstruction is a STORE that only needs the loaded register as store data\\n(rs2), not as the address base (rs1). That bubble is structurally avoidable:\\nstore data is not consumed by the EX ALU, and the actual dmem write data is\\nformed one stage later in MEM. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass showed\\nthat CoreMark has meaningful load-use pressure, but it lost too much Fmax by\\nrouting dmem load data into the EX operand muxes. A late store-data bypass\\ntargets a useful subset of those bubbles while keeping load data off the ALU,\\nbranch compare, and JALR critical paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core still treats every immediate load-use match as a\\nfull one-cycle interlock, including the common case where the younger\\ninstruction is a STORE that only needs the loaded register as store data\\n(rs2), not as the address base (rs1). That bubble is structurally avoidable:\\nstore data is not consumed by the EX ALU, and the actual dmem write data is\\nformed one stage later in MEM. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass showed\\nthat CoreMark has meaningful load-use pressure, but it lost too much Fmax by\\nrouting dmem load data into the EX operand muxes. A late store-data bypass\\ntargets a useful subset of those bubbles while keeping load data off the ALU,\\nbranch compare, and JALR critical paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Allow a legal STORE immediately after a LOAD to proceed without the normal\\nload-use bubble when the load destination matches only the STORE rs2 data\\nfield and not the STORE rs1 address field. The store computes its address in\\nEX as usual, carries its rs2 register number into MEM, and selects the final\\nstore data in MEM from the MEM/WB writeback value when that older i...\n  Line 16: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-009-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny Forward-Branch Override Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already predicts backward conditional branches and\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, reaching 324.48 fitness. The 64-entry agree\\npredictor experiment showed that residual conditional-branch misses still\\nmatter: it reduced CoreMark cycles from 4,259,699 to 4,194,484, but the\\nlarger always-consulted table cost enough Fmax to regress overall fitness.\\nThat result points to a narrower predictor: keep the proven BTFNT and direct\\nJAL behavior intact, but learn only the static predictor's most expensive\\nblind spot, biased forward conditional branches. A tiny forward-only override\\nshould retain much of the cycle-count gain with far less table area, fanout,\\nand IF routing pressure than the full agree predictor.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already predicts backward conditional branches and\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, reaching 324.48 fitness. The 64-entry agree\\npredictor experiment showed that residual conditional-branch misses still\\nmatter: it reduced CoreMark cycles from 4,259,699 to 4,194,484, but the\\nlarger always-consulted table cost enough Fmax to regress overall fitness.\\nThat result points to a narrower predictor: keep the proven BTFNT and direct\\nJAL behavior intact, but learn only the static predictor's most expensive\\nblind spot, biased forward conditional branches. A tiny forward-only override\\nshould retain much of the cycle-count gain with far less table area, fanout,\\nand IF routing pressure than the full agree predictor.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 16-entry PC-indexed two-bit saturating override table that is consulted\\nonly for legal, aligned, forward B-type conditional branches. Backward\\nbranches keep the current static taken prediction, and direct JAL prediction\\nremains unchanged. Forward branches cold-start as strongly not-taken so the\\nreset behavior exactly matches the current...\n  Line 17: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-010-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Cold Multi-Cycle DIV/REM Unit\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\ncombinational EX-stage ALU result path as ADD, address generation, branch\\ncompare support, and MUL. The generated FPGA critical paths are dominated by\\nlong EX-stage carry/mux structures, while the CoreMark timed loop is\\neffectively division-cold: the static binary has only a few DIVU sites, and\\nthose are in setup/printing paths rather than the hot matrix/list/state\\niteration body. Prior multiplier and MEM-lane rewrites showed that reshaping\\nalready-mapped hot datapaths can badly hurt placement; the better\\nmicro-optimization is to remove the cold divider/remainder hardware from the\\none-cycle ALU path entirely while leaving multiply and all RV32I operations\\nsingle-cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\ncombinational EX-stage ALU result path as ADD, address generation, branch\\ncompare support, and MUL. The generated FPGA critical paths are dominated by\\nlong EX-stage carry/mux structures, while the CoreMark timed loop is\\neffectively division-cold: the static binary has only a few DIVU sites, and\\nthose are in setup/printing paths rather than the hot matrix/list/state\\niteration body. Prior multiplier and MEM-lane rewrites showed that reshaping\\nalready-mapped hot datapaths can badly hurt placement; the better\\nmicro-optimization is to remove the cold divider/remainder hardware from the\\none-cycle ALU path entirely while leaving multiply and all RV32I operations\\nsingle-cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases with a small\\niterative divider/remainder unit that is only entered for those four ALU\\nopcodes. Normal RV32I operations, branches, jumps, loads/stores, and all MUL\\nvariants remain one-cycle and keep the current forwarding behavior. When a\\ndivide-class instruction reaches...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-011-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Registered Fetch/Decode Cutpoint\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core still has no real IF/ID register: the PC drives imem, the returned instruction is decoded, the register file is read, and the ID/EX register captures the result in one cycle. That preserves a short frontend latency, but it also leaves a large structural timing path through instruction memory data, IF prediction decode, full instruction decode, immediate generation, and regfile read fanout. Prior elastic-fetch work kept an empty-buffer bypass to avoid latency, so it retained much of this path and lost Fmax. A cleaner structural bet is to deliberately insert the missing fetch/decode cutpoint and trade one extra frontend pipeline stage for a higher median FPGA clock.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core still has no real IF/ID register: the PC drives imem, the returned instruction is decoded, the register file is read, and the ID/EX register captures the result in one cycle. That preserves a short frontend latency, but it also leaves a large structural timing path through instruction memory data, IF prediction decode, full instruction decode, immediate generation, and regfile read fanout. Prior elastic-fetch work kept an empty-buffer bypass to avoid latency, so it retained much of this path and lost Fmax. A cleaner structural bet is to deliberately insert the missing fetch/decode cutpoint and trade one extra frontend pipeline stage for a higher median FPGA clock.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the IF/ID handoff into a real registered pipeline boundary with no zero-latency imem-data-to-decode bypass. IF captures {pc, instr, predicted_taken, predicted_target, valid} when io_imemReady is high and the decode side can accept an entry; ID decodes only the registered entry on the following cycle. Redirects and reset flush the IF/ID register and restart fetch at the resolved target, while load-use and dmem stalls hold the re...\n  Line 20: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-013-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Cold High-Half Multiply Unit\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from moving cold DIV/REM hardware out of the\\nuniversal EX-stage ALU path: CoreMark cycles barely changed, while median\\nFmax rose sharply and LUT pressure dropped. The same shape remains in the\\nmultiply path, but with a narrower target. `rtl/alu.sv` still builds three\\nindependent 64-bit products every cycle for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU.\\nCoreMark's timed kernels need ordinary low-half MUL, but high-half multiply\\nvariants are normally absent or cold in this benchmark. The previous 33x33\\nshared-multiplier rewrite proved that reshaping the hot MUL primitive itself\\ncan badly hurt placement, so the better micro-optimization is to leave the\\nexisting low-half MUL fast path intact and remove only the cold high-half\\nmultiplier variants from the one-cycle ALU.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from moving cold DIV/REM hardware out of the\\nuniversal EX-stage ALU path: CoreMark cycles barely changed, while median\\nFmax rose sharply and LUT pressure dropped. The same shape remains in the\\nmultiply path, but with a narrower target. `rtl/alu.sv` still builds three\\nindependent 64-bit products every cycle for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU.\\nCoreMark's timed kernels need ordinary low-half MUL, but high-half multiply\\nvariants are normally absent or cold in this benchmark. The previous 33x33\\nshared-multiplier rewrite proved that reshaping the hot MUL primitive itself\\ncan badly hurt placement, so the better micro-optimization is to leave the\\nexisting low-half MUL fast path intact and remove only the cold high-half\\nmultiplier variants from the one-cycle ALU.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep ALU_MUL as the only single-cycle hardware multiply in the hot ALU path,\\nusing the existing unsigned 32x32 product low half. Move MULH, MULHU, and\\nMULHSU into a small long-latency `mulh_unit` launched only for those three\\no...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-014-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Single-Entry Retiring Store Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gained a large Fmax and LUT win by moving DIV/REM out\\nof the hot EX path, so the next structural bottleneck is less likely to be\\nraw datapath area and more likely to be cycles lost to the random dmem\\nhandshake model. Today every dmem-not-ready cycle for a STORE freezes the\\nwhole pipeline, even though the store address, replicated data, byte mask,\\nand RVFI memory metadata are already known in MEM and the store produces no\\nregister result for younger ALU/control instructions. A prior two-entry\\nstore buffer proved the idea through formal and cosim but failed placement\\non the larger pre-DIV RTL. A smaller one-entry RAM-store buffer should retain\\nmost of the useful stall hiding while keeping the arbitration, state, and\\nplacement pressure low enough for the post-DIV design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gained a large Fmax and LUT win by moving DIV/REM out\\nof the hot EX path, so the next structural bottleneck is less likely to be\\nraw datapath area and more likely to be cycles lost to the random dmem\\nhandshake model. Today every dmem-not-ready cycle for a STORE freezes the\\nwhole pipeline, even though the store address, replicated data, byte mask,\\nand RVFI memory metadata are already known in MEM and the store produces no\\nregister result for younger ALU/control instructions. A prior two-entry\\nstore buffer proved the idea through formal and cosim but failed placement\\non the larger pre-DIV RTL. A smaller one-entry RAM-store buffer should retain\\nmost of the useful stall hiding while keeping the arbitration, state, and\\nplacement pressure low enough for the post-DIV design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one in-order retiring store slot for legal aligned stores to normal RAM\\nonly. When such a store reaches MEM and io_dmemReady is low, capture its\\nbyte address, replicated write data, byte mask, and RV...\n  Line 22: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-015-r7s2\", \"title\": \"One-Entry Return Target Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches and\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, and the latest winning change moved cold DIV/REM\\noff the critical ALU path. The predictor slot should therefore avoid broad\\nlearned branch tables that have already shown a bad cycle-count versus Fmax\\ntradeoff: the 64-entry agree table and 16-entry forward override both reduced\\nCoreMark cycles but lost more clock than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining high-value predictor miss is JALR return control flow. The\\n4-entry RAS experiment showed returns exist in the timed path, but its stack\\nbookkeeping and IF routing were still too expensive. A single tagged return\\ntarget entry is a narrower bet: it targets recurring canonical return PCs\\nwith one PC tag, one target register, and one IF compare, avoiding a BTB,\\nmulti-entry stack, or conditional-branch table.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches and\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, and the latest winning change moved cold DIV/REM\\noff the critical ALU path. The predictor slot should therefore avoid broad\\nlearned branch tables that have already shown a bad cycle-count versus Fmax\\ntradeoff: the 64-entry agree table and 16-entry forward override both reduced\\nCoreMark cycles but lost more clock than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining high-value predictor miss is JALR return control flow. The\\n4-entry RAS experiment showed returns exist in the timed path, but its stack\\nbookkeeping and IF routing were still too expensive. A single tagged return\\ntarget entry is a narrower bet: it targets recurring canonical return PCs\\nwith one PC tag, one target register, and one IF compare, avoiding a BTB,\\nmulti-entry stack, or conditional-branch table.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry return target predictor for canonical return JALRs. When EX\\nresolves a valid...\n  Line 23: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-016-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Reset-Valid LUTRAM Register File\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The post-DIV best core has removed the largest cold arithmetic block from\\nthe EX critical path, so the next micro-optimization should target FPGA\\nplacement pressure without changing CoreMark cycles. The current register\\nfile is a 32x32 array with a synchronous reset loop that clears every data\\nbit. That resettable array is likely implemented as ordinary flip-flops or\\nreset-muxed logic instead of compact distributed RAM, adding roughly 1024\\nresettable state bits plus reset fanout to the already timing-sensitive\\nfetch/decode/register-read region.\\n\\nArchitectural software only needs the externally visible reset behavior of\\nreading zero before a register has been written. It does not require the\\nphysical register data array itself to be synchronously cleared. A valid-zero\\nmask can preserve the current tests and reset semantics while allowing the\\n32x32 data storage to become resetless.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The post-DIV best core has removed the largest cold arithmetic block from\\nthe EX critical path, so the next micro-optimization should target FPGA\\nplacement pressure without changing CoreMark cycles. The current register\\nfile is a 32x32 array with a synchronous reset loop that clears every data\\nbit. That resettable array is likely implemented as ordinary flip-flops or\\nreset-muxed logic instead of compact distributed RAM, adding roughly 1024\\nresettable state bits plus reset fanout to the already timing-sensitive\\nfetch/decode/register-read region.\\n\\nArchitectural software only needs the externally visible reset behavior of\\nreading zero before a register has been written. It does not require the\\nphysical register data array itself to be synchronously cleared. A valid-zero\\nmask can preserve the current tests and reset semantics while allowing the\\n32x32 data storage to become resetless.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the regist...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-017-r8s1\", \"title\": \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot With Direct Fast Path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\\n\\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\\n\\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot that is used only on a...\n  Line 25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-018-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Funct3-Biased Static Branch Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already has the two predictor features that have\\nactually paid off: backward conditional branches predict taken, and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned predictor attempts point in the\\nother direction: both the 64-entry agree table and the 16-entry forward-only\\noverride table reduced CoreMark cycles, but their table read/update routing\\ncost more Fmax than the saved mispredicts recovered. Return prediction was\\neven less attractive after the post-DIV timing win, with only small cycle\\nsavings and a large clock regression.\\n\\nThe remaining promising predictor angle is therefore a zero-state refinement\\nof the static conditional-branch rule. A quick dynamic profile of the timed\\nCoreMark region shows that the baseline BTFNT rule is especially wrong for\\ntwo simple branch classes: backward BEQ branches, which are mostly not taken\\nin this binary, and forward BNE branches, which are usually taken. Those two\\nclasses account for a large fraction of residual static predictor mistakes,\\nand they can be recognized from the funct3 bits already examined in IF for\\nbranch legality. This targets iterations/cycle without adding a predictor\\nRAM, update feedback path, BTB, RAS, or extra target adder.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already has the two predictor features that have\\nactually paid off: backward conditional branches predict taken, and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned predictor attempts point in the\\nother direction: both the 64-entry agree table and the 16-entry forward-only\\noverride table reduced CoreMark cycles, but their table read/update routing\\ncost more Fmax than the saved mispredicts recovered. Return prediction was\\neven less attractive after the post-DIV timing win, with only small cycle\\nsavings and a large clock regression.\\n\\nThe remaining p...\n  Line 26: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-019-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Hot-Cold ALU Opcode Split\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design already moved DIV/REM out of the universal EX\\ndatapath and added the one-deep store slot, so the remaining micro-optimum\\nis likely in the hot single-cycle EX logic rather than in another stall\\nhiding structure. `rtl/alu.sv` still selects every RV32I operation and all\\nmultiply-class results through one dense ALU opcode case. Even though\\nDIV/REM now return dummy zero in the ALU, the common ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path still shares decode and mux structure with the cold M-extension\\nresult cases.\\n\\nA previous \\\"hot-bit ALU\\\" attempt was never architecturally evaluated because\\nthe implementation touched an off-limits non-RTL helper file. The idea\\nremains attractive after the divider win: leave every arithmetic primitive\\nand all pipeline control unchanged, but make the opcode encoding expose a\\nsimple hot/cold split that synthesis can map into a shallower mux for the\\ncommon RV32I path. This targets median Fmax with no CoreMark cycle-count\\nrisk and avoids the multiplier-primitive rewrites that previously regressed\\nbadly.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design already moved DIV/REM out of the universal EX\\ndatapath and added the one-deep store slot, so the remaining micro-optimum\\nis likely in the hot single-cycle EX logic rather than in another stall\\nhiding structure. `rtl/alu.sv` still selects every RV32I operation and all\\nmultiply-class results through one dense ALU opcode case. Even though\\nDIV/REM now return dummy zero in the ALU, the common ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path still shares decode and mux structure with the cold M-extension\\nresult cases.\\n\\nA previous \\\"hot-bit ALU\\\" attempt was never architecturally evaluated because\\nthe implementation touched an off-limits non-RTL helper file. The idea\\nremains attractive after the divider win: leave every arithmetic primitive\\nand all pipeline contr...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-020-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Store-Slot-Aware Fetch Skid Queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design combines the two changes that have paid off most:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and a one-deep pending-store slot\\nlets a stalled normal-RAM store retire while the physical dmem write drains\\nlater. That store slot reduced CoreMark cycles, but the frontend is still\\nstructurally brittle under the tournament's random imem stalls: `if_stage`\\npresents live imem data directly to decode, and `hazard_unit` still turns\\nraw `io_imemReady=0` into a global pipeline stall.\\n\\nA previous generic elastic fetch buffer regressed because it added permanent\\nfrontend queue/arbitration cost before the divider and store-slot timing\\nwins, and it tried to prefetch through all backend stalls. The better\\nstructural angle now is narrower: use a tiny fetch skid queue only to absorb\\ninstruction-memory backpressure and to keep fetch productive while the new\\nstore slot is draining for older stores. This targets the measured\\nstore-slot cycle win's remaining companion bottleneck without touching the\\nhot EX operand path, load data forwarding, branch predictor state, or RVFI\\nretirement width.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design combines the two changes that have paid off most:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and a one-deep pending-store slot\\nlets a stalled normal-RAM store retire while the physical dmem write drains\\nlater. That store slot reduced CoreMark cycles, but the frontend is still\\nstructurally brittle under the tournament's random imem stalls: `if_stage`\\npresents live imem data directly to decode, and `hazard_unit` still turns\\nraw `io_imemReady=0` into a global pipeline stall.\\n\\nA previous generic elastic fetch buffer regressed because it added permanent\\nfrontend queue/arbitration cost before the divider and store-slot timing\\nwins, and it tried to prefetch through all backend stalls. T...\n  Line 29: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-022-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Collapse Redundant EX/MEM Metadata\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already captured the large available wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is out of the one-cycle ALU path, and the pending store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. The remaining micro-opt slot should therefore\\navoid new predictors, queues, or arithmetic units and instead trim logic that\\nis already proven unnecessary on the hot pipeline boundary.\\n\\nThe EX/MEM bundle still carries several fields that do not add architectural\\ninformation. `write_data` is always assigned the same post-forwarded rs2\\nvalue as `rs2_val`, while `branch_taken` and `branch_target` are registered\\nbut never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, forwarding, or hazard logic. Those bits\\nadd about 65 EX/MEM flops plus mux/load on the EX branch-target and rs2 data\\npaths, exactly where the post-DIV design is trying to preserve Fmax. Pruning\\nthis redundant metadata should not change CoreMark cycles, bus behavior,\\nbranch prediction, RVFI ordering, or any ISA-visible result.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already captured the large available wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is out of the one-cycle ALU path, and the pending store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. The remaining micro-opt slot should therefore\\navoid new predictors, queues, or arithmetic units and instead trim logic that\\nis already proven unnecessary on the hot pipeline boundary.\\n\\nThe EX/MEM bundle still carries several fields that do not add architectural\\ninformation. `write_data` is always assigned the same post-forwarded rs2\\nvalue as `rs2_val`, while `branch_taken` and `branch_target` are registered\\nbut never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, forwarding, or hazard logic. Those bits\\nadd about 65 EX/MEM flops plus mux/load on the EX branch-target and rs2 data\\npaths, exactly where the post-DIV design is trying to preserve Fmax. Pruning\\nthis redundant metadata should not change CoreMark cycl...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-023-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-imem path whenever the external\\nbus is ready, and consult a tiny cached copy only on cycles where imem would\\notherwise stall. CoreMark's timed code repeatedly executes the same matrix,\\nlist, state, CRC, and loop-control instructions over a 10-iteration run, so\\neven a small direct-mapped instruction replay table should turn many random\\niStall bubbles into normal fetch cycles after warmup.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-im...\n  Line 32: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-025-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Segmented RVFI Order Counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large architectural wins:\\nDIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the one-deep store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. Recent micro-opt attempts that reshaped hot\\ndatapaths, predictor target logic, or EX/MEM metadata mostly lost Fmax to\\nplacement noise or new muxing. The next micro-opt should therefore target a\\nguaranteed always-present timing structure without changing IPC.\\n\\nThe top-level RVFI order counter is currently a single 64-bit increment:\\n`rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'b1` on every retirement. The FPGA\\nbenchmark wrapper intentionally retains RVFI fanout by XOR-reducing the RVFI\\noutputs into an LED, so this counter cannot be assumed dead in synthesis.\\nA 64-bit carry self-loop is not architectural work for the CoreMark datapath,\\nbut it can still consume carry-chain/routing budget and compete with the hot\\npipeline for median Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large architectural wins:\\nDIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the one-deep store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. Recent micro-opt attempts that reshaped hot\\ndatapaths, predictor target logic, or EX/MEM metadata mostly lost Fmax to\\nplacement noise or new muxing. The next micro-opt should therefore target a\\nguaranteed always-present timing structure without changing IPC.\\n\\nThe top-level RVFI order counter is currently a single 64-bit increment:\\n`rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'b1` on every retirement. The FPGA\\nbenchmark wrapper intentionally retains RVFI fanout by XOR-reducing the RVFI\\noutputs into an LED, so this counter cannot be assumed dead in synthesis.\\nA 64-bit carry self-loop is not architectural work for the CoreMark datapath,\\nbut it can still consume carry-chain/routing budget and compete with the hot\\npipeline for median Fmax.\\n\", ...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-026-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Narrow Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction side. The 256-entry stall-only replay cache cut CoreMark cycles\\nfrom 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, showing that random iStall bubbles are a large\\npart of the remaining IPC loss, but its wide IF table/tag/data mux dropped\\nmedian Fmax enough to miss the current best.\\n\\nThat result points to a narrower replay structure rather than a broader\\nfetch queue. CoreMark's timed hot code repeatedly executes compact inner\\nloops and call bodies, so a smaller direct-mapped replay table should retain\\nmuch of the iStall hit-rate benefit while reducing the IF storage mux,\\ncomparator fanout, and PC-control load that hurt Fmax in the 256-entry\\nversion. This keeps the zero-wait path direct and targets only cycles that\\nwould otherwise be external imem stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction side. The 256-entry stall-only replay cache cut CoreMark cycles\\nfrom 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, showing that random iStall bubbles are a large\\npart of the remaining IPC loss, but its wide IF table/tag/data mux dropped\\nmedian Fmax enough to miss the current best.\\n\\nThat result points to a narrower replay structure rather than a broader\\nfetch queue. CoreMark's timed hot code repeatedly executes compact inner\\nloops and call bodies, so a smaller direct-mapped replay t...\n  Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-027-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Small Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor chan