{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778854061154,"sessionID":"ses_1d40bf650ffenRB2gPryacWR1U","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_zuvDWdR5FGTI5kXs0psPKRwa","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\n+title: \"Two-Entry Deferred Store Queue\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\n+  that avoid adding logic to the fetch path: first moving DIV/REM off EX, then\n+  buffering one stalled RAM store. The current one-deep store slot still\n+  serializes store bursts behind a single pending write, even though CoreMark\n+  issues clustered stack, list, matrix, and state stores under the random\n+  `io_dmemReady` stall model.\n+\n+  A deeper store queue is the next clean extension of the one change that has\n+  already proven it can raise IPC on this RTL without paying the frontend Fmax\n+  penalties seen in prior predictor experiments.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Replace the current one-deep pending-store slot with a two-entry oldest-first\n+  deferred store queue for aligned normal-RAM stores. When `io_dmemReady` is\n+  low, the oldest queued store keeps draining to the physical bus while a\n+  younger bufferable store can still retire into the second queue entry instead\n+  of freezing the whole pipeline immediately.\n+\n+  Keep the existing zero-wait direct-store fast path when the queue is empty,\n+  and preserve architectural ordering by continuing to fence younger loads,\n+  non-bufferable stores, and EBREAK behind any queued store. This should hide\n+  more of CoreMark's store-side random dmem stalls while staying off the IF and\n+  hot EX timing paths, so the IPC gain has a better chance of surviving FPGA\n+  timing than another fetch or load-replay mechanism.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Replace the one-deep store slot with a two-entry FIFO for aligned normal-RAM stores, keeping direct ready-store issue when the queue is empty and draining queued writes oldest-first.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Distinguish store-queue nonempty versus full so bufferable younger stores may keep flowing until both entries are occupied, while loads, non-bufferable stores, and EBREAK still fence behind pending writes.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the new store-queue occupancy signals between MEM and hazard control in place of the single-slot valid handshake.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add pipeline tests for two queued stores, oldest-first drain ordering, and the required fencing of later loads and EBREAK until the deferred stores commit externally.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\n+title: \"Two-Entry Deferred Store Queue\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\n+  that avoid adding logic to the fetch path: first moving DIV/REM off EX, then\n+  buffering one stalled RAM store. The current one-deep store slot still\n+  serializes store bursts behind a single pending write, even though CoreMark\n+  issues clustered stack, list, matrix, and state stores under the random\n+  `io_dmemReady` stall model.\n+\n+  A deeper store queue is the next clean extension of the one change that has\n+  already proven it can raise IPC on this RTL without paying the frontend Fmax\n+  penalties seen in prior predictor experiments.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Replace the current one-deep pending-store slot with a two-entry oldest-first\n+  deferred store queue for aligned normal-RAM stores. When `io_dmemReady` is\n+  low, the oldest queued store keeps draining to the physical bus while a\n+  younger bufferable store can still retire into the second queue entry instead\n+  of freezing the whole pipeline immediately.\n+\n+  Keep the existing zero-wait direct-store fast path when the queue is empty,\n+  and preserve architectural ordering by continuing to fence younger loads,\n+  non-bufferable stores, and EBREAK behind any queued store. This should hide\n+  more of CoreMark's store-side random dmem stalls while staying off the IF and\n+  hot EX timing paths, so the IPC gain has a better chance of surviving FPGA\n+  timing than another fetch or load-replay mechanism.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Replace the one-deep store slot with a two-entry FIFO for aligned normal-RAM stores, keeping direct ready-store issue when the queue is empty and draining queued writes oldest-first.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Distinguish store-queue nonempty versus full so bufferable younger stores may keep flowing until both entries are occupied, while loads, non-bufferable stores, and EBREAK still fence behind pending writes.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the new store-queue occupancy signals between MEM and hazard control in place of the single-slot valid handshake.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add pipeline tests for two queued stores, oldest-first drain ordering, and the required fencing of later loads and EBREAK until the deferred stores commit externally.\"\n\n","files":[{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml","relativePath":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml","type":"add","patch":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\n+title: \"Two-Entry Deferred Store Queue\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\n+  that avoid adding logic to the fetch path: first moving DIV/REM off EX, then\n+  buffering one stalled RAM store. The current one-deep store slot still\n+  serializes store bursts behind a single pending write, even though CoreMark\n+  issues clustered stack, list, matrix, and state stores under the random\n+  `io_dmemReady` stall model.\n+\n+  A deeper store queue is the next clean extension of the one change that has\n+  already proven it can raise IPC on this RTL without paying the frontend Fmax\n+  penalties seen in prior predictor experiments.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Replace the current one-deep pending-store slot with a two-entry oldest-first\n+  deferred store queue for aligned normal-RAM stores. When `io_dmemReady` is\n+  low, the oldest queued store keeps draining to the physical bus while a\n+  younger bufferable store can still retire into the second queue entry instead\n+  of freezing the whole pipeline immediately.\n+\n+  Keep the existing zero-wait direct-store fast path when the queue is empty,\n+  and preserve architectural ordering by continuing to fence younger loads,\n+  non-bufferable stores, and EBREAK behind any queued store. This should hide\n+  more of CoreMark's store-side random dmem stalls while staying off the IF and\n+  hot EX timing paths, so the IPC gain has a better chance of surviving FPGA\n+  timing than another fetch or load-replay mechanism.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Replace the one-deep store slot with a two-entry FIFO for aligned normal-RAM stores, keeping direct ready-store issue when the queue is empty and draining queued writes oldest-first.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Distinguish store-queue nonempty versus full so bufferable younger stores may keep flowing until both entries are occupied, while loads, non-bufferable stores, and EBREAK still fence behind pending writes.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the new store-queue occupancy signals between MEM and hazard control in place of the single-slot valid handshake.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add pipeline tests for two queued stores, oldest-first drain ordering, and the required fencing of later loads and EBREAK until the deferred stores commit externally.\"\n","additions":39,"deletions":0}],"diagnostics":{},"truncated":false},"title":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml","time":{"start":1778854061145,"end":1778854061152}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_082c665607756e0a016a0728a604a8819aad19954a93a641ec"}},"id":"prt_e2bf6c8ba001KeR1p72FePz9zp","sessionID":"ses_1d40bf650ffenRB2gPryacWR1U","messageID":"msg_e2bf62007001Lg