aller direct-mapped replay table should retain\\nmuch of the iStall hit-rate benefit while reducing the IF storage mux,\\ncomparator fanout, and PC-control load that hurt Fmax in the 256-entry\\nversion. This keeps the zero-wait path direct and targets only cycles that\\nwould otherwise be external imem stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction side. The 256-entry stall-only replay cache cut CoreMark cycles\\nfrom 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, showing that random iStall bubbles are a large\\npart of the remaining IPC loss, but its wide IF table/tag/data mux dropped\\nmedian Fmax enough to miss the current best.\\n\\nThat result points to a narrower replay structure rather than a broader\\nfetch queue. CoreMark's timed hot code repeatedly executes compact inner\\nloops and call bodies, so a smaller direct-mapped replay t...\n  Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-027-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Small Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor changes that have paid so far are the ones that avoid broad\\nlearned direction state: backward conditional branches and direct JAL targets\\nare predicted in IF, while the larger branch tables and return predictors\\nlost more Fmax than their cycle savings recovered. The one exception with a\\nlarge cycle signal was the stall-only instruction replay experiment: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles substantially by serving hot-loop instructions during random\\nimem stalls, but its 256-entry full-tag table pulled median Fmax below the\\ncurrent best.\\n\\nThat result suggests a narrower predictor, not a new branch-direction table.\\nCoreMark's timed region repeatedly executes a relatively small hot text\\nfootprint, so a smaller replay predictor should retain much of the random\\niStall hiding while shortening the IF tag/data muxes that hurt the 256-entry\\nattempt. This stays in the predictor slot because the table predicts the\\ninstruction word for the current PC only when the external fetch bus would\\notherwise provide no word; normal ready fetches still use live imem data.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor changes that have paid so far are the ones that avoid broad\\nlearned direction state: backward conditional branches and direct JAL targets\\nare predicted in IF, while the larger branch tables and return predictors\\nlost more Fmax than their cycle savings recovered. The one exception with a\\nlarge cycle signal was the stall-only instruction replay experiment: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles substantially by serving hot-loop instructions during random\\nimem stalls, but its 256-entry full-tag table pulled median Fmax below the\\ncurrent best.\\n\\nThat result suggests a narrower predictor, not a new branch-direction table.\\nCoreMark's timed region repeatedly executes a relatively small hot text\\nfootprint, so a smaller replay predictor should reta...\n  Line 37: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-030-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Registered Lookahead I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is still the simple static frontend:\\nbackward conditional branches and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF.\\nBroader learned branch tables and return predictors reduced a few control\\nbubbles, but their IF reads, compares, and EX update routing lost more Fmax\\nthan the cycle savings recovered.\\n\\nThe only predictor-family experiment with a large cycle signal was the\\nstall-only instruction replay table. The 256-entry replay version reduced\\nCoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314 by serving hot instructions\\nduring random imem stalls, but its combinational tag/data lookup sat directly\\non the current fetch-ready and predictor path and pulled median Fmax below\\nthe current best. Smaller combinational replay tables kept much of the same\\ntiming problem while giving up hit rate.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore not another direction table\\nor return predictor, but a timing-clean replay predictor: keep the large\\niStall-hiding benefit, while moving the table lookup off the same-cycle IF\\ndecision path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is still the simple static frontend:\\nbackward conditional branches and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF.\\nBroader learned branch tables and return predictors reduced a few control\\nbubbles, but their IF reads, compares, and EX update routing lost more Fmax\\nthan the cycle savings recovered.\\n\\nThe only predictor-family experiment with a large cycle signal was the\\nstall-only instruction replay table. The 256-entry replay version reduced\\nCoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314 by serving hot instructions\\nduring random imem stalls, but its combinational tag/data lookup sat directly\\non the current fetch-ready and predictor path and pulled median Fmax below\\nthe current best. Smaller combinational replay table...\n  Line 42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-035-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Registered MEM Load-Replay Candidate\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest IPC gain from registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay: repeated hot instructions can be supplied during random\\nimem stalls without putting a same-cycle replay-table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. The analogous remaining structural loss is on the data side.\\nLoads still freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low,\\neven when the same normal-RAM word was recently returned by dmem.\\n\\nThe earlier MEM-stage load replay experiment showed the opportunity: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles, but the combinational replay-table lookup, tag compare, and\\nstore merge logic in MEM lowered Fmax enough to miss the current best. The\\npromising structural angle is to keep the replay behavior while moving the\\nlookup off the MEM critical path, just as the successful IF replay predictor\\ndid for instruction stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest IPC gain from registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay: repeated hot instructions can be supplied during random\\nimem stalls without putting a same-cycle replay-table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. The analogous remaining structural loss is on the data side.\\nLoads still freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low,\\neven when the same normal-RAM word was recently returned by dmem.\\n\\nThe earlier MEM-stage load replay experiment showed the opportunity: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles, but the combinational replay-table lookup, tag compare, and\\nstore merge logic in MEM lowered Fmax enough to miss the current best. The\\npromising structural angle is to keep the replay behavior while moving the\\nlookup off the MEM critical path, just as the successful IF replay predictor\\ndid for instruction stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small normal-RAM load replay table with a registered candidate for the\\ninstruction entering MEM...\n  Line 44: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-037-r15s0\", \"title\": \"Valid-Only Pipeline Payload Reset\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by timing-only wins that do not change\\nCoreMark cycles: the segmented RVFI order counter and the compressed\\nresetless IF replay metadata both improved Fmax by removing unnecessary\\ncarry, compare, and reset pressure from always-present logic. The remaining\\npipeline registers still use broad `<= '0` clears on reset, flush, and\\ninjected bubbles, even though most payload bits are architecturally\\nirrelevant whenever the stage valid bit is zero.\\n\\nThose full-bundle clears add synchronous reset/clear muxes and reset fanout\\nto wide ID/EX, EX/MEM, MEM/WB, divide-side metadata, and replay-candidate\\npayload registers. The data fields include PCs, instructions, operands,\\nimmediates, memory words, and RVFI metadata; only a small subset of valid\\nbits and side-effect controls must be known zero to prevent writes,\\nmemory requests, redirects, or incorrect forwarding. A valid-gated payload\\ncleanup is a narrow micro-optimization in the same class as the measured\\nreplay tag reset elision: preserve the exact instruction stream and\\nretirement behavior, but remove needless reset/clear logic from hot\\npipeline boundaries.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by timing-only wins that do not change\\nCoreMark cycles: the segmented RVFI order counter and the compressed\\nresetless IF replay metadata both improved Fmax by removing unnecessary\\ncarry, compare, and reset pressure from always-present logic. The remaining\\npipeline registers still use broad `<= '0` clears on reset, flush, and\\ninjected bubbles, even though most payload bits are architecturally\\nirrelevant whenever the stage valid bit is zero.\\n\\nThose full-bundle clears add synchronous reset/clear muxes and reset fanout\\nto wide ID/EX, EX/MEM, MEM/WB, divide-side metadata, and replay-candidate\\npayload registers. The data fields in...\n  Line 45: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-038-r15s1\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Pending Store Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the proven structural pieces: cold DIV/REM is\\nout of the universal EX path, the one-deep pending-store slot hides some\\nnormal-RAM store backpressure, and the registered instruction replay table\\nremoves most random iStall loss. The remaining structural stalls are now\\nconcentrated on the data side. A single pending-store entry lets one stalled\\nRAM store retire, but the next store still freezes the whole pipeline while\\nthe older slot drains, even though both stores already have their address,\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI memory metadata available in MEM.\\n\\nA two-entry store buffer was attempted before the large post-DIV and replay\\ntiming wins and failed placement on a much larger design. The current RTL is\\nsubstantially smaller and already has the timing-local one-entry direct fast\\npath, so revisiting only a second pending-store slot is the cleanest\\nstructural bet: it targets store bursts under the 22% random dStall model\\nwithout adding load data to EX, changing the branch predictor, widening\\nretirement, or touching the IF replay timing path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the proven structural pieces: cold DIV/REM is\\nout of the universal EX path, the one-deep pending-store slot hides some\\nnormal-RAM store backpressure, and the registered instruction replay table\\nremoves most random iStall loss. The remaining structural stalls are now\\nconcentrated on the data side. A single pending-store entry lets one stalled\\nRAM store retire, but the next store still freezes the whole pipeline while\\nthe older slot drains, even though both stores already have their address,\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI memory metadata available in MEM.\\n\\nA two-entry store buffer was attempted before the large post-DIV and replay\\ntiming wins and failed placement on a much lar...\n  Line 51: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-044-r17s1\", \"title\": \"Tiny MRU Load Replay Latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already uses the two structural ideas that have paid\\nclearly: a one-deep pending-store slot for store-only dmem backpressure and\\na registered instruction replay table for random iStall. The remaining\\nstructural stalls are concentrated on normal-RAM loads. A load still freezes\\nthe whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low, even if the same\\naligned word was returned recently.\\n\\nThe prior 16-entry MEM-stage load replay buffer showed that this stall source\\nis real: it reduced CoreMark cycles on top of the instruction replay design.\\nIt missed the current best because the same-cycle table lookup, tag compare,\\nand store-update logic dropped Fmax too much. A much smaller MRU replay latch\\ntargets the same dmem-stall opportunity while keeping the MEM path close to\\nthe current best design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already uses the two structural ideas that have paid\\nclearly: a one-deep pending-store slot for store-only dmem backpressure and\\na registered instruction replay table for random iStall. The remaining\\nstructural stalls are concentrated on normal-RAM loads. A load still freezes\\nthe whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low, even if the same\\naligned word was returned recently.\\n\\nThe prior 16-entry MEM-stage load replay buffer showed that this stall source\\nis real: it reduced CoreMark cycles on top of the instruction replay design.\\nIt missed the current best because the same-cycle table lookup, tag compare,\\nand store-update logic dropped Fmax too much. A much smaller MRU replay latch\\ntargets the same dmem-stall opportunity while keeping the MEM path close to\\nthe current best design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry most-recently-used normal-RAM load replay latch in MEM. Each\\nentry stores a valid bit, an aligned word address tag, and the raw 32-bit word\\nreturned...\n  Line 53: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-046-r18s0\", \"title\": \"Source-Qualified Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL has already harvested the high-leverage Fmax wins:\\nDIV/REM is cold and iterative, the IF replay predictor hides most random\\niStall loss, replay tags are compressed/resetless, the RVFI order counter is\\nsegmented, and the ALU result mux has a hot/cold split. The next micro-opt\\nshould therefore avoid new memories, predictors, queues, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining always-present cycle loss in the control path is the\\nconservative load-use detector. It currently stalls whenever the load in\\nID/EX has a nonzero rd matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield of the\\nIF/ID instruction. That is necessary for true consumers, but false for many\\nlegal RV32I encodings: OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR do not read rs2; LUI, AUIPC,\\nJAL, FENCE, and EBREAK do not read either source. CoreMark has many pointer\\nupdates, immediate arithmetic, loads, and control instructions after loads,\\nso these false matches can still inject bubbles even after the instruction\\nreplay predictor improves frontend availability.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL has already harvested the high-leverage Fmax wins:\\nDIV/REM is cold and iterative, the IF replay predictor hides most random\\niStall loss, replay tags are compressed/resetless, the RVFI order counter is\\nsegmented, and the ALU result mux has a hot/cold split. The next micro-opt\\nshould therefore avoid new memories, predictors, queues, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining always-present cycle loss in the control path is the\\nconservative load-use detector. It currently stalls whenever the load in\\nID/EX has a nonzero rd matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield of the\\nIF/ID instruction. That is necessary for true consumers, but false for many\\nlegal RV32I encodings: OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR do not read rs2; LUI, AUIPC,\\nJAL, FENCE, and EBREAK do not read either source. CoreMark has...\n  Line 54: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-047-r18s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-Exact Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has strong timing wins from the registered instruction\\nreplay predictor, segmented RVFI order counter, pending-store slot, and\\nhot/cold ALU split. The remaining structural improvement should therefore\\navoid another cache, queue, predictor table, or load-data forwarding path that\\ncould disturb Fmax.\\n\\nThe load-use interlock is still structurally conservative. It stalls whenever\\na load in ID/EX has a nonzero destination matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2\\nbitfield of the IF/ID instruction. That is correct for true consumers, but it\\ncreates false bubbles for instructions where one or both fields are not\\nsource operands: OP-IMM and LOAD do not read rs2, JAL/LUI/AUIPC read neither,\\nFENCE/EBREAK read neither, and JALR reads only rs1. CoreMark's optimized code\\nhas dense immediate arithmetic, address-generation loads, calls, and loop\\ncontrol, so these false one-cycle bubbles are a plausible remaining IPC loss.\\n\\nA prior operand-aware interlock attempt broke selftest, so this hypothesis is\\ndeliberately conservative: decode source-use exactly for known legal\\ninstruction classes, keep reserved or unknown encodings on the current\\nboth-source stall policy, and do not add any MEM-to-EX load-data path. The\\ngoal is to recover only provably false load-use stalls while leaving all true\\nload consumers, dmem waits, branch recovery, divider interlocks, and RVFI\\nordering unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has strong timing wins from the registered instruction\\nreplay predictor, segmented RVFI order counter, pending-store slot, and\\nhot/cold ALU split. The remaining structural improvement should therefore\\navoid another cache, queue, predictor table, or load-data forwarding path that\\ncould disturb Fmax.\\n\\nThe load-use interlock is still structurally conservative. It stalls whenever\\na load in ID/EX h...\n  Line 55: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-048-r18s2\", \"title\": \"Kill-Gated I-Fetch Replay Fills\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design already has the predictor feature with the strongest\\nmeasured payoff: a 256-entry registered lookahead instruction replay table\\nwith compressed tags. It hides random iStall bubbles without putting a\\nsame-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up predictor attempts\\nthat added branch-direction state, return targets, victim entries, wider\\nreplay lines, or replay-cached next-PC metadata either saved too few cycles\\nor lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe next predictor slot should therefore keep the existing replay structure\\nand target replay quality rather than add another table. The current IF\\nstage refreshes the replay table on every live `io_imemReady` fetch, even\\nwhen that fetch is being killed by an EX redirect or IF flush. Those killed\\nwords are architecturally correct immutable instructions, but they are\\nwrong-path for the current execution and can evict useful hot-loop replay\\nentries in the direct-mapped table. Avoiding this pollution is a narrow\\nreplay-predictor improvement with little timing exposure: it changes the\\nreplay write-enable policy, not the current-cycle fetch-ready, branch/JAL\\ntarget, ALU, memory, or retirement paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design already has the predictor feature with the strongest\\nmeasured payoff: a 256-entry registered lookahead instruction replay table\\nwith compressed tags. It hides random iStall bubbles without putting a\\nsame-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up predictor attempts\\nthat added branch-direction state, return targets, victim entries, wider\\nreplay lines, or replay-cached next-PC metadata either saved too few cycles\\nor lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe next predictor slot should therefore keep the existing replay structure\\nand target replay quality rather than add another table. The cu...\n  Line 57: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-050-r19s1\", \"title\": \"Backend-Stall Shadow I-Fetch Prefetcher\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest cycle-count win from the registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table: hot instructions can be supplied during\\nrandom imem stalls without a same-cycle replay lookup on the IF critical\\npath. The remaining structural stalls are now mostly backend stalls from\\ndmem waits and long operations. During those cycles the frontend PC is held,\\ndecode is not accepting a new instruction, and the independent imem port is\\nusually wasted refreshing the same held PC or sitting idle.\\n\\nPrior fetch queues tried to decouple fetch and decode directly, but they put\\nqueue control on the normal zero-wait frontend path and lost Fmax. A more\\ntargeted structural angle is to use backend-stall cycles only as replay-table\\nwarmup time. The architectural fetch/decode stream remains held exactly as it\\nis today, while a shadow prefetch PC walks ahead through the existing static\\nbranch/direct-JAL prediction policy and fills the replay table with future\\ninstruction words. When the backend releases, later external imem stalls are\\nmore likely to hit in the already-proven registered replay structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest cycle-count win from the registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table: hot instructions can be supplied during\\nrandom imem stalls without a same-cycle replay lookup on the IF critical\\npath. The remaining structural stalls are now mostly backend stalls from\\ndmem waits and long operations. During those cycles the frontend PC is held,\\ndecode is not accepting a new instruction, and the independent imem port is\\nusually wasted refreshing the same held PC or sitting idle.\\n\\nPrior fetch queues tried to decouple fetch and decode directly, but they put\\nqueue control on the normal zero-wait frontend path and lost Fmax. A more\\ntargeted structural angle is to use ...\n  Line 60: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-053-r20s1\", \"title\": \"Same-Word Store Slot Coalescing\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already includes the structural wins that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot for normal-RAM store stalls\\nand a registered instruction replay table for random iStall. Broader store\\nbuffering attempts found the right bottleneck but were too expensive:\\ntwo-entry pending-store designs either failed placement, and the same-cycle\\nrefill variant also failed placement. That suggests the next structural\\nstore-side step should add capacity only for cases the existing single slot\\ncan handle locally.\\n\\nCoreMark updates list nodes, matrix cells, stack frames, and scalar state with\\nshort bursts of stores. When a pending normal-RAM store is already occupying\\nthe slot and dmem is not ready, a younger store to the same aligned word does\\nnot need a second physical buffer entry. It can be merged into the pending\\nslot because loads and MMIO are still held behind the slot, and there is no\\narchitectural observer between the two normal-RAM stores. This targets the\\nsame store-burst pressure as the failed two-entry FIFO, but with one address\\ncompare and one byte-mask merge instead of another queue entry or a wider\\ndmem arbiter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already includes the structural wins that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot for normal-RAM store stalls\\nand a registered instruction replay table for random iStall. Broader store\\nbuffering attempts found the right bottleneck but were too expensive:\\ntwo-entry pending-store designs either failed placement, and the same-cycle\\nrefill variant also failed placement. That suggests the next structural\\nstore-side step should add capacity only for cases the existing single slot\\ncan handle locally.\\n\\nCoreMark updates list nodes, matrix cells, stack frames, and scalar state with\\nshort bursts of stores. When a ...\n  Line 63: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-056-r21s1\", \"title\": \"Pending Store Slot Load Forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural pieces that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot hides normal-RAM store\\nbackpressure, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random iStall bubbles. The structural experiments since then show the\\nboundary clearly. Larger store buffers and same-cycle refill failed\\nplacement, same-word store coalescing did not reduce CoreMark cycles, and\\ndata replay tables recovered some load-stall cycles but cost too much Fmax.\\n\\nThe remaining store-slot bottleneck is narrower. When a normal-RAM store has\\nretired into the pending slot, the core currently blocks every younger memory\\noperation until the physical write drains, even if the younger operation is a\\nload of bytes that are already fully present in the pending store entry. That\\ncase does not require a second store-buffer entry or a dmem access: in\\nprogram order the store is already older and retired, so a same-word covered\\nload should read the store slot's bytes. CoreMark's stack, list, and state\\ncode can produce store/load reuse on nearby words, especially under random\\ndmem stalls that keep the slot valid for multiple cycles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural pieces that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot hides normal-RAM store\\nbackpressure, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random iStall bubbles. The structural experiments since then show the\\nboundary clearly. Larger store buffers and same-cycle refill failed\\nplacement, same-word store coalescing did not reduce CoreMark cycles, and\\ndata replay tables recovered some load-stall cycles but cost too much Fmax.\\n\\nThe remaining store-slot bottleneck is narrower. When a normal-RAM store has\\nretired into the pending slot, the core currentl...\n  Line 66: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-059-r22s1\", \"title\": \"Replay-Assisted ALU Pair Dual Issue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is now a fast single-issue design: cold DIV/REM is\\noff the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split improved Fmax, the one-entry\\nstore slot hides some dmem store stalls, and the two-bank registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall bubbles. The largest\\nremaining structural ceiling is therefore the single-retire backend. The\\ntop-level RVFI contract already exposes NRET=2, but channel 1 is unused.\\n\\nA normal dual-issue frontend would be unattractive because the external imem\\nport supplies only one 32-bit word per cycle. The banked replay predictor\\nchanges that tradeoff: after warmup, hot straight-line CoreMark code often\\nhas both the current instruction and the next sequential instruction in the\\nreplay table. That creates a narrow path to use RVFI channel 1 without\\nwidening the external memory interface or adding a full instruction cache.\\n\\nThe most promising structural angle is to issue a second instruction only\\nfor low-risk adjacent simple-integer pairs. This targets CoreMark's dense\\naddress, loop, CRC, and scalar arithmetic sequences while avoiding the\\nplacement failures seen from larger store buffers and the Fmax losses seen\\nfrom load replay tables, return predictors, and wider replay structures.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is now a fast single-issue design: cold DIV/REM is\\noff the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split improved Fmax, the one-entry\\nstore slot hides some dmem store stalls, and the two-bank registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall bubbles. The largest\\nremaining structural ceiling is therefore the single-retire backend. The\\ntop-level RVFI contract already exposes NRET=2, but channel 1 is unused.\\n\\nA normal dual-issue frontend would be unattractive because the external imem\\nport supplies only one 32-bit word per cycle. The b...\n  Line 69: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-062-r23s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Wait Simple-ALU Skid Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is a fast single-issue design with the largest proven\\nwins already in place: cold DIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU\\nsplit improved Fmax, the one-entry pending-store slot hides some store-only\\ndmem stalls, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random imem stalls. The remaining structural loss is therefore mainly\\nbackend pressure from dmem waits, especially loads. Today a load in MEM with\\n`io_dmemReady=0` freezes IF, ID, and EX even when the younger instruction in\\nID/EX is an independent simple ALU operation whose operands are already\\navailable and whose execution has no memory or control-flow side effects.\\n\\nPrior attempts to attack this bottleneck by replaying load data or adding\\nbroader store buffering reduced cycles but cost too much Fmax or placement\\nquality. A smaller structural decoupling point is more promising: let EX do\\none cycle of useful independent simple-ALU work while the older MEM load or\\nnonbufferable store is waiting, but keep retirement strictly in order. This\\ntargets the exact full-pipeline freeze caused by random dmem backpressure\\nwithout adding load data to EX, another replay table, another store-buffer\\nentry, a branch predictor, or a general superscalar backend.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is a fast single-issue design with the largest proven\\nwins already in place: cold DIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU\\nsplit improved Fmax, the one-entry pending-store slot hides some store-only\\ndmem stalls, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random imem stalls. The remaining structural loss is therefore mainly\\nbackend pressure from dmem waits, especially loads. Today a load in MEM with\\n`io_dmemReady=0` freezes IF, ID, and EX even when the younger instruction in\\nID/EX is an independent simp...\n  Line 72: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-065-r24s1\", \"title\": \"Replay-Fed Memory Plus ADDI Pair Issue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is a very strong single-issue design. The large wins\\nalready come from removing cold DIV/REM from the hot EX path, splitting the\\nALU hot/cold mux, adding a one-deep pending-store slot, segmenting the RVFI\\norder counter, and using a two-bank registered instruction replay predictor\\nto hide most random imem stalls. Recent structural attempts show that broad\\nbuffers, data replay tables, and general dual issue are too costly or too\\ndifficult, but they also point at the remaining ceiling: only one instruction\\ncan retire per cycle even when CoreMark presents simple pointer updates next\\nto memory operations.\\n\\nCoreMark's list, matrix, state, and stack code is dense with patterns such as\\na load or store followed by an independent ADDI pointer/index/stack update.\\nA full two-wide machine is risky, but this very narrow pair class uses only\\nthe existing single dmem access plus one younger simple immediate add. It\\ntargets a common structural bubble source without adding another memory port,\\nanother replay table, a load-data-to-EX bypass, a branch predictor, or a\\nsecond general ALU issue path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is a very strong single-issue design. The large wins\\nalready come from removing cold DIV/REM from the hot EX path, splitting the\\nALU hot/cold mux, adding a one-deep pending-store slot, segmenting the RVFI\\norder counter, and using a two-bank registered instruction replay predictor\\nto hide most random imem stalls. Recent structural attempts show that broad\\nbuffers, data replay tables, and general dual issue are too costly or too\\ndifficult, but they also point at the remaining ceiling: only one instruction\\ncan retire per cycle even when CoreMark presents simple pointer updates next\\nto memory operations.\\n\\nCoreMark's list, matrix, state, and stack code is dense with patter...\n  Line 74: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-068-r25s1\", \"title\": \"Single-Entry Last-Load Replay Latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural wins that have survived the\\nFPGA gate: the one-deep pending-store slot hides some store backpressure, and\\nthe two-bank registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall bubbles. The remaining structural stalls are mainly data-side load\\nwaits. When a normal-RAM load reaches MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, the\\nwhole in-order backend freezes even if the same aligned word was just read by\\nan earlier load.\\n\\nLarger load replay attempts proved that this opportunity is real because they\\nreduced CoreMark cycles, but their table lookups, tag compares, and store\\nupdate paths cost too much Fmax. The next structural bet should keep only the\\ncheapest useful form of that idea: one last-load replay entry. CoreMark's\\nstack, list, state, and matrix kernels often reload nearby or identical words\\nacross short loops, so a single MRU word can catch some random dStall cases\\nwithout a table, replacement policy, associative search, or load-data path\\ninto EX.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural wins that have survived the\\nFPGA gate: the one-deep pending-store slot hides some store backpressure, and\\nthe two-bank registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall bubbles. The remaining structural stalls are mainly data-side load\\nwaits. When a normal-RAM load reaches MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, the\\nwhole in-order backend freezes even if the same aligned word was just read by\\nan earlier load.\\n\\nLarger load replay attempts proved that this opportunity is real because they\\nreduced CoreMark cycles, but their table lookups, tag compares, and store\\nupdate paths cost too much Fmax. The next structural bet should keep only the\\ncheapest useful form of that idea: one last-load replay entry. CoreMark's\\nstack, list, state, and matrix ke...","metadata":{"matches":26,"truncated":false},"title":"MEM-to-EX Load-Use Bypass|load-use bypass|load-use","time":{"start":1778855887252,"end":1778855887333}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_05981d9377aa2490016a072fcf1968819b80ab5d5527152e51"}},"id":"prt_e2c12c181001guyX8Mle3xyxyI","sessionID":"ses_1d3eea7f2ffevXBifDVCm56EIH","messageID":"msg_e2c128a4e001KBcv1SDuvITTCH"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778855887355,"sessionID":"ses_1d3eea7f2ffevXBifDVCm56EIH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_DA1p5r6IiBLgpj2sIdRcMmCv","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/rtl/hazard_unit.sv","offset":1,"limit":220},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/v1/rtl/hazard_unit.sv</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: // rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n2: //\n3: // Detects the only data hazard the textbook 5-stage doesn't cover via\n4: // forwarding: load-use. A LOAD in EX produces its data only after MEM,\n5: // so an instruction immediately behind that consumes the LOAD's rd\n6: // must be stalled by exactly one cycle.\n7: //\n8: // Outputs:\n9: //   stall_if / stall_id : freeze the PC reg and the IF/ID combinational\n10: //                          payload (cleared by ID's flush input).\n11: //   flush_if / flush_id : on EX redirect (branch mispredict or jump), kill\n12: //                          the in-flight instructions ahead of the target.\n13: //   flush_id            : also kills ID's own register on load-use to\n14: //                          inject a single-cycle bubble between LOAD\n15: //                          and the dependent instruction.\n16: //\n17: // Latency:        combinational.\n18: // RVFI fields:    n/a (governs validity of subsequent retirements).\n19: module hazard_unit (\n20:   input  logic       id_ex_mem_read,    // ID/EX.ctrl.mem_read (LOAD in EX)\n21:   input  logic [4:0] id_ex_rd,          // ID/EX.rd            (LOAD's dest)\n22:   input  logic [4:0] if_id_rs1,         // IF/ID instr[19:15]  (next rs1)\n23:   input  logic [4:0] if_id_rs2,         // IF/ID instr[24:20]  (next rs2)\n24:   input  logic       redirect,          // EX branch mispredict or jump redirect\n25:   // Effective fetch availability from IF. It is high for a live imem word\n26:   // or for a registered replay hit that can stand in for an external stall.\n27:   input  logic       imem_ready,\n28:   input  logic       ex_long_busy,      // EX has a multi-cycle op occupying ID/EX\n29:   // MEM-stage precise wait: live load/nonbufferable store waiting on dmem,\n30:   // or a younger memory operation blocked behind the pending-store slot.\n31:   input  logic       mem_wait,\n32:   output logic       stall_if,          // PC reg holds\n33:   output logic       stall_id,          // ID/EX register holds (vs. bubble)\n34:   output logic       flush_if,          // IF/ID comb output -> NOP\n35:   output logic       flush_id,          // ID/EX register captures '0\n36:   output logic       stall_ex_mem,      // EX/MEM register holds\n37:   output logic       hold_mem_wb        // MEM/WB clears valid only;\n38:                                         // data fields stay (for fwd)\n39: );\n40: \n41:   logic load_use_hazard;\n42:   logic imem_stall;\n43: \n44:   always_comb begin\n45:     load_use_hazard = id_ex_mem_read\n46:                    && (id_ex_rd == if_id_rs1 || id_ex_rd == if_id_rs2)\n47:                    && (id_ex_rd != 5'b0);\n48:     imem_stall = !imem_ready;\n49: \n50:     // PC reg holds on any stall reason.\n51:     stall_if      = load_use_hazard || imem_stall || mem_wait || ex_long_busy;\n52:     // IF/ID combinational payload: NOP whenever we wouldn't have a valid\n53:     // instruction this cycle (mispredict/jump recovery, or imem didn't\n54:     // deliver).\n55:     flush_if      = redirect || imem_stall;\n56:     // ID/EX register:\n57:     //   - mem_wait    -> hold        (preserve in-flight pipeline state)\n58:     //   - load_use    -> bubble      (1-cycle stall between LOAD + use)\n59:     //   - redirect    -> bubble      (kill wrong-path)\n60:     //   - otherwise   -> capture\n61:     // mem_wait takes precedence over load_use's bubble: re-evaluate\n62:     // load_use next cycle when the bus unblocks. flush_id is 1 only when\n63:     // we want bubble (not hold).\n64:     stall_id      = mem_wait || load_use_hazard || ex_long_busy;\n65:     flush_id      = (load_use_hazard || redirect) && !mem_wait;\n66:     // EX/MEM register: holds on mem_wait (LOAD waits in MEM until the bus\n67:     // delivers, or a younger memory op waits behind the store slot).\n68:     stall_ex_mem  = mem_wait;\n69:     // MEM/WB register: on mem_wait the previously-retired instruction's\n70:     // data fields stay alive for forwarding (e.g. a held BNE needs the\n71:     // LOAD's load_data via fwd_mem_wb), but valid is cleared so we don't\n72:     // double-retire / double-write the regfile.\n73:     hold_mem_wb   = mem_wait;\n74:   end\n75: \n76: endmodule\n\n(End of file - total 76 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"// rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n//\n// Detects the only data hazard the textbook 5-stage doesn't cover via\n// forwarding: load-use. A LOAD in EX produces its data only after MEM,\n// so an instruction immediately behind that consumes the LOAD's rd\n// must be stalled by exactly one cycle.\n//\n// Outputs:\n//   stall_if / stall_id : freeze the PC reg and the IF/ID combinational\n//                          payload (cleared by ID's flush input).\n//   flush_if / flush_id : on EX redirect (branch mispredict or jump), kill\n//                          the in-flight instructions ahead of the target.\n//   flush_id            : also kills ID's own register on load-use to\n//                          inject a single-cycle bubble between LOAD\n//                          and the dependent instruction.\n//\n// Latency:        combinational.\n// RVFI fields:    n/a (governs validity of subsequent retirements).\nmodule hazard_unit (\n  input  logic       id_ex_mem_read,    // ID/EX.ctrl.mem_read (LOAD in EX)","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/v1/rtl/hazard_unit.sv","time":{"start":1778855887272,"end":1778855887354}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_05981d9377aa2490016a072fcf197