{"type":"text","timestamp":1778871113164,"sessionID":"ses_1d30823c8ffeYwNOsLBK2i2tAE","part":{"id":"prt_e2cfb15060019VjtNu6fyD4SIj","messageID":"msg_e2cfacf01001m1sztwpCUfxNSX","sessionID":"ses_1d30823c8ffeYwNOsLBK2i2tAE","type":"text","text":"Writing the YAML now with a structural frontend-buffer hypothesis that stays off the known bad predictor path.","time":{"start":1778871112966,"end":1778871113163},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"msg_00b7aafbac32c5ad016a076b48d8fc81998134d12d5bec241f","phase":"commentary"}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778871137339,"sessionID":"ses_1d30823c8ffeYwNOsLBK2i2tAE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_irXO3nVV8Q448FzsptJHynGM","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r9s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\n+title: \"Decoupled Sequential Fetch Buffer\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The current `bench` frontend still couples decode directly to the live imem\n+  response: `if_stage` emits a combinational IF/ID bundle from the current PC,\n+  and `hazard_unit` turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into an immediate bubble.\n+  That leaves two costs on the table under the tournament's random iStall model:\n+  isolated fetch stalls always reduce IPC, and the raw imem-to-decode path stays\n+  in the timing cone.\n+\n+  The recent negative lessons only rule out fetch-side prediction and replay\n+  structures that add tag compares, target muxing, or decode work onto the live\n+  `pc -> next fetch` path. They do not rule out a simpler decoupling structure\n+  that stays purely sequential and flushes on EX redirect.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Replace the current combinational IF/ID feed with a tiny sequential fetch\n+  buffer: a registered oldest instruction plus one lookahead slot for the next\n+  fallthrough word. `if_stage` keeps prefetching sequential `pc`/`instr` pairs\n+  whenever the buffer has space and `io_imemReady` is high, while ID consumes\n+  the oldest buffered instruction only when it is not stalled.\n+\n+  This changes the frontend structurally without becoming a predictor. There is\n+  no branch-direction guess, no BTB/tag lookup, and no same-cycle target add on\n+  the fetch critical path. On EX redirect, the buffer is simply cleared and\n+  refilled from the architectural target PC. `hazard_unit` should stall on true\n+  frontend starvation instead of raw `io_imemReady`, so buffered instructions\n+  can hide isolated iStall cycles. If successful, this should raise fitness from\n+  both sides: less random-stall IPC loss and a shorter imem-to-decode timing\n+  cone than the current direct combinational feed.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Replace the direct combinational IF output with a tiny sequential fetch queue (registered head plus one lookahead slot), keep the fallthrough prefetch path simple, and flush all buffered wrong-path instructions on EX redirect.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Stop treating raw `io_imemReady` low as an unconditional frontend bubble; instead stall only when the fetch buffer cannot supply a valid instruction, while preserving existing load-use, divider, dmem, and store-slot fencing semantics.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire frontend availability or starvation signals from `if_stage` into `hazard_unit` so the rest of the pipeline sees a decoupled fetch source rather than the live imem handshake directly.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add pipeline tests that force isolated and back-to-back imem stalls, checking that buffered fallthrough instructions still retire in order and that redirects flush any prefetched wrong-path words cleanly.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r9s1.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r9s1.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r9s1.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r9s1.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\n+title: \"Decoupled Sequential Fetch Buffer\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The current `bench` frontend still couples decode directly to the live imem\n+  response: `if_stage` emits a combinational IF/ID bundle from the current PC,\n+  and `hazard_unit` turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into an immediate bubble.\n+  That leaves two costs on the table under the tournament's random iStall model:\n+  isolated fetch stalls always reduce IPC, and the raw imem-to-decode path stays\n+  in the timing cone.\n+\n+  The recent negative lessons only rule out fetch-side prediction and replay\n+  structures that add tag compares, target muxing, or decode work onto the live\n+  `pc -> next fetch` path. They do not rule out a simpler decoupling structure\n+  that stays purely sequential and flushes on EX redirect.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Replace the current combinational IF/ID feed with a tiny sequential fetch\n+  buffer: a registered oldest instruction plus one lookahead slot for the next\n+  fallthrough word. `if_stage` keeps prefetching sequential `pc`/`instr` pairs\n+  whenever the buffer has space and `io_imemReady` is high, while ID consumes\n+  the oldest buffered instruction only when it is not stalled.\n+\n+  This changes the frontend structurally without becoming a predictor. There is\n+  no branch-direction guess, no BTB/tag lookup, and no same-cycle target add on\n+  the fetch critical path. On EX redirect, the buffer is simply cleared and\n+  refilled from the architectural target PC. `hazard_unit` should stall on true\n+  frontend starvation instead of raw `io_imemReady`, so buffered instructions\n+  can hide isolated iStall cycles. If successful, this should raise fitness from\n+  both sides: less random-stall IPC loss and a shorter imem-to-decode timing\n+  cone than the current direct combinational feed.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Replace the direct combinational IF output with a tiny sequential fetch queue (registered head plus one lookahead slot), keep the fallthrough prefetch path simple, and flush all buffered wrong-path instructions on EX redirect.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Stop treating raw `io_imemReady` low as an unconditional frontend bubble; instead stall only when the fetch buffer cannot supply a valid instruction, while preserving existing load-use, divider, dmem, and store-slot fencing semantics.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire frontend availability or starvation signals from `if_stage` into `hazard_unit` so the rest of the pipeline sees a decoupled fetch source rather than the live imem handshake directly.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add pipeline tests that force isolated and back-to-back imem stalls, checking that buffered fallthrough instructions still retire in order and that redirects flush any prefetched wrong-path words cleanly.\"\n\n","files":[{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r9s1.yaml","relativePath":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r9s1.yaml","type":"add","patch":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r9s1.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r9s1.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r9s1.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\n+title: \"Decoupled Sequential Fetch Buffer\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The current `bench` frontend still couples decode directly to the live imem\n+  response: `if_stage` emits a combinational IF/ID bundle from the current PC,\n+  and `hazard_unit` turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into an immediate bubble.\n+  That leaves two costs on the table under the tournament's random iStall model:\n+  isolated fetch stalls always reduce IPC, and the raw imem-to-decode path stays\n+  in the timing cone.\n+\n+  The recent negative lessons only rule out fetch-side prediction and replay\n+  structures that add tag compares, target muxing, or decode work onto the live\n+  `pc -> next fetch` path. They do not rule out a simpler decoupling structure\n+  that stays purely sequential and flushes on EX redirect.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Replace the current combinational IF/ID feed with a tiny sequential fetch\n+  buffer: a registered oldest instruction plus one lookahead slot for the next\n+  fallthrough word. `if_stage` keeps prefetching sequential `pc`/`instr` pairs\n+  whenever the buffer has space and `io_imemReady` is high, while ID consumes\n+  the oldest buffered instruction only when it is not stalled.\n+\n+  This changes the frontend structurally without becoming a predictor. There is\n+  no branch-direction guess, no BTB/tag lookup, and no same-cycle target add on\n+  the fetch critical path. On EX redirect, the buffer is simply cleared and\n+  refilled from the architectural target PC. `hazard_unit` should stall on true\n+  frontend starvation instead of raw `io_imemReady`, so buffered instructions\n+  can hide isolated iStall cycles. If successful, this should raise fitness from\n+  both sides: less random-stall IPC loss and a shorter imem-to-decode timing\n+  cone than the current direct combinational feed.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Replace the direct combinational IF output with a tiny sequential fetch queue (registered head plus one lookahead slot), keep the fallthrough prefetch path simple, and flush all buffered wrong-path instructions on EX redirect.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Stop treating raw `io_imemReady` low as an unconditional frontend bubble; instead stall only when the fetch buffer cannot supply a valid instruction, while preserving existing load-use, divider, dmem, and store-slot fencing semantics.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire frontend availability or starvation signals from `if_stage` into `hazard_unit` so the rest of the pipeline sees a decoupled fetch source rather than the live imem handshake directly.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add pipeline tests that force isolated and back-to-back imem stalls, checking that buffered fallthrough instructions still retire in order and that redirects flush any prefetched wrong-path words cleanly.\"\n","additions":42,"deletions":0}],"diagnostics":{},"truncated":false},"title":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r9s1.yaml","time":{"start":1778871137336,"end":1778871137338}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_00b7aafbac32c5ad016a076b49119c8199b4b68fb6bdbd4b2f"}},"id":"prt_e2cfb15cc001zsqNQPVaMuOf7K","sessionID":"ses_1d30823c8ffeYwNOsLBK2i2tAE","messageID":"msg_e2cfacf01001m1sztwpCUfxNSX"}}
