{"type":"text","timestamp":1778874704039,"sessionID":"ses_1d2d0d4b2ffeUPb3Upo3KYHTCb","part":{"id":"prt_e2d31e0670010jDhL4pq2fN9PD","messageID":"msg_e2d30a021001cooHovKJpHCR7M","sessionID":"ses_1d2d0d4b2ffeUPb3Upo3KYHTCb","type":"text","text":"Writing `cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r11s2.yaml` with the backedge-fold-capacity hypothesis now.","time":{"start":1778874703975,"end":1778874704038},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"msg_0029228ccc8804ec016a07794fd41081988fd1c56c6802468c","phase":"commentary"}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778874718443,"sessionID":"ses_1d2d0d4b2ffeUPb3Upo3KYHTCb","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_n8aHufs6lGne9vKYKG7TPUwi","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r11s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r11s2\n+title: \"Two-Entry Backedge Fold Buffer\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The only predictor family that did not materially hurt `bench` was the\n+  single-slot backedge fold buffer, which matched the current best fitness at\n+  447.40 while keeping `io_imemAddr` on the existing sequential path. That\n+  suggests the remaining predictor headroom is not in more aggressive fetch\n+  redirection, but in improving hit rate inside that same timing-safe\n+  fold-and-inject shape.\n+\n+  CoreMark rotates among several hot counted loops across the list, matrix,\n+  and state kernels. A one-entry fold slot likely thrashes between those\n+  backedges and leaves repeatable taken-branch bubbles uncovered even though\n+  the underlying predictor mechanism appears Fmax-safe on this FPGA.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Replace the one-slot backedge fold idea with a two-entry backedge-only fold\n+  buffer trained from EX. Each entry records a taken backward branch PC, its\n+  resolved target PC, and the first target instruction once that target is\n+  fetched architecturally. On a later hit, `if_stage` still keeps\n+  `io_imemAddr` on the normal sequential path, but tags the in-flight branch\n+  so the following cycle can inject the cached target instruction into IF/ID\n+  and repair the fetch PC to `target + 4` only after the folded target has\n+  entered the pipe.\n+\n+  Keep the scope deliberately narrow: only taken backward conditional\n+  branches, no `JAL`, no `JALR`, no taken-target mux in the live fetch path,\n+  and no multi-instruction stream mode. Compared with the timing-neutral\n+  one-entry fold buffer, the extra capacity should reduce aliasing across\n+  CoreMark's hot loops while preserving the one predictor shape that has not\n+  already cost Fmax on this core.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: low\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add the minimal fold-prediction metadata needed to carry a folded backedge through IF/ID and ID/EX to EX repair logic.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Implement a two-entry backedge fold buffer that injects a cached target instruction on a branch-PC hit while leaving `io_imemAddr` on the existing sequential path.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the fold metadata alongside the branch so EX can distinguish folded hits from normal control flow without re-decoding predictor state.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train the two fold entries from resolved taken backward branches and generate the repair behavior for loop-exit or stale-entry misses.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire fold-buffer train, inject, and repair signals between IF and EX without changing the top-level IO contract.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add pipeline tests for repeated backedge fold hits, loop-exit recovery, and coexistence with random `io_imemReady` stalls.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r11s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r11s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r11s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r11s2.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r11s2\n+title: \"Two-Entry Backedge Fold Buffer\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The only predictor family that did not materially hurt `bench` was the\n+  single-slot backedge fold buffer, which matched the current best fitness at\n+  447.40 while keeping `io_imemAddr` on the existing sequential path. That\n+  suggests the remaining predictor headroom is not in more aggressive fetch\n+  redirection, but in improving hit rate inside that same timing-safe\n+  fold-and-inject shape.\n+\n+  CoreMark rotates among several hot counted loops across the list, matrix,\n+  and state kernels. A one-entry fold slot likely thrashes between those\n+  backedges and leaves repeatable taken-branch bubbles uncovered even though\n+  the underlying predictor mechanism appears Fmax-safe on this FPGA.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Replace the one-slot backedge fold idea with a two-entry backedge-only fold\n+  buffer trained from EX. Each entry records a taken backward branch PC, its\n+  resolved target PC, and the first target instruction once that target is\n+  fetched architecturally. On a later hit, `if_stage` still keeps\n+  `io_imemAddr` on the normal sequential path, but tags the in-flight branch\n+  so the following cycle can inject the cached target instruction into IF/ID\n+  and repair the fetch PC to `target + 4` only after the folded target has\n+  entered the pipe.\n+\n+  Keep the scope deliberately narrow: only taken backward conditional\n+  branches, no `JAL`, no `JALR`, no taken-target mux in the live fetch path,\n+  and no multi-instruction stream mode. Compared with the timing-neutral\n+  one-entry fold buffer, the extra capacity should reduce aliasing across\n+  CoreMark's hot loops while preserving the one predictor shape that has not\n+  already cost Fmax on this core.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: low\n+changes:\n+  - fi