still cost too\\nmuch in tag-compare and target-mux depth for this FPGA.\\n\\nCoreMark is still rich in hot taken loop backedges, though, so there is\\nlikely one remaining predictor shape worth trying: recover a taken-branch\\ncycle without touching `io_imemAddr` generation itself.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor experiments on `bench` have all failed for the same reason:\\nthey improved branch behavior only by adding work to the frontend `pc ->\\nnext_pc` cone. ID-stage BTFNT hurt Fmax by pulling decode and target\\ngeneration forward, and even tiny fetch-side target caches still cost too\\nmuch in tag-compare and target-mux depth for this FPGA.\\n\\nCoreMark is still rich in hot taken loop backedges, though, so there is\\nlikely one remaining predictor shape worth trying: recover a taken-branch\\ncycle without touching `io_imemAddr` generation itself.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry EX-trained branch-folding slot for taken backward branches.\\nWhen EX resolves a taken backedge, record the branch PC and target PC, then\\nfill the slot's cached target instruction the next time that target is fetched\\narchitecturally. On a later hit for the same branch PC, keep the normal\\nsequential PC path, but mark the branch as predicted-taken and inject the\\ncached target instruction/PC into IF->ID on the following cycle instead of the\\nwrong-path sequential word.\\n\\nEX remains the architectural source of truth. A correctly folded branch would\\nrepair the fetch PC to `target + 4` while preserving the injected target in\\nID, and a loop-exit miss would redirect back to `pc ...\n  Line 17: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"RVFI-Safe Load-to-Store Rs2 Bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining bench lessons point at one backend IPC loss that is still both\\nplausible and narrow: a younger STORE always bubbles behind an older LOAD\\nwhen the dependency is only on STORE data (`rs2`), even though the store does\\nnot actually consume that value until MEM. CoreMark is store-heavy enough\\nthat this specific bubble class is worth attacking, and prior results show\\nthe core tolerates backend-only store-side changes much better than any\\nfrontend speculation.\\n\\nThe last two attempts at this idea broke `rvfi_reg_check`, which is the key\\nlesson to preserve here: the win is not in a broader hazard relaxation, but\\nin making the store's late data path and its architectural `rs2_rdata`\\ncanonicalize from the same source.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining bench lessons point at one backend IPC loss that is still both\\nplausible and narrow: a younger STORE always bubbles behind an older LOAD\\nwhen the dependency is only on STORE data (`rs2`), even though the store does\\nnot actually consume that value until MEM. CoreMark is store-heavy enough\\nthat this specific bubble class is worth attacking, and prior results show\\nthe core tolerates backend-only store-side changes much better than any\\nfrontend speculation.\\n\\nThe last two attempts at this idea broke `rvfi_reg_check`, which is the key\\nlesson to preserve here: the win is not in a broader hazard relaxation, but\\nin making the store's late data path and its architectural `rs2_rdata`\\ncanonicalize from the same source.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Relax the load-use interlock only for the single safe case where an older\\nLOAD's `rd` matches a younger STORE's `rs2` but not its address-base `rs1`.\\nKeep every other load-use case unchanged, so the fetch path, EX operand\\ntiming, and general hazard policy stay exactly as they are today.\\n\\nTo make that safe, move the dependent STORE's fina...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"RVFI-Safe Late Store-Data Bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\\nthat leave the fragile fetch path alone: first moving DIV/REM off the hot EX\