one regressed: ID-stage BTFNT added decode and target-generation delay, and\\neven tiny taken-target caches still paid too much tag-compare and target-mux\\ncost in IF. But CoreMark remains dominated by tight taken loop backedges, so\\nalways waiting for EX to redirect still leaves repeatable control bubbles on\\nthe table.\\n\\nThe best remaining predictor angle is therefore one that predicts only the\\nhottest already-seen backedge while leaving `io_imemAddr` generation on its\\ncurrent simple sequential path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every predictor attempt on `bench` that touched the frontend `pc -> next_pc`\\ncone regressed: ID-stage BTFNT added decode and target-generation delay, and\\neven tiny taken-target caches still paid too much tag-compare and target-mux\\ncost in IF. But CoreMark remains dominated by tight taken loop backedges, so\\nalways waiting for EX to redirect still leaves repeatable control bubbles on\\nthe table.\\n\\nThe best remaining predictor angle is therefore one that predicts only the\\nhottest already-seen backedge while leaving `io_imemAddr` generation on its\\ncurrent simple sequential path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry EX-trained branch-fold buffer for taken backward conditional\\nbranches. When EX resolves a taken backedge, record the branch PC and target\\nPC, then capture the target instruction the next time that target is fetched\\narchitecturally. On a later hit for the same branch PC, keep the normal\\nsequential fetch address path, but tag the branch as predicted-taken and\\ninject the cached target instruction/PC into IF->ID on the following cycle\\ninstead of the wrong-path fallthrough word.\\n\\nEX remains the architectural source of truth. A correct prediction repairs\\nthe fetch PC to `target + 4` after the folded target has already ...\n  Line 23: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Zero-Bubble Store Slot Refill\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` core already proved that deferred normal-RAM stores\\nare a winning backend lever, but the one-deep slot still loses a bubble\\nwhenever a pending store finally drains and the next instruction in MEM is\\nanother bufferable RAM store. Hazard control fences that younger store until\\nthe slot bit clears, so bursty CoreMark store traffic still pays a handoff\\npenalty even though the core already knows both stores' addr/data/mask and\\nonly needs to preserve oldest-first external visibility.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` core already proved that deferred normal-RAM stores\\nare a winning backend lever, but the one-deep slot still loses a bubble\\nwhenever a pending store finally drains and the next instruction in MEM is\\nanother bufferable RAM store. Hazard control fences that younger store until\\nthe slot bit clears, so bursty CoreMark store traffic still pays a handoff\\npenalty even though the core already knows both stores' addr/data/mask and\\nonly needs to preserve oldest-first external visibility.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Teach the existing single deferred-store slot to drain and refill in the same\\ncycle instead of growing it into a true two-entry queue. When\\n`store_slot_valid` is set and `io_dmemReady` returns, `mem_stage` should\\ndrive the older deferred store to the bus and, if the current EX/MEM\\ninstruction is another aligned normal-RAM store, immediately capture that\\nyounger store into the same slot while retiring it once through RVFI.\\n\\n`hazard_unit` would then keep fencing loads, MMIO/non-bufferable stores, and\\n`EBREAK` behind a pending slot, but allow exactly one younger bufferable RAM\\nstore through on the drain cycle for a refill. This keeps the proven\\nbackend-only store-buffering strategy, avoids the placement/routing cost that\\nkilled the real two-entry queue, and should recover part of the st...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Canonical Late Store-Data Lane\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The proven `bench` wins have come from backend-only structural changes that\\nimprove IPC or Fmax without touching the fragile fetch path. The remaining\\nbubble that still looks worth chasing is the forced load-use stall when a\\nyounger STORE depends on an older LOAD only for `rs2` write data: the store\\naddress is ready in EX, but the current pipeline still treats store data as\\nan EX-time operand and burns a full bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The proven `bench` wins have come from backend-only structural changes that\\nimprove IPC or Fmax without touching the fragile fetch path. The remaining\\nbubble that still looks worth chasing is the forced load-use stall when a\\nyounger STORE depends on an older LOAD only for `rs2` write data: the store\\naddress is ready in EX, but the current pipeline still treats store data as\\nan EX-time operand and burns a full bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Retiming STORE data to the stage where it becomes architecturally visible is\\nstill the best structural next step, but it has to be implemented as a\\ncoherent data lane rather than a late one-off bypass. Add explicit metadata\\nfor when store data may arrive from WB and carry it through EX/MEM; relax the\\nload-use interlock only for the safe case where a younger STORE's `rs2`\\ndepends on an older LOAD while `rs1` remains independent. In MEM, choose one\\ncanonical final store-data value and use that same value for physical\\n`dmem_wdata`, deferred-store-slot capture, MEM/WB `rs2_val`, and RVFI\\n`rs2_rdata` / `mem_wdata`. This keeps the fetch path unchanged, preserves the\\nexisting zero-wait store fast path, and directly targets a real CoreMark\\nbubble source that prior attempts exposed but did not yet implement\\ncorrectly.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add late-store-dat...\n  Line 25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"One-Entry Short-Loop Stream Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Every prior `bench` predictor that touched the live fetch `pc -> next_pc`\\ndecision regressed, which says the remaining predictor win cannot be another\\nper-fetch target compare or ID-stage redirect. CoreMark is still dominated by\\ntiny hot counted loops under random `io_imemReady` stalls, so the best\\nremaining opportunity is to amortize predictor cost across several loop-body\\ninstructions instead of paying it every taken branch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every prior `bench` predictor that touched the live fetch `pc -> next_pc`\\ndecision regressed, which says the remaining predictor win cannot be another\\nper-fetch target compare or ID-stage redirect. CoreMark is still dominated by\\ntiny hot counted loops under random `io_imemReady` stalls, so the best\\nremaining opportunity is to amortize predictor cost across several loop-body\\ninstructions instead of paying it every taken branch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry EX-trained short-loop stream buffer for small taken backward\\nconditional branches. When EX resolves a taken backedge whose target-to-branch\\nspan is small (for example `<= 4` instructions), capture the loop start PC,\\nbranch PC, and the sequential instructions in that window into a registered\\nbuffer. On a later hit at the loop entry, IF enters a local stream mode and\\nsources the buffered instructions for the predicted-taken iterations instead of\\nre-fetching them from imem one by one.\\n\\nThis keeps EX as the architectural source of truth and avoids the proven bad\\npath of same-cycle frontend target generation. Compared with the earlier\\nsingle-branch predictors, it can hide both the taken-backedge bubble and\\nrandom imem stall bubbles across the whole buffered loop body, so one entry\\nmay repay its control cost better than another fetch-path BTB or fold slot.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": ...\n  Line 26: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Shared RV32M Multiply Datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current `bench` core already found its two big wins by removing costly\\ncontrol and divider work from the always-hot paths, while later fetch-side\\npredictors and MEM-side queue refinements mostly regressed or failed\\nplacement. One localized datapath cost still stands out in `rtl/alu.sv`:\\nthe real build computes three separate 64-bit products (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`,\\n`mul_su`) just to serve the four MUL-family instructions.\\n\\nCoreMark does use multiply in hot code, so turning MUL into a multi-cycle\\npath would likely hurt IPC. A better micro-opt is to keep one-cycle RV32M\\nlatency but remove redundant multiplier hardware and mux fanout from the EX\\ndatapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current `bench` core already found its two big wins by removing costly\\ncontrol and divider work from the always-hot paths, while later fetch-side\\npredictors and MEM-side queue refinements mostly regressed or failed\\nplacement. One localized datapath cost still stands out in `rtl/alu.sv`:\\nthe real build computes three separate 64-bit products (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`,\\n`mul_su`) just to serve the four MUL-family instructions.\\n\\nCoreMark does use multiply in hot code, so turning MUL into a multi-cycle\\npath would likely hurt IPC. A better micro-opt is to keep one-cycle RV32M\\nlatency but remove redundant multiplier hardware and mux fanout from the EX\\ndatapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel MUL-family product trees in `rtl/alu.sv` with a\\nsingle shared sign-configurable multiplier. Use one 33x33 extended product\\nwhose extra sign bits are selected from the opcode so the same result can\\nproduce `MUL`, `MULH`, `MULHU`, and `MULHSU`: low 32 bits for `MUL`, high\\n32 bits for the three high-half variants, with the existing ALTO