{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778877960639,"sessionID":"ses_1d29f129effe6qj5AeaxhR0Fut","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_do1vnEaLjX5xwOw1XadqMo0H","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r13s2.yaml","offset":1,"limit":240},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r13s2.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: id: hyp-20260515-003-r13s2\n2: title: \"Accepted-History Backedge Fold Slot\"\n3: category: predictor\n4: motivation: |\n5:   Predictor work on `bench` has almost no safe frontend budget: ID redirects,\n6:   BTB-style target lookup, and loop streaming all lost more Fmax than they\n7:   gained IPC. The only predictor shape that stayed competitive was the\n8:   one-slot backedge fold buffer because it kept `io_imemAddr` on the existing\n9:   sequential path. Its remaining weakness is fill latency: the slot only\n10:   becomes useful after the target instruction is fetched again\n11:   architecturally, so the first observed taken backedge and any iStall-delayed\n12:   refill still pay full bubbles.\n13: hypothesis: |\n14:   Keep the single active backedge fold slot and the sequential `io_imemAddr`\n15:   path, but train the slot from a tiny recent accepted-fetch history instead\n16:   of waiting for a post-redirect refetch of the target word. When EX resolves\n17:   a taken backward branch, search the last few architecturally accepted\n18:   `(pc, instr)` pairs; if the target PC is present, snapshot that real\n19:   instruction immediately into the fold slot and mark it usable for the next\n20:   loop iteration. Fall back to the old delayed fill only on a history miss.\n21: \n22:   This stays inside the only predictor shape that has been timing-safe on\n23:   `bench`: one lookup entry in IF, no extra target mux on the live fetch cone,\n24:   and no multi-entry select logic. It directly attacks the remaining warm-up\n25:   and refill misses for CoreMark's tight backedges while avoiding the unsafe\n26:   \"injected side value with no accepted backing entry\" behavior called out by\n27:   the last broken fold experiment.\n28: expected_impact:\n29:   fitness_delta_pct: 3\n30:   confidence: medium\n31: changes:\n32:   - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n33:     description: \"Add fold-prediction sideband fields and a compact recent-fetch history record type for a single-slot backedge fold predictor.\"\n34:   - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n35:     description: \"Keep the sequential PC path, record a tiny history of architecturally accepted fetch `(pc, instr)` pairs, and expose the active fold slot plus history data for training.\"\n36:   - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n37:     description: \"Latch fold-hit metadata alongside the instruction so EX can validate or repair folded backedge predictions without re-decoding predictor state.\"\n38:   - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n39:     description: \"Train taken backward branches from the accepted-fetch history, fall back to delayed fill on history miss, and keep EX as the architectural source of truth for redirect and trap behavior.\"\n40:   - file: rtl/core.sv\n41:     description: \"Wire the recent-fetch-history and fold-training signals between IF and EX while preserving the existing RVFI and bus contracts.\"\n42:   - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n43:     description: \"Add regression tests for repeated taken backedges, history-seeded fold fill, and stall/flush interactions so no wrong-path retirement leaks through prediction.\"\n\n(End of file - total 43 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260515-003-r13s2\ntitle: \"Accepted-History Backedge Fold Slot\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Predictor work on `bench` has almost no safe frontend budget: ID redirects,\n  BTB-style target lookup, and loop streaming all lost more Fmax than they\n  gained IPC. The only predictor shape that stayed competitive was the\n  one-slot backedge fold buffer because it kept `io_imemAddr` on the existing\n  sequential path. Its remaining weakness is fill latency: the slot only\n  becomes useful after the target instruction is fetched again\n  architecturally, so the first observed taken backedge and any iStall-delayed\n  refill still pay full bubbles.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep the single active backedge fold slot and the sequential `io_imemAddr`\n  path, but train the slot from a tiny recent accepted-fetch history instead\n  of waiting for a post-redirect refetch of the target word. When EX resolves\n  a taken backward branch, search the last few architecturally accepted\n  `(pc, instr)` pairs; if the target PC is present, snapshot that real\n  instruction immediately into the fold slot and mark it usable for the next\n  loop iteration. Fall back to the old delayed fill only on a history miss.","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r13s2.yaml","time":{"start":1778877960629,"end":1778877960637}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_01f112a280873d91016a0786075580819bbcd38a283d22a4b0"}},"id":"prt_e2d638d030015aWTgDxvdimjP7","sessionID":"ses_1d29f129effe6qj5AeaxhR0Fut","messageID":"msg_e2d6383ff001V0e5tLBnEj4TSy"}}
