till loses a bubble\\nwhenever a pending store finally drains and the next instruction in MEM is\\nanother bufferable RAM store. Hazard control fences that younger store until\\nthe slot bit clears, so bursty CoreMark store traffic still pays a handoff\\npenalty even though the core already knows both stores' addr/data/mask and\\nonly needs to preserve oldest-first external visibility.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` core already proved that deferred normal-RAM stores\\nare a winning backend lever, but the one-deep slot still loses a bubble\\nwhenever a pending store finally drains and the next instruction in MEM is\\nanother bufferable RAM store. Hazard control fences that younger store until\\nthe slot bit clears, so bursty CoreMark store traffic still pays a handoff\\npenalty even though the core already knows both stores' addr/data/mask and\\nonly needs to preserve oldest-first external visibility.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Teach the existing single deferred-store slot to drain and refill in the same\\ncycle instead of growing it into a true two-entry queue. When\\n`store_slot_valid` is set and `io_dmemReady` returns, `mem_stage` should\\ndrive the older deferred store to the bus and, if the current EX/MEM\\ninstruction is another aligned normal-RAM store, immediately capture that\\nyounger store into the same slot while retiring it once through RVFI.\\n\\n`hazard_unit` would then keep fencing loads, MMIO/non-bufferable stores, and\\n`EBREAK` behind a pending slot, but allow exactly one younger bufferable RAM\\nstore through on the drain cycle for a refill. This keeps the proven\\nbackend-only store-buffering strategy, avoids the placement/routing cost that\\nkilled the real two-entry queue, and should recover part of the st...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Canonical Late Store-Data Lane\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The proven `bench` wins have come from backend-only structural changes that\\nimprove IPC or Fmax without touching the fragile fetch path. The remaining\\nbubble that still looks worth chasing is the forced load-use stall when a\\nyounger STORE depends on an older LOAD only for `rs2` write data: the store\\naddress is ready in EX, but the current pipeline still treats store data as\\nan EX-time operand and burns a full bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The proven `bench` wins have come from backend-only structural changes that\\nimprove IPC or Fmax without touching the fragile fetch path. The remaining\\nbubble that still looks worth chasing is the forced load-use stall when a\\nyounger STORE depends on an older LOAD only for `rs2` write data: the store\\naddress is ready in EX, but the current pipeline still treats store data as\\nan EX-time operand and burns a full bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Retiming STORE data to the stage where it becomes architecturally visible is\\nstill the best structural next step, but it has to be implemented as a\\ncoherent data lane rather than a late one-off bypass. Add explicit metadata\\nfor when store data may arrive from WB and carry it through EX/MEM; relax the\\nload-use interlock only for the safe case where a younger STORE's `rs2`\\ndepends on an older LOAD while `rs1` remains independent. In MEM, choose one\\ncanonical final store-data value and use that same value for physical\\n`dmem_wdata`, deferred-store-slot capture, MEM/WB `rs2_val`, and RVFI\\n`rs2_rdata` / `mem_wdata`. This keeps the fetch path unchanged, preserves the\\nexisting zero-wait store fast path, and directly targets a real CoreMark\\nbubble source that prior attempts exposed but did not yet implement\\ncorrectly.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add late-store-dat...\n  Line 25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"One-Entry Short-Loop Stream Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Every prior `bench` predictor that touched the live fetch `pc -> next_pc`\\ndecision regressed, which says the remaining predictor win cannot be another\\nper-fetch target compare or ID-stage redirect. CoreMark is still dominated by\\ntiny hot counted loops under random `io_imemReady` stalls, so the best\\nremaining opportunity is to amortize predictor cost across several loop-body\\ninstructions instead of paying it every taken branch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every prior `bench` predictor that touched the live fetch `pc -> next_pc`\\ndecision regressed, which says the remaining predictor win cannot be another\\nper-fetch target compare or ID-stage redirect. CoreMark is still dominated by\\ntiny hot counted loops under random `io_imemReady` stalls, so the best\\nremaining opportunity is to amortize predictor cost across several loop-body\\ninstructions instead of paying it every taken branch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry EX-trained short-loop stream buffer for small taken backward\\nconditional branches. When EX resolves a taken backedge whose target-to-branch\\nspan is small (for example `<= 4` instructions), capture the loop start PC,\\nbranch PC, and the sequential instructions in that window into a registered\\nbuffer. On a later hit at the loop entry, IF enters a local stream mode and\\nsources the buffered instructions for the predicted-taken iterations instead of\\nre-fetching them from imem one by one.\\n\\nThis keeps EX as the architectural source of truth and avoids the proven bad\\npath of same-cycle frontend target generation. Compared with the earlier\\nsingle-branch predictors, it can hide both the taken-backedge bubble and\\nrandom imem stall bubbles across the whole buffered loop body, so one entry\\nmay repay its control cost better than another fetch-path BTB or fold slot.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": ...\n  Line 26: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Shared RV32M Multiply Datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current `bench` core already found its two big wins by removing costly\\ncontrol and divider work from the always-hot paths, while later fetch-side\\npredictors and MEM-side queue refinements mostly regressed or failed\\nplacement. One localized datapath cost still stands out in `rtl/alu.sv`:\\nthe real build computes three separate 64-bit products (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`,\\n`mul_su`) just to serve the four MUL-family instructions.\\n\\nCoreMark does use multiply in hot code, so turning MUL into a multi-cycle\\npath would likely hurt IPC. A better micro-opt is to keep one-cycle RV32M\\nlatency but remove redundant multiplier hardware and mux fanout from the EX\\ndatapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current `bench` core already found its two big wins by removing costly\\ncontrol and divider work from the always-hot paths, while later fetch-side\\npredictors and MEM-side queue refinements mostly regressed or failed\\nplacement. One localized datapath cost still stands out in `rtl/alu.sv`:\\nthe real build computes three separate 64-bit products (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`,\\n`mul_su`) just to serve the four MUL-family instructions.\\n\\nCoreMark does use multiply in hot code, so turning MUL into a multi-cycle\\npath would likely hurt IPC. A better micro-opt is to keep one-cycle RV32M\\nlatency but remove redundant multiplier hardware and mux fanout from the EX\\ndatapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel MUL-family product trees in `rtl/alu.sv` with a\\nsingle shared sign-configurable multiplier. Use one 33x33 extended product\\nwhose extra sign bits are selected from the opcode so the same result can\\nproduce `MUL`, `MULH`, `MULHU`, and `MULHSU`: low 32 bits for `MUL`, high\\n32 bits for the three high-half variants, with the existing ALTOPS branch\\nleft unchanged.\\n\\nThis keeps the pipeline, hazards, RVFI retirement behavior, and CoreMark\\ncycle count unchange...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Decoupled Sequential Fetch Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current `bench` frontend still couples decode directly to the live imem\\nresponse: `if_stage` emits a combinational IF/ID bundle from the current PC,\\nand `hazard_unit` turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into an immediate bubble.\\nThat leaves two costs on the table under the tournament's random iStall model:\\nisolated fetch stalls always reduce IPC, and the raw imem-to-decode path stays\\nin the timing cone.\\n\\nThe recent negative lessons only rule out fetch-side prediction and replay\\nstructures that add tag compares, target muxing, or decode work onto the live\\n`pc -> next fetch` path. They do not rule out a simpler decoupling structure\\nthat stays purely sequential and flushes on EX redirect.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current `bench` frontend still couples decode directly to the live imem\\nresponse: `if_stage` emits a combinational IF/ID bundle from the current PC,\\nand `hazard_unit` turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into an immediate bubble.\\nThat leaves two costs on the table under the tournament's random iStall model:\\nisolated fetch stalls always reduce IPC, and the raw imem-to-decode path stays\\nin the timing cone.\\n\\nThe recent negative lessons only rule out fetch-side prediction and replay\\nstructures that add tag compares, target muxing, or decode work onto the live\\n`pc -> next fetch` path. They do not rule out a simpler decoupling structure\\nthat stays purely sequential and flushes on EX redirect.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the current combinational IF/ID feed with a tiny sequential fetch\\nbuffer: a registered oldest instruction plus one lookahead slot for the next\\nfallthrough word. `if_stage` keeps prefetching sequential `pc`/`instr` pairs\\nwhenever the buffer has space and `io_imemReady` is high, while ID consumes\\nthe oldest buffered instruction only when it is not stalled.\\n\\nThis changes the frontend structurally w...\n  Line 28: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Decode-Armed Registered Backedge Redirect\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor history on `bench` is unusually clear: every attempt that put\\nbranch decode, tag compare, target muxing, or stream control directly on the\\nlive IF `pc -> next_pc` path regressed badly on Tang Nano 20K timing, even\\nwhen the IPC idea was sound. But the core still pays a repeatable taken-branch\\nbubble on CoreMark's hot loop backedges because conditional branches only\\nbecome known in EX.\\n\\nThe most promising remaining predictor angle is therefore not another faster\\nsame-cycle frontend redirect, but a predictor that uses already-registered\\ninformation to fetch the target one cycle earlier without lengthening the\\ncurrent decode-to-PC critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor history on `bench` is unusually clear: every attempt that put\\nbranch decode, tag compare, target muxing, or stream control directly on the\\nlive IF `pc -> next_pc` path regressed badly on Tang Nano 20K timing, even\\nwhen the IPC idea was sound. But the core still pays a repeatable taken-branch\\nbubble on CoreMark's hot loop backedges because conditional branches only\\nbecome known in EX.\\n\\nThe most promising remaining predictor angle is therefore not another faster\\nsame-cycle frontend redirect, but a predictor that uses already-registered\\ninformation to fetch the target one cycle earlier without lengthening the\\ncurrent decode-to-PC critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry EX-trained backedge predictor that is looked up in decode and\\nconsumed one cycle later from registered state. When EX resolves a taken\\nbackward conditional branch, capture that branch PC and resolved target PC in\\na tiny slot. When the same branch PC appears again in ID, arm a registered\\nprediction for the following cycle instead of redirecting immediately.\\n\\nOn the next cycle, while that branch is in EX, IF uses the registered target\\nPC as `io_ime...\n  Line 29: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Canonical Decode Source Operands\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock keys off raw IF/ID bitfields, not the\\noperands a legal instruction will actually consume. That means single-source\\nor zero-source instructions can still alias an older load's `rd` through\\nunused immediate bits and burn a bubble. The `r4s0` failure showed why a\\ndirect hazard rewrite was unsafe: `bench` currently carries those raw source\\nfields into RVFI, so any safe relaxation has to start by canonicalizing the\\narchitectural source view.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock keys off raw IF/ID bitfields, not the\\noperands a legal instruction will actually consume. That means single-source\\nor zero-source instructions can still alias an older load's `rd` through\\nunused immediate bits and burn a bubble. The `r4s0` failure showed why a\\ndirect hazard rewrite was unsafe: `bench` currently carries those raw source\\nfields into RVFI, so any safe relaxation has to start by canonicalizing the\\narchitectural source view.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Teach decode which source operands are architecturally live for each\\nvalidated opcode/funct form, and canonicalize unused sources to `x0` before\\nthe regfile, hazard unit, and RVFI pipeline state ever see them. In\\npractice, OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR, JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE, and EBREAK would stop\\nadvertising bogus `rs2` or `rs1` aliases, while R-type, BRANCH, STORE, and\\nother true consumers keep their existing source fields.\\n\\nThen drive the existing load-use detector from those canonical source\\naddresses instead of raw `instr[19:15]` / `instr[24:20]`. This stays off the\\nfragile fetch-target and MEM-store paths, removes false dependency bubbles,\\nand makes RVFI source reporting semantically accurate instead of \\\"whatever\\nbits happened to live in the encoding.\\\"\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/de...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Cold High-Half Multiply Offload\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current `bench` ALU still builds three 64-bit multiply products on every\\nEX cycle (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`, and `mul_su`) even though CoreMark's hot path\\nis dominated by plain `MUL`, load/store traffic, and branches rather than the\\nrarer high-half multiply instructions. The successful divider offload showed\\nthat this core benefits when cold RV32M work leaves the always-hot EX path,\\nwhile the later shared-multiplier regression suggests simply reshaping the\\nhot combinational multiplier cone is not enough.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current `bench` ALU still builds three 64-bit multiply products on every\\nEX cycle (`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`, and `mul_su`) even though CoreMark's hot path\\nis dominated by plain `MUL`, load/store traffic, and branches rather than the\\nrarer high-half multiply instructions. The successful divider offload showed\\nthat this core benefits when cold RV32M work leaves the always-hot EX path,\\nwhile the later shared-multiplier regression suggests simply reshaping the\\nhot combinational multiplier cone is not enough.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep single-cycle `MUL` on the fast ALU path, but move only\\n`MULH`/`MULHU`/`MULHSU` onto a small multi-cycle side unit launched from EX,\\nusing the same in-order stall-and-retire discipline already proven by the\\ndivider path. In the real build, `rtl/alu.sv` would stop synthesizing the\\nsigned/high-half multiplier trees on the common EX path; EX would instead\\nlatch operands and retirement metadata for high-half multiply ops, stall\\nyounger issue until the result is ready, and then emit exactly one EX/MEM\\nresult with unchanged RVFI behavior.\\n\\nThis is a structural change rather than another local ALU rewrite: it turns a\\ncold RV32M subset into an explicit long-latency execution lane while\\npreserving one-cycle throughput for the hot `MUL` case. That should reduce EX\\ndatapath pressure an...\n  Line 31: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Slot JAL and Backedge Fold Buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Every `bench` predictor that changed the live fetch `pc -> next_pc` path\\nregressed, but the one-slot backedge fold buffer was effectively timing\\nneutral at 447.40 fitness. That points to a narrow remaining predictor\\nwindow: recover taken-control bubbles by injecting already-cached target\\ninstructions, not by adding another frontend target mux, tag lookup, or\\ndecode-armed redirect.\\n\\nCoreMark still spends cycles on both hot loop backedges and repeated direct\\ncalls. The prior fold buffer only covered backward conditional branches, so\\nit left all direct `JAL` bubbles on the table even though `JAL` is perfectly\\npredictable and should not suffer loop-exit misses.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every `bench` predictor that changed the live fetch `pc -> next_pc` path\\nregressed, but the one-slot backedge fold buffer was effectively timing\\nneutral at 447.40 fitness. That points to a narrow remaining predictor\\nwindow: recover taken-control bubbles by injecting already-cached target\\ninstructions, not by adding another frontend target mux, tag lookup, or\\ndecode-armed redirect.\\n\\nCoreMark still spends cycles on both hot loop backedges and repeated direct\\ncalls. The prior fold buffer only covered backward conditional branches, so\\nit left all direct `JAL` bubbles on the table even though `JAL` is perfectly\\npredictable and should not suffer loop-exit misses.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Introduce a one-slot control-flow fold buffer that trains on two low-entropy\\nclasses only: taken backward conditional branches and direct `JAL`. The slot\\nrecords the control PC, resolved target PC, and the first target instruction\\nonce that target is fetched architecturally. On a later hit, `if_stage`\\nleaves `io_imemAddr` on the normal sequential path but tags the control\\ninstruction so the following cycle can inject the cached target instruction\\ninto IF/ID i...\n  Line 32: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Hazard-Only Live Source Filtering\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"`bench` still detects load-use hazards from raw IF/ID bitfields, so\\ninstructions that do not architecturally read `rs2` or `rs1` can alias an\\nolder load destination through immediate bits and take an unnecessary bubble.\\nThe prior broad hazard-relaxation attempts failed because they also changed\\narchitected source accounting or touched the late STORE-data path; the\\nbackend-only opportunity itself is still there.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"`bench` still detects load-use hazards from raw IF/ID bitfields, so\\ninstructions that do not architecturally read `rs2` or `rs1` can alias an\\nolder load destination through immediate bits and take an unnecessary bubble.\\nThe prior broad hazard-relaxation attempts failed because they also changed\\narchitected source accounting or touched the late STORE-data path; the\\nbackend-only opportunity itself is still there.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep RVFI-visible `rs1_addr` / `rs2_addr` and the existing load->STORE-data\\npolicy unchanged, but make the interlock smarter about which IF/ID sources\\nare actually live for hazard purposes. In `hazard_unit`, derive conservative\\n`uses_rs1_hazard` / `uses_rs2_hazard` bits from the younger instruction's\\nopcode/funct form and ignore only the source fields that are provably unused\\nin EX for legal encodings such as OP-IMM, LOAD, JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE, and\\nEBREAK. Leave STORE `rs2`, BRANCH, R-type, and unknown/illegal encodings\\nconservative.\\n\\nThis targets IPC with almost no new datapath or fetch-path work: it removes\\nfalse load-use bubbles caused by immediate-field aliases while avoiding the\\ncorrectness pitfalls that broke the earlier stage-accurate and\\ncanonicalized-source attempts.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace raw rs-field load-use checks with conservative ...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-Owned Hazard Source Sideband\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining bench structural space is narrow. Fetch-side decoupling,\\nprediction, and wider MEM-side buffering have mostly regressed, while the\\nrecent source-canonicalization failure showed that the false load-use issue\\nis real but cannot be solved by rewriting RVFI-visible `rs1`/`rs2` fields.\\nThe current interlock still compares an older load destination against raw\\nIF/ID bitfields, so legal single-source or zero-source instructions can\\nstill alias through immediate bits and burn bubbles that do not protect any\\nreal dependency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining bench structural space is narrow. Fetch-side decoupling,\\nprediction, and wider MEM-side buffering have mostly regressed, while the\\nrecent source-canonicalization failure showed that the false load-use issue\\nis real but cannot be solved by rewriting RVFI-visible `rs1`/`rs2` fields.\\nThe current interlock still compares an older load destination against raw\\nIF/ID bitfields, so legal single-source or zero-source instructions can\\nstill alias through immediate bits and burn bubbles that do not protect any\\nreal dependency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the decode view used for hazards from the architectural source view\\nused for RVFI. Add conservative `hazard_uses_rs1` / `hazard_uses_rs2`\\nmetadata that says whether the younger instruction truly needs each raw\\nsource for the current pipeline's existing dependency rules, but keep the\\nraw source addresses themselves unchanged through ID/EX, MEM/WB, and RVFI.\\n\\nThen gate the load-use compare with those liveness bits: OP-IMM, LOAD, and\\nJALR stop stalling on bogus `rs2` aliases; JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE, and\\nEBREAK stop stalling on either fake source; BRANCH, R-type, STORE, and\\nother real consumers remain conservative. This stays off the fragile fetch\\npath and avoids the late-store-data / MEM fanout that already regressed...\n  Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Backedge Fold Buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The only predictor family that did not materially hurt `bench` was the\\nsingle-slot backedge fold buffer, which matched the current best fitness at\\n447.40 while keeping `io_imemAddr` on the existing sequential path. That\\nsuggests the remaining predictor headroom is not in more aggressive fetch\\nredirection, but in improving hit rate inside that same timing-safe\\nfold-and-inject shape.\\n\\nCoreMark rotates among several hot counted loops across the list, matrix,\\nand state kernels. A one-entry fold slot likely thrashes between those\\nbackedges and leaves repeatable taken-branch bubbles uncovered even though\\nthe underlying predictor mechanism appears Fmax-safe on this FPGA.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The only predictor family that did not materially hurt `bench` was the\\nsingle-slot backedge fold buffer, which matched the current best fitness at\\n447.40 while keeping `io_imemAddr` on the existing sequential path. That\\nsuggests the remaining predictor headroom is not in more aggressive fetch\\nredirection, but in improving hit rate inside that same timing-safe\\nfold-and-inject shape.\\n\\nCoreMark rotates among several hot counted loops across the list, matrix,\\nand state kernels. A one-entry fold slot likely thrashes between those\\nbackedges and leaves repeatable taken-branch bubbles uncovered even though\\nthe underlying predictor mechanism appears Fmax-safe on this FPGA.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the one-slot backedge fold idea with a two-entry backedge-only fold\\nbuffer trained from EX. Each entry records a taken backward branch PC, its\\nresolved target PC, and the first target instruction once that target is\\nfetched architecturally. On a later hit, `if_stage` still keeps\\n`io_imemAddr` on the normal sequential path, but tags the in-flight branch\\nso the following cycle can inject the cached target instruction into IF/ID\\nand repair the fetch ...\n  Line 35: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Compact MEM/WB Payload and Registered Rd Data\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current `bench` champion already found the big wins that stay off the\\nfragile fetch path: cold divide offload and a one-deep deferred store slot.\\nThe remaining always-hot backend cone still carries avoidable late-stage\\nbaggage, though. `mem_wb_t` stores both `alu_result` and `read_data` even\\nthough only one can ever become architectural `rd_wdata`, and the full\\n`ctrl_t` bundle is dragged all the way into WB even though only a tiny subset\\nis still live there.\\n\\nOn a Tang Nano 20K, that extra register width, control fanout, and the\\nMEM/WB-to-WB select mux all sit on a path that also feeds regfile writeback,\\nsame-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding, and RVFI `rd_wdata`. This looks\\nlike the cleanest remaining Fmax-focused `micro_opt` that does not revisit\\nthe broken hazard-source space or the already-regressive fetch-side ideas.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current `bench` champion already found the big wins that stay off the\\nfragile fetch path: cold divide offload and a one-deep deferred store slot.\\nThe remaining always-hot backend cone still carries avoidable late-stage\\nbaggage, though. `mem_wb_t` stores both `alu_result` and `read_data` even\\nthough only one can ever become architectural `rd_wdata`, and the full\\n`ctrl_t` bundle is dragged all the way into WB even though only a tiny subset\\nis still live there.\\n\\nOn a Tang Nano 20K, that extra register width, control fanout, and the\\nMEM/WB-to-WB select mux all sit on a path that also feeds regfile writeback,\\nsame-cycle regfile bypass, EX forwarding, and RVFI `rd_wdata`. This looks\\nlike the cleanest remaining Fmax-focused `micro_opt` that does not revisit\\nthe broken hazard-source space or the already-regressive fetch-side ideas.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Retire the MEM/WB stage around the architectural result that downstream logic\\nactually consumes. In `mem_stage`...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Hazard Liveness with RVFI Source Repair\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining structural space on `bench` is narrow, and the recent hazard\\nsideband failure clarified exactly why. False load-use stalls from raw\\nIF/ID bitfield aliases are still a real IPC loss, but simply teaching the\\ninterlock which sources are live is not enough because RVFI still expects the\\nraw encoded `rs1`/`rs2` read-data lanes to be fresh at retirement.\\n\\nThat leaves one focused opening: pair the hazard-sideband idea with an\\nexplicit architectural-source repair path, so the core can stop bubbling on\\nprovably-unused EX operands without letting stale or mis-forwarded source\\nvalues leak into `rvfi_rs*_rdata`.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining structural space on `bench` is narrow, and the recent hazard\\nsideband failure clarified exactly why. False load-use stalls from raw\\nIF/ID bitfield aliases are still a real IPC loss, but simply teaching the\\ninterlock which sources are live is not enough because RVFI still expects the\\nraw encoded `rs1`/`rs2` read-data lanes to be fresh at retirement.\\n\\nThat leaves one focused opening: pair the hazard-sideband idea with an\\nexplicit architectural-source repair path, so the core can stop bubbling on\\nprovably-unused EX operands without letting stale or mis-forwarded source\\nvalues leak into `rvfi_rs*_rdata`.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split \\\"source needed for EX timing\\\" from \\\"source value must be correct for\\nRVFI\\\" and handle both deliberately. Decode conservative\\n`hazard_uses_rs1` / `hazard_uses_rs2` sidebands for load-use purposes, but\\nkeep the raw source addresses unchanged in the pipeline. When a younger\\ninstruction is allowed past an older load only because an aliased source is\\nunused in EX, carry a small repair flag so MEM/WB rewrites that source value\\nfrom the matching WB load result before retirement.\\n\\nThis stays off the fragile fetch path, keeps STORE `rs2` conser...\n  Line 37: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Champion-Challenger Backedge Fold Buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor history on `bench` is unusually narrow: almost every branch or\\nfetch predictor that added lookup, redirect, or target-selection work to the\\nlive frontend path regressed badly, but the single-slot backedge fold buffer\\nmatched the current best fitness exactly. That strongly suggests there is\\nstill predictor headroom, but only inside the same timing-safe fold-and-\\ninject shape that leaves `io_imemAddr` on the existing sequential path.\\n\\nThe likely remaining loss is one-slot thrash. CoreMark rotates among several\\nhot counted loops, so a single fold slot can keep relearning a different\\nbackedge and give away otherwise-predictable taken-branch bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor history on `bench` is unusually narrow: almost every branch or\\nfetch predictor that added lookup, redirect, or target-selection work to the\\nlive frontend path regressed badly, but the single-slot backedge fold buffer\\nmatched the current best fitness exactly. That strongly suggests there is\\nstill predictor headroom, but only inside the same timing-safe fold-and-\\ninject shape that leaves `io_imemAddr` on the existing sequential path.\\n\\nThe likely remaining loss is one-slot thrash. CoreMark rotates among several\\nhot counted loops, so a single fold slot can keep relearning a different\\nbackedge and give away otherwise-predictable taken-branch bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the fetch-time predictor lookup exactly one entry wide, but add a small\\noff-path challenger slot and repeat counter that are updated only from EX.\\nThe active fold slot remains the only structure consulted in IF: same one tag\\ncompare, same cached target instruction injection, same sequential\\n`io_imemAddr` path, and no new multi-entry select logic on the live fetch\\ncone.\\n\\nWhen EX resolves a taken backward branch that does not match the active slot,\\ntra...\n  Line 38: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r13s0\", \"title\": \"Word-Fast MEM Load Aligner\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current champion already found the two large backend wins that stay off\\nthe fragile fetch path, but the MEM load lane is still relatively generic.\\n`rtl/mem_stage.sv` always builds a variable right-shifted `dmem_rdata`\\nview and generic shifted byte masks before selecting LB/LH/LW behavior,\\neven though CoreMark's hot working set is dominated by aligned word traffic\\nand the recent MEM/WB regression showed this late load cone is timing\\nsensitive.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current champion already found the two large backend wins that stay off\\nthe fragile fetch path, but the MEM load lane is still relatively generic.\\n`rtl/mem_stage.sv` always builds a variable right-shifted `dmem_rdata`\\nview and generic shifted byte masks before selecting LB/LH/LW behavior,\\neven though CoreMark's hot working set is dominated by aligned word traffic\\nand the recent MEM/WB regression showed this late load cone is timing\\nsensitive.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rewrite the MEM-side load/store byte-lane logic around width-specialized\\nfast paths instead of a universal barrel-shift style aligner. Keep RVFI,\\nmisalignment trapping, and the existing store-buffer contract identical, but\\nmake `LW`/`SW` and aligned word-address traffic flow through the shortest\\ndirect path, with only small byte/half muxes on the colder subword cases.\\n\\nIn practice, `rtl/mem_stage.sv` would replace `shifted = dmem_rdata >>\\n(addr*8)` and generic shifted masks with case-split lane extraction:\\ndirect `dmem_rdata` for word loads, a 2:1 halfword select keyed by\\n`addr[1]`, a 4:1 byte select keyed by `addr[1:0]`, and explicit constant\\nmasks for SB/SH/SW. That targets the sensitive\\n`dmem_rdata -> load_data -> MEM/WB` cone and the aligned-memory metadata\\npath without reopening the already-regressive fetch, hazard, or deeper\\nstore-queue spaces.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": ...\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Disjoint Load Bypass Around Deferred Store Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current champion already proved that a one-deep deferred RAM-store slot\\ncan recover IPC, but it still fences every younger memory operation until the\\nqueued store physically drains. Under the tournament's random dmem stalls,\\nthat means one stalled store can still block unrelated later loads even when\\ntheir addresses do not overlap and the existing slot already holds the older\\nstore's full aligned word address, mask, and data.\\n\\nPrior structural regressions say not to add fetch-side machinery, larger\\nqueues, or replay caches. The most promising remaining backend lever is to\\nmake the existing single-entry store slot less serializing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current champion already proved that a one-deep deferred RAM-store slot\\ncan recover IPC, but it still fences every younger memory operation until the\\nqueued store physically drains. Under the tournament's random dmem stalls,\\nthat means one stalled store can still block unrelated later loads even when\\ntheir addresses do not overlap and the existing slot already holds the older\\nstore's full aligned word address, mask, and data.\\n\\nPrior structural regressions say not to add fetch-side machinery, larger\\nqueues, or replay caches. The most promising remaining backend lever is to\\nmake the existing single-entry store slot less serializing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the current one-deep deferred-store design, but let safe younger loads\\nbypass a queued RAM store when they target a different aligned word. When the\\nstore slot is occupied, `mem_stage` should arbitrate between draining the old\\nstore and serving the live EX/MEM request: aligned normal-RAM loads whose\\naligned word address differs from the queued store may use the dmem port\\nimmediately and leave the store queued for a later cycle, while same-word\\nloads, stores, MMIO accesses, misaligned accesses,...\n  Line 40: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Accepted-History Backedge Fold Slot\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Predictor work on `bench` has almost no safe frontend budget: ID redirects,\\nBTB-style target lookup, and loop streaming all lost more Fmax than they\\ngained IPC. The only predictor shape that stayed competitive was the\\none-slot backedge fold buffer because it kept `io_imemAddr` on the existing\\nsequential path. Its remaining weakness is fill latency: the slot only\\nbecomes useful after the target instruction is fetched again\\narchitecturally, so the first observed taken backedge and any iStall-delayed\\nrefill still pay full bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Predictor work on `bench` has almost no safe frontend budget: ID redirects,\\nBTB-style target lookup, and loop streaming all lost more Fmax than they\\ngained IPC. The only predictor shape that stayed competitive was the\\none-slot backedge fold buffer because it kept `io_imemAddr` on the existing\\nsequential path. Its remaining weakness is fill latency: the slot only\\nbecomes useful after the target instruction is fetched again\\narchitecturally, so the first observed taken backedge and any iStall-delayed\\nrefill still pay full bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the single active backedge fold slot and the sequential `io_imemAddr`\\npath, but train the slot from a tiny recent accepted-fetch history instead\\nof waiting for a post-redirect refetch of the target word. When EX resolves\\na taken backward branch, search the last few architecturally accepted\\n`(pc, instr)` pairs; if the target PC is present, snapshot that real\\ninstruction immediately into the fold slot and mark it usable for the next\\nloop iteration. Fall back to the old delayed fill only on a history miss.\\n\\nThis stays inside the only predictor shape that has been timing-safe on\\n`bench`: one lookup entry in IF, no extra target mux on the live fetch cone,\\nand no multi-entry select logic. It directly attacks the remaining war...","metadata":{"matches":40,"truncated":false},"title":"447\\.40|matched the current best|timing-safe|fold buffer|challenger|accepted-history|outcome","time":{"start":1778879442473,"end":1778879442487}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_01f802be38f76f5d016a078bd0ad5c819aac65f42ac9da20e4"}},"id":"prt_e2d7a2e280019ztQZQg4u1ucbV","sessionID":"ses_1d2863275ffecU7AoS7MPyx9nI","messageID":"msg_e2d7a0c8d0013DZv8XmadtrFF0"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778879442495,"sessionID":"ses_1d2863275ffecU7AoS7MPyx9nI","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_g7kZsvHEBLoe7FyKjiaXL9Bh","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl","offset":13,"limit":30},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n13: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Slot EX-Trained Loop Backedge Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The prior predictor regressions define a narrow safe zone on `bench`: same-cycle\\ndecode and target generation in ID cost too much Fmax, and even a small\\nmulti-entry fetch-side target cache adds more compare and mux work to the\\n`pc -> next_pc` cone than CoreMark can repay. But always waiting for EX to\\nredirect still burns bubbles on the tight backward loop branches that dominate\\nCoreMark's hot inner loops.\\n\\nThe best remaining predictor angle is therefore to predict less often with far\\nless hardware: specialize only for the hottest backward branch that has already\\nbeen proven taken, and keep all target information trained from EX rather than\\nrecomputed on the fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The prior predictor regressions define a narrow safe zone on `bench`: same-cycle\\ndecode and target generation in ID cost too much Fmax, and even a small\\nmulti-entry fetch-side target cache adds more compare and mux work to the\\n`pc -> next_pc` cone than CoreMark can repay. But always waiting for EX to\\nredirect still burns bubbles on the tight backward loop branches that dominate\\nCoreMark's hot inner loops.\\n\\nThe best remaining predictor angle is therefore to predict less often with far\\nless hardware: specialize only for the hottest backward branch that has already\\nbeen proven taken, and keep all target information trained from EX rather than\\nrecomputed on the fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry EX-trained loop-backedge predictor. When EX resolves a taken\\nbackward conditional branch, capture just that branch PC and its resolved target\\ninto a registered slot. On a later fetch of the same PC, IF speculates to the\\nstored target instead of `pc + 4`; on a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is\\ntoday.\\n\\nThis is intentionally smaller than both failed bench predictor families. It\\nremoves ID-stage immediate reconstruct... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n14: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Load-to-Store Data Bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The recent `bench` history says two things clearly: frontend speculation is\\ntoxic on this core's Fmax, while backend-only store-side changes can pay.\\nThe broad stage-accurate load-use idea also already taught the failure mode:\\nrelaxing hazards is only safe if the architectural source value is still\\ncanonical at retirement.\\n\\nA narrower version of that idea still looks promising. Today `hazard_unit`\\ntreats a younger STORE's `rs2` the same as an EX-time operand, so a\\nload-then-store-data dependency always burns a bubble even though the store\\nvalue is only needed when MEM drives `dmem_wdata`. CoreMark's list, matrix,\\nand state kernels are store-heavy enough that trimming this one precise stall\\nclass could improve IPC without touching the fetch path or hot ALU cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The recent `bench` history says two things clearly: frontend speculation is\\ntoxic on this core's Fmax, while backend-only store-side changes can pay.\\nThe broad stage-accurate load-use idea also already taught the failure mode:\\nrelaxing hazards is only safe if the architectural source value is still\\ncanonical at retirement.\\n\\nA narrower version of that idea still looks promising. Today `hazard_unit`\\ntreats a younger STORE's `rs2` the same as an EX-time operand, so a\\nload-then-store-data dependency always burns a bubble even though the store\\nvalue is only needed when MEM drives `dmem_wdata`. CoreMark's list, matrix,\\nand state kernels are store-heavy enough that trimming this one precise stall\\nclass could improve IPC without touching the fetch path or hot ALU cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a dedicated load-to-store-data bypass instead of another broad hazard\\nrewrite. Keep the existing conservative load-use interlock for all normal\\nEX-time consumers, but special-case the one safe case: if the older\\ninstruction is a load and the younger instruction is... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Store-Data Forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The proven `bench` wins so far have all stayed off the fragile frontend:\\nmoving DIV/REM off the universal EX path and adding the one-deep deferred\\nstore slot. The failed predictor, load-replay, and deeper-store-queue tries\\ndefine the boundary clearly: extra fetch-path logic and wider MEM-side\\nbuffering cost too much Fmax or placement quality on this FPGA.\\n\\nOne structural bubble source remains in the existing 5-stage pipeline. A\\nload followed immediately by a STORE of that loaded register still pays the\\nfull textbook load-use stall even when the STORE only needs the value as\\nwrite data (`rs2`), not as its address base (`rs1`). That dependency does\\nnot need an EX-stage bubble because store data is not consumed until MEM.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The proven `bench` wins so far have all stayed off the fragile frontend:\\nmoving DIV/REM off the universal EX path and adding the one-deep deferred\\nstore slot. The failed predictor, load-replay, and deeper-store-queue tries\\ndefine the boundary clearly: extra fetch-path logic and wider MEM-side\\nbuffering cost too much Fmax or placement quality on this FPGA.\\n\\nOne structural bubble source remains in the existing 5-stage pipeline. A\\nload followed immediately by a STORE of that loaded register still pays the\\nfull textbook load-use stall even when the STORE only needs the value as\\nwrite data (`rs2`), not as its address base (`rs1`). That dependency does\\nnot need an EX-stage bubble because store data is not consumed until MEM.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move STORE data consumption to the stage where the core actually uses it.\\nKeep STORE address generation in EX exactly as it is today, but stop\\ntreating a load->STORE `rs2` match as an EX-time hazard. Instead, let the\\nSTORE advance, carry its `rs2` register number and provisional value through\\nEX/MEM, and in `mem_stage` select the final store data... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n16: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Slot EX-Trained Branch Folding Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor experiments on `bench` have all failed for the same reason:\\nthey improved branch behavior only by adding work to the frontend `pc ->\\nnext_pc` cone. ID-stage BTFNT hurt Fmax by pulling decode and target\\ngeneration forward, and even tiny fetch-side target caches still cost too\\nmuch in tag-compare and target-mux depth for this FPGA.\\n\\nCoreMark is still rich in hot taken loop backedges, though, so there is\\nlikely one remaining predictor shape worth trying: recover a taken-branch\\ncycle without touching `io_imemAddr` generation itself.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor experiments on `bench` have all failed for the same reason:\\nthey improved branch behavior only by adding work to the frontend `pc ->\\nnext_pc` cone. ID-stage BTFNT hurt Fmax by pulling decode and target\\ngeneration forward, and even tiny fetch-side target caches still cost too\\nmuch in tag-compare and target-mux depth for this FPGA.\\n\\nCoreMark is still rich in hot taken loop backedges, though, so there is\\nlikely one remaining predictor shape worth trying: recover a taken-branch\\ncycle without touching `io_imemAddr` generation itself.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry EX-trained branch-folding slot for taken backward branches.\\nWhen EX resolves a taken backedge, record the branch PC and target PC, then\\nfill the slot's cached target instruction the next time that target is fetched\\narchitecturally. On a later hit for the same branch PC, keep the normal\\nsequential PC path, but mark the branch as predicted-taken and inject the\\ncached target instruction/PC into IF->ID on the following cycle instead of the\\nwrong-path sequential word.\\n\\nEX remains the architectural source of truth. A correctly folded branch would\\nrepair the fetch PC to `target + 4` while preserving the injected target in\\nID, and a loop-exit miss would redirect back to `pc ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n17: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"RVFI-Safe Load-to-Store Rs2 Bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining bench lessons point at one backend IPC loss that is still both\\nplausible and narrow: a younger STORE always bubbles behind an older LOAD\\nwhen the dependency is only on STORE data (`rs2`), even though the store does\\nnot actually consume that value until MEM. CoreMark is store-heavy enough\\nthat this specific bubble class is worth attacking, and prior results show\\nthe core tolerates backend-only store-side changes much better than any\\nfrontend speculation.\\n\\nThe last two attempts at this idea broke `rvfi_reg_check`, which is the key\\nlesson to preserve here: the win is not in a broader hazard relaxation, but\\nin making the store's late data path and its architectural `rs2_rdata`\\ncanonicalize from the same source.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining bench lessons point at one backend IPC loss that is still both\\nplausible and narrow: a younger STORE always bubbles behind an older LOAD\\nwhen the dependency is only on STORE data (`rs2`), even though the store does\\nnot actually consume that value until MEM. CoreMark is store-heavy enough\\nthat this specific bubble class is worth attacking, and prior results show\\nthe core tolerates backend-only store-side changes much better than any\\nfrontend speculation.\\n\\nThe last two attempts at this idea broke `rvfi_reg_check`, which is the key\\nlesson to preserve here: the win is not in a broader hazard relaxation, but\\nin making the store's late data path and its architectural `rs2_rdata`\\ncanonicalize from the same source.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Relax the load-use interlock only for the single safe case where an older\\nLOAD's `rd` matches a younger STORE's `rs2` but not its address-base `rs1`.\\nKeep every other load-use case unchanged, so the fetch path, EX operand\\ntiming, and general hazard policy stay exactly as they are today.\\n\\nTo make that safe, move the dependent STORE's fina... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"RVFI-Safe Late Store-Data Bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\\nthat leave the fragile fetch path alone: first moving DIV/REM off the hot EX\\ncone, then letting stalled normal-RAM stores retire into a one-deep deferred\\nslot. A remaining bubble source is the textbook load-use stall on a younger\\nSTORE whose dependency is only `rs2` store data, not the EX-time address\\noperand.\\n\\nThat lever is still attractive even though the last attempt broke formal. The\\nfailure was a correctness-accounting problem around canonical RVFI source data,\\nnot evidence that the workload opportunity is gone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\\nthat leave the fragile fetch path alone: first moving DIV/REM off the hot EX\\ncone, then letting stalled normal-RAM stores retire into a one-deep deferred\\nslot. A remaining bubble source is the textbook load-use stall on a younger\\nSTORE whose dependency is only `rs2` store data, not the EX-time address\\noperand.\\n\\nThat lever is still attractive even though the last attempt broke formal. The\\nfailure was a correctness-accounting problem around canonical RVFI source data,\\nnot evidence that the workload opportunity is gone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Retiming STORE data to MEM is still the most promising structural next step,\\nbut it needs an explicit architectural-source shadow instead of a late ad hoc\\nrepair. Relax the load-use interlock only for the safe case where an older\\nLOAD feeds a younger STORE's `rs2` while `rs1` remains independent. Carry\\nstore-data source metadata through EX/MEM, then in `mem_stage` select the\\nfinal store payload from the older writeback/load result when needed.\\n\\nUse that same canonical late-selected value everywhere the STORE becomes\\narchitecturally visible: physical `dmem_wdata`, deferred-store-slot capture,\\nMEM... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n19: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Slot Backedge Branch Fold Buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Every predictor attempt on `bench` that touched the frontend `pc -> next_pc`\\ncone regressed: ID-stage BTFNT added decode and target-generation delay, and\\neven tiny taken-target caches still paid too much tag-compare and target-mux\\ncost in IF. But CoreMark remains dominated by tight taken loop backedges, so\\nalways waiting for EX to redirect still leaves repeatable control bubbles on\\nthe table.\\n\\nThe best remaining predictor angle is therefore one that predicts only the\\nhottest already-seen backedge while leaving `io_imemAddr` generation on its\\ncurrent simple sequential path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every predictor attempt on `bench` that touched the frontend `pc -> next_pc`\\ncone regressed: ID-stage BTFNT added decode and target-generation delay, and\\neven tiny taken-target caches still paid too much tag-compare and target-mux\\ncost in IF. But CoreMark remains dominated by tight taken loop backedges, so\\nalways waiting for EX to redirect still leaves repeatable control bubbles on\\nthe table.\\n\\nThe best remaining predictor angle is therefore one that predicts only the\\nhottest already-seen backedge while leaving `io_imemAddr` generation on its\\ncurrent simple sequential path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry EX-trained branch-fold buffer for taken backward conditional\\nbranches. When EX resolves a taken backedge, record the branch PC and target\\nPC, then capture the target instruction the next time that target is fetched\\narchitecturally. On a later hit for the same branch PC, keep the normal\\nsequential fetch address path, but tag the branch as predicted-taken and\\ninject the cached target instruction/PC into IF->ID on the following cycle\\ninstead of the wrong-path fallthrough word.\\n\\nEX remains the architectural source of truth. A correct prediction repairs\\nthe fetch PC to `target + 4` after the folded target has already ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n20: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"(slot 0 hypothesis-gen failed)\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"hypothesis_gen_failed: Hypothesis agent did not write a file for slot hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (allowed_yaml_ids=['hyp-20260515-001-r7s0', 'hyp-20260515-002-r7s1', 'hyp-20260515-003-r7s2'])\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 7}\n21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"(slot 1 hypothesis-gen failed)\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"hypothesis_gen_failed: Hypothesis agent did not write a file for slot hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (allowed_yaml_ids=['hyp-20260515-001-r7s0', 'hyp-20260515-002-r7s1', 'hyp-20260515-003-r7s2'])\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 7}\n22: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"(slot 2 hypothesis-gen failed)\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"hypothesis_gen_failed: Hypothesis agent did not write a file for slot hyp-20260515-003-r7s2 (allowed_yaml_ids=['hyp-20260515-001-r7s0', 'hyp-20260515-002-r7s1', 'hyp-20260515-003-r7s2'])\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 7}\n23: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Zero-Bubble Store Slot Refill\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` core already proved that deferred normal-RAM stores\\nare a winning backend lever, but the one-deep slot still loses a bubble\\nwhenever a pending store finally drains and the next instruction in MEM is\\nanother bufferable RAM store. Hazard control fences that younger store until\\nthe slot bit clears, so bursty CoreMark store traffic still pays a handoff\\npenalty even though the core already knows both stores' addr/data/mask and\\nonly needs to preserve oldest-first external visibility.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` core already proved that deferred normal-RAM stores\\nare a winning backend lever, but the one-deep slot still loses a bubble\\nwhenever a pending store finally drains and the next instruction in MEM is\\nanother bufferable RAM store. Hazard control fences that younger store until\\nthe slot bit clears, so bursty CoreMark store traffic still pays a handoff\\npenalty even though the core already knows both stores' addr/data/mask and\\nonly needs to preserve oldest-first external visibility.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Teach the existing single deferred-store slot to drain and refill in the same\\ncycle instead of growing it into a true two-entry queue. When\\n`store_slot_valid` is set and `io_dmemReady` returns, `mem_stage` should\\ndrive the older deferred store to the bus and, if the current EX/MEM\\ninstruction 