{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778879472859,"sessionID":"ses_1d2863275ffecU7AoS7MPyx9nI","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_WZgw0zm5uesckkTB2CbVdmW6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260515-003-r12s2.log","offset":1,"limit":220},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260515-003-r12s2.log</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1778876075389,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d2b93825ffeZHZpLmPPs3FrVQ\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_e2d46cd7c001zj4Hm1nFNdeEru\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2d46c891001YeHATfYeXMf0Fz\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d2b93825ffeZHZpLmPPs3FrVQ\",\"snapshot\":\"ab029c21c7961d7e2fcf0ec21f7ee2c2fc867074\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n2: {\"type\":\"text\",\"timestamp\":1778876090242,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d2b93825ffeZHZpLmPPs3FrVQ\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_e2d4705eb0029PMifSGBiGRcUj\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2d46c891001YeHATfYeXMf0Fz\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d2b93825ffeZHZpLmPPs3FrVQ\",\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Reviewing prior predictor attempts and the current `bench` fetch/redirect structure first, then I’ll write a single-slot predictor hypothesis file scoped to `cores/bench/`.\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778876089835,\"end\":1778876090242},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"msg_0d12a084aeda7da2016a077eb9bb10819aa8d2303c7380d411\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"}}}}\n3: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778876090275,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d2b93825ffeZHZpLmPPs3FrVQ\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_V8wuzkMf88oslqdXADZK4NMm\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":400},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.23%): Pulling DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the one-cycle EX ALU and handling them with a stall-on-use iterative unit can buy large Fmax gains because CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by MUL, load/store, and branch traffic, so rare divide stalls cost less than keeping a 32-bit divider on the common critical path.\\n2: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, -14.32%): On this core's combinational IF/ID path, adding ID-stage BTFNT and always-taken JAL prediction pulled decode, immediate-target add, and next-PC select onto the fetch critical path, and the resulting Fmax loss outweighed any CoreMark loop-branch bubble savings.\\n3: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.57%): After DIV/REM already moved to `rtl/div_unit.sv`, splitting `alu_op` into hot/cold classes and replacing one flat ALU result `case` with separate `hot_result`/`m_result` muxes plus an `op[4]` final select regressed, suggesting bench's real EX critical path is elsewhere and extra class-select structure can cost more Fmax than it saves.\\n4: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (improvement, +10.45%): Backend-only buffering of aligned normal-RAM stores is a good lever on this RTL because CoreMark issues enough stack/list/matrix/state stores that turning `io_dmemReady=0` on STOREs from a whole-pipeline freeze into a one-deep deferred write raises IPC, as long as younger memory ops and end-visible markers stay fenced behind the pending slot... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n4: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778876090283,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d2b93825ffeZHZpLmPPs3FrVQ\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_AY9pCv5p0MOFTYEJnqrbuvlR\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"(?i)predict|btfnt|backedge|loop|jalr|ras|return|btb|branch\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 28 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Iterative Cold Divider Off EX Path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The frozen bench core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\\\ncombinational ALU path as ADD, address generation, branch support, and\\\\nMUL. On a Tang Nano 20K, a full 32-bit divider is a strong candidate for\\\\nthe universal EX-stage critical path, yet CoreMark's timed loop is mostly\\\\nmultiply, load/store, and branch traffic and should pay little or no steady\\\\nstate penalty from slower divide instructions.\\\\n\\\\nThis makes divider extraction the highest-leverage micro-opt still missing\\\\nfrom the frozen bench fixture: it targets Fmax directly while preserving the\\\\nhot one-cycle behavior for the instructions that dominate CoreMark.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The frozen bench core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\\\ncombinational ALU path as ADD, address generation, branch support, and\\\\nMUL. On a Tang Nano 20K, a full 32-bit divider is a strong candidate for\\\\nthe universal EX-stage critical path, yet CoreMark's timed loop is mostly\\\\nmultiply, load/store, and branch traffic and should pay little or no steady\\\\nstate penalty from slower divide instructions.\\\\n\\\\nThis makes divider extraction the highest-leverage micro-opt still missing\\\\nfrom the frozen bench fixture: it targets Fmax directly while preserving the\\\\nhot one-cycle behavior for the ins... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n5: {\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1778876090365,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d2b93825ffeZHZpLmPPs3FrVQ\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_e2d4707fb001ehTm5Db2bunxiJ\",\"reason\":