{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778879575549,"sessionID":"ses_1d2863275ffecU7AoS7MPyx9nI","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_tAjhkIcib3EG6InLLjVKUS0T","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r14s2\n+title: \"Sticky Hot-Loop Backedge Fold Slot\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  Predictor work on `bench` has shown that almost any extra work on the live\n+  frontend path costs more Fmax than it recovers in CoreMark IPC. The one\n+  exception was the single-slot backedge fold buffer, which matched the current\n+  best fitness while keeping `io_imemAddr` on the simple sequential path.\n+\n+  That points to a very narrow remaining opportunity: keep the exact same\n+  timing-safe one-entry fold/inject mechanism, but make the single active slot\n+  less eager to forget a hot loop when CoreMark rotates through colder or\n+  shorter-lived backedges.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Keep the fetch-time predictor lookup exactly one entry wide and preserve the\n+  existing fold-buffer shape: one backedge slot, one tag compare, sequential\n+  `io_imemAddr`, and target-instruction injection only after a proven taken\n+  backward branch has been trained. Do not add a second lookup entry, earlier\n+  target fill, or any new redirect mux on the live fetch cone.\n+\n+  Instead, change only the EX-time replacement policy. Give the active fold\n+  slot a tiny saturating confidence counter. Matching taken backedges refresh\n+  the slot and raise confidence; a different taken backedge does not evict the\n+  active slot immediately, but first decays that confidence and only replaces\n+  the slot once the current entry has gone cold. This should reduce one-slot\n+  thrash across CoreMark's multiple kernels while staying well inside the only\n+  predictor shape that has not hurt Fmax on this core.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Keep the single-slot fold/inject fetch path, but add a tiny confidence counter and sticky replacement logic while preserving sequential `io_imemAddr` generation.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Classify taken backward branches for training and emit saturating refresh/decay/replace updates instead of unconditionally overwriting the active fold slot.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add the minimal fold-prediction sideband metadata needed to carry a folded-branch marker through the pipeline without changing the RVFI-visible architectural fields.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the fold sideband through ID/EX so EX can distinguish predicted-folded branches from ordinary control flow when applying repair and training.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the one-slot fold predictor update path between EX and IF while keeping the top-level memory and RVFI contract unchanged.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add pipeline tests that alternate hot and cold backedges to verify sticky replacement, correct redirect repair on loop exit, and strict RVFI PC/order behavior under stalls.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r14s2\n+title: \"Sticky Hot-Loop Backedge Fold Slot\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  Predictor work on `bench` has shown that almost any extra work on the live\n+  frontend path costs more Fmax than it recovers in CoreMark IPC. The one\n+  exception was the single-slot backedge fold buffer, which matched the current\n+  best fitness while keeping `io_imemAddr` on the simple sequential path.\n+\n+  That points to a very narrow remaining opportunity: keep the exact same\n+  timing-safe one-entry fold/inject mechanism, but make the single active slot\n+  less eager to forget a hot loop when CoreMark rotates through colder or\n+  shorter-lived backedges.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Keep the fetch-time predictor lookup exactly one entry wide and preserve the\n+  existing fold-buffer shape: one backedge slot, one tag compare, sequential\n+  `io_imemAddr`, and target-instruction injection only after a proven taken\n+  backward branch has been trained. Do not add a second lookup entry, earlier\n+  target fill, or any new redirect mux on the live fetch cone.\n+\n+  Instead, change only the EX-time replacement policy. Give the active fold\n+  slot a tiny saturating confidence counter. Matching taken backedges refresh\n+  the slot and raise confidence; a different taken backedge does not evict the\n+  active slot immediately, but first decays that confidence and only replaces\n+  the slot once the current entry has gone cold. This should reduce one-slot\n+  thrash across CoreMark's multiple kernels while staying well inside the only\n+  predictor shape that has not hurt Fmax on this core.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Keep the single-slot fold/inject fetch path, but add a tiny confidence counter and sticky replacement logic while preserving sequential `io_imemAddr` generation.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Classify taken backward branches for training and emit saturating refresh/decay/replace updates instead of unconditionally overwriting the active fold slot.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add the minimal fold-prediction sideband metadata needed to carry a folded-branch marker through the pipeline without changing the RVFI-visible architectural fields.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the fold sideband through ID/EX so EX can distinguish predicted-folded branches from ordinary control flow when applying repair and training.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the one-slot fold predictor update path between EX and IF while keeping the top-level memory and RVFI contract unchanged.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add pipeline tests that alternate hot and cold backedges to verify sticky replacement, correct redirect repair on loop exit, and strict RVFI PC/order behavior under stalls.\"\n\n","files":[{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml","relativePath":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml","type":"add","patch":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r14s2\n+title: \"Sticky Hot-Loop Backedge Fold Slot\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  Predictor work on `bench` has shown that almost any extra work on the live\n+  frontend path costs more Fmax than it recovers in CoreMark IPC. The one\n+  exception was the single-slot backedge fold buffer, which matched the current\n+  best fitness while keeping `io_imemAddr` on the simple sequential path.\n+\n+  That points to a very narrow remaining opportunity: keep the exact same\n+  timing-safe one-entry fold/inject mechanism, but make the single active slot\n+  less eager to forget a hot loop when CoreMark rotates through colder or\n+  shorter-lived backedges.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Keep the fetch-time predictor lookup exactly one entry wide and preserve the\n+  existing fold-buffer shape: one backedge slot, one tag compare, sequential\n+  `io_imemAddr`, and target-instruction injection only after a proven taken\n+  backward branch has been trained. Do not add a second lookup entry, earlier\n+  target fill, or any new redirect mux on the live fetch cone.\n+\n+  Instead, change only the EX-time replacement policy. Give the active fold\n+  slot a tiny saturating confidence counter. Matching taken backedges refresh\n+  the slot and raise confidence; a different taken backedge does not evict the\n+  active slot immediately, but first decays that confidence and only replaces\n+  the slot once the current entry has gone cold. This should reduce one-slot\n+  thrash across CoreMark's multiple kernels while staying well inside the only\n+  predictor shape that has not hurt Fmax on this core.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Keep the single-slot fold/inject fetch path, but add a tiny confidence counter and sticky replacement logic while preserving sequential `io_imemAddr` generation.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Classify taken backward branches for training and emit saturating refresh/decay/replace updates instead of unconditionally overwriting the active fold slot.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add the minimal fold-prediction sideband metadata needed to carry a folded-branch marker through the pipeline without changing the RVFI-visible architectural fields.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the fold sideband through ID/EX so EX can distinguish predicted-folded branches from ordinary control flow when applying repair and training.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the one-slot fold predictor update path between EX and IF while keeping the top-level memory and RVFI contract unchanged.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add pipeline tests that alternate hot and cold backedges to verify sticky replacement, correct redirect repair on loop exit, and strict RVFI PC/order behavior under stalls.\"\n","additions":43,"deletions":0}],"diagnostics":{},"truncated":false},"title":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml","time":{"start":1778879575543,"end":1778879575548}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0a517c6419a1c7b5016a078c4d1fb8819b98f69f1c124d92fb"}},"id":"prt_e2d7c0db1001mn6ZlPAhzKMjJV","sessionID":"ses_1d2863275ffecU7AoS7MPyx9nI","messageID":"msg_e2d7b855f001ptkBN1qZRGRY6g"}}
