{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778879580225,"sessionID":"ses_1d2863275ffecU7AoS7MPyx9nI","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_mp2mjOKKtT4zeVywDyIDHCO0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml","offset":1,"limit":220},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: id: hyp-20260515-003-r14s2\n2: title: \"Sticky Hot-Loop Backedge Fold Slot\"\n3: category: predictor\n4: motivation: |\n5:   Predictor work on `bench` has shown that almost any extra work on the live\n6:   frontend path costs more Fmax than it recovers in CoreMark IPC. The one\n7:   exception was the single-slot backedge fold buffer, which matched the current\n8:   best fitness while keeping `io_imemAddr` on the simple sequential path.\n9: \n10:   That points to a very narrow remaining opportunity: keep the exact same\n11:   timing-safe one-entry fold/inject mechanism, but make the single active slot\n12:   less eager to forget a hot loop when CoreMark rotates through colder or\n13:   shorter-lived backedges.\n14: hypothesis: |\n15:   Keep the fetch-time predictor lookup exactly one entry wide and preserve the\n16:   existing fold-buffer shape: one backedge slot, one tag compare, sequential\n17:   `io_imemAddr`, and target-instruction injection only after a proven taken\n18:   backward branch has been trained. Do not add a second lookup entry, earlier\n19:   target fill, or any new redirect mux on the live fetch cone.\n20: \n21:   Instead, change only the EX-time replacement policy. Give the active fold\n22:   slot a tiny saturating confidence counter. Matching taken backedges refresh\n23:   the slot and raise confidence; a different taken backedge does not evict the\n24:   active slot immediately, but first decays that confidence and only replaces\n25:   the slot once the current entry has gone cold. This should reduce one-slot\n26:   thrash across CoreMark's multiple kernels while staying well inside the only\n27:   predictor shape that has not hurt Fmax on this core.\n28: expected_impact:\n29:   fitness_delta_pct: 3\n30:   confidence: medium\n31: changes:\n32:   - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n33:     description: \"Keep the single-slot fold/inject fetch path, but add a tiny confidence counter and sticky replacement logic while preserving sequential `io_imemAddr` generation.\"\n34:   - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n35:     description: \"Classify taken backward branches for training and emit saturating refresh/decay/replace updates instead of unconditionally overwriting the active fold slot.\"\n36:   - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n37:     description: \"Add the minimal fold-prediction sideband metadata needed to carry a folded-branch marker through the pipeline without changing the RVFI-visible architectural fields.\"\n38:   - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n39:     description: \"Latch the fold sideband through ID/EX so EX can distinguish predicted-folded branches from ordinary control flow when applying repair and training.\"\n40:   - file: rtl/core.sv\n41:     description: \"Wire the one-slot fold predictor update path between EX and IF while keeping the top-level memory and RVFI contract unchanged.\"\n42:   - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n43:     description: \"Add pipeline tests that alternate hot and cold backedges to verify sticky replacement, correct redirect repair on loop exit, and strict RVFI PC/order behavior under stalls.\"\n\n(End of file - total 43 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260515-003-r14s2\ntitle: \"Sticky Hot-Loop Backedge Fold Slot\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Predictor work on `bench` has shown that almost any extra work on the live\n  frontend path costs more Fmax than it recovers in CoreMark IPC. The one\n  exception was the single-slot backedge fold buffer, which matched the current\n  best fitness while keeping `io_imemAddr` on the simple sequential path.\n\n  That points to a very narrow remaining opportunity: keep the exact same\n  timing-safe one-entry fold/inject mechanism, but make the single active slot\n  less eager to forget a hot loop when CoreMark rotates through colder or\n  shorter-lived backedges.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep the fetch-time predictor lookup exactly one entry wide and preserve the\n  existing fold-buffer shape: one backedge slot, one tag compare, sequential\n  `io_imemAddr`, and target-instruction injection only after a proven taken\n  backward branch has been trained. Do not add a second lookup entry, earlier\n  target fill, or any new redirect mux on the live fetch cone.\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml","time":{"start":1778879580218,"end":1778879580224}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0d231a16de379370016a078c5bc7988198ad93efd06b3810dd"}},"id":"prt_e2d7c46e9001s0BnkFDEjruFeo","sessionID":"ses_1d2863275ffecU7AoS7MPyx9nI","messageID":"msg_e2d7c3701001Eai9dc76BvOhB2"}}
