 savings recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay misses are therefore most likely direct-mapped conflict\\nmisses in CoreMark's hot text footprint, not a lack of branch-direction\\nsophistication. A tiny victim predictor behind the existing 256-entry replay\\ntable targets those conflicts without widening the main table, adding a new\\nbranch update path, or putting a combinational lookup back onto current-cycle\\nfetch_ready.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design is driven by the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor with compressed, resetless tags. That predictor keeps the\\nlarge random-iStall cycle reduction of the 256-entry replay table while\\navoiding a same-cycle table read on the IF decision path. The experiment log\\nalso shows the boundary clearly: smaller replay tables gave up too much hit\\nrate, two-word replay lines recovered only a little cycle count while losing\\nFmax, and branch-direction or return predictors have consistently lost more\\nclock than their control-flow savings recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay misses are therefore most likely direct-mapped conflict\\nmisses in CoreMark's hot text footprint, not a lack of branch-direction\\nsophistication. A tiny victim predictor behind the existing 256-entry replay\\ntable targets those conflicts without w...\n  Line 47: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-040-r16s0\", \"title\": \"Resetless Replay Candidate Payloads\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result is built around the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. The strongest recent micro-opt win came from compressing\\nreplay tags and making replay table payload arrays resetless behind valid\\nbits; broader attempts to remove the replay candidate PC guard or apply\\nvalid-only reset discipline across the whole pipeline regressed. That points\\nto a narrower follow-up in the same proven area.\\n\\n`if_stage` still synchronously resets the registered replay candidate PC and\\ninstruction payload to `RESET_PC` and `NOP`, even though those fields are\\nconsumed only when `replay_cand_valid_q` is asserted and the existing full-PC\\nguard matches the current fetch PC. Reset muxes and reset fanout on these\\n64 payload bits sit next to the IF replay/fetch-ready path that now matters\\nmost for Fmax. Clearing only the candidate valid bit should preserve the\\ncurrent replay hit rate and all visible behavior while trimming a small but\\ntiming-relevant reset structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result is built around the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. The strongest recent micro-opt win came from compressing\\nreplay tags and making replay table payload arrays resetless behind valid\\nbits; broader attempts to remove the replay candidate PC guard or apply\\nvalid-only reset discipline across the whole pipeline regressed. That points\\nto a narrower follow-up in the same proven area.\\n\\n`if_stage` still synchronously resets the registered replay candidate PC and\\ninstruction payload to `RESET_PC` and `NOP`, even though those fields are\\nconsumed only when `replay_cand_valid_q` is asserted and the existing full-PC\\nguard matches the current fetch PC. Reset muxes and reset fanout on these\\n64 payload bits sit next to the IF replay/fetch-ready path that now matters\\nmost for Fmax. Clearing only ...\n  Line 48: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-041-r16s1\", \"title\": \"Same-Cycle Store Slot Refill\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the two structural wins that matter most:\\nregistered instruction replay hides many random iStall cycles, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot lets a stalled normal-RAM store retire before\\nthe external dmem write is accepted. The remaining store-side bottleneck is\\nnarrower than a full second buffer entry. When the pending slot is valid, the\\nMEM stage currently treats every younger memory operation as blocked even on\\nthe exact cycle where `io_dmemReady` will drain the older buffered store.\\n\\nThat leaves a small but avoidable bubble for bursts of normal-RAM stores:\\nthe older store uses the physical dmem port, while the younger store already\\nhas its address, replicated data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible memory fields\\navailable in MEM and could refill the just-drained slot. A previous\\ntwo-entry FIFO targeted the same store-burst pressure but failed placement.\\nThe existing `store_slot` state machine already supports simultaneous drain\\nand enqueue, so the lower-risk structural angle is to expose that fast refill\\ncase without adding another entry or touching the IF replay path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the two structural wins that matter most:\\nregistered instruction replay hides many random iStall cycles, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot lets a stalled normal-RAM store retire before\\nthe external dmem write is accepted. The remaining store-side bottleneck is\\nnarrower than a full second buffer entry. When the pending slot is valid, the\\nMEM stage currently treats every younger memory operation as blocked even on\\nthe exact cycle where `io_dmemReady` will drain the older buffered store.\\n\\nThat leaves a small but avoidable bubble for bursts of normal-RAM stores:\\nthe older store uses the physical dmem port, while the younger store already\\nhas its address, replicated da...\n  Line 49: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-042-r16s2\", \"title\": \"Replay-Cached Next-PC Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It keeps the large random-iStall cycle\\nwin of the 256-entry replay cache while avoiding a same-cycle table lookup\\non the IF decision path. Later predictor experiments show that adding new\\nbranch-direction tables, return predictors, victim entries, or wider replay\\nlines either saves very few cycles or loses more Fmax than it recovers.\\n\\nThe remaining promising predictor work is therefore inside the replay path\\nthat already wins. On a replay hit, the IF stage currently feeds the cached\\ninstruction word back through the same static branch/JAL decode and target\\nadders used for live imem data. That means an external imem stall served by\\nreplay still has a flop-to-flop path from `replay_cand_instr_q` through\\nbranch/JAL immediate reconstruction, target adders, the next-PC mux, and the\\nPC register. The replay table already knows the instruction for that PC from\\nan earlier live fetch, so its static next-PC prediction can be cached at fill\\ntime and replayed as metadata instead of recomputed in the hot stall path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It keeps the large random-iStall cycle\\nwin of the 256-entry replay cache while avoiding a same-cycle table lookup\\non the IF decision path. Later predictor experiments show that adding new\\nbranch-direction tables, return predictors, victim entries, or wider replay\\nlines either saves very few cycles or loses more Fmax than it recovers.\\n\\nThe remaining promising predictor work is therefore inside the replay path\\nthat already wins. On a replay hit, the IF stage currently feeds the cached\\ninstruction word back through the same static branch/JAL decode and target\\nadders used for live imem data. That ...\n  Line 51: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-044-r17s1\", \"title\": \"Tiny MRU Load Replay Latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already uses the two structural ideas that have paid\\nclearly: a one-deep pending-store slot for store-only dmem backpressure and\\na registered instruction replay table for random iStall. The remaining\\nstructural stalls are concentrated on normal-RAM loads. A load still freezes\\nthe whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low, even if the same\\naligned word was returned recently.\\n\\nThe prior 16-entry MEM-stage load replay buffer showed that this stall source\\nis real: it reduced CoreMark cycles on top of the instruction replay design.\\nIt missed the current best because the same-cycle table lookup, tag compare,\\nand store-update logic dropped Fmax too much. A much smaller MRU replay latch\\ntargets the same dmem-stall opportunity while keeping the MEM path close to\\nthe current best design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already uses the two structural ideas that have paid\\nclearly: a one-deep pending-store slot for store-only dmem backpressure and\\na registered instruction replay table for random iStall. The remaining\\nstructural stalls are concentrated on normal-RAM loads. A load still freezes\\nthe whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low, even if the same\\naligned word was returned recently.\\n\\nThe prior 16-entry MEM-stage load replay buffer showed that this stall source\\nis real: it reduced CoreMark cycles on top of the instruction replay design.\\nIt missed the current best because the same-cycle table lookup, tag compare,\\nand store-update logic dropped Fmax too much. A much smaller MRU replay latch\\ntargets the same dmem-stall opportunity while keeping the MEM path close to\\nthe current best design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry most-recently-used normal-RAM load replay latch in MEM. Each\\nentry stores a valid bit, an aligned word address tag, and the raw 32-bit word\\nreturned...\n  Line 52: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-045-r17s2\", \"title\": \"Registered Lookahead Agree Branch Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor win is the compressed registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table. It attacks random iStall cycles without putting a\\nsame-cycle replay lookup on the IF decision path. Other predictor attempts\\nshow the remaining tradeoff clearly: learned conditional-branch tables did\\nreduce CoreMark cycles, but their combinational IF table reads and EX update\\nrouting cost more Fmax than the saved redirects recovered. Return predictors\\nand replay capacity variants had the same problem or saved too few cycles.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore to revisit dynamic branch\\ndirection with the timing discipline that made instruction replay work.\\nKeep the existing static BTFNT plus direct-JAL predictor as the cold and\\ntarget-generation path, but move the learned direction-table lookup into a\\nregistered lookahead candidate for the next fetch PC. That preserves the\\ncurrent IF replay structure and avoids adding a current-cycle counter-array\\nread to the branch/JAL target and fetch-ready path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor win is the compressed registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table. It attacks random iStall cycles without putting a\\nsame-cycle replay lookup on the IF decision path. Other predictor attempts\\nshow the remaining tradeoff clearly: learned conditional-branch tables did\\nreduce CoreMark cycles, but their combinational IF table reads and EX update\\nrouting cost more Fmax than the saved redirects recovered. Return predictors\\nand replay capacity variants had the same problem or saved too few cycles.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore to revisit dynamic branch\\ndirection with the timing discipline that made instruction replay work.\\nKeep the existing static BTFNT plus direct-JAL predictor as the cold and\\ntarget-generation path, but move the learned d...\n  Line 53: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-046-r18s0\", \"title\": \"Source-Qualified Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL has already harvested the high-leverage Fmax wins:\\nDIV/REM is cold and iterative, the IF replay predictor hides most random\\niStall loss, replay tags are compressed/resetless, the RVFI order counter is\\nsegmented, and the ALU result mux has a hot/cold split. The next micro-opt\\nshould therefore avoid new memories, predictors, queues, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining always-present cycle loss in the control path is the\\nconservative load-use detector. It currently stalls whenever the load in\\nID/EX has a nonzero rd matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield of the\\nIF/ID instruction. That is necessary for true consumers, but false for many\\nlegal RV32I encodings: OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR do not read rs2; LUI, AUIPC,\\nJAL, FENCE, and EBREAK do not read either source. CoreMark has many pointer\\nupdates, immediate arithmetic, loads, and control instructions after loads,\\nso these false matches can still inject bubbles even after the instruction\\nreplay predictor improves frontend availability.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL has already harvested the high-leverage Fmax wins:\\nDIV/REM is cold and iterative, the IF replay predictor hides most random\\niStall loss, replay tags are compressed/resetless, the RVFI order counter is\\nsegmented, and the ALU result mux has a hot/cold split. The next micro-opt\\nshould therefore avoid new memories, predictors, queues, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining always-present cycle loss in the control path is the\\nconservative load-use detector. It currently stalls whenever the load in\\nID/EX has a nonzero rd matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield of the\\nIF/ID instruction. That is necessary for true consumers, but false for many\\nlegal RV32I encodings: OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR do not read rs2; LUI, AUIPC,\\nJAL, FENCE, and EBREAK do not read either source. CoreMark has...\n  Line 54: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-047-r18s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-Exact Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has strong timing wins from the registered instruction\\nreplay predictor, segmented RVFI order counter, pending-store slot, and\\nhot/cold ALU split. The remaining structural improvement should therefore\\navoid another cache, queue, predictor table, or load-data forwarding path that\\ncould disturb Fmax.\\n\\nThe load-use interlock is still structurally conservative. It stalls whenever\\na load in ID/EX has a nonzero destination matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2\\nbitfield of the IF/ID instruction. That is correct for true consumers, but it\\ncreates false bubbles for instructions where one or both fields are not\\nsource operands: OP-IMM and LOAD do not read rs2, JAL/LUI/AUIPC read neither,\\nFENCE/EBREAK read neither, and JALR reads only rs1. CoreMark's optimized code\\nhas dense immediate arithmetic, address-generation loads, calls, and loop\\ncontrol, so these false one-cycle bubbles are a plausible remaining IPC loss.\\n\\nA prior operand-aware interlock attempt broke selftest, so this hypothesis is\\ndeliberately conservative: decode source-use exactly for known legal\\ninstruction classes, keep reserved or unknown encodings on the current\\nboth-source stall policy, and do not add any MEM-to-EX load-data path. The\\ngoal is to recover only provably false load-use stalls while leaving all true\\nload consumers, dmem waits, branch recovery, divider interlocks, and RVFI\\nordering unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has strong timing wins from the registered instruction\\nreplay predictor, segmented RVFI order counter, pending-store slot, and\\nhot/cold ALU split. The remaining structural improvement should therefore\\navoid another cache, queue, predictor table, or load-data forwarding path that\\ncould disturb Fmax.\\n\\nThe load-use interlock is still structurally conservative. It stalls whenever\\na load in ID/EX h...\n  Line 55: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-048-r18s2\", \"title\": \"Kill-Gated I-Fetch Replay Fills\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design already has the predictor feature with the strongest\\nmeasured payoff: a 256-entry registered lookahead instruction replay table\\nwith compressed tags. It hides random iStall bubbles without putting a\\nsame-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up predictor attempts\\nthat added branch-direction state, return targets, victim entries, wider\\nreplay lines, or replay-cached next-PC metadata either saved too few cycles\\nor lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe next predictor slot should therefore keep the existing replay structure\\nand target replay quality rather than add another table. The current IF\\nstage refreshes the replay table on every live `io_imemReady` fetch, even\\nwhen that fetch is being killed by an EX redirect or IF flush. Those killed\\nwords are architecturally correct immutable instructions, but they are\\nwrong-path for the current execution and can evict useful hot-loop replay\\nentries in the direct-mapped table. Avoiding this pollution is a narrow\\nreplay-predictor improvement with little timing exposure: it changes the\\nreplay write-enable policy, not the current-cycle fetch-ready, branch/JAL\\ntarget, ALU, memory, or retirement paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design already has the predictor feature with the strongest\\nmeasured payoff: a 256-entry registered lookahead instruction replay table\\nwith compressed tags. It hides random iStall bubbles without putting a\\nsame-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up predictor attempts\\nthat added branch-direction state, return targets, victim entries, wider\\nreplay lines, or replay-cached next-PC metadata either saved too few cycles\\nor lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe next predictor slot should therefore keep the existing replay structure\\nand target replay quality rather than add another table. The cu...\n  Line 57: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-050-r19s1\", \"title\": \"Backend-Stall Shadow I-Fetch Prefetcher\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest cycle-count win from the registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table: hot instructions can be supplied during\\nrandom imem stalls without a same-cycle replay lookup on the IF critical\\npath. The remaining structural stalls are now mostly backend stalls from\\ndmem waits and long operations. During those cycles the frontend PC is held,\\ndecode is not accepting a new instruction, and the independent imem port is\\nusually wasted refreshing the same held PC or sitting idle.\\n\\nPrior fetch queues tried to decouple fetch and decode directly, but they put\\nqueue control on the normal zero-wait frontend path and lost Fmax. A more\\ntargeted structural angle is to use backend-stall cycles only as replay-table\\nwarmup time. The architectural fetch/decode stream remains held exactly as it\\nis today, while a shadow prefetch PC walks ahead through the existing static\\nbranch/direct-JAL prediction policy and fills the replay table with future\\ninstruction words. When the backend releases, later external imem stalls are\\nmore likely to hit in the already-proven registered replay structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest cycle-count win from the registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table: hot instructions can be supplied during\\nrandom imem stalls without a same-cycle replay lookup on the IF critical\\npath. The remaining structural stalls are now mostly backend stalls from\\ndmem waits and long operations. During those cycles the frontend PC is held,\\ndecode is not accepting a new instruction, and the independent imem port is\\nusually wasted refreshing the same held PC or sitting idle.\\n\\nPrior fetch queues tried to decouple fetch and decode directly, but they put\\nqueue control on the normal zero-wait frontend path and lost Fmax. A more\\ntargeted structural angle is to use ...\n  Line 58: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-051-r19s2\", \"title\": \"XOR-Indexed I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It preserves the large random-iStall\\ncycle reduction of the 256-entry replay predictor while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts show the boundary clearly: extra branch-direction state,\\nreturn targets, replay victim entries, replay-line widening, and cached\\nnext-PC metadata either saved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they\\nrecovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay opportunity is likely direct-mapped conflict behavior.\\nThe table is indexed by `pc[9:2]`, so hot instructions separated by 1 KiB\\nalias even though the CoreMark timed region repeatedly visits several loop\\nbodies and helper routines. Smaller tables lost hit rate, and adding ways or\\nvictim entries added too much logic. A hashed direct-map index targets the\\nsame conflict misses while keeping the table size, registered lookahead\\ntiming model, live-imem-first policy, and compressed tag storage intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It preserves the large random-iStall\\ncycle reduction of the 256-entry replay predictor while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts show the boundary clearly: extra branch-direction state,\\nreturn targets, replay victim entries, replay-line widening, and cached\\nnext-PC metadata either saved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they\\nrecovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay opportunity is likely direct-mapped conflict behavior.\\nThe table is indexed by `pc[9:2]`, so hot instructions separated by 1 KiB\\nalias even though the CoreMark timed region repeatedly visits several loop\\nbodies and helper routines. Smaller tabl...\n  Line 60: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-053-r20s1\", \"title\": \"Same-Word Store Slot Coalescing\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already includes the structural wins that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot for normal-RAM store stalls\\nand a registered instruction replay table for random iStall. Broader store\\nbuffering attempts found the right bottleneck but were too expensive:\\ntwo-entry pending-store designs either failed placement, and the same-cycle\\nrefill variant also failed placement. That suggests the next structural\\nstore-side step should add capacity only for cases the existing single slot\\ncan handle locally.\\n\\nCoreMark updates list nodes, matrix cells, stack frames, and scalar state with\\nshort bursts of stores. When a pending normal-RAM store is already occupying\\nthe slot and dmem is not ready, a younger store to the same aligned word does\\nnot need a second physical buffer entry. It can be merged into the pending\\nslot because loads and MMIO are still held behind the slot, and there is no\\narchitectural observer between the two normal-RAM stores. This targets the\\nsame store-burst pressure as the failed two-entry FIFO, but with one address\\ncompare and one byte-mask merge instead of another queue entry or a wider\\ndmem arbiter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already includes the structural wins that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot for normal-RAM store stalls\\nand a registered instruction replay table for random iStall. Broader store\\nbuffering attempts found the right bottleneck but were too expensive:\\ntwo-entry pending-store designs either failed placement, and the same-cycle\\nrefill variant also failed placement. That suggests the next structural\\nstore-side step should add capacity only for cases the existing single slot\\ncan handle locally.\\n\\nCoreMark updates list nodes, matrix cells, stack frames, and scalar state with\\nshort bursts of stores. When a ...\n  Line 61: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-054-r20s2\", \"title\": \"Banked Registered I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the 256-entry registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table with compressed resetless tags. It is the only\\npredictor-family change with a large measured cycle signal, because it turns\\nrandom imem stalls into useful hot-instruction replays while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts that added branch-direction state, return targets,\\nvictim entries, wider lines, cached next-PC metadata, or XOR indexing either\\nsaved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe best remaining replay opportunity is to reduce direct-mapped conflict\\nmisses without adding an associative lookup or changing the current-cycle\\nfetch-ready path. A full victim buffer added several tag compares and\\nregressed. XOR indexing changed conflicts but did not improve the measured\\nhit-rate/Fmax product. A banked direct-mapped predictor is a narrower\\nalternative: keep two small direct-mapped replay banks and choose between\\nthem with a simple per-set MRU bit during registered lookahead generation.\\nThe current cycle still consumes only the already registered replay\\ncandidate, so any extra bank selection work stays off the IF replay-hit path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the 256-entry registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table with compressed resetless tags. It is the only\\npredictor-family change with a large measured cycle signal, because it turns\\nrandom imem stalls into useful hot-instruction replays while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts that added branch-direction state, return targets,\\nvictim entries, wider lines, cached next-PC m