{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778836957376,"sessionID":"ses_1d5111418ffeMqrGFIyxKLZkLg","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_cgguzJCAqcwvDO63HTILux6q","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r2s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r2s2\n+title: \"Registered lookahead I-fetch replay predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The current bench core still turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into a\n+  frontend bubble, even though CoreMark runs under a random-stall fetch model\n+  and repeatedly revisits a small hot text footprint. The one local predictor\n+  attempt already taught the key negative lesson: pulling branch decode,\n+  immediate reconstruction, and target selection directly onto the current\n+  IF/ID path costs too much Fmax on this microarchitecture.\n+\n+  The most promising predictor angle is therefore not another same-cycle\n+  branch-direction table, but a timing-clean replay predictor that hides\n+  random iStall cycles while leaving the current fetch decision dependent only\n+  on already-registered metadata.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a small registered-lookahead instruction replay predictor in IF. On live\n+  instruction fetches, fill a tagged replay table with the fetched word and a\n+  predicted next-PC for that fetch PC. In parallel, generate and register the\n+  replay candidate for the PC likely to be needed on the next cycle. When the\n+  external imem bus stalls, IF can consume the already-registered candidate\n+  instead of injecting a bubble, and can keep advancing through hot straight-\n+  line or loop code when the replay metadata is correct.\n+\n+  EX remains the architectural source of truth: it still resolves branches,\n+  jumps, and JALR exactly, and requests recovery only when the replayed\n+  next-PC guess was wrong or the instruction was not replayable. This targets\n+  the tournament's random `imemReady` loss directly, but avoids the failed\n+  bench BTFNT shape because the current-cycle IF path chooses only between\n+  live imem data and an already-registered replay candidate rather than doing\n+  fresh branch decode and target adds on the fetch critical path.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 12\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with replay-prediction metadata so EX can validate a replay-supplied next PC without changing the RVFI contract.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add the registered-lookahead replay table, candidate registers, live-fetch fill path, and replay-hit fetch path that can satisfy `io_imemReady=0` cycles from cached instructions.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch IF replay-prediction metadata into ID/EX so the backend can distinguish correct replay progress from a mispredicted replay next PC.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Compare resolved control-flow next PC against the replay predictor metadata and request recovery only on mismatch, while keeping EX the sole architectural authority for branches and jumps.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire IF fetch-ready / replay metadata signals through the top level and route replay-valid fetch availability into the existing hazard machinery.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Stall IF/ID only when neither live imem nor the registered replay candidate can supply the current PC, instead of keying purely off raw `io_imemReady`.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add cocotb coverage for repeated imem stalls with replay hits, including progress, no wrong-path retirement, and strict RVFI order under replay-driven fetch.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r2s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r2s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r2s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r2s2.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r2s2\n+title: \"Registered lookahead I-fetch replay predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The current bench core still turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into a\n+  frontend bubble, even though CoreMark runs under a random-stall fetch model\n+  and repeatedly revisits a small hot text footprint. The one local predictor\n+  attempt already taught the key negative lesson: pulling branch decode,\n+  immediate reconstruction, and target selection directly onto the current\n+  IF/ID path costs too much Fmax on this microarchitecture.\n+\n+  The most promising predictor angle is therefore not another same-cycle\n+  branch-direction table, but a timing-clean replay predictor that hides\n+  random iStall cycles while leaving the current fetch decision dependent only\n+  on already-registered metadata.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a small registered-lookahead instruction replay predictor in IF. On live\n+  instruction fetches, fill a tagged replay table with the fetched word and a\n+  predicted next-PC for that fetch PC. In parallel, generate and register the\n+  replay candidate for the PC likely to be needed on the next cycle. When the\n+  external imem bus stalls, IF can consume the already-registered candidate\n+  instead of injecting a bubble, and can keep advancing through hot straight-\n+  line or loop code when the replay metadata is correct.\n+\n+  EX remains the architectural source of truth: it still resolves branches,\n+  jumps, and JALR exactly, and requests recovery only when the replayed\n+  next-PC guess was wrong or the instruction was not replayable. This targets\n+  the tournament's random `imemReady` loss directly, but avoids the failed\n+  bench BTFNT shape because the current-cycle IF path chooses only between\n+  live imem data and an already-registered replay candidate rather than doing\n+  fresh branch decode and target adds on the fetch critical path.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 12\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with replay-prediction metadata so EX can validate a replay-supplied next PC without changing the RVFI contract.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add the registered-lookahead replay table, candidate registers, live-fetch fill path, and replay-hit fetch path that can satisfy `io_imemReady=0` cycles from cached instructions.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch IF replay-prediction metadata into ID/EX so the backend can distinguish correct replay progress from a mispredicted replay next PC.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Compare resolved control-flow next PC against the replay predictor metadata and request recovery only on mismatch, while keeping EX the sole architectural authority for branches and jumps.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire IF fetch-ready / replay metadata signals through the top level and route replay-valid fetch availability into the existing hazard machinery.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Stall IF/ID only when neither live imem nor the registered replay candidate can supply the current PC, instead of keying purely off raw `io_imemReady`.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add cocotb coverage for repeated imem stalls with replay hits, including progress, no wrong-path retiremen