{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778838599858,"sessionID":"ses_1d4f68be8ffexdCpFlbjjHOpyY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_DD7ntXxa7I0pSVfUy4LpQknV","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r3s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r3s2\n+title: \"Tiny EX-trained taken-target cache\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The two failed predictor attempts exposed the boundary on this core: bench\n+  does not tolerate extra decode, immediate-generation, or replay-control work\n+  on the frontend critical path. But CoreMark is still rich in tight counted\n+  loops and unconditional jumps, so always waiting for EX to redirect leaves\n+  predictable control-flow bubbles on the table.\n+\n+  The remaining predictor opportunity is a much smaller one: predict only from\n+  already-resolved history, with a fetch-side structure light enough that the\n+  Fmax cost stays below the IPC gain.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a very small direct-mapped taken-target cache in IF, trained only by\n+  control-flow instructions once EX has already resolved their real next PC.\n+  On a tag hit, IF speculatively uses the cached target instead of `pc + 4`;\n+  on a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is today. This avoids any ID-stage\n+  branch decode, immediate reconstruction, or same-cycle target add.\n+\n+  The predictor should only cache taken conditional branches and unconditional\n+  jumps, with EX comparing the predicted next PC against the architectural one\n+  and asserting recovery only on mismatch. A last-taken policy is a good fit\n+  for CoreMark's loop backedges: it predicts the many taken iterations while\n+  missing only on loop exit, and the hardware cost is just a tiny tag/target\n+  array plus one fetch-side compare and mux.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add prediction metadata fields to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles so EX can validate speculative next-PC choices against the architectural result.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Implement a tiny direct-mapped taken-target cache indexed by fetch PC, use it to choose speculative next PC, and expose the predicted next-PC metadata alongside the fetched instruction.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the fetch-stage prediction metadata into the ID/EX register without adding any new decode-stage branch or immediate logic.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train the cache from resolved taken branches and jumps, compare predicted vs actual next PC, and request redirect recovery only on mispredict.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the IF predictor update path from EX and keep the existing single-channel RVFI and bus interfaces unchanged while redirect now means recovery-only for correctly predicted taken control flow.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r3s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r3s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r3s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r3s2.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r3s2\n+title: \"Tiny EX-trained taken-target cache\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The two failed predictor attempts exposed the boundary on this core: bench\n+  does not tolerate extra decode, immediate-generation, or replay-control work\n+  on the frontend critical path. But CoreMark is still rich in tight counted\n+  loops and unconditional jumps, so always waiting for EX to redirect leaves\n+  predictable control-flow bubbles on the table.\n+\n+  The remaining predictor opportunity is a much smaller one: predict only from\n+  already-resolved history, with a fetch-side structure light enough that the\n+  Fmax cost stays below the IPC gain.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a very small direct-mapped taken-target cache in IF, trained only by\n+  control-flow instructions once EX has already resolved their real next PC.\n+  On a tag hit, IF speculatively uses the cached target instead of `pc + 4`;\n+  on a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is today. This avoids any ID-stage\n+  branch decode, immediate reconstruction, or same-cycle target add.\n+\n+  The predictor should only cache taken conditional branches and unconditional\n+  jumps, with EX comparing the predicted next PC against the architectural one\n+  and asserting recovery only on mismatch. A last-taken policy is a good fit\n+  for CoreMark's loop backedges: it predicts the many taken iterations while\n+  missing only on loop exit, and the hardware cost is just a tiny tag/target\n+  array plus one fetch-side compare and mux.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add prediction metadata fields to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles so EX can validate speculative next-PC choices against the architectural result.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Implement a tiny direct-mapped taken-target cache indexed by fetch PC, use it to choose speculative next PC, and expose the predicted next-PC metadata alongside the fetched instruction.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the fetch-stage prediction metadata into the ID/EX register without adding any new decode-stage branch or immediate logic.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train the cache from resolved taken branches and jumps, compare predicted vs actual next PC, and request redirect recovery only on mispredict.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the IF predictor update path from EX and keep the existing single-channel RVFI and bus interfaces unchanged while redirect now means recovery-only for correctly predicted taken control flow.\"\n\n","files":[{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r3s2.yaml","relativePath":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r3s2.yaml","type":"add","patch":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r3s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r3s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r3s2.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r3s2\n+title: \"Tiny EX-trained taken-target cache\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The two failed predictor attempts exposed the boundary on this core: bench\n+  does not tolerate extra decode, immediate-generation, or replay-control work\n+  on the frontend critical path. But CoreMark is still rich in tight counted\n+  loops and unconditional jumps, so always waiting for EX to redirect leaves\n+  predictable control-flow bubbles on the table.\n+\n+  The remaining predictor opportunity is a much smaller one: predict only from\n+  already-resolved history, with a fetch-side structure light enough that the\n+  Fmax cost stays below the IPC gain.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a very small direct-mapped taken-target cache in IF, trained only by\n+  control-flow instructions once EX has already resolved their real next PC.\n+  On a tag hit, IF speculatively uses the cached target instead of `pc + 4`;\n+  on a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is today. This avoids any ID-stage\n+  branch decode, immediate reconstruction, or same-cycle target add.\n+\n+  The predictor should only cache taken conditional branches and unconditional\n+  jumps, with EX comparing the predicted next PC against the architectural one\n+  and asserting recovery only on mismatch. A last-taken policy is a good fit\n+  for CoreMark's loop backedges: it predicts the many taken iterations while\n+  missing only on loop exit, and the hardware cost is just a tiny tag/target\n+  array plus one fetch-side compare and mux.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add prediction metadata fields to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles so EX can validate speculative next-PC choices against the architectural result.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Implement a tiny direct-mapped taken-target cache indexed by fetch PC, use it to choose speculative next PC, and expose the predicted next-PC metadata alongside the fetched instruction.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the fetch-stage prediction metadata into the ID/EX register without adding any new decode-stage branch or immediate logic.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train the cache from resolved taken branches and jumps, compare predicted vs actual next PC, and request redirect recovery only on mispredict.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the IF predictor update path from EX and keep the existing single-channel RVFI and bus interfaces unchanged while redirect now means recovery-only for correctly predicted taken control flow.\"\n","additions":40,"deletions":0}],"diagnostics":{},"truncated":false},"title":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cor