repeatedly revisits a small hot text footprint. The one local predictor\\nattempt already taught the key negative lesson: pulling branch decode,\\nimmediate reconstruction, and target selection directly onto the current\\nIF/ID path costs too much Fmax on this microarchitecture.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore not another same-cycle\\nbranch-direction table, but a timing-clean replay predictor that hides\\nrandom iStall cycles while leaving the current fetch decision dependent only\\non already-registered metadata.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small registered-lookahead instruction replay predictor in IF. On live\\ninstruction fetches, fill a tagged replay table with the fetched word and a\\npredicted next-PC for that fetch PC. In parallel, generate and register the\\nreplay candidate for the PC likely to be needed on the next cycle. When the\\nexternal imem bus stalls, IF can consume the already-registered candidate\\ninstead of injecting a bubble, and can keep advancing throu...\n  Line 8: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Prune EX/MEM payload and narrow store-slot control\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` result already found the two large levers that pay\\non this RTL: keep divide/remainder off the hot EX path, and hide some\\nstore-only dmem stalls with the one-deep retirement slot. The failed branch\\nand replay experiments showed the opposite boundary: adding more fetch-side\\ncontrol logic costs too much Fmax on this core.\\n\\nThat leaves the MEM-side control cone as the best micro-opt target. The\\ncurrent store-slot path still carries redundant EX/MEM metadata and uses\\nalways-live wide compares (`instr == 32'h0010_0073`, `addr < 32'h0010_0000`)\\nto drive global stall decisions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` result already found the two large levers that pay\\non this RTL: keep divide/remainder off the hot EX path, and hide some\\nstore-only dmem stalls with the one-deep retirement slot. The failed branch\\nand replay experiments showed the opposite boundary: adding more fetch-side\\ncontrol logic costs too much Fmax on this core.\\n\\nThat leaves the MEM-side control cone as the best micro-opt target. The\\ncurrent store-slot path still carries redundant EX/MEM metadata and uses\\nalways-live wide compares (`instr == 32'h0010_0073`, `addr < 32'h0010_0000`)\\nto drive global stall decisions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Collapse backend metadata and replace wide store-slot control compares with\\nnarrower registered signals. In `ex_mem_t`, remove fields that do not add\\narchitectural information on this core: `write_data` duplicates the already\\nforwarded `rs2_val`, and `branch_taken` / `branch_target` are registered but\\nnever consumed after EX. Replace the top-level EBREAK fence compare with a\\none-bit `is_ebreak` marker captured in EX/MEM, and rewrite the normal-RAM\\nstore-slot range check as an explicit high-bit-zero decode instead of a\\n32-bit less-than comparator.\\n\\nThis keeps CoreMark IPC, RV...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Tiny MEM-stage load replay buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The two successful structural changes on `bench` both stayed off the fetch\\nand hot EX paths: iterative DIV/REM and the one-deep store retirement slot.\\nThe remaining backend stall source is load-side dmem backpressure. Today a\\nnormal-RAM load freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM whenever `io_dmemReady=0`, even\\nif the same aligned word was returned recently and CoreMark's 6 KB working\\nset is revisiting it.\\n\\nThat makes load-side stall hiding the cleanest remaining structural lever:\\nit attacks iterations/cycle without pulling new decode or prediction logic\\nonto the frontend path that already regressed on prior bench attempts.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two successful structural changes on `bench` both stayed off the fetch\\nand hot EX paths: iterative DIV/REM and the one-deep store retirement slot.\\nThe remaining backend stall source is load-side dmem backpressure. Today a\\nnormal-RAM load freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM whenever `io_dmemReady=0`, even\\nif the same aligned word was returned recently and CoreMark's 6 KB working\\nset is revisiting it.\\n\\nThat makes load-side stall hiding the cleanest remaining structural lever:\\nit attacks iterations/cycle without pulling new decode or prediction logic\\nonto the frontend path that already regressed on prior bench attempts.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny normal-RAM load replay structure in MEM, such as a small\\ndirect-mapped raw-word buffer keyed by aligned word address. Live ready\\nloads keep using `io_dmemRData` exactly as they do today and refresh the\\nreplay entry for that word. When `io_dmemReady=0`, a legal aligned\\nnormal-RAM load may complete from the replay buffer on a tag hit: use the\\ncached raw word for sign/zero extension and RVFI `mem_rdata`, suppress the\\nglobal dmem wait for that instruction, and let the pipeline keep retiring in\\norder.\\n\\nReplay must stay conservative. Disable it for...\n  Line 10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny EX-trained taken-target cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The two failed predictor attempts exposed the boundary on this core: bench\\ndoes not tolerate extra decode, immediate-generation, or replay-control work\\non the frontend critical path. But CoreMark is still rich in tight counted\\nloops and unconditional jumps, so always waiting for EX to redirect leaves\\npredictable control-flow bubbles on the table.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is a much smaller one: predict only from\\nalready-resolved history, with a fetch-side structure light enough that the\\nFmax cost stays below the IPC gain.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two failed predictor attempts exposed the boundary on this core: bench\\ndoes not tolerate extra decode, immediate-generation, or replay-control work\\non the frontend critical path. But CoreMark is still rich in tight counted\\nloops and unconditional jumps, so always waiting for EX to redirect leaves\\npredictable control-flow bubbles on the table.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is a much smaller one: predict only from\\nalready-resolved history, with a fetch-side structure light enough that the\\nFmax cost stays below the IPC gain.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small direct-mapped taken-target cache in IF, trained only by\\ncontrol-flow instructions once EX has already resolved their real next PC.\\nOn a tag hit, IF speculatively uses the cached target instead of `pc + 4`;\\non a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is today. This avoids any ID-stage\\nbranch decode, immediate reconstruction, or same-cycle target add.\\n\\nThe predictor should only cache taken conditional branches and unconditional\\njumps, with EX comparing the predicted next PC against the architectural one\\nand asserting recovery only on mismatch. A last-taken policy is a good fit\\nfor CoreMark's loop backedges: it predicts the many taken iterations while\\nmissing only on loop exit, and the hardware cost is just ...","metadata":{"matches":9,"truncated":false},"title":"loop|branch|predict|epoch|confidence|jalr|return-address|RAS","time":{"start":1778853909364,"end":1778853909383}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0c21b767545eb322016a0728153e3c819aae9e44f7506b93c8"}},"id":"prt_e2bf49371002id5nFWNvmGLGhP","sessionID":"ses_1d40bf6c8ffeOZq3R6p3F3gOVu","messageID":"msg_e2bf46c9a001VfmplRYRnHUr2I"}}
