{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778854029597,"sessionID":"ses_1d40bf6c8ffeOZq3R6p3F3gOVu","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_MS2HxmmjifcBkoXiSTwn65XE","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r4s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\n+title: \"Single-Slot EX-Trained Loop Backedge Predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The prior predictor regressions define a narrow safe zone on `bench`: same-cycle\n+  decode and target generation in ID cost too much Fmax, and even a small\n+  multi-entry fetch-side target cache adds more compare and mux work to the\n+  `pc -> next_pc` cone than CoreMark can repay. But always waiting for EX to\n+  redirect still burns bubbles on the tight backward loop branches that dominate\n+  CoreMark's hot inner loops.\n+\n+  The best remaining predictor angle is therefore to predict less often with far\n+  less hardware: specialize only for the hottest backward branch that has already\n+  been proven taken, and keep all target information trained from EX rather than\n+  recomputed on the fetch path.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a one-entry EX-trained loop-backedge predictor. When EX resolves a taken\n+  backward conditional branch, capture just that branch PC and its resolved target\n+  into a registered slot. On a later fetch of the same PC, IF speculates to the\n+  stored target instead of `pc + 4`; on a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is\n+  today.\n+\n+  This is intentionally smaller than both failed bench predictor families. It\n+  removes ID-stage immediate reconstruction, branch decode, and multi-entry table\n+  lookup from prediction, leaving only a single registered tag compare and target\n+  mux in IF. CoreMark's hottest work is dominated by a few tight inner loops, so\n+  a one-slot last-taken-backedge predictor may recover most of the useful branch\n+  IPC gain while keeping the Fmax cost below the earlier predictor attempts. EX\n+  remains the architectural source of truth and only recovers on unpredicted taken\n+  branches or predicted-taken loop exits.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: low\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add minimal predictor metadata fields so a predicted-taken branch can be tracked through the in-order pipe.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a single registered backedge slot and use it to choose between `pc + 4` and a cached loop target on a same-PC hit.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the IF-stage predictor hit metadata into ID/EX without changing decode legality or control defaults.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train the slot only on resolved taken backward branches and request recovery when a predicted-taken branch actually falls through.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the predictor train and recovery signals between IF and EX while preserving the existing top-level contract and RVFI shape.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add loop-backedge and loop-exit pipeline tests that verify correct recovery and unchanged single-channel RVFI retirement behavior.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r4s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r4s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r4s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r4s2.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\n+title: \"Single-Slot EX-Trained Loop Backedge Predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The prior predictor regressions define a narrow safe zone on `bench`: same-cycle\n+  decode and target generation in ID cost too much Fmax, and even a small\n+  multi-entry fetch-side target cache adds more compare and mux work to the\n+  `pc -> next_pc` cone than CoreMark can repay. But always waiting for EX to\n+  redirect still burns bubbles on the tight backward loop branches that dominate\n+  CoreMark's hot inner loops.\n+\n+  The best remaining predictor angle is therefore to predict less often with far\n+  less hardware: specialize only for the hottest backward branch that has already\n+  been proven taken, and keep all target information trained from EX rather than\n+  recomputed on the fetch path.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a one-entry EX-trained loop-backedge predictor. When EX resolves a taken\n+  backward conditional branch, capture just that branch PC and its resolved target\n+  into a registered slot. On a later fetch of the same PC, IF speculates to the\n+  stored target instead of `pc + 4`; on a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is\n+  today.\n+\n+  This is intentionally smaller than both failed bench predictor families. It\n+  removes ID-stage immediate reconstruction, branch decode, and multi-entry table\n+  lookup from prediction, leaving only a single registered tag compare and target\n+  mux in IF. CoreMark's hottest work is dominated by a few tight inner loops, so\n+  a one-slot last-taken-backedge predictor may recover most of the useful branch\n+  IPC gain while keeping the Fmax cost below the earlier predictor attempts. EX\n+  remains the architectural source of truth and only recovers on unpredicted taken\n+  branches or predicted-taken loop exits.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: low\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add minimal predictor metadata fields so a predicted-taken branch can be tracked through the in-order pipe.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a single registered backedge slot and use it to choose between `pc + 4` and a cached loop target on a same-PC hit.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the IF-stage predictor hit metadata into ID/EX without changing decode legality or control defaults.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train the slot only on resolved taken backward branches and request recovery when a predicted-taken branch actually falls through.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the predictor train and recovery signals between IF and EX while preserving the existing top-level contract and RVFI shape.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add loop-backedge and loop-exit pipeline tests that verify correct recovery and unchanged single-channel RVFI retirement behavior.\"\n\n","files":[{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r4s2.yaml","relativePath":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r4s2.yaml","type":"add","patch":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r4s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r4s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r4s2.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\n+title: \"Single-Slot EX-Trained Loop Backedge Predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The prior predictor regressions define a narrow safe zone on `bench`: same-cycle\n+  decode and target generation in ID cost too much Fmax, and even a small\n+  multi-entry fetch-side target cache adds more compare and mux work to the\n+  `pc -> next_pc` cone than CoreMark can repay. But always waiting for EX to\n+  redirect still burns bubbles on the tight backward loop branches that dominate\n+  CoreMark's hot inner loops.\n+\n+  The best remaining predictor angle is therefore to predict less often with far\n+  less hardware: specialize only for the hottest backward branch that has already\n+  been proven taken, and keep all target information trained from EX rather than\n+  recomputed on the fetch path.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a one-entry EX-trained loop-backedge predictor. When EX resolves a taken\n+  backward conditional branch, capture just that branch PC and its resolved target\n+  into a registered slot. On a later fetch of the same PC, IF speculates to the\n+  stored target instead of `pc + 4`; on a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is\n+  today.\n+\n+  This is intentionally smaller than both failed bench predictor families. It\n+  removes ID-stage immediate reconstruction, branch decode, and multi-entry table\n+  lookup from prediction, leaving only a single registered tag compare and target\n+  mux in IF. CoreMark's hottest work is dominated by a few tight inner loops, so\n+  a one-slot last-taken-backedge predictor may recover most of the useful branch\n+  IPC gain while keeping the Fmax cost below the earlier predictor attempts. EX\n+  remains the architectural source of truth and only recovers on unpredicted taken\n+  branches or predicted-taken loop exits.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: low\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add minimal predictor metadata fields so a predicted-taken branch can be tracked through the in-order pipe.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a single registered backedge slot and use it to choose between `pc + 4` and a cached loop target on a same-PC hit.\"