{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778854035784,"sessionID":"ses_1d40bf6c8ffeOZq3R6p3F3gOVu","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_oc1jscGOVTDCn6nzAxrlZEOI","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r4s2.yaml","offset":1,"limit":200},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r4s2.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: id: hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\n2: title: \"Single-Slot EX-Trained Loop Backedge Predictor\"\n3: category: predictor\n4: motivation: |\n5:   The prior predictor regressions define a narrow safe zone on `bench`: same-cycle\n6:   decode and target generation in ID cost too much Fmax, and even a small\n7:   multi-entry fetch-side target cache adds more compare and mux work to the\n8:   `pc -> next_pc` cone than CoreMark can repay. But always waiting for EX to\n9:   redirect still burns bubbles on the tight backward loop branches that dominate\n10:   CoreMark's hot inner loops.\n11: \n12:   The best remaining predictor angle is therefore to predict less often with far\n13:   less hardware: specialize only for the hottest backward branch that has already\n14:   been proven taken, and keep all target information trained from EX rather than\n15:   recomputed on the fetch path.\n16: hypothesis: |\n17:   Add a one-entry EX-trained loop-backedge predictor. When EX resolves a taken\n18:   backward conditional branch, capture just that branch PC and its resolved target\n19:   into a registered slot. On a later fetch of the same PC, IF speculates to the\n20:   stored target instead of `pc + 4`; on a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is\n21:   today.\n22: \n23:   This is intentionally smaller than both failed bench predictor families. It\n24:   removes ID-stage immediate reconstruction, branch decode, and multi-entry table\n25:   lookup from prediction, leaving only a single registered tag compare and target\n26:   mux in IF. CoreMark's hottest work is dominated by a few tight inner loops, so\n27:   a one-slot last-taken-backedge predictor may recover most of the useful branch\n28:   IPC gain while keeping the Fmax cost below the earlier predictor attempts. EX\n29:   remains the architectural source of truth and only recovers on unpredicted taken\n30:   branches or predicted-taken loop exits.\n31: expected_impact:\n32:   fitness_delta_pct: 4\n33:   confidence: low\n34: changes:\n35:   - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n36:     description: \"Add minimal predictor metadata fields so a predicted-taken branch can be tracked through the in-order pipe.\"\n37:   - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n38:     description: \"Add a single registered backedge slot and use it to choose between `pc + 4` and a cached loop target on a same-PC hit.\"\n39:   - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n40:     description: \"Latch the IF-stage predictor hit metadata into ID/EX without changing decode legality or control defaults.\"\n41:   - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n42:     description: \"Train the slot only on resolved taken backward branches and request recovery when a predicted-taken branch actually falls through.\"\n43:   - file: rtl/core.sv\n44:     description: \"Wire the predictor train and recovery signals between IF and EX while preserving the existing top-level contract and RVFI shape.\"\n45:   - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n46:     description: \"Add loop-backedge and loop-exit pipeline tests that verify correct recovery and unchanged single-channel RVFI retirement behavior.\"\n\n(End of file - total 46 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\ntitle: \"Single-Slot EX-Trained Loop Backedge Predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The prior predictor regressions define a narrow safe zone on `bench`: same-cycle\n  decode and target generation in ID cost too much Fmax, and even a small\n  multi-entry fetch-side target cache adds more compare and mux work to the\n  `pc -> next_pc` cone than CoreMark can repay. But always waiting for EX to\n  redirect still burns bubbles on the tight backward loop branches that dominate\n  CoreMark's hot inner loops.\n\n  The best remaining predictor angle is therefore to predict less often with far\n  less hardware: specialize only for the hottest backward branch that has already\n  been proven taken, and keep all target information trained from EX rather than\n  recomputed on the fetch path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a one-entry EX-trained loop-backedge predictor. When EX resolves a taken\n  backward conditional branch, capture just that branch PC and its resolved target\n  into a registered slot. On a later fetch of the same PC, IF speculates to the\n  stored target instead of `pc + 4`; on a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r4s2.yaml","time":{"start":1778854035772,"end":1778854035783}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_083742d72ad57938016a0728934ed8819a9fa033c17b1b641c"}},"id":"prt_e2bf67fa0001JSFOMzwpaUey3Z","sessionID":"ses_1d40bf6c8ffeOZq3R6p3F3gOVu","messageID":"msg_e2bf66a1d001wh48h0k5n5vWxC"}}
