{"type":"text","timestamp":1778856022505,"sessionID":"ses_1d3eea769ffeUJxlp1l32Pe8MA","part":{"id":"prt_e2c14d1b4001IjJrjjaxVhjDYy","messageID":"msg_e2c143e2c001s1i0ThC0fSW5SG","sessionID":"ses_1d3eea769ffeUJxlp1l32Pe8MA","type":"text","text":"Writing the YAML now, with the predictor framed as branch folding rather than another fetch-address predictor.","time":{"start":1778856022452,"end":1778856022504},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"msg_0eff551ec7aaf7c1016a073056532881999fc4d13285570a80","phase":"commentary"}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778856036683,"sessionID":"ses_1d3eea769ffeUJxlp1l32Pe8MA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_Tz9EkScBT7m3W6smvOIyCBh7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r5s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r5s2\n+title: \"Single-Slot EX-Trained Branch Folding Predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The predictor experiments on `bench` have all failed for the same reason:\n+  they improved branch behavior only by adding work to the frontend `pc ->\n+  next_pc` cone. ID-stage BTFNT hurt Fmax by pulling decode and target\n+  generation forward, and even tiny fetch-side target caches still cost too\n+  much in tag-compare and target-mux depth for this FPGA.\n+\n+  CoreMark is still rich in hot taken loop backedges, though, so there is\n+  likely one remaining predictor shape worth trying: recover a taken-branch\n+  cycle without touching `io_imemAddr` generation itself.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a one-entry EX-trained branch-folding slot for taken backward branches.\n+  When EX resolves a taken backedge, record the branch PC and target PC, then\n+  fill the slot's cached target instruction the next time that target is fetched\n+  architecturally. On a later hit for the same branch PC, keep the normal\n+  sequential PC path, but mark the branch as predicted-taken and inject the\n+  cached target instruction/PC into IF->ID on the following cycle instead of the\n+  wrong-path sequential word.\n+\n+  EX remains the architectural source of truth. A correctly folded branch would\n+  repair the fetch PC to `target + 4` while preserving the injected target in\n+  ID, and a loop-exit miss would redirect back to `pc + 4` and flush the folded\n+  wrong-path instruction. This aims to win back one cycle on the hottest loop\n+  backedges while avoiding the extra `next_pc` compare/mux cost that sank the\n+  earlier predictor attempts.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: low\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX metadata with branch-fold prediction bits and predicted target PC fields.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a one-slot branch-fold entry that trains from EX, captures the first target instruction on a live fetch, and can inject that cached instruction without changing the PC increment path.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the fold-prediction metadata alongside the branch instruction so EX can distinguish a correct fold from a repair redirect.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train only taken backward branches, compare predicted versus architectural next PC, and emit separate repair semantics for correct folded hits versus loop-exit misses.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the new IF training and EX repair signals through the top level while keeping the existing nret=1 RVFI contract unchanged.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Refine redirect flushing so a correct folded prediction preserves the injected target in ID while still killing the stray IF word.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add a hot-backedge pipeline test that warms the fold slot and checks for exact retirement order with no duplicated or skipped loop-body instructions on loop exit.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r5s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r5s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r5s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r5s2.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r5s2\n+title: \"Single-Slot EX-Trained Branch Folding Predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The predictor experiments on `bench` have all failed for the same reason:\n+  they improved branch behavior only by adding work to the frontend `pc ->\n+  next_pc` cone. ID-stage BTFNT hurt Fmax by pulling decode and target\n+  generation forward, and even tiny fetch-side target caches still cost too\n+  much in tag-compare and target-mux depth for this FPGA.\n+\n+  CoreMark is still rich in hot taken loop backedges, though, so there is\n+  likely one remaining predictor shape worth trying: recover a taken-branch\n+  cycle without touching `io_imemAddr` generation itself.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a one-entry EX-trained branch-folding slot for taken backward branches.\n+  When EX resolves a taken backedge, record the branch PC and target PC, then\n+  fill the slot's cached target instruction the next time that target is fetched\n+  architecturally. On a later hit for the same branch PC, keep the normal\n+  sequential PC path, but mark the branch as predicted-taken and inject the\n+  cached target instruction/PC into IF->ID on the following cycle instead of the\n+  wrong-path sequential word.\n+\n+  EX remains the architectural source of truth. A correctly folded branch would\n+  repair the fetch PC to `target + 4` while preserving the injected target in\n+  ID, and a loop-exit miss would redirect back to `pc + 4` and flush the folded\n+  wrong-path instruction. This aims to win back one cycle on the hottest loop\n+  backedges while avoiding the extra `next_pc` compare/mux cost that sank the\n+  earlier predictor attempts.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: low\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX metadata with branch-fold prediction bits and predicted target PC fields.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a one-slot branch-fold entry that trains from EX, captures the first target instruction on a live fetch, and can inject that cached instruction without changing the PC increment path.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the fold-prediction metadata alongside the branch instruction so EX can distinguish a correct fold from a repair redirect.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train only taken backward branches, compare predicted versus architectural next PC, and emit separate repair semantics for correct folded hits versus loop-exit misses.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the new IF training and EX repair signals through the top level while keeping the existing nret=1 RVFI contract unchanged.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Refine redirect flushing so a correct folded prediction preserves the injected target in ID while still killing the stray IF word.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add a hot-backedge pipeline test that warms the fold slot and checks for exact retirement order with no duplicated or skipped loop-body instructions on loop exit.\"\n\n","files":[{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r5s2.yaml","relativePath":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r5s2.yaml","type":"add","patch":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r5s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r5s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r5s2.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r5s2\n+title: \"Single-Slot EX-Trained Branch Folding Predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The predictor experiments on `bench` have all failed for the same reason:\n+  they improved branch behavior only by adding work to the frontend `pc ->\n+  next_pc` cone. ID-stage BTFNT hurt Fmax by pulling decode and target\n+  generation forward, and even tiny fetch-side target caches still cost too\n+  much in tag-compare and target-mux depth for this FPGA.\n+\n+  CoreMark is still rich in hot taken loop backedges, though, so there is\n+  likely one remaining predictor shape worth trying: recover a taken-branch\n+  cycle without touching `io_imemAddr` generation itself.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a one-entry EX-trained branch-folding slot for taken backward branches.\n+  When EX resolves a taken backedge, record the branch PC and target PC, then\n+  fill the slot's cached target instruction the next time that target is fetched\n+  architecturally. On a later hit for the same branch PC, keep the normal\n+  sequential PC path, but mark the branch as predicted-taken and inject the\n+  cached target instruction/PC into IF->ID on the following cycle instead of the\n+  wrong-path sequential word.\n+\n+  EX remains the architectural source of truth. A correctly folded branch would\n+  repair the fetch PC to `target + 4` while preserving the injected target in\n+  ID, and a loop-exit miss would redirect back to `pc + 4` and flush the folded\n+  wrong-path instruction. This aims to win back one cycle on the hottest loop\n+  backedges while avoiding the extra `next_pc` compare/mux cost that sank the\n+  earlier predictor attempts.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: low\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX metadata with branch-fold prediction bits and predicted target PC fields.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a one-slot branch-fold entry that trains from EX, captures the first target instruction on a live fetch, and can inject that cached instruction without changing the PC increment path.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the fold-prediction metadata alongside the branch instruction so EX can distinguish a correct fold from a repair redirect.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train only taken backward branches, compare predicted versus architectural next PC, and emit separate repair semantics for correct folded hits versus loop-exit misses.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the new IF training and EX repair signals through the top level while keeping the existing nret=1 RVFI contract unchanged.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Refine redirect flushing so a correct folded prediction preserves the injected target in ID while still killing the stray IF word.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add a hot-backedge pipeline test that warms the fold slot and checks for exact retirement order with no duplicated or skipped loop-body instructions on loop exit.\"\n","additions":46,"deletions":0}],"diagnostics":{},"truncated":false},"title":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r5s2.yaml","time":{"start":1778856036673,"end":1778856036681}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0eff551ec7aaf7c1016a07305664948199829aa3160d51c5bc"}},"id":"prt_e2c14d1e9001ImYhYQrMyaavNq","sessionID":"ses_1d3eea769ffeUJxlp1l32Pe8MA","messageID":"msg_e2c143e2c001s1i0ThC0fSW5SG"}}
