e EX-stage mispredict recovery into hazard control.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Update the PC from a predicted-taken branch or JAL in ID while keeping EX recovery as the highest-priority redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute a static backward-taken/forward-not-taken decision and early BRANCH/JAL target for speculative fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recompute the real branch or jump outcome and request a flush only when the static prediction was wrong.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Turn redirect flushing into mispredict-only recovery so correct ...\n  Line 7: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Registered lookahead I-fetch replay predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench core still turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into a\\nfrontend bubble, even though CoreMark runs under a random-stall fetch model\\nand repeatedly revisits a small hot text footprint. The one local predictor\\nattempt already taught the key negative lesson: pulling branch decode,\\nimmediate reconstruction, and target selection directly onto the current\\nIF/ID path costs too much Fmax on this microarchitecture.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore not another same-cycle\\nbranch-direction table, but a timing-clean replay predictor that hides\\nrandom iStall cycles while leaving the current fetch decision dependent only\\non already-registered metadata.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench core still turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into a\\nfrontend bubble, even though CoreMark runs under a random-stall fetch model\\nand repeatedly revisits a small hot text footprint. The one local predictor\\nattempt already taught the key negative lesson: pulling branch decode,\\nimmediate reconstruction, and target selection directly onto the current\\nIF/ID path costs too much Fmax on this microarchitecture.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore not another same-cycle\\nbranch-direction table, but a timing-clean replay predictor that hides\\nrandom iStall cycles while leaving the current fetch decision dependent only\\non already-registered metadata.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small registered-lookahead instruction replay predictor in IF. On live\\ninstruction fetches, fill a tagged replay table with the fetched word and a\\npredicted next-PC for that fetch PC. In parallel, generate and register the\\nreplay candidate for the PC likely to be needed on the next cycle. When the\\nexternal imem bus stalls, IF can consume the already-registered candidate\\ninstead of injecting a bubble, and can keep advancing throu...\n  Line 10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny EX-trained taken-target cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The two failed predictor attempts exposed the boundary on this core: bench\\ndoes not tolerate extra decode, immediate-generation, or replay-control work\\non the frontend critical path. But CoreMark is still rich in tight counted\\nloops and unconditional jumps, so always waiting for EX to redirect leaves\\npredictable control-flow bubbles on the table.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is a much smaller one: predict only from\\nalready-resolved history, with a fetch-side structure light enough that the\\nFmax cost stays below the IPC gain.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two failed predictor attempts exposed the boundary on this core: bench\\ndoes not tolerate extra decode, immediate-generation, or replay-control work\\non the frontend critical path. But CoreMark is still rich in tight counted\\nloops and unconditional jumps, so always waiting for EX to redirect leaves\\npredictable control-flow bubbles on the table.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is a much smaller one: predict only from\\nalready-resolved history, with a fetch-side structure light enough that the\\nFmax cost stays below the IPC gain.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small direct-mapped taken-target cache in IF, trained only by\\ncontrol-flow instructions once EX has already resolved their real next PC.\\nOn a tag hit, IF speculatively uses the cached target instead of `pc + 4`;\\non a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is today. This avoids any ID-stage\\nbranch decode, immediate reconstruction, or same-cycle target add.\\n\\nThe predictor should only cache taken conditional branches and unconditional\\njumps, with EX comparing the predicted next PC against the architectural one\\nand asserting recovery only on mismatch. A last-taken policy is a good fit\\nfor CoreMark's loop backedges: it predicts the many taken iterations while\\nmissing only on loop exit, and the hardware cost is just ...\n  Line 13: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Slot EX-Trained Loop Backedge Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The prior predictor regressions define a narrow safe zone on `bench`: same-cycle\\ndecode and target generation in ID cost too much Fmax, and even a small\\nmulti-entry fetch-side target cache adds more compare and mux work to the\\n`pc -> next_pc` cone than CoreMark can repay. But always waiting for EX to\\nredirect still burns bubbles on the tight backward loop branches that dominate\\nCoreMark's hot inner loops.\\n\\nThe best remaining predictor angle is therefore to predict less often with far\\nless hardware: specialize only for the hottest backward branch that has already\\nbeen proven taken, and keep all target information trained from EX rather than\\nrecomputed on the fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The prior predictor regressions define a narrow safe zone on `bench`: same-cycle\\ndecode and target generation in ID cost too much Fmax, and even a small\\nmulti-entry fetch-side target cache adds more compare and mux work to the\\n`pc -> next_pc` cone than CoreMark can repay. But always wa