{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778870916967,"sessionID":"ses_1d308243bffeiqTNPiFD1doV7T","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_JPIxy6udmc86DrimJhMjBcbr","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/LESSONS.md","offset":1,"limit":250},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.23%): Pulling DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the one-cycle EX ALU and handling them with a stall-on-use iterative unit can buy large Fmax gains because CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by MUL, load/store, and branch traffic, so rare divide stalls cost less than keeping a 32-bit divider on the common critical path.\n2: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, -14.32%): On this core's combinational IF/ID path, adding ID-stage BTFNT and always-taken JAL prediction pulled decode, immediate-target add, and next-PC select onto the fetch critical path, and the resulting Fmax loss outweighed any CoreMark loop-branch bubble savings.\n3: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.57%): After DIV/REM already moved to `rtl/div_unit.sv`, splitting `alu_op` into hot/cold classes and replacing one flat ALU result `case` with separate `hot_result`/`m_result` muxes plus an `op[4]` final select regressed, suggesting bench's real EX critical path is elsewhere and extra class-select structure can cost more Fmax than it saves.\n4: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (improvement, +10.45%): Backend-only buffering of aligned normal-RAM stores is a good lever on this RTL because CoreMark issues enough stack/list/matrix/state stores that turning `io_dmemReady=0` on STOREs from a whole-pipeline freeze into a one-deep deferred write raises IPC, as long as younger memory ops and end-visible markers stay fenced behind the pending slot.\n5: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (regression, +5.80%): On `bench`, an IF replay cache stops being a good trade once it also predicts and carries `predicted_next_pc`/`replayed` metadata for multi-step stall traversal, because the added branch/jump predecode, replay-control muxing, and EX repair compare cost more Fmax than CoreMark gains from hiding random `io_imemReady` bubbles.\n6: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r3s0 (regression, -8.05%): Once divider offload and the one-deep store slot are in place, `bench` is not obviously bound by wide MEM-side compares, since pruning `ex_mem_t` and replacing `instr`/`addr` compares with `is_ebreak` and high-bit-zero decodes still regressed, suggesting this backend control/data plumbing can cost more routing than it saves.\n7: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r3s1 (regression, -24.32%): On `bench`, even stall-only load replay in `mem_stage` is a bad trade when it adds same-cycle replay-hit/tag-compare, `io_dmemReady` hazard escape, and store-invalidation plumbing, because the MEM-side timing/routing cost outweighs CoreMark's gain from hiding repeated normal-RAM load stalls.\n8: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (regression, -9.20%): On `bench`, even an 8-entry EX-trained taken-target cache regressed, which suggests the IF `next_pc` path cannot afford extra tag-compare and target-mux work because CoreMark's loop-backedge hits do not repay fetch-side predictor timing cost on this core.\n9: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): `bench` currently latches raw `instr[19:15]`/`instr[24:20]` reads into RVFI, so relaxing load-use stalls from field matches to true EX-time consumers needs architectural-source canonicalization or earlier forwarding, not a late MEM/WB repair, or riscv-formal `reg` will catch stale aliased `rs*` values.\n10: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On this FPGA, the one-deep deferred-store win does not scale cleanly to a two-entry oldest-first queue, because doubling queued addr/data/mask state and adding same-cycle `store_queue_full`/`store_queue_nonempty` enqueue-dequeue fencing in `mem_stage` and `hazard_unit` can push MEM-side routing past placement even while staying off the fetch path.\n11: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (regression, -45.37%): On `bench`, the safe zone for fetch-side branch prediction is effectively zero, because even a one-slot EX-trained loop-backedge predictor's single IF tag-compare/target-mux plus `predicted_*` carry-and-repair plumbing into EX costs more Fmax than CoreMark's hot loop backedges repay.\n12: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r8s0 (placement_failed, n/a): On this FPGA, even a one-deep deferred-store slot can hit placement limits if `hazard_unit` and `mem_stage` try to use same-cycle `io_dmemReady` drain-and-refill control to let a younger RAM store retire into the slot, because the ready-gated refill/fence-release path itself becomes expensive under CoreMark's bursty store traffic.\n13: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r8s1 (regression, -10.67%): Even an RVFI-correct `LW -> SW(rs2)` relaxation can regress on `bench` when it turns late store data into a canonical MEM-stage lane feeding `dmem_wdata`, deferred-store-slot capture, MEM/WB `rs2_val`, and RVFI, because the added EX/MEM metadata, hazard special-casing, and MEM-side fanout cost more Fmax than CoreMark recovers from that narrow bubble class.\n14: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r8s2 (regression, -67.99%): On `bench`, even EX-trained short-loop streaming is a bad trade, because bypassing `io_imemReady` with an IF-side loop buffer still adds fetch/decode control state (`stream_valid/pos/start_pc/branch_pc`, local-instruction muxing, and hazard special-casing) that costs more Fmax than CoreMark recovers from hiding tiny-loop backedges and random imem stalls.\n\n(End of file - total 14 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.23%): Pulling DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the one-cycle EX ALU and handling them with a stall-on-use iterative unit can buy large Fmax gains because CoreMark's hot loop is dominated by MUL, load/store, and branch traffic, so rare divide stalls cost less than keeping a 32-bit divider on the common critical path.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, -14.32%): On this core's combinational IF/ID path, adding ID-stage BTFNT and always-taken JAL prediction pulled decode, immediate-target add, and next-PC select onto the fetch critical path, and the resulting Fmax loss outweighed any CoreMark loop-branch bubble savings.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.57%): After DIV/REM already moved to `rtl/div_unit.sv`, splitting `alu_op` into hot/cold classes and replacing one flat ALU result `case` with separate `hot_result`/`m_result` muxes plus an `op[4]` final select regressed, suggesting bench's real EX critical path is elsewhere and extra class-select structure can cost more Fmax than it saves.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (improvement, +10.45%): Backend-only buffering of aligned normal-RAM stores is a good lever on this RTL because CoreMark issues enough stack/list/matrix/state stores that turning `io_dmemReady=0` on STOREs from a whole-pipeline freeze into a one-deep deferred write raises IPC, as long as younger memory ops and end-visible markers stay fenced behind the pending slot.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (regression, +5.80%): On `bench`, an IF replay cache stops being a good trade once it also predicts and carries `predicted_next_pc`/`replayed` metadata for multi-step stall traversal, because the added branch/jump predecode, replay-control muxing, and EX repair compare cost more Fmax than CoreMark gains from hiding random `io_imemReady` bubbles.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r3s0 (regression, -8.05%): Once divider offload and the one-deep store slot are in place, `bench` is not obviously bound by wide MEM-side compares, since pruning `ex_mem_t` and replacing `instr`/`addr` compares with `is_ebreak` and high-bit-zero decodes still regressed, suggesting this backend control/data plumbing can cost more routing than it saves.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r3s1 (regression, -24.32%): On `bench`, even stall-only load replay in `mem_stage` is a bad trade when it adds same-cycle replay-hit/tag-compare, `io_dmemReady` hazard escape, and store-invalidation plumbing, because the MEM-side timing/routing cost outweighs CoreMark's gain from hiding repeated normal-RAM load stalls.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (regression, -9.20%): On `bench`, even an 8-entry EX-trained taken-target cache regressed, which suggests the IF `next_pc` path cannot afford extra tag-compare and target-mux work because CoreMark's loop-backedge hits do not repay fetch-side predictor timing cost on this core.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): `bench` currently latches raw `instr[19:15]`/`instr[24:20]` reads into RVFI, so relaxing load-use stalls from field matches to true EX-time consumers needs architectural-source canonicalization or earlier forwarding, not a late MEM/WB repair, or riscv-formal `reg` will catch stale aliased `rs*` values.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On this FPGA, the one-deep deferred-store win does not scale cleanly to a two-entry oldest-first queue, because doubling queued addr/data/mask state and adding same-cycle `store_queue_full`/`store_queue_nonempty` enqueue-dequeue fencing in `mem_stage` and `hazard_unit` can push MEM-side routing past placement even while staying off the fetch path.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (regression, -45.37%): On `bench`, the safe zone for fetch-side branch prediction is effectively zero, because even a one-slot EX-trained loop-backedge predictor's single IF tag-compare/target-mux plus `predicted_*` carry-and-repair plumbing into EX costs more Fmax than CoreMark's hot loop backedges repay.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r8s0 (placement_failed, n/a): On this FPGA, even a one-deep deferred-store slot can hit placement limits if `hazard_unit` and `mem_stage` try to use same-cycle `io_dmemReady` drain-and-refill control to let a younger RAM store retire into the slot, because the ready-gated refill/fence-release path itself becomes expensive under CoreMark's bursty store traffic.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r8s1 (regression, -10.67%): Even an RVFI-correct `LW -> SW(rs2)` relaxation can regress on `bench` when it turns late store data into a canonical MEM-stage lane feeding `dmem_wdata`, deferred-store-slot capture, MEM/WB `rs2_val`, and RVFI, because the added EX/MEM metadata, hazard special-casing, and MEM-side fanout cost more Fmax than CoreMark recovers from that narrow bubble class.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r8s2 (regression, -67.99%): On `bench`, even EX-trained short-loop streaming is a bad trade, because bypassing `io_imemReady` with an IF-side loop buffer still adds fetch/decode control state (`stream_valid/pos/start_pc/branch_pc`, local-instruction muxing, and hazard special-casing) that costs more Fmax than CoreMark recovers from hiding tiny-loop backedges and random imem stalls.","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md","time":{"start":1778870916962,"end":1778870916967}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0b2cc962c8bf4b88016a076a84d6e88198bcb0c24f457557c5"}},"id":"prt_e2cf81755002D0dv1fIIcgYquF","sessionID":"ses_1d308243bffeiqTNPiFD1doV7T","messageID":"msg_e2cf7dc32001rCMKXyMQnxbaXj"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778870917001,"sessionID":"ses_1d308243bffeiqTNPiFD1doV7T","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_2HeXXai5je8xPMKJf5aVBOok","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"\"category\"\\s*:\\s*\"predictor\"|predictor|branch|loop|stream|btfnt|taken-target|replay cache","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments","include":"log.jsonl"},"output":"Found 14 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Iterative Cold Divider Off EX Path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The frozen bench core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\ncombinational ALU path as ADD, address generation, branch support, and\\nMUL. On a Tang Nano 20K, a full 32-bit divider is a strong candidate for\\nthe universal EX-stage critical path, yet CoreMark's timed loop is mostly\\nmultiply, load/store, and branch traffic and should pay little or no steady\\nstate penalty from slower divide instructions.\\n\\nThis makes divider extraction the highest-leverage micro-opt still missing\\nfrom the frozen bench fixture: it targets Fmax directly while preserving the\\nhot one-cycle behavior for the instructions that dominate CoreMark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The frozen bench core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\ncombinational ALU path as ADD, address generation, branch support, and\\nMUL. On a Tang Nano 20K, a full 32-bit divider is a strong candidate for\\nthe universal EX-stage critical path, yet CoreMark's timed loop is mostly\\nmultiply, load/store, and branch traffic and should pay little or no steady\\nstate penalty from slower divide instructions.\\n\\nThis makes divider extraction the highest-leverage micro-opt still missing\\nfrom the frozen bench fixture: it targets Fmax directly while preserving the\\nhot one-cycle behavior for the instructions that dominate CoreMark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases in `rtl/alu.sv`\\nwith a small iterative divider/remainder unit that is entered only for\\ndivide-class ALU ops. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift/compare, address generation,\\nbranches, jumps, loads/stores, and MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing\\nsingle-cycle datapath.\\n\\nIn EX, detect divide-class operations, latch their operands and control,\\nstall IF and ID while the divider runs, and emit exactly one EX/MEM result\\nwhen the quotient or remainder is ready. Preserve RV32IM edge cases\\nprecisely: divide-...\n  Line 4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Static BTFNT branch predictor with ID redirect\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench core resolves every branch and jump in EX and flushes on every taken redirect. CoreMark is loop-heavy, so repeatedly paying that taken-branch penalty burns IPC. LUT headroom is tight, which makes a low-state predictor more attractive than a larger dynamic structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench core resolves every branch and jump in EX and flushes on every taken redirect. CoreMark is loop-heavy, so repeatedly paying that taken-branch penalty burns IPC. LUT headroom is tight, which makes a low-state predictor more attractive than a larger dynamic structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a decode-stage backward-taken/forward-not-taken predictor for conditional branches and always predict JAL taken, driving a speculative next PC from ID into IF. EX remains the architectural source of truth for branch direction and target, but only flushes and recovers on a mispredict. This should remove most loop-backedge and JAL bubbles in CoreMark while staying much lighter than a tagged BTB/BHT, limiting Fmax and LUT risk.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire an ID-stage prediction path into IF and route EX-stage mispredict recovery into hazard control.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Update the PC from a predicted-taken branch or JAL in ID while keeping EX recovery as the highest-priority redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute a static backward-taken/forward-not-taken decision and early BRANCH/JAL target for speculative fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recompute the real branch or jump outcome and request a flush only when the static prediction was wrong.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Turn redirect flushing into mispredict-only recovery so correct ...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench core's biggest proven gain came from a backend-only timing\\nchange: moving DIV/REM off the one-cycle EX path. The recent failed branch\\nprediction attempt showed the opposite boundary on this RTL: pulling more\\ndecode/target work into the frontend can erase the IPC gain with Fmax loss.\\n\\nThe next structural hypothesis should therefore stay off the fetch path and\\nattack the other obvious whole-core bubble source. Today any `io_dmemReady=0`\\ncycle on a STORE freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM even though the store address,\\nreplicated data, and byte mask are already known in MEM, and CoreMark does a\\nsteady stream of normal-RAM stores to stack, list, matrix, and state data.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench core's biggest proven gain came from a backend-only timing\\nchange: moving DIV/REM off the one-cycle EX path. The recent failed branch\\nprediction attempt showed the opposite boundary on this RTL: pulling more\\ndecode/target work into the frontend can erase the IPC gain with Fmax loss.\\n\\nThe next structural hypothesis should therefore stay off the fetch path and\\nattack the other obvious whole-core bubble source. Today any `io_dmemReady=0`\\ncycle on a STORE freezes IF, ID, EX, and MEM even though the store address,\\nreplicated data, and byte mask are already known in MEM, and CoreMark does a\\nsteady stream of normal-RAM stores to stack, list, matrix, and state data.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot with a direct ready-store fast\\npath. When an aligned STORE to normal RAM `[0x00000000, 0x00100000)` reaches\\nMEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, capture its byte address, replicated write\\ndata, byte mask, and RVFI-visible memory metadata into the slot, retire that\\nstore once, and drain the physical dmem write oldest-first after the stall.\\n\\nKeep the zero-wait STORE path identical to the current d...\n  Line 7: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Registered lookahead I-fetch replay predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench core still turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into a\\nfrontend bubble, even though CoreMark runs under a random-stall fetch model\\nand repeatedly revisits a small hot text footprint. The one local predictor\\nattempt already taught the key negative lesson: pulling branch decode,\\nimmediate reconstruction, and target selection directly onto the current\\nIF/ID path costs too much Fmax on this microarchitecture.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore not another same-cycle\\nbranch-direction table, but a timing-clean replay predictor that hides\\nrandom iStall cycles while leaving the current fetch decision dependent only\\non already-registered metadata.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench core still turns every `io_imemReady=0` cycle into a\\nfrontend bubble, even though CoreMark runs under a random-stall fetch model\\nand repeatedly revisits a small hot text footprint. The one local predictor\\nattempt already taught the key negative lesson: pulling branch decode,\\nimmediate reconstruction, and target selection directly onto the current\\nIF/ID path costs too much Fmax on this microarchitecture.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore not another same-cycle\\nbranch-direction table, but a timing-clean replay predictor that hides\\nrandom iStall cycles while leaving the current fetch decision dependent only\\non already-registered metadata.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small registered-lookahead instruction replay predictor in IF. On live\\ninstruction fetches, fill a tagged replay table with the fetched word and a\\npredicted next-PC for that fetch PC. In parallel, generate and register the\\nreplay candidate for the PC likely to be needed on the next cycle. When the\\nexternal imem bus stalls, IF can consume the already-registered candidate\\ninstead of injecting a bubble, and can keep advancing throu...\n  Line 8: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Prune EX/MEM payload and narrow store-slot control\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` result already found the two large levers that pay\\non this RTL: keep divide/remainder off the hot EX path, and hide some\\nstore-only dmem stalls with the one-deep retirement slot. The failed branch\\nand replay experiments showed the opposite boundary: adding more fetch-side\\ncontrol logic costs too much Fmax on this core.\\n\\nThat leaves the MEM-side control cone as the best micro-opt target. The\\ncurrent store-slot path still carries redundant EX/MEM metadata and uses\\nalways-live wide compares (`instr == 32'h0010_0073`, `addr < 32'h0010_0000`)\\nto drive global stall decisions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best `bench` result already found the two large levers that pay\\non this RTL: keep divide/remainder off the hot EX path, and hide some\\nstore-only dmem stalls with the one-deep retirement slot. The failed branch\\nand replay experiments showed the opposite boundary: adding more fetch-side\\ncontrol logic costs too much Fmax on this core.\\n\\nThat leaves the MEM-side control cone as the best micro-opt target. The\\ncurrent store-slot path still carries redundant EX/MEM metadata and uses\\nalways-live wide compares (`instr == 32'h0010_0073`, `addr < 32'h0010_0000`)\\nto drive global stall decisions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Collapse backend metadata and replace wide store-slot control compares with\\nnarrower registered signals. In `ex_mem_t`, remove fields that do not add\\narchitectural information on this core: `write_data` duplicates the already\\nforwarded `rs2_val`, and `branch_taken` / `branch_target` are registered but\\nnever consumed after EX. Replace the top-level EBREAK fence compare with a\\none-bit `is_ebreak` marker captured in EX/MEM, and rewrite the normal-RAM\\nstore-slot range check as an explicit high-bit-zero decode instead of a\\n32-bit less-than comparator.\\n\\nThis keeps CoreMark IPC, RV...\n  Line 10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny EX-trained taken-target cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The two failed predictor attempts exposed the boundary on this core: bench\\ndoes not tolerate extra decode, immediate-generation, or replay-control work\\non the frontend critical path. But CoreMark is still rich in tight counted\\nloops and unconditional jumps, so always waiting for EX to redirect leaves\\npredictable control-flow bubbles on the table.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is a much smaller one: predict only from\\nalready-resolved history, with a fetch-side structure light enough that the\\nFmax cost stays below the IPC gain.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two failed predictor attempts exposed the boundary on this core: bench\\ndoes not tolerate extra decode, immediate-generation, or replay-control work\\non the frontend critical path. But CoreMark is still rich in tight counted\\nloops and unconditional jumps, so always waiting for EX to redirect leaves\\npredictable control-flow bubbles on the table.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is a much smaller one: predict only from\\nalready-resolved history, with a fetch-side structure light enough that the\\nFmax cost stays below the IPC gain.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small direct-mapped taken-target cache in IF, trained only by\\ncontrol-flow instructions once EX has already resolved their real next PC.\\nOn a tag hit, IF speculatively uses the cached target instead of `pc + 4`;\\non a miss, fetch stays exactly as it is today. This avoids any ID-stage\\nbranch decode, immediate reconstruction, or same-cycle target add.\\n\\nThe predictor should only cache taken conditional branches and unconditional\\njumps, with EX comparing the predicted next PC against the architectural one\\nand asserting recovery only on mismatch. A last-taken policy is a good fit\\nfor CoreMark's loop backedges: it predicts the many taken iterations while\\nmissing only on loop exit, and the hardware cost is just ...\n  Line 11: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Stage-Accurate Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"After divider offload and the one-deep store retirement slot, the cheapest\\nremaining CoreMark IPC loss is likely the still-conservative load-use\\ninterlock. `hazard_unit` compares a load destination against raw IF/ID rs1\\nand rs2 bitfields even when the younger instruction does not actually need\\nthose fields in EX, and it still forces a bubble for STORE rs2 even though\\nstore data is only consumed one stage later in MEM.\\n\\nThat means the core can still lose cycles to false immediate-field matches\\nand to real load-then-store-data dependencies that do not protect any EX\\ncritical operand. This is a narrow backend/control cleanup that attacks IPC\\nwithout adding predictor state, fetch-path muxing, or a new EX datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"After divider offload and the one-deep store retirement slot, the cheapest\\nremaining CoreMark IPC loss is likely the still-conservative load-use\\ninterlock. `hazard_unit` compares a load destination against raw IF/ID rs1\\nand rs2 bitfields even when the younger instruction does not actually need\\nthose fields in EX, and it still forces a bubble for STORE rs2 even though\\nstore data is only consumed one stage later in MEM.\\n\\nThat means the core can still lose cycles to false immediate-field matches\\nand to real load-then-store-data dependencies that do not protect any EX\\ncritical operand. This is a narrow backend/control cleanup that attacks IPC\\nwithout adding predictor state, fetch-path muxing, or a new EX datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make the load-use detector stage-accurate instead of field-accurate.\\nDecode conservative `uses_rs1_ex` / `uses_rs2_ex` bits from the IF/ID\\ninstruction inside `hazard_unit`, and only stall when the older load feeds a\\nvalue that the younger instruction truly needs in EX. Keep unknown or\\nillegal encodings conservative, but stop treating OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR,\\nLUI/AUIP...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Deferred Store Queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\\nthat avoid adding logic to the fetch path: first moving DIV/REM off EX, then\\nbuffering one stalled RAM store. The current one-deep store slot still\\nserializes store bursts behind a single pending write, even though CoreMark\\nissues clustered stack, list, matrix, and state stores under the random\\n`io_dmemReady` stall model.\\n\\nA deeper store queue is the next clean extension of the one change that has\\nalready proven it can raise IPC on this RTL without paying the frontend Fmax\\npenalties seen in prior predictor experiments.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The best `bench` gains so far have come from backend-only structural changes\\nthat avoid adding logic to the fetch path: first moving DIV/REM off EX, then\\nbuffering one stalled RAM store. The current one-deep store slot still\\nserializes store bursts behind a single pending write, even though CoreMark\\nissues clustered stack, list, matrix, and state stores under the random\\n`io_dmemReady` stall model.\\n\\nA deeper store queue is the next clean extension of the one change that has\\nalready proven it can raise IPC on this RTL without paying the frontend Fmax\\npenalties seen in prior predictor experiments.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the current one-deep pending-store slot with a two-entry oldest-first\\ndeferred store queue for aligned normal-RAM stores. When `io_dmemReady` is\\nlow, the oldest queued store keeps draining to the physical bus while a\\nyounger bufferable store can still retire into the second queue entry instead\\nof freezing the whole pipeline immediately.\\n\\nKeep the existing zero-wait direct-store fast path when the queue is empty,\\nand preserve architectural ordering by continuing to fence younger loads,\\nnon-bufferable stores, and EBREAK behind any queued store. This should hide\\nmore of CoreMark's store-s...\n  Line 13: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Slot EX-Trained Loop Backedge Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The prior predictor regressions define a narrow safe zone on `bench`: same-cycle\\ndecode and target generation in ID cost too much Fmax, and even a small\\nmulti-entry fetch-side target cache adds more compare and mux work to the\\n`pc -> next_pc` cone than CoreMark can repay. But always waiting for EX to\\nredirect still burns bubbles on the tight backward loop branches that dominate\\nCoreMark's hot inner loops.\\n\\nThe best remaining predictor angle is therefore to predict less often with far\\nless hardware: specialize only for the hottest backward branch that has already\\nbeen proven taken, and keep all target information trained from EX rather than\\nrecomp