{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778871121523,"sessionID":"ses_1d308243bffeiqTNPiFD1doV7T","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_yvykx653AA8cmokExLee4F07","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r9s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\n+title: \"Decode-Armed Registered Backedge Redirect\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The predictor history on `bench` is unusually clear: every attempt that put\n+  branch decode, tag compare, target muxing, or stream control directly on the\n+  live IF `pc -> next_pc` path regressed badly on Tang Nano 20K timing, even\n+  when the IPC idea was sound. But the core still pays a repeatable taken-branch\n+  bubble on CoreMark's hot loop backedges because conditional branches only\n+  become known in EX.\n+\n+  The most promising remaining predictor angle is therefore not another faster\n+  same-cycle frontend redirect, but a predictor that uses already-registered\n+  information to fetch the target one cycle earlier without lengthening the\n+  current decode-to-PC critical path.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a one-entry EX-trained backedge predictor that is looked up in decode and\n+  consumed one cycle later from registered state. When EX resolves a taken\n+  backward conditional branch, capture that branch PC and resolved target PC in\n+  a tiny slot. When the same branch PC appears again in ID, arm a registered\n+  prediction for the following cycle instead of redirecting immediately.\n+\n+  On the next cycle, while that branch is in EX, IF uses the registered target\n+  PC as `io_imemAddr` rather than `pc + 4`, and EX compares the architectural\n+  next PC against the armed prediction so flush/recovery becomes mispredict-only.\n+  A correct hit recovers one taken-branch cycle on steady-state loop iterations,\n+  but the live fetch cone only sees a mux from registered predictor state, not\n+  same-cycle immediate generation, target addition, or IF-side tag comparison.\n+  This directly addresses the failure mode from prior predictor regressions while\n+  still targeting CoreMark's hot backedge behavior.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: low\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire a one-slot EX-trained backedge predictor between decode, IF, EX, and hazard control, including the registered predicted-target override path and mispredict-only recovery signals.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Detect a decode-time hit on the trained backedge slot and arm a one-cycle-later prediction register for the branch entering EX, without driving an immediate same-cycle PC redirect.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a registered predicted-target fetch override so IF can source `io_imemAddr` from predictor state captured on the previous cycle instead of extending the live `pc + 4` path with branch target logic.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train the predictor only from resolved taken backward conditional branches and compare the predicted next PC against the architectural result so correct hits keep the speculated target instruction while loop-exit misses still redirect safely.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Convert unconditional redirect flushing into mispredict-aware control so a correct registered prediction does not kill the early-fetched target instruction.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add branch-prediction pipeline tests for repeated taken backedges, loop-exit mispredict recovery, and random `io_imemReady` stall interaction so the one-cycle-early target fetch stays RVFI-safe.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r9s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r9s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r9s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r9s2.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\n+title: \"Decode-Armed Registered Backedge Redirect\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The predictor history on `bench` is unusually clear: every attempt that put\n+  branch decode, tag compare, target muxing, or stream control directly on the\n+  live IF `pc -> next_pc` path regressed badly on Tang Nano 20K timing, even\n+  when the IPC idea was sound. But the core still pays a repeatable taken-branch\n+  bubble on CoreMark's hot loop backedges because conditional branches only\n+  become known in EX.\n+\n+  The most promising remaining predictor angle is therefore not another faster\n+  same-cycle frontend redirect, but a predictor that uses already-registered\n+  information to fetch the target one cycle earlier without lengthening the\n+  current decode-to-PC critical path.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a one-entry EX-trained backedge predictor that is looked up in decode and\n+  consumed one cycle later from registered state. When EX resolves a taken\n+  backward conditional branch, capture that branch PC and resolved target PC in\n+  a tiny slot. When the same branch PC appears again in ID, arm a registered\n+  prediction for the following cycle instead of redirecting immediately.\n+\n+  On the next cycle, while that branch is in EX, IF uses the registered target\n+  PC as `io_imemAddr` rather than `pc + 4`, and EX compares the architectural\n+  next PC against the armed prediction so flush/recovery becomes mispredict-only.\n+  A correct hit recovers one taken-branch cycle on steady-state loop iterations,\n+  but the live fetch cone only sees a mux from registered predictor state, not\n+  same-cycle immediate generation, target addition, or IF-side tag comparison.\n+  This directly addresses the failure mode from prior predictor regressions while\n+  still targeting CoreMark's hot backedge behavior.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: low\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire a one-slot EX-trained backedge predictor between decode, IF, EX, and hazard control, including the registered predicted-target override path and mispredict-only recovery signals.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Detect a decode-time hit on the trained backedge slot and arm a one-cycle-later prediction register for the branch entering EX, without driving an immediate same-cycle PC redirect.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a registered predicted-target fetch override so IF can source `io_imemAddr` from predictor state captured on the previous cycle instead of extending the live `pc + 4` path with branch target logic.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train the predictor only from resolved taken backward conditional branches and compare the predicted next PC against the architectural result so correct hits keep the speculated target instruction while loop-exit misses still redirect safely.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Convert unconditional redirect flushing into mispredict-aware control so a correct registered prediction does not kill the early-fetched target instruction.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add branch-prediction pipeline tests for repeated taken backedges, loop-exit mispredict recovery, and random `io_imemReady` stall interaction so the one-cycle-early target fetch stays RVFI-safe.\"\n\n","files":[{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r9s2.yaml","relativePath":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r9s2.yaml","type":"add","patch":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r9s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r9s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r9s2.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\n+title: \"Decode-Armed Registered Backedge Redirect\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The predictor history on `bench` is unusually clear: every attempt that put\n+  branch decode, tag compare, target muxing, or stream control directly on the\n+  live IF `pc -> next_pc` path regressed badly on Tang Nano 20K timing, even\n+  when the IPC idea was sound. But the core still pays a repeatable taken-branch\n+  bubble on CoreMark's hot loop backedges because conditional branches only\n+  become known in EX.\n+\n+  The most promising remaining predictor angle is therefore not another faster\n+  same-cycle frontend redirect, but a predictor that uses already-registered\n+  information to fetch the target one cycle earlier without lengthening the\n+  current decode-to-PC critical path.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a one-entry EX-trained backedge predictor that is looked up in decode and\n+  consumed one cycle later from registered state. When EX resolves a taken\n+  backward conditional branch, capture that branch PC and resolved target PC in\n+  a tiny slot. When the same branch PC appears again in ID, arm a registered\n+  prediction for the following cycle instead of redirecting immediately.\n+\n+  On the next cycle, while that branch is in EX, IF uses the registered target\n+  PC as `io_imemAddr` rather than `pc + 4`, and EX compares the architectural\n+  next PC against the armed prediction so flush/recovery becomes mispredict-only.\n+  A correct hit recovers one taken-branch cycle on steady-state loop iterations,\n+  but the live fetch cone only sees a mux from registered predictor state, not\n+  same-cycle immediate generation, target addition, or IF-side tag comparison.\n+  This directly addresses the failure mode from prior predictor regressions while\n+  still targeting CoreMark's hot backedge behavior.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: low\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire a one-slot EX-trained backedge predictor between decode, IF, EX, and hazard control, including the registered predicted-target override path and mispredict-only recovery signals.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Detect a decode-time hit on the trained backedge slot and arm a one-cycle-later prediction register for the branch entering EX, without driving an immediate same-cycle PC redirect.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a registered predicted-target fetch override so IF can source `io_imemAddr` from predictor state captured on the previous cycle instead of extending the live `pc + 4` path with branch target logic.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train the predictor only from resolved taken backward conditional branches and compare the predicted next PC against the architectural result so correct hits keep the speculated target instruction while loop-exit misses still redirect safely.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Convert unconditional redirect flushing into mispredict-aware control so a correct registered prediction does not kill the early-fetched target instruction.\"\n+  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n+    description: \"Add branch-prediction pipeline tests for repeated taken backedges, loop-exit mispredict recovery, and random `io_imemReady` stall interaction so the one-cycle-early target fetch stays RVFI-safe.\"\n","additions":46,"deletions":0}],"diagnostics":{},"truncated":false},"title":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r9s2.yaml","time":{"start":1778871121520,"end":1778871121522}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_00b0bb9a157e5e1a016a076b42c9fc819bab25bb168fd1290d"}},"id":"prt_e2cfafd4b0028a47Evu59L24Gn","sessionID":"ses_1d308243bffeiqTNPiFD1doV7T","messageID":"msg_e2cfab824002PFnu6xdjOPjynb"}}
