{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778854623008,"sessionID":"ses_1d408eb53ffey4O9FrudRXGFYP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_I2GFfmDejfymhzgCiPSkfthA","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1/cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\n+Implemented a two-entry deferred store queue in `rtl/mem_stage.sv`.\n+\n+What changed\n+- Replaced the one-deep pending-store slot with a two-entry oldest-first queue using two explicit entries plus a 0/1/2 occupancy counter.\n+- Kept the direct zero-wait store fast path when the queue is empty.\n+- Allowed bufferable RAM stores to retire into the queue whenever there is space, including the same cycle that an older queued store drains.\n+- Updated `rtl/hazard_unit.sv` to consume `store_queue_nonempty` and `store_queue_full` instead of the old single-slot valid bit.\n+- Preserved fencing so queued stores still block younger loads, non-bufferable stores, and EBREAK until the queue drains.\n+- Updated `rtl/core.sv` wiring for the new queue occupancy signals.\n+- Extended `test/test_pipeline.py` with accepted-write tracing plus tests for two queued stores, oldest-first drain ordering, load fencing, and EBREAK fencing.\n+\n+Deviations from the initial plan\n+- I kept the queue entirely inside `mem_stage.sv` rather than introducing a separate module, to minimize interface churn.\n+- I allowed same-cycle dequeue+enqueue when the queue is full but `io_dmemReady` is high. This is a small throughput improvement over a strict \"full means stall\" interpretation and still preserves oldest-first ordering.\n+\n+Concerns\n+- I did not run the full cocotb suite in this iteration; the updated Python test file was syntax-checked with `python3 -m py_compile`.\n+\n+Local verification status\n+- Verilator lint: PASS\n+  `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdi